/* Glossary tooltip descriptions, keyed by Wikipedia article slug (decoded). Add terms one at a time with
   `node .claude/add-glossary.js <entry.json>` (see CLAUDE.md). Missing terms fall back to the slug name. */
window.GLOSSARY = {
"Sima_Qian": "Sima Qian was a Han official who succeeded his father as Grand Scribe of the imperial court and is reckoned the first of China's canonical historians.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> His <i>Records of the Grand Historian</i>, written privately rather than on imperial commission, runs from the legendary Xia dynasty to his own day in 130 chapters and five parts — annals, chronological tables, treatises, hereditary houses and biographies — an arrangement the official histories of the later dynasties kept.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Condemned to castration after speaking up for the general Li Ling, he wrote that he bore the punishment rather than take his own life so that he could finish the work.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>",
"Paleolithic": "The Paleolithic, or Old Stone Age, is the earliest and by far the longest phase of the Stone Age, spanning most of human prehistory; John Lubbock coined the name in 1865 from the Greek for 'old stone'.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> It began with the first use of stone tools by early humans, the oldest known assemblages dating to about 2.6 million years ago, and lasted until the end of the last Ice Age some 11,700 years ago, throughout which people lived as nomadic hunter-gatherers.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Stretching over millions of years, it encompasses the emergence and spread of the human species.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>",
"Stone_Age": "The Stone Age is the earliest and by far the longest prehistoric period, the first stage of the three-age system, defined by the manufacture of stone tools before the working of metals.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> It spans roughly the first 99 percent of human history, beginning with the oldest known stone tools and ending with the advent of metallurgy.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Its boundaries vary by region: it closed around 3300 BCE in the Near East, later across Europe and Asia, and in parts of Africa societies passed from stone straight to iron without a bronze-using age at all.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>",
"Bronze_Age": "The Bronze Age is the middle period of the three-age system, between the Stone Age and the Iron Age: the age in which, as Thomsen defined it, weapons and cutting implements were of copper or bronze.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> It is defined by the use of bronze, an alloy of copper and tin, and coincided with the rise of cities, complex states and writing, which appears in Mesopotamia and Egypt late in the 4th millennium BCE.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Because it is marked by technology rather than a date, its span varies by region: about 3300 BCE in the Near East, later elsewhere, and not at all in much of sub-Saharan Africa, where iron came first.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>",
"Iron_Age": "The Iron Age is the final stage of the three-age system, a prehistoric and protohistoric period defined by the widespread smelting and working of iron and steel — in Thomsen's words, the period in which iron was used for the articles to which it is eminently suited.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> It followed the Bronze Age, as iron implements gradually replaced bronze ones once techniques for removing impurities and controlling carbon content were mastered.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Its onset varies widely by region, beginning near the Bronze Age collapse in the Near East and centuries later in Europe, while across much of sub-Saharan Africa iron came first and no Bronze Age preceded it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>",
"Ice_Age": "An ice age is a long period during which global temperatures fall and ice sheets and mountain glaciers spread across large parts of the planet.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The rock record holds several such episodes over the past 2.4 billion years, each lasting many millions of years and alternating between colder glacial and milder interglacial phases; the current one began about 2.58 million years ago and continues, ice sheets still covering Antarctica and Greenland.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> In everyday usage the Ice Age generally means the most recent glacial phase, whose ice sheets were greatest between roughly 26,000 and 19,000 years ago, when sea level stood some 130 m (430 feet) below today's, ending about 11,700 years ago.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup>",
"Middle_Paleolithic": "The Middle Paleolithic is the middle subdivision of the Old Stone Age, spanning roughly 300,000 to 50,000 years ago across Europe, the Near East, and much of Asia.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> It is defined largely by prepared-core knapping methods such as the Levallois technique, which yielded more standardized flakes and supported the first composite tools like hafted stone-tipped spears, set into their shafts with compound adhesives that had to be made to a recipe; the Mousterian is its characteristic industry.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The period is associated with Neanderthals and early anatomically modern humans, and it produced some of the earliest known deliberate burials of the dead.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>",
"Upper_Paleolithic": "The Upper Paleolithic is the final subdivision of the Paleolithic, lasting from roughly 50,000 to 11,700 years ago and ending with the close of the last Ice Age.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> It is associated with the spread of anatomically modern humans across most of the world and with a marked increase in the variety and refinement of stone tools, especially long, narrow blades struck from prepared cores together with specialised implements of bone, antler, and ivory.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The period also saw the first widespread figurative art, including cave paintings, engravings, carved animal and human figurines, and personal ornaments.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>",
"Three-age_system": "The three-age system divides prehistory into three successive periods, the Stone Age, the Bronze Age and the Iron Age, each defined by the material that dominated tool and weapon making.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Several antiquaries had put forward something of the kind before the Danish curator Christian Jürgensen Thomsen, given charge of the Copenhagen antiquities collection in 1816, arranged it into stone, bronze and iron rooms and set the scheme out in a museum guidebook published in 1836.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Later research added refinements such as the Copper Age, and although the scheme fits the Americas and sub-Saharan Africa poorly, it remains the backbone of prehistoric chronology in Europe and the Near East.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>",
"Knapping": "Knapping is the technique of shaping stone by striking flakes from a core with a hammerstone or with a softer hammer of antler, bone, or hard wood, and by pressing flakes off the edge with a pointed tool in the method known as pressure flaking.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> It relies on fine-grained stones such as flint, chert, and obsidian, which break with a smooth, predictable conchoidal fracture that lets the worker control the size and shape of each flake.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The technique dates back to the earliest stone tools of prehistory and produced handaxes, blades, scrapers, and arrowheads; in more recent centuries knappers also made gunflints for firearms and shaped flint for building.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>",
"Hunter-gatherer": "A hunter-gatherer is a member of a society that obtains its food by hunting wild animals, fishing, and gathering wild plants, fruits, and other edible resources.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> All humans lived this way for the overwhelming majority of their existence, until the adoption of agriculture beginning roughly 12,000 years ago, so foraging accounts for well over 90 percent of human history.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Hunter-gatherer groups were typically mobile, living in small bands of a few dozen people that shared food widely under flexible and largely egalitarian leadership, and a small number of communities around the world still follow this way of life today.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>",
"Lower_Paleolithic": "The Lower Paleolithic is the earliest and longest subdivision of the Paleolithic, extending from the first stone tools about 2.6 million years ago — or 3.3 million if disputed finds are counted — to roughly 300,000 years ago.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> It was the age of early hominins such as Homo habilis, Homo erectus, and Homo heidelbergensis, who made the simple flaked cores and choppers of the Oldowan industry and later the bifacial hand axes of the Acheulean.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Some of the earliest evidence for the controlled use of fire also belongs to this period, marking a major step in human technological and behavioural development.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup>",
"Pleistocene": "The Pleistocene is the geological epoch spanning roughly 2.58 million to 11,700 years ago, the first epoch of the Quaternary Period.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> It was marked by repeated cycles of glaciation, the ice ages, during which vast sheets of ice spread across large parts of the Northern Hemisphere and sea levels fell sharply.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The epoch was the age of great land mammals such as mammoths: of the 57 plant-eating species that then weighed a tonne or more only 11 survive today, and the same epoch saw anatomically modern humans evolve in Africa and spread across nearly every continent.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup>",
"Archaeology": "Archaeology is the study of the ancient and recent human past through material remains, a range running from the million-year-old fossils of our earliest ancestors in Africa to 20th-century buildings in New York.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Its evidence is artefacts — objects made, modified or used by people — together with the non-portable ones called features, and context, the relationship artefacts have to each other and to their surroundings.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> It is especially valuable for prehistoric sites, which are by definition those without a written record, and its methods of typology, stratigraphy and technology are what made prehistory a discipline defined by evidence rather than by texts.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>",
"Megafauna": "Megafauna are the large animals of a given region or geological period, conventionally those whose adult body mass exceeds a set threshold, most often placed at about 44 kilograms (97 pounds), although proposed limits range from roughly 10 kilograms (22 pounds) to well over 1,000 kilograms (2,200 pounds).<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The Pleistocene epoch supported an especially rich megafauna, among them mammoths, mastodons, giant ground sloths and woolly rhinoceroses.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Many of these large animals died out toward the end of the last Ice Age, in a wave of extinctions that most researchers attribute to a combination of climatic change and human hunting, though the relative weight of each cause remains debated.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>",
"Australopithecus": "<i>Australopithecus</i> is an extinct genus of early hominins that lived in Africa, walking upright on two legs while keeping a small, ape-sized brain, with long strong arms and curved fingers suggesting they still climbed.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Its species include <i>A. anamensis</i> in eastern Africa from about 4.2 million years ago, <i>A. afarensis</i>, whose remains include the 3.2-million-year-old partial skeleton nicknamed Lucy, and <i>A. africanus</i> in southern Africa, whose Taung child first established that early humans occurred on the continent.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> They ate mainly plants — leaves, fruit, seeds, roots, nuts and insects — and the genus is widely regarded as ancestral to, or closely related to, the human genus <i>Homo</i>.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>",
"Sonia_Harmand": "Sonia Harmand is a French archaeologist and lithic technologist whose work focuses on the earliest stone tools and the origins of tool-making.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> She has led the West Turkana Archaeological Project in northern Kenya since 2012, dividing her career between the Centre National de la Recherche Scientifique in France and Stony Brook University in New York.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> In 2011 her team found flaked stone artefacts at the site of Lomekwi 3 that were dated to 3.3 million years ago, about 700,000 years older than any previously known, pushing the start of the archaeological record back before the appearance of the genus <i>Homo</i>.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>",
"Jason_E._Lewis": "Jason E. Lewis is an American paleoanthropologist whose research spans the earliest stone tools, the fossil record of eastern Africa, and the history of physical anthropology.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Based at Stony Brook University and its Turkana Basin Institute, he co-directs the West Turkana Archaeological Project in northern Kenya.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> He was a co-discoverer of the 3.3-million-year-old artefacts at Lomekwi 3, the oldest stone tools yet found, and he also led a re-measurement of the 19th-century collection of human skulls assembled by Samuel George Morton, which challenged the widely repeated claim that Morton had skewed his data.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>",
"Greece": "Greece is a country of 131,957 km² (50,949 sq mi) in southeastern Europe with roughly 10.4 million people, occupying the southern Balkan peninsula with several thousand islands in the Aegean, Ionian and Mediterranean seas, and its capital at Athens.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Mountains cover about four-fifths of its territory and its deeply indented coastline is the longest of any country in the Mediterranean basin, conditions that favoured seafaring and left inland communities divided by hard ground. Its ancient city-states produced the earliest known democratic government along with lasting traditions of philosophy, drama and mathematics, while the modern Greek state grew out of a war of independence against Ottoman rule and was recognised as sovereign in 1830.",
"John_Lubbock,_1st_Baron_Avebury": "John Lubbock, from 1900 the 1st Baron Avebury, was a British banker, member of parliament and naturalist who helped turn the study of prehistory into a scientific discipline.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> His survey <i>Pre-historic Times</i>, published in 1865 and revised through seven editions, introduced the terms Paleolithic and Neolithic for the earlier and later phases of the Stone Age, and both remain in everyday use.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> In parliament he carried the act that created the country's first statutory public holidays, popularly called St Lubbock's Days; his own ancient monuments bill reached a second reading seven times without becoming law, and the act that protected such sites from 1882 was framed by another hand.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>",
"Neanderthal": "Neanderthals, classified as <i>Homo neanderthalensis</i>, were a population of archaic humans who lived across Europe and western and central Asia.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> They take their name from the Neander valley near Düsseldorf, where a partial skeleton found in 1856 became the type specimen of the species, and the hundreds of fossils recovered since show a stocky, powerfully built body with a large braincase and prominent brow ridges.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> They made prepared-core stone tools, hunted large game, controlled fire and buried some of their dead, and genetic evidence shows they interbred with the ancestors of people living today, leaving a small proportion of Neanderthal DNA in most present-day populations outside Africa.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>",
"Homo_sapiens": "<i>Homo sapiens</i> is the species to which all living people belong and the only surviving member of the genus <i>Homo</i>.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Fossils assigned to the species appear in Africa by about 300,000 years ago, the earliest at Jebel Irhoud in Morocco, combining a modern small face and jaws with a long, low braincase, though these features seem to have emerged piecemeal in populations spread across the continent rather than all at once in one place.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Members of the species later settled every continent and are associated with symbolic art and complex language, and later with plants and animals domesticated independently in several regions, with cities and writing.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup>",
"Homo_erectus": "<i>Homo erectus</i> is an extinct species of early human that appeared in Africa and survived longest on the island of Java, making it one of the longest-lived species in the human genus.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> It was the first known human with body proportions close to those of people today, having long legs, short arms and a narrow trunk suited to walking and running across open country.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> It was also the first human species known to have spread beyond Africa, reaching eastern and southeastern Asia, and its African sites yield the teardrop-shaped hand axes of the Acheulean industry along with some of the earliest evidence for the controlled use of fire.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>",
"Oldowan": "The Oldowan is the earliest widespread stone tool industry, made across much of Africa and later in parts of Eurasia, with recent finds in Kenya suggesting an origin closer to 2.9 million years ago than the conventional 2.6.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Its toolkit is simple: cores struck a few times with a hammerstone to produce sharp-edged flakes, with the choppers and battered stones left behind, used for cutting meat from carcasses, breaking bones for marrow and pounding plant food.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> It takes its name from Olduvai Gorge in Tanzania, where Louis Leakey identified such tools, and its makers are thought to include <i>Homo habilis</i>, early <i>Homo erectus</i> and possibly <i>Paranthropus</i>.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>",
"Acheulean": "The Acheulean is a stone tool industry defined by the large bifacial hand axe, a core worked on both faces into a symmetrical oval or teardrop shape with a continuous cutting edge, usually with cleavers, picks and the flakes struck off in shaping them.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> It began in Africa and ended far earlier in some regions than in others, the longest-running toolmaking tradition known, and its products are found across Africa, the Middle East, Europe and southern Asia.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Named after the gravels of Saint-Acheul near Amiens in northern France, where such tools were recovered in the 1850s, it is attributed mainly to <i>Homo erectus</i> and later <i>Homo heidelbergensis</i>.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>",
"Neolithic": "The Neolithic, or New Stone Age, is the final phase of the Stone Age, in which people came to depend on cultivated crops and domesticated animals rather than wild food alone.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Its characteristic traits are permanent villages, pottery, weaving, and stone tools finished by grinding and polishing rather than flaking alone, and it is the phase in which the first monumental tombs were raised, some reopened across centuries.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Because farming was taken up at different times in different places, the period has no single span: it begins around 10,000 BCE in the Middle East and only in the 3rd millennium BCE elsewhere, ending wherever metalworking took hold.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>",
"Mesolithic": "The Mesolithic is the phase of the Stone Age between the Paleolithic and the Neolithic, covering the hunter-gatherers who adapted to the warmer, wetter world after the Ice Age.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Its tools are dominated by microliths, small blades and points struck from prepared cores and set into hafts, and its sites show heavy use of fish, shellfish, waterfowl and hazelnuts, with domesticated dogs and some of the oldest known boats.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup> Hodder Westropp introduced the term in 1866, and it is used mainly for Europe, since the sequence fits some regions poorly: the corresponding Levantine phase begins thousands of years earlier, and in Africa the label is rarely applied.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup>",
"Lomekwi": "Lomekwi is a fossil-rich stretch of badlands on the western shore of Lake Turkana in northern Kenya, where sediments laid down between roughly 3.6 and 3.2 million years ago lie exposed at the surface.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Excavations there in 1998 and 1999 recovered the flat-faced skull KNM-WT 40000, described as a new species under the name <i>Kenyanthropus platyops</i>, along with hundreds of animal fossils that together reconstruct a landscape of shallow lakes and woodland.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> A separate locality in the same area, Lomekwi 3, yielded flaked stone artefacts dated to 3.3 million years ago, the oldest stone tools known.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>",
"Kenya": "Kenya is a country in East Africa that straddles the equator, with a coast on the Indian Ocean, a central highland plateau split north to south by the Great Rift Valley, and a share of Lake Victoria in the west. Its landscapes run from coral reefs and coastal forest through dry savanna and semi-desert to the glaciated peaks of Mount Kenya, and the Rift Valley sediments, especially those around Lake Turkana, have produced one of the richest records of early human ancestors. More than 40 languages are spoken, with Swahili and English official, and it came under British control in the late 19th century before independence in 1963.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>",
"Olduvai_Gorge": "Olduvai Gorge is a steep ravine about 46 km (29 miles) long cutting through the eastern Serengeti plains of northern Tanzania, where a seasonal stream has laid bare a thick stack of lake sediments and volcanic ash layers.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Because those ash layers can be dated directly, the sequence offers an unusually well-anchored record of early human evolution, and excavations led by Louis and Mary Leakey from the 1930s onward recovered Oldowan stone tools together with hominin fossils, among them the type specimen of <i>Homo habilis</i> and the robust skull once nicknamed Zinjanthropus.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> The name is a European misrendering of the Maasai word <i>oldupai</i>, the wild sisal that grows along the gorge.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>",
"Tanzania": "Tanzania is a country in East Africa with a long Indian Ocean coast, the Zanzibar archipelago offshore, and a share of the three great lakes of the African interior, Victoria, Tanganyika and Malawi. It holds both the highest point in Africa, Kilimanjaro, and the lowest, the floor of Lake Tanganyika, and much of its north lies in the eastern branch of the Great Rift Valley, with the Serengeti plains and the Ngorongoro crater. Swahili serves as a common language across more than a hundred ethnic groups, and the modern state was formed in 1964 when Tanganyika, independent from Britain since 1961, united with the newly independent Zanzibar.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>",
"Homo_habilis": "<i>Homo habilis</i> is an extinct species of early human that lived in eastern and southern Africa.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> It had a braincase larger than earlier hominins', roughly 730 to 820 millilitres in the type specimen though still far short of ours, with a smaller face and teeth and a body that kept comparatively long arms; its name, meaning 'handy man', records the belief that it was the first maker of stone tools.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The species was established in 1964 by Louis Leakey, Phillip Tobias and John Napier from remains at Olduvai Gorge, and whether those fossils belong in <i>Homo</i>, or represent more than one species, has been argued over ever since.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>",
"Saint-Acheul": "Saint-Acheul is a quarter of the city of Amiens in northern France, built on terraces of gravel left by the river Somme, and it is the type site of the Acheulean.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Quarrying there through the 19th century turned up large numbers of bifacial flint hand axes, first reported in 1859, and in 1872 the prehistorian Gabriel de Mortillet borrowed the site's name for the whole industry those tools represent.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Part of the deposits survives as an open-air archaeological garden, listed as a historic monument in 1947 and opened to visitors in 1998.",
"France": "France is a country in western Europe reaching from the English Channel to the Mediterranean and from the Rhine to the Atlantic, with overseas regions in the Caribbean, South America and the Indian and Pacific oceans.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Its territory takes in the plains of the Paris basin, the volcanic Massif Central and the Alps and Pyrenees, and it is a major farming and industrial economy drawing most of its power from nuclear stations.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> A kingdom consolidated through the Middle Ages, it abolished the monarchy in the revolution of 1789, ruled a colonial empire across Africa and Southeast Asia, and holds a permanent seat on the United Nations Security Council.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>",
"Dmanisi": "Dmanisi is an archaeological site in the Kvemo Kartli region of southern Georgia, where hominin remains lie in volcanic and lake deposits beneath the ruins of a medieval town.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Fossils and simple stone tools recovered there since 1991 are the oldest securely dated human remains outside Africa, and the site has produced five skulls along with dozens of other bones, an unusually complete sample for so early a period.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The individuals had small brains and short bodies, and their classification is still contested: most researchers place them within <i>Homo erectus</i>, while others argue for a separate species or even for two kinds of hominin living side by side.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>",
"Georgia_(country)": "Georgia is a country of 69,700 km² (26,900 sq mi) in the Caucasus on the Black Sea, with roughly 3.8 million people and its capital at Tbilisi, where the Greater and Lesser Caucasus enclose a corridor of valleys that has carried traffic between Europe and Asia for thousands of years.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> It has its own language and alphabet, neither related to the tongues of its neighbours, and adopted Christianity in the 4th century, making it one of the oldest Christian states. Ruled at times from Persia, the Ottoman Empire and Russia, it was briefly independent after 1918, spent most of the 20th century as a Soviet republic, and regained independence in 1991.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>",
"Nomadism": "Nomadism is a way of life in which a community moves from place to place instead of settling permanently, timing its movements to the seasons, to grazing and water, or to the ripening of wild foods.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Herders who drive livestock between pastures, foragers who follow game and plant harvests, and travelling craft and trading groups all follow versions of it, and the routes are usually well known and repeated year after year rather than aimless.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Because everything must be carried, nomadic households keep their possessions few and portable, and wealth tends to be held in animals, skills and obligations between people rather than in buildings or land.",
"Pacific_Ocean": "The Pacific Ocean is the largest and deepest ocean basin, about 155 million km² (59.8 million sq mi), close to a third of the Earth's surface, holding more than half the free water on the planet.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> It reaches from the Arctic to the Southern Ocean and from Asia across to the Americas, and its floor holds the deepest known point on the planet, the Challenger Deep, measured in 2020 at 10,935 m (35,876 feet) down.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Ringed by the Pacific Ring of Fire and scattered with tens of thousands of islands, it was settled by seafarers who reached East Polynesia by about 900 CE, centuries before Magellan's fleet entered it in 1520 and named it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>",
"Pacific_Northwest_Coast": "The Pacific Northwest Coast is the narrow, forested strip of North America's western seaboard running from southern Alaska to northern California, hemmed in by mountains and cut into fjords, inlets and thousands of islands. Cool, wet and extraordinarily productive, it carried dense runs of salmon along with shellfish, sea mammals and huge conifers, and anthropologists treat it as a single culture area because its peoples share many practices despite speaking unrelated languages.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Its societies were unusual among foraging peoples in living in large permanent plank houses, storing preserved food on a great scale, and maintaining ranked social orders with inherited titles, hereditary chiefs and slavery.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>",
"Tlingit": "The Tlingit are an Indigenous people of the northern Pacific Northwest Coast, living along the panhandle of southeastern Alaska and in adjoining parts of British Columbia and the Yukon.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Their society is organised by descent through the mother into two great halves, Raven and Eagle or Wolf, subdivided into clans that own crests, songs, names and stretches of hunting and fishing country.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> They lived on salmon, halibut, sea mammals and shellfish, built large timber clan houses and raised carved poles of red cedar, and their language, Lingít, belongs to the Na-Dene family, which links it to the Athabaskan languages of the interior.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>",
"Haida": "The Haida are an Indigenous people of the northern Pacific Northwest Coast whose homeland is Haida Gwaii, an archipelago off British Columbia, with a related community in southeastern Alaska.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Haida society traces descent through the mother and is divided into two halves, Raven and Eagle, made up of clans that hold their own crests, names and stretches of land.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> They are especially known for monumental work in red cedar, including great longhouses and carved poles bearing Eagle and Raven crests, and for a carving tradition that carried through a collapse from introduced disease, from at least 20,000 people in the late 1700s to fewer than 600 a century later.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>",
"Cedar": "Cedar is a name given to a number of unrelated conifers valued for aromatic, straight-grained wood that resists decay.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> It belongs properly to <i>Cedrus</i>, four species from the mountains of the Mediterranean and the western Himalaya, but the same name is applied to many trees of the cypress family, among them the western redcedar and yellow-cedar of North America and the Japanese sugi.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> What such timbers share is heartwood carrying natural fungicidal compounds, which leaves the wood light, easy to split and long-lasting even in wet ground, and has led people in many regions to use it for roofing, boats, chests and coffins, and to burn it as incense.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>",
"Hadza_people": "The Hadza are an Indigenous people of about 1,000 around Lake Eyasi in the Rift Valley of northern Tanzania, some 300 of whom still take most of their food by hunting and by gathering tubers, berries, baobab fruit and wild honey.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> They live in short-term camps of roughly 25 to 75 people, recognise neither formal leaders nor a settled hierarchy and share meat widely, men hunting game with bows and poisoned arrows while women and children do most of the plant gathering.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Their language, Hadzane, includes click consonants and is an isolate with no undisputed relatives, and genetic work places their ancestry among the earliest lineages to diverge in Africa.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>",
"San_peoples": "The San peoples are Indigenous peoples of southern Africa, fewer than 110,000 in all, who historically lived by hunting and gathering in the Kalahari and the drier country around it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> They are not one nation but many communities with their own names, among them the Juǀʼhoansi and the ǃKung, speaking languages full of click consonants; almost every collective label for them was coined by outsiders, and San delegates settled on San in 1993 as the least loaded.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Their genetics include some of the deepest-branching lineages known, and few if any San now live chiefly by foraging, most settled on a mixed economy of hunting, wage work, craft sales and pensions.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>",
"Arctic": "The Arctic is the northernmost region of the Earth, usually defined as the area within the Arctic Circle at about 66° 33′ north, though it is also drawn at the tree line.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> It centres on an ocean rather than a continent, largely ringed by North America, Europe and Asia; above the Circle the Sun does not set at midsummer or rise at midwinter.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Tundra, sea ice and permafrost dominate it, supporting specially adapted life from lichens to caribou, polar bears and whales; much of the American Arctic has been inhabited for some 5,000 years, and the ancestors of today's Inuit and Yup'ik spread across it a thousand years ago.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>",
"Levallois": "Levallois is a method of stone toolmaking in which a core is trimmed and shaped so that a single flake of predetermined size and outline can then be struck from it in one blow.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Because the flake exists in the knapper's intention before it exists in the stone, the method is often taken as evidence of planning ahead, and it leaves a distinctive tortoise-shell pattern of scars on the discarded core.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> It appears in Africa and Eurasia by about 300,000 years ago, with contested claims for earlier examples, and takes its name from Levallois-Perret, a suburb of Paris whose gravel quarries yielded such cores.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>",
"Mousterian": "The Mousterian is a stone tool industry of the Middle Paleolithic, current in Europe, western Asia and North Africa.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Its assemblages consist of flake tools rather than large core tools, chiefly side-scrapers, points and toothed pieces struck from prepared cores, and they vary enough from site to site that archaeologists have long argued whether the differences mark separate groups, different activities, or how far a tool had been reworked before it was discarded.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> In Europe it is the work of Neanderthals and goes out of use at about the time they do, while in the Levant assemblages of the same character are associated with early modern humans as well.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>",
"Le_Moustier": "Le Moustier is a group of three superimposed rock shelters in the valley of the Vézère in the Dordogne, southwestern France, cut into limestone cliffs in a region unusually rich in Paleolithic sites.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Excavated from the early 20th century, first by Otto Hauser and then by Denis Peyrony, its deep sequence of occupation layers supplied archaeologists with the reference collection of Middle Paleolithic flake tools, and the site's name was taken for the Mousterian industry as a whole.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Two Neanderthal individuals were recovered there in the early 20th century, among them a well-known adolescent skeleton whose removal and sale to a German museum caused lasting controversy in France.",
"Prehistory": "Prehistory is the long stretch of the human past that came before written records, beginning with the first stone tools about 2.6 million years ago and closing with the appearance of writing.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Because it left no documents, it is known almost entirely from material traces such as tools, bones, hearths, dwellings, burials and art, which archaeology reconstructs with help from geology, genetics and the study of ancient climates.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> It has no single end date: writing appeared in Mesopotamia and Egypt by about 3200 BCE but reached other regions thousands of years later, and a people first described in the records of its literate neighbours is said to belong to protohistory.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>",
"Denmark": "Denmark is a Nordic country of 42,938 km² (16,579 sq mi) in northern Europe with about 6 million people, made up of the Jutland peninsula and an archipelago of some 400 islands, of which Zealand and Funen are the largest.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> It is low-lying and intensively farmed, with no point much above 170 m (560 feet) and no district far from the sea, and its capital, Copenhagen, stands on the eastern side of Zealand facing Sweden across a narrow strait.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> A constitutional monarchy with a parliamentary government, it forms together with the self-governing territories of the Faroe Islands and Greenland the wider realm known as the Kingdom of Denmark.",
"Christian_Jürgensen_Thomsen": "Christian Jürgensen Thomsen was a Danish antiquarian and curator who established the three-age system, dividing prehistory into Stone, Bronze and Iron Ages, an idea he himself called an old one and which several antiquaries had published before him.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Put in charge of Denmark's national antiquities collection in 1816, he faced a mass of undated objects and sorted them by the material of their tools and weapons, checking the order against finds recovered together in a grave or hoard.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> He set it out in the 1836 guidebook <i>Ledetraad til Nordisk Oldkyndighed</i>, appearing in German the next year and in English in 1848, and it became the framework of prehistoric chronology.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>",
"National_Museum_of_Denmark": "The National Museum of Denmark, in Copenhagen, is the country's largest museum of cultural history, its holdings running from the Stone Age to the present and taking in ethnographic, classical and coin collections.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> It grew out of a royal commission for the preservation of antiquities set up in 1807, its collection beginning in a church loft and arranged by Christian Jürgensen Thomsen; it reached the Prince's Mansion in 1855, and in 1892 the royal collections were gathered into one National Museum.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Its best-known objects are the Trundholm sun chariot, the Gundestrup cauldron and the Bronze Age burials in hollowed oak coffins, including the young woman known as the Egtved Girl.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>",
"Near_East": "The Near East is a name for the lands of western Asia about the eastern Mediterranean, usually taken to cover Anatolia, the Levant, Mesopotamia, Arabia and Iran, and sometimes Egypt and the Caucasus.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> It came into use in 19th-century Europe, which ranked the regions of Asia by their distance from itself, so the name marks a position rather than any shared frontier, culture or language, and its limits have never been firmly drawn.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> It survives chiefly among archaeologists, historians and biblical scholars, for whom it names the region where farming, cities and writing first appeared, while writing on current affairs generally calls much the same area the Middle East.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>",
"Europe": "Europe occupies the western part of the Eurasian landmass, divided from Asia by a conventional line along the Urals, the Caspian, the Caucasus and the Black Sea — a boundary of custom rather than geology, since geologists count Eurasia as one continent.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> At about 10.2 million km² (3.94 million sq mi) it is the second-smallest continent, yet its indented coastline, navigable rivers and mild climate have long supported dense settlement, holding roughly 745 million people in about 50 countries.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Large peninsulas and island groups break its outline — Scandinavia, Iberia, Italy, the Balkans, the British Isles — while much of it lies in the plain running east from France into Russia.",
"Asia": "Asia is the largest and most populous continent, roughly 44.5 million km² (17.2 million sq mi), close to 30 percent of the world's land, with about 4.8 billion people.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> It fills the eastern and greater part of the Eurasian landmass, which geologists count as a single continent, and runs from the Himalaya and the Tibetan plateau through the Siberian forests and the deserts of Arabia to the crowded plains of the Indus, Ganges, Mekong, Yangtze and Yellow.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> It holds both the highest point on land, Everest, and the lowest, the shore of the Dead Sea, and its river valleys were the setting for several of the earliest farming societies, cities and writing systems.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>",
"Homo": "<i>Homo</i> is the genus of hominins that takes in modern people together with their closest extinct relatives, its name simply the Latin word for 'a human being'.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> It was established by Carl Linnaeus in 1758 for living humans and later widened to fossil forms such as <i>Homo habilis</i>, <i>Homo erectus</i> and <i>Homo neanderthalensis</i>, and the oldest fossil generally assigned to it is a jaw from Ethiopia.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Its members are set apart by larger brains relative to body size, smaller teeth and jaws and a long association with stone toolmaking, though how many species the genus holds is still argued over, and only <i>Homo sapiens</i> survives.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>",
"Neolithic_Revolution": "The Neolithic Revolution was the shift from hunting and gathering to farming and settled village life, a change named by the archaeologist V. Gordon Childe in 1936 and now understood as a slow transition rather than a sudden one.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> It began around 12,000 years ago and happened independently in at least 11 regions, among them western Asia, China, Mesoamerica, the Andes, New Guinea and eastern North America, each domesticating its own crops and animals over many generations.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Its consequences reached far: permanent settlements, stored surpluses of food, rapid population growth, private property and inherited rank, and the new diseases that came with crowding and close contact with livestock.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>",
"Fertile_Crescent": "The Fertile Crescent is a curved band of comparatively well-watered land sweeping from the Levantine coast through southern Anatolia and southeast along the Tigris and Euphrates to the Gulf, named by the Egyptologist James Henry Breasted in 1914.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Its rains, rivers and wild grasses gave it an unusual concentration of species suited to domestication: wheat, barley, lentils, sheep, goats, cattle and pigs were all domesticated there between about 12,000 and 11,000 years ago.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Some of the earliest known farming villages, towns, cities and writing systems appeared there, and its first farmers descended from local hunter-gatherers rather than arriving from elsewhere, hence its description as a cradle of civilization.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>",
"Sahara": "The Sahara is the largest hot desert on Earth, stretching across northern Africa from the Atlantic to the Red Sea and covering some 9.5 million km² (3.7 million sq mi).<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Most of it is not dune but rock and gravel — bare plateaus, boulder-strewn plains and dry valleys — with sand seas over roughly a quarter of it and the volcanic massifs of Tibesti and Ahaggar rising above 3,000 m (9,800 feet). Much of it receives under 100 mm (3.9 inches) of rain a year, yet between about 11,000 and 5,000 years ago a wetter climate filled it with lakes, grassland and the herds of its first pastoralists, a landscape recorded in the rock art of Tassili n'Ajjer.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>",
"Australia": "Australia is a country and a continent in the southern hemisphere, made up of the Australian mainland, the island of Tasmania and many smaller islands, covering about 7.7 million km² (3.0 million sq mi), the sixth-largest country in the world.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> It is the driest inhabited continent: deserts and dry scrub fill the interior, so almost all of its roughly 27 million people live along the eastern, southeastern and southwestern coasts, and its long isolation left it with plants and animals found nowhere else.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander peoples have lived there for at least 50,000 years, British colonisation began in 1788, and the separate colonies joined into a single federal nation in 1901.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>",
"Americas": "The Americas are the landmass of the western hemisphere, North and South America with Central America and the Caribbean, running from the Arctic almost to Antarctica across roughly 42.5 million km² (16.4 million sq mi).<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Their western edge is a nearly unbroken chain of mountains from Alaska through the Rockies to the Andes, while the Mississippi, Orinoco, Amazon and Paraná basins drain the lowlands to the east. People reached them from north-eastern Asia by way of Beringia and had spread widely through both continents more than 14,000 years ago, developing farming, cities and states independently; the name comes from Amerigo Vespucci, first given to the southern landmass on a world map of 1507.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>",
"North_America": "North America is the continent forming the northern part of the western hemisphere, linked to the southern landmass by the Isthmus of Panama, with an area of about 24.7 million km² (9.54 million sq mi).<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Its west is dominated by the mountain belt of the Cordillera, its northeast by the worn ancient rock of the Canadian Shield, and between them lie the Great Plains, the Great Lakes and the basin of the Mississippi and Missouri, while its southern reach narrows into tropical Central America and the Caribbean. Around 600 million people live there today, and long before European ships arrived it supported societies ranging from Arctic hunters to the maize-farming cities of Mesoamerica.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>",
"South_America": "South America forms the southern part of the western hemisphere, joined at the Isthmus of Panama, about 17.8 million km² (6.87 million sq mi), holding roughly 433 million people.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The Andes run almost 7,000 km (4,300 miles) down its western side, the longest range on any continent, and east of them the Amazon basin with the largest rainforest and river on Earth, the Pampas, and the dry plateaus of Patagonia and Atacama. It was one of the few places where farming and complex states arose independently, giving the world the potato, quinoa and the llama and societies such as the Moche, Tiwanaku and the Inca, before Spanish and Portuguese conquest in the 16th century reshaped it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>",
"India": "India is a country in South Asia occupying most of the Indian subcontinent, the seventh largest in the world at about 3.3 million km² (1.3 million sq mi) and, with roughly 1.4 billion people, the most populous.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The Himalaya wall it off to the north, the Ganges and Indus plains carry much of its farming and population, the Deccan plateau fills the peninsula, and its year turns on the summer monsoon. It holds the Indus valley cities of the 3rd millennium BCE and the origins of Hinduism, Buddhism, Jainism and Sikhism, recognises 22 languages in its constitution, came under British rule in the 18th and 19th centuries, and became independent in 1947.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>",
"Russia": "Russia is the largest country in the world by area, covering about 17.1 million km² (6.60 million sq mi) and 11 time zones, reaching from the Baltic across eastern Europe and the whole of northern Asia to the Pacific.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Most of its roughly 145 million people live in the western fifth, around Moscow and St Petersburg, while beyond the Urals lie the taiga of the West Siberian Plain and Lake Baikal, the deepest lake on Earth. It grew out of the principality of Moscow into an empire that reached the Pacific in the 17th century, formed the core of the Soviet Union between 1922 and 1991, and has since been the Russian Federation.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>",
"China": "China is a country in East Asia covering about 9.6 million km² (3.7 million sq mi), among the largest in the world, and home to some 1.4 billion people.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Its land falls in steps from west to east: the Tibetan plateau and the Himalaya, the deserts of the northwest, the loess uplands and finally the plains where the Yellow River and the Yangtze reach the sea, along which farming and population have always concentrated. Its farming villages are among the oldest anywhere, its written records run continuously from about 1200 BCE, and after more than two thousand years of dynastic rule it became a republic in 1912 and the People's Republic in 1949.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>",
"Japan": "Japan is an island country in East Asia, an archipelago of more than 14,000 islands off the northeast of the mainland, of which Honshu, Hokkaido, Kyushu and Shikoku carry almost all its land and people, some 124 million.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> About three-quarters of it is mountainous and forested, so cities and farmland crowd onto narrow coastal plains, and because tectonic plates meet beneath it there are frequent earthquakes and over a hundred active volcanoes, among them Fuji. A centralised state took shape from the 7th century, military governments ruled in the emperor's name from 1185, contact with Europe was restricted until the reforms of 1868, and it is now a constitutional monarchy.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>",
"Brazil": "Brazil is the largest country in South America and the fifth largest in the world, covering about 8.5 million km² (3.3 million sq mi) and holding more than 200 million people, and it borders every country on the continent except Chile and Ecuador.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The Amazon basin fills its north with the largest rainforest and river system on Earth, while the interior carries the cerrado and the seasonally flooded Pantanal and most of its cities lie along the Atlantic coast. Claimed by Portugal in 1500 and built on a plantation economy that brought in more enslaved Africans than any other American colony, it became independent in 1822 and a republic in 1889.",
"Albania": "Albania is a country of 28,748 km² (11,100 sq mi) in southeastern Europe with roughly 2.4 million people, on the western side of the Balkan peninsula, with coasts on the Adriatic and Ionian seas facing Italy across a narrow strait.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Mountains cover about three-quarters of it, rising steeply behind a narrow coastal plain where most of the farmland and the larger towns lie, among them the capital, Tirana.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Ruled by the Ottomans for more than four centuries, it became independent in 1912, spent 1944 to 1991 under Enver Hoxha, one of the most diplomatically isolated countries in the world, then joined NATO in 2009 and became a candidate for European Union membership.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup>",
"Andorra": "Andorra is a small landlocked country of 468 km² (181 sq mi) high in the eastern Pyrenees between France and Spain, with about 83,000 people and its capital at Andorra la Vella.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Almost nine people in ten live in towns, 34 per cent of the land is forested, and income per head was 46,812 US dollars in 2023, while the population has grown by about a seventh in a decade.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> It is a co-principality, the Bishop of Urgell in Spain and the President of France serving as its two co-princes under an arrangement centuries old; it ratified a modern constitution on 14 March 1993 and joined the United Nations that July.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>",
"Armenia": "Armenia is a landlocked country of 29,743 km² (11,484 sq mi) in the South Caucasus, bordered by Turkey, Georgia, Azerbaijan and Iran, with roughly 3 million people and its capital at Yerevan.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Almost all of it is high ground, a volcanic plateau averaging about 1,800 m (5,900 feet) and dotted with extinct cones, with Lake Sevan, one of the world's largest high-altitude lakes, in its east. It has its own branch of the Indo-European family and its own alphabet, devised in the early 5th century, adopted Christianity as a state religion at the beginning of the 4th, and after centuries of Persian, Ottoman and Russian rule was a Soviet republic until independence in 1991.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>",
"Austria": "Austria is a landlocked country of 83,879 km² (32,386 sq mi) in central Europe with roughly 9 million people, bordered by eight states and centred on its capital, Vienna, in the northeast.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The Alps cover about three-fifths of it, so most of the population lives in the lower east and along the Danube, which crosses the country and carries its main corridor of towns and farmland. Its ruling house, the Habsburgs, built one of Europe's great dynastic empires from Vienna, which ended with defeat in 1918; the small republic that followed was annexed by Germany in 1938, and since regaining full sovereignty in 1955 it has been a neutral federal republic.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>",
"Azerbaijan": "Azerbaijan is a country of 86,600 km² (33,400 sq mi) in the South Caucasus with roughly 10 million people, lying on the western shore of the Caspian Sea, with its capital at Baku and a separate exclave, Nakhchivan, cut off by Armenian territory.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The Greater and Lesser Caucasus ranges enclose a wide central lowland where the Kura runs to the Caspian, and its climate ranges from semi-desert to humid subtropical. Its people speak a Turkic language and are mostly Muslim; the Baku oilfields were among the first drilled on a large scale in the 19th century, and the country was a Soviet republic from 1922 until independence in 1991.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>",
"Belarus": "Belarus is a landlocked country of 207,600 km² (80,150 sq mi) in eastern Europe with roughly 9 million people, bordered by Russia, Ukraine, Poland, Lithuania and Latvia, and governed from its capital, Minsk.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> It is almost entirely low and flat, a land of forests, some 11,000 lakes and the great Pripet marshes in the south, with no mountains and its highest ground barely above 340 m (1,100 feet). Its territory belonged in turn to the Grand Duchy of Lithuania, the Polish-Lithuanian Commonwealth and the Russian Empire, it lost a quarter of its people in the Second World War, and it was a Soviet republic until independence in 1991.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>",
"Belgium": "Belgium is a country of 30,689 km² (11,849 sq mi) in western Europe with roughly 11.8 million people, facing the North Sea between the Netherlands, Germany and France, with its capital at Brussels.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The land rises gently from coastal polders and the densely built northern plain to the wooded Ardennes, and its position at the mouth of the Scheldt made Bruges, Ghent and Antwerp among the richest trading centres of medieval Europe. It is a federal state divided by language, Dutch-speaking in Flanders, French-speaking in Wallonia and with a small German-speaking community in the east; it broke from the Netherlands in 1830 and hosts the main institutions of the European Union.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>",
"Bosnia_and_Herzegovina": "Bosnia and Herzegovina is a country of 51,209 km² (19,772 sq mi) in the western Balkans with roughly 3.1 million people, almost landlocked apart from a short Adriatic coast at Neum, and governed from Sarajevo.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Most of it is rugged upland: the wooded Dinaric ranges of Bosnia in the north, and the drier limestone karst of Herzegovina in the south, where sinkholes and underground rivers replace surface water. A medieval kingdom, then Ottoman for four centuries, then Austro-Hungarian from 1878 and Yugoslav, it fought a war of three and a half years that the Dayton Agreement of 1995 ended, giving it the constitution that is its Annex 4.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>",
"Bulgaria": "Bulgaria is a country of 110,996 km² (42,856 sq mi) in southeastern Europe with roughly 6.4 million people, with a Black Sea coast and its capital at Sofia.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The Balkan Mountains run across it from west to east, dividing the northern plain along the Danube from the Thracian lowland and the Rhodope and Rila massifs of the south. A Bulgarian state was founded in 681 and its monastic schools developed the Cyrillic alphabet later used across much of the Slavic world; after nearly five centuries of Ottoman rule it regained independence in stages from 1878, spent 1946 to 1989 under communist government, and joined the European Union in 2007.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>",
"Croatia": "Croatia is a country of 56,594 km² (21,851 sq mi) in southeastern Europe with roughly 3.9 million people, curving from the plains of the Danube basin around the Dinaric mountains to a long Adriatic coast, with its capital at Zagreb.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> That coast is its defining feature: more than 1,000 islands and a shoreline of drowned limestone ridges, backed by bare karst uplands, while the fertile Pannonian lowland grows most of its grain. A medieval kingdom, it entered a union with Hungary in 1102 and later came under Habsburg rule, joined Yugoslavia after 1918, declared independence in 1991 and fought a war until 1995, and joined the European Union in 2013.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>",
"Cyprus": "Cyprus is an island country of 9,251 km² (3,572 sq mi) in the eastern Mediterranean, lying south of Turkey, with its capital at Nicosia and about 1.4 million people on the island as a whole.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Two ranges frame it, the Troodos massif in the centre and the Kyrenia ridge along the north coast, with a hot dry plain between them.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Greek and Turkish speaking communities have long shared the island, a British colony from 1925 until independence in 1960; a Turkish-controlled area in the north declared itself a republic in 1983, recognised by Turkey alone, and Eurostat counts only the 980,000 people in the area the government controls.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>",
"Czech_Republic": "The Czech Republic is a landlocked country of 78,871 km² (30,452 sq mi) in central Europe with roughly 10.9 million people, bordered by Germany, Poland, Slovakia and Austria, with its capital at Prague.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> It is made up of the historic lands of Bohemia, a basin ringed by wooded mountains and drained by the Vltava and the Elbe, with Moravia and a corner of Silesia in the east. The Kingdom of Bohemia was a major power of the Holy Roman Empire, the lands passed to the Habsburgs in 1526, joined the new state of Czechoslovakia in 1918, and became a separate republic when that country divided peacefully in 1993.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>",
"Estonia": "Estonia is a country of 45,339 km² (17,505 sq mi) on the eastern shore of the Baltic Sea with roughly 1.4 million people, bordered by Latvia and Russia, with its capital at Tallinn.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> It is low, forested and dotted with bogs and islands, its highest hill reaching only 318 m (1,040 feet), with long winters and a maritime climate.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Its language belongs to the Finnic family rather than to those of its neighbours; independent from 1918, it was annexed by the Soviet Union in 1940 in an incorporation the United States never recognised, its statehood was restored in 1991, and it joined NATO and the European Union in 2004.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>",
"Finland": "Finland is a country of 338,363 km² (130,643 sq mi) in northern Europe with roughly 5.6 million people, bordered by Sweden, Norway and Russia and facing the Baltic, with its capital at Helsinki.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Forest covers about three-quarters of it and some 188,000 lakes fill hollows left by the ice sheets, so most of the land is a low mosaic of water, bog and pine, thinning northward into the fells of Lapland. Its language belongs to the Finnic family, it formed the eastern half of the Swedish realm until 1809 and then an autonomous grand duchy under the Russian tsars, declared independence in 1917, joined the European Union in 1995 and NATO in 2023.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>",
"Germany": "Germany is a country of 357,596 km² (138,069 sq mi) in central Europe with roughly 84 million people, the most populous member of the European Union, governed from its capital, Berlin.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The land rises in three steps from the flat, sandy plain along the North Sea and Baltic coasts, through the wooded central uplands and the valleys of the Rhine and Elbe, to the Alpine foreland and the mountains of the far south. Its territory was for centuries a patchwork of principalities within the Holy Roman Empire, was united under Prussian leadership in 1871, was divided between rival states from 1949 after defeat in the Second World War, and was reunified in 1990.",
"Hungary": "Hungary is a landlocked country of 93,030 km² (35,920 sq mi) in the Carpathian Basin of central Europe, with roughly 9.6 million people and its capital at Budapest.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Most of it is flat and open, the Great Hungarian Plain stretching east of the Danube, with low hills to the north and west and Lake Balaton, the largest lake in central Europe, in its western half. Its language is Uralic and unrelated to those of its neighbours, a Christian kingdom was established there in the year 1000, the land was fought over by Ottomans and Habsburgs, lost about two-thirds of its territory after the First World War, and joined the European Union in 2004.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>",
"Iceland": "Iceland is an island country of 103,000 km² (39,800 sq mi) in the North Atlantic, with roughly 390,000 people at four to the square kilometre, 93.9 per cent of them in towns and 216,400 in the capital, Reykjavík.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Only 0.5 per cent of its land is forested, its population has grown by about a sixth in a decade, and income per head was 80,827 US dollars in 2023 against 52,930 in 2015.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> It took over its own foreign relations in April 1940 after Germany occupied Denmark, became a republic by plebiscite on 17 June 1944, was recognised by the United States that day, and was a founder of NATO in 1949.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>",
"Ireland": "Ireland is a country of 70,273 km² (27,133 sq mi) with roughly 5.4 million people, occupying about five-sixths of its island and governed from Dublin; the northeast, Northern Ireland, is part of the United Kingdom.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> A ring of coastal hills encloses a low, damp plain of pasture, lakes and bog, and a mild wet Atlantic climate keeps grass growing year-round, making livestock its mainstay. Christianised in the 5th century, it came under English and then British rule from the 12th, lost about a quarter of its people in the famine of 1845 to 1852, won independence for 26 of its counties in 1921 and joined what is now the European Union in 1973.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>",
"Italy": "Italy is a country of 302,068 km² (116,629 sq mi) in southern Europe with roughly 59 million people, a boot-shaped peninsula reaching into the Mediterranean with Sicily, Sardinia and smaller islands, governed from Rome.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The Alps close it off in the north above the plain of the Po, the Apennines run the length of the peninsula, and an active plate boundary gives it earthquakes and the volcanoes Vesuvius and Etna. From the peninsula the Roman state grew into an empire ruling the whole Mediterranean; the land was later divided among city-states and foreign powers, produced the Renaissance, and was united into a kingdom in 1861 and a republic in 1946.",
"Kosovo": "Kosovo is a landlocked territory of 10,887 km² (4,203 sq mi) in the western Balkans with roughly 1.6 million people and its capital at Pristina.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> It is a high basin ringed by mountains that rise above 2,500 m (8,200 feet) along its southern and western rims, and its population is largely Albanian-speaking with a Serb minority concentrated in the north.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Held by medieval Serbian rulers and then for four centuries by the Ottomans, it was an autonomous province of Serbia within Yugoslavia until 1989, was placed under United Nations administration after a NATO air campaign in 1999, and declared independence in 2008, a step the International Court of Justice found lawful and Serbia rejects.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>",
"Latvia": "Latvia is a country of 64,594 km² (24,940 sq mi) on the eastern shore of the Baltic Sea with roughly 1.9 million people and its capital at Riga.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> It is low and heavily wooded, forest covering 54.9 per cent of it, with long sandy beaches along the Gulf of Riga and the Daugava reaching the sea at the capital.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Recognised as an independent state by the United States in 1922, it was annexed by the Soviet Union in 1940 — an incorporation the United States never recognised, holding relations to have continued uninterrupted — and its restoration was recognised in 1991, after which it joined NATO and the European Union in 2004.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>",
"Liechtenstein": "Liechtenstein is a landlocked microstate of 160 km² (62 sq mi) in the Alps between Switzerland and Austria, with about 40,000 inhabitants and its capital at Vaduz.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> It occupies the eastern side of the upper Rhine valley, a narrow strip backed by steep slopes rising above 2,500 m (8,200 feet), the heights given over to pasture and forest. It is a constitutional monarchy ruled by a prince, took its name from a princely family that bought two small lordships in the early 18th century, and is closely tied to Switzerland, with which it shares a currency and a customs union, and which conducted its foreign affairs for much of the 20th century.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>",
"Lithuania": "Lithuania is a country of 65,286 km² (25,207 sq mi) on the southeastern shore of the Baltic Sea with roughly 2.9 million people and its capital at Vilnius.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> It is gently rolling lowland scattered with some 3,000 lakes, and its short coast is closed off by the Curonian Spit, a narrow dune barrier. Its language is the most conservative surviving Indo-European tongue; the Grand Duchy of Lithuania was one of the largest states in 14th-century Europe and later joined Poland in a commonwealth, and the country was partitioned away in 1795, annexed by the Soviet Union in 1940, and the first Soviet republic to declare independence restored, in 1990.",
"Luxembourg": "Luxembourg is a landlocked country of 2,586 km² (998 sq mi) in western Europe with roughly 680,000 people, wedged between Belgium, Germany and France, with its capital of the same name built above deep river gorges.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The wooded hills and valleys of the Ardennes fill its northern third, while the lower, more fertile Gutland in the south carries most of the population, the farmland and the vineyards along the Moselle.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> It is a grand duchy and a parliamentary constitutional monarchy, its people commonly use Luxembourgish, French and German side by side, it was a founding member of the European Communities in 1958, and its income per head is about 128,900 US dollars.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>",
"Malta": "Malta is an island country of 316 km² (122 sq mi) in the central Mediterranean south of Sicily, with roughly 570,000 people at more than 1,700 to the square kilometre, and its capital is Valletta.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The islands are mainly low, rocky and flat, with many coastal cliffs and the three largest inhabited; only 1.4 per cent of the land is forested and 94.7 per cent of the people live in towns.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> It was a Crown Colony of the United Kingdom from 1814 until independence in 1964, when it joined the Commonwealth, and it entered the European Union in 2004, the Schengen area in 2007 and the euro area in 2008.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>",
"Moldova": "Moldova is a landlocked country of 33,846 km² (13,068 sq mi) in eastern Europe with roughly 2.4 million people, lying between Romania and Ukraine, with its capital at Chișinău.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Rolling hills and deep black soil lie between the Prut and Dniester rivers, and farming employs half of everyone in work while making a tenth of gross value added.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Known historically as Bessarabia, it passed to the Russian Empire in 1812, to Romania after 1918 and to the Soviet Union, whose republic it was until 1991; a strip east of the Dniester has lain outside government control since a conflict in 1992, and an OSCE mission has worked towards a settlement since 1993.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>",
"Monaco": "Monaco is a sovereign principality of 2 km² (0.77 sq mi) on the Mediterranean coast, surrounded on land by France, with about 38,000 people at 25,732.2 to the square kilometre.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Services make up 88.5 per cent of its gross value added, income per head reached 256,581 US dollars in 2023, and 70.2 per cent of its residents were born abroad.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Annexed to the First French Republic in 1793, it regained its independence in 1814, stood under Piedmont-Sardinia from 1815 to 1861 and under the military safeguard of France since, joined the United Nations in 1993, and began receiving foreign ambassadors after renegotiating its 1918 treaty with France in 2005.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>",
"Montenegro": "Montenegro is a country of 13,812 km² (5,333 sq mi) in the western Balkans with about 620,000 people, holding a stretch of Adriatic coast, with its capital at Podgorica.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Behind a narrow shore of bays and beaches, including the deeply indented Bay of Kotor, the land climbs abruptly into bare limestone mountains cut by the Tara canyon, with Lake Skadar on the Albanian border.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The Great Powers recognised it as an independent principality at the Congress of Berlin in 1878; it was absorbed into Yugoslavia after the First World War, declared independence again from its union with Serbia in 2006, and has since joined NATO and become a candidate for European Union membership.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>",
"Netherlands": "The Netherlands is a country of 41,543 km² (16,040 sq mi) in western Europe with roughly 18 million people, facing the North Sea between Belgium and Germany; its capital is Amsterdam and its government sits at The Hague.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> It is exceptionally flat and low: about a quarter of the land lies below sea level and would flood without a constantly maintained system of dunes, dikes, pumps and polders. Its 17th-century merchant republic built a trading empire reaching Asia, the Americas and southern Africa and a celebrated age of painting and science; the modern kingdom dates from 1815, and it was one of the six founding members of what became the European Union.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>",
"North_Macedonia": "North Macedonia is a landlocked country of 25,713 km² (9,928 sq mi) in the southern Balkans with roughly 1.8 million people, with its capital at Skopje.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Mountains cover most of it and the Vardar cuts a broad valley through the middle, carrying the main route from central Europe to the Aegean, while forest covers about two-fifths of the land.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Its Slavic-speaking majority lives alongside a large Albanian minority; the territory became a republic of Yugoslavia and gained independence in 1991, took its present name in 2019 under the Prespa Accord with Greece, joined NATO in 2020 and has been a candidate for European Union membership since 2005.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>",
"Norway": "Norway is a country of about 324,000 km² (125,000 sq mi) in northern Europe with roughly 5.6 million people, on the western side of the Scandinavian peninsula, with its capital at Oslo.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Mountains and high plateau cover most of it, glaciers of the last ice age carved the deep fjords of its coast, and the warm Atlantic current keeps its harbours ice-free far north. Its seafarers ranged across the north Atlantic in the Viking age; after unions with Denmark and Sweden it regained full independence in 1905, and since oil and gas were found offshore in 1969 it has placed much of the revenue in one of the world's largest public investment funds.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>",
"Poland": "Poland is a country of 312,696 km² (120,733 sq mi) in central Europe with roughly 37 million people, on the Baltic Sea; its capital is Warsaw.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Most of it is open lowland plain crossed by the Vistula and the Oder, rich in farmland and coal but without natural defences east or west; the Carpathians rise along its southern border. A Christian kingdom was founded there in 966; it joined Lithuania in a commonwealth that was 16th-century Europe's largest state, was partitioned out of existence in 1795, returned to the map in 1918, lost about a fifth of its people in the Second World War, and joined the European Union in 2004.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>",
"Portugal": "Portugal is a country of 92,230 km² (35,610 sq mi) on the western edge of Iberia with roughly 10.5 million people, bordered on land only by Spain and including the Azores and Madeira; its capital is Lisbon.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The Tagus divides it: a green, rainier north of small farms and terraced vineyards, and a hotter, flatter south of plains, cork oak and olive groves down to the Algarve. An independent kingdom from 1143, it opened sea routes down Africa and across the oceans in the 15th and 16th centuries, building an empire lasting into the 1970s; it became a republic in 1910, returned to democracy in 1974 and joined the European Union in 1986.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>",
"Romania": "Romania is a country of 238,398 km² (92,046 sq mi) in southeastern Europe with roughly 19 million people, where the lower Danube meets the Black Sea, and its capital is Bucharest.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The Carpathians sweep through it in a great arc, enclosing the plateau of Transylvania, while the Danube ends in a broad delta that is one of the largest wetlands in Europe. Its language is Romance, descended from the Latin of Roman Dacia and unusual among its Slavic and Hungarian-speaking neighbours; Wallachia and Moldavia united in 1859, the kingdom that followed doubled in size after the First World War, and after communism fell in 1989 the country joined the European Union in 2007.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>",
"San_Marino": "San Marino is a landlocked microstate of 61 km² (24 sq mi) enclosed entirely by Italy, near the Adriatic coast, with about 34,000 inhabitants and its capital in the town of the same name.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Its territory is a cluster of hills dominated by Monte Titano, a limestone ridge rising to 739 m (2,420 feet) whose three fortified towers stand above the old town and appear on the state's flag. It presents itself as the oldest surviving republic in the world, tracing its foundation by tradition to the year 301, keeps a constitution based on statutes of 1600, and is governed by two captains regent elected together for terms of six months.",
"Serbia": "Serbia is a landlocked country in the central Balkans with roughly 6.6 million people and its capital at Belgrade, covering 88,444 km² (34,148 sq mi) on a United Nations figure whose count of the population expressly excludes Kosovo.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The Pannonian plain fills the north and the land to the south rises into hills and wooded mountains. A medieval kingdom that became an empire in the 14th century, it fell under Ottoman rule, won autonomy from 1815 and independence in 1878, formed the core of the Yugoslav state of 1918, became a republic when its union with Montenegro ended in 2006, and contested Kosovo’s 2008 independence before the International Court of Justice.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>",
"Slovakia": "Slovakia is a landlocked country of 49,035 km² (18,933 sq mi) in central Europe with roughly 5.4 million people, with its capital at Bratislava on the Danube.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The Carpathian mountains fill its north and centre, rising in the High Tatras above 2,600 m (8,500 feet) and cut by wooded valleys and caves, while the south opens into the lowland plain along the Danube where most of the farmland lies. Its lands were part of the Kingdom of Hungary until 1918, when they joined the Czech lands in Czechoslovakia; it became a separate republic when that country divided in 1993, and joined the European Union in 2004 and the euro in 2009.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>",
"Slovenia": "Slovenia is a country of 20,273 km² (7,827 sq mi) in central Europe with roughly 2.1 million people, where the Alps, the Pannonian plain and the Dinaric uplands meet above a short strip of Adriatic coast, with its capital at Ljubljana.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Forest covers 61.3 per cent of it, and its limestone southwest gave the word karst to geography, a landscape of sinkholes, disappearing rivers and long caves.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Its Slavic-speaking population lived for centuries under Habsburg rule and joined Yugoslavia after 1918; it won independence in 1991, joined NATO and the European Union in 2004, adopted the euro in 2007 and entered the Schengen area the same year.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>",
"Spain": "Spain is a country of 506,008 km² (195,371 sq mi) in southwestern Europe with roughly 48 million people, filling most of the Iberian peninsula with the Balearic and Canary Islands; its capital is Madrid.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Much of it is high, the central Meseta sitting above 600 m (2,000 feet) inside a ring of mountains, making Spain one of the driest and most mountainous countries in Europe. Muslim and Christian states shared the peninsula for nearly eight centuries before Castile and Aragon were joined at the end of the 15th century; a global empire followed, and after civil war and dictatorship the country returned to democracy in the 1970s and joined the European Union in 1986.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>",
"Sweden": "Sweden is a country of 447,424 km² (172,750 sq mi) in northern Europe with roughly 10.6 million people, on the eastern side of the Scandinavian peninsula, with its capital at Stockholm.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Forest covers about two-thirds of it and roughly 100,000 lakes fill hollows left by the ice sheets; its farmland and cities sit in the milder south, the far north inside the Arctic Circle. A great military power around the Baltic in the 17th century, it lost that position by 1721, stayed out of both world wars while building an extensive welfare state, joined the European Union in 1995 and, after two centuries of non-alignment, joined NATO in 2024.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>",
"Switzerland": "Switzerland is a landlocked country of 41,291 km² (15,943 sq mi) in central Europe with roughly 9 million people; its federal seat is Bern.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The Alps cover some three-fifths of it and the Jura another tenth, so nearly everyone lives on the plateau between them, and the high country holds the sources of the Rhine and the Rhône and glaciers feeding much of western Europe's water. It has four national languages, German, French, Italian and Romansh, traces its confederation to alliances among mountain communities in the late Middle Ages, has kept armed neutrality since 1815, decides many questions by direct popular vote, and has stayed outside the European Union.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>",
"Turkey": "Turkey is a country of 783,562 km² (302,535 sq mi) with roughly 85 million people across two continents: Anatolia in western Asia and a corner of southeastern Europe, with Ankara its capital and Istanbul on the Bosporus.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Most of it is a high interior plateau ringed by mountains rising above 5,000 m (16,000 feet) in the east, it lies on major fault lines and suffers severe earthquakes, and the Tigris and Euphrates rise there. Its land carries some of the earliest farming villages known, was ruled by Hittites, Greeks, Romans and Byzantium, was the centre of the Ottoman Empire from about 1300 until 1922, and became a secular republic in 1923.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>",
"Ukraine": "Ukraine is a country of 603,550 km² (233,030 sq mi) in eastern Europe, the largest lying wholly within it, with roughly 44 million people before the 2022 invasion and 39 million now; its capital is Kyiv.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> It is mostly flat: open steppe and rolling farmland on some of the world's deepest black soil, drained by the Dnieper to the Black Sea. Kyivan Rus grew up along its rivers, its lands were later divided between Poland, Russia and the Habsburgs, a man-made famine killed millions in 1932 and 1933 under Soviet rule, independence came in 1991, and war has continued since Russia annexed Crimea in 2014 and invaded in 2022.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>",
"United_Kingdom": "The United Kingdom is an island country of 243,610 km² (94,058 sq mi) off northwestern Europe with roughly 68 million people, made up of Great Britain and the northeast of Ireland; its capital is London.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> It is a union of four countries, England, Scotland, Wales and Northern Ireland, whose landscapes run from the farmed southeast to the mountains and sea lochs of the Highlands, under a mild, wet Atlantic climate. England and Scotland joined crowns in 1603 and parliaments in 1707, Ireland was added in 1801 and mostly left in 1922; the country industrialised first, ruled the largest empire in history, and left the European Union in 2020.",
"Vatican_City": "Vatican City is a walled enclave of 0.44 km² (0.17 sq mi) inside Rome, the seat of the Roman Catholic Church's government, which the United Nations files as the Holy See, with 882 residents at the end of 2024.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Its grounds hold St Peter's Basilica and its square, the Apostolic Palace, the Sistine Chapel, gardens over half the territory, and celebrated museums and archives. Popes ruled a much larger territory across central Italy for more than a thousand years until the new Italian kingdom absorbed it in 1870; the present state was created by treaty with Italy in 1929 and is governed by the pope, who holds legislative, executive and judicial power.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>",
"Afghanistan": "Afghanistan is a landlocked country of 652,864 km² (252,072 sq mi) in south-central Asia with roughly 44 million people and its capital at Kabul.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The Hindu Kush runs through it from northeast to southwest, so about three-quarters of the land is mountain or high plateau, with the irrigated valleys, the northern plains and the Helmand basin holding most of the farming and the population. Its passes have carried armies and caravans between Central Asia, Iran and India for thousands of years; a unified Afghan state dates from 1747, and the country has seen almost continuous conflict since the Soviet invasion of 1979, with the Taliban returning to power in 2021.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>",
"Bahrain": "Bahrain is an island country of 778 km² (300 sq mi) in the Persian Gulf with roughly 1.6 million people, and its capital is Manama.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> It is an archipelago of low, barren limestone islands with almost no rain and no rivers, though springs rising through the seabed made it habitable, and some 2,100 people live on each square kilometre.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Its position on the sea route between Mesopotamia and the Indus made it the centre of the Dilmun trading civilisation, and it lived on pearl diving until oil was struck in 1932; under British protection until the sheikhdoms’ treaties were wound up, it declared itself fully independent on 15 August 1971.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>",
"Bangladesh": "Bangladesh is a country of 148,460 km² (57,321 sq mi) in South Asia with roughly 171 million people, almost surrounded by India, with a coast on the Bay of Bengal and its capital at Dhaka.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Nearly all of it lies on the delta where the Ganges, Brahmaputra and Meghna meet the sea, the largest in the world, which gives it fertile silt but leaves much of it barely above sea level and exposed to floods and cyclones. Bengal was a wealthy centre of textile production under the Mughals and the British, was partitioned in 1947 so that its eastern half became East Pakistan, and became independent as Bangladesh after a war in 1971.",
"Bhutan": "Bhutan is a landlocked country of 38,394 km² (14,824 sq mi) in the eastern Himalaya with about 800,000 people, wedged between China and India, with its capital at Thimphu.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The land climbs within a short distance from subtropical plains through temperate valleys, where most of the towns and farms lie, to glaciated peaks above 7,000 m (23,000 feet), and forest covers 71.2 per cent of it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Vajrayana Buddhism shapes its law, architecture and calendar, a monarchy was established in 1907 and became constitutional in 2008, it joined the United Nations in 1971, and the government measures its progress by an index it calls gross national happiness.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>",
"Brunei": "Brunei is a small country of 5,765 km² (2,226 sq mi) on the northern coast of the island of Borneo in Southeast Asia, with about 465,000 people and its capital at Bandar Seri Begawan.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The South China Sea lies to its north and the Malaysian state of Sarawak on every other side, dividing it in two; forest covers 72.1 per cent of the land, and industry supplies 60.7 per cent of value added against agriculture’s 1.2.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> It became a British protectorate in 1888, and although a constitution of 1959 declared it self-governing, Britain kept its foreign affairs, security and defence until it regained full independence in 1984.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>",
"Cambodia": "Cambodia is a country of 181,035 km² (69,898 sq mi) in mainland Southeast Asia with roughly 18 million people, and governed from Phnom Penh.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Its centre is a low basin around the Tonlé Sap, a lake whose outflowing river reverses each monsoon so that the lake swells several times over, flooding the forest and producing one of the world's richest inland fisheries. The Khmer Empire ruled the region from the 9th to the 15th century and left the temple city of Angkor, the country was a French protectorate from 1863 and independent from 1953, and it lost perhaps a quarter of its population under the Khmer Rouge government of 1975 to 1979.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>",
"Indonesia": "Indonesia is a country of 1,910,931 km² (737,814 sq mi) in Southeast Asia with roughly 280 million people, the fourth most populous in the world and the largest archipelago, some 17,000 islands strung along the equator, governed from Jakarta.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> It sits where several tectonic plates grind together, giving it about 130 active volcanoes and frequent earthquakes, while its rainforests on Sumatra, Borneo and New Guinea hold some of the greatest concentrations of species on Earth. Its islands were the source of the spices that drew traders from India, China, Arabia and later Europe, most of the archipelago became the Dutch East Indies, and independence was proclaimed in 1945 and recognised in 1949.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>",
"Iran": "Iran is a country of 1,648,195 km² (636,371 sq mi) in western Asia with roughly 92 million people, with coasts on the Caspian Sea and the Persian Gulf and its capital at Tehran.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Its heart is a high, dry plateau ringed by mountains, the Zagros along the west and the Alborz across the north, which trap the rain and leave two great salt deserts, so settlement clusters in oasis cities and mountain valleys. Persian empires ruled from here from the 6th century BCE, Islam arrived in the 7th CE, the Safavids made the country a Shia state from 1501, and a revolution in 1979 replaced the monarchy with an Islamic republic.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>",
"Iraq": "Iraq is a country of 435,052 km² (167,974 sq mi) in western Asia with roughly 47 million people, governed from Baghdad.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The Tigris and Euphrates run its whole length and join near the head of the Persian Gulf, watering a plain farmed by irrigation for thousands of years, while mountains rise in the Kurdish north and desert stretches west. This is the land of ancient Mesopotamia, where some of the earliest cities, writing and law codes appeared; Baghdad was founded in 762 as the Abbasid capital, and the country was under Ottoman and then British control before becoming a kingdom in 1932 and a republic in 1958.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>",
"Israel": "Israel is a country of about 22,000 km² (8,500 sq mi) at the eastern end of the Mediterranean with roughly 10 million people; its government sits in Jerusalem, a status most other states do not recognise.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The land runs from a fertile coastal plain through central highlands to the Jordan Rift Valley, which holds the Sea of Galilee and the Dead Sea, the lowest exposed land on Earth, while the arid Negev fills the south. It was established in 1948 after the British Mandate over Palestine and the Nazi genocide of European Jews, has fought several wars with neighbouring states, and has occupied the West Bank since 1967.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>",
"Jordan": "Jordan is a country of 89,318 km² (34,486 sq mi) in western Asia with roughly 12 million people, a single short outlet to the sea at Aqaba, and its capital at Amman.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Most of it is arid plateau and desert, but along its western edge the ground drops into the Jordan Rift Valley, where the river Jordan runs down to the Dead Sea, and rainfall on the highlands above supports nearly all the farmland and population. It holds the rock-cut city of Petra, capital of the Nabataean kingdom, was Ottoman until 1918 and then the British-administered Emirate of Transjordan, became fully independent in 1946, and has since taken in large numbers of refugees.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>",
"Kazakhstan": "Kazakhstan is a country of 2,724,900 km² (1,052,100 sq mi) in Central Asia with roughly 21 million people, the largest landlocked state in the world and the ninth largest of any kind, with its capital at Astana.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Most of it is open steppe and semi-desert, thinly settled, with mountains only along the southeastern rim; diversion of its rivers for irrigation in the Soviet period shrank the Aral Sea to a fraction of its size, one of the severest environmental losses of the 20th century. Its Turkic-speaking population was largely nomadic until the 20th century, the region was absorbed by the Russian Empire, became a Soviet republic, and gained independence in 1991.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>",
"Kuwait": "Kuwait is a country of 17,818 km² (6,880 sq mi) at the head of the Persian Gulf with 4.9 to 5 million people by the two international counts, governed from Kuwait City on the deep natural harbour that gave the state its start.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> It is almost entirely flat desert with no rivers and very little rainfall, so nearly all its drinking water is distilled from the sea. A trading and pearling port under the Al Sabah family from the 18th century, it came under British protection in 1899, struck oil in 1938, gained independence in 1961, and was occupied by Iraq in 1990 before an international coalition freed it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>",
"Kyrgyzstan": "Kyrgyzstan is a landlocked country of 199,949 km² (77,201 sq mi) in Central Asia with roughly 7.3 million people, with its capital at Bishkek.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The Tien Shan ranges cover almost all of it and more than nine-tenths of the land lies above 1,500 m (4,900 feet), so life concentrates in a few valleys and around Issyk-Kul, a large mountain lake that never freezes, being deep and slightly salty. Its people were traditionally nomadic herders summering on high pasture, the region passed to the Russian Empire in the 19th century and became a Soviet republic, and the country has been independent since 1991, with an economy resting on livestock, gold mining and hydroelectric power.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>",
"Laos": "Laos is a landlocked country of 236,800 km² (91,430 sq mi) in mainland Southeast Asia with roughly 7.9 million people, with its capital at Vientiane on the Mekong.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Mountains and forested plateaus cover most of it, and the Mekong runs the length of its western border, providing the lowland strips where most farming and settlement are found and, through a chain of dams, much of the electricity it sells to its neighbours. The kingdom of Lan Xang ruled here from 1353, the country became part of French Indochina and gained independence in 1953, it was bombed heavily during the Vietnam War, and a communist government has held power since 1975.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>",
"Lebanon": "Lebanon is a country of 10,452 km² (4,036 sq mi) on the eastern Mediterranean with roughly 5.8 million people and its capital at Beirut.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Two mountain ranges run the length of the country parallel to the coast, enclosing the fertile Bekaa valley, and snow on the higher ridges gives it more water than its neighbours and once fed its famous cedar forests. Its coastal cities were the harbours of the Phoenicians, who carried an alphabet across the Mediterranean; it came under French mandate after 1918, became independent in 1943 under a system dividing state offices among religious communities, and endured civil war from 1975 to 1990.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>",
"Malaysia": "Malaysia is a country of 330,621 km² (127,653 sq mi) in Southeast Asia with roughly 36 million people, in two parts separated by the South China Sea: the southern Malay peninsula and the northern edge of Borneo, with its capital at Kuala Lumpur.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Both are hot and wet the year round, with rainforest among the oldest on Earth, though much of the lowland has been cleared for rubber and oil palm. Melaka's port made the peninsula a hinge of trade between the Indian Ocean and China; Portuguese, Dutch and British powers ruled in turn from 1511, the peninsula became independent in 1957, the federation was formed in 1963, and Singapore left in 1965.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>",
"Maldives": "The Maldives is an island country in the Indian Ocean, with about 300 km² (116 sq mi) of land, roughly 530,000 people, its capital at Malé and India and Sri Lanka its nearest neighbours.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> It is made up of around 1,200 small coral islands and sandbanks sheltered by barrier reefs, about 200 of them inhabited, and its reefs are the seventh largest in the world, holding about 5 per cent of the world’s reef area.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Britain ruled it until independence in 1965, it became a republic in 1968, and it joined the Commonwealth in 1982, withdrew in 2016 and rejoined in 2020; tourism dominates, with 1,675,000 visitors arriving in 2022.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>",
"Mongolia": "Mongolia is a landlocked country of 1,564,116 km² (603,908 sq mi) in East Asia with roughly 3.5 million people, wedged between Russia and China at 2.3 people to the square kilometre; its capital is Ulaanbaatar.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Most of it is high, treeless steppe above 1,000 m (3,300 feet), giving way to the Gobi desert in the south, and winters can fall below minus 40 degrees. Herding on horseback has shaped its life for millennia; Genghis Khan united its tribes in 1206 and his descendants built the largest contiguous land empire in history, the country was later ruled from Qing China, became independent in the early 20th century, and left communist rule in 1990.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>",
"Myanmar": "Myanmar is a country of 676,577 km² (261,228 sq mi) in Southeast Asia with roughly 55 million people, with a long coast on the Bay of Bengal and its capital at Naypyidaw.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> A horseshoe of mountains encloses it on three sides and the Irrawaddy runs down the middle to a wide delta, where most people live; teak, jade and gemstones have long been among its exports. Buddhist kingdoms centred on Pagan and later Mandalay ruled here from the 11th century, Britain annexed the country in stages during the 19th century and governed it from India, independence came in 1948, and the military has held power for most of the period since 1962.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>",
"Nepal": "Nepal is a landlocked country of 147,181 km² (56,827 sq mi) in South Asia with roughly 30 million people, between China and India on the southern flank of the Himalaya, and its capital is Kathmandu.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Within about 200 km (120 miles) the land rises from the subtropical Tarai, through the middle hills where most towns and farms are, to eight of the world's ten highest peaks, among them Everest at 8,849 m (29,030 feet) on the Chinese border. A kingdom unified in the 18th century and never formally colonised, it opened to outsiders only in the mid-20th century, endured a Maoist insurgency from 1996 to 2006, and abolished the monarchy in 2008 to become a federal republic.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>",
"North_Korea": "North Korea is a country of 120,540 km² (46,541 sq mi) on the northern half of the Korean peninsula, with roughly 27 million people, bordered by China, Russia and, across a fortified line, South Korea, and governed from Pyongyang.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> About four-fifths is mountain and upland, with the little arable land on western coastal plains and mineral deposits that are hard to work. Japan ruled the peninsula from 1910 to 1945, after which it was divided at the 38th parallel; the northern state was founded in 1948, fought the Korean War of 1950 to 1953, which ended in an armistice, not a peace treaty, and has since been a one-party state under one family.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>",
"Oman": "Oman is a country of 309,500 km² (119,500 sq mi) on the southeastern corner of the Arabian peninsula with roughly 5.5 million people, with coasts on the Arabian Sea and the Gulf of Oman and its capital at Muscat.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Behind a narrow coastal plain the Hajar mountains rise above 3,000 m (9,800 feet) and catch the rain that feeds the channels watering its date gardens. Its frankincense was traded to Egypt and Rome, its sailors ranged across the Indian Ocean, and Omani rulers later held an empire on the East African coast where their forts guarded the sultanate of Zanzibar’s interests; the United States signed a treaty of commerce at Muscat in 1833.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>",
"Pakistan": "Pakistan is a country of about 881,900 km² (340,500 sq mi) in South Asia with roughly 245 million people, with a coast on the Arabian Sea and its capital at Islamabad.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The Indus and its tributaries run the length of the country and feed one of the largest irrigation networks in the world, with the Karakoram and Himalaya rising in the north to K2 at 8,611 m (28,250 feet). The Indus valley held one of the earliest urban civilisations, from about 2600 BCE; the modern state was created by the 1947 partition of British India, its eastern wing separated as Bangladesh in 1971, alternating between elected and military governments ever since.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>",
"State_of_Palestine": "The State of Palestine holds non-member observer State status at the United Nations, accorded in 2012 by 138 votes to 9, and covers the West Bank and Gaza, together 6,025 km² (2,326 sq mi), with about 5.6 million people.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The two are separated by Israeli land: the West Bank is hilly upland west of the Jordan valley, while Gaza is a small, densely built coastal strip on the Mediterranean. They were part of the British Mandate until 1948; the General Assembly speaks of Palestinian territory occupied since 1967, statehood was proclaimed by the Palestine National Council in 1988, and the status of Jerusalem is left to negotiation.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>",
"Philippines": "The Philippines is an island country of about 300,000 km² (100,000 sq mi) in Southeast Asia with roughly 114 million people, made up of some 7,600 islands, the largest being Luzon and Mindanao, with its capital at Manila.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> It lies on the Pacific Ring of Fire and in the typhoon belt, so earthquakes, eruptions and some 20 tropical storms a year are part of ordinary life. Its islands traded with China and the Malay world long before Spain claimed them in 1565 and ruled for three centuries, leaving Catholicism the majority religion; the United States took control in 1898 and the country became fully independent in 1946.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>",
"Qatar": "Qatar is a country of 11,586 km² (4,473 sq mi) on a peninsula reaching into the Persian Gulf, with roughly 3 million people, bordered on land only by Saudi Arabia and governed from Doha.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> It is flat, low desert of gravel and sand with no permanent rivers and very little rain, so its drinking water comes almost entirely from desalination; most people live in and around the capital. Long a small pearling and fishing society under the Al Thani family, it was under British protection from 1916 until independence in 1971, and a vast offshore natural gas field has since carried its income per head to about 66,600 US dollars.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>",
"Saudi_Arabia": "Saudi Arabia is a country of about 2,150,000 km² (830,000 sq mi) on the Arabian peninsula, with roughly 35 million people, coasts on the Red Sea and the Persian Gulf, and Riyadh as its capital.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> It has no permanent rivers, and much of the interior is desert, including the Rub' al Khali, the largest continuous body of sand on Earth; mountains along the Red Sea catch what little rain falls. Mecca and Medina, the two holiest cities of Islam, lie in its west, drawing millions of pilgrims each year; the kingdom was founded in 1932 by Abdulaziz Ibn Saud, and oil found in 1938 made it one of the world's largest crude exporters.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>",
"Singapore": "Singapore is an island city-state of about 728 km² (281 sq mi) at the southern tip of the Malay peninsula, with roughly 6 million people, among the most densely populated countries in the world.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> It is low and flat, and has steadily enlarged itself with land reclaimed from the sea, beside the Strait of Malacca, through which a large share of the world's seaborne trade passes. Founded as a British trading post in 1819, it grew into one of the busiest harbours in the world, was occupied by Japan in the Second World War, joined Malaysia in 1963 and separated from it in 1965, and is now a major financial and shipping centre.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>",
"South_Korea": "South Korea is a country of 100,401 km² (38,765 sq mi) on the southern half of the Korean peninsula, with roughly 52 million people, bordered only by North Korea, across a fortified line, and otherwise by sea; its capital is Seoul.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> About seven-tenths of it is hill and mountain, so population and industry crowd onto the western and southern plains, and around half the country lives around Seoul. Japan ruled the peninsula from 1910 to 1945, after which it was divided; the southern state was founded in 1948, fought the Korean War of 1950 to 1953, industrialised rapidly from the 1960s, and moved from military rule to competitive elections in 1987.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>",
"Sri_Lanka": "Sri Lanka is an island country of 65,610 km² (25,330 sq mi) in the Indian Ocean off the southeastern tip of India, with roughly 22 million people, its commercial centre at Colombo and its legislature at Sri Jayawardenepura Kotte.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Coastal plain rings central highlands above 2,500 m (8,200 feet), which catch two monsoons, divide the island into a wet southwest and a drier north and east, and carry its tea plantations. Its ancient capital Anuradhapura and its Theravada Buddhist tradition go back more than two thousand years; Portuguese, Dutch and finally British powers ruled the island, independence came in 1948, and a civil war with Tamil separatists lasted from 1983 to 2009.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>",
"Syria": "Syria is a country of 185,180 km² (71,498 sq mi) in western Asia with roughly 26 million people and a short Mediterranean coast, governed from Damascus.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> A narrow fertile coastal strip gives way to mountains and a wide inland plateau that dries into desert, the Euphrates crossing the northeast and providing most of its irrigation water. Damascus and Aleppo are among the world's longest continuously inhabited cities, the region held some of the earliest farming settlements and the Umayyad capital, it was Ottoman and then French before independence in 1946, and after decades of Assad family rule and a civil war beginning in 2011 the government was overthrown at the end of 2024.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>",
"Taiwan": "Taiwan is an island of 36,197 km² (13,976 sq mi) separated from mainland China by a strait about 180 km (110 miles) wide, with roughly 23 million people and its seat of government at Taipei. Mountains cover about two-thirds of it, rising on the eastern side to 3,952 m (12,970 feet), while the population, farmland and industry crowd onto the western coastal plain. Settled first by Austronesian peoples, it saw Dutch and Spanish outposts, was ruled by the Qing from 1683, ceded to Japan in 1895 and returned in 1945; the Republic of China's government moved there in 1949 after losing the civil war, and its status remains disputed with the People's Republic, which claims it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>",
"Tajikistan": "Tajikistan is a landlocked country of 141,400 km² (54,590 sq mi) in Central Asia with roughly 11 million people, with its capital at Dushanbe.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Mountains cover about 93 percent of it, rising in the east to the Pamirs, long called the roof of the world, and its glaciers and rivers hold much of the water on which its downstream neighbours depend. Its language is a form of Persian, unlike the Turkic tongues of the rest of the region; the area passed through Persian and Islamic states, was absorbed by the Russian Empire and became a Soviet republic, gained independence in 1991, and endured a civil war from 1992 to 1997.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>",
"Thailand": "Thailand is a country of 513,120 km² (198,120 sq mi) in mainland Southeast Asia with roughly 72 million people, coasts on the Gulf of Thailand and the Andaman Sea, and its capital at Bangkok.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Its centre is the broad plain of the Chao Phraya, one of the world's great rice-growing regions, with mountains in the north, the dry Khorat plateau in the northeast and a narrow southern isthmus. Kingdoms centred on Sukhothai and then Ayutthaya ruled from the 13th century, it was the only state in the region never colonised by a European power, changed its name from Siam in 1939, and has been a constitutional monarchy since 1932, with frequent military coups.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>",
"East_Timor": "East Timor is a country of 14,919 km² (5,760 sq mi) in Southeast Asia with roughly 1.4 million people, occupying the eastern half of the island of Timor with a small enclave on its northwest coast, and its capital is Dili.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> A rugged spine of mountains rises to almost 3,000 m (9,800 feet) close behind the northern shore, leaving little flat land, and only about three in ten of its people live in towns.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> A Portuguese colony until 1975 and under Indonesian sovereignty from 1976 to 1999, it was placed under a United Nations transitional administration after a referendum held that year and became independent on 20 May 2002.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>",
"Turkmenistan": "Turkmenistan is a country of 491,210 km² (189,660 sq mi) in Central Asia with roughly 7.6 million people and a long Caspian shore, its capital at Ashgabat.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> About four-fifths of it is the Karakum, a sand and gravel desert with almost no rainfall, so settlement clings to the southern mountain foot and the Amu Darya, and its natural gas reserves are among the largest in the world. Silk Road caravan routes crossed it through cities such as Merv, the region was absorbed by the Russian Empire in the late 19th century and became a Soviet republic, and since independence in 1991 it has declared a policy of permanent neutrality.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>",
"United_Arab_Emirates": "The United Arab Emirates is a country of 83,600 km² (32,300 sq mi) on the southeastern Arabian peninsula with roughly 11 million people and a long Persian Gulf coast, its capital at Abu Dhabi.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> It is mostly flat desert of sand and salt flats, rising to the Hajar mountains in the east, with no permanent rivers; the great majority of its residents are foreign nationals who have come to work. It is a federation of seven emirates formed in 1971 when British protection ended, each keeping its own ruler; oil found in the late 1950s funded rapid growth, and Dubai has built itself into a hub for trade, aviation and tourism.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>",
"Uzbekistan": "Uzbekistan is a country of 448,969 km² (173,348 sq mi) in Central Asia with roughly 37 million people, one of only two doubly landlocked states in the world — every neighbour is landlocked too — its capital Tashkent.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Much of it is Kyzylkum desert and dry steppe, with life along the Amu Darya and Syr Darya, whose diversion for cotton has shrunk the Aral Sea to a fraction of its old size. Samarkand, Bukhara and Khiva were among the great cities of the Silk Road and later the Timurid Empire; the Russian Empire conquered the region in the 19th century, it became a Soviet republic, and has been independent since 1991.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>",
"Vietnam": "Vietnam is a country of 331,344 km² (127,933 sq mi) in Southeast Asia with roughly 101 million people, a long narrow S down the eastern Indochinese peninsula, with its capital at Hanoi.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Two crowded deltas hold most of its people and rice, the Red River in the north and the Mekong in the south, joined by a coastal strip where the Annamite range presses close to the sea. It was ruled from China for about a thousand years until 938, then by Vietnamese dynasties, joined French Indochina in the 19th century, was divided in 1954, reunified in 1976 after a long war, and opened to markets from 1986.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>",
"Yemen": "Yemen is a country of 527,968 km² (203,850 sq mi) on the southwestern Arabian peninsula with roughly 42 million people, coasts on the Red Sea and the Arabian Sea, and its recognised capital at Sana'a.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Behind a hot coastal plain the land climbs into highlands above 3,000 m (9,800 feet) that catch a summer monsoon, making it the best-watered part of the peninsula, with terraced farming and the coffee for which it has long been known. Ancient kingdoms here grew rich on the incense trade, north and south were separate states until they merged in 1990, and a war beginning in 2014 has divided control and caused a severe humanitarian crisis.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>",
"Algeria": "Algeria is a country of 2,381,741 km² (919,595 sq mi) in North Africa, the largest on the continent, with roughly 47 million people, a Mediterranean coast and its capital at Algiers.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> More than four-fifths of it is Sahara, so almost the whole population lives on the coastal plain and in the Atlas valleys behind it, while the desert holds its oil and gas. Its coast was Numidian, then Roman and Byzantine before the Arab conquests of the 7th century, and an Ottoman regency for three centuries; France invaded in 1830 and settled it as a colony, and independence came in 1962 after a war of nearly eight years.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>",
"Angola": "Angola is a country of 1,246,700 km² (481,350 sq mi) in southern Africa with roughly 39 million people, facing the Atlantic, with a separate northern province, Cabinda, cut off from the rest, and its capital at Luanda.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> A narrow coastal plain rises sharply to an interior plateau above 1,000 m (3,300 feet), cooler and better watered than the coast and carrying most of the farming. Portugal held trading posts here from the 16th century and shipped enormous numbers of enslaved people to Brazil, colonial rule was formalised in the 19th century, independence came in 1975, and a civil war then continued until 2002; oil and diamonds dominate the economy.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>",
"Benin": "Benin is a country of 114,763 km² (44,310 sq mi) in West Africa with roughly 15 million people, a narrow strip running north from a short Atlantic coast, with Porto-Novo as its official capital and the government seated at Cotonou.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Behind coastal lagoons and sandbars the land rises through a humid plateau of oil palm and farmland to drier savanna and the Atakora hills. The kingdom of Dahomey ruled much of the region from the 17th century and grew powerful on the Atlantic slave trade through Ouidah, France made it a colony in the 1890s, independence came in 1960, and a Marxist government held power from 1972 until multiparty elections in 1991.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>",
"Botswana": "Botswana is a landlocked country of 581,730 km² (224,610 sq mi) in southern Africa with roughly 2.6 million people, bordered by South Africa, Namibia, Zimbabwe and Zambia, with its capital at Gaborone.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Most of it is covered by deep Kalahari sand, with rocky hills in the south-east, but in the northwest the Okavango spreads out and dies in the desert, creating a World Heritage site whose wetland draws great concentrations of wildlife.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> It had been under British sovereignty as Bechuanaland, became independent on 30 September 1966 and joined the Commonwealth that year, and diamonds, copper, nickel, cattle and wildlife are its listed natural resources.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>",
"Burkina_Faso": "Burkina Faso is a landlocked country of 274,200 km² (105,900 sq mi) in West Africa with roughly 24 million people and its capital at Ouagadougou.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> It sits mostly on a low savanna plateau in the Sahel, with a single rainy season of three or four months, thin soils and the three branches of the Volta providing much of its water; cotton and gold are its main exports. The Mossi kingdoms held the central plateau from about the 15th century, France took the territory in the 1890s and ruled it as Upper Volta, independence came in 1960, and the country took its present name, meaning 'land of upright people', in 1984.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>",
"Burundi": "Burundi is a landlocked country of 27,834 km² (10,747 sq mi) in East Africa with roughly 14 million people, with Gitega as its political capital and Bujumbura, on Lake Tanganyika, as its largest city.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Almost all of it is hilly plateau between about 1,400 and 2,000 m (4,600 to 6,600 feet), cool for the tropics and densely farmed on small plots, with the Nile and Congo watersheds meeting along its highest ridge; coffee and tea are its main exports. A kingdom ruled here for centuries before German and then Belgian administration, independence came in 1962, and repeated violence between Hutu and Tutsi communities culminated in a civil war lasting from 1993 until the mid-2000s.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>",
"Cape_Verde": "Cape Verde is an island country of 4,033 km² (1,557 sq mi) in the Atlantic Ocean off West Africa, with roughly 525,000 people and its capital at Praia.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Two of its people in three live in towns, 11 per cent of the land is forested, and tourism outweighs the population, 785,000 non-resident tourists having stayed in its hotels in 2022.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The islands were under Portuguese sovereignty until 1975, when the United States recognised the new republic on 5 July and it joined the United Nations on 16 September; in 2013 it was renamed Cabo Verde, the form the United Nations now uses.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>",
"Cameroon": "Cameroon is a country of 475,442 km² (183,569 sq mi) in Central Africa with roughly 30 million people, a coast on the Gulf of Guinea and its capital at Yaoundé.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> It is sometimes called Africa in miniature because almost every landscape of the continent appears within it, from southern rainforest through central plateaus to the dry edge of the Sahel, with the active volcano of Mount Cameroon near the coast. Germany claimed the territory in 1884, France and Britain divided it as mandates after the First World War, and the French and British portions became independent in 1960 and 1961 and joined, leaving both French and English as official languages.",
"Central_African_Republic": "The Central African Republic is a landlocked country of 622,984 km² (240,535 sq mi) near the middle of the continent with roughly 5.5 million people and its capital at Bangui on the Ubangi river.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Most of it is a rolling savanna plateau between about 600 and 700 m (2,000 to 2,300 feet) forming the watershed between the Chad and Congo basins, with rainforest in the southwest and drier country toward the Sahel. France ruled the territory as Ubangi-Shari, where more than forty concession companies divided the ground between them; independence came in 1960, and armed conflict since has brought a United Nations stabilisation mission to a country whose income per head is about 366 US dollars.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>",
"Chad": "Chad is a landlocked country of 1,284,000 km² (495,800 sq mi) in north-central Africa with roughly 21 million people and its capital at N'Djamena.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> It falls into three bands: Sahara desert across the north, rising to the volcanic Tibesti massif above 3,400 m (11,000 feet), Sahelian scrub through the middle, and wetter savanna in the south where most of the farming and population are found. Lake Chad on its western border, once one of the largest lakes in Africa, has shrunk drastically since the 1960s; the Kanem-Bornu empire dominated the region for close to a thousand years, France conquered it around 1900, independence came in 1960, and civil war and border conflicts have recurred since.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>",
"Comoros": "The Comoros is an island country of 1,861 km² (719 sq mi) in the Indian Ocean, at the northern end of the Mozambique Channel, with roughly 880,000 people and its capital at Moroni.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> It comprises three volcanic islands and their islets, steep and densely farmed at 474 people to the square kilometre; a fourth island of the archipelago, Mayotte, is governed by France, and the United Nations General Assembly has treated it as Comorian since 1976.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Settled by seafarers from East Africa, Arabia and the Malay world, the islands became a Muslim trading society, France took them in the 19th century, independence came in 1975, and many coups have followed.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>",
"Republic_of_the_Congo": "The Republic of the Congo is a country of 342,000 km² (132,000 sq mi) in Central Africa with roughly 6.5 million people, straddling the equator with a short Atlantic coast and its capital at Brazzaville.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Dense rainforest covers about two-thirds of the country, drained by the Congo and its tributary the Ubangi, which form its long eastern border; a coastal plain and plateaus lie to the west. The area lay within the Kingdom of Kongo's sphere before France took control in the 1880s and made Brazzaville the capital of French Equatorial Africa; independence came in 1960, a Marxist government ruled from 1969 to 1992, and the economy rests largely on offshore oil.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>",
"Democratic_Republic_of_the_Congo": "The Democratic Republic of the Congo is a country of 2,344,858 km² (905,354 sq mi) in Central Africa, the second largest on the continent, with roughly 113 million people and its capital at Kinshasa.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Most of it lies in the Congo basin, whose river crosses the equator and carries more water than any but the Amazon, feeding the world's second largest rainforest. Held from 1885 as the personal possession of King Leopold II of Belgium, whose rubber regime killed vast numbers, it passed to Belgium in 1908 and became independent in 1960; wars from 1996 to 2003 drew in neighbouring armies, and fighting continues in the mineral-rich east.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>",
"Ivory_Coast": "Ivory Coast is a country of 322,463 km² (124,504 sq mi) in West Africa with roughly 33 million people, with a coast on the Gulf of Guinea, Yamoussoukro as its official capital and the government seated at Abidjan.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> A flat coastal belt of lagoons gives way to rainforest across the south and to savanna in the north, with the country's few mountains in the far west. Its plantations make it the world's largest producer of cocoa by a wide margin, France ruled it as a colony from 1893, independence came in 1960 under Félix Houphouët-Boigny, and the country went through political crises and two civil conflicts between 2002 and 2011.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>",
"Djibouti": "Djibouti is a country of 23,200 km² (8,960 sq mi) in the Horn of Africa with roughly 1.2 million people, facing Yemen across the Bab el-Mandeb strait, its capital of the same name.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> It is volcanic desert and salt pan, holding Lake Assal, with forest over 0.2 per cent of it and nearly four in five people living in towns.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Its position at the mouth of the Red Sea is its chief asset, services employing 92.9 per cent of the workforce and exports rising from 251 million dollars in 2015 to 4,466 million by 2020; France ruled it, latterly as the French Territory of the Afars and the Issas, until independence in 1977.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>",
"Egypt": "Egypt is a country of 1,001,450 km² (386,662 sq mi) in the northeastern corner of Africa with roughly 118 million people, with coasts on the Mediterranean and the Red Sea and its capital at Cairo.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Almost all of it is desert and almost all of its people live along the Nile and in its delta, a strip of irrigated land that is among the most densely settled ground anywhere. Farming villages appeared along the river before 5000 BCE and a unified kingdom by about 3100 BCE; the country was later Persian, Greek, Roman, Arab and Ottoman, was occupied by Britain from 1882, and became a republic in 1953.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>",
"Equatorial_Guinea": "Equatorial Guinea is a country of 28,051 km² (10,831 sq mi) in Central Africa with roughly 1.9 million people, made up of a mainland between Cameroon and Gabon and islands in the Gulf of Guinea, with its capital at Malabo on Bioko.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The mainland is coastal plain rising to forested hills, forest covering 86.7 per cent of the land, while Bioko is a steep volcanic island.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> It was under Spanish colonial rule for most of the modern era, becoming independent on 12 October 1968 with one of the highest incomes per head and the highest literacy rates in Africa; that income has since fallen from 9,069 US dollars in 2015 to 6,558.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>",
"Eritrea": "Eritrea is a country of 121,144 km² (46,774 sq mi) in the Horn of Africa with roughly 3.6 million people, with a long Red Sea coast including the Dahlak archipelago, and its capital at Asmara.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> A hot, dry coastal strip climbs steeply to a central highland spine above 2,000 m (6,600 feet), cooler and better watered, where most people live. Italy made it a colony in 1890 and built the modernist Asmara, Britain administered it after 1941, it was federated with and then annexed by Ethiopia, and after a war of independence lasting thirty years it became a sovereign state in 1993, following a referendum the United Nations sent a mission to verify.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>",
"Eswatini": "Eswatini is a landlocked country of 17,364 km² (6,704 sq mi) in southern Africa with roughly 1.3 million people, almost entirely surrounded by South Africa, with Mbabane its administrative capital and Lobamba its legislative one.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> It falls into four belts running north to south: a mountainous highveld in the west, a middleveld of hills, a hot lowveld of savanna, and the Lubombo escarpment in the east.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> A Swazi kingdom took shape in the 18th and 19th centuries, the territory came under British sovereignty, independence followed on 6 September 1968, and it was renamed from Swaziland in 2018; it is the only absolute monarchy in southern Africa.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>",
"Ethiopia": "Ethiopia is a landlocked country of 1,104,300 km² (426,370 sq mi) in the Horn of Africa with roughly 135 million people, the second most populous on the continent, and its capital is Addis Ababa.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Its core is a broad highland massif above 2,000 m (6,600 feet), split by the Great Rift Valley and well watered, where most of the population farms and Lake Tana feeds the Blue Nile. The kingdom of Aksum adopted Christianity in the 4th century; the country kept its own script, calendar and church, was the only African state to defeat a European invasion and stay independent but for Italian occupation from 1936 to 1941, and kept its Solomonic monarchy until 1974.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>",
"Gabon": "Gabon is a country of 267,668 km² (103,347 sq mi) in Central Africa with roughly 2.6 million people, straddling the equator on the Atlantic coast, with its capital at Libreville.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Forest covers 91.2 per cent of the land and just over half of the sites that matter most for land-based biodiversity are protected, so its small population leaves large tracts of forest and coastline undisturbed.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Independence from French Equatorial Africa was proclaimed in 1960, and the United Nations puts income per head at about 8,100 US dollars, with industry making up 53.4 per cent of gross value added and nearly nine in ten people living in towns.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>",
"The_Gambia": "The Gambia is a country of 11,300 km² (4,360 sq mi) in West Africa with roughly 2.8 million people, the smallest country in West Africa, surrounded by Senegal but for a short Atlantic coast, its capital Banjul.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Rarely more than 50 km (31 miles) wide, it is flat and low, and life turns on the river, navigable far inland and flooding rice fields and mangrove each rainy season. British posts on the Gambia river from the 17th century became a colony in the 19th, when the estuary was a route for the Atlantic slave trade; independence came in 1965, and a leader who seized power in 1994 ruled until an election defeat in 2016.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>",
"Ghana": "Ghana is a country of 238,535 km² (92,099 sq mi) in West Africa with roughly 35 million people, with a coast on the Gulf of Guinea and its capital at Accra.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Most of it is low plains and plateau, with rainforest and cocoa farms in the southwest, savanna across the north, and the Volta dammed at Akosombo to form the largest reservoir in the world by surface area. Gold worked here for centuries drew European traders to build coastal forts, which also became a centre of the Atlantic slave trade, the Ashanti Empire dominated the interior before British conquest, and in 1957 Ghana became the first colony in sub-Saharan Africa to gain independence.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>",
"Guinea": "Guinea is a country of 245,857 km² (94,926 sq mi) in West Africa with roughly 15 million people, an Atlantic coast and its capital at Conakry.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The highlands of the Fouta Djallon are sometimes called the water tower of West Africa, since the Niger, the Senegal and the Gambia all rise there, and the country holds the largest known reserves of bauxite, the ore of aluminium. Ruled by France from the late 19th century, in 1958 it was the only territory to reject membership of the French Community and took immediate independence at the cost of an abrupt French withdrawal; a single party governed until 1984 and coups have been repeated since.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>",
"Guinea-Bissau": "Guinea-Bissau is a country of 36,125 km² (13,948 sq mi) in West Africa with roughly 2.2 million people, with an Atlantic coast that includes the Bijagós archipelago and its capital at Bissau.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> It is low and flat, cut by wide tidal estuaries and fringed with mangrove and swamp rice fields, with forest covering about seven-tenths of the land, and farming employs half of everyone in work.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Portugal claimed the coast in the 15th century and used it as a slaving station, inland rule came only in the early 20th century, an armed independence movement fought from 1963, and the United States recognised the republic in 1974, the year the United Nations admitted it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>",
"Lesotho": "Lesotho is a landlocked country of 30,355 km² (11,720 sq mi) in southern Africa with roughly 2.4 million people, entirely surrounded by South Africa, with its capital at Maseru.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> It is the only country in the world whose whole territory lies more than 1,000 m (3,300 feet) above sea level, more than three-quarters of it is mountainous, and a treaty of 1986 sends water from those heights to South Africa’s Gauteng region.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> The Basotho nation was formed under Moshoeshoe I in the 1820s and 1830s on its mountain strongholds, it became the British protectorate of Basutoland, which spared it absorption into South Africa, and it became an independent kingdom on 4 October 1966.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>",
"Liberia": "Liberia is a country of 111,369 km² (43,000 sq mi) in West Africa with roughly 5.7 million people, with its capital at Monrovia.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Behind a sandy coast of lagoons and mangrove the land rises through forested hills to low mountains, and it holds some of the largest remaining blocks of West Africa's rainforest along with iron ore and rubber. It was founded from 1822 as a settlement for free and formerly enslaved African Americans organised by the American Colonization Society, declared itself a republic in 1847, was long dominated by that settler minority, and endured civil wars ended by a peace agreement adopted at Accra in 2003.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>",
"Libya": "Libya is a country of 1,759,540 km² (679,362 sq mi) in North Africa with roughly 7.5 million people, with its capital at Tripoli.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Over nine-tenths of it is Sahara, and almost the entire population lives on the coastal strip and in a few oases, drawing much of its fresh water from a vast fossil aquifer pumped north through pipelines. Its coast held Phoenician and Greek cities and later Roman ones such as Leptis Magna, it was Ottoman and then an Italian colony from 1912, became independent as a kingdom in 1951, and was ruled by Muammar Gaddafi from 1969 until his overthrow in 2011.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>",
"Madagascar": "Madagascar is an island country of 587,041 km² (226,658 sq mi) in the Indian Ocean off the southeast coast of Africa, with roughly 33 million people and its capital at Antananarivo.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Long isolated from other landmasses, it is the fourth largest island in the world, and most of its plants and animals are found nowhere else, among them lemurs and baobabs, though much of its original forest has been cleared for farming. Its people descend from Austronesian seafarers from the region of Borneo and later Bantu-speaking settlers from Africa; the Merina kingdom unified most of the island in the 19th century, France conquered it in the 1890s, and independence came in 1960.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>",
"Malawi": "Malawi is a landlocked country of 118,480 km² (45,745 sq mi) in southeastern Africa with roughly 22 million people, long and narrow, with its capital at Lilongwe.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Lake Malawi fills its eastern side and covers about a fifth of its area, a deep rift lake holding more species of fish than any other lake in the world, while highland plateaus rise on either side and most people farm small plots of maize and tobacco. Missionaries and traders followed David Livingstone's journeys in the 19th century, Britain declared the protectorate of Nyasaland in 1891, independence came in 1964, and one-party rule under Hastings Banda lasted until multiparty elections in 1994.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>",
"Mali": "Mali is a landlocked country of 1,240,192 km² (478,841 sq mi) in West Africa with roughly 25 million people, and its capital is Bamako.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The northern half is Sahara and the centre Sahel, while the Niger river arcs through the country and spreads into a vast inland delta whose seasonal flood supports rice, fishing and herding and carries most of the population. The medieval empires of Ghana, Mali and Songhai grew rich on trans-Saharan trade in gold and salt and made Timbuktu and Djenné centres of learning; France conquered the region in the late 19th century, independence came in 1960, and since 2012 the north and centre have been contested by armed groups.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>",
"Mauritania": "Mauritania is a country of 1,030,700 km² (397,960 sq mi) in northwest Africa with roughly 5.3 million people, with a long Atlantic coast and its capital at Nouakchott.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Around three-quarters of it is Sahara, and the advancing desert has pushed herders into towns, turning a nomadic society into a largely urban one within a lifetime; only the far south along the Senegal river is reliably cultivable. Its coastal waters are rich fishing grounds and it exports iron ore; France ruled it until independence in 1960, and slavery was first explicitly outlawed in 1981 but not made a crime until 2007, the constitution classing it as a crime against humanity in 2012.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>",
"Mauritius": "Mauritius is an island country of 2,040 km² (788 sq mi) in the Indian Ocean, with roughly 1.3 million people and its capital at Port Louis.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The main island is 67 km (42 miles) long and 46 km (29 miles) at its widest, of submarine volcanic origin, and its forests were largely cleared for sugar cane, which helped drive the dodo to extinction within a century of European arrival.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Uninhabited until the Dutch settled it in 1638, it passed to France and then to Britain, its plantations worked by enslaved Africans and then by indentured labourers from India, whose descendants are now the largest part of the population; independence came on 12 March 1968.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>",
"Morocco": "Morocco is a country of 446,550 km² (172,410 sq mi) in northwest Africa with roughly 38 million people, facing the Atlantic and the Mediterranean, with its capital at Rabat; it also administers most of the disputed territory of Western Sahara.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Three ranges of the Atlas mountains cross it, rising above 4,000 m (13,000 feet) and catching the rain that feeds its farmland, beyond which the land falls into the Sahara. Berber dynasties such as the Almoravids and Almohads ruled an empire reaching into Spain and West Africa, the country became a French and Spanish protectorate in 1912, regained independence in 1956, and remains a monarchy under a dynasty in place since the 17th century.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>",
"Mozambique": "Mozambique is a country of 799,380 km² (308,642 sq mi) in southeastern Africa with roughly 36 million people, with a long Indian Ocean coast and its capital at Maputo.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Coastal lowlands make up nearly half the country and are crossed by the Zambezi, dammed at Cahora Bassa for hydroelectricity, while plateaus and highlands rise inland; cyclones and floods strike the coast regularly. Swahili trading towns lined the shore for centuries before Portugal took the coast from 1498 and eventually the interior, independence came in 1975 after a ten-year war, a civil war followed until 1992, and offshore gas fields found since 2010 now dominate its investment.",
"Namibia": "Namibia is a country of 825,229 km² (318,623 sq mi) in southwestern Africa with roughly 3 million people at 3.8 to the square kilometre, with a long Atlantic coast and its capital at Windhoek.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The Namib desert runs the length of that coast and is thought to be the oldest on Earth, its dunes among the highest anywhere, with the Kalahari to the east. Germany colonised the territory in 1884 and suppressed the Herero and Nama uprisings of 1904 to 1908 in a campaign now widely recognised as genocide, South Africa ruled it after 1915 and extended apartheid there, and independence came only in 1990 after a long guerrilla war.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>",
"Niger": "Niger is a landlocked country of 1,267,000 km² (489,200 sq mi) in West Africa with roughly 28 million people, and its capital is Niamey.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> About four-fifths of it is Sahara or semi-arid Sahel, with the volcanic Aïr massif in the centre and the Niger, its only permanent river, crossing the far southwest, where nearly all the farmland and population are. Trans-Saharan caravan routes ran through it for centuries under the sultanate of Agadez, France conquered the region around 1900, independence came in 1960, and its uranium mines supply a significant share of world output while drought and conflict in the Sahel press on daily life.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>",
"Nigeria": "Nigeria is a country of 923,769 km² (356,669 sq mi) in West Africa with roughly 230 million people, by far the most populous in Africa, with its capital at Abuja and Lagos its largest city.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The Niger and Benue meet in the middle, dividing the country into rainforest in the south, savanna in the centre and drier Sahel in the north; oil from the Niger delta supplies most export earnings. More than 250 ethnic groups live within borders that enclosed the Sokoto Caliphate, the kingdom of Benin and the Oyo Empire before Britain made them one colony in 1914; independence came in 1960, and Biafra's secession brought war from 1967 to 1970.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>",
"Rwanda": "Rwanda is a landlocked country of 26,338 km² (10,169 sq mi) in East Africa with roughly 15 million people, and its capital is Kigali.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Known as the land of a thousand hills, it is almost entirely upland between about 1,500 and 2,500 m (4,900 to 8,200 feet), with volcanoes along its northwestern border and terraced smallholdings covering the slopes, and it is the most densely populated country on the African mainland. A kingdom ruled here before German and then Belgian administration hardened the distinction between Hutu and Tutsi, independence came in 1962, and in 1994 a genocide killed some 800,000 people, mostly Tutsi, in about a hundred days.",
"São_Tomé_and_Príncipe": "São Tomé and Príncipe is a two-island state of 964 km² (372 sq mi) in the Gulf of Guinea, with about 240,000 people at 250 to the square kilometre and its capital at São Tomé.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Its forest is shrinking, from 57.3 per cent of the land in 2015 to 52.8 per cent by 2022, and nearly three-quarters of its people live in towns.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The islands were under Portuguese sovereignty until 1975, when the United States recognised the new republic on 12 July and it joined the United Nations on 16 September; tourism is small, and the country is still negotiating its accession to the World Trade Organization.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>",
"Senegal": "Senegal is a country of 196,712 km² (75,951 sq mi) in West Africa with roughly 19 million people, wrapped around The Gambia, with its capital at Dakar on the westernmost point of the African mainland.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> It is mostly low and flat, sandy in the drier north and greener toward the south, with groundnuts, fishing and phosphates supporting much of the economy. Gorée island off Dakar was a European trading post from the 15th century and a site associated with the Atlantic slave trade; France made Dakar the capital of French West Africa, independence came in 1960, and Senegal has had one of the steadier records of civilian government since.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>",
"Seychelles": "Seychelles is an island country of 457 km² (176 sq mi) in the western Indian Ocean with about 130,000 people and its capital at Victoria on Mahé.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> It comprises over 100 islands in two groups, an inner group of about 40 that are rocky and mountainous and an outer group that is low-lying and coralline; forest covers 73.3 per cent of the land, and Kenya lies about 1,600 km (1,000 miles) west and Madagascar about 1,100 km (700 miles) south.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> It attained independence from British sovereignty on 29 June 1976, joining the Commonwealth that year and the United Nations on 21 September, and 332,000 visitors arrived in 2022, two and a half times its own population.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>",
"Sierra_Leone": "Sierra Leone is a country of 71,740 km² (27,700 sq mi) in West Africa with roughly 8.8 million people, its capital Freetown built around one of the largest natural harbours in the world.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Mangrove swamps line the shore, rainforest and plateau fill the interior, and it lies in one of the wettest belts in Africa; diamonds, rutile and bauxite are its chief exports. Freetown was founded in 1787 as a settlement for freed and rescued slaves, the interior became a British protectorate in 1896, independence came in 1961, and a civil war fuelled partly by diamonds ran from 1991 to 2002, followed by a severe Ebola epidemic in 2014.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>",
"Somalia": "Somalia is a country of 637,657 km² (246,201 sq mi) in the Horn of Africa with roughly 20 million people, holding the longest mainland coastline in Africa, its capital Mogadishu. It is mostly dry plateau and semi-desert, and only the Jubba and the Shabelle in the south allow settled farming, so herding camels, goats and cattle has long been the way of life for much of the population. Its coastal towns traded across the Indian Ocean for centuries, the territory was divided between Britain and Italy and reunited at independence in 1960, and the collapse of central government in 1991 was followed by decades of conflict, the northwest functioning separately as Somaliland.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>",
"South_Africa": "South Africa is a country of 1,221,037 km² (471,445 sq mi) at the continent's southern end with roughly 65 million people and coasts on both the Atlantic and the Indian Ocean; it has three capitals: Pretoria for the executive, Cape Town for parliament, Bloemfontein for the courts.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> A high interior plateau is separated from a narrow coastal belt by the Great Escarpment, and its mines lead the world in platinum and gold. Dutch settlement began at the Cape in 1652, British rule followed, the Union was formed in 1910, apartheid enforced racial segregation and disenfranchised the Black majority from 1948, and the first democratic elections in 1994 brought Nelson Mandela to the presidency.",
"South_Sudan": "South Sudan is a landlocked country of 644,329 km² (248,777 sq mi) in East-Central Africa with roughly 12 million people, with its capital at Juba.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The White Nile runs the length of the country and spreads in the middle into the Sudd, one of the largest wetlands in the world, whose seasonal floods long made travel extremely difficult; savanna and forest lie beyond. Two long wars against Khartoum, from 1955 to 1972 and from 1983 to 2005, ended in a referendum that made it the world's newest widely recognised state in 2011; a civil war followed from 2013 to 2018, and oil provides almost all government revenue.",
"Sudan": "Sudan is a country of 1,878,000 km² (725,100 sq mi) in northeastern Africa with roughly 52 million people and a Red Sea coast; its capital Khartoum stands where the Blue and White Niles meet.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Desert covers the north, giving way to Sahel and then savanna in the south, almost all settlement follows the Nile, and Darfur in the west rises to the volcanic Marra mountains. The Nubian kingdoms of Kush and Meroë ruled here and once conquered Egypt, Britain and Egypt governed it jointly until independence in 1956, it lost its southern third when South Sudan seceded in 2011, and a war between rival armed forces broke out in 2023.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>",
"Togo": "Togo is a country of 56,785 km² (21,925 sq mi) in West Africa with roughly 8.6 million people, a narrow strip inland from a short coast on the Gulf of Guinea, with its capital at Lomé.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Lagoons and sandbars line the shore, hills divide the wetter south from the drier northern savanna, and phosphate, cocoa, coffee and cotton are its main exports. Germany claimed the territory as Togoland in 1884,<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Britain and France divided it along a line agreed in 1919 and the French part went from mandate to United Nations trusteeship,<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> independence came in 1960,<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> and the presidency passed to Faure Gnassingbé on Gnassingbé Eyadéma’s death in 2005.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup>",
"Tunisia": "Tunisia is a country of 163,610 km² (63,170 sq mi) in North Africa, the smallest Maghreb state, with roughly 12 million people, a Mediterranean coast and its capital at Tunis.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The north is hilly and green, watered enough for grain and olives, the centre dry steppe, and the south passes into salt flats and the Sahara; the country juts toward Sicily. Carthage, founded near modern Tunis, contested the western Mediterranean with Rome until its destruction in 146 BCE; the region was later Roman, Arab, Ottoman and from 1881 a French protectorate, independence came in 1956, and protests beginning in 2010 unseated its president and set off uprisings across the region.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>",
"Uganda": "Uganda is a landlocked country of 241,550 km² (93,263 sq mi) in East Africa with roughly 51 million people, with its capital at Kampala.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Most of it is a plateau around 1,100 m (3,600 feet) between the two branches of the Great Rift Valley, with much of Lake Victoria in the southeast, where the White Nile begins, and the glaciated Rwenzori on the western border. The kingdom of Buganda dominated the region before Britain declared a protectorate in 1894, independence came in 1962, it suffered under Idi Amin from 1971 to 1979 and the wars that followed, and its economy rests on coffee, fish and increasingly oil found near Lake Albert.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>",
"Zambia": "Zambia is a landlocked country of 752,612 km² (290,585 sq mi) in southern Africa with roughly 22 million people, bordered by eight states and named after the Zambezi river, with its capital at Lusaka.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Most of it is a high plateau between about 1,000 and 1,400 m (3,300 to 4,600 feet), and the Zambezi forms much of its southern border, dropping 108 m (354 feet) at Victoria Falls, one of the largest sheets of falling water in the world. The Copperbelt has been mined for a century and copper still provides most export earnings; the region became the British territory of Northern Rhodesia, independence came in 1964, and one-party rule lasted until multiparty elections in 1991.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>",
"Zimbabwe": "Zimbabwe is a landlocked country of 390,757 km² (150,872 sq mi) in southern Africa with roughly 17 million people, with its capital at Harare.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> A central ridge of high veld above 1,200 m (3,900 feet) carries most of the towns and farms, falling away to the hotter low veld along the Zambezi and the Limpopo, with the Eastern Highlands near the Mozambican border. The stone city of Great Zimbabwe, built from the 11th century at the centre of a gold-trading state, gives the country its name; Britain's chartered company took the territory in 1890, a white minority government declared independence unilaterally in 1965, and majority rule came in 1980 after a long guerrilla war.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>",
"New_Zealand": "New Zealand is an island country of 268,107 km² (103,517 sq mi) in the southwest Pacific southeast of Australia, with roughly 5.3 million people and its capital at Wellington.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> It consists of two large islands and many smaller ones: the volcanic North Island, with geysers, and the South Island, split lengthwise by the Southern Alps and their glaciers and deep fiords. Its long isolation produced a fauna dominated by birds, with no native land mammals but bats; Polynesian settlers arrived around 1280 and became the Māori, Britain and Māori chiefs signed the Treaty of Waitangi in 1840, and it has been self-governing since the early 20th century.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>",
"Papua_New_Guinea": "Papua New Guinea is a country of 462,840 km² (178,700 sq mi) in Oceania with roughly 10.8 million people, occupying the eastern half of New Guinea with some 600 smaller islands; its capital is Port Moresby.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> A spine of mountains rises above 4,500 m (15,000 feet), separating coastal swamps from forested highland valleys, terrain so broken that many communities were effectively isolated until recent decades. That isolation helped produce more than 800 living languages, more than any other country, and its highland agriculture is among the oldest known anywhere; Germany and Britain divided it in the 19th century, Australia ruled it for most of the 20th, and independence came in 1975.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>",
"Fiji": "Fiji is an island country of 18,272 km² (7,055 sq mi) in the South Pacific with roughly 930,000 people, made up of about 300 islands of which only about 100 are inhabited, and governed from Suva.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The two main islands are volcanic and mountainous, dividing a wet windward side of rainforest from a drier leeward side of sugar cane, while the outer islands are low coral; forest covers 60.6 per cent of the country.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> It became a British colony in 1874, and indentured labourers brought from India to work its sugar estates left a large Indo-Fijian population; independence came in 1970, and coups from 1987 saw the Commonwealth suspend its membership three times.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>",
"Solomon_Islands": "Solomon Islands is a country of 28,896 km² (11,157 sq mi) in the southwest Pacific with roughly 840,000 people, two parallel chains of volcanic islands and coral atolls, with its capital at Honiara on Guadalcanal.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> It is mainly mountainous and covered in forest, with some extensive plains, and sits on an active plate boundary that brings frequent earthquakes and tsunamis; its nearest neighbours are Vanuatu and Papua New Guinea.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Settled from Papua and the wider Pacific thousands of years ago, the islands came under British sovereignty, saw some of the fiercest fighting of the Pacific war at Guadalcanal, gained independence in 1978 and joined the Commonwealth that year.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>",
"Vanuatu": "Vanuatu is an island country of 12,189 km² (4,706 sq mi) in the southwestern Pacific with roughly 330,000 people and its capital at Port Vila, about 800 km (500 miles) west of Fiji.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> It is a double chain of 13 principal islands and many smaller ones, volcanic and coral in origin and around 65 of them inhabited; forest covers 36.3 per cent of the land and only a quarter of its people live in towns.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Britain and France held the islands together under the joint sovereignty of a condominium until independence on 30 July 1980, and the United States, which recognised the republic that year, opened an embassy at Port Vila only in July 2024.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>",
"Samoa": "Samoa is an island country of 2,842 km² (1,097 sq mi) in the central South Pacific about halfway between New Zealand and Hawaii, with roughly 219,000 people and its capital at Apia.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> It is an archipelago of nine islands, four inhabited and the largest Savai’i and Upolu, mountainous and volcanic; forest covers 57.8 per cent of the land and only 18.1 per cent of its people live in towns.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Western Samoa was a trust territory administered by New Zealand until independence on 1 January 1962; it joined the Commonwealth in 1970, took the official name Independent State of Samoa in 1997 and hosted the Commonwealth heads of government in 2024.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>",
"Tonga": "Tonga is an island country of 747 km² (288 sq mi) in the South Pacific with roughly 104,000 people and its capital at Nuku’alofa, two roughly parallel chains of around 170 islands of which only 36 are inhabited.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Its western islands are volcanic, four of them with active volcanoes, and sit well above sea level, while those to the east are coral and lower-lying; forest covers 12.4 per cent of the land.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> A unified kingdom was established in 1845 under King George Tupou I and became a constitutional monarchy in 1875; Tonga was a British protectorate from 1900, became independent within the Commonwealth on 4 June 1970 and remains a constitutional monarchy.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>",
"Kiribati": "Kiribati is an island country of 811 km² (313 sq mi) in the central Pacific with roughly 136,000 people, made up of 33 islands of which only 20 are inhabited, and governed from Tarawa.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Most of them are very low-lying atolls scattered widely across the ocean, so rising seas threaten the country’s habitability, and it holds the South Pacific’s largest marine reserve.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> With the Ellice Islands the Gilberts became a British protectorate in 1892 and a colony in 1916; the two separated in 1975 and Kiribati became independent on 12 July 1979, joining the Commonwealth that year and the United Nations in 1999.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>",
"Federated_States_of_Micronesia": "The Federated States of Micronesia is an island country of 702 km² (271 sq mi) of land in the western Pacific with roughly 114,000 people, governed from Palikir, and a federation of four states: Kosrae, Chuuk, Pohnpei and Yap.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Forest covers 92.1 per cent of its land, fewer than a quarter of its people live in towns, and the United States dollar is its currency.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> After the Second World War the islands were part of the United Nations Trust Territory of the Pacific Islands under American administration; the United States recognised their independence on 3 November 1986, and the Security Council determined that the trusteeship had terminated on 22 December 1990.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>",
"Marshall_Islands": "The Marshall Islands is an island country of 181 km² (70 sq mi) of land in the central Pacific with roughly 36,000 people, made up of 29 coral atolls and five islands in two long chains, with its capital at Majuro.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Almost all of it stands about two metres (6 ft 7 in) above the sea, so flooding and saltwater in its soil and wells are pressing problems. Settled by Micronesian navigators, the islands passed from Spain to Germany and Japan and were administered by the United States after 1944; 67 nuclear weapons were tested at Bikini and Enewetak between 1946 and 1958, displacing communities that in some cases have never returned, and independence came in 1986.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>",
"Palau": "Palau is an island country of 459 km² (177 sq mi) in the western Pacific with roughly 18,000 people, an archipelago of more than 340 islands east of the Philippines; the United Nations names Melekeok as its capital.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Most of its people live on a few islands; the Rock Islands to the south are hundreds of uninhabited limestone mounds, and the surrounding waters hold rich coral, most of its exclusive economic zone being closed to commercial fishing. Settled from island Southeast Asia millennia ago, Palau was held in turn by Spain, Germany and Japan, saw heavy fighting in 1944, and after American administration under a United Nations trusteeship became independent in 1994.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>",
"Nauru": "Nauru is an island country of 21 km² (8 sq mi) in the central Pacific with roughly 12,000 people; it has no official capital, and government offices stand in Yaren.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The island is a raised coral platform with a narrow fertile coastal strip where almost everyone lives and a central plateau of limestone pinnacles left after a century of phosphate mining stripped roughly four-fifths of the surface. Germany annexed it in 1888, Australia, Britain and New Zealand administered it after 1914, independence came in 1968, and phosphate wealth that briefly gave it one of the highest incomes per head anywhere was largely exhausted by the 1990s.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>",
"Tuvalu": "Tuvalu is an island country of 26 km² (10 sq mi) in the central Pacific with roughly 10,000 people, nine low coral atolls and reef islands, and its seat of government on Funafuti.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Nothing on it rises more than about 4.6 m (15 feet) above sea level, so tidal flooding reaches inland several times a year and rising seas are the country’s central problem. Settled by Polynesian voyagers, the islands were a British protectorate from 1892 within the Gilbert and Ellice Islands, separated in 1975 and became independent in 1978; fishing licences and its internet domain provide much of its income.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>",
"Canada": "Canada is a country of 9,984,670 km² (3,855,100 sq mi) in northern North America, the second largest in the world by area, with about 41 million people and its capital at Ottawa.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Most of it is boreal forest, tundra and the Canadian Shield, and its coastline on three oceans is the longest of any country, so most of its people live in a narrow band near the southern border. First Nations, Inuit and Métis peoples lived there long before French and British colonies were founded from the 17th century; the British colonies federated in 1867, gained full legislative independence by 1931, and English and French are both official.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>",
"United_States": "The United States is a federal republic of 9,830,000 km² (3,800,000 sq mi) in North America with about 340 million people, bordered by Canada and Mexico, of 50 states and a federal capital district at Washington.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Its land runs from the Atlantic coastal plains over the Appalachians to the Great Plains, the Rockies and the Pacific, and takes in Alaska and Hawaii. Thirteen British colonies declared independence in 1776 and secured it in 1783; the country expanded west at the expense of Native American nations, was torn by a civil war over slavery from 1861 to 1865, and in the 20th century became the world's largest economy and dominant military power.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>",
"Mexico": "Mexico is a country of 1,964,375 km² (758,449 sq mi) in southern North America with about 130 million people, with coasts on the Pacific and the Gulf of Mexico and its capital at Mexico City.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> High plateaus lie between the Sierra Madre Occidental and the Sierra Madre Oriental, with volcanoes above 5,000 m (16,000 feet), desert across the north, forest in the south and the flat limestone shelf of Yucatán. Seat of the Olmec, Maya, Zapotec and Aztec civilisations, it was made the centre of New Spain by the Spanish conquest of 1519 to 1521; independence came in 1821, a revolution begun in 1910 remade the country, now the most populous Spanish-speaking nation.",
"Guatemala": "Guatemala is a country of 108,889 km² (42,042 sq mi) in Central America with roughly 19 million people, with coasts on both the Pacific and the Caribbean and its capital at Guatemala City.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> A chain of volcanoes runs above the southern highlands, where most people live and where earthquakes are frequent, while the north holds the low limestone forest of the Petén. It was the heartland of the Maya, whose cities at Tikal and elsewhere flourished for centuries before Spanish conquest in the 1520s; independence came in 1821, a civil war from 1960 to 1996 killed some 200,000 people, most of them Maya, and about two fifths of the population is Indigenous.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>",
"Belize": "Belize is a country of 22,965 km² (8,867 sq mi) on the Caribbean coast of Central America with about 420,000 people, bordered by Mexico to the north and Guatemala to the west and south, and its capital at Belmopan.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Its long east coast is mainly flat and swampy, low mountains rise in the south, and tropical forest covers 58.4 per cent of the land.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> A British colony from 1862, it took the name Belize in June 1973 and became independent on 21 September 1981; its territorial dispute with Guatemala went to the International Court of Justice, which was seised of it on 12 June 2019.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>",
"El_Salvador": "El Salvador is a country of 21,041 km² (8,124 sq mi) on the Pacific coast of Central America with about 6.4 million people, bordered by Guatemala and Honduras, and its capital at San Salvador.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> It is the smallest and most densely settled state on the Central American mainland, with two volcanic ranges and a central plateau of rich ash soils where coffee grows and most people live. Pipil and other Indigenous peoples farmed the valleys before Spanish conquest in 1524; independence came in 1821, land concentrated in few hands through the coffee boom, and a civil war from 1980 to 1992 drove hundreds of thousands abroad, whose remittances still sustain the economy.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>",
"Honduras": "Honduras is a country of 112,492 km² (43,433 sq mi) in Central America with roughly 11 million people, with a long Caribbean coast, a short Pacific one on the Gulf of Fonseca, and its capital at Tegucigalpa.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Most of it is rugged forested mountain, with the broad valleys and banana plantations of the north coast, the sparsely peopled rainforest and wetlands of the Mosquitia in the east, and reefs along the Bay Islands offshore. The Maya city of Copán, famous for its carved stelae, stood in the west before Spanish conquest from 1524; independence came in 1821, and from the late 19th century American fruit companies shaped much of its politics.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>",
"Nicaragua": "Nicaragua is a country of 130,374 km² (50,338 sq mi) in Central America with about 7 million people, the largest state on the isthmus, with coasts on the Pacific and the Caribbean and its capital at Managua.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> A line of active volcanoes runs down the Pacific side beside Lake Nicaragua, the biggest in Central America, while the Caribbean lowland is thinly settled rainforest and swamp, home to Miskito and Creole communities. Spanish conquest began in 1524 and independence came in 1821; the Somoza family ruled from 1936 until the Sandinista revolution of 1979, and the contra war of the 1980s left it among the poorest in the hemisphere.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>",
"Costa_Rica": "Costa Rica is a country of 51,100 km² (19,700 sq mi) in Central America with about 5 million people, bordered by Nicaragua and Panama, with coasts on the Pacific and the Caribbean and its capital at San José.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Volcanic ranges run the length of the country and enclose a temperate central valley where most of the population lives, and it holds an outsized share of the world's species. Independence from Spain came with the rest of Central America in 1821<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> and Costa Rica declared itself a separate republic in 1848;<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> its constitution of 1949 proscribes the army as a permanent institution, and the country lives on coffee, bananas, microchips and ecotourism.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>",
"Panama": "Panama is a country of 75,320 km² (29,080 sq mi) at the southern end of Central America with about 4.5 million people, bordered by Costa Rica and Colombia, and its capital at Panama City.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> It occupies the narrowest neck of the Americas, an S-shaped isthmus of forested mountains with coasts on the Caribbean and the Pacific, broken only by the roadless Darién rainforest. Spanish treasure crossed it from the 16th century; after separating from Colombia in 1903 with United States backing, it was cut through by the Panama Canal, opened in 1914 and handed to Panamanian control at the end of 1999; the canal, banking and its shipping registry underpin its economy.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>",
"Cuba": "Cuba is an island country of 109,884 km² (42,426 sq mi) in the northern Caribbean with about 10.9 million people, the largest island state in the region, lying south of Florida, with its capital at Havana.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Most of it is low rolling farmland well suited to sugar cane and tobacco, with the Sierra Maestra rising above 1,900 m (6,200 feet) in the southeast and hurricanes crossing it most years. Taíno peoples lived there before Columbus landed in 1492 and Spain built a plantation colony worked by enslaved Africans; independence came in 1898 after a long struggle and American intervention, and the revolution of 1959 brought a one-party communist state that survived decades of embargo.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>",
"Jamaica": "Jamaica is an island country of 10,991 km² (4,244 sq mi) in the Caribbean with about 2.8 million people, lying south of Cuba, with its capital at Kingston.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> A limestone spine runs down the middle, rising to the Blue Mountains above 2,200 m (7,200 feet) in the east and falling to the cockpit country of sinkholes and steep hummocks in the west, ringed by coastal plains and beaches. Taíno inhabitants were nearly wiped out after the Spanish arrived in 1494; the English took the island in 1655 and made it one of the richest sugar colonies in the world with enslaved African labour, and emancipation came in 1834 and independence in 1962.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>",
"Haiti": "Haiti is a country of 27,750 km² (10,710 sq mi) occupying the western third of the Caribbean island of Hispaniola, with roughly 12 million people and its capital at Port-au-Prince.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Two mountainous peninsulas enclose the Gulf of Gonâve, the interior is steep and heavily deforested, and the island sits on active faults that produced the catastrophic earthquake of 2010. It was the French sugar colony of Saint-Domingue, the richest in the Caribbean, until enslaved people rose in 1791 and defeated French, Spanish and British forces to declare independence in 1804, making it the first Black republic, though a crushing indemnity paid to France and long periods of dictatorship and instability followed.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>",
"Dominican_Republic": "The Dominican Republic is a country of 48,671 km² (18,792 sq mi) occupying the eastern two thirds of the island of Hispaniola in the Caribbean, with about 12 million people, bordered by Haiti, and its capital at Santo Domingo.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Four mountain ranges cross it, including the Cordillera Central with Pico Duarte at 3,098 m (10,160 feet), the highest point in the Caribbean, and between them lie fertile valleys and dry southwestern plains. Santo Domingo, founded in 1496, was the first lasting European city in the Americas; the country won independence from Haiti in 1844, endured the dictatorship of Rafael Trujillo from 1930 to 1961, and now lives largely on tourism, mining and remittances.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>",
"The_Bahamas": "The Bahamas is an island country of 13,940 km² (5,382 sq mi) in the western Atlantic with about 400,000 people, nearly 700 coral islands of which around 30 are inhabited, and its capital at Nassau.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The islands are generally flat and low-lying, 100 km (60 miles) southeast of Florida and 80 km (50 miles) northeast of Cuba, with forest over half the land and more than four people in five living in towns.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> It became independent from the United Kingdom in 1973 and joined the Commonwealth that year, the United States recognising it on 10 July by opening an embassy at Nassau; tourism dominates, 1,452,000 visitors having arrived in 2022.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>",
"Barbados": "Barbados is an island country of 431 km² (166 sq mi) in the eastern Caribbean with about 283,000 people at 657 to the square kilometre, and its capital at Bridgetown.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> It is a fairly flat island rising through terraced tablelands to Mount Hillaby at 336 m (1,102 feet), surrounded by coral reefs, with Saint Lucia to the north and Trinidad and Tobago to the south.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The English settled it in 1627 and worked it as a sugar colony with enslaved Africans, whose descendants form most of its people; it became independent within the Commonwealth on 30 November 1966, joined the United Nations nine days later and became a republic in 2021.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>",
"Trinidad_and_Tobago": "Trinidad and Tobago is a country of 5,130 km² (1,980 sq mi) at the southern end of the Caribbean with about 1.4 million people, two main islands and several smaller ones lying close to South America, with its capital at Port of Spain.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Trinidad has three mountain ranges, high waterfalls and sandy beaches, while smaller Tobago has a single range, beaches and coral reefs.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Spain held Trinidad from 1498 and Britain from 1797, while Tobago changed hands many times; after emancipation, indentured labourers came from India, and since independence in 1962 oil and gas have made it one of the most industrialised countries in the English-speaking Caribbean.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>",
"Antigua_and_Barbuda": "Antigua and Barbuda is a dry tropical country of 442 km² (171 sq mi) in the eastern Caribbean with about 94,000 people and its capital at Saint John’s, made up of three islands.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Antigua has secluded bays, sandy beaches, coral reefs and volcanic rocks rising to about 400 m (1,300 feet) at Mount Obama; Barbuda is a flat coral island with a large lagoon, and tiny Redonda is rocky and uninhabited.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> It became an independent state within the Commonwealth on 1 November 1981, with Queen Elizabeth as its first Queen and Vere Cornwall Bird as its first prime minister, joining the United Nations ten days later, and 265,000 visitors arrived in 2022.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>",
"Dominica": "Dominica is an island country of 750 km² (290 sq mi) in the eastern Caribbean with about 66,000 people, lying between the French islands of Guadeloupe and Marie-Galante to the north and Martinique to the south, with its capital at Roseau.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> It was formed by volcanic activity and has a range of high mountains covered in forest and several volcanoes still active; forest covers 63.8 per cent of its land and agriculture supplies 18.4 per cent of value added.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Great Britain granted independence to the Commonwealth of Dominica on 3 November 1978, and it joined the Commonwealth that year and the United Nations on 18 December.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>",
"Grenada": "Grenada is a volcanic island country of 345 km² (133 sq mi) in the eastern Caribbean about 160 km (100 miles) north of Venezuela, with about 117,000 people and its capital at Saint George’s.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> A ridge of mountains runs from north to south with steep valleys, tropical rainforest covers large areas and 52.1 per cent of the land, and some of the southern Grenadines are a dependency of the country.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> It became independent from the United Kingdom on 7 February 1974 and joined the United Nations that September, and American and Organization of Eastern Caribbean States forces invaded the island in 1983 after the assassination of Prime Minister Maurice Bishop.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>",
"Saint_Kitts_and_Nevis": "Saint Kitts and Nevis is a country of 261 km² (101 sq mi) in the eastern Caribbean with about 47,000 people, made up of two volcanic islands, with its capital at Basseterre.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Saint Kitts is dominated by the forested cone of Mount Liamuiga at 1,156 m (3,793 feet), its lower slopes once terraced in cane fields, while rounder Nevis rises to a single cloud-capped peak. English settlers arrived in 1623, making these the site of Great Britain’s first Caribbean settlement, building a sugar economy on enslaved labour; independence came in 1983, sugar production ended in 2005, and tourism and offshore services replaced it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>",
"Saint_Lucia": "Saint Lucia is a volcanic island country of 616 km² (238 sq mi) in the eastern Caribbean between Martinique and Saint Vincent, with about 180,000 people and its capital at Castries.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> A central ridge of mountains runs down it, with many rivers, boiling sulphur springs and sandy beaches; forest covers 34 per cent of the land, and 356,000 visitors arrived in 2022, twice its own population.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> British colonial rule was established in 1815; the island joined the West Indies Federation from 1958 to 1962, became a state associated with the United Kingdom in 1967 and became fully independent on 22 February 1979, joining the Commonwealth and the United Nations that year.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>",
"Saint_Vincent_and_the_Grenadines": "Saint Vincent and the Grenadines is a country of 389 km² (150 sq mi) in the eastern Caribbean with about 100,000 people, made up of the island of Saint Vincent and the northern Grenadines, a chain of 32 smaller islands and cays, with its capital at Kingstown.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> It is volcanic and mountainous with one volcano still active, 73.2 per cent of its land is forested, and the southern Grenadines belong to Grenada.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Saint Vincent was ceded to Great Britain in 1763 and, after a referendum, became the last of the Windward Islands to reach independence, on 27 October 1979, joining the Commonwealth that year and the United Nations in 1980.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>",
"Colombia": "Colombia is a country of 1,141,748 km² (440,831 sq mi) in northwestern South America with about 53 million people, the only South American state with coasts on both the Caribbean and the Pacific, and its capital at Bogotá.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The Andes split into three ranges as they enter the country, enclosing the Cauca and Magdalena valleys where most cities stand, while the east is savanna and Amazon rainforest. Muisca and other peoples worked the gold that fed the El Dorado legend before Spanish conquest from 1499; independence was won in 1819 under Simón Bolívar, and a long conflict involving guerrillas, paramilitaries and the drug trade led to a peace agreement in 2016.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>",
"Venezuela": "Venezuela is a country of 916,445 km² (353,841 sq mi) in northern South America with roughly 28 million people, with a long Caribbean coast and its capital at Caracas.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Its landscapes range from Andean peaks and the oil-rich basin of Lake Maracaibo to the wide Orinoco plains and the sandstone tabletops of the Gran Sabana, where Angel Falls drops 979 m (3,210 feet), the highest uninterrupted waterfall in the world. Spanish settlement began in the early 16th century, independence was won under Simón Bolívar by 1821, and oil discovered in the 1910s made it wealthy before political crisis and economic collapse after 2013 drove nearly 8 million people to leave.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>",
"Guyana": "Guyana is a country of 214,969 km² (83,000 sq mi) on the northeastern shoulder of South America, with about 840,000 people and its capital at Georgetown.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Almost all its people live on a coastal strip partly below sea level, behind Dutch-built seawalls and canals, while the interior is rainforest and sandstone highland, forest covering 93.8 per cent of it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The Dutch colonies of Essequibo, Demerara and Berbice were ceded to Britain at the Congress of Vienna and made one colony in 1831, its plantations worked by enslaved Africans and then indentured Indians; independence came in 1966, and oil has carried income per head from 6,779 US dollars in 2020 to 20,189 in 2023.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>",
"Suriname": "Suriname is a country of 163,820 km² (63,251 sq mi) on the northeastern coast of South America with about 640,000 people, and its capital at Paramaribo.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Forest covers 95 per cent of its land and almost all its people live on the coastal plain, behind which lie granite hills and a vast reservoir created by the Afobaka dam.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The Dutch took it from England in 1667 and ran a brutal plantation economy on enslaved African labour, from which escapees formed the Maroon communities still living in the interior; after emancipation, workers came from India, Java and China, and independence followed on 25 November 1975.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>",
"Ecuador": "Ecuador is a country of 257,217 km² (99,312 sq mi) in western South America with about 18 million people, bordered by Colombia and Peru, with a Pacific coast and its capital at Quito, and it also governs the Galápagos Islands.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The equator crosses it, giving it its name, and two Andean ranges run down the middle with volcanoes such as Chimborazo, whose summit is the point on the Earth's surface farthest from its centre. Quito was a northern seat of the Inca Empire before the Spanish conquest of the 1530s; independence came in 1822 and the country left Gran Colombia in 1830, and oil, bananas, flowers and shrimp now dominate its exports.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>",
"Peru": "Peru is a country of 1,285,216 km² (496,224 sq mi) in western South America with about 35 million people, with a Pacific coast and its capital at Lima.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Three zones divide it: a narrow desert coast where the cold Humboldt current feeds rich fisheries, the high Andes with peaks above 6,700 m (22,000 feet), and Amazon lowland covering more than half the country. Seat of Norte Chico, one of the oldest urban societies in the Americas, and later of the Inca Empire, whose capital Cusco and estate at Machu Picchu still stand, it was conquered by Spain from 1532; independence was declared in 1821 and secured in 1824.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>",
"Bolivia": "Bolivia is a landlocked country of 1,098,581 km² (424,164 sq mi) in central South America with about 12.6 million people, its constitutional capital at Sucre and its seat of government at La Paz.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The western third is high Andean plateau, holding Lake Titicaca and the salt flat of Uyuni at around 3,650 m (12,000 feet), while the east falls away to the Amazon basin. The silver of Potosí funded the Spanish empire for two centuries at enormous cost in Indigenous and African lives; independence came in 1825, the Pacific coast was lost to Chile in a war ending in 1884, and a majority Indigenous population elected one of its own as president in 2005.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>",
"Paraguay": "Paraguay is a landlocked country of 406,752 km² (157,048 sq mi) in central South America with about 7 million people, and its capital is Asunción.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The Paraguay river cuts it in two: the east is rolling grassland where nearly all the population lives, while the west is the Chaco, a hot thorn-scrub plain with few inhabitants; the Paraná on the Brazilian border drives the enormous Itaipú dam. Jesuit missions organised Guaraní communities under Spanish rule, independence came in 1811, and the War of the Triple Alliance from 1864 to 1870 killed a large share of the population; Guaraní remains an official language spoken by most people alongside Spanish.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>",
"Chile": "Chile is a country of 756,102 km² (291,932 sq mi) along the western edge of South America with about 20 million people, bordered by Peru, Bolivia and Argentina, and its capital at Santiago.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> It stretches more than 4,000 km (2,500 miles) north to south while rarely exceeding 200 km (120 miles) in width, running from the Atacama, the driest desert on Earth, through a fertile centre to Patagonian fjords, with the Andes along its eastern flank. Mapuche communities held out against Spanish rule for centuries; independence was won by 1818, nitrate and later copper made it a mining economy, and a military coup in 1973 was followed by dictatorship until democracy returned in 1990.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>",
"Argentina": "Argentina is a country of 2,780,400 km² (1,073,500 sq mi) in southern South America with about 46 million people, the second largest state on the continent, and its capital at Buenos Aires.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The Andes form its western edge and rise at Aconcagua to 6,961 m (22,840 feet), the highest summit outside Asia, while the fertile pampas carry the cattle and grain that long made the country rich and the south is the plateau of Patagonia. Spanish settlement grew slowly until independence in 1816, after which mass immigration from Italy and Spain reshaped the population; the 20th century brought Peronism and a military dictatorship from 1976 to 1983 that killed and disappeared thousands.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>",
"Uruguay": "Uruguay is a country of 176,215 km² (68,037 sq mi) in southeastern South America with about 3.4 million people, between Brazil and Argentina on the Atlantic and the Río de la Plata; its capital is Montevideo.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> It is almost entirely rolling grassland with a mild climate, so cattle and sheep ranching has shaped land and economy since colonial times; nearly half the population lives in the capital. Spain and Portugal contested it before independence in 1828 as a buffer between two large neighbours; it built one of the region's first welfare states, endured a military dictatorship from 1973 to 1985, and is known for stable democracy and liberal social laws.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>",
"Lake_Turkana": "Lake Turkana is a long, narrow lake in the arid Rift Valley of northern Kenya, its far tip crossing into Ethiopia, both the largest permanent desert lake and the largest alkaline lake anywhere.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> About nine-tenths of its water arrives from the Ethiopian highlands down the Omo, and with no outlet the water is brackish and takes a green cast from algae, earning it the name Jade Sea.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> It was called Lake Rudolf until 1975, and the dry fossil beds along its shores, Koobi Fora and West Turkana, have yielded one of the richest records of early hominins known; its national parks joined the World Heritage List in 1997.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>",
"Badlands": "Badlands are dry, thinly vegetated country in which soft sedimentary rocks such as clay, silt and shale have been carved by water and wind into a maze of steep gullies, sharp ridges and flat-topped hills.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> They develop where rain falls seldom but hard on ground too loose and impermeable to hold much plant cover, so each downpour strips the surface faster than soil and vegetation can rebuild it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The constantly retreating slopes lay bare fresh rock, which makes badlands prized hunting ground for fossils, and the name translates a French phrase for the eroded country of the northern Great Plains, echoing a Lakota expression meaning bad land.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>",
"Lomekwi_3": "Lomekwi 3 is a site in the badlands west of Lake Turkana in northern Kenya, where flaked stone artefacts lay in deposits dated to about 3.3 million years ago, the oldest such tools known.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Found in 2011 and published in 2015, its roughly 150 cores, flakes and anvils were made by striking the core down onto a block on the ground, a heavy two-handed method giving large thick flakes.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Because that date lies some 700,000 years before the oldest Oldowan tools and before the genus <i>Homo</i> appears, many take the finds as evidence that toolmaking began among earlier hominins, while others question how securely they and their age are established.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>",
"Gona,_Ethiopia": "Gona is a research area of roughly 500 km² (200 sq mi) of fossil-bearing badlands in the Afar Region of northeastern Ethiopia, cut by several tributaries of the Awash River.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Excavations there in the early 1990s recovered thousands of flaked stones dated to about 2.6 million years ago, for many years the oldest known tools of the Oldowan, some of them lying among broken animal bones that show carcasses were being cut up and cracked open for marrow.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Its deposits span more than six million years and have also yielded fossils of <i>Ardipithecus kadabba</i> and <i>Ardipithecus ramidus</i>, an unusually complete <i>Homo erectus</i> pelvis, and early Acheulean handaxes.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>",
"Lomekwian": "The Lomekwian is the name proposed in 2015 for the stone tool industry of Lomekwi 3, a site west of Lake Turkana in northern Kenya.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Its knappers worked large blocks against heavy stones, mostly by bringing the block down in both hands onto an anvil set on the ground or holding it steady while striking from above with a hammerstone, leaving big thick flakes and pieces battered from pounding.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Because the industry rests on a single excavated assemblage and a long gap separates it from the next known tools, its standing as a distinct tradition is debated, and some would rather wait for further sites before adopting the name.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>",
"Australopithecus_afarensis": "<i>Australopithecus afarensis</i> is an extinct hominin species of eastern Africa, known from the remains of more than 300 individuals found in Ethiopia, Kenya and Tanzania.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> It walked upright on short legs, with a broad pelvis and an arched foot, yet kept long arms, curved fingers and a brain averaging about 446 cubic centimetres, close to a third of the modern human average; adults stood between roughly 1.1 and 1.5 metres (3 ft 7 in to 4 ft 11 in) tall.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The species was named in 1978 by Donald Johanson, Tim White and Yves Coppens after the Afar region of Ethiopia, and its best-known remains are the partial skeleton nicknamed Lucy and the many individuals recovered together at Hadar.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>",
"Kenyanthropus_platyops": "<i>Kenyanthropus platyops</i> is an extinct hominin known chiefly from KNM-WT 40000, a badly shattered but reconstructed skull from Lomekwi, west of Lake Turkana in northern Kenya.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Meave Leakey and her colleagues named it in 2001, taking the species name from Greek words for 'flat face', since the cheekbones sit far forward and the muzzle is unusually flat for so early a hominin, while the molars are small and the braincase ape-sized.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Whether it deserves a genus of its own is disputed: some researchers place it within <i>Australopithecus</i>, and others hold that the crushing and distortion of the skull are too severe to support a new species at all.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>",
"Hominini": "A hominin is any member of Hominini, the group within the family Hominidae containing modern humans and the extinct species on the human side of the split from the other African apes.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> In its most common use it takes in <i>Homo</i> along with earlier genera such as <i>Australopithecus</i>, <i>Paranthropus</i> and <i>Ardipithecus</i>, marked out above all by habitual upright walking, and of them all only <i>Homo sapiens</i> survives.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Where the group's boundary falls is a matter of classification rather than of fact, and the word is easily confused with hominid, which once carried almost the meaning hominin now has and today covers all the great apes.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>",
"Mosaic_evolution": "Mosaic evolution is the pattern in which the parts of an organism change at different rates and different times, so that a lineage can carry newly evolved features alongside others left much as they were in its ancestors.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> It follows from natural selection acting on particular structures rather than on the body as an indivisible whole, which is why fossils of transitional forms often combine traits once assigned to separate stages.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Palaeontologists invoke it for <i>Archaeopteryx</i>, feathered and winged yet toothed and long-tailed, and for early hominins whose hips and feet were remodelled for upright walking long before their brains grew large.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>",
"Raymond_Dart": "Raymond Dart was an Australian-born anatomist who spent his career in South Africa, heading the anatomy department at the University of the Witwatersrand.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> In 1925 he described the fossil skull of a child from the Taung lime works as a new species, <i>Australopithecus africanus</i>, arguing that it stood between apes and humans and that Africa, rather than Asia or Europe, was where humans had emerged.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> His claim met years of scepticism from established scholars, and although later discoveries bore out his reading of Africa's place in human origins, his further idea that these creatures hunted and made tools of bone, tooth and horn has not been sustained.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>",
"Taung_Child": "The Taung Child is the fossilized skull of a young <i>Australopithecus africanus</i>, from lime workings at Taung in South Africa, recovered in 1924.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> It preserves the face, much of the braincase and the jaws with milk teeth and first molars in place, putting the child's age at death at three or four years, and the limestone filling the braincase hardened into a natural cast of the brain's surface.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Raymond Dart's description of it in 1925 was the first announcement of an early hominin from Africa; puncture damage around the eye sockets has since suggested to some researchers that the child was killed by a large bird of prey.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>",
"Lucy_(Australopithecus)": "Lucy is a partial skeleton of <i>Australopithecus afarensis</i>, catalogued as AL 288-1, found in 1974 at Hadar in Ethiopia by a team led by Donald Johanson and Maurice Taieb.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Several hundred fragments make up one of the most complete early hominin skeletons known, and the shape of the hip and leg bones shows that this small-brained female, about 1.05 metres (3 ft 5 in) tall, walked upright.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> She was nicknamed after the Beatles song playing in camp that night and is also known as Dinkinesh, Amharic for you are marvellous; the bones are kept at the National Museum of Ethiopia.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>",
"Hadar,_Ethiopia": "Hadar is a cluster of fossil localities in the badlands of the Afar region of northeastern Ethiopia, where the Awash River cuts through lake and floodplain sediments laid down between about 3.45 and 2.95 million years ago.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Recognised in 1970 by the French geologist Maurice Taieb and excavated from 1973 by an international team, it has produced hundreds of <i>Australopithecus afarensis</i> fossils, among them the partial skeleton nicknamed Lucy and the remains of at least 13 individuals gathered at one spot, known as the First Family.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Its younger levels have also yielded an upper jaw of early <i>Homo</i> dated to about 2.35 million years ago along with simple stone tools.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>",
"Laetoli_footprints": "The Laetoli footprints are trails of hominin footprints preserved in hardened volcanic ash at Laetoli in northern Tanzania.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The best-known trail, uncovered in 1978 by Mary Leakey's team, runs some 27 metres (89 feet) and holds about 70 prints left by three individuals moving together, the third setting its feet in the largest one's tracks; the impressions show a big toe in line with the others and a heel-first stride.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> They are usually attributed to <i>Australopithecus afarensis</i>, whose bones occur in the same beds, and are the earliest direct evidence that upright walking was habitual, though prints nearby have been argued to belong to a second hominin with a cross-stepping gait.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>",
"Genus": "A genus, plural genera, is a rank in the classification of living and fossil organisms sitting above the species and below the family, grouping species judged closely related by descent.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Its name forms the first half of a species' two-part scientific name, must begin with a capital letter, and by the rules of zoological naming is set in a type face different from the surrounding text, usually italics, as in <i>Homo sapiens</i>.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Two-part names were popularized by Carl Linnaeus in the 18th century, and because a genus expresses a judgement about relationships rather than a measurable boundary, taxonomists regularly disagree and split or merge genera as evidence appears.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>",
"Anatomy": "Anatomy is the branch of biology concerned with the structure of living things: the shape, position and relationships of the bones, muscles, organs and tissues that make up a body.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> It is studied at every scale, from the gross anatomy visible to the naked eye and explored by dissection down to the microscopic anatomy of tissues and cells, and comparing the same structures across different species shows how bodies have been remodelled over the course of evolution.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> A specialist in the subject is called an anatomist, and because structure and function are so closely tied, the discipline underpins medicine, surgery, veterinary science and the study of fossil remains.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>",
"Johannesburg": "Johannesburg is South Africa's largest city and the capital of Gauteng province, spread across the Highveld plateau at about 1,750 m (5,740 feet) above sea level.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> It was founded in 1886 after gold was found on the Witwatersrand beneath it, and the rush turned a mining camp into the economic centre of the country, drawing workers from across southern Africa and giving rise to the migrant labour system and to segregated townships such as Soweto.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> It is none of South Africa's three national capitals, though the Constitutional Court sits at Constitution Hill, and the Cradle of Humankind, the richest source of hominin fossils anywhere, lies to the north-west.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>",
"Taung": "Taung is a town in the North West province of South Africa, towards the eastern edge of the Kalahari; its name means 'place of the lion', from the Setswana word <i>tau</i> and a chief who bore it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Quarrying for lime at the nearby Buxton works cut into ancient tufa and calcrete deposits, and in 1924 blasting there released the fossil skull of a young hominin, which Raymond Dart described the following year as the first known member of <i>Australopithecus africanus</i>.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The fossil deposits at the quarry were added to a UNESCO World Heritage Site in 2005, and the name of the town is now attached to the specimen itself.",
"Australopithecus_africanus": "<i>Australopithecus africanus</i> is an extinct hominin species of southern Africa, the first early hominin described from the continent.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> It walked upright on two legs and carried a brain averaging about 460 cc, a little larger than a chimpanzee's, with a lightly built face, small canines and thickly enamelled cheek teeth suited to a varied plant diet, while its long arms and curved finger bones suggest climbing still mattered.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Raymond Dart named the species in 1925 from a child's skull found at Taung, and further remains from Sterkfontein, Makapansgat and Gladysvale have kept it at the centre of debate over which australopith, if any, gave rise to <i>Homo</i>.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>",
"Africa": "Africa is the world's second-largest continent, about 30.3 million km² (11.7 million sq mi), holding some 1.5 billion people in 54 recognised states.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> It reaches well into both temperate zones, so its belts run from equatorial rainforest through savanna to the Sahara and the Kalahari, and the East African Rift splits it from the Red Sea to Mozambique, its basins rich in human fossils.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The oldest traces of the human lineage and of <i>Homo sapiens</i> come from it; it saw early states such as Egypt, Aksum, Mali and Great Zimbabwe, was drained of people by the slave trades, partitioned among European powers in the late 19th century, and largely independent again by the 1970s.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>",
"Afar_Region": "The Afar Region is a regional state of Ethiopia, a hot, arid lowland in the north-east named for the Afar people, many of whom herd camels, goats and cattle across it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> It lies in the Afar Triangle, where three arms of the Earth's crust are pulling apart, and takes in the Danakil Depression, a salt-crusted basin more than 100 m (330 feet) below sea level and among the hottest inhabited places on Earth.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The same rifting steadily exposes layer upon layer of ash-dated sediment, and localities such as Hadar and the Middle Awash have yielded some of the most important early hominin fossils known, including the skeleton nicknamed Lucy.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>",
"Awash_River": "The Awash is a river of about 1,250 km (777 miles) in Ethiopia, rising west of Addis Ababa and running north-east down the Great Rift Valley into the Afar lowlands.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> It reaches no sea: its basin is endorheic, and the river ends in saline lakes near the Djibouti border, losing much of its water to evaporation and to the irrigation that makes it the most heavily used river in the country.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Its valley cuts through thick sediments interleaved with datable volcanic ash, exposing an exceptional record of hominin fossils and early stone tools; the Lower Valley of the Awash became a UNESCO World Heritage Site in 1980.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>",
"Ardipithecus_kadabba": "<i>Ardipithecus kadabba</i> is an extinct hominin species from the Middle Awash of Ethiopia, close to the time the human and chimpanzee lineages are thought to have separated.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> It is known from a modest set of fossils, mostly teeth and jaw fragments; the teeth still partly hone the upper canine against the lower premolar, as an ape's do, and a single toe bone has been read as a sign of an upright stride.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Yohannes Haile-Selassie published the material in 2001 and, with Gen Suwa and Tim White, raised it to a species in 2004; the small sample leaves its status and its position in the human lineage uncertain.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>",
"Ardipithecus_ramidus": "<i>Ardipithecus ramidus</i> is an extinct hominin species from Ethiopia, described in 1994 by Tim White, Gen Suwa and Berhane Asfaw from fossils at Aramis and given its own genus the year after.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> More than 100 specimens are known, among them a partial female skeleton nicknamed Ardi announced in 2009, together showing a brain of roughly 300 to 350 cc, canines reduced towards the human pattern, a pelvis suggesting upright walking, and a grasping big toe for climbing.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Its anatomy has been used to argue that the last common ancestor of humans and chimpanzees was not chimpanzee-like, a reading other researchers dispute, as they do its reconstructed wooded habitat.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>",
"KNM-WT_40000": "KNM-WT 40000 is a fossil hominin cranium found in 1999 by Justus Erus, of a team led by Meave Leakey, at Lomekwi west of Lake Turkana in northern Kenya.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The skull is mostly complete but came out in two pieces and considerably distorted; its striking features are a tall, flat face with high cheekbones and unusually small, thickly enamelled teeth, which the team judged enough to warrant a new genus and species, <i>Kenyanthropus platyops</i>, in 2001.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Other researchers have argued that the distortion makes the shape of the face unreliable and that the specimen may belong within <i>Australopithecus</i>, so its identity remains contested.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>",
"Quaternary": "The Quaternary is the most recent period of geological time, made up of the Pleistocene and Holocene epochs.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> It is defined by the repeated growth and collapse of great ice sheets: dozens of glacial and interglacial swings, paced by slow changes in the Earth's orbit, which moved sea level up and down by more than 100 m (330 feet) and redrew coastlines, rivers and the ranges of plants and animals.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Its base was fixed in 2009 at a rock section at Monte San Nicola in Sicily, and the period covers the whole span in which the genus <i>Homo</i> spread out of Africa and across the world.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>",
"Antarctica": "Antarctica is the southernmost continent, about 14 million km² (5.4 million sq mi) centred on the South Pole under an ice sheet averaging close to 1.9 km (1.2 miles) thick.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> It is the coldest and driest of the continents — the lowest air temperature ever measured on Earth, −89.2 °C (−129 °F), was recorded at Vostok Station in 1983 — and so little snow falls inland that it counts as a desert, yet its ice holds some 60% of the world's fresh water.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Nobody lives on it permanently and no state governs it: the Antarctic Treaty, signed in 1959, sets the territorial claims aside, reserves the continent for peaceful purposes and keeps research free and open.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>",
"Greenland": "Greenland is the world's largest island, about 2.2 million km² (850,000 sq mi) between the Arctic and the North Atlantic, some 81% of it under an ice sheet over 3 km (1.9 miles) thick.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> About 57,000 people live there, most of them Inuit and nearly all in coastal towns on the ice-free southwest, the capital Nuuk the largest; fishing is its main trade.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Norse farmers brought agriculture to the southern fjords in the late 10th century and had left by the early 15th, Denmark colonised it in 1721, and the island won home rule in 1979 and self-government in 2009, staying in the Kingdom of Denmark after leaving the European Economic Community in 1985.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>",
"Holocene": "The Holocene is the current epoch of geological time, beginning as the last glacial phase ended, when the ice sheets drew back, sea level rose and forests spread over former tundra.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Its climate has been mild and unusually steady by the standards of the preceding two million years, and within it people took up farming independently in at least eleven parts of the world, built the first cities and states, and grew from a few million to billions.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> Geologists divided it formally in 2018 into three ages — the Greenlandian, the Northgrippian and the Meghalayan — fixed in Greenland ice cores and a stalagmite from a cave in north-eastern India.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup>",
"Cryogenian": "The Cryogenian is a period of geological time within the Neoproterozoic, long before animals with hard skeletons appear in the rocks.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> It is named for the most severe glaciations known in Earth's history, the Sturtian and the Marinoan, when glacial deposits formed even at tropical latitudes and ice may have reached almost around the planet, a state often called Snowball Earth.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Each freeze is thought to have ended as volcanic carbon dioxide built up in an atmosphere from which ice had shut off the usual weathering, and the thaws left distinctive carbonate layers, followed within tens of millions of years by the first unmistakable animal fossils.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>",
"Equator": "The equator is the imaginary circle drawn around the middle of the Earth, halfway between the poles, splitting the globe into a northern and a southern half and marking the zero point from which latitude is measured. It is the planet's longest line of latitude, about 40,075 km (24,901 miles) around, and because the Earth bulges outward there, ground on it stands some 21 km (13 miles) farther from the centre than the poles do.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Along it the Sun passes almost directly overhead twice a year, day and night stay close to 12 hours long all year round, and the line crosses 13 countries, in 11 of them over land.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>",
"Milankovitch_cycles": "Milankovitch cycles are slow, regular changes in the Earth's orbit and axial tilt that alter how sunlight falls over the planet through the year.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Three matter most: the orbit stretches and rounds over roughly 100,000 years, the axial tilt rocks between about 22.1 and 24.5 degrees every 41,000 years, and the axis wobbles so the seasons shift around the orbit over some 19,000 to 23,000 years.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Milutin Milanković argued that they pace the ice sheets, calculating their effect on summer sunlight at 65 degrees north; deep-sea cores found those rhythms in 1976, and the theory is now central to explaining the ice ages, though not their 100,000-year swings.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>",
"Sicily": "Sicily is the largest island in the Mediterranean, about 25,700 km² (9,920 sq mi) off the toe of Italy across the Strait of Messina, with about 4.8 million inhabitants and Palermo as its capital.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Most of it is hilly and dry, farmed for wheat, olives, citrus and vines, and its east is dominated by Mount Etna, the most active volcano in Europe, whose recorded eruptions reach back to 1500 BCE.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Greeks and Phoenicians settled its coasts from the 8th century BCE, Carthage and Rome fought over it until it became Rome's first province, and Byzantines, Arabs, Normans and Spanish kings ruled it in turn before it joined the Italian kingdom in 1860.",
"Northern_Hemisphere": "The Northern Hemisphere is the half of the Earth lying north of the equator, taking in all of Europe and North America, most of Asia and Africa and the northern part of South America. Land makes up about 39% of its surface, a high proportion for the planet, and roughly 87% of the world's people live there; the Arctic Ocean sits at its pole, ringed by tundra and boreal forest. Summer falls around June and winter around December, and the Coriolis effect deflects moving air and water to the right, so tropical cyclones spin anticlockwise while the great ocean gyres turn clockwise.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>",
"Southern_Hemisphere": "The Southern Hemisphere is the half of the Earth south of the equator: Antarctica and Australia, most of South America, about a third of Africa and wide oceans. Only about 19% of its surface is land and roughly an eighth of the world's people live there, so ocean governs its weather and narrows its temperature range; an unbroken belt of water around 60° south carries the planet's strongest winds. Summer falls around December and winter around June, the Coriolis effect deflects air and water to the left, so tropical cyclones spin clockwise and the gyres anticlockwise; the Southern Cross orients its night sky, no bright star marks the pole.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>",
"George_Washington": "George Washington was an American planter and soldier who commanded the Continental Army in the Revolutionary War and became the first president of the United States, serving from 1789 to 1797.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> He presided over the convention that drafted the Constitution in 1787 and, as president, shaped the practical workings of the new executive office, from the cabinet to the conduct of foreign policy.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> His decision to step down after two terms set a precedent that held for nearly a century and a half, and he remained the enslaver of hundreds of people until his death.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>",
"John_Adams": "John Adams was a Massachusetts lawyer and political writer who argued for American independence, served as the first vice president, and was the second president of the United States from 1797 to 1801.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> His single term was dominated by an undeclared naval conflict with France, which he settled by negotiation rather than full war, and by the Alien and Sedition Acts, which restricted immigrants and punished criticism of the government.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> He was the first occupant of the White House and spent a long retirement writing letters, including a famous correspondence with Thomas Jefferson.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>",
"Thomas_Jefferson": "Thomas Jefferson was an American statesman and the principal author of the Declaration of Independence, who served as the third president of the United States from 1801 to 1809.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> His presidency roughly doubled the territory of the country through the Louisiana Purchase of 1803 and sent the Lewis and Clark expedition to the Pacific, while his second term was consumed by an embargo intended to keep the young republic out of the European wars.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> A prolific writer, architect and founder of the University of Virginia, he argued as a young politician for prohibiting slavery in new American territories, and never freed his own slaves.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>",
"James_Madison": "James Madison was an American political thinker and the fourth president of the United States, in office from 1809 to 1817.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> He was the leading architect of the Constitution of 1787, a principal author of the Federalist essays written to win its ratification, and the drafter of the amendments that became the Bill of Rights.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> As president he led the country through the War of 1812 against Britain, a conflict in which British troops burned the Capitol and the president's house in 1814 and which ended with no change of territory.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>",
"James_Monroe": "James Monroe was an American statesman and the fifth president of the United States, serving from 1817 to 1825.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> A veteran of the Revolutionary War who had been a diplomat and secretary of state, he governed during a stretch of one-party dominance known as the Era of Good Feelings, broken by the financial panic of 1819 and by the fierce dispute over slavery that produced the Missouri Compromise of 1820.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> His annual message of 1823 set out what later became known as the Monroe Doctrine, warning European powers against new colonization in the Americas.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>",
"John_Quincy_Adams": "John Quincy Adams was an American diplomat and the sixth president of the United States, in office from 1825 to 1829.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> As secretary of state he negotiated the treaty that transferred Florida from Spain and drafted much of the substance of the Monroe Doctrine, and he reached the presidency in 1825 when the House of Representatives chose him after no candidate won a majority of electoral votes.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Defeated after one term, he then spent seventeen years in the House of Representatives, attacking the rule that tabled antislavery petitions and arguing the <i>Amistad</i> case before the Supreme Court.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>",
"Andrew_Jackson": "Andrew Jackson was an American general and the seventh president of the United States, in office from 1829 to 1837.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Famous for defeating a British army at New Orleans in 1815, he built the coalition that became the Democratic Party and championed a politics of the common white man, expanding the reach of the presidency in a long fight against the Second Bank of the United States and in a confrontation with South Carolina over nullification.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> He also signed the Indian Removal Act of 1830, under which tens of thousands of Native Americans were forced west on marches remembered as the Trail of Tears.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>",
"Martin_Van_Buren": "Martin Van Buren was an American politician and the eighth president of the United States, in office from 1837 to 1841.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> A New York party organizer of rare skill, he helped invent the modern national party machine and served as secretary of state and vice president before winning the presidency.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> His single term was consumed by the Panic of 1837 and the long depression that followed, and he lost his campaign for re-election; in 1848 he ran again as the candidate of the Free Soil Party, which opposed extending slavery into the western territories.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>",
"William_Henry_Harrison": "William Henry Harrison was an American army officer and the ninth president of the United States, whose term in 1841 lasted only 31 days, the shortest in the country's history.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> He had made his reputation as governor of Indiana Territory and as the commander who defeated Tecumseh's confederacy at Tippecanoe in 1811 and a combined British and Native American force at the Thames in 1813, and he won the presidency in a boisterous campaign built on that military fame.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> He fell ill soon after his inauguration and died in office, becoming the first president to do so.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>",
"John_Tyler": "John Tyler was an American politician and the tenth president of the United States, serving from 1841 to 1845.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> He was the first vice president raised to the office by a president's death, and by insisting that he was president in full rather than a caretaker he set a precedent later written into the Constitution.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> A states' rights Virginian who vetoed his own party's banking bills and was expelled from it, he secured the annexation of Texas in 1845 and, at the end of his life, sided with the Confederacy.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>",
"James_K._Polk": "James K. Polk was an American politician and the eleventh president of the United States, in office from 1845 to 1849.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> A Tennessee protégé of Jacksonian politics and former Speaker of the House, he entered office pledged to a single term and to a programme of expansion, and he carried it out: a treaty with Britain in 1846 fixed the Oregon boundary, while the war with Mexico from 1846 to 1848 brought California and the Southwest into the United States.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The territory he added reopened the question of whether slavery would spread west, a dispute that dominated the following decade.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>",
"Zachary_Taylor": "Zachary Taylor was an American career soldier and the twelfth president of the United States, in office from 1849 until his death in 1850.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> He spent four decades in the army, commanded on the frontier, and became a national figure through victories in the war with Mexico, notably at Buena Vista in 1847, after which he was elected president with almost no political experience.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Although a Louisiana slaveholder, he resisted extending slavery into the newly won western territories, and he died suddenly in July 1850 while that quarrel was still unsettled.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>",
"Millard_Fillmore": "Millard Fillmore was an American politician and the thirteenth president of the United States, in office from 1850 to 1853 after rising from the vice presidency on the death of the sitting president.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> He signed the Compromise of 1850, a package of laws that admitted California as a free state and organised the western territories, but which also included a harsh Fugitive Slave Act that obliged citizens across the country to assist in the capture of escaped slaves.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Denied his party's nomination in 1852, he ran again in 1856 as the candidate of the anti-immigrant Know Nothing movement and carried only one state.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>",
"Franklin_Pierce": "Franklin Pierce was an American politician and the fourteenth president of the United States, in office from 1853 to 1857.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> A New Hampshire Democrat chosen as a compromise candidate, he sought to quiet the argument over slavery by enforcing existing law and looking abroad for expansion, buying a strip of land from Mexico in the Gadsden Purchase.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Instead his support for the Kansas–Nebraska Act of 1854, which let settlers decide the question for themselves, unleashed violent struggle in Kansas and split his party so badly that it refused to renominate him.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>",
"James_Buchanan": "James Buchanan was an American diplomat and politician who served as the fifteenth president of the United States from 1857 to 1861.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> He came to the office with a long record in Congress, as minister to Russia and Britain and as secretary of state, and he believed the quarrel over slavery could be settled by the courts and by admitting Kansas under a proslavery constitution.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> During the winter after the election of 1860 seven southern states declared their secession while he was still in office, and he left the crisis unresolved for his successor; he was also the only president who never married.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>",
"Abraham_Lincoln": "Abraham Lincoln was an American lawyer and the sixteenth president of the United States, in office from 1861 until his assassination in 1865.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Born in a Kentucky log cabin and largely self-taught, he was elected on a pledge to halt the spread of slavery, and his election prompted seven southern states to secede and form the Confederacy, with four more joining once the fighting began.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> He led the Union through four years of fighting, issued the Emancipation Proclamation in 1863, defined the war's purpose in the Gettysburg Address, and pushed through the amendment abolishing slavery before being shot in Washington in April 1865.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>",
"Andrew_Johnson": "Andrew Johnson was an American politician and the seventeenth president of the United States, in office from 1865 to 1869 after taking over on the assassination of the sitting president.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> A Tennessee tailor turned senator, he was the only southern senator to stay loyal to the Union in 1861, and as president he offered the defeated Confederate states lenient terms while resisting measures to protect the rights of freed people.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> His fights with Congress led the House of Representatives to impeach him in 1868, and the Senate acquitted him by a single vote; his administration also purchased Alaska from Russia in 1867.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>",
"Ulysses_S._Grant": "Ulysses S. Grant was an American general and the eighteenth president of the United States, in office from 1869 to 1877.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> As commanding general of the Union armies he pressed the campaigns that ended the Civil War and received the surrender of the main Confederate army at Appomattox in April 1865.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> As president he supported the amendment guaranteeing Black men the vote, used federal force to break the Ku Klux Klan, and pursued a peaceful settlement with Britain, while his two terms were also marked by corruption scandals among his appointees and by the depression that followed the panic of 1873.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>",
"Rutherford_B._Hayes": "Rutherford B. Hayes was an American lawyer and soldier who served as the nineteenth president of the United States from 1877 to 1881.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> A wounded Civil War officer and governor of Ohio, he took office only after the disputed election of 1876 was settled by a special electoral commission, and whether a bargain with southern politicians went with it is still argued over.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> He then ordered the last federal troops protecting Reconstruction governments back to their barracks, worked for a professional civil service against the wishes of party bosses, and kept a promise to serve only one term.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>",
"James_A._Garfield": "James A. Garfield was an American politician and the twentieth president of the United States, whose term in 1881 lasted only about six months.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> A college teacher, Civil War general and long-serving congressman from Ohio, he was nominated unexpectedly at a deadlocked convention and won a very narrow popular majority.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> He was shot in a Washington railway station in July 1881 by a disappointed office-seeker and died in September of infection from his wounds, and public anger at the killing helped push Congress to reform the awarding of government jobs.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>",
"Chester_A._Arthur": "Chester A. Arthur was an American lawyer and party official who served as the twenty-first president of the United States from 1881 to 1885, having become president when his predecessor was assassinated.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> He had risen through the New York political machine as collector of the port's customs house, and few expected reform from him, yet he signed the Pendleton Act of 1883, which created a merit examination system for federal jobs.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> His term also brought a modernised steel navy and the Chinese Exclusion Act of 1882, which barred Chinese labourers from immigrating, and ill health and party hostility kept him from a second nomination.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>",
"Grover_Cleveland": "Grover Cleveland was an American politician who served as the twenty-second and twenty-fourth president of the United States, from 1885 to 1889 and again from 1893 to 1897.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> A former mayor of Buffalo and governor of New York, he built his reputation on honest administration and used the veto more than any earlier president, opposing what he saw as favours to private interests and defending the gold standard and low tariffs.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> His second term opened with the financial panic of 1893 and the deep depression that followed, during which he sent federal troops to break the Pullman railway strike of 1894, a decision that divided his own party.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>",
"Benjamin_Harrison": "Benjamin Harrison was an American lawyer and the twenty-third president of the United States, in office from 1889 to 1893.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> An Indiana senator and Civil War officer, and the grandson of the ninth president, he won the presidency in 1888 with a majority of electoral votes although he trailed in the popular count.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> His single term saw the first federal law against business monopolies, a steep protective tariff, a large expansion of pensions for Union veterans, the admission of six new western states and the first Pan-American Conference; he lost the rematch with his predecessor in 1892.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>",
"William_McKinley": "William McKinley was an American politician and the twenty-fifth president of the United States, in office from 1897 until his assassination in 1901.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> An Ohio congressman known for high protective tariffs, he won office promising prosperity and put the currency firmly on a gold basis.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> In 1898 he led the country into war with Spain, a brief conflict that ended Spanish rule in Cuba and gave the United States the Philippines, Puerto Rico and Guam, while Hawaii was annexed the same year; six months into his second term he was shot by an anarchist in Buffalo and died eight days later.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>",
"Theodore_Roosevelt": "Theodore Roosevelt was an American politician, writer and naturalist who served as the twenty-sixth president of the United States from 1901 to 1909, taking office at 42 as the youngest person ever to hold it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> He used the presidency to break up large industrial combinations, regulate railways and food, and set aside vast tracts of forest and land as public reserves, while abroad he expanded the navy and secured the route for the Panama Canal.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> He won the Nobel Peace Prize in 1906 for mediating an end to the war between Russia and Japan, and ran again in 1912 on a third-party ticket after breaking with his successor.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>",
"William_Howard_Taft": "William Howard Taft was an American lawyer and judge, the twenty-seventh president of the United States and later chief justice of the Supreme Court from 1921 to 1930, the only person to hold both offices.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Before the presidency he had governed the Philippines and served as secretary of war, and in office he brought more antitrust suits than his predecessor, pressed for a presidential budget that Congress refused him, and encouraged investment abroad in a policy nicknamed dollar diplomacy.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> A quarrel over tariffs and conservation split his party in 1912 and he finished third, though he thought his years on the court the happiest of his career.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>",
"Woodrow_Wilson": "Woodrow Wilson was an American scholar and politician who served as the twenty-eighth president of the United States, having been a professor and president of Princeton University and governor of New Jersey.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> His first term produced the Federal Reserve system, a lower tariff, a federal income tax and new laws on trade and labour, and extended racial segregation within the federal service.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> He kept the country out of the First World War until 1917, then led it in, set out war aims in his Fourteen Points, and campaigned for the League of Nations, which the Senate refused to join after a stroke left him gravely weakened in 1919.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>",
"Warren_G._Harding": "Warren G. Harding was an American newspaper publisher and politician who served as the twenty-ninth president of the United States.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> An Ohio senator with an easy manner, he won a landslide by promising a return to normal life after war and upheaval, then cut taxes and spending, raised tariffs, put federal spending into a single presidential budget for the first time and hosted a naval conference in Washington that limited the great powers' fleets.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> He died suddenly in San Francisco in August 1923, and investigations soon exposed the Teapot Dome affair, in which his interior secretary had taken bribes for oil leases on public land.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>",
"Calvin_Coolidge": "Calvin Coolidge was an American politician and the thirtieth president of the United States, who succeeded to the office on the death of the sitting president.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> A Vermont-born lawyer who became governor of Massachusetts, he was famous for his silence and thrift, and he governed on the belief that the government should tax and spend as little as possible while business ran the economy.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> His years coincided with the prosperity of the 1920s and included the immigration law of 1924, which sharply restricted arrivals from southern and eastern Europe, and a law granting citizenship to Native Americans; he chose not to run again in 1928.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>",
"Herbert_Hoover": "Herbert Hoover was an American mining engineer and administrator who served as the thirty-first president of the United States.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> He had made a fortune in mining and won worldwide respect for organising food relief in Europe during and after the First World War, then served as secretary of commerce before winning the presidency by a wide margin.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Seven months into his term the stock market collapsed, and the Great Depression deepened through his presidency; he favoured voluntary cooperation and loans to banks and states over direct federal relief, signed a steep tariff in 1930 that worsened world trade, and lost the election of 1932 in a landslide.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>",
"Franklin_D._Roosevelt": "Franklin D. Roosevelt was an American politician and the thirty-second president of the United States, the only person elected four times.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> Stricken in 1921 by a paralytic illness that left him unable to walk unaided, he entered the presidency in the depths of the Great Depression and answered it with the New Deal, a broad programme of relief, public works, banking regulation and social insurance that reshaped the government's role.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> He then led the country through most of the Second World War and built the alliance against Germany and Japan, dying in April 1945 weeks before the fighting in Europe ended; a constitutional amendment soon limited presidents to two terms.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup>",
"Harry_S._Truman": "Harry S. Truman was an American politician and the thirty-third president of the United States, who succeeded on the death of his predecessor.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> A Missouri farmer and shopkeeper who rose through the Senate, he took over months before the end of the Second World War, ordered the atomic bombings of Hiroshima and Nagasaki in August 1945, and saw the United Nations founded.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> He then shaped the early Cold War with aid to Greece and Turkey, the Marshall Plan for European recovery, the Berlin airlift and the creation of NATO, ordered the desegregation of the armed forces in 1948, and sent American troops to fight in Korea from 1950.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>",
"Dwight_D._Eisenhower": "Dwight D. Eisenhower was an American general and the thirty-fourth president of the United States.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> As Supreme Commander of the Allied Expeditionary Force he planned and directed the invasion of Normandy in June 1944 and the campaign that defeated Germany, and was sought as a candidate by both parties before running as a Republican.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> As president he ended the fighting in Korea by armistice in 1953, cut conventional forces while building up nuclear weapons to deter the Soviet Union, launched the interstate highway system in 1956, sent federal troops to enforce school desegregation at Little Rock in 1957, and warned in his farewell address of a growing military-industrial complex.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup>",
"John_F._Kennedy": "John F. Kennedy was an American politician and the thirty-fifth president of the United States, killed in office in 1963.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> A decorated naval officer in the Pacific war and senator from Massachusetts, he was the youngest man elected to the office and the first Catholic to hold it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> His short presidency included the failed invasion of Cuba at the Bay of Pigs, the confrontation over Soviet missiles there in October 1962 that came close to nuclear war, the first nuclear test-ban treaty, the pledge to land astronauts on the Moon and a civil rights bill sent to Congress; he was shot dead in Dallas in November 1963.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup>",
"Lyndon_B._Johnson": "Lyndon B. Johnson was an American politician and the thirty-sixth president of the United States, taking over on the assassination of his predecessor.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> A Texas schoolteacher turned congressman, he had been an unusually powerful Senate majority leader, and used that mastery to pass the Civil Rights Act of 1964 and the Voting Rights Act of 1965, which dismantled legal segregation and protected Black voters, along with the Great Society programmes that created Medicare and Medicaid and expanded school aid.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> At the same time he greatly escalated the war in Vietnam, and the division at home led him to announce in March 1968 that he would not seek another term.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>",
"Richard_Nixon": "Richard Nixon was an American politician and the thirty-seventh president of the United States, the only one to resign the office.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> A California congressman and senator who had served eight years as vice president, he entered the White House in 1969 with ending the war in Vietnam as his most pressing task; American forces left in 1973 under a negotiated agreement.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> His foreign policy reopened relations with China in 1972 and produced the first arms-limitation treaties with the Soviet Union, while at home his administration created the Environmental Protection Agency; the Watergate break-in and the cover-up that followed forced him to resign in August 1974 to avoid near-certain removal.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup>",
"Gerald_Ford": "Gerald Ford was an American politician and the thirty-eighth president of the United States, the only person to hold the office without having been elected either president or vice president.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> A long-serving Michigan congressman and House minority leader, he was appointed vice president in 1973 under the constitutional provision for filling a vacancy, and became president when his predecessor resigned.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> He granted that predecessor a full pardon a month later, a decision that cost him public support, and his short term was marked by high inflation and recession, the Helsinki Accords of 1975 and the fall of Saigon that same year; he narrowly lost the election of 1976.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup>",
"Jimmy_Carter": "Jimmy Carter was the thirty-ninth president of the United States.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> A naval officer turned peanut farmer and governor of Georgia, he ran as an outsider promising honest government and made human rights an explicit part of foreign policy.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> His achievements included the Camp David Accords of 1978, leading to a peace treaty between Egypt and Israel, treaties transferring the Panama Canal, new energy and education departments and the protection of much of Alaska, while double-digit inflation and the seizure of American hostages in Iran cost him the 1980 election; in a long retirement he monitored elections, fought disease and built houses, receiving the Nobel Peace Prize in 2002.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup>",
"Ronald_Reagan": "Ronald Reagan was an American film actor and politician, the fortieth president of the United States, having been governor of California and, at 69, the oldest person yet elected.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> He cut income taxes sharply, pressed to roll back federal regulation and backed tight money to break double-digit inflation, presiding over a deep recession, a long expansion and large federal deficits.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Abroad he greatly increased military spending and confronted the Soviet Union, then negotiated with Mikhail Gorbachev and signed the 1987 treaty eliminating intermediate-range nuclear missiles; his second term was shadowed by the Iran-Contra affair, in which officials secretly sold arms to Iran and sent the proceeds to Nicaraguan rebels.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>",
"George_H._W._Bush": "George H. W. Bush was an American politician, the forty-first president of the United States.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> A navy pilot shot down in the Pacific war, he was in turn congressman, ambassador to the United Nations, director of central intelligence and vice president before winning the presidency.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> His term spanned the end of the Cold War: the Berlin Wall fell, Germany reunified with his support and the Soviet Union dissolved, while he assembled the coalition that drove Iraq out of Kuwait in 1991; at home he signed a landmark disability rights law and, facing large deficits, accepted a tax increase that angered his party, losing the 1992 election amid a weak economy.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup>",
"Bill_Clinton": "Bill Clinton is an American politician, the forty-second president of the United States.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> A former governor of Arkansas, he presided over a long expansion in which the budget moved from deficit into surplus, and his record includes the North American Free Trade Agreement, a higher minimum wage, and a 1996 law replacing federal cash welfare with block grants to the states.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Abroad he sent forces to Haiti and the Balkans and brokered the 1995 agreement that ended the war in Bosnia; in 1998 the House impeached him over his conduct in a lawsuit and an investigation, the Senate acquitted him in 1999 and he served out his term.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>",
"George_W._Bush": "George W. Bush is the forty-third president of the United States.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> A former governor of Texas and son of the forty-first president, he won the very close election of 2000 after a disputed Florida count ended by the Supreme Court.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> His presidency was defined by the attacks of 11 September 2001, after which he launched a war in Afghanistan, created a homeland security department and broad surveillance powers, and invaded Iraq in 2003 on weapons claims that proved unfounded; he also cut taxes, expanded school testing and elderly drug coverage, and began a large AIDS programme in Africa, and his final months were dominated by the financial crisis of 2008.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>",
"Barack_Obama": "Barack Obama is the forty-fourth president of the United States, the first Black person elected to the office.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Born in Hawaii to a Kenyan father and an American mother, he taught law at the University of Chicago and sat in the Illinois legislature and the Senate before winning the presidency during the financial crisis.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> He answered it with a large stimulus and a car-industry rescue, and signed the Affordable Care Act of 2010, extending health insurance to millions; abroad he ordered the killing of Osama bin Laden in 2011 and joined the Iran nuclear agreement and the Paris climate accord; he received the Nobel Peace Prize in 2009.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup>",
"Donald_Trump": "Donald Trump is the forty-fifth and, from 2025, the forty-seventh president of the United States, the first since the 19th century to win non-consecutive terms.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> A property developer and television personality with no previous public office, he won in 2016 promising to restrict immigration, cut taxes and repeal the Affordable Care Act, and his first term brought a large tax cut, three Supreme Court appointments and accords between Israel and several Arab states.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> He was impeached twice by the House, in 2019 over dealings with Ukraine and in 2021 over the Capitol attack, and acquitted both times; he lost in 2020, disputed the result and won again in 2024.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>",
"Joe_Biden": "Joe Biden is an American politician, the forty-sixth president of the United States and the oldest ever to take office.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> He represented Delaware in the Senate for 36 years, chaired its judiciary and foreign relations committees, and served two terms as vice president before winning the presidency in 2020.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> In office he oversaw a mass vaccination campaign against COVID-19, signed a large relief package, a bipartisan infrastructure law, a semiconductor act and a 2022 law directing major spending to clean energy, and organised Western support for Ukraine after the Russian invasion; he completed the withdrawal from Afghanistan in 2021, and in July 2024 ended his campaign for a second term.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>",
"Middle_Stone_Age": "The Middle Stone Age is the middle division of the African Stone Age, its boundaries gradual and shifting regionally.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Its assemblages are marked by prepared cores, points and blades, the knapper shaping a block so a flake of planned size and edge came away in one blow; some of the earliest dated examples, about 315,000 years old, lay with the oldest known <i>Homo sapiens</i> fossils at Jebel Irhoud.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The name is John Goodwin's, from a paper of 1928, elaborated the next year in <i>The Stone Age Cultures of South Africa</i> with Clarence van Riet Lowe, setting out an Earlier, a Middle and a Late Stone Age for Africa.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>",
"Skhul_Cave": "Skhul, in full Mugharet es-Skhul, is a small cave and rockshelter on the slope of Mount Carmel in northern Israel, standing in the Wadi el-Mughara or Valley of the Caves, a short way inland from the Mediterranean.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The American student Theodore McCown dug it in 1931 and 1932, mostly the ground in front of the shelter rather than the cave itself, as part of the seven seasons at the Mount Carmel caves that Dorothy Garrod led between 1929 and 1934.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Its Middle Palaeolithic deposits yielded early <i>Homo sapiens</i> remains, of which at least four and possibly seven are read as deliberate burials, one skeleton apparently clasping a wild boar's jaw.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>",
"Qafzeh_Cave": "Qafzeh is a cave on the slope of Har Qedumim, also called Jebel Qafzeh, in Lower Galilee in northern Israel, above the bank of the Nahal Mizra creek at the edge of the Jezreel Valley.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> It was first excavated by René Neuville and Moshe Stekelis between 1933 and 1935, and dug again by Bernard Vandermeersch from 1965 to 1979.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Its Middle Palaeolithic layers, dated to roughly 100,000 to 90,000 years ago, have produced the largest collection of hominin remains of that period in south-western Asia, 27 individuals in all, along with purposeful human burials, hearths, ochre and marine shells.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>",
"Skhul_and_Qafzeh_hominins": "The Skhul and Qafzeh hominins are human fossils from two caves in northern Israel, Skhul on Mount Carmel and Qafzeh in Lower Galilee, read as marking an early expansion of <i>Homo sapiens</i> out of Africa.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The Skhul individuals are anatomically somewhat more primitive than those from Qafzeh, though the preserved faces of both are short and flat like our own.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Several of the dead were buried on purpose, one Skhul skeleton with a wild boar's jaw seemingly clasped in its arms and an adolescent at Qafzeh laid in a pit with deer antlers on the chest; both caves yielded perforated marine shells worn from hanging on a string, and ochre.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>",
"Interglacial": "An interglacial is a warm phase of a glacial cycle, an interval of low land-ice extent and correspondingly high sea level.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> On a sea-level definition eleven have been identified in the last 800,000 years, each typically lasting 10,000 to 30,000 years, and their onsets involve rapid, non-linear responses of ice volume, carbon dioxide and temperature to astronomical forcing, while their ends are slower, a global sequence of changes rather than a single switch.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Alternations of this kind first appeared about 2.6 million years ago at the start of the Quaternary, and the present interglacial is the Holocene, which began about 11,700 years ago.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>",
"Late_Stone_Age": "The Late Stone Age is the last of the three divisions of the African Stone Age, running from about 40,000 years ago into historical times.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Its earliest phase is generally informal, combining prepared cores with bipolar technique and microlithic work, and fully microlithic industries such as the Wilton appear only much later, between about 8,000 and 4,000 years ago.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The three-stage scheme it belongs to was laid out by John Goodwin and Clarence van Riet Lowe in 1929, and the label is applied well beyond southern Africa, reaching the hunter-gatherers of Taforalt in Morocco between 15,000 and 13,000 years ago.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>",
"Denisovan": "Denisovans are an extinct group of archaic humans named after Denisova Cave in southern Siberia, where the finger bone of a juvenile was excavated in 2008 and its genome sequenced.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The genome showed a population sharing a common origin with Neanderthals, from whom they separated more than 390,000 years ago, and the two groups are known to have met: one girl whose remains come from the same cave had a Neanderthal mother and a Denisovan father.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> They contributed some 4 to 6 percent of the genetic material of present-day Melanesians, which suggests the group may once have been widespread in Asia.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>",
"Ochre": "Ochre is a blanket term for a variety of rocks characterised by a red or yellow colour or streak and containing a high proportion of iron oxides, taking in everything from soil lumps to ore minerals and from shale and siltstone to hardened clay and weathered dolerite.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Yellow ochres usually derive their colour from goethite and red ochres from haematite, and both often contain other components such as quartz, clays, gypsum or mica.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Archaeologically it survives as pieces modified by grinding, flaking, scraping and pitting to produce a red powder, and it was also an ingredient of compound adhesives, mixed with plant gum, used to haft stone tools.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>",
"Blombos_Cave": "Blombos Cave is a South African site set in an ancient wave-cut cliff of the Bredasdorp Group, a short way above the Indian Ocean on the southern Cape coast.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> First excavated in 1991, its Middle Stone Age sequence falls into three phases, the earliest dated by optically stimulated luminescence and uranium-thorium to between 101,000 and 94,000 years ago, with Still Bay layers at about 78,000 to 71,000.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Among its finds are 41 pierced marine tick shells covered in red ochre, two engraved ochres, and an abalone shell used to mix a compound of ochre, bone, charcoal and quartz, with a cobble fitted into it as grinder.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>",
"Tar": "Tar is an adhesive synthesised by the dry, or destructive, distillation of organic material, commonly birch bark or pine wood.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Making it needs no pottery: experiments have produced usable quantities by heating rolled bark under a mound of embers and ash, in a pit, or inside a raised earthen structure, at temperatures ranging from about 250 °C (480 °F) to over 500 °C (930 °F).<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> It is one of the earliest transformative technologies known, and the oldest evidence for it consists of two unretouched flakes partly covered in birch bark tar from Campitello in Italy, made at a time when only Neanderthals were present in Europe.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>",
"Eurasia": "Eurasia is the landmass made up of Europe and Asia, and one of the six commonly recognised geological continents, along with Africa, North America, South America, Antarctica and Australia.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Those continents are spatially isolated by geologic or bathymetric features, and tectonic plate boundaries provide the basis for continent-continent boundaries such as the one between Africa and Eurasia, so the customary line drawn along the Ural Mountains is a boundary of convention rather than of geology.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Hominins reached it early: cut-marked bones from Grăunceanu in Romania are dated to more than 1.95 million years ago, and hominins were present at Dmanisi in Georgia by 1.8 million years ago.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>",
"Sulawesi": "Sulawesi is the largest island of Wallacea, about 174,000 km² (67,200 sq mi), a biogeographically distinct zone of oceanic islands lying between continental Asia and Australia.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> A lowland tower-karst region of some 450 km² (170 sq mi) on its south-western peninsula holds around 300 caves and shelters with rock art on their walls.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Uranium-series analysis of a small speleothem overlying a painted pig at Leang Tedongnge gave that image a minimum age of 45,500 years, which its excavators called the earliest known representational work of art in the world, though a narrative scene at Leang Karampuang has since been dated to at least 51,200 years ago.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>",
"Swabian_Jura": "The Swabian Jura is an upland region of south-western Germany whose caves have produced the oldest portable art and musical instruments of humankind, six of them, in the Ach and Lone valleys, inscribed on the UNESCO World Heritage List in 2017.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Its early Aurignacian dates to around 42,000 years ago on the radiocarbon record, and the ivory figurines from these caves are the oldest portable art known.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Among the finds are a lion-headed human figure 31.1 cm (12.2 inches) tall, carved from mammoth tusk and recovered from the Stadel cave, and a flute 21.7 cm (8.5 inches) long made from a griffon vulture's bone, found at Hohle Fels in 2008.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>",
"Chauvet_Cave": "Chauvet-Pont d'Arc is a decorated Upper Palaeolithic cave in the Ardèche, in southern France.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> It was found on 18 December 1994 by Jean-Marie Chauvet, Éliette Brunel and Christian Hillaire, who felt a cool draught coming out of the rock and dug their way through to it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> A high-precision chronological model places human presence there in two phases, an Aurignacian one about 37,000 to 33,500 years ago and a Gravettian one about 31,000 to 28,000, which makes its drawings among the oldest known, although a minority of specialists dispute that chronology on stylistic grounds.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>",
"Lascaux": "Lascaux is a decorated cave in the Dordogne, in south-western France, found on 12 September 1940 by four boys, two of whom became its guides when it opened to visitors in 1948.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> It is traditionally divided into seven sectors, among them the Hall of the Bulls, and carries some 600 animals, horses by far the most numerous, then stags and aurochs, then ibexes and bison, with one human figure among them.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Recognised by UNESCO in 1979, it has been shut to tourists since 1963, up to about 1,800 visitors a day having altered its temperature, light and carbon dioxide and brought on outbreaks of green, white and black staining.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>",
"Cave_of_Altamira": "The Cave of Altamira lies at Santillana del Mar in Cantabria, in northern Spain, and its painted ceiling was the first Palaeolithic cave art the world had to argue about.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Uranium-series dates on the calcite crusts over its images show a sequence spanning more than 20,000 years, from a red sign of at least about 35,550 years ago, in the Aurignacian, to Magdalenian work near the end.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Its authenticity was rejected by the leading prehistorians of the day, and Émile Cartailhac only recanted in 1902, in an article he titled <i>Mea culpa d'un sceptique</i>.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>",
"Sungir": "Sungir is an open-air Upper Palaeolithic camp on the Klyazma river at Vladimir, east of Moscow, found in the 1950s and dug by Otto Bader between 1957 and 1977.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Its graves are the most elaborate Upper Palaeolithic burials known: one man lay under about 3,000 mammoth ivory beads with twelve pierced fox canines across his forehead and 25 ivory arm bands, two children head to head under roughly 5,000 and 5,400 more, with 16 ivory spears.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The burials date to about 34,000 calendar years ago, and the genomes of four of the dead show none was a close relative of another, not even the two children sharing a grave.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>",
"Aurignacian": "The Aurignacian is an Early Upper Palaeolithic technocomplex of Europe, dated to circa 42,000 to 32,000 calendar years ago and subdivided into a Proto- and an Early Aurignacian.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> It is characterised by distinct lithic and bone technologies and tool types, and the ivory figurines from its levels in the Swabian Jura, where the early Aurignacian dates to around 42,000 years ago on the radiocarbon record, are the oldest portable art known.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Its people painted as well as carved: the earlier of the two phases of human presence at Chauvet-Pont d'Arc, about 37,000 to 33,500 years ago, is Aurignacian.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>",
"Gravettian": "The Gravettian is an Upper Palaeolithic technocomplex dated to circa 34,000 to 24,000 calendar years ago and found in Europe.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> It is characterised by distinct lithic and bone technologies and tool types, is often described as having numerous regional variants, or facies, and is marked by an abundance of elaborately buried individuals across its geographic range; its shaped personal ornaments are few in kind, chiefly symmetric drop-shaped ivory pendants, stone pendants and tubular bone beads.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Its portable art includes the small female figurines conventionally called Venuses, among them the limestone statuette from Willendorf in Austria, which is roughly 30,000 years old.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>",
"Solutrean": "The Solutrean is an Upper Palaeolithic industry confined to south-western France and the Iberian Peninsula and occurring within a moderately short chronological range, circa 25,000 to 19,000 calendar years ago.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> That timespan roughly matches the course of the Last Glacial Maximum, the coldest stretch of the last glacial period.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Its defining innovation is a suite of techniques for shaping stone points by unifacial and bifacial invasive flat retouch, which leaves very fine grazing removals with no clear point of impact and yields foliate, shouldered and tanged forms, above all the laurel leaf, with pressure flaking entering only at the final stage of shaping.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>",
"Magdalenian": "The Magdalenian is the last Upper Palaeolithic industry of western Europe, taking its name from the rock shelter of La Madeleine in the Dordogne.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> It is marked by the richness of its toolwork in bone and cervid antler and by the abundance of its art, and its barbed harpoon heads change steadily through the sequence, from very small teeth to a single row of barbs and then to two, which is why archaeologists use them to place a site within the period.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Magdalenian groups spread north as the ice drew back, and Gough's Cave in Somerset records their presence in Britain.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>",
"Western_Europe": "Western Europe is a conventional grouping of countries, and the United Nations standard for statistical use places nine in it — Austria, Belgium, France, Germany, Liechtenstein, Luxembourg, Monaco, the Netherlands and Switzerland — noting that such assignments imply no assumption about political affiliation.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> In prehistory the term marks out a region with a sequence of Upper Palaeolithic industries of its own, and ancient genomes connect the human groups that lived there before and after the Last Glacial Maximum.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> A 23,000-year-old individual from Cueva del Malalmuerzo in southern Iberia links those populations and supports reading Iberia as a refugium through the coldest phase of the last glacial period.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>",
"Bronze": "<b>Bronze</b> is an alloy of copper and tin, harder than copper alone and easier to cast, and the material that names the Bronze Age. Tin makes copper more fluid in the mould and, at about 10 per cent, stiffens it more than arsenic or zinc do; a content above 1 per cent is usually taken to show deliberate alloying rather than an accident of the ore.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The alloy was known in Mesopotamia and western Iran by the late 4th millennium BCE, but spread unevenly, and much of sub-Saharan Africa, the arctic tundra, island Southeast Asia and Oceania had no bronze metallurgy until late in their history.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>",
"Iron": "<b>Iron</b> is a hard, common metal that occurs in nature almost entirely as ore rather than as the metal, so it has to be smelted before use. It was won from its ores in a bloomery, where charcoal supplied both the heat and the reducing atmosphere, giving a spongy mass called a bloom; gangue minerals and furnace materials formed slag, which had to be driven out by repeated heating and hammering.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Holding the metal at high temperature in a carbon-rich atmosphere carburised it into steel, graded by carbon content; in much of sub-Saharan Africa iron production begins early and without a preceding age of bronze at all.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>",
"Chronology": "A chronology is an ordering of events in time, and it is essential for all archaeologies.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> One that draws on every strand of dating evidence — radiocarbon measurements, stratigraphy, coins, typology — is more reliable than one resting on a single strand, and Bayesian modelling is how those strands are combined into one estimate.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Radiocarbon dating rewrote archaeological chronologies around the world and calibration followed; where a boundary has to be fixed exactly it is defined at a chosen point in a reference section, as the base of the Holocene is at 11,700 calendar years before 2000 CE.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>",
"Swabia": "Swabia is a historical region of south-western Germany named after the Suebi, who settled there and who from the 6th to the 12th century were increasingly identified with the Alemanni.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The name first denoted their settlement land, the stem duchy of Alemannia, and then the duchy of Swabia that grew out of it, a province with fixed frontiers against Bavaria, Franconia, Italy and Burgundy, which the duke had to defend.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The duchy was extinguished for good with the execution of Conradin in 1268, and the name now covers parts of Baden-Württemberg together with the Bavarian administrative district of Bavarian Swabia, consolidated in 1803.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>",
"Scandinavia": "Scandinavia is a region of northern Europe whose human history begins late, because the ice of the last glacial period had to withdraw before people could follow it north.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The pioneers arrive in the Late Palaeolithic and are grouped into the Hamburg, Bromme and Ahrensburg cultures, the Hamburg people hunting reindeer in Arctic tundra and the Ahrensburgians following the retreating ice between about 10,700 and 9000 cal BCE.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Farming societies followed, and the Late Neolithic of southern Scandinavia, from 2350 to 1700 BCE, is marked by the reintroduction of metal and the sudden spread of elaborate bifacial flint tools and weapons, of which the daggers are the outstanding objects.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>",
"Jens_Jacob_Worsaae": "Jens Jacob Asmussen Worsaae was a Danish archaeologist who took Christian Jürgensen Thomsen's classification of antiquities by material and tested it against contextual evidence from stratigraphic excavations.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Rising through the Danish antiquities service, he became inspector for the preservation of antiquities in 1847, succeeded Thomsen as director of the collections in 1866 and later served as a minister.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> His <i>Danmarks Oldtid</i> of 1843, translated six years later as <i>The Primeval Antiquities of Denmark</i>, carried the three periods of Stone, Bronze and Iron into Britain, and his testing established a method as much as a sequence: prehistory became a discipline defined by typology, stratigraphy and technology rather than by texts.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>",
"Hesiod": "Hesiod is an early Greek poet who put himself into his own verse, coming from Ascra in Boeotia and quarrelling with his brother Perses over their inheritance.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Two poems are his, the <i>Works and Days</i> and the <i>Theogony</i>, which opens with the Muses of Helicon initiating him as a shepherd and telling him they know how to say falsehoods that look like the truth, and also true things.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> It sets out five races of men — gold, silver, bronze, a god-like race of heroes and the iron race he puts himself in — each worse than the last, in a decline where the fourth is not a metal.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>",
"Oceania": "Oceania is the region of the Pacific which the United Nations standard for statistical use divides into four parts: Australia and New Zealand, Melanesia, Micronesia and Polynesia.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Melanesia takes in Fiji, New Caledonia, Papua New Guinea, the Solomon Islands and Vanuatu; Micronesia takes in Guam, Kiribati, the Marshall Islands, Micronesia, Nauru and Palau; and Polynesia takes in Samoa, Tonga, Tuvalu, the Cook Islands and French Polynesia.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Such groupings are made for statistical convenience and imply no assumption about political affiliation, and the region is one where the conventional sequence of ages fits badly, since it had no bronze metallurgy until late in its history.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>",
"Mesopotamia": "Mesopotamia is the land of the Tigris and Euphrates, rising in the Taurus Mountains and running parallel to the Zagros, which built the vast low-gradient alluvial plain of the south out of their own sediment.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The earliest network of city-states emerged there about 5,000 years ago, knit together by shared traditions and economic interests, and during the Uruk period settlement grew denser and more hierarchical, Uruk itself passing 250 hectares (620 acres) and 25,000 people.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Writing appears toward the end of the 4th millennium BCE, the boundary conventionally drawn between prehistory and history, though the traditional date of 3200 BCE is itself more or less a convention.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>",
"Chert": "<b>Chert</b> is a hard sedimentary rock made almost entirely of silica, its quartz crystals too fine to be resolved by eye. Rocks of this kind were commonly chosen for making stone tools, which is presumed to be due to their predictable fracturing properties and good knapping qualities, and quality is commonly related to the mineralogical structure and purity of the material.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Stones whose mechanical properties are the same in every direction are generally reckoned better for toolmaking; one comparison of knapping stones found its chert to be composed entirely of length-fast chalcedony, and stone of this sort was still worked commercially into modern times.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>",
"Flint": "Flint is one of the silica-rich sedimentary rocks — with chert, silicified shale and their relations — that knappers preferred, a choice presumed to rest on the predictability with which such stone fractures.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Grain size and shape and the purity of the material govern how well a piece works, and stone that behaves the same in every direction is generally reckoned the better sort.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> It is dug where it lies in chalk, and the trade outlasted the Stone Age by thousands of years: gun-flints were still being struck by hand in English workshops in 1878.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>",
"Obsidian": "Obsidian is a naturally forming glass, an igneous rock produced only by the rapid cooling of silica-rich lava without the formation of crystals.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> It is often jet black but can also be brown, tan or green, and although strong it can be easily shaped by knapping, giving an edge that can be honed so thin that a modern obsidian scalpel cuts between cells rather than tearing through tissue.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> It was quarried for toolmaking at Obsidian Cliff in Yellowstone more than 11,000 years ago and carried extraordinary distances: obsidian from there and from Bear Gulch in Idaho reached earthworks in the Scioto River Valley of Ohio, over 2,400 kilometres (1,500 miles) away.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>",
"Conchoidal_fracture": "Conchoidal fracture is the smooth, shell-like way a homogeneous brittle solid breaks under a blow, and in the study of stone tools it is treated as Hertzian fracture, the mechanics worked out for a load pressed onto an elastic surface.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Glass is the standard medium in knapping experiments precisely because it fractures conchoidally, and a hard-hammer blow leaves a clear point of initiation and a well-formed bulb of percussion where a soft hammer, working by bending fracture, leaves a more diffused one.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The rocks that behave this way are the silica-rich sedimentary stones, which is presumed to be why they were the ones commonly chosen for toolmaking.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>",
"Handaxe": "A handaxe is a large cutting tool made by striking a big flake and then shaping it with smaller flakes struck round the edges, a form in use by about 1.76 million years ago and the one that characterises the Acheulean toolkit.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Their distribution underlies the so-called Movius Line, and their frequent symmetry has been argued to be an indicator of good genes displayed in courtship, a proposal that has received credible criticism.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> What they were for is still argued about: experiments show bifaces capable of numerous woodworking activities, butchery trials found no strong correlation between symmetry and effectiveness, and their suitability for digging has been largely overlooked.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>",
"Debitage": "Debitage is the material detached from a core in the course of knapping, taken together — the flakes and the debris of percussion flaking rather than the finished tool.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> In descriptions of a prepared-core industry the word covers both the flakes and the cores they came from, as when an assemblage is characterised by tanged tools, Levallois and micro-Levallois debitage, and side-scrapers.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> It is not waste to an archaeologist but evidence, and it can be measured against the pieces the knapper was aiming at: preferential Levallois flakes proved thicker across their surface area as a whole, and of relatively even thickness, when compared with the debitage flakes struck alongside them.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>",
"Gunflint": "A gunflint is a shaped piece of flint held in the lock of a firearm, which crashes down on a steel plate so that the shower of red-hot sparks ignites gunpowder in a shallow pan and sets off the main charge inside.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Making them kept flint knapping alive as a trade long after the Stone Age: at Brandon in Suffolk 26 men and boys were still at work in four shops in April 1878, having been 36 in 1868.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The Geological Survey, publishing its memoir on the industry that year, described a manufacture continuously carried on from what geologists have called Neolithic times down to the present day.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>",
"Musket": "A musket is a long-barrelled shoulder firearm, and in its flintlock form it fired when a piece of flint crashed down on a steel plate, the shower of red-hot sparks igniting priming powder that set off the main charge inside.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Loading it was a drill rather than an action: the sequence prescribed for United States troops in the middle of the 19th century ran to twelve separate motions, and the Mexican drill to eleven.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Its ignition depended on a knapped stone, so it kept a Stone Age craft in industrial employment, the gun-flint trade being substantial enough to earn a Geological Survey memoir of its own in 1879.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>",
"Subsistence": "Subsistence is how a society gets its living from its surroundings, by hunting, fishing and gathering or by agriculture and livestock farming.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> It is reconstructed from what people left behind: use-wear showing that sickles were used for harvesting cereals, charred grain, the bones of game animals, pollen records of crop rotation, stable isotopes, and dental caries, whose commonness tracks the carbohydrate in a cereal diet.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> It is not only how much food there is but how it is distributed, since clumped, defensible resources — salmon runs above all — underwrote a class system of hereditary nobility, commoners and slaves among foragers who did no farming at all.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>",
"Salmon": "Salmon are migratory fish that gather in great numbers on their spawning runs, and that predictability made them one of the most important wild foods in prehistory.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> They are the most commonly depicted fish in Magdalenian art, and stable isotopes from Upper Palaeolithic human remains suggest aquatic resources were contributing up to half of dietary protein from the middle of the period onwards.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Because a run is both abundant and defensible, it can underwrite social forms that farming is usually credited with: along the North Pacific coast, foragers who grew nothing had hereditary nobility, commoners and slaves, and salmon above all is the resource behind it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>",
"Geology": "Geology is the science of the Earth: the processes that have shaped present and past landscapes, and the way its rocks and minerals formed.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Its subject is old — the Earth has been cooling since it formed 4.6 billion years ago — and its branches run from erosion, deposition and weathering through earthquakes, volcanoes and landslides to fossils and geological time.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Where it needs a boundary in that time to be exact it drives a marker into a rock face rather than arguing from definitions, agreeing a point in a chosen section that all later work refers to, as the Quaternary's base was fixed in Sicily in 2009.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>",
"Geological_epoch": "A geological epoch is a formally defined division of the geological timescale, one rank below a period, and the rock body corresponding to it is called a series.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Its base is fixed by a Global Stratotype Section and Point, a marker agreed at a chosen level in a chosen exposure: the Pleistocene's is at Monte San Nicola in Sicily, dated to 2.58 million years ago and ratified in 2009.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> A boundary need not be in rock at all — the base of the Holocene was placed at 1492.45 m (4,896 feet) in the NGRIP Greenland ice core, at 11,700 calendar years before 2000 CE, the first such point defined in ice.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>",
"Cenozoic_Era": "The Cenozoic Era is the most recent era of geological time, and the Quaternary Period, with its Pleistocene and Holocene epochs, is its last division.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> It is the interval across which the planet cooled into its present state: carbon dioxide fell from about 1,000 parts per million in the Early Cenozoic to between 170 and 300 in the Pleistocene, driven by shifting plate tectonics, which control both the volcanic emissions releasing the gas and the weathering removing it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> That cooling glaciated Antarctica first and then, in the most recent 3 to 5 million years, grew the Northern Hemisphere ice sheets, which still stand in Greenland and Antarctica.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>",
"Stratum": "A stratum is a distinguishable layer of deposit, and a sequence of them read in order is the oldest tool either geology or archaeology has for telling what came before what.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Geology fixes its formal time boundaries inside such sequences, agreeing a level in a chosen exposure — the base of the Quaternary in a section at Monte San Nicola in Sicily, the base of the Holocene at a measured depth in a Greenland ice core.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Archaeology uses the same relations as evidence rather than as definition, one strand among the radiocarbon dates, coins and typologies that a reliable chronology draws on together.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>",
"Beringia": "Beringia is the region of the Bering Strait and the lands either side of it, dry during the last glacial lowstand: the strait is only about 53 m (170 feet) deep, so it stood exposed as a land bridge and permitted human migration.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> It was flooded about 11,000 calendar years ago as the sea came back up.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> While it stood it carried the mammoth steppe, an open grassland across which the woolly mammoth ranged; mainland populations declined about 12,900 years ago in North America and in the early Holocene in northern Asia, under habitat change and human hunting, while island populations lasted to about 4,000 years ago.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>",
"Siberia": "Siberia is the great northern expanse of Asia, and its cold has made it the richest source of ancient DNA on Earth.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> A finger bone of a juvenile excavated in 2008 at Denisova Cave, in the south of the region, was sequenced to reveal the Denisovans, a population sharing a common origin with Neanderthals and contributing genetic material to present-day Melanesians.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> In the north-east a mammoth specimen from Krestovka, 1.2 to 1.1 million years old, held the oldest DNA yet sequenced from an animal, and the same deposits record the steppe mammoth lineage turning into the woolly mammoth.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>",
"Alaska": "Alaska is the largest American state and the north-western extremity of North America, covering 1,723,337 square kilometres (665,384 square miles)<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> with fewer than 750,000 inhabitants.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> It is divided from Siberia by the Bering Strait, which is only about 53 m (170 feet) deep and stood dry as a land bridge until it flooded about 11,000 calendar years ago<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>; a terminal Pleistocene genome recovered there revealed the first founding population of Native Americans.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Claimed by Russia from the 1720s and sold to the United States in 1867 for $7.2 million<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>, it was a territory for nearly a century before entering the Union as the 49th state on 3 January 1959.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup>",
"Mammoth": "<b>Mammoths</b> are extinct proboscideans of the genus <i>Mammuthus</i>, close relatives of the living elephants, with long curving tusks and a range that at its widest covered Africa, Eurasia and North America. Body sizes across the genus have been estimated by volumetric methods tested against living elephants.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Their deep history has been read straight out of ancient DNA: a specimen from Krestovka in north-eastern Siberia, 1.2 to 1.1 million years old, held the oldest DNA yet sequenced from an animal, and the same sequences catch one species turning into another, the Adycha steppe lineage of about a million years ago being directly ancestral to woolly mammoths.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>",
"Woolly_mammoth": "The <b>woolly mammoth</b>, <i>Mammuthus primigenius</i>, was an extinct elephant, small-eared and short-tailed under a thick coat of hair, standing about 2.7 to 3.5 m (8 ft 10 in to 11 ft 6 in) at the shoulder and weighing 5.5 to 8 tonnes (6.1 to 8.8 tons).<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Its country was the mammoth steppe, an open grassland running across Beringia; mainland populations declined about 12,900 years ago in North America and in the early Holocene in northern Asia, under habitat change and human hunting.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Island populations lasted far longer: the Wrangel Island animals were cut off about 10,000 years ago and died out about 4,000 years ago after more than 200 generations, showing inbreeding depression without a genomic meltdown.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>",
"Elephant": "Elephants are the surviving proboscideans, and genomics has settled that the African ones are two species and not one: forest elephants are a lineage distinct from savanna elephants, the two having split some 5 to 2 million years ago.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Despite hybridisation where their ranges meet, for conservation purposes forest and savanna elephants are appropriately viewed as reproductively distinct units, meeting the definition of the biological species concept.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The Asian elephant is not their near relative but the mammoths': Asian elephants and mammoths are estimated to have split at about the same time as the split between <i>Loxodonta</i> and the straight-tusked elephants.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>",
"Smilodon": "<i>Smilodon</i> is a genus of saber-toothed cat of the Americas, one of the genera placed in the machairodontine subfamily.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Two of its species are much studied and both originate in North America: <i>S. fatalis</i>, with fossil records through the whole American continent, and <i>S. populator</i>, restricted to South America and the more widely distributed of the two there.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> <i>S. fatalis</i> is known above all from the asphalt seeps of Rancho La Brea: its upper canines had crowns about 130 mm (5.1 inches) high and erupted at some 6 mm (0.24 inches) a month, and dental microwear puts it closer to the African lion than to a strict flesh specialist.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>",
"Last_Glacial_Maximum": "The Last Glacial Maximum is the interval when the ice sheets of the last glacial period stood at their greatest extent, holding those positions between about 26,500 and 19,000 years ago — a lowstand plateau some 7,500 years long rather than a single peak.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> So much water was locked into ice that global sea level stood roughly 130 to 134 m (430 to 440 feet) below the present, once estimates are constrained for glacial isostatic adjustment.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The Laurentide, Cordilleran, Scandinavian, British-Irish and Barents-Kara sheets all reached their limits then, the Laurentide standing 3 to 4 km (1.9 to 2.5 miles) thick over the Hudson Bay dome, and deglaciation began as summer insolation in the Northern Hemisphere rose.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>",
"Stratigraphy": "Stratigraphy is the study of layered deposits and the order in which they were laid down, one of the methods by which prehistory became a discipline defined by evidence rather than by written texts.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> On an excavation those relationships are recorded formally — single-context recording and Harris matrices are standard practice — so many sites already carry a great deal of relative dating information before any sample is measured.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> That information is not superseded by scientific dating but combined with it, and where geology needs a boundary to be exact it fixes that boundary inside such a sequence, as the Quaternary's base is fixed at Monte San Nicola in Sicily.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>",
"Laurentide_ice_sheet": "The Laurentide Ice Sheet was the great ice sheet of North America, known to have initiated over the Arctic and sub-Arctic plateaux of eastern Canada and reaching its maximum extent towards the end of the glacial cycle.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Reconstructions put it at some 3 to 4 km (1.9 to 2.5 miles) thick over the Hudson Bay dome, with ice-sheet models simulating 4 to 5 km (2.5 to 3.1 miles).<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> It stood at its greatest extent through the Last Glacial Maximum, between about 26,500 and 19,000 years ago, alongside the Cordilleran, Scandinavian, British-Irish and Barents-Kara sheets, and the Northern Hemisphere ice of that reconstruction amounts to about 100 m (330 feet) of sea-level equivalent.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>",
"Fennoscandian_ice_sheet": "The Fennoscandian Ice Sheet was centred on northern Europe, one of the Northern Hemisphere sheets that stood at their limits through the Last Glacial Maximum, between about 26,500 and 19,000 years ago.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> It was the dominant part of the Eurasian ice sheet, which changed in area more than any other Northern Hemisphere sheet, growing by over 1,000 per cent at the glacial maximum relative to the warmer intervals.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Its retreat has been mapped in calendar-year isochrons from 22 thousand years ago to the final decay of the ice after 9.7 thousand, with an ice-sheet-wide interruption during the Younger Dryas between 12.7 and 11.6 thousand years ago.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>",
"Wisconsin_glaciation": "The Wisconsin glaciation is the last glaciation of North America, the advance of the Laurentide Ice Sheet that began over the Arctic and sub-Arctic plateaux of eastern Canada and reached its maximum extent towards the end of the glacial cycle.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> In the state it is named after, the late Wisconsin ice reached its greatest extent between about 18,000 and 15,000 years ago, and permafrost persisted in the south until roughly 14,000 years ago and in the north until about 13,000.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Its close belongs to the wider Last Glacial Maximum, when the Laurentide stood alongside the Cordilleran, Scandinavian, British-Irish and Barents-Kara sheets between about 26,500 and 19,000 years ago.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>",
"Doggerland": "Doggerland is the name given, after Bryony Coles in 1998, to the Mesolithic landscape of the southern North Sea, now under water.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Almost everything known of it has been dredged up, washed ashore or read off seismic data: oil-industry three-dimensional surveys have been reworked into palaeolandscape maps over more than 23,000 km² (8,900 sq mi), and the first find to be recognised was a barbed red-deer antler harpoon brought up in 1931 from the Leman and Ower banks.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The Storegga tsunami struck it about 8,150 years ago, but the argument since 2020 has been that inexorable sea-level rise, rather than that single wave, took the last of it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>",
"Great_Britain": "Great Britain is the official collective name of England, Scotland and Wales and their associated islands.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> It does not include Northern Ireland and should therefore never be used interchangeably with the United Kingdom, whose full name — the United Kingdom of Great Britain and Northern Ireland — refers to the political union of all four; the British Isles is a purely geographical term covering the islands of Great Britain and Ireland together with some 5,000 smaller ones.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Its human history is broken by the ice: after the last glaciation Magdalenian groups moved north as the sheets drew back, and Gough's Cave in Somerset records their presence.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>",
"Woolly_rhinoceros": "The woolly rhinoceros, <i>Coelodonta antiquitatis</i>, was a cold-adapted megaherbivore, abundant from western Europe to north-east Siberia through the Middle and Late Pleistocene.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The genus originated north of the Himalayan–Tibetan uplift around 2.5 million years ago and spread westwards into Europe, the earliest documented immigration being about 460,000 to 400,000 years ago, and its European mitochondrial lineage proves long separated from the Siberian one.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> It was one of the animals of the mammoth steppe, the relatively homogeneous steppe–tundra flora that dominated the Arctic at the Last Glacial Maximum, alongside the woolly mammoth and the steppe bison, and its demise begins about 10,000 years ago.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>",
"Cave_bear": "The cave bear, <i>Ursus spelaeus</i>, is an extinct bear of Ice Age Europe, largely plant-eating, which denned in caves in such numbers that its remains are often the commonest thing in them.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Chauvet-Pont d'Arc holds about 300 bear hollows scooped into its floor and the remains of some 190 individuals, with one skull set deliberately on a block of stone.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Its bones are also at the centre of a long argument about the earliest music, since punctured cave bear femora have been read both as flutes and as the bone-crushing damage left by spotted hyenas scavenging bear cubs in the dens.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>",
"Irish_elk": "The Irish elk, <i>Megaloceros giganteus</i>, is an extinct giant deer — neither confined to Ireland nor an elk — that stands out among the Pleistocene megafauna for its antlers, which spanned up to 4 m (13 feet) and weighed up to 45 kg (99 pounds).<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> It appears in the fossil record around 400,000 years ago and its populations ranged from Ireland to Lake Baikal, returning to the north-west only in the Late Glacial Interstadial, with a purported final extinction about 6,900 years ago in Siberia.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Complete mitochondrial genomes support a <i>Dama</i>–<i>Megaloceros</i> clade, placing it with the fallow deer, and it is among the animals drawn on the walls at Chauvet-Pont d'Arc.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>",
"Saber-toothed_cat": "Saber-toothed cats are the Machairodontinae, an extinct subfamily of the cat family recovered as monophyletic and made up of three major saber-tooth lineages, traditionally the tribes Metailurini, Smilodontini and Homotheriini.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The genera placed among them include <i>Dinofelis</i>, <i>Megantereon</i>, <i>Smilodon</i>, <i>Homotherium</i> and <i>Xenosmilus</i>, and the large ones sit more peripherally in cranial morphospace than living big cats do, so that their skull shapes are more varied than any living cat's.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> They were not confined to one continent: saber-toothed cats are among the carnivores of the Middle Pleistocene fauna at Zhoukoudian in China, alongside giant deer, straight-tusked elephant, woolly rhinoceros and horses of the genus <i>Equus</i>.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>",
"Weichselian_glaciation": "The Weichselian is the last cold stage of the Pleistocene in northern central Europe; its name, with the older Elsterian and Saalian, entered the literature through the Prussian Geological Survey's mapping from 1910.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> It opens with boreal vegetation giving way to subarctic after the Eemian warm stage, and glaciation in northern Germany most probably began about 25,000 years ago, though glaciers may have reached the western Baltic between 70,000 and 50,000 years ago.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Ice reached Brandenburg in the east and the northern edge of Hamburg further west, tills recording three advances named after Brandenburg, Pomerania and Mecklenburg, and the Late Glacial began as the ice melted about 14,500 years ago.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>",
"Würm_glaciation": "The Würm is the last glaciation of the Alps and their northern foreland, at the young end of the fourfold scheme of Günz, Mindel, Riss and Würm that Albrecht Penck built from local place names in alphabetical order and set out with Eduard Brückner from 1901.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The type locality of the last glacial advance is the Birrfeld in northern Switzerland, where luminescence dating puts the maximum ice extent at roughly 25,000 years ago.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Continental records put the last most extensive ice generally between 26,500 and 19,000 years ago, and Penck's morphostratigraphy, pairing each glaciation's moraines with a gravel train, remains applicable almost everywhere to Würmian sediments.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>",
"Devensian_glaciation": "The Devensian is the last cold stage of the British Pleistocene, its stratotype at Four Ashes in Staffordshire.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> It is divided into three substages: a Late Devensian from 26,000 to 10,000 years before present, a Middle Devensian from 50,000 to 26,000, and an Early Devensian covering all that precedes 50,000; above it the Flandrian begins at 10,000 radiocarbon years before present.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> These definitions come from Philip Gibbard's compilation of British Quaternary stages, modified from Frederick Shotton's 1973 scheme, and the international correlation table for the last 2.7 million years sets the Devensian against the north-west European, North American and New Zealand stages and the marine isotope record.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>",
"Fennoscandia": "Fennoscandia is a region of northern Europe named in 1898 by the Finnish geologist Wilhelm Ramsay, who argued that Kola, Norway, Sweden, Finland, Russian Karelia and Onega form a physico-geographical area sharply differing from its surroundings.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> He coined it because no name existed: northern Europe covers a larger area, and the Baltic Shield leaves out the Palaeozoic mountains of Norway and Scania, so the region is wider than the Precambrian shield now generally called the Fennoscandian Shield.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Climate modelling places it between 58 and 71° N and from sea level to 2,469 m (8,100 feet), with boreal forest running inland from the coast, mountain birch above it, and tundra above the treeline.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>",
"Rhinoceros": "Rhinoceroses are the Rhinocerotidae, a family of odd-toed ungulates with five living species: the black and white rhinoceroses of Africa, <i>Diceros</i> and <i>Ceratotherium</i>, and in Asia the Indian and Javan <i>Rhinoceros</i> and the Sumatran <i>Dicerorhinus</i>.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The three Asian species are set apart by their tusked incisors, and the family once ranged far beyond its present limits: <i>Stephanorhinus</i> and <i>Coelodonta</i> were present in western Europe through the Middle and Late Pleistocene.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Ancient protein sequences from those extinct genera place the African species as the most basal clade among living rhinoceroses, <i>Dicerorhinus</i> and the two extinct genera forming a clade that groups in turn with <i>Rhinoceros</i>.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>",
"Holocene_climatic_optimum": "The Holocene climatic optimum is the warm interval of the early and middle Holocene, warmest near 6,500 years ago.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> It goes by several other names — the Hypsithermal, the Altithermal, the Holocene Thermal Maximum and the Mid-Holocene Warm Period — a fair sign of how loosely its edges are drawn.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The warmth was neither global nor simultaneous, arriving early and strongly at high northern latitudes but later and more faintly in the tropics, and its peak is dated at individual sites anywhere between 11,000 and 4,000 years ago, a spread usually laid to the North American ice sheet holding temperatures down until it had gone.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>",
"Post-glacial_rebound": "Post-glacial rebound is the slow rise of ground that ice sheets once pressed down, still lifting long after the ice has melted, and it forms one part of what geophysicists call glacial isostatic adjustment.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The crust does not simply compress under an ice load: mantle rock creeps sideways, so the hollow beneath the ice is opened by rock flowing outwards, raising a broad swell called a forebulge, and because that rock is extremely stiff the recovery takes thousands of years.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The land around the Gulf of Bothnia and Hudson Bay still rises about a centimetre (0.4 inches) a year, while the collapsing forebulge carries the ground beyond the old ice margin down.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>",
"8.2-kiloyear_event": "The 8.2-kiloyear event was an abrupt turn to cold, dry conditions registered in Greenland ice cores and in lake muds, pollen, cave formations and ocean sediments on four continents, the sharpest climate reversal of the Holocene.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> It is usually explained by a sudden release of glacial meltwater into the North Atlantic, from lakes ponded against the shrinking North American ice sheet or from the collapse of the Hudson Bay ice saddle, slowing the circulation that carries warmth north.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Its signature is sharp enough to serve as a stratigraphic boundary: a Greenland ice-core horizon was ratified in 2018 as the base of the Northgrippian, the middle age of the Holocene.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>",
"Aegean_Bronze_Age": "The Aegean Bronze Age is the period in which the societies of Crete, the Cyclades and the Greek mainland took up bronze, built towns and then palaces.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Archaeologists tell it as three parallel regional sequences — Minoan for Crete, Cycladic for the islands, Helladic for the mainland — each cut into Early, Middle and Late and those into I, II and III, so an object can be placed without a calendar year being claimed.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Three scripts appear on Crete: Cretan hieroglyphic remains undeciphered apart from its numerals, Linear A has drawn many proposed decipherments and no accepted one, and Linear B, deciphered by Michael Ventris in 1952, records Greek.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>",
"Cycladic_civilisation": "The Cycladic civilisation is the Early Bronze Age culture of the Cyclades, the islands of the central Aegean.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Its phases carry the names Colin Renfrew gave them after type sites, Grotta-Pelos, Keros-Syros and Phylakopi I, and almost everything known of these islanders comes from cemeteries, most of them looted rather than excavated, so they are far better known in death than in life.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> What survives is above all the islands' own marble, cut into thin-walled vessels and into figures with folded arms, along with lead, silver and copper from Siphnos, Kythnos and the Laurion ores, and the obsidian quarried on Melos that reached the Adriatic, Crete and western Anatolia.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>",
"Cycladic_figurines": "Cycladic figurines are the marble human figures carved in the islands of the central Aegean during the Early Bronze Age, most of them recovered from tombs.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> They range from schematic forms, some no more than ovoid pebbles, to the canonical folded-arm type, never meant to stand upright, its feet and knees rendered for a figure lying on its back.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The white marble is an accident of survival, for they were painted — black and blue for eyes, hair and the pubic triangle, red for necklaces and diadems — and where the paint has gone it can leave a 'paint ghost', a smoother patch where the covered stone weathered differently.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>",
"Keros": "Keros is a rugged, uninhabited island at the centre of the Cyclades, the largest of the Small Cyclades, south of Naxos.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Two deposits of deliberately broken Early Bronze Age marble figurines, stone vessels and pottery lie at Kavos on its western tip, with no human bone and no carving waste; the fragments do not fit together, each object present as a single piece whose remainder is absent, so the breaking happened elsewhere and one fragment was carried in by sea.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Facing Kavos, the islet of Dhaskalio was terraced and built up in marble ferried from Naxos and worked metal on a scale unmatched in the Cyclades of its day.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>",
"Early_Minoan_Crete": "Early Minoan Crete is the island in the Early Bronze Age, the first of the three Minoan phases, ending before any palace was built.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> It is known far better from its dead than its living, above all from the tholos tomb, a round chamber of fieldstones entered from the east, more than 70 known from over 45 sites, nearly all around the Mesara plain, while the north-east built rectangular house tombs.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Its settlements are few and have repeatedly been reinterpreted downwards in scale: the hilltop warren of Myrtos Fournou Korifi was read first as one complex under a central authority and later as five or six ordinary houses.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>",
"Minoan_civilisation": "The Minoan civilisation is the Bronze Age culture of Crete, conventionally divided into four ages named for its great buildings, Prepalatial, Protopalatial, Neopalatial and Postpalatial.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Its palaces appear around 1900 BCE, are destroyed around 1720 and are rebuilt grander in the Neopalatial age that ends near 1470; the island was never one kingdom, the eastern valleys and the west holding palace states with towns of their own.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Its script was Linear A, written from about 1800 to 1450 BCE and undeciphered still, while the records kept at Knossos and Chania for up to two centuries after the Neopalatial destructions are in Linear B, which is Greek.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>",
"Arthur_Evans": "Arthur Evans was an English archaeologist, Keeper of the Ashmolean Museum, who excavated the Bronze Age palace of Knossos on Crete from 1900.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> He had come to the island in 1894 chasing a script rather than a palace, having met engraved stones he took for a pre-Phoenician writing, and the two phases he distinguished, one pictorial and one linear, grew into the three scripts he named Cretan Hieroglyphic, Linear A and Linear B.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> He gave the civilisation he uncovered the name Minoan and published it in the six volumes of <i>The Palace of Minos</i>, and rebuilt much of the site, restoration the Ashmolean itself calls extensive and controversial.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>",
"Knossos": "Knossos is the largest Minoan palace site, standing on the low hill of Kephala in north-central Crete a little inland from Heraklion.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> It is a mound as much as a palace, since a Neolithic settlement lies beneath it and modelled radiocarbon puts the first occupation at 6900 to 6600 BCE, while the Bronze Age building above is arranged around a paved central court with blocks of rooms about it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Minos Kalokairinos made a small excavation there in 1878, and Arthur Evans dug the site from 1900 to 1931; what stands over the ruins now is largely his restoration, which the museum holding his archive calls extensive and controversial.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>",
"Minoan_palace": "A Minoan palace is a complex built around a rectangular central court, the building type that defines Crete from about 1900 BCE and is best known from Knossos, Phaistos, Malia and Zakros.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The court is usually laid out north to south and measures 54 by 27 metres (177 by 89 feet) at the largest, but the plan is centrifugal, almost all daily activity happening in the rooms around it rather than out on it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The word palace is Arthur Evans's inheritance and excavators now often say 'court-centred building' instead, since there are more of them than the handbooks suggest, on a wider range of scales and not all founded together.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>",
"Throne_Room_at_Knossos": "The Throne Room at Knossos is a small chamber among the cult rooms on the west side of the palace's central court, uncovered by Arthur Evans in 1900 and surviving almost intact.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> A gypsum throne with a high, undulating leaf-shaped back stands 1.06 metres (3 ft 6 in) tall against one wall, above the benches running round the room, and a sunken tank faces it, reached by six steps and with no outlet at all.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The walls carried a landscape of running water, rushes and palms, with a pair of couchant griffins flanking the inner doorway, and the room is later than the palace around it, its griffin fresco belonging to Late Minoan II.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>",
"Last_Glacial_Period": "The Last Glacial Period was the most recent cold stage of the current ice age, within the Late Pleistocene, reaching its coldest around 20,000 years ago.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Geologists give it a different name in each region — the Würm in the Alps, the Weichselian in northern Europe, the Wisconsinan in North America, the Devensian in Britain — and at its height ice sheets covered roughly a third of the land.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> So much water was locked into ice that the sea stood some 130 metres (430 feet) lower, laying bare land bridges such as Beringia and Doggerland, and the period closed as the world warmed into the Holocene.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>",
"Homo_ergaster": "<i>Homo ergaster</i> is the name proposed in 1975 by Colin Groves and Vratislav Mazák for early human fossils from eastern and southern Africa, built on a lower jaw catalogued KNM-ER 992 from Koobi Fora.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Whether it names a real species is the whole question, since the fossils are otherwise much like the <i>Homo erectus</i> long known from Java and China: some keep the African material apart under this name, others treat the two as one widespread species.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Neither brain size nor age settles it: the African, Georgian, Chinese and Indonesian braincases form one broad range rather than two, and later work narrowed the difference in time between them.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>",
"Turkana_Boy": "The Turkana Boy is the skeleton of a boy of about eight or nine from Nariokotome in northern Kenya, spotted by Kamoya Kimeu in 1984 and catalogued KNM-WT 15000.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> About 40 per cent of it was recovered, which makes it the most complete early human skeleton known: he stood 1.6 metres (5 ft 3 in), weighed 48 kilograms (106 pounds), and his braincase held 880 cubic centimetres.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> He had been growing at a rate closer to a living great ape's than to ours, and his rib cage is short, wide and deep rather than shallow like a modern one, so modern body shape came later than the skeleton was long taken to show.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>",
"Wonderwerk_Cave": "Wonderwerk Cave is a long, level tunnel running about 140 metres (460 feet) into a hillside in the Northern Cape of South Africa; its Afrikaans name means 'miracle'.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The deposits on its floor cover roughly 2 million years: Oldowan tools near the bottom, the oldest evidence anywhere of people living inside a cave rather than merely passing one, then Acheulean hand axes, and near the top engraved stones and painted walls.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> It is best known for fire: ashed plant material and burnt bone found in undisturbed Acheulean sediment about 30 metres (100 feet) inside, in layers around a million years old, are the earliest solid evidence of fire used by human hands.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>",
"Java_Man": "Java Man is the name given to a skullcap, a molar and a thigh bone dug out of the bank of the Solo River at Trinil in eastern Java, the first remains of <i>Homo erectus</i> ever found.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The Dutch physician who found them, Eugène Dubois, had gone there expressly to look for a fossil link between apes and humans, and in 1894 named it <i>Pithecanthropus erectus</i>, 'the upright ape-man'.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The reception was cold, chiefly because the thigh bone is essentially modern where the skullcap is low and heavily browed; the genus was later folded into <i>Homo erectus</i>, and the braincase is now put at about 940 cubic centimetres.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>",
"Peking_Man": "Peking Man is the name for the <i>Homo erectus</i> remains from Zhoukoudian, a limestone hill south-west of Beijing.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Davidson Black named a new genus and species, <i>Sinanthropus pekinensis</i>, in 1927 on the strength of a single lower molar, and by the late 1930s the hill had given up parts of more than 40 individuals, braincases running from about 915 to 1,225 cubic centimetres.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The most important fossils were packed into two footlockers in 1941 for shipment to New York and never seen again; what saved the science was Franz Weidenreich, who had them photographed, measured and cast beforehand, so it is still studied largely from plaster.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>",
"Zhoukoudian": "Zhoukoudian is a limestone hill in the suburbs of Beijing, known in Chinese as Dragon Bone Hill and riddled with fossil localities.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The most famous, Locality 1, is a karst deposit whose filling took several hundred thousand years to gather, and out of it came the Peking Man fossils with thousands of stone tools and a rich Ice Age fauna.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Higher up the same hill the Upper Cave, dated to roughly 38,300 to 33,500 years ago, belongs to a wholly different world: its people were modern humans who left ochre-coated skulls, a bone needle with an eye, and ornaments of pierced teeth, shell and bone.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>",
"Homo_antecessor": "<i>Homo antecessor</i> is an extinct human species known almost entirely from one layer of one site, level TD6 of the Gran Dolina at Atapuerca in Spain, among the oldest in Europe.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> A team led by José María Bermúdez de Castro named it in 1997 from the Latin for 'a scout or forerunner', and its fame rests on a partial face of a boy of about 10, startlingly modern for its antiquity.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The same layer holds the oldest known evidence of cannibalism, and ancient proteins read from tooth enamel in 2020 placed it as a close cousin of the line leading to modern humans, Neanderthals and Denisovans, not at its root.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>",
"Atapuerca_Mountains": "The Atapuerca Mountains are a low limestone range east of Burgos in northern Spain, riddled with caves that filled with sediment and were laid open by a railway cutting driven through the hill.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Systematic excavation began in 1978 and the range became a World Heritage Site in 2000; five hominin species are represented in its caves, among them Homo antecessor from the Gran Dolina.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Its strangest site is the Sima de los Huesos, the Pit of Bones, which has yielded the remains of some 29 people about 430,000 years old, read by anatomy and ancient DNA not as a separate species but as a basal population of the Neanderthal line.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>",
"Homo_heidelbergensis": "<i>Homo heidelbergensis</i> is a proposed human species of the Middle Pleistocene, named in 1908 by Otto Schoetensack from a single lower jaw from a sand pit at Mauer near Heidelberg, dated in 2010 to about 609,000 years old.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> For most of the 20th century it was the common ancestor from which Neanderthals grew in Europe and modern humans in Africa, but the name has since carried almost any Middle Pleistocene fossil that is plainly not <i>Homo erectus</i>, not Neanderthal and not us.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Ancient DNA then removed its best European exhibit by placing the Pit of Bones people on the Neanderthal line, and most workers now use the term narrowly.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>",
"Denisova_Cave": "Denisova Cave is a limestone cave above the Anuy river in the Altai mountains of southern Siberia, the only place known to have been lived in by three kinds of human.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Cool and steady in temperature, it preserves ancient DNA far better than warm sites do: a finger bone found in 2008 revealed the Denisovans, and another splinter came from the one known child of a Neanderthal mother and a Denisovan father.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> It also proved that human DNA can be pulled straight from sediment with no bone at all, which sketched the order of its tenants: Denisovan DNA first, Neanderthal later, modern human DNA from at least 45,000 years ago.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>",
"Homo_naledi": "<i>Homo naledi</i> is a small-brained human species from the Rising Star caves in South Africa, named in 2015, naledi meaning 'star' in Sesotho.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> More than 1,550 pieces from at least 15 individuals have come up from a chamber down a chimney narrowing to some 20 centimetres (8 inches); the anatomy is a mosaic, a brain of only 465 to 610 cubic centimetres and climbing shoulders but legs and feet for walking far.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Dates published in 2017 put it alongside the earliest <i>Homo sapiens</i>, and how they came to lie so deep is still argued: deliberate burial has been claimed, one reviewer judging the 2025 paper convincing where another found it incomplete.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>",
"Homo_floresiensis": "<i>Homo floresiensis</i> is a very small human species from Flores, known to almost everyone as the hobbit, first found at Liang Bua in 2003.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The type specimen is a partial female skeleton about a metre (3 ft 3 in) tall whose braincase held about 426 cubic centimetres, under a third of ours, yet stone tools lie in the same deposits; a 2016 restudy fixed the layers' age.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Many specialists refused to accept a new species and proposed microcephaly, a thyroid disorder, Laron syndrome and Down syndrome, but in the features separating healthy from sick the skeleton falls in with fossil humans, and jaws from the So'a basin about 700,000 years old are as small.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>",
"Liang_Bua": "Liang Bua is a large limestone cave on the Indonesian island of Flores, its name meaning 'cool cave' in Manggarai, with a mouth broad enough to light much of the floor.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> In September 2003, nearly 6 metres (20 feet) down, an Indonesian and Australian team lifted out the small skeleton that became Homo floresiensis.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Its second lesson was about reading layers rather than bones: a restudy in 2016 found that ages taken from younger sediment had been wrongly attached to far older bones, and the corrected stratigraphy moves the skeletons back to between 100,000 and 60,000 years, with a 2025 study dating the last of them to 61,000 years ago.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>",
"Homo_luzonensis": "<i>Homo luzonensis</i> is an archaic human species from Callao Cave on Luzon, named in 2019 from a dozen or so bones and teeth.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The mixture is strange: the teeth are small and simple, close in size to ours, yet the finger and toe bones are curved like an australopith's, and it was that climber's anatomy on what seems to have been a very small body that earned it a species.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Doubt lingers: the material is fragmentary with no skull, and analyses place it beside <i>Homo erectus</i> or far older African forms depending which bones are fed in; Luzon has stayed cut off by sea, so whoever reached it crossed water.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>",
"Neanderthal_extinction": "The Neanderthal extinction is the disappearance of the Neanderthals from Europe and western Asia after several hundred thousand years, its chronology rebuilt in 2014 from 40 sites, Spain to Russia.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> It was not a single moment: they went at different times in different regions, and where both peoples are found their overlap with arriving modern humans runs to roughly 2,600 to 5,400 years.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Three explanations are argued over, none excluding the others: competition from incoming groups, the violent cold swings of the period, and demography, which the genomes have made the strongest, since inbreeding, the difficulty of finding mates in tiny bands and bad luck could alone have finished them.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>",
"Toba_catastrophe_theory": "The Toba catastrophe theory is Stanley Ambrose's 1998 proposal that Toba's eruption in Sumatra brought our species close to extinction, a volcanic winter crashing the population to a few thousand breeding individuals.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The eruption is not in doubt: Toba expelled some 5,300 cubic kilometres of magma, the largest known in two million years, and laid ash from the Arabian Sea to the South China Sea.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The near-extinction is: stone tools below and above the Indian ash beds look much the same, Lake Malawi cores record a cooling of only about 1.5 degrees rather than a volcanic winter, and at Pinnacle Point in South Africa occupation grows busier after the ashfall.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>",
"Omo_remains": "The Omo remains are two early human individuals, Omo I and Omo II, from the Kibish Formation on the lower Omo river in Ethiopia, found in 1967 under Richard Leakey.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Omo I is unmistakably modern, with a rounded braincase and a chin, while Omo II is a nearly complete braincase, longer, lower and thicker-walled, looking archaic though from beds of the same age.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Their age has moved three times, from about 130,000 years on shells to 195,000 by argon dating in 2005 and beyond 233,000 in 2022, when ash from an eruption above Omo I was dated; nothing about the bones changed, only the reading of the rock.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>",
"Jebel_Irhoud": "Jebel Irhoud is a worked-out barite mine in Morocco, holding the oldest fossils yet assigned to our species.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Miners blasting the hillside in the early 1960s turned up a skull long read as an African Neanderthal; digs from 2004 recovered 22 more fossils from five or more people, and burnt flint dated the layer some 100,000 years older than anything called Homo sapiens before.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The faces are small and tucked in, while the braincase behind is long and low rather than the globe we carry, so our features came together piece by piece; its position in north-west Africa helped move specialists from one birthplace to a population spread across Africa.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>",
"Homo_sapiens_idaltu": "<i>Homo sapiens idaltu</i> is a proposed subspecies of our own, named in 2003 for skulls recovered in 1997 at Herto in the Middle Awash of Ethiopia, idaltu meaning 'elder' in Afar.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Volcanic beds around them were dated by potassium-argon decay, and the best-preserved braincase holds about 1,450 cubic centimetres, at the roomy end of the modern range, in a high, rounded vault rather than a long, low one.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The skulls are ruggedly built and stand apart from living people while lacking any Neanderthal feature, which is why their describers named them, but few have kept the name; all three carry defleshing cut marks read as treatment of the dead.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>",
"Mitochondrial_Eve": "Mitochondrial Eve is the woman from whom everyone alive descends through an unbroken chain of mothers, down to the present.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Mitochondria carry a scrap of DNA that a fertilised egg keeps from the mother alone and never shuffles with a father's, so comparing it across living people builds one tree of mothers, meeting at a single woman; estimates put her in Africa roughly 150,000 to 200,000 years ago.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Cann, Stoneking and Wilson published the finding in 1987, and the nickname misleads: she was not the first woman, nor the only woman of her time, and the title slides forward whenever the last woman in a maternal line dies childless.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>",
"Y-chromosomal_Adam": "Y-chromosomal Adam is the man from whom every living man descends through an unbroken line of fathers.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The Y chromosome passes only from father to son and escapes almost all the shuffling that mixes the others, so it travels nearly intact, its copying errors sorting living men into a branching tree meeting at him, placed in Africa about 200,000 to 300,000 years ago.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> That figure has moved further and more often than almost any in the field: in 2013 a sample matching no known branch, traced to a few Mbo men in Cameroon, pushed him back some two hundred thousand years; and the nickname misleads as its female counterpart does.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>",
"Aterian": "The Aterian is a Middle Stone Age toolkit of North Africa and the Sahara, named after Bir el Ater in Algeria, though both ends of its date range are debated.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> What sets it apart is the tang, a narrow stem worked into a point's base so it could be bound into a split shaft — the clearest early sign of a tool made to be mounted, not held.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Its makers were <i>Homo sapiens</i>, settled by skulls and jaws from the Moroccan coast, who pierced sea snail shells and rubbed them with red ochre some 82,000 years ago; specialists now hesitate over the word, the points varying greatly.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>",
"Howiesons_Poort": "The Howiesons Poort is a toolmaking tradition of the southern African Middle Stone Age, found across South Africa, Lesotho and Namibia and named after a shelter near Makhanda.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Its knappers struck narrow blades from prepared cores of fine-grained stone, snapped them short and blunted one edge by steep retouch, leaving the other razor-sharp: a crescent often no longer than a fingernail, set in a shaft as a replaceable part.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Single-grain luminescence dating gives a shared style lasting some 5,000 years at sites hundreds of kilometres apart, though older ages from Diepkloof leave the span disputed, and why the backed segments largely drop out above is still argued over.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>",
"Sibudu_Cave": "Sibudu Cave is a rock shelter in a sandstone cliff above the uThongathi River in KwaZulu-Natal, South Africa, a broad overhang rather than a deep cave.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Luminescence dating places its Middle Stone Age deposits between roughly 77,000 and 38,000 years ago, from a pre-Still Bay phase through the Still Bay and Howiesons Poort; bone survives here unusually well.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Its most celebrated find is bedding — mats of sedges and rushes laid on the floor about 77,000 years ago, topped with insect-killing Cryptocarya woodii leaves — and its Howiesons Poort segments carry a glue of acacia gum and powdered ochre, heated, a recipe judged by eye and feel.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>",
"Border_Cave": "Border Cave is a rock shelter high on the Lebombo scarp in KwaZulu-Natal, South Africa, whose dry, ashy sediment preserves wood, plant fibre and resin that rot away at almost every site of its age.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Beneath its floor, brown sand and white ash hold a Middle Stone Age record reaching back about 227,000 years, one of the longest archives of human occupation in the region.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Sheaves of broad-leaved grass laid on ash about 200,000 years ago are the earliest bedding known, at least 100,000 years older than any found before; later layers gave up an infant buried with an ochre-coated Conus shell, shell beads and the oldest worked beeswax.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>",
"Klasies_River_Caves": "The Klasies River Caves sit at the foot of a sea cliff on South Africa's southern Cape coast, preserving a near-continuous Late Pleistocene record in a deep bank of Middle and Late Stone Age deposit.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The oldest Middle Stone Age layers are more than about 115,000 years old, and much of the deposit is shell, in thick middens of mussel and turban snail; speleothems dated to about 110,000 years put those middens earlier still, the clearest early sign of systematic coastal foraging.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Scattered through the layers are the fragmentary remains of many people, nearly 40 catalogued specimens, which have run through arguments about modern human origins ever since.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>",
"Pinnacle_Point": "Pinnacle Point is a headland on South Africa's south coast, its sea cliffs pierced by caves in hard quartzite, found in 1997 during a survey before a golf estate was built above them.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Cave 13B produced shellfish middens dated to about 164,000 years ago, the earliest firm evidence anywhere that people gathered food from the shore, with ground red ochre and bladelets too small to hold in the hand.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Silcrete was heated there until it fractured like flint, and Curtis Marean has argued that the Cape coast served as a refuge during a cold, dry glacial period, though geneticists who find no sign of a severe bottleneck remain unconvinced.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>",
"Behavioural_modernity": "Behavioural modernity is the bundle of habits taken to mark a mind like our own, not merely a body like it: art, ornament, burial, music, and above all symbols and language.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> None leaves a fossil of thought, so each is read as a proxy, a pierced bead proof of intended meaning; the idea took shape as excavators saw such things appear abruptly in Europe after about 45,000 years ago.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> That suddenness is now widely doubted: nearly every item turns up in African sites tens of thousands of years earlier, Neanderthals used pigment and pierced shells in Spain about 115,000 years ago, and critics urge dropping it for what people did.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>",
"Madjedbebe": "Madjedbebe is a sandstone rock shelter at the foot of the Arnhem Land escarpment in northern Australia, on Mirarr country, last dug under an agreement giving the Mirarr control of it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Sand grains from its lowest band of artefacts, dated by luminescence, put first occupation at about 65,000 years ago, 5,000 to 15,000 years earlier than any other Australian site; the same layer holds the earliest known ground-edge hatchet heads.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The date is contested — termites have worked the loose sand and artefacts could have sunk into it — but charred pandanus nuts and yams show what was eaten there, so it records a way of living, not one visit.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>",
"Lake_Mungo_remains": "The Lake Mungo remains are two burials from a dune in western New South Wales, in Mutthi Mutthi, Barkindji and Ngiyampaa country: a woman, burnt and her crushed bones buried in a pit, found in 1968, and a man laid under red ochre, found in 1974.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Their age was settled in 2003, leaving the ochre burial the oldest ritual burial known, though her burning is incomplete and no pyre survives, so specialists now hedge the word 'cremation'.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> A 2001 study reporting a mitochondrial lineage carried by nobody living was shown in 2016 to rest on contamination; both ancestors were returned to their Traditional Owners, reburial approved in 2022.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>",
"Settlement_of_the_Americas": "The settlement of the Americas was our species' last great continental expansion, carrying people from north-eastern Asia into two continents no human had set foot on; their ancestors separated from their East Asian relatives deep in the Ice Age.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The road ran through Beringia, the grassland plain joining Siberia to Alaska when glacial seas fell, and genetics suggests they were held there for thousands of years while the ice sheets sealed the way south.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The old view that Clovis hunters were first has collapsed, and fossil footprints at White Sands push it back to between 23,000 and 21,000 years ago; the earliest dates are contested, the Asian origin is not.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>",
"Paleo-Indians": "The Paleo-Indians were the first peoples of the Americas, hunters and foragers who spread through two continents as the Ice Age closed; Frank H. H. Roberts Jr. used the name in 1940 without defining it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Their signature is the fluted point, a spear tip with a channel flake struck from the base up each face to sit in a bone or ivory socket, the best known the Clovis point of about 13,050 to 12,750 years ago.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> They moved in small bands, carrying little, yet worked stone from hundreds of kilometres away, and within a few thousand years the mammoth and giant bison were gone, from hunting or climate or both.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>",
"Cro-Magnon": "Cro-Magnon is the name long given to the early modern humans of Ice Age Europe, after a rock shelter in the French Vézère valley where roadworkers struck human remains in 1868: four adults and an infant, buried with pierced shells and antler.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The name is local and plain, a <i>cros</i> in the local Occitan being 'a hollow' or 'a cave', and the find mattered because it placed people who look like living Europeans among Ice Age animals.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> A pierced shell from the burials is radiocarbon dated to the early Gravettian, and the word has left the literature, which prefers European early modern humans and keeps Cro-Magnon for the shelter.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>",
"Châtelperronian": "The Châtelperronian is a stone tool industry of France and northern Spain, made as the Middle Palaeolithic gave way to the Upper; Dorothy Garrod named it in 1938.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Its signature tool is the Châtelperron knife, a blade blunted down one curved edge so it could be gripped or hafted safely, with bone awls and pierced teeth beside it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The quarrel is about who made it: a Neanderthal skeleton at Saint-Césaire lay in a Châtelperronian level and proteins from the Grotte du Renne were read as archaic in 2016, but that association was challenged in 2018, the Renne layers look churned, and a modern newborn was found there in 2023.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>",
"Lion-man": "The Lion-man is a standing figure with a lion's head, cut from a single mammoth tusk, found in August 1939 in the Stadel cave of the Hohlenstein cliff in southern Germany, on the dig's last day.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The boxed splinters lay unstudied until Joachim Hahn opened them in 1969 and fitted some 200 pieces together; later fragments and two rebuilds brought it to 31.1 centimetres (12.2 inches).<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Whoever carved it — an experiment with flint tools of the period ran to hundreds of hours — was making neither a lion nor a person but something that never walked the earth, and no older sculpture of an imagined creature is known.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>",
"Hohle_Fels": "Hohle Fels is a cave in the Ach valley of the Swabian Jura in southern Germany; its name means 'hollow rock', and its chamber is among the largest in the region.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Diggers opened it in 1870, and its floor holds Aurignacian, Gravettian and Magdalenian layers, so people returned to the shelter across tens of thousands of years.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> What made it famous lies at the bottom of that sequence: a woman about 6 centimetres (2.4 inches) tall carved from mammoth ivory, and a flute cut from a griffon vulture's wing bone — with those from neighbouring caves the oldest known anywhere, and the reason it joined the World Heritage List in 2017.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>",
"Venus_of_Hohle_Fels": "The Venus of Hohle Fels is a woman carved from mammoth ivory, 6 centimetres (2.4 inches) tall and barely 33 grams (1.2 ounces), the oldest undisputed image of a human being anywhere.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> She has no head: in its place sits a small carved ring polished by wear, so the figure hung from a cord, while everything else is emphasised — heavy breasts, broad belly, wide hips and a carefully cut vulva.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> She is at least 35,000 years old and, on the current dating of the Swabian Aurignacian, probably closer to 40,000; what her maker meant is unknown, the readings running from fertility figure to lucky charm to a woman's view of her own body.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>",
"Divje_Babe_flute": "The Divje Babe flute is a bone about 11 centimetres (4.3 inches) long, from a cave bear cub's thigh, with two clean round holes in a line, lifted in 1995 from a Mousterian layer in western Slovenia.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> If the holes were cut deliberately it is an instrument older than the Swabian ivory and bird-bone flutes, and music was not something modern humans brought: in Europe at that date there were Neanderthals and nobody else.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The trouble is that a carnivore's canine punches a neat round hole through thin bone: sceptics call it a gnawed bone, its finders answer that no bite aligns holes down a shaft, and the field has not settled.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>",
"Venus_figurines": "The Venus figurines are small carvings of women made across Ice Age Europe and Siberia, over some 6,000 kilometres (3,700 miles) from the French Pyrenees to Lake Baikal.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> They were cut from mammoth ivory, limestone, steatite, antler and, at one Moravian site, fired clay, and the strange thing is that they were carved alike across that whole distance: breasts, belly, hips and thighs swollen, arms thin or missing, head small and faceless.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The exceptions are the most interesting: the oldest, a little ivory woman from Hohle Fels in Germany, predates the main series by several thousand years, while the Mal'ta figures in Siberia are slender rather than heavy.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>",
"Venus_of_Willendorf": "The Venus of Willendorf is a woman 11.1 centimetres (4.4 inches) tall, cut from oolitic limestone and still tinted with red ochre that probably once covered her, found in 1908 above the Danube in Lower Austria.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Her arms are thin and laid across heavy breasts, her belly and hips enormous, the head wrapped in bands that leave no face — read as braided hair, a woven cap or a netted snood, and so as a picture of a lost textile.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The stone does not occur near Willendorf: a scan of her interior in 2022 found its closest match by Lake Garda, some 730 kilometres (450 miles) on the far side of the Alps.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>",
"Dolní_Věstonice": "Dolní Věstonice is one of a group of open-air Pavlovian camps below the Pavlov Hills in Moravia, a Moravian Gravettian known for elaborate burials and, uniquely for their age, fired ceramics.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> More than 12,000 ceramic figurines and fragments have come from them, among the oldest anywhere, made from the loess underfoot; many are cracked by thermal shock, so the firing and exploding of the figures may have been the point.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The dead are as striking, above all a triple burial of three young people under ochre and burnt spruce; whether the middle one was a woman was argued for decades, until DNA in 2016 showed all three male.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>",
"Mal'ta-Buret'_culture": "The Mal'ta-Buret' culture is named for two Upper Palaeolithic camps on the rivers west of Lake Baikal in south-central Siberia, dug by Mikhail Gerasimov from 1928.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Its carvers made about thirty small female figures from mammoth ivory, slender rather than heavy and marked in a way that looks like fitted fur clothing, along with birds with their wings out as though in flight.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Its fame rests on a Mal'ta grave holding a boy of about four: his genome, published in 2013, showed him related to the ancestors of western Eurasians rather than East Asians, and shared with living Native Americans, who draw much of their ancestry from people like him.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>",
"Microlith": "A microlith is a small retouched stone tool struck from a slender bladelet, with no agreed size ceiling and published limits from about 25 to 50 millimetres (1 to 2 inches).<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The smallness is the point, because a tool this size is useless in the hand: it sat in a groove cut along a shaft of wood, bone or antler, fixed with tar, several working as one edge; knappers sort them into backed forms and geometric ones — triangles, crescents, trapezes.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Built this way a weapon is repairable in the field, where a large point that snaps is finished, and it stretches scarce good stone, one blank serving as barb, tip or cutting edge.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>",
"Spear-thrower": "A spear-thrower is a rod or board whose hook engages a light spear's butt and extends the arm, so whipping it forward sends the hook through a longer arc than the hand could.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> That leverage becomes speed at the tip: controlled tests give darts launched this way roughly two-thirds more velocity than hand-thrown javelins, and one study found it evens the gap between men's and women's throwing speeds.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The device is older than its names, atlatl from Nahuatl and woomera from Australia, both in use when Europeans arrived; the oldest definite hooks are about 20,000 years old, though impact fractures have been read as use some 31,000 years ago.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>",
"Bow_and_arrow": "A bow and arrow stores muscular effort and releases it at once: the archer bends a springy stave and the string drives that energy into a shaft faster than an arm could throw.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Because the effort is stored, a bow can be drawn from cover or held at full draw while the target walks into range, arrows light enough to carry by the dozen.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The weapon almost never survives: the oldest complete bows are only about 10,000 years old and the earliest evidence indirect: bone arrow points in Sri Lanka about 48,000 years old, impact damage in France at 54,000, and stone segments in southern Africa about 64,000, read tentatively.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>",
"Cave_painting": "A cave painting is an image on a cave or shelter wall or ceiling, one form of parietal art; the palette is narrow and earthy, reds and yellows iron oxides, blacks manganese or charcoal.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Painters spread the colour most often with the hand itself, and with brushes, swabs and stencils, and by blowing a mist of pigment, which around a hand held against the rock leaves the pale hand shapes found on three continents.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Large game dominates, people being rare and sketchy, and the oldest securely dated are Indonesian, a warty pig at least 45,500 years old and a narrative scene at 51,200; Chauvet was drawn around 36,000 years ago.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>",
"Cosquer_Cave": "Cosquer Cave opens in the Calanque de Morgiou near Marseille, its mouth 37 metres (121 feet) below the Mediterranean; at the height of the last ice age the sea stood about 120 metres (390 feet) lower and the painters simply walked in.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Henri Cosquer dived into it in 1985 and the cave became public in 1991; radiocarbon on its charcoal falls into two clusters of visits between roughly 33,000 and 20,000 years ago.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Of its nearly 500 figures, 65 are hand stencils, most missing a finger or more; what makes it unlike any other painted cave is the sea in it — seals, fish, jellyfish and great auks among the horses, bison and ibex.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>",
"Quaternary_extinction_event": "The Quaternary extinction event is the loss of most of the world's very large land animals, and it selected by size: almost everything over about 44 kilograms (97 pounds) was at risk while small animals came through largely untouched.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The damage was very unevenly spread, from about a fifth of Africa's large genera to nearly nine in ten in Australia, a gradient that tracks how long each continent's animals had lived alongside hominins.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Two explanations have been argued over for decades: pressure from arriving modern humans and the violent climate swings at the end of the last glacial period, and a 2025 review found the field still divided along disciplinary lines.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>",
"Younger_Dryas": "The Younger Dryas was a sharp return to near-glacial cold across the northern hemisphere, lasting about 1,200 years and named for <i>Dryas octopetala</i>, an arctic flower whose pollen crowds its sediments.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> It struck after nearly two thousand years of warming, central Greenland perhaps 10 to 14 degrees colder than today; the exit was faster, ice cores recording the closing warm-up in 40 to 50 years — the formal start of the Holocene.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The standard explanation is meltwater from the shrinking North American ice sheet, which freshened the North Atlantic and slowed the current carrying warmth north; a comet airburst has been proposed, its claimed markers largely failing replication.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>",
"Clovis_culture": "Clovis is a North American toolmaking tradition of the end of the Ice Age, named after Blackwater Draw near Clovis, New Mexico, and its points turn up across the continent and into Central America.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Its signature is a lance-shaped point with a flute, a channel flake driven off the base up each face — a risky last removal, since a badly struck flute wrecks a point already close to finished.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The startling thing is the timing: redating puts the whole tradition inside three centuries, so it is not the work of the first Americans, and it ends as the Younger Dryas sets in, giving way to the smaller Folsom point.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>",
"Clovis_point": "A <b>Clovis point</b> is a leaf-shaped stone spear tip, worked on both faces and thinned to a shallow lens, with a flute struck from the base and edges ground blunt where it met the shaft.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Knappers chose fine cherts, jaspers and obsidians that flake predictably and set the base into a foreshaft, the flutes thinning it so it could be bound in neatly; the largest known example is a 24.5-centimetre (9.6-inch) agate point from a Washington state cache.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> How it was used is the open question: a spear-thrower dart, a hand-thrown javelin, a close-thrust spear and a braced pike are all on the table, impact damage giving some support to each.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>",
"Folsom_tradition": "The Folsom tradition is a Great Plains and Southwestern hunting culture named for Folsom, New Mexico, where in 1927 a point left in place between an extinct bison's ribs settled a long argument about human antiquity in the Americas.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Its point is small, seldom over 8 centimetres (3 inches), fluted almost from base to tip on both faces, far harder than a shallow flute: an estimated 10 to 60 per cent were ruined in making.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> These were <i>Bison antiquus</i> specialists, taken in traps rather than singly, and at one Oklahoma kill a bison skull painted with a red zigzag lay in the bone bed, the first known Paleoindian art outside a grave.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>",
"Monte_Verde": "Monte Verde is a site on Chinchihuapi Creek in southern Chile, where from 1977 Tom Dillehay and Mario Pino uncovered a settlement sealed under waterlogged peat, so wood, cordage and plants survived.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> They found the frame of a long hide-covered shelter, hearths, digging sticks, chewed medicinal leaves, wild potato, seaweed from the coast, and a human footprint.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Dating put the main layer well before Clovis, at the far end of two continents supposedly entered from the north, so it was resisted until a panel of archaeologists accepted it in 1997; a 2026 paper argues the deposit is thousands of years younger, and the matter is open.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>",
"Meadowcroft_Rockshelter": "Meadowcroft Rockshelter is a sandstone overhang above Cross Creek in Pennsylvania, dug from 1973 by James Adovasio in a famously meticulous excavation separating eleven layers from a sterile base to historic times.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Its deepest cultural layer is dated by the excavators to about 14,000 to 14,500 radiocarbon years ago, well before any Clovis point, and held an unfluted lanceolate point and prismatic blades struck from prepared cores.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Their main argument is arithmetic rather than eloquent — more than 50 radiocarbon dates in the order the layers require, which contamination would be unlikely to produce — against the objection that carbon from nearby coal-bearing rock could make them too old.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>",
"Petroglyph": "A petroglyph is an image cut into rock, made by taking stone away rather than adding pigment, and that difference gives the form its extraordinary durability.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The commonest technique is pecking, striking the surface repeatedly with a hammerstone to leave a broad line of fresh, pale stone, and in arid country the effect depends on desert varnish, a thin dark skin of clay and manganese, so a new cut reads bright against a near-black ground.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Dating them absolutely is hard, because varnish does not thicken at a steady rate and no laboratory method works across every rock type, so a credible age rests on several converging arguments rather than one.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>",
"Control_of_fire": "The control of fire covers three achievements: using a blaze nature lit, keeping one alive, and kindling one at will, each harder than the last.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Scorched ground proves nothing, since wildfires leave much the same residue: a case needs burning that happened where it lies, deep in a cave or in one patch; the oldest widely accepted traces are about a million years old, fire becoming routine only around 400,000 years ago.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Even those show fire used, not made, and the oldest proof of deliberate ignition was long hand axes struck with pyrite some 50,000 years ago, until a 2025 report from Barnham pushed it to about 400,000.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>",
"Epipaleolithic": "The Epipaleolithic is the last stretch of the Palaeolithic in the Levant and the wider Near East; its name is built on the Greek <i>epi</i>, meaning 'upon', the age laid on top of the Old Stone Age rather than a break from it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Archaeologists reach for the name where the retreat of the ice altered the landscape comparatively little and farming followed too closely for any long middle age.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Its late phase, the Natufian, is why the period matters: people cut curved houses into hillsides, harvested wild cereals with flint-bladed sickles, ground and stored them, kept the dog, and baked the oldest known bread some 4,000 years before farming.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>",
"Nordic_Stone_Age": "The Nordic Stone Age is the Stone Age of Scandinavia, late at both ends because the region spent the last glaciation under ice, settlement beginning only as the Weichselian ice sheet withdrew.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The first arrivals were reindeer hunters following the ice north, and the Mesolithic runs through three Danish cultures — Maglemose, Kongemose, Ertebølle — whose people fished, gathered oysters and made pottery while still hunting.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Farming arrives late and abruptly with the Funnelbeaker culture around 4000 BCE, whose people raised the dolmens and passage graves, and it ends with a dagger period from about 2350 BCE, Jutland's knappers working stone more finely than ever just before giving it up.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>",
"Preboreal": "The Preboreal is the first Holocene chronozone of northern Europe, the cool centuries after the Younger Dryas; it belongs to a regional scheme rather than the global timescale — the Blytt–Sernander sequence read from Danish and Scandinavian peat bogs.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Jan Mangerud and colleagues made those phases datable chronozones in 1974 by pinning radiocarbon boundaries to them, setting the Preboreal between 10,000 and 9,000 radiocarbon years before present.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> In the pollen diagrams it shows birch woodland spreading fast over open ground, with pine gathering strength towards the end; the phases run at different times in different places, and those who codified them warn that the scheme works only regionally or locally.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>",
"Boreal": "The Boreal is the second Holocene chronozone of northern Europe, following the Preboreal in the Blytt–Sernander sequence read from Scandinavian peat.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Mangerud and colleagues set it between 9,000 and 8,000 radiocarbon years before the present, which calibrates into the early Holocene warming — so it does not reach down to 8,000 calendar years ago, as loose usage suggests.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> In the pollen record it opens with the rise of hazel, one of the sharpest lines in a European diagram, with oak, elm and lime following while pine held the drier ground; waterlogged Mesolithic camps at Lake Duvensee in Germany preserve bark mats and hearths connected with roasting its nuts.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>",
"Atlantic_period": "The Atlantic period is the third Holocene chronozone of northern Europe and the warmest and wettest of the Blytt–Sernander phases; its name records that wetness, not the ocean.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Mangerud and colleagues fixed it at 8,000 to 5,000 radiocarbon years before present, summers in northern Europe running about one and a half degrees warmer than today.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The woods that answer to that warmth are the mixed wildwood of oak, elm, lime and alder, and what closes the zone is not a temperature but a tree: elm pollen falls sharply about 5,000 years ago, and whether disease, farmers' clearance or wetter ground was the cause is still argued.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>",
"Blytt–Sernander_sequence": "The Blytt–Sernander sequence is a regional scheme of climate phases dividing the northern European Holocene, its evidence peat: Scandinavian bogs preserve dark humified bands where the surface dried, paler peat where wet.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Axel Blytt argued in 1876 that such layers recorded a climate swinging between dry and wet spells, Rutger Sernander extended it around 1900 to Preboreal, Boreal, Atlantic, Subboreal and Subatlantic, and Jan Mangerud's team dated the boundaries in 1974.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> It reads as a climate story, yet a bog dries and floods for its own reasons, so the layers are no thermometer; it does not travel beyond the north, and newer methods have overtaken it, though the vocabulary stuck.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>",
"Phaistos": "Phaistos is a Minoan palace on a hill above the plain of the Mesara in southern Crete, occupied almost without a break from the Neolithic into the Hellenistic age.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Its central court is the longest of any Minoan palace at 63 by 22.5 metres (207 by 74 feet), with a paved west court before the main façade, though its excavators stress the court was never closed on four sides.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> The Italian Archaeological Mission began digging in 1900, and campaigns by Luigi Pernier, Doro Levi and the University of Catania followed; much of the first palace is still visible because the New Palace's west façade was set east of the old one.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>",
"Malia": "Malia is a Minoan palace and the town about it on the north coast of Crete, settled from the Early Minoan II period, long before its first palace.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Its central court measures 48 by 23 metres (157 by 75 feet) and the palace some 7,600 square metres (81,800 square feet), more of its ground floor given to storage than any other Cretan palace, while the court carries a central pit and two cup-marked stones set for ceremony.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Joseph Hazzidakis uncovered the palace in 1915 and the French School at Athens has dug there since 1921, opening the Mu Quarter, a workshop complex 300 metres (980 feet) west that a fire sealed with its contents inside.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>",
"Zakros": "Zakros is a Minoan palace and harbour town at the eastern end of Crete, on a delta below a gorge some 8 kilometres (5 miles) long, beside a natural port.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> It is the smallest of the Cretan palaces at some 2,800 square metres (30,000 square feet), with no major magazines and a central court of 29 by 12 metres (95 by 39 feet) aligned north-east to south-west rather than north to south.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Its port was reckoned the last on the directest sea route from the Aegean to North Africa, copper reached it as oxhide ingots, and a house on the shore held nearly 500 clay sealings and a tablet in the script of Knossos.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>",
"Gournia": "Gournia is a Minoan town on the north coast of the Isthmus of Ierapetra in eastern Crete, and the most extensively cleared town of the Neopalatial period on the island.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Some fifty well-preserved houses stand along cobbled streets about a central court and a small palace whose principal façade is built of cut poros limestone, with a one-room shrine near the summit and a cemetery beyond.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Harriet Boyd found the site in May 1901 and cleared it in three seasons, and work on the shore below has since identified a monumental shipshed, fortification walls with towers and a cobbled street running up to the town.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>",
"Protopalatial_period": "The Protopalatial period is the age of the first Cretan palaces, from Middle Minoan IB to the end of Middle Minoan IIB, about 1900 to 1750 BCE, though the figures are given variously.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The first certain palaces rise at Knossos and Phaistos, potters take up the fast wheel and thin their walls to eggshell Kamares ware, a hieroglyphic script and then Linear A come into use, and hilltops and caves first show signs of cult.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Why palaces appeared is argued rather than settled, read variously as redistribution, élite coercion, mutual insurance against bad years, or the wealth of trade; it closes in a destruction horizon, probably an earthquake.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>",
"Neopalatial_period": "The Neopalatial period is the age of the second Cretan palaces, from Middle Minoan IIIA to the end of Late Minoan IB, about 1750 to 1470 BCE, though the figures are given variously.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> New palaces rise over the ruins of the old at Knossos, Phaistos and Malia, the villa appears as a new class of building, and pier-and-door partitions, lustral basins and paired halls recur across the island.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Minoan pottery, Linear A, frescoes and those same architectural forms spread through the Aegean, most heavily on Thera, Melos and Kea, and the age closes in the Late Minoan IB destructions that took nearly every Minoan site.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>",
"Pithos": "A pithos is a large ceramic storage jar of the Aegean world, standing in rows in the magazines of the Bronze Age Cretan palaces to hold oil, wine and grain.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> At Knossos thirteen such magazines open west off a paved gallery 53 metres (174 feet) long, and the jars stood thickly in them: over twenty were counted in one, nine in a row along the wall of another, and burnt grain survived inside some.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Rectangular cists lined with lead sheeting are sunk into the paved floors beneath them, in one magazine a second range under the first, and Arthur Evans, finding them empty, took them for hiding places for valuables.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>",
"Minoan_palace_economy": "The Minoan palace economy is what the Bronze Age Cretan palaces did with goods: gathering staples in bulk, recording them, organising craftsmen and bringing in what the island could not supply.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Magazines take up much of every palace's ground floor, copper came ashore as oxhide ingots of some 20 kilograms (45 pounds) cast from Cypriot ore, and gold, silver, ivory, tin and spices for perfumed oils were imported.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The accounting survives in clay, nearly 500 sealed nodules in one house at Zakros and a file of inscribed tablets on a magazine floor at Knossos, while workshops for seal-cutting, potting and smelting stood in the town at Malia.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>",
"Cretan_hieroglyphs": "Cretan hieroglyphic is a pictorial script of Bronze Age Crete, appearing in Middle Minoan IA about 2050 to 2000 BCE and running on into Middle Minoan IIIB.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Its earliest examples are cut on seal-stones of three or four sides, later texts appear on clay bars, medallions, cones and sealings, and the surviving inscriptions are few and very short, coming from Knossos, Phaistos and Malia.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Arthur Evans set out a signary of 135 signs in 1909, 45 of them known only from seal-stones and 43 only from clay; the direction of writing is not fixed, and beyond its decimal numerals the script is undeciphered.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>",
"Linear_A": "Linear A is the writing of the Bronze Age Cretan palaces, appearing by Middle Minoan IIA and found in no inscription later than Late Minoan I, which closes about 1470 BCE.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> It reads from left to right, never appears on seals, and is written most often on clay tablets but also on stone libation tables, gold and silver pins, bronze ingots and, once, in cuttlefish ink inside a cup.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Arthur Evans set Crete's linear writing out as Classes A and B in 1909, and because 45 of Linear B's syllabic signs have close equivalents here many signs can be given a sound, though the language stays unread.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>",
"Phaistos_Disc": "The Phaistos Disc is a fired clay disc about 16 centimetres (6.3 inches) across, stamped on both faces with pictographic signs and brought to light at Phaistos in Crete in July 1908.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Every character was pressed into the wet clay with a punch, one stamp for each variety of sign, so that 45 stamps produced all 241 characters, set in 61 groups that coil outward from the centre of each face.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Its context is Middle Minoan IIIB, about 1700 BCE; those 45 signs suggest a syllabary rather than an alphabet, it has no close parallel on Crete and is probably an import, and it remains undeciphered.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>",
"Minoan_frescoes": "Minoan frescoes are the wall paintings of Bronze Age Crete, and every known figured example is of Neopalatial date, roughly 1750 to 1470 BCE.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Most of the corpus comes from Knossos and Ayia Triada, the subjects running to bull-leaping, boxing, processions, heraldic griffins and scenes of nature with cats, monkeys and flowers, and men are painted red where women are painted white.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Relief was modelled physically in the plaster rather than suggested, the Cup-bearer preserves a life-sized youth carrying a silver cup, and much of what stands at Knossos today is Arthur Evans's restoration, called extensive and controversial by the museum holding his archive.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>",
"Bull-leaping_fresco": "The bull-leaping fresco is a Minoan wall painting from Knossos, uncovered in 1901, showing acrobats vaulting and somersaulting over galloping bulls.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Arthur Evans reassembled parts of two bulls from the broken stucco, each about a quarter of natural size and drawn much larger than the human figures, with one performer somersaulting over a bull's back and another gripping the horns of a charging animal as they pass under her armpits.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Every performer wears the same loin-cloth, girdle, striped socks and slippers, and Evans read the white-skinned figures as women on the convention, general in Minoan painting, that men are red and women white.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>",
"Snake_Goddess_figurines": "The Snake Goddess figurines are faience figures from the Temple Repositories at Knossos, stone cists sunk under a floor and filled with Middle Minoan III material, opened in 1903.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The larger stands 34.2 centimetres (13.5 inches) as reconstituted, in a tiara, embroidered jacket and flounced skirt, with three snakes coiling about her and one held out in her right hand, while a smaller votary had lost her head and left arm.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> How much is ancient is part of the object, since the left forearm and one snake's head are restorations; the figures are usually taken for a household divinity, though telling a Minoan goddess from a worshipper is seldom straightforward.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>",
"Kamares_ware": "Kamares ware is the fine polychrome pottery of the first Cretan palaces, made between about 2000 and 1750 BCE and named after the cave above the village of Kamares on Mount Ida where it was first found.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> It arrives with the fast potter's wheel and with walls thinned until the best pieces are called eggshell ware, decorated light on dark in white, red, orange and yellow on a black ground.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Some pieces imitate metal vessels down to the bosses and the way the handles are attached, others carry clay applied as grapes, leaves or ears of corn, and the richest assortment comes from the palace at Phaistos.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>",
"Marine_Style": "Marine Style is a class of Cretan pottery of the Late Bronze Age, painted with densely packed sea creatures drawn as though from life: the murex, the argonaut, the octopus, the dolphin, seaweed, the sea anemone and the star.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> It is confined to a narrow range of shapes, conical and piriform rhyta, bridge-spouted jugs and stirrup jars, with very few cups, and since rhyta are fillers it has been suggested the vases were made for religious use.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> On one vessel dug at Phaistos the corals interlace their branches and the argonaut stretches out arms covered with suckers, sketched freehand in sepia, one motive repeated with slight variations.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>",
"Minoan_sealstone": "A Minoan sealstone is a small portable object of Bronze Age Crete, mostly of stone but also of bone, ivory, metal or artificial paste, engraved with a design and made to be worn on the body.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Pressed into clay it secured a container or a door, marked what an object held or who owned it, or authorised a dealing on a free-standing lump; it was ornament, amulet and badge of rank besides.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The early Cretan pieces are of soft stone or ivory and include the three-sided prism, whose flat faces took lines of hieroglyphic writing; the bossed lentoid and the signet ring's bezel displaced them, and bold pictures replaced calligraphy.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>",
"Peak_sanctuary": "A peak sanctuary is a Cretan Bronze Age cult place at or just below the top of a prominent local hill, recognised by deep layers of ash and great quantities of clay human and animal figurines.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The ash holds no animal bones and is read as bonfire debris rather than blood sacrifice, metal objects are exceptional and pottery scarce, and many of the human figurines are single arms, legs or heads pierced for suspension.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Of some fifty hilltops claimed as such at least 25 are generally accepted, none with evidence of cult before Middle Minoan I, and their appearance is tied to the rise of the first palaces.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>",
"Cretan_cult_cave": "A Cretan cult cave is a cave on Crete used for worship during the Bronze Age, the best known being Amnisos, the Idaean cave, Kamares, Psychro, Skoteino and Stavromyti.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Caves on the island were lived in during the Neolithic and then used widely as burial places, and only early in the Middle Minoan period do they show unambiguous signs of cult, at about the time the first palaces were built.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> What they contain is pottery, animal figurines and now and then bronze work, and several hold quantities of animal bones, mostly deer, ox and goat, left from some form of sacrifice.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>",
"Horns_of_consecration": "Horns of consecration are a piece of Minoan ritual furniture, a base or impost ending at each side in a horn-like projection, at times shaped like real ox horns and more often a conventional imitation of them.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> They survive as objects of stone or terracotta, often stuccoed, and are painted or carved on murals, altars, vases, seals and larnakes, serving either as stands for a narrow range of cult implements or as crowning members on altars and roofs.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> What they originally meant is uncertain: stylised bulls' horns, the crescent moon, the rising sun and an odd form of pot support have all been proposed.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>",
"Labrys": "The labrys is the double axe, the commonest of all Minoan religious symbols, made from the middle of the Early Minoan period onwards.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Some large bronze examples were working tools, but miniatures in gold, silver, lead, steatite and terracotta, and bronze ones too fragile to swing, can only be symbolic; in pictures the axe is usually set in a stone base or between horns of consecration.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The name comes from Plutarch, who reports that the Carian word for a double axe was labrys, and Evans connected it with the Carian Zeus Labraundos and with the sign cut into the pillars at Knossos.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>",
"Minoan_religion": "Minoan religion is the cult practice of Bronze Age Crete, known almost entirely from material remains, since Linear A is undeciphered and no contemporary text about it survives.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The evidence is the places where cult happened, among them caves, hilltop sanctuaries and shrines in towns, palaces and houses, together with pictures of cult on seals, rings, murals and pottery and the furniture used in it, from horns of consecration and double axes to libation jugs and offering tables.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Figures plausibly divine rather than mortal are overwhelmingly female, while Evans read the sacred trees and pillars that recur in the art as aniconic forms the divinity could enter.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>",
"Ayia_Triada_sarcophagus": "The Ayia Triada sarcophagus is a limestone burial chest of the early 14th century BCE from southern Crete, painted on lime plaster with scenes unique in Aegean funerary art.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Burial in Late Minoan III Crete usually meant a chamber tomb with the body on the floor or in a terracotta larnax or pithos, which makes a painted stone chest a rarity.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Its front shows women pouring liquid into a krater between double axes with birds perched on them, the back a bull strapped for sacrifice above a rhyton set to drain the blood into the earth, and each end a chariot of women drawn by wild goats or griffins.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>",
"Larnax": "A larnax is a terracotta chest used to hold the dead in Bronze Age Crete, the commonest container of a Late Minoan burial.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Its form is a wooden household chest translated into painted clay, on four feet under a gabled lid, with drain-holes in the bottom and handles along the sides; its panels carry spirals and marine and Nilotic motifs, and now and then a griffin or a column bearing sacred horns and a double axe.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The narrow capacity suggested to early excavators that these were ossuaries filled once the flesh had gone, but examples have been found holding a skeleton laid on its back with the knees drawn up.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>",
"Mesara_tholos_tomb": "A Mesara tholos tomb is a circular stone burial chamber of southern Crete, 4 to 13 metres (13 to 43 feet) across, walled with unworked fieldstones bonded with clay and built above ground on bedrock.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Its doorway is small, closed by a slab and almost always on the east, its inner wall corbels inward, and whether the larger examples ever carried a complete stone vault has been argued for a century, a full vault now allowed only to the smaller.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Hundreds of inhumations went into most of them with no regard for earlier burials, leaving a confused mass of bones and grave goods, and traces of fires on the floor are read as fumigation.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>",
"Minoan_road": "Minoan roads are the paved ways of Bronze Age Crete, known from the causeways, roadways and cobbled streets that survive at and around the palaces and towns.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> At Knossos a roadway ran north from the palace carrying its own main drain and flanked by a portico of twelve pillars, and where it met a second paved road running west a tower or guard-house faced a massive bastion.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Inside a town the roads are streets: at Palaikastro and Pseira cobbled streets define the blocks of living units, the same arrangement found in contemporary settlements of the Cyclades.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>",
"Minoan_water_management": "Minoan water management is the catching, carrying and draining of water in the palaces and towns of Bronze Age Crete, best preserved at Knossos.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Stone shafts descend from the upper floors to a conduit under flat covering slabs, about a metre (3 feet) high and half that wide, coated inside with cement and large enough to walk along; its first purpose was to carry off surface water from courts and roofs, and terracotta pipes socketed one into the next ran beneath some floors.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Because the conduits were flushed hard by the island's torrential rain, sanitary arrangements such as the latrines at Knossos and Phaistos were tied into the same system.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>",
"Mochlos": "Mochlos is a circular mass of limestone about 180 metres (200 yards) off the north coast of eastern Crete, rising in cliffs on its north side and sloping to sea level on its south, where a Minoan town stood.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The water between island and mainland is very shallow at one point, and the American archaeologist Richard B. Seager, who excavated it from 1908, reasoned that subsidence of the kind seen elsewhere in Crete had once joined the two, giving the town an excellent harbour.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Its cemetery yielded nearly 150 gold ornaments and eleven necklaces along with fine stone vases, which upset the belief that Early Minoan Crete was a poor place.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>",
"Pseira": "Pseira is a barren island about 3 kilometres (2 miles) off the north coast of eastern Crete, its west side a line of cliffs and its east sloping to three sandy coves, on the middle of which a Minoan town stood.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The place had little to offer settlers besides a sheltered harbour open only to the east, and Seager thought the town's prosperity rested on that port.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Its streets and houses cover the top and sides of a rocky point, the level roads floored with natural rock and every side alley running down to the sea by a paved stepway, with heavy Late Minoan I walls built over lighter Middle Minoan work.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>",
"Palaikastro_Kouros": "The Palaikastro Kouros is a Minoan statuette of a young man about 50 centimetres (20 inches) high, recovered in several hundred fragments from an urban sanctuary in eastern Crete and now in the museum at Sitia.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> It is made of several materials at once: a body of hippopotamus ivory on a wooden armature, a head of stone, eyes of rock crystal and thin sheets of gold for the loincloth and sandals.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The figure stands with both fists against the chest and the left foot slightly advanced, and it comes from a Late Minoan IB destruction level of the middle of the 15th century BCE.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>",
"Petras": "Petras is a Minoan settlement on a hill east of modern Siteia in eastern Crete, looking out over Siteia Bay, with a small palatial building at its centre and a town spread around it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Occupation runs from a Late Neolithic settlement on the eastern slope through an extensive Early Minoan I one on the north, and a Late Minoan III wall was later thrown round the site.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> On the neighbouring hill lies a cemetery of house tombs built much as those at Mochlos are, with a rock shelter beside them that held a deep secondary deposit of bones, vases and jewellery.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>",
"Akrotiri": "Akrotiri is a Bronze Age town on the island of Thera, buried by the eruption of the island's volcano and preserved under metres of volcanic debris.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> An earthquake had wrecked much of it years earlier, and the demolition and rebuilding that followed were unfinished when the volcano erupted; no bodies and hardly any portable valuables were found, so its people had warning and left.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Its frescoes survive in larger pieces than those of any other Aegean site and can be placed back on their own walls, so whole painted rooms can be reconstructed, and radiocarbon dates from beetles in its jars run earlier than the pottery's.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>",
"Minoan_eruption": "The Minoan eruption was a Late Bronze Age eruption on the Aegean island of Thera, among the largest of the Holocene, which destroyed the island's middle and left a caldera 10 by 7 kilometres (6 by 4 miles) across.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Its deposits record four phases: a plinian pumice fall, surges thrown up where seawater reached the vent, a coarse ignimbrite 55 metres (180 feet) thick, and a finer, hotter one accompanying the caldera's collapse.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Radiocarbon places it between 1627 and 1600 BCE, and since the caldera was sealed from the sea as it formed, the waves recorded on Crete are attributed to pyroclastic flows running into the water rather than to its collapse.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>",
"Flotilla_fresco": "The Flotilla fresco is a miniature painted frieze from a Bronze Age town on the Aegean island of Thera, running round the upper walls of a single room in the building known as the West House.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Its south wall, 40 centimetres (16 inches) high, carries a fleet of ships passing between two towns; the north wall shows a hilltop ceremony and warriors landing under tower shields and boars'-tusk helmets, and the east wall a river landscape.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> It is among the most complex works of Aegean Bronze Age art to survive nearly whole, and the identity of its ships and warriors and the whereabouts of its towns are all disputed.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>",
"Theran_tephra": "Theran tephra is the ash and pumice thrown out by the Late Bronze Age eruption on the Aegean island of Thera, and the layer by which it is traced across the eastern Mediterranean.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> It left its own island uninhabitable for a century or two, fell most thickly to the south-east, and has been identified in deep-sea cores, in cuttings on Crete and in lake sediments in western Anatolia.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Because it lies in Late Minoan IA levels on Rhodes and Melos as well as Crete, it fixes the eruption well before the Late Minoan IB destructions, and its own date is still argued between about 1600 BCE and the mid-16th century.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>",
"Minoan_trade_with_Egypt": "Minoan trade with Egypt is the exchange of goods between Bronze Age Crete and the Nile valley, known almost entirely from objects found out of place rather than from any written account of it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The earliest contacts were indirect, with Levantine centres standing between the two; the firmest early evidence is Cretan pottery at Kahun and Harageh in levels of the early 19th century BCE.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Because the fine Cretan pottery that travelled was otherwise restricted to the palaces and to mountain and cave sanctuaries, its presence abroad is usually read as gift-giving between ruling classes rather than as an open market.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>",
"Keftiu": "Keftiu is the name Egyptian texts and tomb paintings give to a land and a people beyond the sea, generally identified with Crete and the Aegean islands.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> In Theban tombs of the 18th Dynasty a man of Keftiu personifies the west among the four quarters of the world, dressed in a waistcloth and marked off from the Syrians who stand for the north, and files of such men are shown carrying metal vessels into Egypt.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Several of those vessels are drawn in shapes known from Crete and the Greek mainland, and the name was later attached to the plain straight-sided cup of Minoan pottery.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>",
"Minoan_thalassocracy": "The Minoan thalassocracy is the sea-empire that later Greek writers credited to Minos of Crete, Thucydides naming him the first man known by tradition to have built a navy, to have ruled the Cyclades and to have put down piracy there.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Whether it describes anything in the 2nd millennium BCE is unsettled: if such a power existed it would belong to the Neopalatial period or to the century after it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> A few settlements outside Crete look to have held Cretans and nobody else, but at the sites of deepest Minoan influence the evidence will not show Minoans in charge, and trade alone may account for the rest.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>",
"Minoan_Kythera": "Minoan Kythera is the Cretan settlement on the island lying between Crete and the southern Peloponnese, centred on Kastri on the east coast and probably founded by colonists from Crete around 2000 BCE.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> In the earliest phases of the Late Bronze Age it appears to have held Cretans and nobody else, and its dead were laid in chamber tombs whose form and contents are thoroughly Minoan.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> A peak sanctuary of the Cretan kind stood above the settlement on the hill called Vouno, and it is one of the few places outside Crete to have yielded stone libation vessels inscribed in Linear A.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>",
"Destruction_of_the_Minoan_palaces": "The destruction of the Minoan palaces is the wave of burning that ended the Neopalatial period on Crete around 1490 to 1470 BCE, reaching nearly every settlement of consequence on the island.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The palaces at Mallia, Phaistos and Zakros were never rebuilt as palaces afterwards, and only Knossos went on, under an authority nobody has been able to name.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> What caused it is unsettled, the argument running between human and natural agency with no agreement on which natural disaster is meant, and the burnt levels cannot be lined up neatly across the east, west and centre of the island.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>",
"Mycenaean_Knossos": "Mycenaean Knossos is the name given to the Cretan palace and its territory in the century or so after the other palaces burned, when Knossos alone still functioned as one.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The period runs from Late Minoan II to IIIA2, roughly 1490 to 1375 BCE, and is now called Final Palatial; around the palace lie richly furnished tombs, the Warrior Graves, whose forms and contents have no Cretan precedent and much in common with the Greek mainland.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Whether those buried in them were incoming Mycenaeans or a local elite adopting mainland habits is disputed, and specialists remain divided on it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>",
"Knossos_Linear_B_archive": "The Knossos Linear B archive is the body of inscribed clay tablets recovered from the palace at Knossos, written in an early dialect of Greek and preserved because fire baked the clay hard.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> It has no central archive room: the tablets came from deposits scattered through the building, they are more broken and carry fewer signs than the comparable texts from Pylos, and the deposits need not all belong to one moment.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Some 66 to 75 scribal hands have been counted in it, most keeping to a single commodity, place or group of people, and when the tablets were written remains disputed by a century or more.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>",
"Postpalatial_Crete": "Postpalatial Crete is the island in the centuries after fire gutted the palace at Knossos, about 1385 to 1375 BCE, when no single centre governed it again.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Independent polities appear across the island for the first time since the Late Minoan IB destructions, among them a kingdom in the Mesara seated at Ayia Triada and a second at Chania in the west, where Linear B was still written in the 13th century BCE.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Material culture grows steadily more regional, pottery loses its lustrous dark-on-light decoration in favour of plain wares, and the shrines of the period are small rooms rather than palace sanctuaries.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>",
"Eteocretan": "Eteocretan is the name given to a pre-Greek language of eastern Crete, known from a small number of inscriptions cut in the Greek alphabet between the 6th and the 3rd centuries BCE and found chiefly at Praisos.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The name comes from a Homeric line, quoted by Strabo, that lists Eteocretans among the mingled peoples of the island beside Achaeans, Cydonians, Dorians and Pelasgians, and Strabo took them for its original inhabitants.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The texts are written in Greek letters but are not in Greek, and apart from them nothing in the material remains of Praisos distinguishes it from any other Cretan city.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>",
"Indo-European": "Indo-European is a family of related languages whose branches include Anatolian, Tocharian, Greek, Armenian, Albanian, Indic, Iranian, Baltic, Slavic, Germanic, Italic and Romance, and Celtic.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Its members are held to descend from a common ancestor, Proto-Indo-European, reconstructed from the inherited words the daughter languages share; one open dataset traces those shared words across 160 of them.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Ancient DNA has tied the family's spread to the Yamnaya complex of the steppes north of the Black and Caspian seas, whose people and their descendants carried these languages outward between about 3300 and 1500 BCE.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>",
"Idaean_Cave": "The Idaean Cave is a sanctuary on Mount Ida, the loftiest mountain in Crete, which Strabo places in the widest part of the island with the best of its cities around it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> It was held to be a cave of Zeus: Strabo reports that Minos would go up to it every ninth year and return with written commandments he said were the god's own ordinances.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Worship on the mountain reaches back to the Bronze Age, and votive bronze double axes, the emblem of the Cretan Zeus, have been found in cave sanctuaries of the god here and on Dicte alike.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>",
"Paranthropus": "<i>Paranthropus</i> is an extinct genus of African hominins, the robust australopithecines, whose skulls are built for heavy chewing: flaring cheekbones, a crest along the midline anchoring large jaw muscles, and huge cheek teeth under thick enamel.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Three species are recognised — <i>P. aethiopicus</i> in eastern Africa from about 2.7 million years ago, <i>P. boisei</i> there until roughly 1.2 million years ago, and <i>P. robustus</i> in southern Africa — and the name, given by Robert Broom in 1938, means 'beside man'.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Robust describes the teeth and face rather than the body, and most scholars place the genus on a side branch that led nowhere.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>",
"Ledi-Geraru": "Ledi-Geraru is a research area in the Afar Region of northern Ethiopia whose sediments cover a stretch of time, between roughly 3 and 2 million years ago, that is poorly represented almost everywhere else and correspondingly thin in the fossil record.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Fossils recovered there place the genus <i>Homo</i> at 2.78 and 2.59 million years ago and an <i>Australopithecus</i> not yet assigned to a species at 2.63 million, indicating that the two lived alongside one another in the region before 2.5 million years ago.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Its Bokol Dora 1 locality also yielded flaked stone dated to more than 2.58 million years ago, published in 2019 as the earliest known Oldowan artefacts.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>",
"Nyayanga": "Nyayanga is an archaeological and palaeontological locality on the Homa Peninsula in western Kenya, on the southern shore of Lake Victoria's Kavirondo Gulf, where deposits dated to between about 3.04 and 2.58 million years ago hold one of the oldest known accumulations of Oldowan stone tools.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Excavation has recovered artefacts alongside butchered hippopotamus remains and fossil molars attributed to <i>Paranthropus</i>, the first direct association of that genus with flaked stone.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Which hominin made the tools is unsettled, but most of the stone was carried in from sources up to 13 km (8 miles) off, well beyond the distances over which living primates move raw material.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>",
"Louis_Leakey": "Louis Leakey was a palaeoanthropologist born at a mission station near Nairobi and raised among the Kikuyu, whose career rested on a conviction then out of favour — Europe and Asia being the fashionable candidates — that humankind had originated in Africa.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> From 1931 he worked at Olduvai Gorge in Tanzania, where across more than 30 years he and his wife Mary recovered stone tools and hominin fossils, among them the type specimen of <i>Homo habilis</i>, the species he named in 1964 with Phillip Tobias and John Napier.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> A gifted publicist, he set Jane Goodall, Dian Fossey and Biruté Galdikas to their long studies of wild apes.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>",
"Mary_Leakey": "Mary Leakey, born Mary Nicol, was a British archaeologist and palaeoanthropologist whose fieldwork in Kenya and Tanzania from 1935 produced several defining finds in the study of human origins.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> She came to the subject through drawing — at seventeen she was illustrating finds on an English excavation — and found the 18-million-year-old fossil ape <i>Proconsul africanus</i> on Rusinga Island in 1948 and, at Olduvai Gorge in 1959, the massive skull nicknamed Zinj, now placed in <i>Paranthropus boisei</i>.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The find she valued most came at Laetoli in 1976 and 1977, where her team uncovered hominin footprints set in volcanic ash, left 3.66 million years ago by <i>Australopithecus afarensis</i>.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>",
"Phillip_Tobias": "Phillip Tobias was a South African anatomist who trained first as a physician, qualifying in 1950, and held the chair of anatomy at the University of the Witwatersrand from 1959 until 1990.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Invited by Louis and Mary Leakey in 1959 to describe the cranium they had just excavated at Olduvai Gorge, he began an association with their material that produced full monographs on <i>Australopithecus boisei</i> and on <i>Homo habilis</i>, the species he named in 1964 with Leakey and John Napier.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> He also studied living Khoesan peoples, wrote against theories ranking the intelligence of human races, and opposed apartheid from his student years onward.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>",
"John_Napier": "John Napier was a British anatomist who trained as a surgeon, turned from nerve-injury work to the functional anatomy of hand and foot, and founded Britain's first primatology unit before directing the Smithsonian's primate biology programme.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> His classification of grasping into a precision grip, an object pinched between the fingertips and thumb, and a power grip, clamped against the palm by the folded fingers, was widely adopted and is still applied in robotics.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> He analysed the Olduvai Gorge hand bones for Louis Leakey and, with Leakey and Phillip Tobias, named <i>Homo habilis</i> in 1964 on evidence of manual dexterity and a lowered brain-size threshold for the genus <i>Homo</i>.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>",
"Grahame_Clark": "Grahame Clark was a British prehistorian who spent his career at Cambridge, editing the <i>Proceedings of the Prehistoric Society</i> for 35 years and elected to the Disney Professorship of Archaeology in 1952.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> His excavation of the Mesolithic site of Star Carr in Yorkshire and his <i>Prehistoric Europe: The Economic Basis</i> of 1952 pressed the case that prehistory is best read as economy and ecology — how people fed, clothed and equipped themselves from what grew around them.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> He then attempted a single account of the human past everywhere in <i>World Prehistory</i>, first published in 1961, whose 1969 edition introduced the numbered technological modes still used of stone industries.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>",
"Mode_1": "Mode 1 is the first of the numbered technological modes set out by Grahame Clark in <i>World Prehistory: A New Outline</i> in 1969, a way of comparing stone industries by the technique they demanded rather than by region or period.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> It covers assemblages worked by simple direct percussion, in which a core is struck to detach sharp flakes and the battered choppers, discoids and polyhedrons are left behind, with no shaping of the core to a planned form.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The grade corresponds broadly to the Oldowan, but it is a description of technique and not a date: Mode 1 assemblages persist in eastern Asia long after Mode 2 handaxes appear elsewhere.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>",
"Latin": "Latin is an Indo-European language of the Italic branch, the language of ancient Rome, and the ancestor of the Romance languages — among them French, Italian, Portuguese, Spanish, Catalan and Romanian — which grew out of its later spoken form from the 3rd century CE onward.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> It is heavily inflected: nouns, adjectives and pronouns are marked for five cases, three genders and two numbers, so words can be ordered for emphasis rather than held to a fixed pattern as in English.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Its alphabet was taken from Greek by way of Etruscan, and long after Rome it remained the language of learning, law, government and the Western church.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>",
"Jonny's_Child": "Jonny's Child is the nickname of OH 7, the fossil remains from Olduvai Gorge in Tanzania that serve as the type specimen of <i>Homo habilis</i> — the specimen against which every later claim to the species is measured.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> A team led by Louis and Mary Leakey recovered them between 1960 and 1963, and the nickname records that it was their son Jonathan who found them.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Much of the case for admitting the species to <i>Homo</i> rested on the hand bones among them, its describers weighing manual dexterity above brain size, and the braincase is now reckoned to have held between 729 and 824 millilitres.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>",
"Jonathan_Leakey": "Jonathan Leakey was the eldest son of Louis and Mary Leakey and worked with them at Olduvai Gorge in Tanzania.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> In 1960, near where his mother had found the massive-jawed skull nicknamed Zinj the year before, he uncovered a humanlike jaw that proved more consequential still: it became part of OH 7, the type specimen of <i>Homo habilis</i>, which is nicknamed Jonny's Child after him.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> His father described the species in 1964 with Phillip Tobias and John Napier, calling it 'handy man' on the suspicion that it had made the thousands of stone tools lying in the same deposits.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>",
"Arthur_Keith": "Arthur Keith was an anatomist, born in Aberdeenshire, who taught at the London Hospital Medical College and, with his pupil Martin Flack, first described the sino-atrial node that sets the rhythm of the heart.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> From 1908 he was Conservator of the Hunterian Museum at the Royal College of Surgeons, and the last forty years of his life went to anthropology: <i>The Antiquity of Man</i> of 1915, the presidency of the Royal Anthropological Institute, and a standing through the 1920s as a one-man court of appeal for physical anthropologists.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> He is remembered chiefly for the cerebral Rubicon, the brain size he set as the threshold of the genus <i>Homo</i>.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>",
"Cerebral_Rubicon": "The cerebral Rubicon is the proposal that a fossil belongs in the genus <i>Homo</i> only if its brain exceeded a fixed size — a line Arthur Keith drew at 750 millilitres and glossed as the point at which humanity was reached.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> It was moved as soon as it obstructed a find: describing <i>Homo habilis</i> in 1964, Louis Leakey, Phillip Tobias and John Napier lowered it to 600 millilitres so their small-brained Olduvai fossils could pass, treating hand function and upright walking as the weightier evidence.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Where the genus's boundary should fall has been argued ever since, Bernard Wood and Mark Collard proposing the species be removed altogether.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>",
"Bernard_Wood": "Bernard Wood is a medically trained palaeoanthropologist, University Professor of Human Origins at George Washington University.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> He came to the subject through Michael Day, who arranged his place on Richard Leakey's first expedition to East Rudolf — now Lake Turkana — in 1968, and took a doctorate at London in 1975 on sexual dimorphism in the skeletons of higher primates.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> His work runs to identifying species in the hominin fossil record and reconstructing how they are related, and with Mark Collard he has argued that <i>Homo habilis</i> is too unlike the rest of its genus in its whole way of life to be left within it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>",
"Mark_Collard": "Mark Collard is an archaeologist and biological anthropologist at Simon Fraser University in British Columbia, where he holds the Canada Research Chair in Human Evolutionary Studies and directs the university's Human Evolutionary Studies Program.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> He joined the department in 2007 after posts at University College London, Washington State University and the University of British Columbia, and works on identifying species in the hominin fossil record, reconstructing their relationships, and estimating body mass, stature and age from skeletal remains.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> With Bernard Wood he has argued that <i>Homo habilis</i> differs too far from the rest of the genus in its adaptive profile to remain in it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>",
"Serengeti": "The Serengeti is a region of open plains in northern Tanzania, lying south of the Kenyan border between about two and four degrees south.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The ecosystem is reckoned at some 25,000 km² (9,700 square miles) and is bounded less by any line on a map than by how far its migrating wildebeest travel; Serengeti National Park, proclaimed in 1940, covers 14,700 km² (5,700 square miles) of it and the Ngorongoro Conservation Area a further 8,300.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> How large the migration is has lately been reopened: the figure of 1.3 million animals has stood since the 1970s, while a satellite survey published in 2025 counted fewer than 600,000.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>",
"Ngorongoro_Conservation_Area": "The Ngorongoro Conservation Area is a protected area of about 8,300 km² (3,200 square miles) in northern Tanzania, established in 1959 and making up, with Serengeti National Park, most of the Serengeti ecosystem; it is a UNESCO World Heritage Site and Biosphere Reserve.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> It is a multiple-use area, in which people live among the wildlife rather than outside it: some 87,000 residents in 2020, most of them dependent on livestock and practising transhumant pastoralism, moving herds with the seasons to reach grazing and water.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Divided into eleven administrative wards, it also contains Olduvai Gorge.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>",
"Maasai": "The Maasai are a people of southern Kenya and northern Tanzania whose livelihood centres on herding cattle, sheep and goats.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> They are semi-nomadic, moving with the seasons to manage grazing and water, with temporary settlements in the wet season and more permanent dwellings in the dry, and they speak Maa.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> They are held to have come from what is now Sudan and the lower Nile valley, migrating slowly south along the Rift Valley; among the many peoples of Tanzania they are reckoned indigenous less by descent than by the keeping of a distinct way of life through a long history of political and social marginalisation.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>",
"Proboscidea": "Proboscidea is the order of mammals holding the living elephants together with the mammoths and the many other proboscideans known only as fossils.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> It is one of six orders making up Afrotheria, the African radiation that also produced the manatees, the aardvark, the tenrecs, the hyraxes and the elephant shrews — a grouping proposed on molecular evidence and since firmly supported.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Proboscideans loom large in the human record as well: at Olduvai Gorge the butchery of megafaunal carcasses is scarce in the Oldowan of Bed I and becomes frequent and widespread after 1.8 million years ago, with proboscideans and hippopotamids the animals most often worked.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>",
"Hippopotamus": "The hippopotamus is a large semi-aquatic mammal found through the rivers, shallow lakes and swamps of sub-Saharan Africa, needing water deep enough to submerge in and coming ashore to graze the grassland along the banks.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Adults weigh between 1,300 and 3,200 kg (2,900 and 7,100 pounds) and stand about 150 to 165 cm (59 to 65 inches) high; the skin is thin and almost hairless, without sweat glands, and mucous glands instead spread a thick reddish fluid across it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The familiar picture of an animal resting in water by day and grazing all night has not survived observation: one study found them feeding for only a quarter of the night and as active by day as after dark.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>",
"Sivatherium": "<i>Sivatherium</i> is an extinct genus of the Giraffidae, the family of the living giraffe and okapi, known in Africa from the Miocene onward.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <i>S. hendeyi</i>, described from Langebaanweg on the west coast of South Africa — one of the richest Neogene fossil vertebrate sites on the continent — is the more basal of its African species, and <i>S. maurusium</i> the commoner.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The genus lasted long enough to be met by toolmaking humans: <i>Sivatherium</i> carcasses at the 1.3-million-year-old site of BK in Olduvai Gorge carry the kind of dynamic bone breakage that comes of being cracked open deliberately.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>",
"Paranthropus_boisei": "<i>Paranthropus boisei</i> is an extinct early human of eastern Africa, one of the heavy-jawed <i>Paranthropus</i> species, living between about 2.3 and 1.2 million years ago.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> It has the thickest dental enamel of any known early human, with cheek teeth four times the size of ours behind a dish-shaped face; Mary Leakey found the type specimen, the cranium OH 5, at Olduvai Gorge in 1959, and the Leakeys announced it as <i>Zinjanthropus</i>, which the press turned into Nutcracker Man.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> What it ate is unsettled: the skull is built to crush hard food, yet the microwear on its teeth resembles a fruit-eater's, suggesting the heavy machinery was kept for lean seasons.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>",
"Hamburg_culture": "The Hamburg culture is the earliest of the three Late Palaeolithic cultures into which the first human pioneers of southern Scandinavia are grouped, the others being Bromme and Ahrensburg.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> It flourished in an Arctic tundra landscape during the Bølling warming, its economy built on hunting the reindeer that spread across ground newly clear of ice.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> In the wider taxonomy of Final Palaeolithic Europe it is placed in the Shouldered Point Complex, one of the two great groupings by which archaeologists sort the flint industries of the period, and it is the shape of the points that does the sorting.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>",
"Bromme_culture": "The Bromme culture is the middle of the three Late Palaeolithic cultures into which the first human pioneers of southern Scandinavia are grouped, following the Hamburg culture and preceding the Ahrensburg.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> In the taxonomy of Final Palaeolithic Europe it falls within the Tanged Point Complex, and it is distinguished inside that complex by its large tanged points, where the younger groups of the same tradition made smaller forms.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Its people lived on ground only lately freed of ice, in a region reachable at all only because the ice sheets had drawn back far enough to open it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>",
"Ahrensburg_culture": "The Ahrensburg culture is the last of the three Late Palaeolithic cultures into which the first human pioneers of southern Scandinavia are grouped, dated to roughly 10,700 to 9000 cal BCE.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Its people followed the retreating ice north to hunt reindeer and seal, and the retreat set the limits of where they could go: an ice sheet blocked any passage between the Oslofjord and Sweden until about 9000 cal BCE, so reindeer reached western Sweden from Norway only after that.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Within the Tanged Point Complex it belongs among the younger groups, those making the smaller point forms.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>",
"Nordic_Bronze_Age": "The Nordic Bronze Age is the Bronze Age of southern Scandinavia, opening about 1700 BCE as the Late Neolithic closed.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> It rested wholly on imported metal, since the region's own copper deposits were not worked before the Middle Ages: bronze reached it along trade routes that shifted repeatedly between about 2100 and 500 BCE, the earliest arrivals coming through central German hubs from the Austrian Inn valley and Slovakia, and the age reached its height before 1300 BCE.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Cremation was introduced during it, and the change can be tracked region by region through the isotopes locked in burnt bone.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>",
"Funnelbeaker_culture": "The Funnelbeaker culture is the first farming culture of southern Scandinavia, and its appearance from about 3900 BCE marks the advent of agriculture in Denmark.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> It arrived among people who had lived for millennia by fishing, hunting and gathering shellfish — the Danish shell middens hold European oyster shells running from about 5,660 down to 2,600 BCE — and its own final phase, called Store Valby, runs from roughly 3000 to 2600 BCE.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Its megalithic tombs outlasted the culture itself: people of the later Battle Axe culture were still burying their dead in them.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>",
"Pitted_Ware_culture": "The Pitted Ware culture is a Middle Neolithic culture of southern Scandinavia, present in Denmark from about 3100 to 2200 cal BCE and dated in the Swedish material to roughly 3100 to 2700 BCE.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> It was not alone in the region: it overlapped the closing Store Valby phase of the Funnelbeaker culture and, in western Jutland, the Single Grave culture of about 2800 to 2250 BCE, so that several archaeological cultures were present in southern Scandinavia at once.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Its people are known from sites such as Ajvide on the Baltic island of Gotland.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>",
"Dolmen": "A dolmen is a megalithic tomb whose chamber is walled by large upright stones, the orthostats, and roofed by capstones laid across them; megaliths of this kind are the earliest monumental stone architecture in Europe, the first chambers appearing in France in the 5th millennium BCE.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> From the last third of that millennium such graves could be reopened, and that begins a practice new to the whole continent: a tomb built for successive depositions of human remains across centuries.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The reopening long outlived the builders, later peoples burying their own dead in tombs first raised by earlier farmers.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>",
"Single_Grave_culture": "The Single Grave culture is a culture of western Jutland dated to roughly 2800 to 2250 BCE, in the Middle Neolithic B of the southern Scandinavian sequence.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> It was one of several traditions sharing the region and the centuries: its span overlaps that of the Battle Axe culture of Scandinavia and of the continental Corded Ware culture the Battle Axe resembles.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Whether its people also took over the megalithic tombs raised by the earlier farmers is not settled, but a man buried in the Kyndeløse passage grave, radiocarbon-dated within that shared span, carries the steppe ancestry those incoming groups brought, which would suggest they did.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>",
"Bering_Strait": "The Bering Strait is the seaway between Chukotka, at the eastern end of Asia, and western Alaska, carrying Pacific water north into the Arctic Ocean.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> It is shallow — about 53 m (170 feet) — so a moderate fall in sea level closes it altogether, and it has opened and shut repeatedly: a geochemical record of Pacific water reaching the Arctic shows it open from at least 46,000 years ago until the ice sheets grew, and it flooded again around 11,000 calendar years ago.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> It was across the continental shelf laid bare while it stood closed that the ancestors of Native Americans moved out of northern Asia.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>",
"East_Asia": "East Asia is the eastern region of Asia, which the United Nations statistical scheme takes to be China with its Hong Kong and Macao special administrative regions, Japan, Mongolia and the two Koreas.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> It is one of the few parts of the world where writing appears very early: the Chinese script is in use by the end of the 2nd millennium BCE, its first substantial body the oracle bones of the Shang state around 1200 BCE.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Its Palaeolithic has long been read against Europe's across the supposed Movius Line, which set western handaxe traditions apart from simpler eastern industries — a division recent work in northern China has reopened.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>",
"Chronozone": "A chronozone is a formal unit of chronostratigraphy of no fixed rank: the body of rock formed anywhere during the time span of some already designated unit, such as a fossil zone or a magnetic polarity interval; the matching division of time is a chron.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The best-known set in Quaternary work is Mangerud's scheme of 1974, which cut the Holocene of northern Europe by the Blytt–Sernander pollen zones and dated them by radiocarbon: the Atlantic chronozone runs from 8,000 to 5,000 radiocarbon years before present.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Their weakness is built in, the defining vegetation having arrived at different times in different places, so the zones are time-transgressive and hold only regionally.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>",
"Mycenaean_Greece": "Mycenaean Greece is the Late Bronze Age culture of the Greek mainland, named after Mycenae in the Argolid and known equally as Late Helladic, after the mainland's own pottery sequence.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Its rulers were buried first in the shaft graves at Mycenae, whose gold, silver and amber are the richest finds made in the Aegean, and afterwards in corbel-vaulted tholos tombs; from about 1400 BCE they held palaces built around a megaron and walled with unworked boulders.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Their administrators wrote Greek in Linear B, and archives at Mycenae, Tiryns, Pylos, Thebes, Knossos and Chania record a state headed by a <i>wanax</i>, or king; the palaces burned around 1200 BCE.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup>",
"Heinrich_Schliemann": "Heinrich Schliemann was a German merchant and excavator, born at Neubukow in Mecklenburg-Schwerin, who left trade in 1864 and dug at Hisarlik, Mycenae, Tiryns and Orchomenos.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> From April 1870 he drove a trench through the mound at Hisarlik, demolishing later strata to reach the city he expected beneath, and in 1873 cut out a hoard of gold he named Priam's Treasure, half of which the Ottoman government was owed.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> At Mycenae from 1876 he opened the shaft graves and their gold masks and took the dead for Agamemnon and his companions, a claim as mistaken as his Trojan one; he died at Naples in 1890.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>",
"Mycenae": "Mycenae is a citadel above the Argive plain in southern Greece, the site that named the Mycenaean civilisation and which Homer makes Agamemnon's seat and calls rich in gold.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Its walls of great unworked boulders, finished about 1250 BCE, enclose a palace built around a megaron and a circle of much older shaft graves, with nine tholos tombs around them; a destruction level marks the end of its palatial age about 1200 BCE.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> It alone in the Argolid sent men to Thermopylae, for which Argos besieged and razed it in 468 BCE, though the walls and the gate with the lions still stood when Pausanias saw them.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup>",
"Lion_Gate": "The Lion Gate is the main entrance to the citadel of Mycenae in southern Greece, named for the two opposing animals carved in relief and set in the relieving triangle above its door.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> They stand with their forepaws on altars either side of a column carrying a capital and the beam-ends of a roof; their heads are missing, and a study of the tool marks reconstructs them as having turned backward, out of the face of the relief.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> It belongs with the final fortifications of about 1250 BCE, and it has never been lost: Pausanias saw it standing in the ruined wall and put it down to the Cyclopes.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>",
"Grave_Circle_A": "Grave Circle A is a ring of upright stone slabs inside the citadel of Mycenae in southern Greece, enclosing six shaft graves cut into the rock beneath it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The graves were dug about 1600 to 1500 BCE and held gold and silver vessels, weapons, amber, stone vases, seals and gold masks laid over the faces of the dead; the ring above them is a double row of slabs packed with stone and earth, with an entrance facing the Lion Gate.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> It was raised later than the graves it encloses, since it cuts a corner off two of them, and carved stelae and a round altar stood in the fill above.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>",
"Grave_Circle_B": "Grave Circle B is the older of the two shaft-grave cemeteries at Mycenae in southern Greece, lying outside the fortification walls west of the Lion Gate and enclosing fourteen shaft graves.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> It was in use from about 1650 to 1550 BCE and held burials of the Mycenaean elite, among them a man in Grave Gamma wearing a face-mask of electrum, the natural alloy of gold and silver, and a patterned fabric woven in the tapestry technique.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Facial reconstruction of seven of its skulls and ancient DNA typing of its bones have both been used to ask whether those buried together were relatives.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>",
"Mask_of_Agamemnon": "The Mask of Agamemnon is a gold funerary mask found over the face of a man buried in Shaft Grave V of Grave Circle A at Mycenae, and now in the National Archaeological Museum in Athens.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Its eyes are ringed by a double rim and shown closed, and the eyebrows and full beard are raised in relief and worked over with sunk lines, where the beardless mask from the same grave is cut far more plainly.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The graves it comes from were dug about 1600 to 1500 BCE, so the name, which follows Schliemann's reading of the dead as Agamemnon and his companions, is out by some three centuries.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>",
"Shaft_grave": "A shaft grave is a deep rectangular pit cut straight down into rock, with the dead laid at the bottom and the shaft filled in over them.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The form is known above all from the twenty at Mycenae in southern Greece, whose sides stand 3 to 5 metres (10 to 16 feet) high, their walls lined with quarry-stones and clay, the bodies bedded in river pebbles among their ornaments and weapons under a lid of slate slabs carried on heavy beams.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> A limestone stele stood over each one, and the type belongs to about 1650 to 1500 BCE, after which the tholos tomb takes its place.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>",
"Tholos_tomb": "A tholos tomb is a round burial chamber roofed by a corbelled vault, built of stone into a hillside and entered along an open passage called a dromos.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Each course of stone is laid a little further in than the one below until the courses meet overhead, a technique Mycenaean builders carried to spans of eight metres (26 feet) as a matter of course and past fourteen (46 feet) in the largest tombs, behind monumental doorways framed by sawn mouldings.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The earliest are in Messenia and the form spread through the Argolid, Laconia and Attica, serving the uppermost stratum of society between about 1525 and 1275 BCE.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>",
"Treasury_of_Atreus": "The Treasury of Atreus is the biggest and best preserved of the nine tholos tombs at Mycenae in southern Greece, cut into the eastern slope of the hill and popularly called the Tomb of Agamemnon.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> A passage 35 metres (115 feet) long ends at a façade about 14 metres (46 feet) high, where half-columns of dark grey alabaster flank a doorway spanned by a single block of some 120 tonnes (120 tons); the chamber behind is about 15 metres (50 feet) across and as high, in 33 courses closed by one slab.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> It was empty when opened, and its carved façade is now divided between London and Athens.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>",
"Cyclopean_masonry": "Cyclopean masonry is walling built of huge unworked limestone boulders roughly fitted together, with the gaps between them packed out by smaller stones.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> It is the fortification masonry of Late Bronze Age Greece, used at Mycenae, Tiryns, Argos, Krisa and the Athenian acropolis, and it makes walls 3 to 7 metres (10 to 23 feet) thick and locally twice that, the broadest stretches holding galleries within them like casemates, while cut stone is reserved for the gateways.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The name is a Greek explanation rather than a modern one, the stones being held too heavy for men and credited instead to the Cyclopes.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>",
"Tiryns": "Tiryns is a fortified Mycenaean citadel on a low limestone rock in the Argive plain of southern Greece, about 300 metres (328 yards) long and standing 18 metres (59 feet) above the plain.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> A palace occupied its upper terrace, laid out around a forecourt, a megaron with hearth and columns and a bathroom, and its Cyclopean walls, finished about 1250 BCE, survive better than any others, with galleries running inside the broadest stretches.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Homer calls it famed for its walls, and Pausanias, who saw the ruins, found the wall the only part still standing and credited it to the Cyclopes.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>",
"Palace_of_Nestor": "The Palace of Nestor is the Mycenaean palace at Ano Englianos in Messenia, southern Greece, which the ministry that keeps the site calls the best preserved of them all and which takes its name from the old king Homer places at Pylos.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> It is a complex of four main buildings with 105 ground-floor rooms, among them a throne room with a circular hearth ringed by four columns, a room holding a clay bath tub, and storerooms; painted walls ran throughout.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Its archive of 1,107 Linear B tablets is the largest on the mainland, and the palace burned early in the 12th century BCE.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>",
"Mycenaean_Thebes": "Mycenaean Thebes is the palace centre on the Kadmeia, the low citadel of Thebes in Boeotia, Greece, and one of the most important administrative centres of the mainland.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Because the town has been occupied without a break and a modern one stands over the ancient remains, it is known from rescue digs rather than as a plan; the palace of Kadmos lay near the middle of the citadel, its painted annexes covering much of the hill, and it has yielded a Room of the Treasure, an Armoury and inscribed stirrup jars.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Some 250 Linear B tablets came from the Armoury, and the palace burned late in the 13th century BCE.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>",
"Midea": "Midea is the third of the fortified Mycenaean citadels of the Argolid in southern Greece, after Mycenae and Tiryns, ringed by a Cyclopean wall built of somewhat smaller stones than theirs.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Its circuit has an east and a west gate, and on the terraces below stand a megaron with a large off-centre hearth ringed by four columns and a shrine set against the wall, which yielded a miniature tripod offering table and a naturalistic terracotta figure of a woman.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> An earthquake and the fires that followed it ended the Late Helladic IIIB period on the site, and Pausanias found only foundations left.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>",
"Gla": "Gla is a Mycenaean fortress on a low limestone island in the Kopais basin of Boeotia, central Greece, built as an administrative centre and strongpoint guarding the works that drained the lake into farmland.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Its walls are of large limestone slabs rather than the boulders used in the Argolid and went up all at once early in Late Helladic IIIB, and it is unusual in having four major gates at roughly the cardinal points, each reached by a long artificial ramp.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The citadel and the drainage together dwarf any other Mycenaean building project known, and both were destroyed well before the end of the 13th century BCE.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>",
"Megaron": "A megaron is the great hall at the heart of a Mycenaean palace, divided along one axis into a columned porch, a vestibule and a main chamber holding the ruler's throne.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> A large circular hearth stands at the centre of that chamber with four columns set square about it; the floors of all three parts were plastered and painted, frescoes covered the walls, and the throne stood against the middle of one side.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The plan descends from the ordinary house of Early and Middle Helladic Greece rather than from Crete, and a palace commonly holds a second, smaller unit of the same shape.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>",
"Mycenaean_fresco": "A Mycenaean fresco is a wall painting from one of the palaces of Late Bronze Age mainland Greece, and substantial sets survive from Mycenae, Orchomenos, Thebes, Tiryns and Pylos.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Painting ran through the great hall over porch, vestibule and throne room alike, showing processions on a large scale and, on a small one, hunting, preparation for battle, sacrifice and feasting, with lions and wingless griffins as emblems of royal power and sea creatures kept to the floors.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The motifs alter so little across five centuries that a mural cannot be dated by its subject alone.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>",
"Linear_B": "Linear B is the writing of the Mycenaean palaces, a principally syllabic script of some 89 signs for vowels and consonant-vowel combinations, with more than a hundred ideograms beside them.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> It was pressed into unbaked clay and survives because fire hardened the tablets; archives are known from Mycenae, Tiryns, Pylos and Thebes on the Greek mainland and from Knossos and Chania on Crete, most of them dating to about 1200 BCE, and everything written in it that survives is administrative.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Reading it in 1952 showed the language to be Greek, pushing the earliest written Greek back more than 3,000 years.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>",
"Decipherment_of_Linear_B": "The decipherment of Linear B is the reading of the Mycenaean palace script, shown in 1952 by the British architect Michael Ventris to record Greek.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The philologist John Chadwick, then newly appointed to a lectureship at Cambridge, was the first scholar to accept it as correct, and the two published the decipherment in the <i>Journal of Hellenic Studies</i> in 1953.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Confirmation came on 7 April 1955, when Chadwick joined fragments of the horse tablet Ca 895 in the Herakleion Museum and read <i>i-qo</i>, the Mycenaean <i>hikkwos</i>, which is the later Greek <i>hippos</i>, a horse.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>",
"Linear_B_scribe": "A Linear B scribe is one of the writers of the administrative records kept in the palaces of Bronze Age Greece and Crete, and the term claims nothing about their standing: they may have been palace administrators, writers assisting them, or workers whose own job included recording what they produced.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Individual writers are told apart chiefly by palaeographic analysis of their handwriting, and on that basis 1,107 tablets at Pylos are the work of 32 hands and 3,369 at Knossos of 100.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> No tablet describes their work or their training, but the same conventions of spelling and record-keeping hold across every site, so all were trained in one tradition.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>",
"Pylos_tablets": "The Pylos tablets are the Linear B records kept at the Palace of Nestor in Messenia, the largest such archive from the Greek mainland, dated by the burnt destruction contexts in which they lay to the end of the 13th century BCE.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> About 80 per cent lay in the Archives Complex, two small rooms inside the palace's main entrance, the rest scattered through a pottery storeroom, three rooms holding oil and two outlying buildings.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> They record the administration of a kingdom: land tenure above all, with the wanax and the lawagetas among its major landholders, alongside bronze, textile work, dedications and bodies of troops called watchers of the sea.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>",
"Wanax": "The wanax is the ruler at the head of a Mycenaean kingdom, written WA-NA-KA in the Linear B tablets and surviving into the <i>Iliad</i> as an archaic word for a king beside the ordinary <i>basileus</i>.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> He is the greatest landholder in his kingdom, with an estate three times the size of the lawagetas', and the adjective <i>wanakteros</i>, royal, marks a potter, a fuller and jars of wine or oil as belonging to his household.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The title carries a religious charge as well, since the tablets appear in places to use it of a god, though nothing in them makes the ruler himself a priest-king.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>",
"Lawagetas": "The lawagetas is the second man of a Mycenaean kingdom, written ra-wa-ke-ta in Linear B, attested at both Pylos and Knossos and apparently a single figure at either place.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> His title is built from <i>laos</i> and <i>agein</i>, leader of the people, and in the Pylos list of major landholders he stands immediately after the king, with an estate a third the size.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> What the office involved is unresolved: the name and a Germanic parallel suggest a war leader, but no solid evidence from Mycenaean archaeology shows him in that role, and he may have been a crown prince or something else again.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>",
"Qa-si-re-u": "The qa-si-re-u is a minor official of the Mycenaean palaces, attested at Pylos, Knossos and Thebes, who heads work groups and is in the tablets no grander than the chief of a small body of men, in one case a group of bronzesmiths.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> In some contexts he may have had charge of small outlying districts, though on one strict reading the word never appears outside contexts involving craftsmen at all.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Because his standing rested on local communities rather than on the palace, the office outlasted the palatial collapse and rose with it, becoming Homer's <i>basileus</i> and the ordinary later Greek word for a king.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>",
"Damos": "The damos is the local community of a Mycenaean kingdom, written da-mo in Linear B and perhaps best rendered as village, a word that can mean either the people of a community or the land they hold.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> It can allocate landholdings, and the tablets suggest it is nothing more than a group of individual landholders acting as a collective body, though each such community kept a significant degree of independence from the palace it lay within.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Nothing indicates that the word carried in the Bronze Age the political sense it took on in the Archaic and Classical periods.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>",
"Mycenaean_palace_economy": "The Mycenaean palace economy is what the mainland palaces did with goods, and it ran by allocation: managers gathered dispersed raw materials, issued them to specialists who added value through skilled labour, and redistributed the finished work to a restricted group of recipients.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The industries organised in this way were chariots, perfumed oils and textiles, and an allocation of raw material carried the name <i>ta-ra-si-ja</i> in the tablets.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Palatial control was partial rather than total, and at least three spheres of economic influence can be posited within such a state: the palace, the sanctuaries and the local landholding communities.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>",
"Mycenaean_textile_industry": "The Mycenaean textile industry is the largest single operation the Linear B archives record, organised through the <i>ta-ra-si-ja</i>, by which a palace allocated raw material to specialist workers and expected finished cloth in return.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Its workforce was female: at Knossos every woman producer whose duties can be identified is occupied with textiles, largely as corvée labour rather than in slavery, and monthly rations to such groups are recorded.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> A series of tablets sets production targets for central and western Crete covering the cloths <i>te-pa</i>, <i>pa-we-a</i> and <i>tu-na-no</i>, with different targets for groups designated by ethnicity and occupation.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>",
"Mycenaean_land_tenure": "Mycenaean land tenure is the system of holdings recorded in the Linear B tablets, known chiefly from one run of texts at Pylos which set down assessed lands and were used to calculate taxes in kind.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Holdings fall into named kinds, the ruler and the chief officials holding <i>temenoi</i> while <i>ka-ma</i> land differs from these in a way not yet settled, and officials called telestai held theirs in return for service.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Whether the arrangement was feudal, with land held from the ruler for services rendered, has been argued both ways, and one comparison drawn is with the economy of medieval monasteries.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>",
"Mycenaean_religion": "Mycenaean religion is the cult practice of the Late Bronze Age Greek mainland and of Crete under mainland rule, and it is known from texts as well as from remains, since the Linear B tablets name its gods.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Many of those names are Olympian — Poseidon, who is the leading deity at Pylos, Zeus with a female counterpart Diwia, Hera, Artemis and the mistress called Potnia — while Enyalios and Paiawon appear as gods in their own right and survive later only as epithets.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Offerings are bloodless and small, honey, spices and jugs of oil, and the sanctuaries receiving them cannot have been supported by such gifts alone.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>",
"Potnia": "Potnia is a major deity of the Linear B tablets, worshipped at Knossos and at Pylos, whose name is simply the Greek word for mistress.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> She seldom appears without a qualifier: at Knossos she is Potnia of the Labyrinth and the Potnia of Atana, which may be Athena, while at Pylos the additions are mostly place names, together with Hippeia, perhaps meaning of horses.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Whether these leave one goddess with many local faces or several sharing a title is unsettled, and one distinction proposed is grammatical, the unqualified name belonging to a goddess and the qualified one possibly to a queen.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>",
"Mycenaean_chariot": "The Mycenaean chariot is the light horse-drawn vehicle of Late Bronze Age Greece, first attested in the scenes carved on the stelae that stood over the Shaft Graves at Mycenae, where whether the subject is warfare, hunting or races at funeral games is undecided.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Its horses are recorded in Linear B as <i>i-qo</i>, and palace art shows them harnessed to chariots at Tiryns and at Mycenae.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Building and assembling the vehicles was palace work, placing them alongside armour and furniture among industries that supplied the elite with prestige, and the road networks of the Argolid and elsewhere, with their bridges and culverts, were designed to carry such wheeled traffic.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>",
"Dendra_panoply": "The Dendra panoply is a suit of bronze plate armour from a chamber tomb near the village of Dendra in the Argolid, and among the oldest complete suits of armour from the European Bronze Age.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Its plates were edged with leather strips and joined by leather thongs, its head covering imitated a boar's tusk helmet, and the surviving bronze is estimated at about 18 kilograms (40 pounds), a handguard and part of the greaves being lost.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Long thought too cumbersome for anything but ceremony or for a man riding rather than walking, it was tested in 2024 by volunteers who wore a replica through an eleven-hour simulation of Bronze Age combat.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>",
"Boar's_tusk_helmet": "A boar's tusk helmet is the characteristic headgear of Late Bronze Age Aegean warriors, a cap of hide faced with plates cut from the tusks of wild boar.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Homer describes one in the tenth book of the <i>Iliad</i>, wrought of hide and made stiff within by many a tight-stretched thong, with the white teeth of a boar set thick on either side and a lining of felt fixed inside, and he traces its ownership through four hands before it reaches Odysseus.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Warriors wearing them appear in relief on a silver krater from the Shaft Graves at Mycenae and in a miniature fresco at Akrotiri on Thera.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>",
"Figure-of-eight_shield": "The figure-of-eight shield is the waisted body shield of Bronze Age Aegean art, named from the outline made by its two lobes.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Shields on that scale are the ones Homer gives his greatest fighters, built up in hide by a specialist craftsman, reaching to the feet as a defence against javelins and cumbersome enough that a man turning to flee could trip on the rim and be killed as he fell.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The shape became a decorative motif in its own right, painted as a frieze on an upper floor of the palace at Knossos and used on pottery beside the double axe, the sacral knot and the sea anemone.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>",
"Stirrup_jar": "The stirrup jar is the characteristic closed pot of the Late Bronze Age Aegean, a shape originating in Crete and copied widely, which becomes common on the Greek mainland during Late Helladic IIIA and is among the vessels most often found in tombs of that period.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Its coarse-ware version served as a transport container, carrying perfumed oil and probably wine, and most examples were produced in Crete, their clay separating into three main production areas.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Around two hundred carry Linear B painted on before firing, usually a personal name, a place name or both, whose purpose is disputed between marks of gift-giving and records of a Cretan administration.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>",
"Vapheio_cups": "The Vapheio cups are a pair of gold cups recovered from the tholos tomb at Vapheio in Laconia, in southern Greece, and the standard study of them proposed that one was made by a Minoan craftsman and the other in a Mycenaean workshop.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Their straight-sided shape takes its name from the find and derives from Cretan prototypes, being common in the islands as well as on the mainland.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> One of the two carries a hobbled bull, an image often cited to show a preference in Aegean art for the moment immediately following violent action, and paired vessels of this kind have been read as instruments of guest-friendship between rulers.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>",
"Mycenaean_trade": "Mycenaean trade is the movement of goods between the Late Bronze Age Greek mainland and the wider Mediterranean, and what travelled divides into raw materials such as metals, ivory and spices, manufactured goods such as pottery, seals and textiles, and produce such as wine, oil and wool.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Baltic amber reached Greece from about 1725 BCE, almost always as beads or perforated plaques, while Aegean-type pottery reached Italy, where it is known from 103 mainland sites and was also made locally.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Who carried it is disputed, since private traders working outside palace control seem probable, though the pottery reaching Egypt has been read as an organised exchange between courts.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>",
"Uluburun_shipwreck": "The Uluburun shipwreck is a Late Bronze Age merchant vessel that sank off the southern coast of Turkey about 1320 BCE, found by a sponge diver in 1982 and excavated over eleven seasons.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Built of Lebanese cedar, it carried copper and tin ingots in the ratio that makes bronze, together with glass ingots, elephant tusks, ostrich eggshells, gold jewellery and at least 149 jars of terebinth resin.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Analysis of its tin traced two-thirds to the Taurus mountains and a third to Central Asia more than 3,000 kilometres (1,900 miles) away, while the excavator argued that two men of Aegean origin were aboard and the ship bound for the Aegean.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>",
"Ahhiyawa": "Ahhiyawa is a land named in Hittite records of the 14th and 13th centuries BCE, written <i>ah-hi-ya-wa-a</i> in cuneiform, whose kings dealt with the Hittite court and whose people the Hittites met repeatedly on Anatolian soil.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Whether the name denotes the Greek world, and so corresponds to the Achaeans of later Greek, has been argued since the 1920s, with locations proposed from the Greek mainland and the Aegean islands to Cyprus, Rhodes and southern Anatolia.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> It is now almost unanimously identified with an Aegean Late Bronze Age and apparently palatial power, though sceptics note that Achaeans are first attested in Greek only in Homer, which leaves the equation undemonstrable.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>",
"Mycenaean_Greek": "Mycenaean Greek is the language of the Linear B tablets, an early dialect of Greek and the oldest recorded form of the language.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The writing that preserves it was not designed for its sounds, so a single sign covers a family of consonants, final stops go unwritten and aspiration is noted only irregularly, leaving forms such as <i>pa-te</i> to be read from context as either father or all.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Some questions about its sounds therefore cannot be settled from spelling, and where the dialect stands in relation to the later Greek dialects remains under discussion.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>",
"Mycenaean_expansion": "Mycenaean expansion is the spread of mainland Greek material culture and influence through the Aegean islands and along the Anatolian coast after Cretan dominance ended, settlements Minoan in character until about 1500 BCE appearing Mycenaean thereafter at Trianda, Ayia Irini, Phylakopi and elsewhere.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Sites in the Dodecanese, coastal Anatolia, Cyprus, the Levant and the Cyclades were affected to varying degrees, though few if any seem to have been colonies or under mainland control, and the vectors were trade, diplomacy and raiding rather than conquest.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Exported pottery fell away late in the period and then rose sharply at several eastern sites, where small groups from the mainland may have settled.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>",
"Mycenaean_Miletus": "Mycenaean Miletus is the Bronze Age settlement on the Anatolian coast beneath the later Greek city, occupied from about 1700 BCE and Minoan in character before it was Mycenaean, with Minoan pottery, cult practice, wall-painting technique and Linear A inscribed on locally made vessels.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> One phase ended in a destruction whose level contains tephra from the Theran eruption, and the rebuilt town fell again with the destructions that closed Minoan power in the Aegean.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Mainland material dominates thereafter, in pottery, figurines, a Corridor House and Mycenaean burial rites, and the excavator identifies the town of this period with the Millawanda of Hittite records.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>",
"Drainage_of_Lake_Copais": "The drainage of Lake Copais was a Mycenaean land-reclamation scheme in Boeotia, central Greece, that emptied a shallow seasonal lake by leading the two rivers feeding it east to the natural sinkholes at the basin's north-eastern corner.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> A levee some 22 kilometres (14 miles) long, faced with Cyclopean retaining walls and cored with waterproof lake clay, held the combined flow off the reclaimed ground, and an unfinished tunnel at Kephalari was begun to increase the outflow.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Pottery places the work in Late Helladic IIIB, and the lake returned near the end of the Mycenaean period once the sinkholes became plugged, whether by nature or by hand.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>",
"Mycenaean_road_network": "The Mycenaean road network is the system of built roads that joined the citadels of Late Bronze Age Greece, best attested in the Argolid, where it linked the major sites of the Argive plain and ran on beyond them.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> It was carried over broken ground on terraces, took smaller streams beneath the roadbed through stone channels roofed by inward-stepped courses, and crossed ravines on bridges of unwrought limestone boulders laid without mortar, four of which survive near Arkadiko.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> One abutment near Mycenae measures 5 metres (16 feet) across, leaving a usable roadway of about 3.8 metres (12 feet), and two of the Arkadiko bridges are still crossed today for local farm work.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>",
"Isthmian_wall": "The Isthmian wall is a fortification of large unworked stones found on the Isthmus of Corinth in southern Greece, usually dated to about 1200 BCE and read as a barrier drawn across the only land approach to the Peloponnese.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Its cleared stretches run inland from the Saronic Gulf for roughly two kilometres (1.2 miles), each built as two faces of boulders around a rubble core, about four metres (13 feet) thick in all, with small towers projecting from the northern side.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Whether those stretches belong to a single structure, whether all of them are of Bronze Age date, and whether the work was ever carried through to the far coast are all disputed.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>",
"Troy": "Troy is an ancient city on the mound of Hisarlık in north-western Anatolia, known to Greek and Roman writers as Ilios or Ilion and lived on for roughly 4,500 years from about 3000 BCE.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The site is a stack of successive settlements rather than a single city, each raised over the levelled wreckage of the last, and excavators number them from Troy I at the base to the Roman town of Troy IX on the summit.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> It is the city of Greek epic, an identification the Greeks were already making early in the Archaic period, though the size and standing of its Late Bronze Age settlement are disputed.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>",
"Troy_VIIa": "Troy VIIa is the Late Bronze Age settlement built in the ruins of the preceding city at Troy, in north-western Anatolia, reusing its patched fortification circuit and lasting until roughly 1180 BCE.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Its houses are small, sturdy and densely packed, of one to three rooms sharing party walls, standing a single storey high, with large storage jars sunk into pits below their floors and capped with stone slabs.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> It ended in a general fire that consumed buildings inside the citadel and beyond it and left human bones unburied, and it is the layer most often proposed as the city besieged in Greek epic, an identification a substantial minority rejects.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>",
"Lower_city_of_Troy": "The lower city of Troy is the Late Bronze Age settlement that lay outside the fortified citadel at Hisarlık in north-western Anatolia, on the plateau to its south.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Magnetic survey and trial excavation traced a ditch cut into the rock some 400 metres (440 yards) from the citadel, 4 metres (13 feet) wide, running for over 600 metres (660 yards) and broken at one point by a causeway with post-holes for a wooden palisade, with a second ditch beyond it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Its excavators read those works as defences enclosing 25 to 35 hectares (62 to 86 acres) and several thousand people, while critics put the settled area at a third of that and deny the ditches were defensive.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>",
"Dardanelles": "The Dardanelles is the narrow strait in north-western Anatolia joining the Aegean Sea to the Sea of Marmara, and with the Bosporus it forms the only sea passage between the Mediterranean and the Black Sea.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Brackish water flowing out of the Black Sea runs south-west along its surface into the northern Aegean in a volume greater than every river of that sea put together, with saltier Mediterranean water returning beneath.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> That outflow and the winds that prevail from the north-east make the strait hard for a sailing vessel to enter, so ships bound north have waited at its mouth for favourable conditions.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>",
"Wilusa": "Wilusa is a land of western Anatolia known from the Hittite archives at Hattusa, one of the four vassal states of the kingdom of Arzawa and the one occupying the Troad, alongside Mira, the Seha River Land and Hapalla.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Its rulers corresponded with Hittite kings, and a treaty of the 13th century BCE binds one of them, Alaksandu, to Muwattalli II, while a separate letter implies that Hatti and the kingdom called Ahhiyawa had disputed possession of the land.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Because the name answers to Wilios, an older form of Ilios, most scholars identify it with the state whose capital stood at Troy, though no written document has been recovered there.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>",
"Late_Bronze_Age_collapse": "The Late Bronze Age collapse is the sequence of destructions, abandonments and political failures that ended the palace states of the eastern Mediterranean, running from the mid-13th century BCE into the 12th and reaching from Greece to Mesopotamia's borders.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Explanations offered for it include migration, attack from outside, struggles within and between ruling houses, climate change, earthquakes and disease, and none accounts for changes that fell at different times in different regions.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Because several regions came through it intact or even flourished, and long-distance trade declined rather than ceasing everywhere at once, it is often described instead as a transformation with substantial cultural continuity.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>",
"Sea_Peoples": "The Sea Peoples is a modern label for groups named in Egyptian, Hittite and Ugaritic records of the 14th to 11th centuries BCE, among them the Peleset, Tjeker, Shekelesh, Denyen, Weshesh, Sherden and Lukka.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The fullest account is an inscription of Ramesses III at Medinet Habu, which has them laying waste the lands from Anatolia to Cyprus and Syria before Egypt defeats them on land and at sea.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Because the names are scattered across four centuries and several archives, the Egyptian texts are rhetorical, and proposed origins reach from Sardinia to the steppe, it is unknown whether these groups ever acted as one force.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>",
"Destruction_of_Pylos": "The destruction of Pylos is the fire that ended the Mycenaean Palace of Nestor in Messenia, in the south-west Peloponnese, at the turn from Late Helladic IIIB to IIIC; the palace was never rebuilt.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> It burned fiercely enough to leave hard red mudbrick and black char across the floors of the main building, beneath the collapsed upper storey.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Lists of men posted to watch the coast, found in the palace archive, have been taken as evidence that a seaborne attack was expected, but no cause for the fire is established, and a nearby cave record shows no drought coinciding with it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>",
"Postpalatial_Greece": "Postpalatial Greece is the period on the Greek mainland between the burning of the Mycenaean palaces and the Early Iron Age, known as Late Helladic IIIC and running through the 12th century BCE.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> No palace was rebuilt and writing and palatial accounting were not resumed, but the citadels were not deserted: houses rose again inside the walls at Mycenae, Tiryns rebuilt its lower town, and substantial building is known at Lefkandi and Asine.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Further destructions followed through the century, Messenia and Laconia were left nearly empty, cremation and single burial in cist graves spread, and the uniform culture of the palace age broke into regional styles.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>",
"Greek_Dark_Ages": "The Greek Dark Ages is the conventional name for the centuries in Greece between the end of Mycenaean civilisation and the Archaic period, running roughly from 1050 to 700 BCE.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Writing, palace buildings and the administrations that used them had ceased around 1200 BCE and were not resumed for centuries, so the period is known almost entirely from graves and pottery, and the surviving data are limited enough that conclusions reaching beyond a single site rest heavily on theory.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The name itself is disputed, with Early Iron Age and a designation drawn from the pottery sequence both proposed in its place.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>",
"Submycenaean_period": "The Submycenaean period is a style of Greek pottery, and the phase named after it, falling between the latest Mycenaean wares and the Protogeometric, known chiefly from graves in Attica and on Salamis, above all the Kerameikos at Athens.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Its range of shapes and ornament is narrow, and the Attic material has been sorted into four stages, the last of them transitional to the Protogeometric.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Because almost all of it comes from tombs rather than from settlements, some specialists hold that it is not a period at all but the funerary pottery of the final Mycenaean phase, and radiocarbon puts its end at about 1020 to 1000 BCE.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>",
"Protogeometric_period": "The Protogeometric period is the phase of Greek pottery, and the age named after it, that follows the last Mycenaean wares in central and southern Greece and ends about 900 BCE.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Its characteristic ornament is the concentric circle and semicircle, drawn with a multiple brush turning on a pivot rather than freehand, and the style is known chiefly from Athens and from Lefkandi on Euboea.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Its absolute dates are disputed: radiocarbon from Lefkandi, Kalapodi and Corinth opens the style around 1020 to 1000 BCE, while a series of dates from Sindos in northern Greece would place it a century or more earlier.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>",
"Geometric_period": "The Geometric period is the last phase of the Greek Early Iron Age, running from about 900 to 700 BCE and named after the pottery style of those centuries.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> It is conventionally divided into Early, Middle and Late Geometric, each split again in two, though the absolute dates rest on a small number of Greek sherds found in dated levels in the Levant.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Its closing century saw rapid social and material change across the Greek world, including the first overseas colonies, and the wells and graves of Athens have been read both as evidence of a rising population and as the record of a drought and the deaths it caused.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>",
"Depopulation": "Depopulation is a sustained decline in the number of people living in a place, and the term applies at any scale, from a whole country down to a single district or village.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> It is produced by some combination of fewer births, more deaths and people moving away, and the three are linked, since those who leave are mostly young adults, so their going lowers the birth rate and leaves behind an older population among whom deaths outnumber births.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Where the loss runs deep enough an area is said to have lost the capacity to replace its own population, and the effect is usually sharpest locally rather than nationally.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>",
"Lefkandi": "Lefkandi is a coastal archaeological site on the western side of the Greek island of Euboea, beside the narrow strait that divides it from the mainland.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Its settlement mound, known as Xeropolis, was walled and occupied continuously from the Middle Helladic period, and the 12th century BCE levels there have yielded two-storey houses, a later grid of small square units, evidence of metalworking and one of the earliest iron knives in the Aegean.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Its cemeteries, which run from about 1050 to 825 BCE, produced burials rich enough to change how the intervening centuries are described.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>",
"Toumba_building": "The Toumba building is a monumental hall of about 50 by 13.8 metres (164 by 45 feet) raised at Lefkandi on the Greek island of Euboea in the middle of the 10th century BCE, with an apsidal western end, a central row of wooden columns and the earliest known veranda in Greek architecture.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Two shafts sunk into its central room held four horses in one and, in the other, a cremated man with iron weapons and a woman buried unburnt with gold jewellery.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The hall was pulled down soon afterwards and covered by an earth mound, and the ground before its eastern door was then used as a cemetery.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>",
"Nichoria": "Nichoria is an archaeological site on a flat-topped ridge in Messenia, in the southwestern Peloponnese, about 2 kilometres (1.2 miles) inland from the Messenian Gulf.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> A Mycenaean village there was destroyed late in the 13th century BCE, but occupation resumed and can be followed through the Early Iron Age in a sequence of phases running into the 8th century.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Its best-known structure is Unit IV-1, an apsidal hall of 13.60 by 8 metres (45 by 26 feet) with a pit hearth and a line of internal roof supports, read by its excavators as a dwelling rather than a temple and among the largest such buildings known from the period.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>",
"Zagora": "Zagora is an Early Iron Age settlement on a headland on the south-western coast of Andros, in the Cyclades, covering about 6.7 hectares (17 acres) and defended on its landward side by a fortification wall with an elaborate gate.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Excavation has opened around a tenth of the town, revealing 55 stone-built rooms that form at least 25 houses in clustered groups, and no spring or well has been found, so rainwater was caught from the flat roofs.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Because the site was left almost undisturbed after its abandonment at the end of the 8th century BCE, it preserves the plan of a whole town of its period.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>",
"Iron_metallurgy": "Iron metallurgy is the extraction of iron from its ores and the working of the metal into objects.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> In antiquity it was done by the bloomery process: ore and charcoal burned together in a shaft furnace at around 1,200 °C (2,200 °F), where the waste ran off as liquid slag while the iron stayed solid, leaving a spongy mass, or bloom, that had to be hammered clean before use.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Because the metal could not be melted and cast, objects were shaped by prolonged hammering, and steel was made by taking up carbon in the smithing hearth, then hardened by quenching and softened again by tempering.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>",
"Cremation_burial": "Cremation burial is the funeral rite in which a body is burned on a pyre and the remains are then buried, either where the burning took place or in a container carried elsewhere.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The fire leaves a record of itself: bone calcined white has passed above 700 °C (1,300 °F), while several colours within a single fragment indicate a body burned with its flesh still on.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> A completely cremated adult yields roughly 2,400 grams (5 pounds) of bone, but the quantity recovered from ancient graves is often far smaller, so how much was gathered from a pyre, and how deliberately, is a question in its own right.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>",
"Cist_grave": "A cist grave is a rectangular grave sunk into the ground and lined to form a box, most often with upright stone slabs, and normally holding a single body.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Other linings are known, including walls of large boulders capped with rough courses of smaller stones, sides cut directly into earth or bedrock, and rubble walling built up from the floor.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The type is long-lived in Greece, where it was never wholly abandoned during the Bronze Age and became common again in the 12th century BCE as collective chamber tombs went out of use, and it could also be built to hold a cremation urn rather than a body.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>",
"Kerameikos": "The Kerameikos is a district in the north-west of ancient Athens named after the potters who worked there, on low, well-watered ground beside the Eridanos.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> It was extensive enough to take in the city's civic centre, as a boundary stone found in place at the north-west corner of the Classical Agora shows, and to reach out past the walls towards the Academy, though the modern labels Inner and Outer Kerameikos are not ancient ones.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Part of it served as the principal burial ground of Athens, and its excavated cemeteries, which begin before 1000 BCE, are a chief source for Greek funerary practice.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>",
"Dipylon_Amphora": "The Dipylon Amphora is a monumental Athenian clay vase of about 760 to 750 BCE, made not for use but to stand above a grave as its marker.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> It is the name piece of the unidentified painter called the Dipylon Master, and belongs to a group of thirteen figure-decorated kraters and three monumental amphorae found in a burial plot near the Dipylon Gate of Athens.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Its principal scene is a prothesis, the ceremonial laying out of a corpse among mourners, one of the earliest large figured compositions in Greek art after the centuries in which the human figure had almost disappeared from it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>",
"Geometric_pottery": "Geometric pottery is the painted Greek ware of roughly 900 to 700 BCE, decorated in dark glaze on the pale clay with banding, zigzags, hatched maeanders and panels of pattern, and it gives the Geometric period its name.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Its later phases add ornament by the unit rather than in continuous friezes, together with birds, animals and, on the grandest vessels, human figures.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Alongside such showpieces the same tradition produced ordinary household vessels, among them banded amphorae for hauling water and coarse micaceous jugs for cooking, and the shapes placed in graves were not always those used in daily life.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>",
"Hero_cult": "A hero cult is the worship of a named dead person, whether a figure of legend or a remembered individual, at a tomb or a shrine.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Its principal offering was a thysia sacrifice followed by dining, the rite made to the gods and not to the dead at large, and heroes accordingly stood somewhere between the two, worshipped locally at shrines that were mostly small buildings or open precincts.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Offerings left at Bronze Age tombs from the 8th century BCE are often read as evidence of the practice, though whether the visitors meant worship or an ordinary funeral among the ancestors is disputed.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>",
"Aoidos": "An aoidos was an early Greek singer of tales, who told stories in verse to the sound of a lyre and made the song in the act of performing it rather than repeating a memorised one.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The Odyssey portrays four of them, all living in other men's halls and singing at feasts of the expedition to Troy, the returns from it and the doings of the gods, in short pieces chosen to suit the moment.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Such singers credited their art to the Muse or to a god who had planted songs in the mind, and they were succeeded by rhapsodes, who recited poems that writing had fixed.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>",
"Homer": "Homer is the name Greek tradition gave to the poet of the <i>Iliad</i> and the <i>Odyssey</i>, about whom nothing is securely known: no dated life, no agreed birthplace and no contemporary testimony.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The language of the poems, in which Ionic predominates over Aeolic, Mycenaean and Attic elements, places the beginnings of Greek epic in Ionia, and the objects they describe put their composition not much before 700 BCE.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Ancient accounts of his life are late and made from motifs shared with other early poets, and his name once carried more verse than it does now, including parts of the epic cycle that most sources gave to others.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>",
"Homeric_Question": "The Homeric Question is the name given to a cluster of disputes about the making of the two Greek epics: their authorship, the share in them of an inherited tradition, and the point at which they stopped changing.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Its modern phase begins with Friedrich August Wolf in 1795 and ran through a century of scholars hunting for the seams where shorter songs had been joined.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Milman Parry's fieldwork among the singers of Yugoslavia recast it, since the poems' traditional diction is now generally read as the mark of song composed in performance, leaving the manner of their writing down as the part still in dispute.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>",
"Iliad": "The Iliad is an ancient Greek epic, the older of the two poems handed down under Homer's name, whose subject is the anger of Achilles rather than the Trojan war entire.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> It runs from a quarrel between Achilles and Agamemnon, through the defeat of the Greeks and the killing of Patroclus, to the death of Hector, and it stops before the fall of Troy, which the poems of the epic cycle told instead.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Its familiar division into 24 books is the work of Alexandrian scholars rather than of the poet, and it was memorised, quoted and taught in classical Greece as a standard of poetic excellence.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>",
"Odyssey": "The Odyssey is one of the two ancient Greek epics ascribed to Homer, and it follows Odysseus from Troy back to Ithaca and the killing of the suitors who had taken over his house.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Half of it is set before he reaches home, and its most famous episodes are told by the hero himself to the Phaeacians rather than by the narrator.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Ancient scholars judged its closing book and part of one underworld scene to be later additions, and modern ones long treated the poem as a different poet's work from that of the <i>Iliad</i>.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>",
"Dactylic_hexameter": "Dactylic hexameter is a verse line of six feet, each of them a dactyl of one long syllable and two short or a spondee of two long, measured by syllable length rather than by stress.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> It is the metre of ancient Greek epic, and since Hermann Fränkel's work of 1926 the line has usually been analysed into word-groups called cola, divided by breaks in the sense, rather than into its six feet.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Greek poets used it well beyond epic, in didactic verse, elegy, drama and the long poems of the Hellenistic and later periods, and its origin is disputed.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>",
"Oral-formulaic_composition": "Oral-formulaic composition is a way of making narrative verse in performance, in which a singer works from a stock of ready phrases, each fitted to a particular stretch of the metrical line.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Milman Parry established it for Homer, showing that a repeated phrase occupying a fixed metrical slot for a fixed sense belongs to a large and economical system rather than to a poet's mannerism.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Later students widened the idea to cover repeated scenes and looser word-groups, and the wider the definition grew the less it could be used to prove that a poem was composed orally.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>",
"Rhapsode": "A rhapsode was a professional performer of epic poetry in ancient Greece, reciting verse that was already fixed instead of composing it during the performance.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The word is often connected with a verb meaning to stitch, and Pindar uses the image of stitched verses of the Homeridai, a Chian family that later became a guild of reciters claiming no descent from the poet.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Rhapsodes travelled between cities and recited Homer at festivals such as the Panathenaia at Athens, and they are one of the means by which the poems were carried, altered and in the end settled.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>",
"Homeric_society": "Homeric society is the social order shown in the <i>Iliad</i> and the <i>Odyssey</i>, marked by the exchange of gifts and by rank, and it is not securely datable to any one period.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The poems keep material from the Bronze Age while taking for granted objects and customs no older than about 700 BCE, so the world they show has been assigned to the Mycenaean age, the Dark Age and the poet's own century in turn.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Because an oral tradition carries old and new together, a feature that looks late need not be a late addition, which makes the society hard to read as a record of any single time.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>",
"Oikos": "An oikos was a Greek household taken as a whole: the dwelling, the farmland attached to it, the goods and livestock, and every person belonging to it, free or enslaved.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> It was the working unit of ancient Greek society, and the ties that ran through it, between spouses, between parents and children and between owner and slave, mattered more in daily life than membership of any wider kin group.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Historians once held that the growth of the city-state destroyed it, but the household kept its importance alongside the polis and was expected to mirror the city's own political order.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>",
"Xenia": "Xenia was the ancient Greek bond of guest-friendship, obliging a host to receive, feed and protect a traveller who came to the door, and obliging the guest not to abuse what he was given.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The relationship was sealed and renewed by presents passing between the two households, and it outlasted the visit itself.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Because it applied to strangers rather than to kin or neighbours, Greek literature uses it as a measure of character, and the treatment of visitors in the <i>Odyssey</i> is a standard against which nearly every figure in the poem is weighed.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>",
"Kleos": "Kleos is the ancient Greek word for the renown that survives a person, literally what is heard said of them, and in early epic it is the compensation a warrior accepts for a short and dangerous life.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Because renown of that kind depends on being repeated, poets are its keepers rather than its subjects, and epic represents the singing of famous men as the means by which their fame reaches the sky.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The concern with it long outlived epic and shaped the praise poetry of the following centuries, which celebrated the winners of athletic contests by setting them beside the heroes of the past.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>",
"Basileus": "A basileus was a Greek leader or ruler, the word later used for a king, though its earliest form in the Linear B tablets belongs to officials well below the top of the palace hierarchy.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> After the collapse of the palaces the title rose in standing, and a community could hold several such men at once, heads of leading families rather than a single monarch.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Their position rested on wealth, on trade in metal and fine goods, on the feasts they gave and on their part in cult, as much as on any success in war.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>",
"Theogony": "The <i>Theogony</i> is an early Greek poem in hexameters, ascribed to Hesiod, that sets the gods in a single genealogy reaching from the earliest powers to the established reign of Zeus.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Its first beings are Chaos, Gaia and Eros, and what Chaos denotes — a gap, or a power that goes on to bear other gods — remains disputed.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Sovereignty in it changes hands by violence across three generations, and editors differ over how much of the transmitted text is the poet's, several verses and the ending being marked as spurious by some and defended by others.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>",
"Works_and_Days": "The <i>Works and Days</i> is a poem of instruction in hexameters, handed down under Hesiod's name, which sets moral argument beside practical advice and is addressed to a brother with whom the poet is in dispute over an inheritance.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> It divides into a section of myth, a long central part on justice and on the farming year, and a closing list of days held to be favourable or unfavourable for particular tasks.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Its farming year is timed by the appearance and disappearance of particular stars rather than by any civil calendar, which makes it the fullest surviving evidence for how early Greeks reckoned the seasons.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>",
"Ascra": "Ascra was a village in Boeotia, in the Valley of the Muses below Mount Helicon, known above all as the home of the poet Hesiod, whose father had come there from Aeolic Cumae.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Field survey has identified the site and turned up only slight traces of the Protogeometric and Geometric periods, so that the settlement of about 700 BCE was small, though by Classical times it covered some 11 hectares (27 acres) and may have held around 1,300 people.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Whether it governed itself in the earlier period or was already a dependency of the neighbouring city of Thespiae is disputed, the same evidence being read both ways.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>",
"Greek_alphabet": "The Greek alphabet is a writing system adapted from the Phoenician script, differing from its model in giving the vowels letters of their own, four of them taken over from Semitic signs and a fifth drawn off a consonant.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Scholars agree that the adaptation was made once rather than several times over, though where and in what setting is disputed, and the extra letters phi, chi and psi have been explained both as the adapter's inventions and as a later borrowing.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> It was not at first one alphabet but many local ones, whose regional groupings look the more fluid the more closely their letter forms are compared.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>",
"Phoenician_alphabet": "The Phoenician alphabet is a script used along the Levantine seaboard in the early 1st millennium BCE, a set of 22 signs standing for consonants alone, so that a reader supplies the vowels.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Its ancestry is traced to inscriptions of the early 2nd millennium BCE in which Egyptian writing was adapted to a West Semitic language, and comparable systems are known at Ugarit by about 1400 BCE.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Whether a script without vowel signs should be called an alphabet at all is disputed, one account preferring the term consonantal syllabary; the Aramaic script and, by a further adaptation, the Greek one descend from it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>",
"Dipylon_inscription": "The Dipylon inscription is a line of Greek cut into an Athenian wine jug of the Late Geometric period, conventionally dated to about 740 BCE and among the earliest surviving writing in the Greek alphabet.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> It scans as a complete dactylic hexameter, the metre of epic verse, and carries the letters ypsilon and chi, which are among the signs Greek added to the alphabet it took over.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The jug, now in the National Archaeological Museum at Athens, was bought from an Athenian dealer who had excavated it himself, so the burial it came from was never properly recorded.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>",
"Nestor's_Cup": "Nestor's Cup is a clay drinking cup, made in the eastern Aegean and found in a cremation burial at Pithekoussai on Ischia, carrying three lines of Greek scratched into it about 730 BCE.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The verses claim the cup as Nestor's and promise that whoever drinks from it will be seized by desire for Aphrodite, set against the enormous vessel the <i>Iliad</i> gives that king.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> It is among the earliest surviving Greek writing, and a study of the burnt remains published in 2021 found at least three individuals and animal bone rather than the single child long supposed.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>",
"Pithekoussai": "Pithekoussai was a Greek settlement on the island of Ischia, off the Bay of Naples, founded by Euboeans in the 8th century BCE and the earliest lasting Greek foundation in the western Mediterranean.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Several hundred graves have been excavated from a much larger cemetery of the later 8th century, and they held Levantine amphorae, north Syrian jugs and Phoenician lamps beside Greek pottery, indicating a population of mixed origin.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The settlers smelted and worked metal, taking ore from Elba, and made pottery for export, and whether the place is best described as a trading station or as a colony proper is disputed.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>",
"Al_Mina": "Al Mina is a settlement on the north Syrian coast, at the point where the Orontes reaches the sea, whose excavated buildings held Greek pottery in quantity, Euboean during the 8th century BCE and Attic in the centuries after.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The finds were long taken to show a Greek trading settlement in the Levant, an interpretation since questioned on the grounds that pottery does not count as population and that much undecorated ware was discarded when the site was dug.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Rival readings make it a Syrian town in which a few Euboean merchants worked, or a settlement of mercenaries rather than traders.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>",
"Euboea": "Euboea is a Greek island whose two chief cities in antiquity were Chalcis and Eretria, divided by the plain of the river Lelas between them.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Chalcis was settled in the 11th century BCE and took a large part in the metal trade, while Eretria was founded in the 9th at a good harbour and controlled Andros, Tenos and Keos.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Traders and settlers from the island were active in the Levant, in the northern Aegean and in Italy during the 8th century BCE, until a long war between the two cities over that plain ended the pre-eminence of both.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>",
"Phoenicians": "The Phoenicians were the people of the cities of the Levantine coast, whose traders, craftsmen and goods reached across the Mediterranean and into the Aegean during the early 1st millennium BCE.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Metal bowls, ivories, faience, glass beads, scarabs and the techniques of jewellery-making passed from them into Greek hands, and the Greek alphabet derives from their script.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Whether they formed a single people or the name is a label imposed by others is argued, one recent case holding that they shared ritual and cultural traits and so make a meaningful category rather than a construct of ancient writers and modern nationalisms.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>",
"Ionians": "The Ionians were a division of the Greeks who held the middle of the western seaboard of Asia Minor, their twelve cities forming the dodekapolis, with a common sanctuary and a festival held there.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Their foundation stories, preserved by Strabo and Pausanias, make Athens the mother city and give individual cities founder heroes such as Neileus at Miletos and Androklos at Ephesos.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Those stories supplied a base for a shared identity that was maintained by festivals at the Panionion, and whether they record a real migration from the mainland, or were fastened onto local identities later, is disputed.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>",
"Aeolians": "The Aeolians were a division of the Greeks associated with the north-western seaboard of Anatolia, with Lesbos and the smaller islands beside it, and with Thessaly and Boeotia on the Greek mainland.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Their name attaches to a dialect group — Lesbian, Thessalian and Boeotian — all of them descended from a common Aeolic.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Tradition had them crossing from mainland Greece to Anatolia in the early Iron Age, but the absence of archaeological support has prompted the reverse proposal, that Aeolic was spoken in Anatolia in the Bronze Age and its speakers moved westward instead.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>",
"Dorians": "The Dorians were one of the main groupings of the Greeks, whom later tradition held to have entered the Peloponnese and the south of Greece in the generations after the Mycenaean palaces fell.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> That movement, long called the Dorian invasion and long used to explain the end of the Bronze Age, has been put back among the myths, the collapse of the palaces being explained instead by internal strife, natural disaster and systems failure.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The stories themselves show different traditions run together, and where regions share a dialect or an institution, contact and adoption serve as well as any invasion.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>",
"Heracleidae": "The Heracleidae were the descendants of Heracles, whose return to the Peloponnese Greek tradition presented as the recovery of a right rather than as a conquest of somebody else's land.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Sparta's kings claimed descent among them and drew their authority from it, ranking with the founders who led colonies out; the family entered Laconian tradition perhaps by the opening of the 7th century BCE.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The Spartan case for annexing Messenia is implicit in the same story, which is a separate tradition from the Dorian invasion and had to be reconciled with it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>",
"Ancient_Greek_dialects": "The ancient Greek dialects are the local forms in which the language was written and spoken before a common Greek spread, and they are known above all from inscriptions.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Four groups are usually distinguished: Ionic; Doric, otherwise West Greek; Aeolic; and the Achaean pair, Arcadian with Cypriot, to which Pamphylian is commonly joined as well.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Whether such a group marks off a people is disputed, since shared features can come from contact as readily as from common descent, and in literature a dialect belongs to a genre rather than to a place.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>",
"Arcadocypriot": "Arcadocypriot is a group of ancient Greek dialects comprising Arcadian, spoken in the central Peloponnese, and Cypriot, spoken on Cyprus, with Pamphylian usually joined to them as a third member.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Their resemblance across so great a distance has been connected with Mycenaean Greek, and one study asks whether a prepositional usage they share with it is an isogloss of Bronze Age date.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Cypriot Greek was written in a syllabic script carried on from Bronze Age practice, and it shared the island with Eteocypriot, a language that is not Greek and is written in the same script.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>",
"Cypriot_city-kingdoms": "The Cypriot city-kingdoms were the independent states that shared the island of Cyprus through the 1st millennium BCE, Salamis, Paphos, Kition, Amathous, Idalion and Marion among them.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> How and when they came into being is disputed, some scholars tracing them back across the collapse of the Bronze Age and others dating the earliest of them several centuries later, to the 9th or 8th century BCE.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Their wealth rested largely on copper and on trade with the Levant, and control of the metal drew the inland kingdom of Idalion into conflict, ending with its annexation by the coastal kingdom of Kition.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>",
"Cypriot_syllabary": "The Cypriot syllabary is the syllabic script in which Greek was written on the island of Cyprus from the 8th century BCE until the end of the 3rd, sharing the island with the Greek alphabet from the 6th century onward.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> It was read from 1872, long before the Aegean syllabic scripts it resembles, and more than a thousand texts survive in it, covering the ordinary run of epigraphic genres rather than administration alone.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> It descends in some fashion from Cypro-Minoan, the unread writing of the Late Bronze Age island, and it also served Eteocypriot, a language that is not Greek.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>",
"Synoecism": "Synoecism is the process by which several separate Greek communities came to form a single state, a sense preserved in the name of the Athenian festival of the Synoikia, 'the coming together of houses'.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> It covers two things worth keeping apart: a political union between communities that remain in their own places, and a physical gathering of their people into one town, which may follow centuries afterwards or never.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Checked against excavation the process is often less abrupt than ancient accounts of it suggest, and by the 4th century BCE a ruler could impose one on settlements that had not sought it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>",
"Polis": "A polis is the form of political community that most of the Greek world was organised into, a word meaning both a town and the citizen body that town belonged to.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Independence was not a condition of the status, since dependent poleis existed and were still so called, and the clearest sign of it is the city-ethnic, what a person of the place was called rather than the place.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> An inventory compiled by listing every settlement that contemporary sources explicitly call a polis discusses 1,035 of them across the archaic and classical periods, with hundreds more of uncertain standing listed beside them.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>",
"Ethnos": "An ethnos is a Greek people occupying a whole region and going by a single name, such as the Thessalians, Boeotians, Aetolians, Acarnanians, Phocians, Arcadians or Achaeans.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> When such a people acted as a state it used that name of itself, where modern scholarship speaks of a koinon, an ancient word applied in antiquity to a community, to the body that passed decrees and to a federal assembly.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Shared sanctuaries, festivals and sacred officials did much of the work of holding one together, although a common name did not always yield a political union, and some ethnic claims read as the work of cities pressing their own interests.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>",
"Olympia": "Olympia is a sanctuary in the western Peloponnese, seat of the Olympic games, whose founding was placed by tradition in 776 BCE.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Cult there can be reliably traced from about 900 to 600 BCE, in ash from its altar and in terracotta and bronze offerings, while the mound under the later Pelopion is far older, raised in the 3rd millennium BCE and still visible when those offerings began.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> What was given there changed with its reach: small figurines, then through the 8th century BCE bronze tripods of growing size carried from workshops elsewhere, which fall away in the 7th as a settlement grew at Elis beside it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>",
"Delphi": "Delphi is a sanctuary of Apollo on the slopes of Parnassus in central Greece, the seat of an oracle consulted from across the Greek world.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Settlement in the area is attested from the Mycenaean period onward, while the cult begins late, in the 8th century BCE, the settlement standing first on the ground and being displaced by the sanctuary that grew over it, and the earliest historic temple of Apollo is put at about 600 BCE.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Its early bronze offerings, tripods and cauldrons and vessels fitted with griffin, siren and bull protomai, were carried there from north Syria, Samos, Crete and Cyprus.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>",
"Heraion_of_Samos": "The Heraion of Samos is Hera's great sanctuary on that island, and its rise is dated by its early altars to the arrival of Greeks there at the close of the Bronze Age.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> A monumental temple stood there perhaps as early as the 8th century BCE and was replaced twice over by much larger buildings in the 6th, when the Great Altar and its sculptural dedications were raised, with ten temple-like buildings in the archaic sanctuary.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> It is remarkable for what was carried to it, no other excavation in Greece having produced as many imports, while a study of 1,105 dedications finds Hera tied to agriculture and vegetation.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>",
"Perachora": "Perachora is a Heraion standing across the water from Corinth, one of the sanctuaries by which that city shaped its own sacred landscape, and it grew greatly during the 8th century BCE.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> One argument identifies its goddess as Hera Akraia, placing that sanctuary here instead of in Corinth itself, and makes the site the only seat of the rites owed to the children of Medea.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> It is notably rich in small valuable objects and in imports: a survey of Hera's sanctuaries records 3,417 votives from the site, terracotta building models and Egyptian figures of Bes among them.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>",
"Votive_dedication": "A votive dedication is a thing offered to a god and left standing in a sanctuary, at times inscribed with the names of giver and deity.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Such an inscription can be read as a compressed account of the act, taken up again whenever somebody sees it and speaks the words aloud, so that the gift becomes a charis, an exchange between human and divine renewed at every reading.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> What was given includes greenery woven into wreaths, cakes shaped into figures and offerings cut from wood by the giver, and not every one was devout, those in gymnasia answering to political convention.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>",
"Bronze_tripod_cauldron": "A bronze tripod cauldron is a vessel of bronze standing on three legs, which started in use for cooking and heating and had by the Bronze Age become a status symbol, a princely gift and a prize.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Through the Geometric and Early Archaic periods such vessels were the most prestigious offerings made in Greek sanctuaries, and some were monumental, 8th-century examples from the Athenian Acropolis standing over 2 m (6 ft 7 in) tall.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Figures were attached to them from the 9th century BCE, bulls' heads and birds first, then horses and human forms, and orientalising versions with griffin and siren attachments follow in the 7th.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>",
"Warrior_burial": "A warrior burial is a grave whose occupant is taken for a fighting man because weapons were laid in it, an identification that generally rests on the weapons alone.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The association holds where samples are large: a study of 1,146 Macedonian burials of the 6th to 4th centuries BCE found weapons to be men's goods, with elite men shown in death as warriors and few weapons among women.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> It remains an inference about a life drawn from objects, and examining a skeleton can overturn what its goods appeared to say, as with the Athenian burial long known as the 'Young Lady', which is probably male.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>",
"Eighth-century_revival": "The eighth-century revival is the name given to a concentration of change in Greece at the close of the Dark Age, when settlement, burial and sanctuary evidence all become far more plentiful.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> It was long explained by a rise in population, on a burial count from Athens, Attica and the Argolid published in 1980, but it ran adult and child graves together and stopped short of the 7th century, so demographic growth no longer explains the figures.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> One estimate puts the yearly increase in Attica at no more than 0.4 per cent against the 3 to 4 per cent once assumed, and the period's absolute chronology is itself under challenge.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>",
"Dreros": "Dreros is an ancient city on Crete whose sanctuary of Apollo Delphinios yielded the oldest law surviving from the Greek world, and the earliest text in which the word 'polis' occurs.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Cut into the temple's east wall between 650 and 600 BCE, it forbids a man who has held the office of kosmos to hold it again for ten years, sets punishments for breaking it, and records an oath sworn by the kosmos, the damioi and the ikati.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Excavation on the west acropolis has identified three phases in the sanctuary, opening with a deposit of cultic objects and animal bones of the late 8th or early 7th century BCE.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>",
"Chora": "A chora is the territory belonging to an ancient Greek city, distinguished from the astu or town, and it held rural dwellings, tombs and shrines as well as farmland.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> City and territory are treated as inseparable in modern scholarship, the great inventory of archaic and classical poleis recording the size of a territory for each of the 1,035 places it discusses, and ancient writers used the word 'polis' for the hinterland as readily as for the urban centre.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Some were deliberately planned: the territory of Chersonesos on the Black Sea was organised on orthogonal lines, as was the European part of the Bosporan kingdom, divided into rectangular land-plots.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>",
"Acropolis": "An acropolis is the fortified high ground at the heart of an ancient Greek town, the stronghold a settlement gathered around; the word 'polis' is held to have widened outward from it, meaning first that urban settlement and then a whole territorial state.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> It names a kind of fortification as well as a place, one that leans on natural defences rather than on masonry alone, so that a town with a defensible height commonly worked it into the walls.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Cult belonged there as much as defence, the community's own temple or sanctuary often standing on the summit, though which deity held a given height is not always recoverable.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>",
"Agora": "An agora is both the assembly of a Greek community's adult men and the open public space at the heart of its town, a political institution and an architectural type at once.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Stoas, council houses, temples, shrines, monumental gateways and market halls stood around one, and legal business and public proclamation went on there alongside trade.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Their development varied widely: Megara Hyblaia in Sicily reserved ground for one from its 8th-century foundation and filled it over the next two centuries, while the area of the Athenian agora was a potters' field and cemetery, undeveloped as such until near the end of the archaic period.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>",
"Polites": "A polites is a member of a Greek polis, a community defined as a town that was also a body of citizens, the standing marked most clearly by the city-ethnic a person of the place bore.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Archaic Greece left little evidence of a clear concept of citizenship or a word for it, and scholars divide over whether it was inherited by descent, performed as behaviour, or held as a legal status.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Women, metics and slaves did essential civic work without being institutionalised in the same way, and most citizens were poor, the working class making up 90 per cent of Athens' citizen body in the early 6th century BCE.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>",
"Phyle": "A phyle is the largest of the divisions an ancient Greek city cut its citizen body into, taking newcomers in, structuring councils and armies, building internal solidarity and working against tyranny.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Their number varied from city to city — three at Kyrene, four at Sikyon, eight at Korinth — and the Aeolic cities had none, while one scholarly account derives them from bands of settlers rather than from artificial creation.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Civic subdivisions are standard enough to be indexed for every entry in the great inventory of archaic and classical poleis, and a citizen belonged to smaller associations below the phyle, the phratry and the gene among them.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>",
"Phratry": "A phratry is one of the associations an ancient Greek citizen belonged to below the phyle, organised by kinship rather than by democratic principle, and its uniform cult practice helped build a common civic identity.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Almost everything known about them is Attic, and whether every Athenian belonged to one in the archaic period is unsettled, one argument resting on the implications of Draco's homicide law.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Below the phratry lay smaller groups including the genos and the oikos, though the older picture of Greek society as a system of descent groups has been attacked, the household counting for more in daily life than any extended bloodline.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>",
"Genos": "A genos is one of the smaller groups an ancient Greek phratry divided into, alongside the oikos, the orgeones and the thiasos, sorted by kinship and standing rather than by any democratic rule.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> It is commonly rendered as the clan of an old aristocratic family, a usage the sources do not support before the 4th century BCE, and two studies of 1976 concluded independently that there is little evidence it was ever more than a group transmitting priestly office.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Later work has taken the Athenian gene to be geographical rather than aristocratic in origin, and has questioned whether early Greek society rested on descent groups at all.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>",
"Archaic_aristocracy": "The archaic aristocracy is the elite of Greece in the 7th and 6th centuries BCE, a category modern scholarship has largely dismantled: the word is now held only rarely useful, medieval and modern images of nobility having encouraged the view that such elites were closed and hereditary.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Status had instead to be earned and continually re-earned, through claimed descent, marriage, funerals, luxury goods and dedications, and access to the higher ranks was open to enterprising men.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The drinking party was one arena in which that contest was settled, open on equal terms to anyone trained in its conventions whatever his background.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>",
"Hoplite": "A hoplite is the heavily armed infantryman of the ancient Greek city, and the familiar picture of a middle-class hoplite army has been called a myth resting on a single ill-founded ancient generalisation.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> These were amateurs rather than professionals, archaic and classical land armies being made up of ill-trained non-specialists raised as citizen militias that were haphazard about command, supply and discipline.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Their equipment was substantial, the bronze bell cuirass of one surviving panoply weighing 3.360 kilograms (7.4 pounds), and they never fought alone, cavalry, slingers and archers serving beside them without receiving comparable credit in antiquity.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>",
"Phalanx": "A phalanx is the close-order formation of massed heavy infantry in ancient Greek warfare, men standing in files and ranks with shields overlapping; the word carries an older sense of 'beam' or 'roller'.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> That shield was about 90 centimetres (three feet) across and some 6.8 kilograms (15 pounds), locked to the elbow by a central armband and a grip at the rim, covering its bearer's left side and leaving his right bare.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> How such a line fought is uncertain: where contingents were placed beforehand mattered more than manoeuvre, since untrained militias could not change formation once moving, and whether the ranks shoved has been argued over for a century.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>",
"Aspis": "An aspis is the round, deeply bowled shield carried by the heavy infantry of the ancient Greek city, roughly 90 centimetres (three feet) across and about 6.8 kilograms (15 pounds).<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> It hangs on two grips rather than one, a band at the centre called the porpax taking the left forearm to the elbow and a handle at the rim called the antilabe, which locks it to the arm and leaves its bearer's right side uncovered.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The design is bound up with fighting in formation, since such a shield cannot be shifted across to the right as a single-handled one can, nor slung on the back in flight.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>",
"Corinthian_helmet": "A Corinthian helmet is the bronze helmet of the heavy infantry of the ancient Greek city, covering the whole head and most of the face and standing about 23 centimetres (nine inches) high; it was made far beyond the city it is named for.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> It gave more protection than anything else a Greek wore and took vision, hearing and movement in exchange, and the horsehair crest it often carried has not survived.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Its bronze is deliberately uneven, thin over the crown and several times thicker at nose and cheeks, and the type had all but gone out of use by the early 5th century BCE.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>",
"Hoplite_reform": "The hoplite reform is the proposed change by which the double-grip shield brought a sudden revolution in Greek tactics early in the archaic period, and with it a shift of political weight towards the men of the phalanx.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The idea descends from a remark of Aristotle's and became the standard account in English during the 20th century, crediting its farmers with remaking the city and founding the idea of citizenship.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> It has been contested since the 1960s by arguments that the change was gradual, that the equipment was lighter than supposed, and that the middling farmer at the heart of the case did not exist in numbers.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>",
"Chigi_vase": "The Chigi vase is a Protocorinthian olpe of about 640 BCE, 26.2 centimetres (10 inches) high, painted with three friezes of human figures and among the most ambitious pots made at Corinth.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Its top frieze carries the earliest surviving picture of massed hoplites with a piper beside them, the middle band a lion hunt and a mounted procession, and the lowest a hare hunt, while the Judgement of Paris under the handle is the oldest known picture of that story.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> It was buried in an Etruscan princely tomb outside Veii, and its warriors carry two spears each, which complicates reading the top frieze as a picture of the phalanx.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>",
"Tyrannos": "A tyrannos is a ruler who took sole power in an ancient Greek city outside its ordinary order, and the tyrants of the archaic age resist easy definition.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Tyranny grew out of the crises many cities went through, and a tyrant commonly rose in answer to a local one, often holding an office of the city and acting as its benefactor.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The line between such a ruler and a lawgiver is thin enough to look artificial, both holding unlimited power for a time, and the distinction ancient writers drew between king and tyrant has been read as literary rather than real.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>",
"Cypselus": "Cypselus is the man who ended the rule of the Bacchiadae at Corinth and made himself sole ruler of the city, founding a dynasty that is one of the standard examples of archaic tyranny.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Almost everything told of him is legend rather than record: his father Eetion came from outside the Bacchiad clan and his mother was Labda, and the tradition around the family is fictive beyond reasonable doubt.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> He is said to have needed neither bodyguard nor mercenaries once the Bacchiadae were gone, though Herodotus has him drive many Corinthians into exile and kill many more.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>",
"Periander": "Periander is the second of the Cypselid tyrants of Corinth, the son of Cypselus, and in the tradition that grew up around him one of the Seven Sages.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> He faced a generation of nobles who wanted power for themselves and disputed his, and held on to it by keeping a bodyguard and dealing ruthlessly with his enemies, turning the tyranny towards coercion.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The stories told of him are largely invention, among them the consultation of his dead wife's ghost and the parable of the ears of corn, in which the advice to cut down whatever stands tallest is a lesson in reducing one's opponents.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>",
"Corinth": "Corinth is a Greek city on the Isthmus, lying below Acrocorinth, a rock 575 metres (1,886 feet) high that controlled the road across the Isthmus, the pass south towards Argos and the coastal road west to Sicyon.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Its great archaic building is the temple of Apollo, put up in the middle of the 6th century BCE to replace a destroyed predecessor, with six monolithic columns at each end and 15 along each side, seven of which are standing still.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> At the western edge of the walled city the Potters' Quarter worked for three centuries, turning the white marl clay of the place into pots, figurines, painted plaques and votive shields.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>",
"Diolkos": "The Diolkos is the paved trackway that carried boats overland across the Isthmus of Corinth, sparing them the long and dangerous voyage round the Peloponnese.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> It ran about 8 kilometres (5 miles) and was 3.5 to 6 metres (11 to 20 feet) wide, was built in the 6th century BCE, and is associated with Periander; a ship was moored and emptied of its cargo before being moved on a wheeled vehicle.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Whether it was a commercial thoroughfare has been questioned, one re-examination arguing that it carried no constant flow of ships and cargoes and served the transport and strategic ends of Corinth and her allies.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>",
"Cleisthenes_of_Sicyon": "Cleisthenes of Sicyon is an Orthagorid tyrant, of the dynasty that held Sicyon for about a century, and his own reign is put at about 600 to about 569 BCE.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> He took part in the First Sacred War, won the four-horse chariot race at the Pythian games in 582/81, and gave the Dorian tribes of his city new names that Herodotus says stayed in use for 60 years after his death.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> His daughter Agariste married into the Alcmaeonid house at Athens; an athletic victory of the kind he won was one of the ways a ruler displayed the excellence by which such rule was justified.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>",
"Polycrates": "Polycrates is the tyrant of Samos who was killed at Magnesia at the start of 522 BCE by Oroetes, the Persian governor at Sardis, who baited him with an offer of the wealth his projects needed.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> His navy was said to be one of the greatest in archaic Greece and is described most fully in Herodotus's <i>Histories</i>, though the accounts conflict over how it was used and made up; its ships were penteconters, triremes and samainae, the last a type tradition says he invented.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> He was joined in guest-friendship to Amasis of Egypt, and the lavish dipteral temple of Hera on his island was commissioned under his rule.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>",
"Tunnel_of_Eupalinos": "The Tunnel of Eupalinos is the aqueduct bore cut through the Kastro mountain on Samos in the middle of the 6th century BCE; Herodotus, the first to describe it, names the engineer as Eupalinos, son of Naustrophus, of Megara, and records that the digging began at two portals at once.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> It runs about 1,040 metres (3,400 feet), with a cross-section of roughly 1.8 metres (5 ft 11 in) square, and the fall is carried not by the floor, which is almost level, but by a deep trench along it holding the pipes.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Water came from a spring at Agiades and delivered an estimated 400 cubic metres a day.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>",
"Pheidon": "Pheidon is the ruler of Argos whom Aristotle describes as a hereditary king who made himself a tyrant, which makes his the earliest example of archaic tyranny that can be named; by the reckoning of many scholars that would place him near in time to the poets Archilochus and Semonides.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> He is credited with a set of standards, among them weights, measures, coinage and the iron spits called obeliskoi, and is given a hand in the running of Olympia.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> His date is the hardest question about him, since Herodotus places him relatively late while other accounts synchronise him with Lycurgus of Sparta or with the end of the Corinthian monarchy.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>",
"Argos": "Argos is the chief city of the Argive plain in the Peloponnese, and the rise of its power in the north-east Argolid is dated to the second half of the 8th century BCE, when the cult of Apollo Pythaieus was spreading through the region and the great sanctuary of the Argive Heraion stood in its plain.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Its neighbour Asine was destroyed and its people moved to Korone in Messenia, and Argos fought border wars with Sparta for centuries afterwards, most of them in the archaic age.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Three phylai are reckoned for the city in that period, with a fourth mentioned only from about 460.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>",
"Aspis_hill": "The Aspis hill is a height above the city of Argos in the Peloponnese, and its summit carries the extensive remains of a major fortified settlement of the Middle Helladic period, about 2000 to 1700 BCE.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Votive material found there, chiefly miniature pottery, terracotta figurines, small objects and bronze offerings, spans the Late Geometric to the Early Hellenistic period, and most of it belongs to the 7th and early 6th centuries, which confirms a sanctuary on the hilltop.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The ordinary pottery recovered from the hill is largely of the end of the Classical and the Early Hellenistic periods, and bears on how long the ground was occupied.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>",
"Aegina": "Aegina is an island polis of the Saronic Gulf in Greece, whose harbour mound at Kolonna was occupied from the Bronze Age and produced the gold hoard known as the Aegina Treasure, probably from Middle Helladic tombs on the site.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Its archaic history has been set out as a sequence of dominations, Argive and then Epidaurian, before an oligarchy of its own from about 618 or 613 BCE, and its long quarrel with Athens was almost wholly naval.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Its weights, measures and coins gave their name to a standard used far beyond the island, while the sanctuary of Aphaia is its great building.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>",
"Chalcis": "Chalcis is a city on the island of Euboea in Greece, established in the 11th century BCE on the strait between the island and the mainland, a crossing that has been bridged since the late 5th century and makes Euboea one of only two Greek islands a traveller can reach on foot.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Its name is read as the city of the bronzeworkers, and although it stands in a very favourable place it is not a well known city.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> It was governed by aristocrats called Hippobotai, or horse-feeders, and it planted colonies in Chalcidice, in the Levant around Al Mina and in the west.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>",
"Eretria": "Eretria is a city on the island of Euboea in Greece and one of the main archaeological sites of the Greek Early Iron Age, the ground beneath it occupied as far back as the Late Neolithic.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Its name is read as the city of the rowers, and a votive inscription found near its theatre records a body called the Aeinautai, the ever-sailors, and carries the earliest trace of the cult of Hermes there.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Its heroon at the West Gate, its House of the Mosaics and its sanctuary of Apollo Daphnephoros are standard illustrations in the handbooks of Greek history.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>",
"Lelantine_War": "The Lelantine War is the conflict between Chalcis and Eretria over the plain lying between them on the Greek island of Euboea, and its title is a modern one.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Three brief ancient notices of the hostilities survive, in Herodotus, Thucydides and Aristotle, and none of them names that plain as the object of the fighting, though Thucydides took the quarrel for the most notable among the Greeks before his own war and said the rest of Greece took sides in it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Allies are named on both sides, and the burning of Lefkandi-Xeropolis about 700 BCE is the archaeological support usually offered for a real war.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>",
"Megara": "Megara is an ancient Greek city-state that draws attention chiefly for three things: it was a prominent colonizer, it was a target of the aggression of its neighbours, and it is the reputed home of the poet Theognis.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Its colonies were planted around the Propontis and the Black Sea, and one argument sets it in a colonising alliance with Miletus during the archaic period, the elites of the two cities bound by a claimed mythical kinship reaching back to the Lelantine War, in which both took the side of Eretria.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Its political organisation included the oligarchic office of the aisymnetai, and Apollo Pythios had a part in its colonising ventures.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>",
"Miletus": "Miletus is a Greek city on the Anatolian coast, standing between the Greek world and that of the Near East, and its heyday came before the Classical age.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Its pottery was wanted from the Black Sea to Egypt after the middle of the 7th century BCE, and workshops making it have been identified in the Hellespont, around the Black Sea and in the Nile Delta, the very regions it was settling.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The sanctuary that mattered most to it stood outside the walls, the oracle of Apollo at Didyma, joined to the city by a Sacred Way, whose temple ranked among the largest cult buildings of the archaic period.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>",
"Ionia": "Ionia is the stretch of the western seaboard of Asia Minor held by the Ionian Greeks, running between the Hermos and the Maiandros rivers and taking in the islands of Samos and Chios.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Herodotus counted twelve cities there, the dodekapolis, and the name stayed slippery afterwards, meaning in some later writers the cities of the Ionian League and in others the Greeks of Asia Minor at large.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Its neighbours were Lydians and Karians as well as Dorians and Aeolians, and its people served as mercenaries in Egypt and the Near East, so that its identity stayed soft and open until the Persian invasion.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>",
"Panionion": "The Panionion is the common sanctuary of the Ionian Greeks and the place of the festival called the Panionia, which was held either there or in one of the member states, repeated attendance keeping the bonds between them alive.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Its god is Poseidon Helikonios, one of three divinities counted as distinctively Ionian, and individual cities and the Ionian colonies on the Black Sea kept shrines of their own to him.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Where the archaic sanctuary stood is disputed, one identification placing it in the Mycale beside the Carian town of Melia, which would date the founding of the Ionian League to about 600 BCE.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>",
"Artemision_at_Ephesus": "The Artemision at Ephesus is the sanctuary of Artemis outside that Greek city on the Anatolian coast, counted in Hellenistic times among the seven wonders of the world.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Beneath its 4th-century temple lies an archaic predecessor, which ranked with the temple of Hera on Samos and the temple of Apollo at Didyma among the largest and most spectacular cult buildings of its age.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The temple was lost for centuries and found again only in 1870, and the community that raised it remains far less well understood than the sanctuary, its earlier levels lying under substantial Hellenistic and Roman ruins.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>",
"Greek_colonisation": "Greek colonisation is the modern name for the settlements Greek communities planted overseas between roughly the 8th and 6th centuries BCE, reaching from southern Italy and Sicily to the Propontis and the shores of the Black Sea.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Foundations sent out from a mother city are distinguished from those sent out by earlier colonies, and the movement of settlers and their later settling-down are treated as two ends of one process rather than separate subjects.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Much of the older picture has been questioned since the 1990s, particularly how far such ventures were organised at all and how the settlers stood towards the populations already living where they arrived.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>",
"Apoikia": "An apoikia is a settlement planted by an ancient Greek community away from the place that sent it out, and it has been read as an organised emigration whose emphasis falls on departure from the oikos, the household.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Those who founded one were apoikoi, while settlers despatched later to strengthen a foundation already standing were epoikoi, and in the 4th century BCE nearly all Athenian foundations became cleruchies and their settlers cleruchs.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The word stands beside emporion, and where the line between the two falls is much argued over, one position making the emporion a proto-settlement rather than a trading station.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>",
"Oikist": "An oikist is the leader of an ancient Greek expedition sent out to found a settlement overseas, a part that fell to members of the elite, and among his duties as founder was the introduction of religious and political institutions brought from the homeland.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> He was invested with sacral authority by Delphi, and it was probably he who marked out the temenoi, the precincts of the gods, in the new place.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> After death he was honoured with a cult of his own and buried in the middle of the city, an arrangement that gave a new settlement, which had no old monuments, one way of making itself a past.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>",
"Cyrene": "Cyrene is the Greek city planted inland in Libya, and on both the written and archaeological evidence it was the largest and most prosperous Greek city there, standing at the head of a group of towns including Tauchira, Barca, Euesperides and the coastal Apollonia.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Thera's part in the original foundation is attested by many writers, though the island was only a starting point, and other parts of the Greek world shared in the settlement or in the expansion that followed.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Its economy was tied to silphium, and power lay at first with the Battiad monarchy, restructured in the 6th century BCE by Demonax of Mantinea.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>",
"Battus": "Battus is the Theran who led the settlers to Cyrene in Libya and was acknowledged by the Cyreneans as founder and ruler, holding both roles at once; his tomb stood in the agora, and the royal privileges the first colonists conceded him passed to his descendants.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> No other individual founder of a Greek settlement receives as much attention in the ancient literary sources.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The tradition about him was shaped by those who reported it, so that a hero cult set him beside the heroes of the Mycenaean past while informants hostile to his dynasty passed on an unflattering picture of the man himself.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>",
"Syracuse": "Syracuse is the Greek city planted in Sicily by Corinth, and Thucydides names Archias, a Bacchiad, as the man who led the expedition out.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> It grew from the island of Ortygia, which carries the spring Arethusa, whose nymph is prominent on the city's coins of the late 6th and early 5th centuries BCE, and where votive deposits go back to the 7th.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Artemis is associated with the island, and her cult there is at once uniquely Syracusan and a link back to Corinth, from which it was imported; the Peloponnesian river Alpheos, which runs past Olympia, was held to be the source of the spring.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>",
"Megara_Hyblaea": "Megara Hyblaea is the Greek city planted in eastern Sicily by Megarians shortly after Syracuse, which in turn sent out Selinus, and it arrived among the well-developed native Pantalica and Thapsos culture.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> It had a town plan from its earliest years, shown by 8th-century foundations that keep domestic and public ground apart and by the early separation of its cemeteries from the settlement.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Governed by an oligarchy broad enough to stop any member setting up as tyrant, it was destroyed by Gelon of Syracuse in 483 or 482 BCE, and its people were absorbed.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>",
"Gela": "Gela is the Greek city of Sicily planted by settlers from Crete and Rhodes in 688 BCE, some 45 years after Corinth founded Syracuse and about the time the Sicilian colonies began sending out daughters of their own.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Of the Sicilian sites carrying evidence for early town planning it is the one studied in most detail, and the evidence from it proves a preconceived urban structure.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The small sanctuary at Bitalemi just outside the city, probably a Thesmophorion, was founded in the middle of the 7th century BCE and mostly abandoned after the Carthaginians destroyed the city in 405 BCE.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>",
"Acragas": "Acragas, the modern Agrigento, is a Greek city of Sicily that has come down with no named founders at all, which left Pindar free to place the ancestors of its tyrant Theron in those legendary roles and to build the family a Panhellenic heroic genealogy.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The crab on its coins stands for the river Akragas, which the odes written before Theron came to power already dwell on, so that the later emphasis tied him to a symbol the city already had.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Its temples are famous enough to distort study of it, the splendour of the Valley of the Temples drawing attention from the ground between and around them.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>",
"Selinus": "Selinus is the Greek city planted in western Sicily by Megara Hyblaea in the second half of the 7th century BCE, and it brought the Greeks up to the edge of Carthage's sphere of influence.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Hemmed in by Phoenicians to the west, Greeks to the east and Elymians and other native peoples to the north, it had a position that governed its foreign policy and its internal development alike.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Between the late 7th century and 460 BCE it carried through one of the greatest building programmes of the ancient world, no fewer than seven temples of more than 112,000 tons of stone.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>",
"Sybaris": "Sybaris is the Greek city planted in southern Italy by an expedition from Achaia towards the end of the 8th century BCE, which sent out daughters of its own, founding Metapontion about 630 BCE with help from Corinth and Poseidonia a generation later without it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> It shared with Croton, Metapontion and Poseidonia a set of conspicuous extra-urban sanctuaries, each with a temple of Hera standing in the chora, which may have been read as marks of a common Achaean identity.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Croton destroyed it, and enough survives to show it covered an enormous area; the site was settled again as Thurii, a Panhellenic foundation of 444 to 443 BCE.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>",
"Croton": "Croton is the Greek city planted in southern Italy by an expedition from Achaia towards the end of the 8th century BCE, whose great sanctuary was the Heraion on Cape Lacinium south of the city.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> It claimed an antiquity of its own by taking Herakles for an ancestor, then took the cult of Philoktetes from the non-Greek communities between it and Sybaris, and destroyed that city after defeating it in 510 BCE.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Pythagoras of Samos emigrated there and founded a puritanical way of life built on the doctrine of reincarnation, and after that victory the city seems to have been dominant in the region under a community led by him.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>",
"Taras": "Taras is the Greek city of southern Italy founded from Sparta in the 8th century BCE, whose foundation traditions are tied to the Messenian war and turn on the Partheniai, surviving in variant forms both in the colony and in Sparta itself.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Archytas presided over it from the mid-380s to the mid-350s BCE in what seems to have been a form of democracy, being said to have been elected general seven times, and under him the city led the Italian League.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Hostilities with Rome broke out in 282 and 281 BCE and ended with the city seeking military help from Pyrrhus of Epirus.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>",
"Cumae": "Cumae is the first Greek colony on the Italian mainland, dated by archaeology no later than 730 to 720 BCE, and founded a little after the island settlement of Pithekoussai off the Bay of Naples.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> It is where the Cumaean Sibyl gave her prophecies, and a long gallery cut into the rock has for many years been shown as her grotto, though as it now looks it began as a covered way for troops climbing to the acropolis.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> A votive deposit from the lower terrace of that acropolis held terracotta body parts, masks, small altars and statuettes, every one of the statuettes found without its head.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>",
"Human_evolution": "Human evolution is the process of change by which modern people descended from apelike ancestors, and the field of study that reconstructs it from fossils, stone tools and ancient DNA.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Humans and the great apes of Africa share a common ancestor that lived between about 8 and 6 million years ago, and most of what came after took place in Africa, where habitual upright walking is evident well before brains grew large.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Some 15 to 20 species of early human are recognised, several of them alive at the same time, and of them all only Homo sapiens, which appears in Africa around 300,000 years ago, survives.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>",
"Sahelanthropus": "Sahelanthropus tchadensis is an extinct African primate, the oldest yet proposed as a member of the human branch, known from fossils recovered in the Djurab Desert of northern Chad and named in 2002.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Cosmogenic dating places it between 6.8 and 7.2 million years ago, close to the parting of the human and chimpanzee lines, and its braincase held roughly 320 to 380 cubic centimetres, much like a chimpanzee's.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> A forward-set foramen magnum and relatively small canines have been read as marks of upright walking, but the limb bones from the same locality sit closest to chimpanzees, and whether it belongs on the human branch at all is disputed.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>",
"Bipedalism": "Bipedalism is habitual movement on two legs, and in humans it means walking as the only way of getting about rather than one option among several.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> It is the oldest trait marking the human branch, evolving over 4 million years ago and long before large brains or stone tools, and reaching it rebuilt the skeleton: the opening for the spinal cord moved forward so that the head balances on the spine, and the lower back gained a forward curve found in no other animal.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The earliest direct evidence is a trail of footprints left in volcanic ash at Laetoli in Tanzania about 3.6 million years ago.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>",
"Ardipithecus": "Ardipithecus is an extinct genus of early hominins known from Ethiopia, its name joining the Afar word for 'ground' to the Greek for 'ape'.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> It holds two species, A. kadabba from about 5.8 to 5.2 million years ago and the much better known A. ramidus at about 4.4 million, which between them straddle the presumed parting of the human and chimpanzee lines.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Its anatomy is a mosaic, pairing a pelvis and foot capable of upright walking with a grasping big toe, and the argument that this makes the ancestor shared with chimpanzees unlike any living ape is both what the genus is best known for and contested.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>",
"Out_of_Africa_I": "Out of Africa I is the name given to the first spread of hominins beyond Africa, associated with Homo erectus and dated to roughly two million years ago.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Hominins were present at Dmanisi in Georgia by 1.8 million years ago, cut-marked animal bones in Romania have been dated earlier still, and the record reaches Java before 1.5 million years ago.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Those who made the journey were not large-brained, braincases of the period ranging from about 546 cubic centimetres upward, and the numeral distinguishes the episode from the far later spread of Homo sapiens, a species that appears only about 300,000 years ago.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>",
"Cooking_hypothesis": "The cooking hypothesis proposes that the control of fire for cooking drove the adaptations that define early Homo, in particular a reduced digestive system and an increased brain volume.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Its argument is energetic: heat breaks food down before it is eaten, so more energy is recovered and less gut is needed to recover it, which would make a costly brain affordable; the shift is dated to around 1.7 million years ago.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> It remains contested, because burnt material genuinely in place goes back only about a million years, and because the measured energy gain from cooking meat may be too small to repay the cost of a fire.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>",
"Shell_bead": "A shell bead is a marine or freshwater shell perforated so that it can be strung and worn, and such beads are the oldest personal ornaments whose purpose is not in doubt.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> What distinguishes a worn bead from a gathered shell is a deliberate hole together with the wear that stringing leaves: at Qafzeh in Israel, around 120,000 years ago, pierced shells carry striations beside the hole and polish where one rubbed against the next.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Pierced sea snails coated in red ochre are known from Taforalt in Morocco at roughly 82,000 years ago, and the practice extends across North Africa and southern Africa in the Middle Stone Age.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>",
"Paleolithic_burial": "A Paleolithic burial is the deliberate interment of a body during the Old Stone Age, recognised from the cut of the pit, the arrangement of the remains and any objects placed with them.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The oldest African example known to include an ornament holds an infant of four to six months at Border Cave in South Africa, laid in layers dated to 74,000 years with a perforated shell.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Later burials can be lavish: at Sungir in Russia, about 34,000 years ago, three individuals were interred with some 13,000 mammoth ivory beads between them, representing more than 2,500 hours of work.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>",
"Paleolithic_music": "Paleolithic music is the music of the Old Stone Age, known almost entirely from surviving instruments, since sound itself leaves no trace.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The oldest undisputed examples are the bone and ivory flutes of the Swabian Jura in southern Germany, among them a griffon vulture wing bone 21.7 centimetres (8.5 inches) long with four surviving finger holes and a bevelled notch cut for the mouth.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> A pierced cave bear thigh bone from Divje Babe in Slovenia, from a layer of 60,000 to 50,000 years ago, would be far older and Neanderthal, but whether its holes were cut or bitten by a carnivore remains disputed.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>",
"Recent_African_origin_of_modern_humans": "The recent African origin of modern humans is the model holding that our species arose in Africa and spread out of it, so that everyone alive descends from that African population.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> It displaced multiregional evolution, which had Homo erectus becoming modern in place across the Old World, and the case for it rested on mitochondrial DNA traced back to one African woman and on the oldest fossils of our lineage, from Jebel Irhoud in Morocco.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Its strict replacement form fell to the draft Neanderthal genome of 2010, so what stands is a recent African origin with mixing beyond the continent and subdivided populations within it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>",
"Southern_dispersal_route": "The southern dispersal route is the proposed path of modern humans out of Africa across the Strait of Bab-el-Mandeb at the foot of the Red Sea, then eastward along the shores of Arabia and southern Asia.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> It is set against a northern route by the Nile and the Sinai land bridge, and climate modelling opens a window near 65,000 years ago when the northern road shut and the southern one reopened.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Its weakness is that Bab-el-Mandeb must be crossed by water on thin archaeological evidence, and the stone tools once offered as its signature have not held up.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>",
"Archaic_human_admixture": "Archaic human admixture is the interbreeding between modern humans and the older human populations they met, chiefly Neanderthals and Denisovans, and it is read from the DNA of people living today rather than from fossils.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The draft Neanderthal genome of 2010 found Neanderthals sharing more genetic variants with living Eurasians than with people in sub-Saharan Africa, which points to gene flow, and everyone descended from those who left Africa carries a per cent or two.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Denisovans contributed 4 to 6 per cent of the genetic material of present-day Melanesians, and some inherited stretches still work, among them a Denisovan version of an oxygen-response gene carried by Tibetans.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>",
"Ancient_DNA": "Ancient DNA is genetic material recovered from the remains of the past, and it survives only in a fragmented and chemically altered form, the usable pieces typically 30 to 100 base pairs long.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> That decay is also what authenticates it, since the breakdown of cytosine leaves a distinctive pattern of changes at the ends of each fragment, separating genuinely old material from the modern contamination that is the field's standing danger.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> It has reshaped prehistory rather than decorated it: a draft Neanderthal genome showed interbreeding with our own ancestors, and a Siberian finger bone revealed the Denisovans, a population known from its DNA before its bones.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>",
"Sahul": "Sahul is the name for the single landmass that New Guinea, Australia and Tasmania formed while the sea stood low, a continent that lasted until roughly 9,000 years ago.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> It was separated from the exposed Southeast Asian shelf by Wallacea, a belt of islands joined to neither side, so it could only ever be reached across open water by a series of crossings of which at least one approached 100 kilometres (60 miles).<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> When people first arrived is contested between a long chronology of 60,000 to 65,000 years ago and a short one of 47,000 to 51,000.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>",
"New_Guinea": "New Guinea is the large island lying north of Australia, divided politically between Indonesia in the west and Papua New Guinea, which takes the eastern half together with many smaller offshore islands.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> It is mainly mountainous with low-lying plains in the south, and it formed the northern part of Sahul, the continent it made with Australia and Tasmania while the sea stood low, until roughly 9,000 years ago.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Because the belt of islands to its west was joined to neither landmass, it could only ever be reached across open water, by crossings of which at least one approached 100 kilometres (60 miles).<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>",
"Peopling_of_Europe": "The peopling of Europe is the settlement of the continent by modern humans, which happened in waves rather than once, the earliest arriving by at least 45,000 years ago while Neanderthals were still there.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Several of those first groups left no descendants at all, and the ancestry of Europeans today was built up afterwards, joined by farmers coming out of the Near East and later by herders off the eastern grasslands.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Most living Europeans therefore descend from at least three sharply different populations: western hunter-gatherers, ancient north Eurasians, and early farmers of largely Near Eastern origin.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>",
"Star_Carr": "Star Carr is an Early Mesolithic site on the shore of a vanished lake in North Yorkshire, in use between about 9300 and 8500 BCE and returned to over some eight centuries.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Along the water's edge its people laid a platform of large split timbers, the earliest post-built structures known in Britain, and wood of that age survives almost nowhere else.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> It is best known for red deer skulls worked into headdresses, the antlers cut down and the bone pierced, of which the site holds more than 90 per cent of every example known from prehistoric Europe.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>",
"Natufian_culture": "The Natufian culture is the last phase of the Epipalaeolithic in the Levant, running from about 14,500 to 11,600 years ago, and it is where people settled down before they took up farming.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> At 'Ain Mallaha, occupied for some 3,000 years, semi-subterranean curved houses of undressed limestone were cut back into the slope, in a village of about 2,000 square metres (half an acre) holding perhaps 50 to 100 people.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Its people were already reaping wild cereals, wear on their flint sickle blades showing a harvest worked through the spring some 4,500 years before those grains were domesticated.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>",
"Domestication": "Domestication is the process by which a wild species is reshaped under human keeping, typically protracted and driven more by unconscious selection than by deliberate breeding, so that using a species and altering it can be separated by thousands of years.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> It leaves a recognisable set of marks on unrelated creatures, known as the domestication syndrome: in mammals, tameness, changed coat colour, smaller teeth, floppy ears, breeding out of season and reduced brain size.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Plants show their own version, chiefly the loss of seed shattering, larger seeds and reduced dormancy, and crops from centres with no contact converge on it through the same genes.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>",
"Neolithic_founder_crops": "The Neolithic founder crops are the small group of plants first brought under cultivation in and around the Fertile Crescent, which spread as a package rather than one species at a time.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Their core is the cereals, three wheats and barley domesticated there by about 10,000 years ago, with lentils and peas as the winter pulses that travelled beside them.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Flax is the odd member, grown for oil and fibre rather than food, and the origins are not single: einkorn and emmer point to southeast Turkey, while barley may have been domesticated both in the crescent and on the Iranian plateau.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>",
"Cereal_domestication": "Cereal domestication is the change from grasses that scatter their own seed to crops that hold it, and the loss of that natural grain dispersal is the most critical step in the process.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> A wild wheat or barley breaks up along the spike as it ripens and drops its grain, a shattering governed by two genes, Brittle rachis 1 and 2, which were the target of the mutations that domesticated the crop.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> A tough spike leaves the plant unable to sow itself, and the usual picture of how it came about is slow and unconscious, though wheat in southeast Anatolia appears to have changed faster than that.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>",
"Animal_domestication": "Animal domestication is the coevolutionary relationship through which a wild species is taken into human keeping and reshaped by it, and the world's livestock arose inside settled farming communities in three independent cradles.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The oldest is Southwest Asia, where sheep, goats, pigs and cattle were taken up between about 10,500 and 10,000 years ago; then China, where pigs came after millet and rice, and the Andes, with llamas and alpacas between 9,000 and 8,000 years ago.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The relationship runs ahead of any visible change in the skeleton, Zagros goats being genetically distinct from wild ones by around 8200 BCE with morphological change following roughly a thousand years afterwards.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>",
"Massalia": "Massalia is the Greek city founded about 600 BCE by settlers from Phocaea in Ionia on the site of Marseille, and the most important Greek city of the far west.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> It lay on a strip of land between a sheltered inlet and three hills, the highest 42 metres (140 feet) above the sea, and not one of its temples has been found, so the sanctuaries are placed from what Strabo and Justin wrote long afterwards.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> It lived by trade in tin, slaves and wine, and the wine it sent inland changed the peoples of southern Gaul, who took the imported and hybrid wares into pottery of their own.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>",
"Emporion": "Emporion is the Greek trading town on the Bay of Roses in Catalonia, settled by Phocaean merchants out of Massalia in the second quarter of the 6th century BCE on a promontory local people had long held.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> It began on what Strabo calls the Palaia Polis, +g+, spread after mid-century to a mainland nucleus now called the Neapolis, and stayed a small commercial port of three or four hectares (7 to 10 acres) that struck its own silver.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Strabo has the Greek and Iberian settlements later taken into one town divided by an inner wall, and the inscriptions show the two peoples cooperating more closely than the Roman-period literary sources suggest.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>",
"Black_Sea_colonisation": "Black Sea colonisation is the planting of Greek settlements round the Pontos, mostly in the 7th and 6th centuries BCE, running from Apollonia and Histria in the west to Phasis and Dioscurias in the east.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Miletus founded most of them, the exception being Mesambria, the one Dorian polis on those shores, settled from Megara, Byzantium and Chalcedon.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Its written sources are thin and mostly much later than the events, so the dates they give are weighed against excavated material and the two often disagree: Eusebius puts the first settlement on Berezan at 647 BCE where the earliest houses and graves are of the last quarter of the century.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>",
"Byzantium": "Byzantium is the Greek city on the European side of the Bosporus, founded from Megara in the 7th century BCE and refounded by Constantine in 330 CE as the capital that took his name.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> It became a mother city itself, joining Megara and Chalcedon in settling Mesambria, but its wealth came of where it stood: currents and winds let ships through the strait only at certain times, so they gathered in convoys to wait.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Athens set it high on the tribute lists and taxed shipping there, and once Athenian sea power waned the city guaranteed passage itself and brought Rhodes to war in 220 BCE.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>",
"Olbia": "Olbia is the Milesian city on the estuary of the Bug in what is now southern Ukraine, the leading Greek settlement of the northern Black Sea.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> It grew out of the island settlement of Berezan as that place worked its crafts and pushed its farmland up the river, was laid out with cult buildings and houses from the start, and in time became a state that absorbed its own parent.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The small bronze dolphins and arrowheads found in quantity around it were long taken for early money, but they fit no known pattern of coinage and are better read as offerings to Apollo that later served as tokens of exchange.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>",
"Naucratis": "Naucratis is the Greek trading settlement in the Nile Delta, where Greeks from many cities lived and traded under an Egyptian king, given to them by Amasis on Herodotus's account as a place to live and as land for shrines and altars.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Its date is disputed, the earliest pottery showing firm occupation being put at about 615 to 610 BCE, some forty years before Amasis.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Its greatest sanctuary was the Hellenion, founded by nine cities together, and it is the one archaic settlement where the idea of Hellenism is explicit, its dedications addressed to the gods of the Hellenes rather than to any single city's god.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>",
"Greek_mercenaries_in_Egypt": "Greek mercenaries in Egypt served the pharaohs from the reign of Psammetichus I onwards, and the 26th dynasty was among the three great employers of hired Greek soldiers in the archaic age, with the tyrants of mainland Greece and of Sicily.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The Carians who served beside them are in fact better documented than the Greeks, the reverse of the impression the ancient literary sources give.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Their best-known trace is at Abu Simbel, where two men of Greek and Carian descent cut five lines into the leg of a colossus of Ramesses II recording the campaign Psammetichus II sent against the Nubians in 593 and 592 BCE.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>",
"Ancient_Italy": "Ancient Italy is the Italian peninsula in the centuries before Roman rule absorbed it, a land divided among peoples who spoke unrelated languages: Etruscan, which belongs to no Indo-European family;<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Italic tongues including Latin, Umbrian and Oscan;<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> and Messapic, Indo-European but assigned to no branch.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Writing reached them from the Greek settlements at Pithekoussai and Cumae in the first half of the 8th century BCE, and until the 6th century Greek, Etruscan, Latin and Italic writing shared a single alphabetic model.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Etruscan inscriptions cease in the 1st century BCE, by which date Latin had replaced the language even in the northern towns that held it longest.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>",
"Apennines": "The Apennines are a system of parallel mountain ranges running about 1,200 kilometres (750 miles) from north-west to south-east along the Italian peninsula, conventionally divided into northern, central and southern sectors, with the highest ground in the centre where the Gran Sasso reaches 2,912 metres (9,554 feet).<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The chain is still rising, by as much as 1 millimetre (0.04 inches) a year in its central sector, and the extensional faults taking up that movement produce earthquakes of magnitude 5.8 to 6.9.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Flat land lies to either side, sloping west towards the Tyrrhenian Sea and east towards the Adriatic,<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> while the southern front of the range is built of clay-rich flysch whose slopes fail readily.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>",
"Tiber": "The Tiber is the third-longest river in Italy, running about 405 kilometres (250 miles) from the Apennines to the Tyrrhenian Sea beside Rome and draining a catchment of roughly 17,400 square kilometres (6,700 square miles).<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Its discharge averages around 225 cubic metres a second but passes 1,500 in the highest years and falls to 60 in the lowest,<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> and a sandbank at its mouth forced laden ships to tranship into lighters.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Three phases of silting brought its floodplain at Rome to a stable 6 to 8 metres (20 to 26 feet) above sea level by the late 3rd century BCE, and the Tiber Island that splits the channel there is of tectonic origin.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>",
"Latium": "Latium is the coastal region of west-central Italy south of the Tiber, resting on a thick pyroclastic plateau laid down by two volcanic districts, Monti Sabatini north of Rome and the Alban Hills to the south, which last erupted about 70,000 and 36,000 years ago.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Its coast is shallow lagoons behind dunes, with a wide plain south of the Anzio promontory, while inland the limestone of the Monti Lepini and Monti Ausoni holds caves used for burial in the Copper and Bronze Ages.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> It was occupied from the Lower Palaeolithic onwards,<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> and by the Middle Bronze Age a workshop on the Caprolace lagoon was producing salt by boiling brine.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>",
"Alban_Hills": "The Alban Hills, known to geologists as Colli Albani, are a volcanic caldera about 20 kilometres (12 miles) south-east of Rome rising to 949 metres (3,114 feet),<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> whose eruptions from about 600,000 years ago helped lay the pyroclastic plateau the city stands on.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Their youngest vents are maars, one of which holds Lake Albano, and the date of the last eruption is unsettled between about 36,000 and 23,000 years ago, with phreatic activity reported into historical times.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The volcano is quiescent rather than extinct: the ground above its youngest vents is inflating, and carbon dioxide carrying hydrogen sulphide still vents at Cava dei Selci, where it has killed animals and forced evacuations.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>",
"Bronze_Age_Italy": "Bronze Age Italy is the period in which the peninsula and its islands developed by region rather than as one society, the communities of the Po plain settling progressively through the 2nd millennium BCE into large defended centres drawing on networks that reached continental Europe and the eastern Mediterranean.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Copper from ores in the southern Alps was traded so widely that it was almost omnipresent in the western and central Balkans,<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> while mining near the Sila Plateau made Calabria a significant metallurgical territory.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> In Sardinia the age is marked by the nuraghi, drystone towers raised between about 1700 and 1100 BCE.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>",
"Terramare_culture": "The Terramare culture is the Middle and Recent Bronze Age culture of the Po plain, named for <i>terra marna</i>, a local word for the organically rich earth of the mounds its villages left, quarried as fertiliser in the 19th century and so leading to their discovery.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Its villages were banked and sub-quadrangular, raised on alluvial ridges and laid out to a plan, the huts standing on the ground or on built platforms aligned to the perimeter works.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> They ran from a hectare or two up to 15 to 20 hectares (37 to 49 acres), lived by farming and herding, and carried the densest population of Bronze Age Italy before collapsing around 1150 BCE.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>",
"Villanovan_culture": "The Villanovan culture is the Early Iron Age culture of the Tyrrhenian side of central Italy and the southern Po plain, running from the end of the 10th century to the 8th BCE and recognised by a cremation rite in which burnt bone was sealed in a necked urn under an in-turned bowl and set in a pit with the ashes of the pyre.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Its people gathered onto plateau sites of 120 to 200 hectares (300 to 490 acres) ringed by cemeteries, a nucleation interpreted as a form of early state formation.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Those settlements became the cities of Etruria, and groups of the culture founded Pontecagnano in Campania early in the 9th century BCE.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>",
"Italic_peoples": "The Italic peoples are those of ancient Italy who spoke a language of the Italic branch of Indo-European, a grouping made by language rather than nation and one that leaves much of the peninsula outside it. Before Rome absorbed them they were many separate groups, each with its own territory, culture and language, and those identities were not primordial: distinct Italic ethnicities emerged in the Late Bronze Age and consolidated through the Iron Age.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The branch holds several languages — Latin and Faliscan on one side,<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> the Sabellic tongues of the centre and south on the other<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> — written in alphabets taken from the Etruscans or the Greeks.",
"Latins": "The Latins were the people of Latium vetus, the country between the Tiber and the Liri,<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> sharing the Latial culture, similar burial customs and a similar political organisation but never a single state.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Their settlements numbered between 78 and 107 between about 950 and 509 BCE and were linked by a dense web of roads, though power among them was unequal and Rome had become much the largest by the earlier 9th century BCE.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> They emerged as a distinct Italic ethnicity in the Late Bronze Age,<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> and DNA from Latins at Rome shows a genetic profile shared with their Etruscan neighbours despite the two speaking unrelated languages.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>",
"Latin_League": "The Latin League is a modern name for the alliances that bound the communities of Latium, and the singular is misleading: the ancient writers describe assemblies meeting at three separate sanctuaries, the spring and grove of Ferentina, the grove of Diana at Aricia and the Alban Mount, almost always run together.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The grove at Aricia was dedicated by Egerius Baebius of Tusculum, dictator of the Latins, on behalf of eight named peoples, in an inscription Cato copied out.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> On the Alban Mount 47 cities are said to have shared the festival of Jupiter Latiaris, a rite Rome directed and kept on after dissolving the league in 338 BCE.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>",
"Sabines": "The Sabines were the people of the ager Sabinus, the hill country north-east of Rome, which was never a political unit and whose borders were loose enough that ancient authors disagree over which towns lay in it; Cures, Reate and Amiternum are always counted Sabine.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Their Iron Age settlements stood on heights commanding the routes of the Tiber valley, drawing together from the 7th century BCE into single centres with their cemeteries on the hills around them.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> What they spoke is far less certain, resting on 39 words the ancient grammarians label Sabine whose sound-changes point to several languages rather than one.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>",
"Samnites": "The Samnites were the people of the central and southern Apennines, whose country was full of hillforts, sanctuaries and dispersed farms but had few if any cities of the kind the Latins and Etruscans built.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> A lidar survey of 15,300 square kilometres (5,900 square miles) found 305 suspected hillforts, and geophysics at one of 18 hectares (44 acres) turned up no trace of permanent housing, only enclosures that suit livestock.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Public life ran instead through sanctuaries such as Pietrabbondante, the centre of the Pentri, where the people met under arms and dedicated the weapons they had captured, Roman armour among them.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>",
"Oscan": "Oscan was the language of much of southern Italy, one of the Sabellic languages beside Umbrian, and is known from some 800 inscriptions found across Campania, Samnium, Lucania and Bruttium.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> It was written in an alphabet developed from the Etruscan one where Etruscan culture predominated and in a Greek-derived alphabet where Greek culture did, with the Latin alphabet used for later texts.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Most surviving texts are short dedications, epitaphs and boundary markers; after the Social War it was used in no further official document, though graffiti show it still written at Pompeii when the town was buried in 79 CE.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>",
"Umbrians": "The Umbrians were the people of ancient Umbria, a country larger than the modern region, running east and west of the Tiber and over the Apennines into what are now the Marche and Emilia-Romagna.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Pliny called them the oldest people of Italy, and by the 9th and 8th centuries BCE they were among the first communities of central Italy with a well-defined identity, though around the 6th century the Etruscans took the western ground and the Tiber became the border.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Their sanctuaries were mostly open air, and their language survives chiefly in one long ritual text from Gubbio with some thirty short inscriptions besides.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>",
"Iguvine_Tables": "The Iguvine Tables are seven bronze tablets from Iguvium, the modern Gubbio, carrying the longest ritual text to survive from ancient Italy, with no liturgy in Latin or Greek approaching them for detail.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> They were inscribed between the 3rd and 1st centuries BCE, first in the alphabet the Umbrians took from the Etruscans and later in the Latin one, and record the proceedings and prayers of a priestly association called the Atiedian Brothers.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Setting out purifications and sacrifices in about 4,000 words, they are also nearly the whole of the Umbrian language, whose other attestations are some thirty short inscriptions.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>",
"Volsci": "The Volsci were a people of central Italy who in the 5th century BCE came down from the Apennine country towards the Pontine plain south-east of Rome, where ancient writers say they took the Latin town of Satricum in 488 BCE.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Roman tradition made them a byword for hostility, though the Satricum cemetery attributed to them yielded only 16 weapons in almost 200 graves.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Their Sabellic language is counted among the minor Italic traditions of a Middle-Italic area, and survives chiefly on a miniature lead axe from a child's grave, in an alphabet unknown in Latium.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>",
"Ligurians": "The Ligurians were the Iron Age people of north-western Italy and the coast beyond, settled across what are now Liguria, Piedmont, western Lombardy and Emilia and into southern France, and taking shape between about 1600 and 900 BCE.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> They lived in castellari, hilltop forts ringed with dry-stone walls and set where the ground already defended them, and buried their leading men with weapons.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Greek and Roman writers called them barbarians and liars, a charge traceable to Cato, while Rome answered their long resistance to its expansion by moving some 47,000 of them from Liguria to Samnium in 180 and 179 BCE.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>",
"Veneti": "The Veneti were the people of north-eastern Italy, in what is now the Veneto, whose Indo-European language, close to Latin, survives on several hundred brief inscriptions of about 550 BCE to 50 CE.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Hierarchy and proto-urban settlement appear from the 8th century BCE, and their chief centres, Padua and Este, may have had a full urban layout by the 6th.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> They wrote in an alphabet taken from the Etruscans and left the bronze tablets and styluses used to teach it at the sanctuary of Reitia; they kept their independence through the Celtic invasion of the Po plain and were annexed by Rome only in the 1st century BCE.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>",
"Messapians": "The Messapians were the people of the heel of Italy, one of the three cultures — with the Peucetians and the Daunians — that made up the Iapygian civilisation of Iron Age Apulia, and all three names come from Greek documents rather than from what the peoples wrote about themselves.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Their language, Messapic, survives on some 545 recorded inscriptions running from the middle of the 6th century to the end of the 2nd century BCE, written in an alphabet modelled on the Laconian one of neighbouring Taranto.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Most are funerary, and the vase peculiar to them is the trozzella, buried chiefly with high-ranking women.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>",
"Cisalpine_Gauls": "The Cisalpine Gauls were the transalpine Celtic communities established on the Italian peninsula between the 4th and the 1st centuries BCE, chiefly in the Po valley and the Apennine hills behind it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Their arrival is dated in the epigraphy to about 400 BCE, when incomers carrying La Tène material reached a lake country that had until then belonged to the Lepontii, whose Celtic speech they absorbed before Latin in turn absorbed theirs.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Bioarchaeology finds the resulting communities quickly mixed: burials at Monterenzio Vecchio near Bologna hold locals and incomers whose grave goods cannot be told apart, and at Verona almost every individual tested was local.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>",
"Etruscan_civilisation": "The Etruscan civilisation was that of the rich communities of Tyrrhenian central Italy in the 1st millennium BCE, between the Arno, the Tiber and the sea, and absorbed at last into the Roman empire.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> A gathering of population in the 10th century BCE turned scattered villages into centres on plateaus of up to 185 hectares (460 acres); by the Archaic period these were rival city-states 15 to 25 kilometres (9 to 16 miles) apart, some lasting centuries and others dispersing within a few generations.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> No single centre ever mastered the rest, and the language, written from the 8th century BCE to the 1st, reached beyond Etruria into Campania and the Po valley before Latin replaced it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>",
"Etruscan_origins": "Etruscan origins are the long-disputed question of where the people of ancient Etruria came from, argued in antiquity between Greek writers who derived them from Anatolia and Dionysius of Halicarnassus, who held they had grown up locally.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Archaeology now favours the second answer, tracing the civilisation to late Bronze Age communities of about 900 BCE, while the question is kept alive by their non-Indo-European language.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Genomes from 82 people buried between 800 BCE and 1000 CE found steppe ancestry and no recent Anatolian admixture, and the Tyrsenian family that Etruscan shares with Lemnian and Raetic is thought to have split before the Bronze Age.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>",
"Etruscan_cities": "Etruscan cities stood on volcanic plateaus, and the four great ones of southern Etruria — Veii, Tarquinia, Caere and Vulci — each covered between 125 and 175 hectares (310 and 430 acres), on a scale nothing earlier approached.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> They seem not to have been built up all over, the evidence pointing to separate nuclei of people on each plateau, and large-scale effort went into walling them.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> A few were planned rather than accreted: Marzabotto in the Apennines was laid out at the end of the 6th century BCE on a regular plan of insulae, apparently oriented by astronomical events, and abandoned in the 4th as Celts reached the Po plain.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>",
"Etruscan_league": "The Etruscan league is the modern name for the association of twelve peoples that ancient writers describe meeting at a shared sanctuary; Livy calls it a <i>concilium</i> or <i>foedus</i>, words of much more transitory meaning than 'league'.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> No list of member cities has ever been agreed, the membership may have shifted, and no evidence from the Etruscans themselves says they were organised in such a way, their own titles being open to more than one reading.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The meeting place has nonetheless been identified as Campo della Fiera below Orvieto, where excavation has uncovered temples, a sacred way and a great field of offerings taken for the Fanum Voltumnae.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>",
"Etruscan_language": "The Etruscan language was the non-Indo-European speech of ancient Etruria, written from the 8th century BCE to the 1st in an alphabet taken from Greek colonists and then pruned of letters for sounds it did not have, among them the voiced plosives and the vowel /o/.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> It inflects nouns for four cases and a locative, leaves the verb uninflected for person or number, and forms family names in <i>-na</i> and <i>-ra</i>.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Its isolation is not total, since it shares several traits with Raetic and Lemnian, but most of what survives is names and repeated formulas, and it was largely extinguished by the end of the 1st millennium BCE.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>",
"Pyrgi_Tablets": "The Pyrgi Tablets are three gold sheets found in 1964 in the sanctuary at Pyrgi, the harbour town of the Etruscan city of Caere, two of them inscribed in Etruscan and the third in Phoenician.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> All three record a dedication made about 500 BCE by Thefarie Velianas, whom the Phoenician text calls king over Cisra, an old name for Caere, giving a sacred place to Astarte, the goddess the Etruscan sheets call Uni-Astre.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The two languages do not correspond word for word, so the sheets fall well short of a Rosetta Stone for Etruscan, but they count among the oldest direct historical records surviving from pre-Roman Italy.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>",
"Etruscan_religion": "Etruscan religion is the body of belief and ritual practised in Etruria, known mostly from what its practice left behind, since little of its literature survives.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Offerings were made in caves, water and the ground before cult buildings and altars appear, words are inscribed on votive objects from about the second quarter of the 1st millennium BCE, and temple facades were dressed in painted terracotta.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Roman categories fit it poorly, for one sanctuary could take dedications to several gods, while the reading of divine will from thunder, lots and the entrails of sacrificed animals became the practice for which the Etruscans were best known abroad.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>",
"Haruspicy": "Haruspicy is divination by inspecting the entrails of a sacrificed animal, above all the liver, whose head, the <i>caput iecinoris</i>, mattered more to the reader than any other part.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> It is associated with the Etruscans, who may owe something to the older extispicy of Assyria and Babylonia, where clay models of a sheep's organs were made in quantity and carried west.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The bronze liver of Piacenza, made around 100 BCE, is cut into cells inscribed with divine names and marks a favourable side off from an unfavourable one, and at Rome the answers haruspices gave the Senate favoured expansion from the early 3rd century BCE.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>",
"Etrusca_disciplina": "The Etrusca disciplina is the Roman name for the body of Etruscan religious learning, the rules by which the signs the gods sent were recognised and read.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> It was set down in books the Romans sorted into three classes, on entrails, on lightning and on ritual, and its origin was told as a revelation made to the Etruscans by the child Tages and the nymph Vegoia.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Great families kept the books; Rome read them in Latin translation, and what survives is quotation and paraphrase in Roman authors rather than any Etruscan copy.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>",
"Etruscan_tomb_painting": "Etruscan tomb painting is the wall painting of rock-cut chamber tombs in Etruria, most of it at Tarquinia, and it is the fullest picture surviving of how the Etruscans buried their dead.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Small stereotyped trees with blue-green leaves and black berries punctuate the walls, birds in their branches, and a ring of them is thought to have made the chamber a sacred precinct.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The subjects are the funeral, with games, dances and the banquet, while ceilings copy woven cloth to evoke the tents raised over the rites, and later tombs turn to the underworld and its demons.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>",
"Tarquinia": "Tarquinia was a city of southern Etruria, its town on the Civita plateau and its cemeteries on the Monterozzi ridge opposite, which holds the painted chambers it is best known for.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Ancient writers made Tarchon its founder and put near it the furrow from which Tages emerged, while Roman tradition traced the Tarquin kings to a Corinthian settled in the city.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Its temple was the Ara della Regina, its port the Greek-frequented emporion of Gravisca, and Rome absorbed it by stages, planting a colony at Gravisca in 181 BCE and making Tarquinia a municipium after the Social War.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>",
"Caere": "Caere was an Etruscan city on the plateau where Cerveteri now stands, about 150 hectares (370 acres) of tufa between two stream valleys, known to Greek traders as Agylla and the most thoroughly hellenised of the Etruscan cities.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> An Athenian colony was settled there by about 600 BCE, an Ionian workshop made the painted Caeretan hydriae between 540 and 510, and Attic pottery reached the city in quantity.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Its ports were Pyrgi and Alsium, its cemetery was the Banditaccia, and its long friendship with Rome ended in annexation, the Caerites being remembered as the first people made Roman citizens without the vote.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>",
"Banditaccia_necropolis": "The Banditaccia necropolis is the principal cemetery of the Etruscan city of Caere, covering a plateau across a valley from the town, its rock-cut chamber tombs set under earthen mounds.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Chamber interiors imitate the rooms of a house and develop in plan until about the middle of the 6th century BCE, after which the tombs are laid out along planned roads with uniform exteriors.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> From the 4th century BCE the façades cease and tombs are cut along roads sunk into the tuff, while cippi, stelae and stone lions above ground carried the memory of the dead.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>",
"Veii": "Veii was an Etruscan city on the right bank of the Tiber, the nearest of the Etruscan cities to Rome, standing on a tufa plateau bounded by the Valchetta stream and the Fosso Piordo, its walls enclosing 190 hectares (470 acres).<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Roman writers remembered it as the most powerful city in Etruria in the earliest days and recorded a long series of wars with Rome, ending in its storming by Camillus in 396 BCE.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Its sanctuary at Portonaccio drew gifts from across Etruria, and although the city ended, excavation shows the site occupied at lower intensity for centuries afterwards.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>",
"Vulci": "Vulci was an Etruscan city above the river Fiora in southern Etruria, ranking with the greatest and worshipping Uni, Vei, Fufluns, Suri and Thufltha.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The emperor Claudius named two Vulcian brothers, Aulus and Caelius Vibenna, in the speech preserved on a bronze tablet at Lyon, adding that their companion Mastarna ruled Rome as Servius Tullius.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Rome took its coastal strip in 280 BCE, and its cemeteries, where the Cuccumella tumulus carried sphinxes, lions and two giant tower-cippi, were opened on a huge scale from 1828 and sent Etruscan and Attic material into museums and private collections across Europe.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>",
"Etruscan_bronzework": "Etruscan bronzework is the metalwork of Etruria in copper alloy, comprising vessels, mirrors, statuettes and furniture fittings, and it travelled further than anything else the Etruscans made.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Beaked jugs of the type called Schnabelkanne, most of them made between 525 and 400 BCE, reached Switzerland, the Rhine valley and Gaul in large numbers, and corded cistae, situlae and stamnoi went with them.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> At home the engraved mirrors are the most closely studied class, while the tin that the alloy requires has no proven Etruscan source, no tin slag having been found in Tuscany.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>",
"Bucchero": "Bucchero is the black pottery of Etruria, coloured right through its thickness rather than coated, and it was already being called the national pottery of the Etruscans in the 19th century.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Technically it is a primitive ceramic: firing and the process after it are exclusively reducing, at a low 600 to 700 °C (1,100 to 1,300 °F), the lustre comes from burnishing before firing, and there is no slip.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Finely made pieces appear around Caere in the early 7th century BCE, production later intensifies in the relief-figured workshops of inland Etruria, and the ware runs down to coarser undecorated shapes.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>",
"Apollo_of_Veii": "Apollo of Veii is a terracotta statue of the god made at the end of the 6th century BCE for the roof of a temple in the Portonaccio sanctuary at Veii, and it retains much of its original paint, brown on the bare flesh and white on the black-hemmed clothing.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> It strides barefoot with the archaic smile, one arm thrust forward, and formed part of a group in which Herakles has seized the golden-horned hind sacred to Artemis.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The temple was dismantled around 300 BCE and its sculpture ritually buried, which is how the statue survived almost intact.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>",
"Etruscan_Campania": "Etruscan Campania is the Etruscan settlement and rule that reached south of Latium into the plain behind the Bay of Naples, with Etruscan-speaking communities attested in two clusters, one around Capua and one around Pontecagnano.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Roman writers made Capua's foundation Etruscan and remembered an Etruscan lordship over the region, while Pliny wrote that the ager Picentinus to the south had once belonged to the Etruscans.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> What kind of political control lay behind that is much disputed, and the Etruscan phase closed in the later 5th century BCE as Greek settlement checked it and the Campanians formed themselves into a people.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>",
"Magna_Graecia": "Magna Graecia, or <i>Megale Hellas</i>, is the name for the Greek-settled coasts of southern Italy, and it shrank in meaning until in the Roman period it indicated the Greek inhabited areas from Cumae to Tarentum.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Settlement began in the mid-8th century BCE at Pithekoussai on Ischia and ran through Cumae, Poseidonia, Sybaris, Kroton and Taras, closing with the Panhellenic foundation of Thurii in 444 to 443 BCE.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The cities shared a common Italiote League, but there is little reliable evidence of what sense of communal regional identity their inhabitants had, and the region may owe more to Roman perceptions than to regional self-assertion.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>",
"Coinage": "Coinage is metal issued in standardised pieces under a stamp, the stamp adding an authority's guarantee to weighed bullion so that a payment can be counted instead of weighed and tested.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The western series began in seventh-century Anatolia and in electrum, a pale alloy of gold and silver, with the Lydian issues opening probably under Gyges, while spade coins were being cast in bronze at Guanzhuang in Henan at about the same date.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Most explanations of its adoption settle on the state, which needed to make acceptable payments and then wished to standardise and protect the currency, though what Greece used before coins is still unsettled.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>",
"Electrum": "Electrum is a pale alloy of gold and silver, occurring naturally in the stream beds of western Anatolia, and it was the metal of the earliest coinage.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The coins were not struck from the alloy as it came: analysis of the Lydian lion-head issues shows a metal artificially debased to about 54 per cent gold, 44 per cent silver and one or two per cent copper.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Refining installations outside the walls of Sardis parted gold from silver with salt and heat in the first half of the 6th century BCE, and around its middle Croesus replaced the electrum coinage with pure gold and pure silver.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>",
"Croesus": "Croesus is the last king of Lydia, fifth of the Mermnad line, who held the throne from about 560 BCE and under whom the Lydian empire reached its greatest extent, east as far as the river Halys.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Lydian wealth in gold and silver was proverbial, drawn from the mountain streams north of Mount Tmolus, and he replaced the kingdom's electrum with a coinage of pure gold and pure silver.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Sardis fell to Cyrus after a fourteen-day siege, dated to 547 BCE on the Nabonidus Chronicle, and what is handed down of his dynasty's rise and his legendary wealth is largely tales.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>",
"Weight_standard": "A weight standard is the weight a coinage's denominations are struck to, and it decides whether one city's silver can be paid out against another's.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> It is not stated in the ancient sources but inferred from the coins, by taking the heaviest surviving examples of a denomination, or the 80th percentile of a large sample, to approximate it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Much early electrum clings to the Lydo-Milesian standard, whose stater runs to about 14.1 grams (0.50 ounces), the Aeginetan stater to about 12.4 grams (0.44 ounces), and the Attic standard came in time to dominate the coinage of the Greek world.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>",
"Panhellenic_sanctuary": "A Panhellenic sanctuary is a sanctuary used by Greeks from many cities rather than by one, the four best known being Olympia, Delphi, Isthmia and Nemea, which held the crown games.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The label reaches wider than those four, taking in Dodona, Delos and the sanctuary of Zeus on Mount Lykaion in Arcadia, and the games at each grew with the site that housed them.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The idea that such places stood for Greek unity sits badly with the evidence: they were fought over, and dedication in them was conspicuous and regularly confrontational, a monument read against its neighbours.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>",
"Olympic_Games": "The Olympic Games are the athletic festival held at the sanctuary of Zeus at Olympia, the senior of the four crown games of the Greek world.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The traditional date of 776 BCE rests on a victor list compiled around 400 BCE by Hippias of Elis, who had no such list to work from and settled on that year because it synchronised Iphitus with Lycurgus.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The events fall into running, combat, pentathlon and equestrian classes, though the programme can be reconstructed only hypothetically, and the games were held until at least 385 CE.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>",
"Olympic_truce": "The Olympic truce, the <i>ekecheiria</i>, is the arrangement surrounding the games at Olympia, and it guaranteed a temporary safe trip for athletes, coaches and spectators rather than any suspension of war.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> During the Olympic Games arms were not laid down in the Greek world, and the truce served as a political instrument like any other: the secession of Mytilene was planned during the games of 428 BCE.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Commentators wanting to stress the ideals of peace and brotherhood claimed by the modern Olympics have made much of this so-called sacred truce, which in no way sought to promote peace.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>",
"Sparta": "Sparta is the Greek city of Laconia, made up of several villages with outlying Amyklai rather than a single walled town, holding a territory of roughly 8,000 square kilometres (3,100 square miles).<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> It rested on the helots, enslaved as a nation to the Spartan state, and every Spartiate was a hoplite where in other cities the hoplites were the wealthiest 30 to 50 per cent.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Its history is hard to unlock, since there are very few direct Spartan sources, what survives is mostly non-Spartan, and all of it is severely influenced by the Spartans' manipulation of their own image.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>",
"Laconia": "Laconia is the region of the southern Peloponnese that Sparta ruled, its heartland the Eurotas valley, framed by Mount Taygetos on the west and Mount Parnon on the east.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Sparta's territory ran to roughly 8,000 square kilometres (3,100 square miles), and survey of the country east of the city found very little long-term continuity, each broad period laying down anew its own pattern of settlement.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Who worked the land is a question the survey raised and could not settle, whether Spartiates, perioikoi, helots or some inferior group, and Laconian helotage outlasted Messenian liberation by centuries.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>",
"Lacedaemon": "Lacedaemon is the Spartan state, distinct from Sparta the city: the free inhabitants of the many towns of Laconia and Messenia, along with Sparta itself, constituted the state known as Lakedaimon.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Those towns are the perioikic communities, <i>poleis</i> in their own right but politically dependent on Sparta, and whether their people were citizens of the Spartan <i>polis</i> at all is disputed.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <i>Spartiatai</i> and <i>Lakedaimonioi</i> can each be used of the <i>homoioi</i>, or more broadly of the citizens of the Spartan <i>polis</i> and of the Lakedaimonian state, and below both orders stood the helots.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>",
"Messenian_Wars": "The Messenian Wars are the wars by which Sparta took Messenia, whose people were reduced to the status of helots after the conquest of their country in the 8th and 7th centuries BCE.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Their historicity is not easily reconstructed: for fifth-century historians such as Antiochus of Syracuse and Thucydides there was only one archaic war, and an account with two was established in the 4th century, elaborated by Myron of Priene and Rhianus and adapted freely by Pausanias.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> When Sparta expanded there was no Messenian ethnic identity, the region becoming coherent only under Spartan control, and a free Messenian state came only with the liberation of 370 to 369 BCE.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>",
"First_Messenian_War": "The First Messenian War is the earlier of the two archaic wars in which Greek tradition held that Sparta conquered Messenia and reduced its people to the status of helots.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Tyrtaeus, the nearest thing to a contemporary witness, named King Theopompus, wrote of the spearmen fathers of his hearers' fathers, and gave the fighting nineteen years, the enemy fleeing the uplands of Ithome in the twentieth.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Pausanias dated it to 743 to 724 BCE on Hellenistic reckoning from the Spartan kinglists, though a lower chronology, argued from Epaminondas's statement that he had freed Messenia after 230 years of subjection, puts it around 690 to 670 BCE.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>",
"Second_Messenian_War": "The Second Messenian War is the later of the two archaic wars between Sparta and Messenia, and the ancient accounts make it a revolt of Messenians already under Spartan rule rather than a fresh conquest.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Tyrtaeus, who rallied the Spartans in it, wrote later than Archilochus and Callinus and no earlier than about 640 BCE, which lodges the war in the second half of the 7th century against Pausanias's date of 685 to 668.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Its Messenian hero is Aristomenes, who won several victories before being betrayed and withdrawing to Mount Eira, and whose existence as a real person cannot be established.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>",
"Tyrtaeus": "Tyrtaeus is the Spartan elegiac poet of the 7th century BCE whose verse is the nearest thing to a contemporary account of Sparta's conquest of Messenia, and Alexandrian scholars had five books of him.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> They held a poem on citizenship, exhortations in elegiac verse and war songs, and Athenaeus records that on campaign each Spartan was to give one of them once the paean had been sung.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The hortatory poem later called the <i>Eunomia</i> urged the people to obey the kings and the elders, and Aristotle cites it as evidence that men pressed by the war demanded a redistribution of the land.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>",
"Helots": "Helots are the enslaved population of Laconia and Messenia who worked the land for the Spartiates of ancient Sparta, having been reduced to that status by conquest.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> They rendered a proportional share of the crop, put at half, normally stayed attached to the holdings they cultivated rather than to particular masters, and were at once the private property of individual Spartiates and subjects of a communal domination that freed and killed them without reference to those masters.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> What to call the arrangement is disputed, one account describing a nation enslaved to the Spartan state and another an individual ownership restricted enough not to be chattel slavery.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>",
"Perioikoi": "Perioikoi are the free people of the towns spread across Laconia and Messenia that made up the ancient Greek state of Lakedaimon alongside Sparta itself.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> They had a degree of local autonomy and their communities were thought of as poleis in their own right, yet they were politically dependent on the Spartans and could neither hold office in Sparta nor sit in its assembly.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Their settlements were small, of 400 to 600 people, they farmed on their own behalf and on that of the kings, and whether they were really city-states or villages, and their people citizens of Sparta at all, has been argued both ways.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>",
"Spartiates": "Spartiates are the citizens of Sparta itself in ancient Greece, the word being used narrowly of those with full political rights, the <i>homoioi</i> or 'equals', and broadly of anyone belonging to the Spartan <i>polis</i> by birth.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Full standing rested on monthly contributions of produce to the common messes, and a man who could not make them dropped into the <i>hypomeiones</i>, the 'lesser men'.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Their equality was an ideal rather than a fact, since land could be alienated and daughters inherited alongside sons, and Aristotle has their number fall from 10,000 in the 5th century BCE to fewer than 1,000 in the 4th.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>",
"Krypteia": "The krypteia is a Spartan institution whose name means 'hidden thing', attributed by the ancient sources to the lawgiver Lycurgus and first mentioned in Plato's <i>Laws</i>, where it appears among the things that instilled endurance.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Aristotle, preserved by Plutarch, instead has magistrates send the most discreet young men out with daggers to hide by day and by night kill every helot they caught, with the ephors declaring war on the helots each year so that the killing carried no impiety.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Modern readings divide it three ways, as an initiation rite, a policing service and a military unit, and Plutarch himself refused to believe Lycurgus capable of it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>",
"Lycurgus": "Lycurgus is the lawgiver to whom the Spartans of ancient Greece credited their constitution and their whole way of living, honoured not with the hero cult other lawgivers received at home but as a god.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Plutarch presents the Great Rhetra as a Delphic oracle received by him, and almost every Spartan institution was put down to him, though Plato and Aristotle both made the ephorate a later addition.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> His biography is treated as unrecoverable, but the tradition is old: an independent 7th-century tradition already ascribed the Rhetra's institutions to a Lycurgus, and his character, laws and customs were redefined by additions and subtractions thereafter.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>",
"Great_Rhetra": "The Great Rhetra is an early constitutional charter of the Spartan state in ancient Greece, ordering a shrine to Zeus Syllanius, the people divided into <i>phylai</i> and <i>obai</i>, and a council of thirty including the kings.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The assembly is called from time to time between Babyca and Cnacion, proposals put and withdrawn there, and the main decision and power given to the <i>damos</i>.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Two provisions pull against that, the council's power to set the agenda and a rider allowing the elders and kings to annul a crooked choice, and it survives only through Plutarch and Diodorus, its sentiments dating at least to Tyrtaeus's time.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>",
"Spartan_dual_kingship": "The Spartan dual kingship gave ancient Sparta two hereditary kings at once, one from each of two royal families, constitutionally equal in authority so that each was a check upon the other's power.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Under the Great Rhetra they sat in the council of thirty as two of its members with no special privileges, and Aristotle limited their importance to the leadership of the army on campaign.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Strictly speaking the kings were not among the <i>homoioi</i>, identifying themselves as Achaeans rather than Dorians and not passing through the Spartan upbringing, and they had special ties with the perioikoi, who farmed on their behalf.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>",
"Agiads_and_Eurypontids": "The Agiads and Eurypontids are the two royal houses of ancient Sparta, each supplying one of the two kings who ruled together, and both claiming descent from Heracles through his son Hyllus.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Tradition has Aristodemus conquering Laconia and his twin sons Eurysthenes and Procles becoming the first co-rulers, though the houses are named not for those twins but for Agis and Eurypon, mythical kings further down each line.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The Agiads were traditionally the senior house, the two lists of reigns are how early Sparta gets dated at all, and the descent underneath was a construction serving hereditary right, priestly function and a title to dominion.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>",
"Gerousia": "The gerousia is the council of elders of ancient Sparta, thirty strong, 28 <i>gerontes</i> with the two kings making up the number, each chosen for life from men past sixty.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> It introduced proposals to the assembly and withdrew them, so that the people could approve what it put but promote no law of their own, and it was the only Spartan court that could pronounce death or exile.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Election was by acclamation, candidates passing through the assembly in silence while sealed judges marked the loudness of each shout, a procedure Aristotle called childish and which has since been read as an early form of approval voting.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>",
"Ephors": "The ephors are the magistrates who oversaw the state in ancient Sparta, an office the ancient sources date very differently, Plato making it a third stage in the constitution and Aristotle crediting King Theopompus with adding it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Xenophon says they could fine whom they chose and enact immediate payment, deprive magistrates of office, imprison them and prefer a capital charge, punishing a lawbreaker the moment they saw one.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> They could also set business before the assembly without the council of elders having to agree among itself, and their social origin is sought in well-placed aristocratic families rather than in the people.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>",
"Apella": "The apella is the assembly of the citizens of ancient Sparta, and even the name is doubtful, since no ancient writer but Plutarch uses it and the word goes back to Apollo, Apellon in the Doric dialect.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> It met between Babyca and Cnacion with neither halls nor any other kind of building, on the reasoning that statues, paintings and decorated roofs made the serious purposes of an assembly foolish and futile.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> No one assembled there was permitted to make a motion, what the elders and kings laid before them being accepted or rejected and nothing more, and the decision was taken by shouting.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>",
"Agoge": "The agoge is the public upbringing of boys in ancient Sparta, in which charge of them passed from the father to a Warden drawn from the class that filled the highest offices, backed by youths carrying whips.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The boys went unshod and wore one garment through the year, were fed moderately enough to know hunger unsatisfied, and were allowed to relieve it by stealing, a boy caught being beaten with many stripes.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The word itself belongs to the Hellenistic and Roman periods and is not used before the middle of the 3rd century BCE, so how far the later system continued the classical one is disputed.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>",
"Emery": "Emery is a dark, granular rock hard enough to grind and cut other stone, made up largely of corundum, the crystalline form of aluminium oxide, together with iron oxides and a few other minerals.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> It forms where bauxite laid down in weathered limestone is buried and metamorphosed, so its deposits sit as lenses inside marble; in Greece such bodies occur through the metamorphic Attic-Cycladic massif, most abundantly on Naxos and on Ikaria.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Its hardness made it an abrasive long before modern industry: Bronze Age carvers cut marble with pointed emery tools, incised detail with sharp emery flakes, and polished the finished surface with emery sand.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>",
"Pumice": "Pumice is a highly vesicular volcanic rock, ejected in eruptions and typically silicic in composition: it is essentially magma that has been frothed up by escaping gases and then cooled and solidified as it erupted.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The gas bubbles trapped as the melt stiffened leave it riddled with cavities and very light for its size, and the same violent frothing gives its broken surfaces a harsh, rasping texture.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Those two properties account for its uses, the main one being as an aggregate in lightweight building blocks and building products, alongside work as an abrasive, an absorbent, a concrete admixture, a filter aid and a horticultural medium.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>",
"Skarkos": "Skarkos is an Early Bronze Age settlement on the island of Ios in the Cyclades, standing on a hill above a coastal plain about a kilometre (0.6 miles) from one of the largest natural harbours in the island group.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> It is the largest known site of its period in the islands and much the best preserved: at least 55 rectangular two-storey buildings have been uncovered, set out with wide streets and squares and a drainage system, their walls still standing four metres (13 feet) high.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Radiocarbon dates on animal bone put the end of its main phase between about 2550 and 2500 BCE.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>",
"Ios": "Ios is one of the Cyclades, in the south-central Aegean, a hilly island whose coastal plain on the western side runs down to one of the largest natural harbours in the island group.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Ancient writers tied the island to Homer: Pausanias records an oracle telling the poet that Ios was his mother's homeland and would receive him when he died, and adds that the islanders showed visitors a tomb they said was his.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The island also carries substantial prehistoric remains, among them the Early Bronze Age settlement on the hill of Skarkos above that western plain.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>",
"Aegean_Sea": "The Aegean Sea is the north-eastern arm of the Mediterranean, lying between the coasts of eastern and northern Greece and those of western Turkey, with Crete closing it off to the south; one recent study puts its area at roughly 160,000 km² (62,000 sq mi).<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> For a partly enclosed basin it is unusually well connected: the Dardanelles link it in the north-east to the Sea of Marmara and the Black Sea, the Cretan passages carry water to and from the central Mediterranean, and the straits of Antikythera, Kythera and Elaphonissos open west to the Ionian.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Its islands are scattered across 800 kilometres (500 miles) from north to south.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>",
"Cyclades": "The Cyclades are a group of islands in the south-central Aegean Sea, forming a virtual land bridge between mainland Greece and Asia Minor; the largest of them is Naxos, at about 428 km² (165 sq mi).<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The name is at least as old as the 5th century BCE, used by Herodotus and Thucydides, and it describes islands understood as a circle drawn around Delos.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Geologically they are the peaks of a mountainous landmass submerged some five million years ago: the northern islands are rich in crystalline rock such as granite, gneiss, marble and schist, part of the metamorphic massif that also carries the region's emery,<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> while Melos, Kimolos and Thera are volcanic.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>",
"Early_Bronze_Age": "The Early Bronze Age is the first of the three phases — Early, Middle and Late — into which archaeologists divide the Bronze Age of a region.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Because the Bronze Age is marked by a technology rather than by a date, the phase opens and closes at different times in different places: the Near East reaches it by about 3300 BCE, other regions much later,<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> while in central Europe radiocarbon dating places it between roughly 2150 and 1700 BCE.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Each region keeps its own labels and subdivisions, so the same phase is Early Minoan on Crete and Early Cycladic in the islands, each split again into I, II and III.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>",
"Dhaskalio": "Dhaskalio is a small rocky islet off the western tip of the island of Keros in the Cyclades, joined to it by a narrow causeway while both were in use in the Early Bronze Age.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Its steep slopes were cut into terraces to make level ground, and the buildings raised on them, some of two storeys, were of good marble that had to be brought by sea from Naxos some ten kilometres (6 miles) north; more than 7,000 tonnes (7,700 tons) of it were imported.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Massive entrances, stone-flagged stairways, a drainage system running across the whole islet and installations for casting copper mark it out among sites of its date.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>",
"Naxos": "Naxos is the largest of the Cyclades, an island of about 428 km² (165 sq mi) in the south-central Aegean.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Its bedrock belongs to the crystalline Attic-Cycladic massif, and it carries two materials that made it valuable well beyond its own shores: white marble, and lenses of emery, the corundum-rich rock long used as an abrasive, which sit within those marbles across five zones of increasing metamorphic grade.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Its stone was already being shipped across open water in the 3rd millennium BCE, when more than 7,000 tonnes (7,700 tons) of Naxian marble were carried some ten kilometres (6 miles) south to build on the islet of Dhaskalio.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>",
"Kavos": "Kavos is a coastal site at the western tip of the island of Keros in the Cyclades, where two Early Bronze Age deposits of deliberately broken marble figurines, stone vessels, pottery and obsidian were laid in the ground.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The two lie about 110 metres (360 feet) apart; the northern one was looted before archaeologists reached it, while the southern was excavated intact and yielded no human bone at all and no marble waste from either carving or breaking, showing that neither was done on the spot.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The objects were therefore broken elsewhere and single fragments carried in, most of them across open water.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>",
"Melos": "Melos is a volcanic island in the south-western Cyclades, lying on the arc of volcanoes that runs across the southern Aegean, and it is built of andesite, obsidian and other volcanic rock rather than the crystalline stone of the northern islands.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Its eruptions left beds of obsidian, the natural glass that flakes to a very sharp edge, at two places on opposite sides of the island: Agia Nychia, also called Sta Nychia, near Cape Bombarda, and Demenegakion.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> A supposed third source at Agios Ioannis was tested by neutron activation analysis and proved to be material from those two, and Melian obsidian itself travelled widely around the Aegean.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>",
"Dodecanese": "The Dodecanese are a group of Greek islands in the south-eastern Aegean, lying close to the south-western coast of Anatolia and counted with the East Aegean islands, the Sporades and the Cyclades among the archipelagos of that sea.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> They were Italian territory until the Treaty of Peace with Italy, signed in Paris in 1947, ceded fourteen named islands and their dependent islets to Greece in full sovereignty and laid down that they should be and should remain demilitarised.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Among them is Giali, a small volcanic island whose obsidian was quarried in prehistory and carried to sites across the Aegean.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>",
"Anatolia": "Anatolia is the peninsula that makes up the Asian part of Turkey, between the Black Sea, the Aegean and the eastern Mediterranean; it sits on a tectonic plate of its own, whose south-eastern edge is the East Anatolian Fault Zone, a plate-bounding strike-slip fault capable of large earthquakes.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Central Anatolia held some of the earliest farming societies outside the Fertile Crescent, with agriculture reaching it by about 8300 BCE; ancient genomes indicate its first farmers drew roughly 90 per cent of their ancestry from the hunter-gatherers already living there.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Its volcanic glass travelled far, obsidian from Göllü Dağ in the interior reaching sites out in the Aegean islands.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>",
"Radiocarbon_dating": "Radiocarbon dating establishes the age of once-living material from its content of carbon-14, a radioactive isotope made in the upper atmosphere when cosmic-ray neutrons strike nitrogen, which oxidises and mixes through the exchangeable carbon reservoir that living things draw on.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Once an organism dies it takes up no more, and the isotope decays with a half-life of 5,730 years, so the amount left is a measure of elapsed time.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> A radiocarbon age is not a calendar age, because atmospheric carbon-14 has varied; it must be converted using a calibration curve built from tree rings and other dated archives, of which the agreed northern-hemisphere version, IntCal20, reaches back 55,000 years.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>",
"Crete": "Crete is the largest and most heavily populated island of Greece, lying across the southern end of the Aegean Sea and forming its boundary with the wider Mediterranean.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> It stretches roughly 260 kilometres (161 miles) from west to east and is about 60 kilometres (37 miles) across at its widest point, and its terrain is rugged, taking in mountains, plateaus and several deep gorges.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The passages between the island and its neighbours are among the main channels through which water is exchanged between the Aegean and the central Mediterranean, so it stands athwart the sea routes as well as the currents.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>",
"Myrtos_Fournou_Korifi": "Myrtos Fournou Korifi is an Early Bronze Age settlement on the south coast of Crete, about 12 kilometres (7 miles) west of Ierapetra, on a hilltop only 30 by 50 metres (100 by 160 feet) across.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Some 65 rooms, corridors and open areas are packed into a sixth of a hectare; the whole was destroyed by a violent fire and never reoccupied, so nothing later was built over it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> First read as one complex under a single authority, it was reinterpreted by Todd Whitelaw as an agglomeration of five or six independent units of essentially the same size and complexity, and its pottery has since been examined for what its vessel shapes were used for.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>",
"Todd_Whitelaw": "Todd Whitelaw is Professor of Aegean Archaeology at the Institute of Archaeology, University College London.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> His work is on the prehistory of the southern Aegean and on reading excavated settlements for the households and communities that lived in them; his reinterpretation of the Early Minoan site of Myrtos Fournou Korifi as five or six independent units of much the same size, rather than one complex under a single authority, is the best-known instance.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> He leads the Knossos Urban Landscape Project, an intensive survey that made some 21,000 collections across 11 km² (4 sq mi) and documented occupation from about 7000 BCE to the early 20th century.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>",
"Myrtos": "Myrtos is a coastal settlement on the south coast of Crete, west of Ierapetra, whose name is attached to two separate Bronze Age sites in its neighbourhood.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> One of them, Fournou Korifi, is a small hilltop settlement of the Early Bronze Age that burned down and was never rebuilt.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The other, Myrtos-Pyrgos, stands a short distance away and was occupied far longer, from Early Minoan II to Late Minoan IB, and ended in a destruction whose radiocarbon dates are among those weighed in the long argument over when the volcano of Thera erupted.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>",
"Vasiliki": "Vasiliki is a Bronze Age settlement in eastern Crete, on the west side of the isthmus of Ierapetra.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Its excavator named the building he uncovered the House on the Hilltop and took it for a single structure; later work showed it to be two separate buildings of slightly different date, and both were destroyed by fire towards the end of the Early Minoan IIB phase.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The site gives its name to Vasiliki ware, a solidly painted but deliberately mottled, generally dark-surfaced pottery that became overwhelmingly dominant in that phase, and which museum collections date to about 2600–2200 BCE.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>",
"Genome": "A genome is the entire set of DNA instructions found in a cell, the complete collection of genetic material an organism carries.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> In humans it is held on 23 pairs of chromosomes in the nucleus together with a small separate chromosome in the mitochondria, and it runs to roughly 3 billion base pairs, of which an estimated 20,000 to 25,000 stretches are genes coding for proteins.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Reading one means determining the order of those bases: the Human Genome Project produced a very high-quality version of the human sequence, freely available in public databases, and was completed in April 2003.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>",
"Bhimbetka_rock_shelters": "The Bhimbetka rock shelters are a group of more than 700 natural hollows and caves in the Vindhyan hills of Madhya Pradesh, in central India, over 400 of them bearing paintings.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Their floors carry stone tools running from the Lower Palaeolithic through the Middle and Upper Palaeolithic to a Mesolithic industry worked in chalcedony, and one deposit is nearly four metres (13 feet) deep.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The paintings are mostly red or white, made with pigment from local haematite, and later artists worked over earlier ones, so a single shelter can show five superimposed layers running from the Mesolithic into historic times.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>",
"Dog_domestication": "Dog domestication is the process by which grey wolves became dogs, the first domestic animal and the only one taken up before farming, though where and when it happened remain unknown.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Bones alone cannot settle it, several famous Palaeolithic finds having turned out under nuclear genome analysis to be now-extinct wolves, and the earliest widely accepted dogs are European, from about 17,000 to 14,000 years ago.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Genomes place the main wolf source in eastern Eurasia, with a second southwest Eurasian source behind up to half the ancestry of dogs in the Near East and Africa, and at least five lineages had diverged by 11,000 years ago.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>",
"Pre-Pottery_Neolithic": "The Pre-Pottery Neolithic is the first part of the Neolithic in Southwest Asia, in which farming and settled villages came before pottery, and it divides into the PPNA and the PPNB.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The two were first distinguished at Jericho and differ in their stone tools, their houses and their food, with no clear sign of domestic animals in the PPNA and sheep and goats plainly domesticated in the PPNB.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Round buildings give way to square ones across the change, and the PPNB is marked by a mortuary practice in which skulls were detached, modelled over in plaster and kept.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>",
"Göbekli_Tepe": "Göbekli Tepe is a Pre-Pottery Neolithic mound on a bare limestone ridge in south-eastern Turkey, north-east of Şanlıurfa, whose earliest horizon holds circular enclosures 10 to 30 metres (33 to 98 feet) across, each ringed by T-shaped limestone pillars.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The pillars stand up to 5.5 metres (18 feet) high and carry reliefs of foxes, boars, bulls, cranes, snakes and scorpions, and several bear arms, hands and belts, so the T is usually read as a stylised human figure.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Long described as a set of sanctuaries with no houses, it has since yielded smaller rectangular buildings and more than 7,000 grinding tools, so how far anyone lived there is argued over.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>",
"Jericho": "Jericho is a town in the rift valley north of the Dead Sea whose mound, Tell es-Sultan, records more than 10,000 years of occupation, from the Late Natufian to the Ottoman period, with no clear break.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Its Pre-Pottery Neolithic levels gave the terms PPNA and PPNB their definition and hold a stone tower over 8 metres (26 feet) high with a staircase inside it, a wall beside it, and ten skulls with faces modelled over them in plaster.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The walled Bronze Age city on the same mound burnt down at the end of the Middle Bronze Age, radiocarbon on charred grain placing that fire between about 1600 and 1520 BCE.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>",
"Çatalhöyük": "Çatalhöyük is a Neolithic town on the Konya Plain in central Anatolia, known for its size, its well-preserved mudbrick architecture and its wall paintings.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Its rectangular houses were packed against one another with no streets and no doorways, so people crossed their neighbours' roofs and came down a ladder, and every building excavated was lived in, with no communal architecture found below the topmost levels.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The dead were buried beneath the floors, at least 741 of them, their walls painted with red panels, geometric designs and handprints, and ancient genomes show that those laid under one house were rarely close relatives.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>",
"'Ain_Ghazal": "'Ain Ghazal is a Neolithic settlement on the Zarqa river at the northern edge of Amman in Jordan, and one of the largest in the Levant, still growing past 12 hectares (30 acres) as the Pre-Pottery Neolithic B sites around it were abandoned.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Its best-known finds are two caches of plaster statuary buried under house floors, about ten figures 80 to 90 centimetres (31 to 35 inches) tall and eight or more busts, each moulded around a bundle of reeds bound with twine.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The final aceramic phase of the southern Levant, the PPNC, was defined from its deposits.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>",
"Sedentism": "Sedentism is a way of living with limited or no residential movement, not a stage every society passes through but one option among several.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Archaeologists read it from permanent structures, grinding stones too heavy to carry, occupation deposits deep enough to imply long repeated stays, and the animals that come to live alongside people, though terms like prolonged or regular occupation are hard to turn into measurements.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> It arose separately in several regions and often well before farming, hunter-gatherers in the Levant having built huts and drawn house mice into their settlements thousands of years before any crop was sown.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>",
"Pottery": "Pottery is fired clay shaped into vessels, and it belongs to the story of hunter-gatherers as much as to that of farmers.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The oldest dates for pots anywhere come from caves in southern China, about 20,000 to 17,000 years ago, and foragers were making their own in Japan, across the Sahara and the Nile valley, and around the Baltic, with the African evidence pointing to two or three separate inventions rather than one.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Fats absorbed into the walls of pots show what was cooked in them, and in the Baltic the pattern follows regional custom rather than local environment.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>",
"Rice_domestication": "Rice domestication is the long process by which wild wetland grasses of the genus <i>Oryza</i> became crop plants, marked above all by the loss of seed shattering, which leaves the grain on the stalk for a harvester instead of dropping it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Asian rice, <i>Oryza sativa</i>, was first domesticated in China as the japonica type from about 9,000 years ago, and the process was protracted, non-shattering forms making up barely a tenth of the rice at the earliest Lower Yangtze sites.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> West Africa domesticated a separate species, <i>Oryza glaberrima</i>, from the wild <i>Oryza barthii</i>.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>",
"Millet_domestication": "Millet domestication is the process by which two wild grasses of northern China, the ancestors of foxtail millet and broomcorn millet, became crops, marked by the loss of shattering, larger grains and denser heads and placed between about 8300 and 4300 BCE.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The earliest finds lie in a semi-arid belt of loess country between woodland and steppe, and the oldest directly dated grains, from Dadiwan, are about 7,900 years old.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Millets yield far less per hectare than wet rice, and they travelled: four waves carried dry-land farming west along the eastern Silk Road, and millet was being eaten in Atlantic Iberia by about 1200 BCE.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>",
"Maize_domestication": "Maize domestication is the process that turned teosinte, a Mexican grass carrying a handful of hard-cased seeds, into the world's most productive staple crop, beginning in south-western Mexico about 9,000 years ago.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Selection was surprisingly gentle: across 18 domestication traits its intensity was weak for every one, and the total weight of grain per plant is the one trait it does not appear to have improved at all.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Maize reached South America by at least 7,000 years ago, and whether it arrived already domesticated or was finished there is still argued over.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>",
"Independent_origins_of_agriculture": "The independent origins of agriculture are the finding that farming was not invented once and handed on but arose separately in at least eleven centres, each working whatever grew or grazed nearby, in the 12,000 to 11,000 years since the last ice age.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The vocabulary comes from Nikolai Vavilov, who coined the phrase centre of origin for the place where a crop's diversity is greatest, reasoning that a crop is oldest where it varies most.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The clearest case outside the famous centres is the New Guinea highlands, where a Neolithic emerged about a thousand years before migrants reached the region from South-East Asia.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>",
"Neolithic_demographic_transition": "The Neolithic demographic transition is the surge in human numbers that followed the arrival of farming, driven by rising fertility rather than by better survival, since mortality rose alongside it, and the growth follows the crops rather than preceding them.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> It is read from two independent proxies: the rising share of children among skeletons in cemeteries, and the count of radiocarbon dates from archaeological sites, which track each other closely.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The pattern is a boom of some four centuries followed by a decline twice as long, and the cause of the falling half is disputed between climate change and conflict driven by crowding.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>",
"Zoonotic_disease": "Zoonotic disease is illness that passes between animals and people, and keeping livestock changed the ecology of both, giving pathogens new ways to invade and persist in each.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Screening 1,313 ancient Eurasians across 37,000 years found that most kinds of microbe run through the whole record while the zoonotic ones do not: they appear from about 6,500 years ago and peak around 5,000, as livestock keeping spread.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Plague complicates rather than confirms that picture, since it turns up in Siberian hunter-gatherers 5,500 years ago, far outside the farming world, and the earliest strains lacked the machinery for the flea-borne bubonic form altogether.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>",
"Lactase_persistence": "Lactase persistence is the continued production of lactase into adulthood, which lets a person digest the sugar in milk, where losing the enzyme after weaning is the ancestral condition for humans and for every other mammal.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> About 35 per cent of people worldwide keep it, through variants in an enhancer beside the lactase gene that have arisen separately in Europe, in eastern and western Africa, in Arabia and in South Asia, and selection on the European one is among the strongest yet found in the human genome.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Milk had been widely used for thousands of years before the trait became common, which leaves what drove it up in dispute.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>",
"Secondary_products_revolution": "The secondary products revolution is a proposal, set out in 1981, that livestock were at first kept for what a carcass yields, and that the uses of a living animal — milk, wool, traction and transport — arrived together in the 4th millennium BCE and spread west from Southwest Asia.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Its 4th-millennium evidence stands, including a yoke for a pair of cattle dated 3384–3370 BCE and plough furrows sealed beneath a long barrow in southern England.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> What has been given up is the single horizon, since cattle were being worked in Southwest Asia by about 8200 BCE and milk residues sit on European pottery from the beginning of the Neolithic.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>",
"Linear_Pottery_culture": "The Linear Pottery culture is the first farming society of central Europe, running from about 5500 to 5000 BCE and named for the incised bands that wrap its fine pottery.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Its settlements cluster on the loess soils of river plains and its houses are post-framed timber longhouses, a way of building and a style of decorating that stayed strikingly uniform from the Paris basin to Ukraine.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Its people descend chiefly from Anatolian farmers with a small share of European hunter-gatherer ancestry, they seem to have lived in patrilocal households, and the culture ends around 5000 BCE amid a fall in population and several massacres.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>",
"Megalith": "A megalith is a large stone raised or set into a structure, from the Greek for big and for stone, and the word covers dolmens, passage and gallery graves, single standing stones, stone circles and rows, and megalithic buildings.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Some 35,000 survive in Europe, mostly near a coast, and modelling of 2,410 radiocarbon dates places the first megalithic graves in northwest France in the second half of the 5th millennium BCE, spreading afterwards by sea.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Structures of the kind were raised by many unrelated societies at many different dates, from Ireland to Korea, so no single origin accounts for all of them.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>",
"Stonehenge": "Stonehenge is a stone circle on Salisbury Plain in Wiltshire, built and rebuilt over some six centuries and made of two kinds of stone: the great sarsens, of which 52 of an original 80 or so remain, and the smaller bluestones raised before them.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Fifty of the 52 sarsens match the silcrete of West Woods, about 25 kilometres (15 miles) north, while the bluestones come from west Wales and the central Altar Stone from northeast Scotland, at least 750 kilometres (470 miles) away.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> It served as a burial ground from early on, holding the cremated remains of up to 58 people, several of whom had not lived on the surrounding chalk.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>",
"Ötzi": "Ötzi is a man who died high in the Ötztal Alps between 3350 and 3120 BCE and whose body was preserved in a glacier, making him one of the oldest glacier mummies known and the best-recorded European of his age.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> His genome carries no detectable Steppe ancestry and the highest Anatolian-farmer ancestry of any European of his time, and indicates darker skin than Europeans today.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> He was killed by an arrow, whose head lies between his rib cage and left shoulder blade, and the copper axe found beside him was made from ore in southern Tuscany.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>",
"Neolithic_Europe": "Neolithic Europe is the age of the continent's first farmers, which begins about 8,600 years ago with the movement of people out of western Anatolia and the Aegean into a Europe that still lived entirely by hunting and gathering.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Farming arrived by two routes — an inland one through the Balkans and up the Danube and the Rhine, and a sea route along the northern Mediterranean coast — reaching the Atlantic by roughly 4000 BCE.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Farmers and foragers then overlapped for more than 3,000 years and mixed slowly, so that between 10 and 15 per cent of the ancestry of Europeans today is hunter-gatherer.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>",
"Skara_Brae": "Skara Brae is a Neolithic village of stone houses on the Bay of Skaill in Orkney, its dwellings joined to one another and banked about with thick midden so that the settlement formed a single low mound.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The fittings inside were built of stone rather than wood and so have survived: a dresser of shelves, beds framed in slabs, a hearth, and drains laid beneath the floors.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Founded in the centuries around 2900 BCE, it appears to have been left after a short occupation, settled again in the 28th century BCE, and finally abandoned as Neolithic Orkney fell quiet.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>",
"Newgrange": "Newgrange is a Neolithic passage tomb above the River Boyne in eastern Ireland, built and used about 3200 to 3000 BCE: a round mound of more than 200,000 tonnes of earth and stone, ringed by a kerb, with a stone-lined passage running in to a cruciform chamber of three recesses.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The passage is aligned on the solstice sunrise, and the chamber it lights would have held only a few people at a time.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> A skull fragment from that chamber came from a man whose parents were full siblings or a parent and child, read in 2020 as evidence of a dynastic elite and disputed since.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>",
"Varna_necropolis": "The Varna necropolis is a Copper Age cemetery on the Black Sea coast of Bulgaria, in use for about 250 years between 4590 and 4340 BCE, whose roughly 270 burials are furnished more richly than anything else of their date in the Old World.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Its gold is very unevenly spread: a third of all the gold objects come from a single grave and a further third from five rich graves and deposits that held no body.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> It is usually read as the first clear evidence of ranked society in Europe, a reading now argued with, since burying wealth also takes it out of circulation.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>",
"Chalcolithic": "The Chalcolithic, or Copper Age, is the phase between the Neolithic and the Bronze Age in which copper was worked; in the Balkans it runs from the first use of copper minerals about 6200 BCE to about 3700 BCE.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> It is a regional label rather than a stage every society passed through, since metallurgy followed no single path and the same centuries carry different period names in different places.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The oldest known copper smelting is slag of about 5000 BCE from Belovode in Serbia, while the earliest metal in the southern Levant is an awl of the late 6th millennium BCE made from Caucasian ore.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>",
"Metallurgy": "Metallurgy is the working of metal, and it begins with metal that is already metal: copper and gold occur native and can be hammered cold without any fire, which is how the oldest worked gold in the Americas was made.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Smelting is the real threshold, since it produces metal that never existed as metal in nature, and it wants a temperature of around 1100 to 1200 °C (2000 to 2200 °F).<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> None of its steps follows from the one before, so metallurgy took no single path from one society to the next, and the question is no longer whether it was invented once but how many times.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>",
"Yangshao_culture": "The Yangshao culture is the Neolithic culture of the middle Yellow River in China, which flourished roughly between 5000 and 3000 BCE across what are now Henan, Shaanxi and Shanxi, with more than 3,000 sites in Henan alone.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> It is known for settled villages, painted pottery, domestic pigs and dogs, and dryland farming of broomcorn and foxtail millet, on fields manured to keep the nitrogen-poor loess in crop.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Ancient genomes from its core and its edges are alike enough that the culture's spread is read as a movement of people rather than of ideas, and Yangshao ancestry is one of the roots of East Asian populations today.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>",
"Longshan_culture": "The Longshan culture is the late Neolithic of the Yellow River plains in China, the period running from about 5,000 to 4,000 years ago.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Its potters made thin black wares, polished and standardised and carrying the marks of the wheel, alongside the grey and brown vessels of daily use, and its settlements could be walled, as Pingliangtai in Henan was.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Cattle reached the Central Plains in these centuries, and their shoulder blades were heated until they cracked so that the cracks could be read, which is the divination later famous from the inscribed oracle bones of the Shang.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>",
"Jōmon_period": "The Jōmon period is the long hunter-fisher-gatherer age of the Japanese archipelago, beginning more than 16,000 years ago and running for over ten thousand years without farming.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Its people are read as direct descendants of those who carried Upper Palaeolithic stone tools into the islands about 38,000 years ago, and the genome of a man buried at Ikawazu parts from mainland East Asian lineages earlier than East Asians and Native Americans part from each other.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> It closed as rice and millet farmers crossed from Korea about 3,000 years ago, though its people did not vanish: Jomon is one of three strands in the modelled ancestry of Japanese today.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>",
"Mehrgarh": "Mehrgarh is a Neolithic complex on the Bolan River in Balochistan, Pakistan, and the only site in northwest South Asia whose Neolithic deposits have been excavated on any scale.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> It is usually named as the earliest evidence of farming in the subcontinent, and its farming was half brought in and half its own: sheep, goats and wheat came from the west, while barley and zebu cattle were taken in hand locally, which makes the site the reference point for the domestication of zebu.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> New radiocarbon dates on tooth enamel from 23 burials place its aceramic cemetery between 5200 and 4900 BCE, three thousand years later than the chronology they replace.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>",
"Syssitia": "The syssitia were the compulsory common meals of the adult male citizens of Sparta, listed by Herodotus among the institutions of war attributed to Lycurgus and known to the Spartans themselves as <i>phiditia</i>.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Plutarch describes companies of about fifteen men, each contributing monthly a set quota of barley meal, wine, cheese and figs, and admitting a candidate by a ballot of bread pellets in which one pellet squeezed flat kept him out.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Aristotle judged the arrangement badly made, since every man had to pay his own way and by ancient custom one who could not contribute was not allowed to keep his rights of citizenship.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>",
"Kleros": "A kleros was the allotment of land that kept a citizen of ancient Sparta, worked not by its holder, who was forbidden every mechanical art, but by helots who handed over its produce.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Plutarch credits Lycurgus with cutting the land into 30,000 lots for the surrounding communities and 9,000 for the Spartans, each reckoned to yield seventy measures of barley a year for a man and twelve for his wife.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Aristotle, writing in the 4th century BCE, says the land had gathered in a few hands instead, since the law shamed selling an inheritance while letting anyone give or bequeath it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>",
"Spartan_women": "Spartan women were the women of Sparta's citizen class, and their upbringing is what the Greek sources single out: Xenophon says Lycurgus ordered bodily training and contests in running and strength for women as for men, holding a free woman's highest work to be bearing children.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Plutarch has the maidens exercise in running, wrestling, casting the discus and hurling the javelin, and marry at full growth rather than in childhood.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Aristotle, who is hostile, says many things at Sparta were managed by the women, that they were useless when Thebes invaded, and that nearly two-fifths of the country was held by women through heiresses and dowries.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>",
"Spartan_austerity": "Spartan austerity is the plainness of living for which Sparta was known, and which Thucydides turned into a paradox: were Lacedaemon deserted, its remains would be so slight that posterity would doubt its power.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> He says the city was composed of villages in the old fashion rather than adorned with temples and public buildings, and that a modest style of dress was adopted there first, the rich assimilating their way of life to the common people's.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Plutarch adds a rhetra ordaining that a house have its roof fashioned by the axe and its doors by the saw, since nobody carries gold cups into a house like that.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>",
"Spartan_army": "The Spartan army was the hoplite force of classical Sparta, and Herodotus has Demaratus tell Xerxes that the Lacedaemonians are best of all men in a body, Law being set over them as a master that bids them never flee.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Thucydides says every command proceeded from the king in the field and passed down the ranks, so that almost the whole army consisted of officers under officers, and at Mantinea it advanced slowly to flute-players keeping the line even.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Xenophon credits Lycurgus with its crimson uniform and bronze shield, and Aristotle judged that a constitution regarding only the soldier's virtue held its power in war and lost it in empire.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>",
"Mora_(military_unit)": "A mora was the largest division of the classical Spartan army, and Xenophon says Lycurgus formed six of them from cavalry and heavy infantry, each commanded by one polemarch with four lochagoi, eight penteconters and sixteen enomotarchs under him.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Its size was already disputed in antiquity, Plutarch reporting five hundred men according to Ephorus, seven hundred according to Callisthenes and nine hundred according to Polybius among others.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Xenophon shows one in the field outside Lechaeum, caught by peltasts and losing about 250 men, its polemarch ordering out first the ten-years-service men and then the fifteen, who could not come at troops that would not close.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>",
"Hippeis": "Hippeis is the ordinary Greek for horsemen, and several cities used it to name a class or a body of men rather than cavalry. At Athens it was the second of the four property classes Aristotle assigns to Solon, made up of those producing three hundred measures a year or, as some said, of those able to keep a horse, a definition the class name was held to support.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> At Sparta it named three hundred picked men who guarded the king, chosen a hundred each by three hippagretai whom the ephors appointed, and shown by Thucydides fighting round King Agis in the centre of the line at Mantinea.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>",
"Iron_money": "Iron money was the currency in use at Sparta while other Greek cities coined silver, and Plutarch credits Lycurgus with withdrawing gold and silver and giving a great weight of iron a trifling value, so that ten minas' worth wanted a store-room and a yoke of cattle to move it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> It was quenched red-hot in vinegar, which left it brittle and useless for anything else, and Xenophon says gold and silver were liable to search with a penalty on whoever held them.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Polybius says it served Sparta at home and failed once its armies went abroad, which needed a currency in general circulation.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>",
"Hyacinthia": "The Hyacinthia was a festival of Apollo held at Amyclae in Laconia, where Pausanias saw a colossal image whose pedestal was shaped as an altar and was said to hold the grave of Hyacinthus, offerings passing to him through a bronze door.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Athenaeus, quoting Polycrates, says it ran three days, with no garlands and no bread at the feasts while Hyacinthus was mourned, and a great spectacle on the middle day of harpers, riders, choruses and girls in curved chariots.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Herodotus reports the Spartans keeping it on the eve of Plataea while Athenian envoys waited, and Thucydides records a treaty renewed there each year.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>",
"Menelaion": "The Menelaion was a sanctuary of Menelaus and Helen at Therapne in Laconia, which Pausanias describes as a temple of Menelaus where the Spartans said the pair were buried.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Isocrates says that in his own day the Spartans still offered sacrifices there to both of them not as to heroes but as to gods, and Herodotus tells of an ugly child made the fairest woman in Sparta at Helen's temple on the same ground.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Polybius, who calls it the Menelaïum, places it on rocky hills across the Eurotas that command the level ground below the city.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>",
"Artemis_Orthia": "Artemis Orthia was a goddess worshipped at a sanctuary in the marshy quarter of Sparta, whose wooden image Pausanias says was also called Lygodesma because it was found in a thicket of willows that held it upright.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> He reports that an oracle required her altar to be stained with human blood, and that a sacrifice by lot was replaced by a scourging of youths, with the priestess holding the image while it was done.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Xenophon knew instead a contest of stealing cheeses from the shrine under the lash, and Plutarch and Cicero describe a Roman-era ordeal in which boys were whipped, sometimes to death.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>",
"Laconian_pottery": "Laconian pottery is the earthenware of Sparta and its region, known above all for a black-figure ware of the 6th century BCE made up largely of slender kylikes, painted in black on a cream to buff slip with incised lines and purple details.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Its most famous piece is the Arkesilas cup, which shows the king of Kyrene aboard ship superintending the weighing of a cargo, and the class was long grouped under the name Cyrenaic after it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Ancient writers noticed the plainer ware instead, Critias praising the kothon as the cup best suited to campaign because its inturned brim held back the dirt in bad water.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>",
"Alcman": "Alcman was an archaic Greek lyric poet who composed at Sparta, credited by the Suda with six books of verse and with being the first to sing in metres other than the hexameter.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Ancient writers disagreed about where he came from: the Suda calls him a Laconian from Messoa while recording that Crates made him a Lydian out of Sardis, and an epigram of Antipater says two continents dispute him because minstrels have many mothers.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> His longest surviving poem is a maiden-song on papyrus, sung by a chorus of girls who praise their leader Hagesichora and a second girl, Agido.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>",
"Spartan_mirage": "Spartan mirage is the name modern scholarship gives to the distance between Sparta's reputation and the Sparta that the surviving evidence will bear.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The ancient writers left the gap open themselves: Thucydides could not put a number on the Spartan army because the secrecy of their government kept it unknown, and Plutarch opens his life of Lycurgus by admitting that nothing about the lawgiver is undisputed.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Aristotle reckoned that a country able to maintain 30,000 hoplites had fewer than a thousand citizens, and much of the custom credited to Lycurgus is now assigned to later centuries.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>",
"Peloponnesian_League": "Peloponnesian League is the modern name for the system of alliances through which Sparta led much of the Peloponnese from the 6th century BCE, and which in antiquity was called simply the Lacedaemonians and their allies.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Each member was bound by a separate treaty to Sparta rather than to the others, and Thucydides says the Spartans exacted no tribute but secured their allies' subservience by establishing oligarchies among them.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Business was settled in a congress at Sparta where every allied state voted, great and small alike, and a majority bound the rest unless the gods or heroes stood in the way.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>",
"Chilon_of_Sparta": "Chilon of Sparta was a Lacedaemonian of the 6th century BCE counted among the Seven Sages, and so brief a speaker that Aristagoras of Miletus is said to have called the manner Chilonean.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Diogenes Laertius has him hold the ephorate and first propose that ephors be joined to the kings as auxiliaries, though the writer behind that claim appears to have recorded only his first term of office.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Diodorus credits him with the three maxims engraved at Delphi, know thyself, nothing overmuch, and a pledge, and ruin is nigh, while other accounts give only the last as his own.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>",
"Cleomenes_I": "Cleomenes I was king of Sparta from the Agiad house from about 521 to about 490 BCE.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Herodotus calls him a man on the verge of madness and yet the most upright of men at home, since he refused the gold and silver cups of Maeandrius of Samos and the fifty talents Aristagoras of Miletus offered him to fight Persia.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> In the field he was ruthless: after beating the Argives he called about fifty men out of their sanctuary by name on a false promise of ransom and killed them, burned the grove the rest had fled to, and at last died by his own hand in the stocks.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>",
"Demaratus": "Demaratus was Eurypontid king of Sparta from about 515 to 491 BCE, deposed when his Agiad colleague Cleomenes I revived an old oath of Ariston's that the boy born to his wife in less than ten months could not be his son.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Cleomenes had the prophetess at Delphi bribed to confirm it, and Pausanias says flatly that Demaratus was deposed not rightfully but because Cleomenes hated him.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> He fled to Persia and marched with Xerxes, telling the king that the Lacedaemonians are free and yet not free in all things, since Law is set over them as a master.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>",
"Thornax": "Thornax is a place in Laconia, on the road running north from Sparta, where an image of Apollo Pythaeus stood in the style of the one at Amyclae.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Herodotus records that Lacedaemonian envoys came to Sardis to buy gold for that image and that Croesus, king of Lydia, gave it to them free, a service that helped secure the oaths of friendship and alliance the two states later exchanged.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Pausanias reports that the gold was in the end spent on the Amyclaean image, and the hill itself has never been securely identified on the ground.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>",
"Socles": "Socles was the envoy of Corinth at the congress of Sparta's allies, held about 504 BCE, whose speech is the earliest surviving statement that the Spartans were known for putting tyrants down.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Herodotus gives it at length: told that Lacedaemon meant to restore Hippias to Athens, he replied that heaven would sooner lie below the earth, and challenged the Spartans to set up a despot in their own state before establishing them elsewhere.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The other allies, who had kept silence, then adopted his opinion, and the plan was abandoned, Hippias returning to Sigeum.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>",
"Battle_of_the_Fetters": "Battle of the Fetters is the modern name for a defeat suffered by Sparta at Tegea in Arcadia around the middle of the 6th century BCE, which the ancient sources describe without naming.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Herodotus reports that the Spartans, promised by Delphi a Tegean plain to measure with line and divide, marched carrying fetters with which to enslave the Tegeans, and that those taken alive worked the plain in those same irons, which still hung in the temple of Athena Alea in his day.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Sparta prevailed only after fetching home bones from Tegea that an oracle had identified as those of Orestes.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>",
"Battle_of_the_Champions": "Battle of the Champions is the modern name for a fight of three hundred picked men a side, agreed between Sparta and Argos to decide possession of the border district of Thyrea and conventionally dated to about 546 BCE.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Herodotus reports that by nightfall three men were left of the six hundred, two Argives and the Spartan Othryades, that both sides claimed the victory, and that the general engagement which followed was won by Sparta.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Argos told the story differently, showing on a monument in its theatre an Argive killing Othryades, and the two states were still disputing the district more than a century later.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>",
"Battle_of_Sepeia": "Battle of Sepeia is the name given to the defeat of Argos by a Spartan army under King Cleomenes near Tiryns, usually dated to 494 BCE.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Herodotus reports that the Argives, fearing a trick, took to obeying the Spartan herald's proclamations, and that Cleomenes had breakfast called and attacked as they ate, the survivors being killed or burnt in a sacred grove; the dead are given as six thousand by Herodotus, five thousand by Pausanias and 7,777 by a tradition Plutarch rejects.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Argive power was broken for a generation, and later writers credit the poetess Telesilla with arming the women to hold the city.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>",
"Spartan_burial_custom": "Spartan burial custom is known mainly from Plutarch, who credits Lycurgus with permitting burial inside the city and beside the sanctuaries so that the young should not fear the dead.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Nothing was buried with the body, which was wrapped in a scarlet robe and olive leaves; mourning lasted eleven days and closed with a sacrifice to Demeter on the twelfth; and no name was cut on a tomb save for a man fallen in war or a woman who had died in sacred office, a clause many editors emend to death in childbirth.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Kings were buried with compulsory public lamentation and honoured as heroes.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>",
"Spartan_divination": "Spartan divination was the reading of the gods' will from sacrificial victims and from oracles, and at Sparta it was woven into the offices of the state: each king chose two Pythioi to consult Apollo at Delphi and kept the oracles they brought back, and every army took a seer.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> A campaign opened with a sacrifice at home to Zeus the Leader, from whose altar a fire-bearer carried fire ahead of the army, and a second at the frontier, the diabateria, which had to be accepted before the king crossed.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Unfavourable victims could turn a whole mobilisation back unfought, and Thucydides records two such reversals in one summer.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>",
"Attica": "Attica is the peninsula at the south-eastern corner of the Greek mainland, sea on two sides and the Boeotian mountains on the third, with Athens in its largest plain and the cape of Sounion at its southern point.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Its soil is thin, and Thucydides made that poverty the reason the region was never worth invading and so kept its inhabitants unchanged while richer parts of Greece were fought over; what it did have was Pentelic and Hymettian marble, honey, and the silver-bearing ore of Laurion.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The whole region formed the territory of a single city, Athens, its countryside organised into demes that Strabo thought too numerous to list.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>",
"Synoecism_of_Attica": "The synoecism of Attica is the tradition, first set out at length by Thucydides, that the region's independent townships — each with its own council-chamber and magistrates — were merged by Theseus into a single state whose one political centre was Athens.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Private property and settlement were left alone, so people went on living where they had while counting as citizens of Athens, and the union was commemorated in classical times by a state-funded festival, the Synoecia.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Strabo reports that the towns joined were the twelve of Cecrops, and the Athenians placed the event in the heroic age, far earlier than any archaeological horizon that could be identified with it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>",
"Athenian_autochthony": "Athenian autochthony was the claim that the Athenians had sprung from the soil of Attica and had occupied it without interruption ever since, having come from nowhere else.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> It rested on a line of early kings — Cecrops, Cranaus, Amphictyon and the earth-born Erichthonius, whose parents were said to be Hephaestus and Earth — and on the tokens on the Acropolis of Athena's contest with Poseidon for the land.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Athenian speakers used it constantly, Pericles opening his funeral oration with it, and after the citizenship law of 451 BCE, which required two Athenian parents, birth and descent became the test of who counted as a citizen.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>",
"Archon": "An archon was one of the chief magistrates of ancient Athens, of whom there were nine.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The Constitution of the Athenians treats three as the oldest — the King, who was ancestral, the War-lord and the Archon — and says the six Lawgivers came later, once the offices had become annual, the nine having at first not even shared a building.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> By the classical period they served a single year and were allotted, one of them gave his name to the year so that events could be dated by him, and each faced a scrutiny of his descent, his family cults and his military service before taking office.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>",
"Areopagus": "The Areopagus was a council at Athens named after the hill of Ares on which it met, whose members were former archons and sat for life.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> It began as the guardian of the laws and, in the <i>Constitution of the Athenians</i>, administered the most important affairs of state and punished offenders without appeal, until Ephialtes stripped it of those powers and handed them to the Five Hundred, the assembly and the jury-courts.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> What it kept was jurisdiction over the gravest killing — deliberate murder and wounding, death by poison and arson — and the hill was remembered as where Ares and later Orestes had been tried.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>",
"Eupatridae": "The Eupatridae were the nobility of early Athens, the class from which the archonships were filled while appointment to the supreme offices went by birth as well as wealth.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Plutarch's account of Theseus makes them one of three orders alongside husbandmen and handicraftsmen, and gives them the care of religious rites, the supply of magistrates, the teaching of the laws and the interpretation of the will of Heaven.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> They are named as an order in the settlement that ended the strife over the archonship after Damasias was expelled, when Athens elected ten archons — five Eupatridae, three Agroikoi or rustics and two Demiourgoi or artisans.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>",
"Cylon": "Cylon was an Athenian of good birth, a victor at the Olympic games and son-in-law of Theagenes, tyrant of Megara, who seized the Acropolis in an attempt to make himself tyrant of Athens.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The Athenians besieged the citadel, and his companions, driven by hunger to take sanctuary at the altar, were led out and killed, an act for which the men responsible and their descendants were called accursed.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The families held guilty were tried before three hundred jurors of noble birth, banished for ever with their dead cast out of the tombs, and Epimenides of Crete was brought in to purify the city.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>",
"Draco": "Draco was the Athenian lawgiver under whom, as the <i>Constitution of the Athenians</i> puts it, a code of laws was first published at Athens, in the archonship of Aristaechmus, a reform it places among its eleven changes of constitution, after Theseus and before Solon.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Aristotle's <i>Politics</i> says he legislated for a constitution that already existed and that nothing in his laws is worth mentioning except their severity in imposing heavy punishment.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Plutarch reports that almost every offence carried the death penalty, that Demades said the laws were written not with ink but with blood, and that Solon repealed them all except those concerning homicide.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>",
"Draco's_homicide_law": "Draco's homicide law was the part of his code the Athenians kept when Solon repealed the rest, and its wording is known because they had it cut on a stele in 409/8 BCE and set up in front of the Royal Stoa.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> It exiled a man who killed without forethought, gave the kings the pronouncement of responsibility and the ephetai the decision, and let the dead man's father, brother and sons grant reconciliation only if all agreed.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> It also bound earlier killers, required a proclamation in the agora, named the kin who joined a prosecution, and protected an exile who kept away from frontier markets and rites.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>",
"Hektemoroi": "The hektemoroi were the dependent farmers of Attica before Solon, so called because they tilled the land of the rich and paid over a sixth of what it produced.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The <i>Constitution of the Athenians</i> calls them Sixth-part-tenants, says the whole country was in few hands, and adds that a man who failed to pay could be arrested with his children, since all borrowing was on the security of the debtor's person.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Solon's own poetry, quoted in that text, calls the Black Earth to witness that he removed the boundary-posts implanted in her, and says he brought home men who had been sold abroad.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>",
"Solon": "Solon was the Athenian whom both parties to a long and violent struggle jointly chose as arbitrator and archon, of the first rank by birth and reputation but of the middle class by wealth.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> He established a constitution and made laws, written up on the Boards in the Royal Colonnade and fixed to stand unaltered for a hundred years, after which the Athenians observed none of Draco's ordinances except those on homicide.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The settlement disappointed both factions, so that he might have made himself tyrant by taking either side, and he chose instead to incur the enmity of both and go abroad for ten years.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>",
"Seisachtheia": "The seisachtheia was Solon's relief of debt at Athens, a name meaning the shaking-off of burdens, since the people shook off their load.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The <i>Constitution of the Athenians</i> says he freed the people at the time and for the future by prohibiting loans secured on the person and enacting cancellations of debts both private and public, and Solon's own verse says he took away the record-stones planted in the mortgaged land.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> What it did was disputed in antiquity: Plutarch reports that most writers took it for a removal of all debt, while Androtion and others held that the poor were relieved by a reduction of interest instead.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>",
"Solon's_property_classes": "Solon's property classes were the four ranks into which the Athenians were divided by assessment, named in the <i>Constitution of the Athenians</i> as Five-hundred-measure man, Horseman, Teamster and Labourer, at thresholds of 500, 300 and 200 measures of dry and liquid produce together.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The offices were held from the top three ranks in proportion to assessment, while those rated in the lowest were admitted to membership of the assembly and the law-courts alone.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Plutarch gives their Greek names as Pentakosiomedimnoi, Hippada Telountes, Zeugitai and Thetes, and says the share left to the last seemed at first of no moment but afterwards proved of the very highest importance.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>",
"Pentakosiomedimnoi": "The Pentakosiomedimnoi were the highest of the four classes into which Solon divided the Athenians by assessment, the name meaning five-hundred-measure men.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The <i>Constitution of the Athenians</i> says a man was rated in the class when the produce of his estate came to 500 dry and liquid measures jointly, and that the ten Treasurers of Athena were drawn by lot from it under a law of Solon still in force.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The same work adds that the man on whom the lot fell held the office even though he was quite a poor man.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>",
"Zeugitai": "The Zeugitai were the third of the four classes into which Solon divided the Athenians by assessment, ranking below the five-hundred-measure men and the horsemen.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The <i>Constitution of the Athenians</i> says men were rated in the class who made 200 measures, wet and dry together, from their land, and that they held the ordinary offices while the archonship went to the two classes above them.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Five years after the death of Ephialtes they were made eligible for the roll from which the nine archons were drawn by lot, and the first of them to hold that office was Mnesitheides.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>",
"Thetes": "The Thetes were the fourth and lowest of the classes into which Solon divided the Athenians, taking in everyone whose land fell short of the 200 measures that made a teamster.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The <i>Constitution of the Athenians</i> says they were admitted to no office at all, having a share in the membership of the assembly and the law-courts alone.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Plutarch says the same and adds that this last privilege seemed at first of no moment but afterwards proved of the very highest importance, since most disputes came in the end before those jurors.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>",
"Council_of_Four_Hundred": "The Council of Four Hundred was the second council of Solon's settlement at Athens, made up of a hundred men from each of the four tribes.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Plutarch says its business was to deliberate on public matters before the people did, and to let no matter come before the popular assembly without such deliberation first, so that a city with two councils rode as it were at double anchor.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The <i>Constitution of the Athenians</i> says Solon made it and gave the Council of the Areopagus the duty of guarding the laws, as it had overseen the constitution before.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>",
"Heliaia": "The Heliaia, also spelled Heliaea, was the popular court of Athens, a tribunal of jurors drawn from the citizens and known as Heliasts.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The <i>Constitution of the Athenians</i> counts the right of appeal to the jury-court as one of the three most democratic features of Solon's constitution, and the one said to be the chief basis of the power of the multitude, since the people holding the vote become sovereign.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> It adds that because the laws are not drafted simply or clearly, many disputes arise and the court is the umpire in all business both public and private.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>",
"Solon's_poems": "Solon's poems are the verses in which the Athenian lawgiver argued his own case, and they survive because later writers quoted them.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Plutarch says he composed poetry at first as amusement and diversion, and later put philosophic maxims into verse and interwove political teachings in it, because the poems contained justifications of his acts along with exhortations, admonitions and rebukes for the Athenians.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The <i>Constitution of the Athenians</i> gives a whole chapter to quoting him, including the lines where he says he gave the people grace enough and stood protecting both sides with a strong shield.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>",
"Solon's_travels": "Solon's travels were the ten years the Athenian lawgiver spent abroad after making his laws.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The <i>Constitution of the Athenians</i> says that rather than alter the laws or stay and incur enmity he went to Egypt to trade and to see the country, holding it unfair that he should stay and explain them instead of everybody carrying out their provisions for himself, while Herodotus says he sailed forth so as not to be compelled to repeal any of them.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Plutarch takes him on to Cyprus, where a king renamed his refounded city Soli after him, and to Sardis.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>",
"Athenian_party_strife_after_Solon": "Athenian party strife after Solon was the disorder that followed the lawgiver's departure and ran until the tyranny of Peisistratus.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The <i>Constitution of the Athenians</i> reports that in two separate years no archon was appointed, that Damasias held the office for two years and two months until he was driven out by force, and that ten archons were then chosen at once from the nobles, the farmers and the artisans.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> It names three parties from where their farms lay, the Men of the Coast, the Men of the Plain and the Hillmen, while Herodotus makes the feud a quarrel between coast and plain into which Peisistratus raised a third faction.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>",
"Peisistratus": "Peisistratus was the Athenian tyrant whom Herodotus introduces as the son of Hippocrates, ruling an Attica divided into factions after he had raised up a third of them because he coveted the sovereign power.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> He had won a reputation in command against Megara, taking Nisaea, and the <i>Constitution of the Athenians</i> calls his rule moderate and more constitutional than tyrannic, kindly and mild, and merciful to offenders.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> He grew old in office and died of disease in the archonship of Philoneos, leaving two sons by his wedded wife, Hippias and Hipparchus, and two by an Argive consort.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>",
"Peisistratus'_three_bids_for_power": "Peisistratus' three bids for power were the attempts by which he made himself tyrant of Athens, was expelled twice, and returned at last by force.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> For the first he wounded himself and blamed his enemies to win a bodyguard, whose club-bearers seized the Acropolis; for the second Megacles brought him back by dressing a tall woman as Athena and driving her into the city beside him.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> For the third he raised money near Pangaeus and hired soldiers, then returned from Eretria with Theban and Naxian support, landed at Marathon and won the battle at the temple of Pallenian Athena.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>",
"Peisistratid_Athens": "Peisistratid Athens was the city as governed by Peisistratus and his sons, whose administration the <i>Constitution of the Athenians</i> calls moderate and more constitutional than tyrannic.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Peisistratus lent money to the poor so that they might farm, levied a tithe on produce, and organised local justices who went on circuit, keeping men on their land and out of public business.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Thucydides says the family exacted no more than a twentieth of income, adorned the city and kept its laws in force, taking care only that one of them should always hold office.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>",
"Hippias_(tyrant)": "Hippias was the elder son of Peisistratus and the tyrant who held Athens after him, statesmanlike and wise by nature according to the <i>Constitution of the Athenians</i>.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Thucydides asserts that he and not his brother Hipparchus was the reigning tyrant, arguing from the pillar set up on the Acropolis, which names five of his children and none of his brothers'.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> After Hipparchus was killed at the Panathenaic festival he put many citizens to death, and was deposed in the fourth year by the Lacedaemonians and the exiled Alcmaeonidae, guiding the Persians to Marathon twenty years later.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>",
"Hipparchus_(son_of_Peisistratus)": "Hipparchus was a son of Peisistratus and the brother of Hippias, with whom Athenian affairs lay after their father's death, though the <i>Constitution of the Athenians</i> says the elder brother controlled the government.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> That work calls him fond of amusement and love-making, with literary tastes, and credits him with bringing poets such as Anacreon and Simonides to Athens.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> He was killed by Harmodius and Aristogeiton by the Leocoreum as the Panathenaic procession was being formed<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>, and Thucydides, who insists the elder brother was the reigning tyrant, says it was that death which got him the credit with posterity of having been one.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>",
"Harmodius_and_Aristogeiton": "Harmodius and Aristogeiton were the two Athenians who killed Hipparchus, son of Peisistratus, at the Panathenaic festival, and whom Herodotus describes as men of Gephyraean descent.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Thucydides makes their action the consequence of a love affair, Harmodius being in the flower of youthful beauty and Aristogeiton a citizen of the middle rank who was his lover, and says the pair struck after an insult to Harmodius's sister.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Harmodius was killed on the spot; Aristogeiton escaped through the crowd running up, but was afterwards taken and dispatched in no merciful way.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>",
"Tyrannicides": "Tyrannicides is the name Athens gave Harmodius and Aristogeiton, the pair who killed Hipparchus, son of Peisistratus, and whom the city honoured as its liberators.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Statues of them stood in the city, some of the figures by Critius and the older ones by Antenor, and Pausanias says Xerxes carried these off among the spoils and Antiochus afterwards restored them.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The <i>Constitution of the Athenians</i> lists memorial offerings to them among the duties of the War-lord, and drinking songs preserved by Athenaeus promise to wreathe a sword in a myrtle bough.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>",
"Alcmaeonids": "The Alcmaeonids were an Athenian family whom Herodotus calls men of renown even in the old days, their standing rising from the time of Alcmaeon, who was given as much of Croesus's gold as he could carry on his person and left with his boots, tunic, hair and mouth full of it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> A marriage into the house of Cleisthenes of Sicyon produced the Cleisthenes who gave the Athenians their tribes and their democracy, and two generations later the mother of Pericles.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> They were also held to be accursed, Thucydides tracing it to the killing of Cylon's companions after they had been raised from the altar on a promise of safety.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>",
"Fall_of_the_Peisistratids": "The fall of the Peisistratids was the end of the tyranny at Athens, four years after Hipparchus was killed and brought about by a Spartan army.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The exiled Alcmaeonids, unable to return by force, took the contract to build the temple at Delphi, and Herodotus reports the Athenian story that they bribed the Pythian priestess to tell every Spartan who consulted her to set Athens free.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> A first expedition was destroyed by Thessalian cavalry, but Cleomenes came by land, besieged the family and took its terms when their children were caught being smuggled out.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>",
"Herodotus": "Herodotus was a Greek prose writer from Halicarnassus, a Carian city on the coast of Asia Minor then subject to the Persian king, and the author of the <i>Histories</i>.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Ancient authority placed his birth about 484 BCE; he travelled widely in Egypt, the Near East and the Aegean, lived for a time at Athens, and joined the colony sent to Thurii in southern Italy, where he is said to have died.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The nine books trace the rise of the Persian empire and its wars with the Greek cities, carrying ethnography, geography and reported speech alongside the narrative, and it was Cicero who called him the 'Father of History'.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>",
"Thucydides": "Thucydides was an Athenian of a wealthy family holding mining interests in Thrace, and the author of an unfinished account of the war between Athens and Sparta.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Elected to command, he lay at Thasos with a squadron of seven ships when the Spartan Brasidas seized Amphipolis in 424 BCE; arriving too late to save it, he secured Eion further down the Strymon instead.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> He wrote as a participant who knew many of the leading men of his day, and the history runs to eight books, uses composed speeches to carry argument, and breaks off unfinished in 411 BCE.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>",
"Aristotle": "Aristotle was a philosopher from Stagira in Macedonia who came to Athens at about seventeen to study in Plato's Academy, remained associated with it until Plato's death in 347 BCE, and later spent two or three years tutoring the young Alexander at the Macedonian court.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> In 335 BCE he set up his own school in a public exercise ground sacred to Apollo Lykeios, from which it took the name Lyceum, its members becoming known as Peripatetics.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> About 31 treatises survive, in the form of lecture notes and drafts written for an inner circle rather than a general readership, ranging from logic and metaphysics to ethics, politics, rhetoric and empirical biology.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>",
"Plato": "Plato was an Athenian citizen of high status, a pupil of Socrates, and the writer through whom Socrates is chiefly known; where the master ends and the pupil begins is not easy to say.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Nearly everything he wrote takes the form of a dialogue, the <i>Apology</i> being the standing exception, and the surviving pieces range across ethics, politics, metaphysics and the theory of knowledge, alongside works whose authorship has long been doubted.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> He founded a school in a grove sacred to the hero Academus, a little beyond the walls of Athens, which took the name Academy and was his base for the rest of his life.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>",
"Plutarch": "Plutarch was a Greek writer from Chaeronea in Boeotia who held office at Delphi, including the priesthood of Apollo, became an Athenian citizen, and was appointed procurator of Achaea by the emperor Hadrian.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> He is best known for the <i>Parallel Lives</i>, biographies of distinguished Greek and Roman statesmen and commanders examined in pairs, which turn as much on character and its formation as on politics and war.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> A second body of work, the <i>Moralia</i>, gathers essays on ethics, psychology, natural philosophy, theology and the arts, and throughout he wrote as a Platonist trying to draw a coherent system out of Plato.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>",
"Xenophon": "Xenophon was an Athenian of the equestrian class who knew Socrates and wrote a series of sketches of him in conversation.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> In 401 BCE he joined a Greek force serving the Persian prince Cyrus; when Cyrus fell and the generals were killed by treachery, he helped lead the stranded army home, an ordeal he recorded in the <i>Anabasis</i>.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Service under the Spartan king Agesilaus brought a decree of exile from Athens, and on an estate near Olympia given him by Sparta he wrote most of his surviving work, including the <i>Hellenica</i>, a Greek history covering 411 to 362 BCE, and treatises on horsemanship, hunting and household management.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>",
"Strabo": "Strabo was a Greek writer from Amasia in Pontus, born in 64 or 63 BCE into a family wealthy enough to let him study under Aristodemus at Nysa, travel, and spend his life on scholarship.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> His <i>Geography</i> surveys the inhabited world region by region, from Iberia and Italy to Armenia and Libya, folding history, myth and ethnography into the description of each.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Written under Roman rule, it is oriented towards the needs of empire — knowing a country's terrain, past and customs being an advantage to those governing it — and modern scholars mine it for trade, roads and ethnography in the Augustan age.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>",
"Pausanias": "Pausanias was a Greek writer of the 2nd century CE, working in the reign of Marcus Aurelius, about whose own life almost nothing is known.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> His <i>Description of Greece</i>, in ten books, is a traveller's account of the southern and central mainland, working through region after region and setting out the sanctuaries, monuments, images and local stories he found at each.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Because he describes buildings and objects since lost or ruined, and ties the stories to the places that carried them, the work is a standing source for archaeologists and for the study of how Greek myth was told and localised.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>",
"Pindar": "Pindar was a Greek lyric poet, born at Cynoscephalae just outside Thebes in Boeotia to Daiphantus and Cleodice, and proud throughout his work of his Theban birth and training.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> He recorded that his birth fell during a Pythian festival at Delphi; the lexicographer Suidas put it in the 65th Olympiad, which gives 518 BCE, while the ancient description of him as flourishing at the time of the Persian war has been used to argue for 522 instead.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Alexandrian editors divided his poetry into 17 books, of which only the 44 victory odes written for the four Panhellenic festivals survive intact.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>",
"Livy": "Titus Livius, known in English as Livy, was born at Patavium, the modern Padua, in 59 BCE and died there in 17 CE.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> His family were well enough off to give him the standard training in Greek and Latin literature and rhetoric, and to let him spend a long life on the unremunerative work of writing.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <i>Ab Urbe Condita</i>, his history of Rome from the founding of the city, ran to 142 books; for the war against Hannibal, which fills its third decade, he worked directly from Polybius, and the differences between the two accounts are now read as deliberate choices rather than carelessness.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>",
"Apollo": "Apollo was one of the principal Greek gods, and Delphi — where tradition had him take the shrine from the serpent Python, and where he shared it with Dionysus — was his most important sanctuary.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> There his priestess, the Pythia, answered enquiries put to the god, at a place the Greeks called the navel of the world and whose maxim was 'know thyself'; its Pythian games ranked second only to the Olympics and held musical contests as well as athletic ones.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Like any Greek deity he was worshipped in many local forms, as Apollo Pythios at Delphi, and on Mount Kotilion beside Artemis as a helper in time of need.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>",
"Zeus": "Zeus was the chief god of the Greek pantheon, worshipped as Zeus Olympios at Olympia, where his sanctuary held a great altar of ash, a chryselephantine statue of him and the games given in his honour.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> In the north-western Peloponnese he was also worshipped at mountaintop sites, which began at the end of the Geometric period, flourished through the 7th century BCE and then fell away as those districts were drawn into the control of the cities.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Those hilltop cults answered to the collective religious life of neighbouring rural communities, where a sanctuary such as that of Zeus Soter at Megalopolis, founded around 340 BCE, carried a frankly political weight.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>",
"Artemis": "Artemis was a Greek goddess whose commonest Homeric epithet, <i>iocheaira</i>, means 'arrow-pouring', who was worshipped as Agrotera in her character as goddess of the hunt, and whom scholars connect with the Mistress of Animals of the eastern Mediterranean.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Her sanctuaries were many and unalike: Brauron in Attica with its initiation rites and processions, Artemis Orthia at Sparta, and the great temple at Ephesus.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Across the Peloponnese she carried many cult epithets and stood as a helper in time of need and a presider over initiation, and her cult travelled with Greek settlement into Sicily and southern Italy, where her sanctuaries tend to lie in boundary zones.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>",
"Hera": "Hera was a Greek goddess whose cult, as the objects dedicated at her sanctuaries show, differed markedly from the formidable figure of epic.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Votives from the Argive Heraion, Perachora, Tiryns, Samos and the two Heraia at Poseidonia point to concerns with pregnancy, childbirth and growing up, with marriage, home and family, with agriculture, and with protection in war — a range wide enough that scholars warn against calling her simply a fertility goddess.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Her sanctuaries also did public work, drawing scattered settlements together, and at Perachora the offerings served to display personal wealth.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>",
"Athena": "Athena was a Greek goddess worshipped at Athens as Athena Polias and Athena Parthenos, and the Parthenon built for her carries the frieze usually read as the Panathenaic procession held in her honour.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Like other Greek deities she was identified by a name in three parts — the god's own name, a functional epithet and a cult toponym — so that one city could keep several versions of her at once.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Her character was not fixed across the Greek world: at Tegea in Arcadia her sanctuary shows a goddess of many sides, taking in fertility, the mastery of animals, the protection of the town and the patronage of weaving.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>",
"Poseidon": "Poseidon was a Greek god whose worship is often used to show how a Greek deity was identified — by a personal name, a functional epithet such as Soter, and a cult toponym, as at Sounion in Attica.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> His sanctuary at Kalaureia served to draw a group of neighbouring states together, an association of the kind that gave a shared cult political as well as religious weight.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> At Isthmia the excavated remains show elaborate sacrifice and communal dining growing up through the 8th and 7th centuries BCE, and at Methana another cult may have occupied his sanctuary before him.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>",
"Achilles": "Achilles is the central figure of Homer's <i>Iliad</i>, and his anger at an insult from Agamemnon is what drives the poem forward — behaviour a modern reader is apt to find exaggerated, though Homer's own audience would have read it otherwise.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> He is among the most eloquent speakers in the poem, for all that he denies any gift for speech.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Later Greek tragedy took him up as a paradigm rather than a portrait: Aeschylus, Sophocles and Euripides used him to measure the distance between the heroic world of myth and their own, and in Euripides the famous impulsiveness is dropped altogether for a man who decides by reason.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>",
"Odysseus": "Odysseus is the central figure of Homer's <i>Odyssey</i> and one of the Greek leaders at Troy in the <i>Iliad</i>, marked out among Homeric heroes by his eloquence.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The poem follows his homecoming from that war, and turns on recognition and on loyalty quite as much as on the fantastic episodes, his stay with Circe among them, for which it is best remembered.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Greek tradition placed him in the class it called heroes, figures of the remote past whom it marked off from ordinary mortals by giving them cult after their deaths.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>",
"Agamemnon": "Agamemnon is the king of Mycenae in Greek myth and the leader of the Greek expedition against Troy in Homer's <i>Iliad</i>, where he brings a hundred ships and the largest contingent in the army.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The contempt with which he treats Achilles sets off the quarrel the poem opens on, and that friction is the structure the <i>Iliad</i> is built on, since his insult makes Achilles withdraw and the withdrawal is the condition of everything that follows.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The mythographers make him and his brother Menelaus the sons of a house torn by strife, brought up by Polyphides and Oeneus, and record that he married Clytaemnestra while Menelaus married Helen.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>",
"Minos": "Minos is the legendary king of Crete, a son of Europa and brother of Sarpedon and Rhadamanthys, who came to the kingdom on the death of Asterius, Europa's husband.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The story most firmly attached to him concerns his wife Pasiphae, who conceived a passion for a bull that Poseidon had sent out of the sea and gave birth to the Minotaur.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> His name was carried into modern archaeology by Arthur Evans, who went to Crete in search of physical traces of Greek myth — the labyrinth of Daedalus among them — and called the Bronze Age civilisation he uncovered there Minoan.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>",
"Heracles": "Heracles is the most widely known of the Greek heroes, remembered above all for the labours he carried out at the command of Eurystheus, a grandson of Perseus.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> They took him across the Greek world and past its edges — fetching the Cretan bull, carrying off the mares of Diomedes the Thracian, winning the belt of Hippolyta — and the mythographers hang a crowd of further exploits on the journeys between them, from the founding of Abdera to the rescue of Hesione.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> He belongs to the class the Greeks called heroes, who were held to owe their more than human powers to descent from a god.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>",
"Athens": "<b>Athens</b> is the capital and largest city of Greece, with about 3.2 million people in the wider agglomeration that takes in Piraeus and the surrounding municipalities.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The site has seen more than 5,000 years of continuous human occupation,<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> and excavation of its ancient civic square, the agora, has brought up Neolithic, Early Helladic and Middle Helladic material along with a Mycenaean cemetery of forty-odd tombs and graves.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> That square served as the civic and commercial centre of the city from about 600 BCE until 267 CE,<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> and from 508/7 BCE Athenian citizens governed themselves directly in assembly rather than through the representatives familiar from modern democracies.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>",
"Thera": "<b>Thera</b>, or Santorini, is a volcanic island group in the southern Aegean, on the Hellenic arc: a steep-walled caldera rim ringing a bay with an active volcanic island at its centre.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> It is built of overlapping shield volcanoes cut by at least four calderas, the oldest about 180,000 years ago, Skaros about 70,000, Cape Riva about 21,000, and the youngest cut by the Late Bronze Age eruption that forced the island to be abandoned.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Eruptions from 197 BCE onwards raised the two islands in the middle of the bay, the last of them in 1950, while the Bronze Age town of Akrotiri lies buried under the older deposits.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>",
"Levant": "The <b>Levant</b> is the land at the eastern end of the Mediterranean, between the sea and the Syrian desert, running from the Taurus mountains down to Sinai. Fossils of <i>Homo sapiens</i> at Skhul, dated to about 130,000 to 100,000 years ago, and at Qafzeh, from about 110,000 to 90,000, record an early expansion of the species out of Africa.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Modern humans and Neanderthal-like hominins both lived there between roughly 130,000 and 80,000 years ago and shared one set of behaviours, including deliberate burial and ochre placed in the graves,<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> and its Natufian camps later became settled and ran on into the first farming villages.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>",
"Phylakopi": "<b>Phylakopi</b> is a Bronze Age town on the north coast of Melos, standing on a cliff that the sea has eaten into, so that a slice of the settlement has gone into the water; the British School at Athens dug it from 1896 and found three successive cities inside a great fortification wall.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Wall paintings from the second city include the flying-fish frieze, which its excavators called among the most beautiful of Mycenaean works of art.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> It is one of only three places outside Crete to have produced true texts in Linear A, the others being Ayia Irini on Kea and Akrotiri on Thera.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>",
"Ayia_Triada": "<b>Ayia Triada</b> is a Minoan site in southern Crete where two villas of the Late Minoan I period stood, with a later building raised over the northern of the two in the reoccupation period after the Late Minoan IB destructions.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> It produced an archive of 168 clay tablets in Linear A, dated to Late Minoan IA and one of the two largest known.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> A looted tomb of the early 14th century BCE there held the Ayia Triada sarcophagus,<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> and the colonnade and row of square chambers raised at the site in Late Minoan IIIA, known as its Agora, may be what the outer court at Tiryns was modelled on.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>",
"Palaikastro": "<b>Palaikastro</b> is a Minoan town at the eastern end of Crete, laid out in blocks of living units divided by cobbled streets — the same plan as Pseira, and as the Cycladic towns at Akrotiri, Phylakopi and Ayia Irini.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Its cemetery holds house tombs of both Minoan kinds, a run of long narrow parallel chambers inside one rectangular building and a group of square and oblong rooms inside another.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The peak sanctuary that served it, Petsofa, stands at an elevation of 215 metres (705 feet) and is one of only two excavated and published in full, its cult use beginning early in the Middle Minoan period.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>",
"Mesara": "The <b>Mesara</b> is the great plain of southern Crete, with Phaistos on the hill above it and its port at Kommos, which shipped the region’s produce out in a trademark short-necked amphora.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> It is the heartland of the Minoan tholos tomb, and the earliest of them, on the Asterousia slopes beyond the plain proper, were in use as early as the Final Neolithic.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> For over a thousand years it was the only burial form commonly used in southern Crete, and because the tombs are so alike in size and contents, Early Minoan Mesara society, Phaistos apart, looks less stratified than that of Mochlos, Malia or Gournia in the north.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>",
"Ayia_Irini": "<b>Ayia Irini</b> is a Bronze Age town on Kea in the western Cyclades, one of the island settlements laid out as insulae, blocks of houses bounded by cobbled streets, and enclosed by a fortification wall.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Its frescoes owe a great deal to Minoan wall painting but are not purely Minoan, belonging with those of the other two towns to one or more distinct Late Cycladic schools.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> An underground water source just outside its wall, of Late Helladic II or IIIA date, is the earliest sign in the Aegean Bronze Age of the concern with siege that later produced the water systems of Mycenae, Tiryns and Athens.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>",
"Quartier_Mu": "<b>Quartier Mu</b> is a district of the Minoan town at Malia, about 300 metres (980 feet) west of the palace, and the largest complex known on Crete from the age of the first palaces. Over 3,000 square metres (32,000 square feet) were cleared from 1966 to 1991: two large buildings, and around them five small houses identified as craftsmen's — a metalworker's, a potter's, a seal-engraver's, a smelter's and one of mixed trades.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> It was built about 1800 BCE and burnt about 1700, at the end of Middle Minoan II,<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> and the collapse sealed tablets in Cretan hieroglyphic and 150 seals that define the Malia style of Minoan seal-cutting.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>",
"Ugarit": "<b>Ugarit</b> is a Bronze Age city and kingdom on the north Syrian coast, whose territory ran south to Gibala and which had four harbours of its own.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> A French expedition began digging the ruins in 1929 and found clay records in a cuneiform script nobody knew, most of whose alphabet Hans Bauer, Édouard Dhorme and Charles Virolleaud worked out.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The kingdom fell shortly after 1200 BCE, its coastal towns destroyed in the first quarter of the 12th century in a collapse with several causes, among them the sea and land migrations called the Sea Peoples,<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> whom most scholars are willing to see as the destroyers of Ugarit itself.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>",
"Messenia": "<b>Messenia</b> is the region at the south-western corner of the Peloponnese, and the territory whose Mycenaean centre was the palace at Pylos.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Tholos tombs are so common there that they may have been used by classes below the ruling families, where in Attica, Boeotia and the Argolid the form seems reserved for royalty; one tomb near Pylos held burials in storage jars as well as bodies laid in pits cut into its floor.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> It has produced no Late Helladic IIIB fortification system of any importance, which sets it apart from the Argolid and Boeotia, where citadels are thick on the ground.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>",
"Argolid": "The <b>Argolid</b> is the plain and surrounding hills at the north-eastern corner of the Peloponnese, and the densest concentration of Mycenaean citadels anywhere: Mycenae, Tiryns, Midea, Argos, Asine and probably Nauplion.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Palaces are known at Mycenae and Tiryns, and probably stood at Midea and Argos before erosion or later building removed them.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Because the region is usually taken to have been ruled by a single monarch in the later 14th and 13th centuries BCE, its fortifications are generally read as defence against a threat from outside the Mycenaean world rather than against its neighbours.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>",
"Rhodes": "<b>Rhodes</b> is the largest island of the Dodecanese, off the south-western coast of Asia Minor.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Intensive Minoan contact with it begins in Middle Minoan IIIB or a little earlier, the evidence being largely pottery from Trianda and Ialysos, and Trianda is the only site in the Dodecanese or on the Anatolian coast where architecture from before the end of Late Minoan IA has been exposed on any scale.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Airfall ash from the eruption of Thera settled on Trianda, and radiocarbon dates from just below it are part of the evidence for when that eruption happened.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>",
"Chania": "<b>Chania</b> is a town in western Crete, the ancient Kydonia, whose Bronze Age settlement lies buried beneath the Greek, Roman, Venetian, Turkish and modern cities built over it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Its movable finds are therefore more impressive than its architecture: a salvage excavation in the early 1990s recovered Linear B tablets from 13th-century BCE levels, apparently the record of a kingdom separate from Knossos.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> With Knossos it is one of only two places off the Greek mainland to have produced such tablets,<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> and it had earlier held one of the two major archives of Linear A.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>",
"Rome": "<b>Rome</b> is the capital of Italy, a city on the Tiber in Latium, with about 4.2 million people in the metropolitan city.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Its site is the river’s work: the valley was cut tens of metres below present sea level at the Last Glacial Maximum and filled again in three stages, and the plain holding the Forum Boarium and the city’s earliest harbour settled at 6 to 8 metres above the sea only in the late 3rd century BCE.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> By the earlier 9th century BCE, when its burial grounds moved from the Forum out to the Esquiline and the Quirinal, Rome had become by far the largest settlement in Latium.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>",
"Boeotia": "<b>Boeotia</b> is the region of central Greece north of Attica, its heart the Kopais basin, which held a shallow seasonal lake until it was drained.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> It is one of the two regions where Mycenaean fortresses are common, the other being the Argolid: Gla, Eutresis, Haliartos and several lesser sites ring the basin, and palaces stood at Thebes and Gla and probably Orchomenos.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> In historical times its cities formed the Boeotian koinon, a federal state whose emergence and workings across the Archaic, Classical and Hellenistic periods are the region’s largest contribution to Greek political history.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>",
"Peloponnese": "The <b>Peloponnese</b> is the large southern peninsula of Greece, joined to the rest of the mainland only at the Isthmus of Corinth, which the Mycenaeans began to wall off against attack from the north and never finished.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Several of the regions the Mycenaean world was divided into lie within it, among them the Argolid, Messenia and Laconia, and with them the palaces at Mycenae, Tiryns and Pylos.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> It has rarely if ever acted as a single political unit, its cities grouping instead by region, and in the early Hellenistic period Argolis, Corinthia, Arcadia, Laconia, Messenia and Elis each went their own way.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>",
"Samos": "<b>Samos</b> is an island of the eastern Aegean, close to the Ionian coast of Asia Minor, whose Minoan imports are among the very few found that far east.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Beneath the later sanctuary of Hera lies a prehistoric settlement with a nearly continuous stratified sequence running from at least the Middle Chalcolithic, in the second half of the 5th millennium BCE, into the Middle Bronze Age.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> By the late Early Bronze Age it was one of the largest settlements in the Aegean and western Anatolia, some 35,000 square metres (377,000 square feet) inside a circuit wall, larger than Troy or Kolonna on Aegina.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>",
"Mediterranean": "The <b>Mediterranean</b> is the semi-enclosed sea between Europe, Africa and Asia, joined to the Atlantic only through the Strait of Gibraltar, 13 kilometres (8 miles) across at its narrowest; evaporation there outruns rain and rivers, so Atlantic water enters at the surface and saltier water leaves beneath it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Between 5.97 and 5.33 million years ago the Messinian Salinity Crisis choked and then blocked that exchange, laying down nearly a million cubic kilometres of salt and drawing sea level down by 1.7 to 2.1 kilometres in the east.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Its human history is now written basin-wide, from its geological making to the classical age, as a world whose unity lay in relations across it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>",
"Black_Sea": "The <b>Black Sea</b> is the inland sea between south-eastern Europe and Anatolia, an estuarine basin whose only outlet to the ocean is the Bosphorus, a strait about a kilometre wide and some 30 metres (100 feet) deep.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> So much river water arrives that the surface stands near 18 practical salinity units against the Mediterranean’s 36, and the two layers scarcely mix.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Below them the water holds no oxygen: the euxinic zone begins between 54 and 130 metres depending on the station<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> and dominates the column below about 150 metres,<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> making this the largest semi-enclosed permanently anoxic basin on the planet.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>",
"Hittites": "The <b>Hittites</b> were the people of a Bronze Age kingdom in central Anatolia, a great power contemporary with pharaonic Egypt, Minoan Crete, Mycenaean Greece, the Hurrians of Mitanni and the Assyrians and Babylonians of Mesopotamia.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> They appear first as Nesites early in the 2nd millennium BCE, and their history runs from Labarna, Hattusili I and Mursili I to the breakdown of Hittite power under Tudhaliya IV late in the 13th century.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Their great king was high priest, chief commander and chief judge, and was deified at death; until the 19th century they were known, if at all, as an obscure people of biblical Palestine.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>",
"Hellenistic_period": "The <b>Hellenistic period</b> is conventionally the three centuries between the death of Alexander the Great in 323 BCE and that of Cleopatra VII in 30 BCE, though its bounds are argued: some open it with the conquest of Persia and close it at Actium,<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> and others argue for a long Hellenistic age running on under Roman rule.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> It has no single centre such as Athens or Rome, and no ancient narrative through it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The evidence is abundant all the same — inscriptions, papyri, coins and architectural remains beside Diodorus, Livy, Plutarch and Polybius — and on almost any measure more is known of it than of Archaic or Classical Greece.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>",
"Thessaly": "<b>Thessaly</b> is the region of northern Greece whose wide plains lie between Mount Olympus and the mountains of central Greece, opening to the sea in the south-east; a substantial building at Iolkos is claimed as a Mycenaean palace by its excavator, though the part exposed does not prove it one.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Its epigraphic record is rich by Greek standards, one study cataloguing 474 inscribed dedications and 45 excavated sanctuaries at 30 sites, most of it Hellenistic.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> That evidence has dismantled the old picture of a feudal state run by a few great landowners, showing the ethnos and the oligarchic polis working alongside each other.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>",
"Achaeans": "The <b>Achaeans</b> are one of the three names by which Homeric poetry calls the Greek warriors of its heroic age, the others being Argives and Danaans.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> In historical Greece the name belonged to a people of the Peloponnese who acted as a federal state, their Achaian koinon standing beside the Arkadian and the Aitolian among the koina of southern Greece.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> That league is, with the Boeotian and the Aetolian, one of the three best known Greek federations,<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> and its own usage kept the word koinon for the whole body of citizens while naming their meetings synodoi and synkletoi.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>",
"Middle_Minoan": "<b>Middle Minoan</b> is the middle of the three divisions of the Cretan Bronze Age, running from about 2050 to 1700 BCE and cut into MM IA, IB, IIA–B, IIIA and IIIB.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> MM IIA and IIB are ceramic styles current only at the palaces of Knossos, Phaistos and Mallia, so at most Cretan sites MM IIIA follows MM IB directly and the label carries little chronological weight elsewhere.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The pottery of MM III is serviceable but dull beside what came before: most tableware is unpainted, solidly painted or patterned in white on a dark ground, polychromy is rare, and the carinated cup gives way to straight-sided and semiglobular ones.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>",
"Late_Minoan": "<b>Late Minoan</b> is the last of the three divisions of the Cretan Bronze Age, cut into LM IA, IB, II, IIIA1–2, IIIB and IIIC and followed by the Subminoan period, already the Iron Age.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Its opening is marked in painted pottery by the turn from light on dark to dark on light, and LM I is the height of Minoan influence across the southern Aegean, from the Peloponnese to the Dodecanese and south-western Anatolia.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Not one of the palaces burnt late in LM IB was rebuilt as a palace, and most ruined sites lay empty until LM IIIA; LM II is known from few places but richly from Knossos.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>",
"Archaic_period": "The <b>Archaic period</b> is the age of Greek history between the Dark Age and the Classical, closing with the Persian Wars; where it opens is argued, one standard history reaching back to about 1200 BCE and running to 479,<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> while starting it in the 8th century BCE has been called problematic, since that breaks the Middle Geometric in two.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Its written sources are mostly much later than the events they report, and are held not to allow a narrative of events at all, the Lelantine War being the usual example.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> It is often labelled the age of experiment, and is no longer read as a preparation for Classical Greece.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>",
"Po_valley": "The <b>Po valley</b> is the largest floodplain in Italy, the filled depression between the Alps and the Apennines, much of it built up during the Last Glacial Maximum before the Po’s Alpine tributaries cut down into it, leaving terraces and shifting deposition downstream.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Its rivers have gone on moving since: the Crostolo was diverted away from Reggio Emilia in the Roman period and again in 1250 and 1571, as the stream migrated west.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Its older landscapes are largely buried: channels, Roman field grids and Bronze Age villages hold moisture differently from the soil around them and are read as crop and soil marks from the air.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>",
"Iberia": "<b>Iberia</b> is the peninsula of south-western Europe, cut off from the rest of the continent by the Pyrenees, and takes in modern Spain and Portugal. It served as a southern genetic refugium through the Last Glacial Maximum, holding a population before, during and after it: a man buried in the south about 23,000 years ago carries ancestry linking the Aurignacian to the later Magdalenian.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The claim that Neanderthal bands held out in the far south long after the north did not survive redating with better purification,<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> and Greek contact with its eastern seaboard intensified only in the 6th century BCE, when the Phocaeans founded Emporion on that coast.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>",
"Balkans": "The <b>Balkans</b> are the peninsula of south-eastern Europe, between the Adriatic, the Aegean and the Black Sea. The overland route by which farming reached Europe from the Near East ran across it and into central Europe, and farmers moved in shorter steps there than along the Mediterranean coast, though on both routes alike about 3.6 per cent of them took in hunter-gatherers.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> In the Late Bronze and Iron Ages the region was a frontier where societies met rather than a line, and those are the centuries in which its best-known ancient peoples took shape; modern borders cut across it, so that Thrace is now shared among Greece, Bulgaria and Turkey.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>",
"Cyclopes": "The <b>Cyclopes</b> are the one-eyed giants of Greek myth, and the ancient world knew three distinct kinds of them: the pastoral ogre Odysseus meets, the giant metalworkers who forged the weapons of the gods, and the monstrous builders of the great walls of Tiryns and Mycenae.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Their enormous size and single eye make them among the most recognisable monsters of the Greek world, and Polyphemus's meeting with Odysseus in the ninth book of the <i>Odyssey</i> won them a lasting place in western literature.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Euripides turned that episode into <i>Cyclops</i>, the only satyr play to survive complete, in which the monster becomes a gourmand and a philosopher.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>",
"Alps": "The <b>Alps</b> are the mountain arc of south-central Europe, curving from the Mediterranean coast around the northern edge of Italy and east into Austria and Slovenia. They were raised by the Adriatic microplate, a promontory of Africa that has driven into Europe since the late Cretaceous, an indentation still deforming the eastern end of the range.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The barrier is also a bridge: wide valleys and passes, the Adige and the Brenner among them, carried contact between the northern and southern sides, though ancient DNA from the Iceman's territory shows genetic exchange there stayed limited from the Middle Neolithic to the Middle Bronze Age.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>",
"Atlantic_Ocean": "The <b>Atlantic Ocean</b> is the second largest of the ocean basins, after the Pacific, and lies between the Americas on one side and Europe and Africa on the other.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The Mid-Atlantic Ridge runs down its middle, part of the mid-ocean ridge system that is the most extensive chain of mountains on Earth at nearly 65,000 kilometres (40,000 miles), where new sea floor is made as the plates spread apart 2 to 5 centimetres (1 to 2 inches) a year.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Its overturning circulation brings warm water north and cold water south, surface water cooling at high latitudes until the salt left behind by forming sea ice makes it dense enough to sink and return at depth.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>",
"Bologna": "<b>Bologna</b> is a city in northern Italy where the southern edge of the Po plain meets the foothills of the Apennines. The Etruscans knew it as Felsina, and it was the leading centre of Etruria padana, the Etruscan country north of the Apennines, whose sculpture of the late 8th to the 6th centuries BCE comes largely from the city itself.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Its hills were a meeting ground: at Monterenzio Vecchio, 30 kilometres (19 miles) south-east in the Idice valley, nearly every tomb of the 4th and 3rd centuries BCE holds local and non-local elements together, and strontium and dental evidence puts people of Etruscan and Celtic ancestry there at the same time.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>",
"Settlement": "A <b>settlement</b> is a place where people lived, and the archaeology that maps them keeps the word narrow: a dwelling place, as against a cemetery, a temporary camp or an industrial zone such as a mine.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Most are found by walking the ground rather than digging it, teams crossing a landscape at fixed intervals and recording every scatter of ancient pottery, whose extent and density then stand in for how many people lived there.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Ranked by size on logarithmic axes, the settlements of a region show how it was organised: a system holding one or two great centres among many small places is called primate.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>",
"Sanctuary": "A <b>sanctuary</b> is ground set apart for a god, and remarkably little is needed to make one: an altar and boundary markers are the only necessary features, though many also hold a temenos wall, a gateway, a temple, treasuries, stoas, dining rooms, athletic buildings, theatres and fountain houses.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> What went on in them was festival, sacrifice, dining, dance, initiation, purification and healing, and they are classed by where they stand: interurban, urban, suburban, extra-urban and rural.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The boundary itself could be drawn in several ways, by walls, fences, basins, boundary stones, monuments or planting.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>",
"Inscription": "An <b>inscription</b> is a text cut into a durable surface, stone above all, and the Greek ones run from the 8th century BCE through to the Byzantine period.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> What was cut, and how, followed from limited literacy, the architecture of public display, political conditions, aesthetic concerns and other competing demands, so an inscription is a monument as much as a document.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The kinds recur across the Greek world — decrees, honorific and proxeny decrees, dedications and ex-votos, funerary inscriptions and records of manumission — and reading them is a discipline of its own, epigraphy.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>",
"Cemetery": "A <b>cemetery</b> is ground given over to burial, its graves grouped together rather than scattered, and archaeologists mapping a landscape count it apart from the settlements whose dead it holds.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> One can be dug on a very large scale: the Early Iron Age graves found under the Athenian Agora belong to several such grounds, and the volume publishing them catalogues 83 tombs with their contents.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> A cemetery records change as much as death, those Athenian grounds showing a gradual shift from inhumation to cremation, though their excavators warn against assuming any significant standardisation in funerary ritual.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>",
"Tomb": "A <b>tomb</b> is a burial place built or cut as a chamber rather than dug as a pit for one body; the Mycenaean kind is quarried out of soft rock, an open passage called the dromos running down to a blocked doorway, the stomion, and the chamber beyond.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> What separates it from a grave is that it was meant to be opened again: at one cemetery in the Nemea valley the undisturbed passages had been dug out and refilled five or six times.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Earlier occupants were moved rather than cleared away, and such tombs are read as a family's over generations.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>",
"Temple": "A <b>temple</b> is a building raised for a god, and in Greece it is best understood as a gift: temple buildings are collective dedications, votive offerings to the god and embellishments of the sanctuary they stand in.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> They did not evolve by degrees, the record showing a surprisingly rapid jump from unassuming shrines to grandiose stone peripteral temples, and the colonnaded form was invented around 600 BCE.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Inside stood the cult statue, and the building served as a treasury and a place to display writing as well as a setting for ritual.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>",
"Clay": "<b>Clay</b> is fine-grained sediment built of layered hydrated aluminosilicates, along with coarser mineral grains and a little water, and it is among the commonest materials in the ground.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The clay fraction proper is everything finer than two micrometres, and the minerals in it are chiefly kaolinite — the most abundant in the Earth's crust — with illite, smectite, montmorillonite and chlorite beside it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Wet, it can be worked by hand and holds whatever shape it is given; fired, it shrinks and hardens for good, and those two properties together make it the raw material of every ceramic, from a cooking pot to a written tablet to a mudbrick wall.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>",
"Copper": "<b>Copper</b> is a soft red metal, and the working of it came in stages: green copper minerals were first ground for pigment and ornament, native copper was then hammered into shape, and only later were ores smelted to get the metal out.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Those ores are the carbonates malachite and azurite and the sulphides beneath them, and smelting takes a hot reducing fire, though one experiment on Cyprus reached 850 °C (1,560 °F) on wood alone, without bellows.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> What comes out is not a clean ingot but prills of metal caught in slag, which is why grinders, mortars and pestles lie thick about ancient furnaces.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>",
"Silver": "<b>Silver</b> is a soft white metal notable for its ductility, malleability and reflectivity.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> It seldom occurs on its own, and most of it was won from lead ores by cupellation: silver-bearing lead is heated until the lead oxidises away, leaving the silver behind, and the lead oxide driven off is litharge, which appears across Mesopotamia, Iran, Anatolia and the Levant with the first silver in the 4th millennium BCE.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Purity costs metal, since the finer the silver the more goes into the litharge, and the Greek world drew silver and lead from one deposit where Carthage and Rome added lead to ores too poor in it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>",
"Gold": "<b>Gold</b> is a soft, dense, yellow metal that does not tarnish and occurs in nature chiefly as the metal itself, worked for ornament and exchange since prehistory. It is spread thinly through the Earth’s crust, and a deposit worth working is one where some process has concentrated it a hundred to ten thousand times over.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Saying which district an object came from is harder, because the ore sources have rarely been measured themselves; lead isotopes separate deposits of different geological age, with copper and silver isotopes now coupled to them, as in the Limousin of central France, whose ores fed Celtic jewellery and coin.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>",
"Ivory": "<b>Ivory</b> is the dentine of a tusk or large tooth, dense and easily carved, taken chiefly from elephants but also from hippopotamus, walrus and sperm whale. It has been worked into ornaments for tens of thousands of years: a decorated pendant from Stajnia Cave in Poland, directly radiocarbon dated to about 41,500 years ago, is the oldest known ornament of its kind in Eurasia.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Telling which animal a piece came from is harder than it looks — identification by eye is unreliable and ancient DNA usually yields too little material — while peptide mass fingerprinting does better, identifying every ivory sample from one Iberian burial ground as African elephant.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>",
"Marble": "<b>Marble</b> is limestone recrystallised by heat and pressure into a mass of interlocking calcite grains, hard enough to take a polish and even enough to carve in any direction. It was quarried across the ancient Mediterranean for building and for sculpture, and establishing where a given block came from is a question about trade as much as about stone, bearing on quarry production, on distribution and on what buyers valued.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> No single measurement settles it: combining isotope ratios, chemical composition and the analysis of inclusion fluids discriminates the important fine-grained white marbles almost perfectly, and can separate one Carrara quarry district from another.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>",
"Tin": "<b>Tin</b> is the metal that, alloyed with copper, makes bronze, and where the Bronze Age world got it is one of the standing questions of prehistoric archaeology.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Earlier attempts to fingerprint the metal all failed, and only tin isotopes, lead isotopes and trace elements together have narrowed the sources down: 27 ingots from Mochlos on Crete and other eastern Mediterranean sites point to European deposits, Cornish mines likeliest for those found in Israel.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Ingots from the Uluburun wreck, of about 1320 BCE, divide instead between the Taurus Mountains and Central Asia, evidence of a network resting on small regional communities as much as on large centralised states.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>",
"Amber": "<b>Amber</b> is fossilised tree resin, buried in organic-rich clayey rocks laid down in coastal areas and river channels, often holding insects trapped while it was still soft.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Deposits differ in their infrared spectra, so a bead can be matched to its source by infrared spectroscopy; in prehistoric Iberia that separates local amber, worked from the Palaeolithic to the Bronze Age, from Sicilian simetite, present from the 4th millennium BCE, and from Baltic succinite.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> A bead from a Late Neolithic burial cave in northeastern Iberia, dated between 3634 and 3363 BCE, matches the Baltic spectra exactly, putting Baltic amber in Western Europe a thousand years earlier than had been thought.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>",
"Horse": "The <b>horse</b>, <i>Equus caballus</i>, is a large grazing mammal of the open grassland, hunted long before it was ridden. It was among the large game most heavily hunted in Ice Age Europe: in the Magdalenian bone bed at Solutré in France horse accounts for 3,577 identified bones against 271 of reindeer and 142 of bison.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The horses alive now descend from none of the earliest keepers of them — the Botai lineage of about 3500 BCE left none — but from the western Eurasian steppes and above all the lower Volga-Don, a line brought under close reproductive control around 2200 BCE and then spread across Eurasia.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>",
"Cattle": "<b>Cattle</b> are large domesticated bovines kept for meat, milk, hides and traction, and among the most numerous livestock animals in the world. They were domesticated twice over: taurine cattle, <i>Bos taurus</i>, in the Fertile Crescent about 10,500 years ago, and zebu, <i>Bos indicus</i>, in the Indus Valley some 2,500 years after that, and both lines then travelled with the people who kept them, so the genome of a living breed is a mosaic.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Zebu reached Africa in at least two separate arrivals out of the Indian subcontinent, at dates that fall alongside state formation, the spread of ironworking and the intensification of herding.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>",
"Sheep": "<b>Sheep</b> are domesticated ruminants, <i>Ovis aries</i>, kept for meat, wool, skin and milk, and they descend chiefly from the Asiatic mouflon, <i>Ovis gmelini</i>, a wild sheep of Anatolia, the Caucasus and Iran.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> How much other wild species of <i>Ovis</i> contributed is still argued, from mitochondrial haplogroups and from whole genomes, and the wild and domestic lines did not part cleanly at domestication.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The mouflon of Iran show it most clearly: they diverged from the domestic sheep's direct ancestor and afterwards bred back into it, so their genomes are read both for where sheep came from and for the wild ancestry that entered the flocks later.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>",
"Goat": "The <b>goat</b>, <i>Capra hircus</i>, is a small domesticated bovid kept for milk, meat, hair and hides, able to browse rough ground that will not carry cattle. Its herds were among the earliest kept anywhere, which makes the animal a natural test case for what being kept does to a genome; most ancient goat DNA survives at too low a coverage to read directly, so the missing genotypes are inferred against a reference panel of more than 1,300 living animals.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Among living breeds one maternal lineage runs from Türkiye to Iraq, with a second in Turkish herds alone and rather more diversity on the Turkish side.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>",
"Pig": "The <b>pig</b> is the domesticated form of the wild boar, <i>Sus scrofa</i>, taken independently from two genetically distinct wild populations in western and eastern Eurasia.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Pig keeping began about 10,000 years ago at both ends of Asia, which puts the animal among the earliest livestock anywhere, and within China the Yellow River basin, the Yangtze and the Qinghai-Tibet Plateau are the recognised centres of the indigenous breeds.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Ancient genomes spanning 5800 BCE to 1300 CE point to northern China rather than the southwest as the origin of the modern East Asian pig, and follow the black coat that appeared once it lived among people.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>",
"African_humid_period": "The African humid period is the interval of the late glacial and early Holocene when a strengthened West African monsoon carried rain far into what is now the Sahara, filling it with grassland, rivers and lakes.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Its driver was orbital, precession bringing northern summers nearer the Sun and drawing the rain belt north to about 31 degrees, where rainfall reached a median near 640 millimetres (25 inches) a year, ten times today's.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Hunter-fisher-gatherers and later herders lived across the region throughout, and the drying that ended it came neither everywhere at once nor everywhere abruptly, a course tied variously to falling insolation, vegetation feedbacks and grazing.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>",
"Saharan_rock_art": "Saharan rock art is the tens of thousands of paintings and engravings on the cliffs and rockshelters of the desert's massifs, densest in the Tadrart Acacus of Libya and the Tassili n'Ajjer of Algeria.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Convention divides it into styles held to run in sequence, from wild animals through the featureless Round Heads and the Pastoral cattle to horses, chariots and finally camels, spanning the early Holocene to the 1st millennium CE.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Direct dating has largely failed, so the chronology rests on style, superimposition, varnish and dated deposits, and the imagery is read as a record of a Sahara that once held water and herds.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>",
"African_cattle_pastoralism": "African cattle pastoralism is the herding of cattle as a livelihood across the continent, and it is the older half of African food production, since herds were kept long before crops were sown.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> It is first clear in the Sahara by about 7,000 years ago, and as that region dried the herds moved on, reaching the Turkana basin around 5,000 years ago and southern Africa only about 2,000.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Ancient genomes show herders moving with their animals and mixing with the foragers they met; the cattle are a mixture too, Fertile Crescent stock crossed with wild African aurochs, and African milk-drinkers evolved their own lactase variants.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>",
"Nabta_Playa": "Nabta Playa is an internally drained basin in the Western Desert of southern Egypt, about 100 kilometres (60 miles) west of Abu Simbel, which held a seasonal lake through the African humid period.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Its people dug deep walk-in wells that let them stay in the desert all year, built rows of huts and storage pits, and later raised megalithic alignments, a small stone circle and mounds over buried cattle.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> It is best known for the claim that its cattle were domestic by 11,000 years ago, the earliest in Africa, which remains disputed on both the dating and the animals' status.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>",
"Kuk_Swamp": "Kuk Swamp is a wetland at about 1,550 metres (5,090 feet) in the upper Wahgi valley of the Papua New Guinea highlands, and it is where agriculture is shown to have begun independently in New Guinea.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Its peat preserves six phases of cultivation spanning ten thousand years, from plant tending on the wetland margin about 10,000 years ago through mounded plots to networks of drainage ditches.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The crops were taro, bananas, yams and sugarcane rather than cereals, and there were no herds, so the evidence is microscopic: banana phytoliths, taro starch on stone tools and pollen from cleared forest.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>",
"Austronesian_expansion": "The Austronesian expansion is the dispersal of Austronesian-speaking people from Taiwan through Island Southeast Asia and across the Pacific and Indian oceans, carrying a family of about 1,250 languages, the world's most widely spread before European colonisation.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Taiwan is the accepted homeland because nine of the ten primary branches are spoken there, and archaeology puts rice and millet farmers on the island about 5,000 years ago and their descendants in the Philippines by 4,200.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Settlement reached Remote Oceania between 3,000 and 2,800 years ago and Madagascar about 1,400 years ago, while eastern Polynesia, the last habitable land on earth to be occupied, was taken within the past millennium.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>",
"Lapita_culture": "The Lapita culture is an archaeological culture of the western Pacific, appearing about 3,450 years ago and known above all for pottery decorated with fine toothed stamps in elaborate repeating designs.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Its sites run from the Bismarck Archipelago east to Samoa and hold polished stone adzes, worked shell ornaments and hamlets of rectangular houses, some raised on stilts over the reef, while obsidian from those northern islands travelled its sea routes as far as Fiji and Borneo.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Its makers were the first people to reach Vanuatu, New Caledonia, Fiji, Tonga and Samoa, and ancient DNA shows them descended almost undiluted from the population that carried Austronesian languages out of Taiwan.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>",
"Eastern_Agricultural_Complex": "The Eastern Agricultural Complex is the set of seed crops that Indigenous people domesticated in eastern North America, making the region one of the world's independent centres of plant domestication.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Its four founding species are pepo squash, sunflower, marshelder and goosefoot, dated directly to between about 5,025 and 3,700 years ago and identified as domesticates by larger seeds or thinner seed coats; erect knotweed, maygrass and little barley were added later.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Maize was folded into these fields rather than replacing them, and several of the crops fell out of cultivation only around European colonisation, leaving the domesticated forms of marshelder, goosefoot and erect knotweed extinct.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>",
"Poverty_Point": "Poverty Point is an earthwork complex on a bluff above the Mississippi floodplain in northeastern Louisiana, built between about 3,700 and 3,100 years ago and inscribed on the World Heritage List in 2014.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Six concentric, semi-elliptical ridges wrap a plaza across roughly 200 hectares, with five mounds of the main period, the largest being the second-biggest earthen mound in North America; about a million cubic metres of earth were moved in all.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Its builders hunted, fished and gathered rather than farming, and had neither draft animals nor wheels, yet imported stone from sources hundreds of kilometres away.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>",
"Andean_domestication": "Andean domestication is the process by which people along the western spine of South America brought plants and animals alike into cultivation and keeping, making the region one of the world's independent centres of domestication.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Its crops came from wild relatives on the same slopes: potato, oca and ullucu among the tubers, quinoa, cañihua and amaranth grown for seed, and beans, peppers, squashes and cotton, the potato's origin lying in the high south-central Andes.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The llama was domesticated from the guanaco and the alpaca is bound up with the vicuña, camelid herding generally placed between about 9,000 and 8,000 years ago, and the guinea pig kept by around 4,000.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>",
"Chinchorro_mummies": "Chinchorro mummies are the artificially preserved dead of marine hunter-gatherers who lived on the coast of the Atacama Desert, in what is now northern Chile and southern Peru.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Preparing one involved taking a body completely apart and reassembling it, rebuilding the anatomy and finishing the outside with clay, pigments and other materials; the practice runs from about 8,000 years ago until roughly 4,400, and is the oldest deliberate mummification known in the Americas.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Infants and children were mummified first and adults only later, and the finished bodies were handled repeatedly rather than buried and left.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>",
"Prehistoric_warfare": "Prehistoric warfare is organised violence between groups before written records, reconstructed from skeletal injuries, weapons, mass graves, defensive works and depictions of combat.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Lethal violence between individuals is very old, a skull of about 430,000 years from Spain carrying two blows from one object, while the earliest good evidence for recurrent violence between groups is the Nile cemetery of Jebel Sahaba, where two thirds of the dead carry injuries.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Cranial trauma runs at about a tenth across 2,363 Neolithic skeletons from northwestern Europe, a rise on the foragers before them, though it has also held steady for ten thousand years among coastal groups who never farmed.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>",
"Social_inequality": "Social inequality is the unequal distribution of wealth and standing between households within a society, measured archaeologically by comparing the sizes of contemporaneous houses at a settlement and expressing the spread as a Gini coefficient.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Across 1,173 sites and 47,019 residential structures spanning over 10,000 years, persistent wealth inequality typically lags the onset of plant cultivation by more than a millennium, appearing instead with population growth, production limited by land rather than labour, and hierarchies of settlement size.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Inheritance sustains it, material wealth passing strongly to children in farming and herding societies and weakly among foragers.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>",
"Fibre_technology": "Fibre technology is the making of cord, thread, netting and cloth out of plant and animal fibres, and it is among the least visible of ancient crafts, because fibre decays.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The oldest direct evidence is a three-ply cord of conifer inner bark found on a stone flake at the Abri du Maras in France, between 41,000 and 52,000 years old, whose making implies knowledge of plants, seasons and retting.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Dyed bast fibres appear on tools in north China by 28,000 years ago, probable spindle whorls in the Levant by 12,000, and loom-woven cloth in Iberia from the second half of the 4th millennium BCE.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>",
"Plough": "A plough is a tool dragged through soil to open a furrow for sowing, and the earliest form, the ard, cut a shallow groove without turning the sod, so a field was often worked twice at right angles.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Because the implement was wood it has rarely survived, and is known instead from the criss-cross scratches left in buried ground surfaces and from wear on the foot bones of the cattle that pulled it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Drawn by an animal rather than swung by hand, it multiplied the ground one household could break, and is variously argued to have made extensive field agriculture possible, widened wealth gaps and reshaped labour.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>",
"Irrigation": "Irrigation is the supply of water to crops by human effort rather than by rainfall, which allows farming where or when the rain does not fall. It ranges from lifting water by hand or by counterweighted lever to canals, terraces and reservoirs, and where it left no standing works it is inferred from the water-loving plants that grew among the crop.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> It is one of the practices meant by intensive agriculture, and although states and intensive farming are closely associated worldwide, the stronger relationship runs from the presence of a state to the adoption of the practice.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>",
"Prehistoric_trade": "Prehistoric trade is the movement of goods between communities before written records, and because nothing was set down it is reconstructed from objects recovered far from the source of their material. Provenance analysis makes this possible, since obsidian, amber, metal ores and some shells carry a chemical signature of the deposit they came from, so a worked piece can be tied to one outcrop or seam.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Distance alone does not show that anyone travelled it, however, because goods can pass from group to group in short steps, a pattern that leaves the greatest quantities near the source and the least far away.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>",
"Ubaid_period": "The Ubaid period is the earliest attested phase of permanent settlement on the alluvial plain of southern Mesopotamia, running from roughly 6000 to 4000 BCE and recognised over a wide area by its painted pottery.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Its settlements were relatively small and scattered with little hierarchy among them, their people farming, herding, fishing and hunting, with irrigation practised at a local scale and craft specialisation only beginning to appear.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> From about 4500 BCE its material is found well beyond the plain, along the Persian Gulf coasts and in northern Mesopotamia and western Iran, while social ranking, public temples and sites above 10 hectares (25 acres) belong to its later centuries alone.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>",
"Urban_revolution": "The urban revolution is the name given in 1936 by V. Gordon Childe to the appearance of cities, the second of two stages into which he divided the change from hunter-gatherers to the first civilisations.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> He defined it by craft specialisation, metallurgy, long-distance trade in craft goods, and surplus and wealth accumulating in cities that held ruling classes and monumental public buildings, all of it driven by a farming surplus that freed specialists from growing their own food.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Later work complicates it: proto-urban settlements reached 320 hectares (790 acres) in Ukraine and Moldova centuries before Uruk, and urbanism took low-density forms in Amazonia, where mound centres were linked by canals and causeways.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>",
"Sumer": "Sumer is the collective name for the earliest network of city-states, which emerged about 5,000 years ago in southern Mesopotamia and was held together by shared cultural traditions and economic interests rather than by a single crown.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> It was agrarian and sustained by large-scale irrigation, control over labour was institutionalised around the hydraulic works this demanded, and Ur, Uruk, Kish, Lagash and Umma competed for water and irrigable land, formed alliances and went to war.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> After an Akkadian imperial interlude the city-state dynasties revived, but the fall of the Third Dynasty of Ur, which ruled from 2112 to 2004 BCE, ended Sumer's primacy as a political network.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>",
"Uruk": "Uruk is an ancient city on the alluvial plain of southern Iraq, known today as Tell al-Warka, which underwent full-fledged urbanisation during the 4th millennium BCE and gave its name to the archaeological period.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> At its height it covered over 250 hectares (620 acres) and housed at least 25,000 people, and through the period settlement across the region grew denser and clustered around urban institutions while techniques were introduced or improved in agriculture, textiles, ceramics, metal and architecture.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> From about 3550 BCE its settlements and cultural influence spread north into upper Mesopotamia and east onto the Susiana plain, where clay tokens, bevelled-rim bowls and cylinder-seal impressions turn up in workshops and stores.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>",
"Cuneiform": "Cuneiform is the wedge-shaped writing of the ancient Near East, a common medium there from the late 4th millennium BCE to the 1st century CE.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> A scribe impressed its signs into moist clay with a stylus and let the tablet dry in the open air, firing the clay only occasionally, which is why buried tablets are so vulnerable to the salts that temperature and humidity draw to their surface.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> It is a script and not a language, and one of the languages written in it is Akkadian, attested from about 2700 BCE to 75 CE and rarely written syllabically, since almost all of it also uses Sumerian logograms.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>",
"Writing_system": "A writing system is a graphic code, a set of standardized pairings between symbols and meanings in which the symbols take the form of images that can endure.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> One was invented from nothing only a handful of times, in Mesopotamia, Egypt, China and Mesoamerica, and a few more, among them the scripts of Crete and Anatolia, count as secondary inventions made in the knowledge that writing exists.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> All of them lean on iconicity, since their earliest signs are pictures, but a picture is rarely used for the thing it shows, and what turns one into a sign is metaphor, whereby one entity stands for another.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>",
"Cylinder_seal": "A cylinder seal is a small pierced stone engraved in reverse, so that rolling it across damp clay leaves a raised frieze, and it is attested in Iran and Mesopotamia from about 3500 BCE.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The clay it marked was more often a fastening than a document, closing a door, a pot mouth, the tied neck of a sack or the knotted wrapping of a reed basket, and roughly 2,000 survive from the six centuries between 3300 and 2300 BCE.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> They were cut with abrasive slurries worked by hand, palm, pump and bow drills, and no seal or bead harder than quartz has been found.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>",
"Ziggurat": "A ziggurat is a stepped platform of brick raised over a temple, and the best preserved example stands at Ur, where the lowest stage measures about 40 by 59 metres (130 by 195 feet) and the tower rose some 28 metres (92 feet).<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Its core is unbaked brick with a thick facing of baked brick laid in pitch, bonded at intervals by reed mats dipped in pitch, and weeper-holes were left through the facing so that rain could drain rather than swell the core.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Others were built across the plain, among them the three-stepped hill about 12 metres (39 feet) high reconstructed for Eridu in the Third Dynasty of Ur.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>",
"Eridu": "Eridu, the modern Abu Shahrayn, is an ancient city southwest of Nasiriyah in southern Iraq, occupied from the 6th to the 2nd millennium BCE and belonging to Enki, the god of fresh water and wisdom.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> A deep sounding through its temple mound found 18 architectural levels one above another, the lowest numbered XVIII, and the pottery through that sequence is what the chronology of the Ubaid period was built on.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The earliest shrine is a small building of rectangular mud bricks with a long central sanctuary, an altar and an offering podium, its facades already alternating buttresses with recesses.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>",
"Sumerian_city-state": "A Sumerian city-state was a city with its own ruler, its own god and its own irrigated hinterland, and the Sumerian king list treats the arrangement as the natural order, handing the kingship from one city to the next as each in turn falls.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Survey at Lagash has mapped roughly 500 hectares (1,240 acres) cut by monumental walls into distinct quarters, which at 100 to 200 people per hectare implies 60,000 to 120,000 inhabitants.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Production there was scattered rather than central, with pottery kilns concentrated in one area and separate zones for metal, stone and shell, and the canals that fed such cities are attested from about 4,500 years ago.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>",
"Ur": "Ur was an ancient city in southern Iraq belonging to Nanna, the Sumerian moon god, its mound holding some 5,000 years of occupation with the earliest habitation reaching back to about 5500 BCE.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> It gives its name to the Third Dynasty of Ur, whose second king Shulgi is dated by one excavated tablet, and its ziggurat, re-faced with modern brick in the 1960s, is the one part of the site that still stands out.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Excavation there uncovered a royal cemetery of 16 spectacular tombs and thousands of plainer graves, along with the largest continuous expanse of Old Babylonian housing found anywhere.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>",
"Royal_Cemetery_at_Ur": "The Royal Cemetery at Ur is a burial ground of more than 2,000 graves in southern Iraq, dug into waste land below the city walls where rubbish had long been tipped.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Only 16 of the graves had the structure, the wealth and the evidence of human sacrifice that convinced its excavators they were royal, and seal impressions in the surrounding rubbish place those tombs in the century between about 2600 and 2500 BCE.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> One held a woman whose cylinder seal reads Puabi, Queen, and the largest of the pits without a tomb chamber held 74 bodies, most of them young women in elaborate dress.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>",
"Gilgamesh": "Gilgamesh is the king of Uruk in Sumerian tradition, whom the Sumerian king list makes the city's fifth ruler and lord of Kullab, with a father it calls a phantom and a reign of 126 years.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> That list is a mixture of myth, legend and historical information rather than history as we understand it, and its fullest copy was written on clay about 1800 BCE, long after the reign it records.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> A drier text, the history of the Tummal, simply has him build in Enlil's temple at Nippur after Enmebaragesi of Kish and Mesannepada of Ur, while the Sumerian poems make him a king granted kingship but not eternal life.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>",
"Epic_of_Gilgamesh": "The Epic of Gilgamesh is a long Babylonian poem about the king of Uruk, which its own readers knew by its opening line, <i>ša nagba imuru</i>, 'He that has seen all things'.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Its mature form, the Standard Babylonian version, sets some 3,000 lines out on twelve tablets and was copied between the 8th and the 2nd centuries BCE, while its ten oldest surviving manuscripts are a thousand years older still.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> It survives in pieces: the standard edition works from 184 fragments making up about 73 manuscripts, no complete set is preserved in any one hand, and between a third and a fifth of the poem has never been found.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>",
"Sargon_of_Akkad": "Sargon of Akkad was the founder of the dynasty that ruled Mesopotamia from the city of Agade, which the Sumerian king list says he built and over which it gives him a reign of 56 years.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> That list names him the son of a gardener and the cupbearer of Ur-Zababa of Kish, and has the kingship pass to Agade from Lugal-zage-si of Uruk, whom copies of Sargon's own monuments describe him leading away a prisoner.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The Louvre, which holds a fragment of one of those monuments, dates his reign to about 2334 to 2279 BCE on the conventional chronology.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>",
"Akkadian_Empire": "The Akkadian Empire was the Mesopotamian state ruled from the city of Agade by Sargon and his descendants, to whose dynasty the Sumerian king list gives 11 kings and 181 years.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Copies made at Nippur of those kings' own monuments put its limits at Elam and the Persian Gulf in the south and the Lebanon, the Taurus and the Hittite land in the north-west, and describe boats from Magan, Meluhha and Dilmun at the city's quays.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Its most famous monument is the limestone stele on which Sargon's grandson Naram-Sin, dated by the Louvre to about 2254 to 2218 BCE, climbs a mountain over his enemies in a horned tiara.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>",
"Third_Dynasty_of_Ur": "The Third Dynasty of Ur was the line of kings who ruled Sumer from the city of Ur for over a century after Utu-hegal of Uruk drove out the Gutians, styling themselves kings of Sumer and Akkad.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Its founder Ur-Nammu began the temple of Nanna and Ningal there, with the brick tower whose lower stage measures about 40 by 59 metres (130 by 195 feet), and his son Shulgi finished it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The Louvre dates Shulgi's reign to about 2094 to 2047 BCE; the dynasty ended under Ibbi-Sin, when the Elamites and their allies from Mari plundered city and temple and carried the king and the statue of Nanna off to Susa.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>",
"Code_of_Ur-Nammu": "The Code of Ur-Nammu is a collection of Sumerian laws issued in the name of Ur-Nammu, who founded an independent state centred on Ur in 2112 BCE, and is the oldest such collection yet recovered.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> It is known from a broken tablet in the Istanbul Museum of the Ancient Orient, number 3191 of its Nippur collection, whose eight columns carry a long prologue and only five restorable laws.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> That prologue claims the king fixed honest weights and measures and kept the orphan and the widow from falling prey to the wealthy and the powerful, and three of the laws set silver fines for injuries later codes punished in kind.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>",
"Old_Babylonian_period": "The Old Babylonian period is the stretch of Mesopotamian history running from about 2000 to 1500 BCE, when the south was divided among rival cities held by dynasties of Amorite descent, the most successful of them the Babylon of Hammurabi.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Akkadian was the language people spoke by then, while Sumerian was kept alive as a language of learning and copied out by students in the scribal schools, which is how most surviving Sumerian literature reached the present.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Its housing at Ur, more than fifty dwellings along narrow lanes with the dead buried beneath the floors, is the largest such stretch known.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>",
"Hammurabi": "Hammurabi was the Amorite king who ruled Babylon from 1792 to 1750 BCE, in the line called the first dynasty of that city, while in his earlier years the greater power lay north of him in Shamshi-Adad’s Assyrian realm.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Though Akkadian was the spoken language, his court had praise poetry made for him in Sumerian, and a Larsa official dedicated a kneeling figure of copper and gold to the Amorite god Amurru for his life.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> He is best known for the stele of laws whose relief shows him before the sun god Shamash, a monument later carried off as booty and recovered at Susa in Iran.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>",
"Code_of_Hammurabi": "The Code of Hammurabi is a collection of laws cut into a basalt stele over two metres tall for the king who ruled Babylon from 1792 to 1750 BCE, with a relief at its top showing him before the sun god Shamash.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Numbered to 282 between a prologue and an epilogue of curses, its provisions are graded by rank: an eye is taken for an eye between men of equal standing, while the same injury done to a freed man is settled in silver.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The stele was found at Susa in Iran rather than in Babylonia and is now in the Louvre, 34 paragraphs no longer surviving on it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>",
"Babylon": "Babylon was a city on the Euphrates in southern Mesopotamia, raised into a capital by an Amorite dynasty in the 18th century BCE and bound from the start to the god Marduk, whose temple Esagila stood at its centre.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Hammurabi’s laws close by asking that his name be repeated for ever in that temple, and eleven centuries afterwards Nebuchadnezzar II still called himself the provider of Esagila on the bricks of his own rebuilding.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> It is that later city, with its processional way of glazed and moulded brick, that survives to be seen; it passed to Persia when Cyrus the Great took it in 539 BCE.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>",
"Mesopotamian_religion": "Mesopotamian religion was the worship of the gods of the cities of the Tigris and Euphrates, each city having a patron deity of its own, Nanna at Ur and Enlil at Nippur among them, who was held to live in the temple as in a house.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Kings built and rebuilt those temples, burying inscribed tablets and small figures of a man carrying a basket of clay beneath the walls to record what had been built and for which god.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Worshippers dedicated statues and offerings for their own life or another's, and much everyday practice went on at neighbourhood shrines and in chapels kept inside ordinary houses.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>",
"Enuma_Elish": "Enuma Elish is the Babylonian poem of the creation, written out on seven tablets of some 994 lines and called after its own first two words, “When in the height”.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> It tells how the gods came out of primeval water, how Marduk was made their champion against Tiamat and split her body to make a covering for heaven, and how man was formed last of all from the blood of the god Kingu.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Known in Europe for centuries only through a Greek summary written by a priest of Babylon, it was recovered from broken tablets copied for the library at Nineveh.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>",
"Babylonian_mathematics": "Babylonian mathematics is the arithmetic, algebra and geometry recorded on clay tablets in Mesopotamia, most of them from the Old Babylonian period of roughly 1900 to 1700 BCE.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Its numbers are sexagesimal, counted in sixties and written in a notation of only two symbols, and the commonest tablets of all are the arithmetical tables that trainee scribes copied out at school.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> A round tablet at Yale carries a square with its diagonals and an accurate approximation of the square root of 2, and a table at Columbia shows that a method existed for generating whole-number right triangles a millennium and a half before Euclid.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>",
"Babylonian_astronomy": "Babylonian astronomy is the observation and recording of the sky in Mesopotamia, kept up in cuneiform for more than seven centuries in the astronomical diaries, which run from about 750 BCE to perhaps as late as 75 CE.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Observers reported to Assyrian kings on the moon, the planets and eclipses, and read what they saw as omens, so that Jupiter appearing in a given month was taken to mean the land would eat good food.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Later tablets from Uruk set the twelve signs of the zodiac against the twelve months of the year and cast horoscopes on the movement of the planets.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>",
"Edubba": "An edubba, or tablet house, was a school in ancient Mesopotamia where pupils were taught to write cuneiform, and most of what is known about it comes from the practice tablets those pupils left behind.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Training began with the mechanics of the reed stylus and moved through syllables to long lists of personal names and of words grouped by subject, and only afterwards to literature: myths, royal hymns, city laments, proverbs and comic pieces about school life.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Few schoolrooms have been identified, and in the Old Babylonian period much of the teaching appears to have gone on in private houses.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>",
"Mesopotamian_trade": "Mesopotamian trade was the long-distance exchange that supplied the cities of southern Iraq, whose alluvium yielded mud, reeds, bitumen, poor stone and dates but no metals, no hardwood timber and no hard, colourful stones.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> What the south had in surplus was grain and the wool of upland flocks, and by the end of the 4th millennium BCE cloth was its chief manufactured commodity and the main thing it offered in exchange.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Goods came overland from the Iranian plateau and Anatolia and by sea from the Gulf, where cuneiform texts name three lands in a fixed order: Dilmun, Magan and Meluhha.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>",
"Kassites": "The Kassites were the people whose kings ruled Babylonia through much of the later 2nd millennium BCE, governing as a caste that kept its own names while gradually taking on the civilisation of the country it had conquered.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> They built a new capital at Dur-Kurigalzu near Babylon, and their most durable change was to date documents by the years of a king's reign rather than by naming each year after some notable event.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> They are chiefly known now for the kudurru, a carved stone recording a royal grant of land under divine emblems and a string of curses, and much else about them remains obscure.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>",
"Elam": "Elam was the civilisation of what is now south-western Iran, centred on Susa in the lowland plain and on Anshan in the highlands of Fars, and named from a word for highland that its Mesopotamian neighbours used for the country east of them.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Its Proto-Elamite accounting tablets of about 2900 to 2600 BCE are still undeciphered, because they use number signs and logograms that reveal nothing of the language behind them.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Elamite rule ran from the end of the 3rd millennium BCE until Ashurbanipal's army destroyed Susa about 640 BCE, its high point the Middle Elamite empire of roughly 1300 to 1100 BCE.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>",
"Mitanni": "Mitanni was a kingdom of northern Mesopotamia and Syria that was amassing power by about 1500 BCE and stood, in the century that followed, among the great powers of the ancient Near East beside Assyria, Babylon, Egypt and Hatti.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Its ruling class was of Indo-European descent, which shows in royal names such as Artatama and Tushratta, in the horse-breeding terminology of its texts, and in the word <i>mairya-</i> used of its military aristocracy.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Its own voice survives chiefly in letters 17 to 30 of the Amarna archive, before a vassal treaty brought the kingdom under the lordship of the Hittite king Suppiluliuma I.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>",
"Etruscan_sea_power": "Etruscan sea power is the shipping, trade and naval strength the cities of Etruria exercised over the Tyrrhenian and Adriatic seas, with the reputation for piracy Greek writers attached to it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Roman tradition held that Etruscan power reached widely by sea and that both seas around Italy bore Etruscan names,<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> and Diodorus called the Tyrrhenians masters of the sea in 474 BCE, when Syracuse beat them off Cumae.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The material record is of ports and cargoes, not fleets — Pyrgi, Gravisca, Spina, Adria, merchants on the coasts of Gaul,<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> a wreck off Giens holding a thousand wine amphorae<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> — so how far it amounted to naval dominance is debated.",
"Battle_of_Alalia": "The Battle of Alalia is the naval engagement of about 540 BCE in which a Phocaean fleet on Corsica fought an allied force of Etruscans and Carthaginians.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Herodotus has sixty Phocaean ships against sixty from each ally and a Cadmean victory — forty lost, the rest disabled — after which they left Corsica, the prisoners taken by the men of Agylla being stoned to death.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> He places it only in the Sardinian sea, so the battle’s modern name is the colony’s; Corsica afterwards held the only necropolis of Etruscan character recorded in France,<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> and a temple at Velia has yielded helmets read by its excavators as spoils of the fight.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>",
"Etruscan_influence_on_Rome": "Etruscan influence on Rome is the body of practice, insignia and learning that Roman writers traced to Etruria, and the modern argument over how much of early Rome it accounts for.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Livy derived the twelve lictors, the curule chair and the toga praetexta from Etruscan usage,<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> put workmen from all Etruria on the Capitoline temple and Etruscan soothsayers at the reading of public prodigies,<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> and the Latin alphabet reached Rome from Greek by way of Etruscan.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> How far that made Rome an Etruscan city is disputed, the literary picture of these neighbours being a construction of its own<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> and their presence in the city a hard problem still.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>",
"Roman_Kingdom": "Roman Kingdom is the period Roman writers placed at the beginning of their own history, from the founding of the city to the expulsion of its last king, which Livy reckoned at 244 years.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The throne was remembered as elective rather than hereditary,<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> and its kings were credited with institutions the Republic went on using, among them a senate of a hundred Patres whose descendants were the patricians.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> How far that account is history is a real question, since Romans did not begin writing their own until about 200 BCE,<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> while excavation shows the same centuries levelling the marshy Forum valley and raising the great temple on the Capitol.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>",
"Seven_Hills_of_Rome": "Seven Hills of Rome are the Palatine, Capitoline, Aventine, Caelian, Esquiline, Viminal and Quirinal, the high ground on the left bank of the Tiber that the ancient city grew across.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Roman writers describe them taken in by stages, with the earliest settlement on the Palatine, the Capitoline held with its citadel and the Caelian and Aventine occupied by incomers,<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> and the Quirinal, Viminal and Esquiline enclosed last of all behind a moat, a mound and a wall.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The fixed list of seven came later than the ground itself, and a festival called the Septimontium counted a different seven, places rather than these hills.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>",
"Palatine_Hill": "Palatine Hill is the central hill of ancient Rome, an irregular quadrilateral about 2 kilometres (1.2 miles) round whose highest point stands 43 metres (141 feet) above the Tiber.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Flat-topped and ringed by cliffs, almost surrounded by marshy valleys and joined to higher ground by only a narrow ridge, it was a strong position commanding the one permanent crossing of the lower river; ancient tradition put the city's earliest settlement on it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Roman writers set the augury before the founding there and the Lupercalia on it,<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> and because the emperors afterwards lived and built on the hill its Latin name passed to their residence and then to any palace.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>",
"Capitoline_Hill": "Capitoline Hill is the smallest of Rome's hills, about 460 metres (500 yards) long, ringed by steep cliffs except where it was reached from the forum valley, and falling into two summits with a dip between them.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The northern summit carried the citadel, or Arx, and the southern the temple of Jupiter, Juno and Minerva, dedicated in the republic's first year,<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> which made the hill the city's religious centre.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Roman writers derived the name Capitolium from <i>caput</i>, 'head', on a story of a human head found in the temple's foundations,<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> and its south-west cliff was where those convicted of capital crimes were thrown.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>",
"California": "California is a state on the Pacific coast of the United States and the most populous of the fifty, with a resident population of about 39.4 million.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> It covers 423,967 square kilometres (163,695 square miles), which makes it the third largest state by extent after Alaska and Texas, and its ground runs from the coast through the farmed Central Valley to the Sierra Nevada and down into the desert of Death Valley.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Admitted to the Union as the thirty-first state in September 1850, it drew mass migration after the discovery of gold and built an unusually broad economy taking in agriculture, film and the technology industry of Silicon Valley.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>",
"Texas": "Texas is a state in the south-centre of the United States, bordering Mexico along the Rio Grande, and at 695,662 square kilometres (268,596 square miles) it is the largest state apart from Alaska.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Some 31.3 million people live there, making it the second most populous state after California, with Houston its largest city and Austin its seat of government.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> It declared independence from Mexico in 1836 and existed for nearly a decade as the Republic of Texas before entering the Union as the twenty-eighth state in December 1845, the only state to join directly from independent nationhood.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>",
"Florida": "Florida is a state of the United States occupying the long peninsula that separates the Atlantic Ocean from the Gulf of Mexico, together with a panhandle running west along the Gulf coast.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> It became the twenty-seventh state in March 1845 and is now the most populous of the south-eastern states, with about 23.4 million residents,<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> its capital at Tallahassee in the panhandle and its largest city at Jacksonville.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Low and wet in the south, where the sawgrass of the Everglades drains slowly seaward, it has built an economy on tourism and services rather than on the agriculture and manufacturing that preceded them.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>",
"Rhode_Island": "Rhode Island is the smallest state of the United States, lying on the New England coast between Connecticut and Massachusetts and covering 4,001 square kilometres (1,545 square miles), much of which is Narragansett Bay rather than land.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> About 1.11 million people live there, most of them clustered around the bay and along the corridor running north-east towards Boston, and its capital and largest city alike is Providence.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Founded as a refuge for religious dissenters in the 1630s and long suspicious of central authority, it was the last of the thirteen original states to ratify the federal constitution, entering the Union in May 1790.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>",
"Providence": "Providence is the capital of Rhode Island and its largest city, standing at the head of Narragansett Bay with a population of roughly 195,000.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> It was founded in 1636 by Roger Williams, who had been banished from the Massachusetts Bay colony over his religious opinions and was sheltered by the region's Native peoples; he settled at a place the Narragansett called Moshassuck and renamed it in acknowledgement of a deliverance he took to be merciful.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Built on the principle that no one should be molested for a belief or a worship merely religious, the town became a refuge for dissenters and later a centre of textiles, jewellery and silverware.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>",
"Spartan_intervention_at_Athens": "The Spartan intervention at Athens was the series of expeditions Sparta sent into Attica around the fall of the Athenian tyranny, which Herodotus counted within a longer reckoning of four Dorian comings, twice as invaders and twice as helpers.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> In the best-known of them the Spartan king Cleomenes came at the invitation of Isagoras, banished seven hundred households as accursed and tried to dissolve the Council, until the citizens besieged him on the acropolis and let him leave under truce on the third day.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> A larger expedition afterwards reached Eleusis and broke up without a battle when the Corinthians withdrew and the second Spartan king did the same.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>",
"Isagoras": "Isagoras was one of the two men who held power at Athens after the tyrants were removed, of a notable house whose lineage Herodotus says he cannot give, noting only that his kinsfolk sacrificed to Zeus of Caria.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The <i>Constitution of the Athenians</i> calls him a friend of the tyrants, and his rival Cleisthenes, worsted among the political clubs, answered by taking the people into his party.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Losing ground, he called in the Spartan king Cleomenes, whose troops banished seven hundred households and tried to dissolve the Council, until the citizens besieged them on the acropolis and let them leave under truce.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>",
"Cleisthenes_of_Athens": "Cleisthenes of Athens was the Alcmaeonid who reorganised the Athenian citizen body after the fall of the tyranny, and whom Herodotus names as the man who gave the Athenians their tribes and their democracy.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Losing ground to Isagoras among the political clubs, he took the commons into his party, was demanded as one of the Accursed and withdrew, and was sent for again when the Spartan force left the acropolis under truce.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> He divided the citizens into ten tribes instead of four, enlarged the Council to five hundred, made the deme a man's official designation, and, the <i>Constitution of the Athenians</i> says, enacted the law about ostracism.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>",
"Cleisthenic_tribes": "The Cleisthenic tribes were the ten phylai into which Cleisthenes divided the Athenian citizen body in place of the four tribes named after the sons of Ion, taking the new names from heroes all native except Aias.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The <i>Constitution of the Athenians</i> gives the purpose as mixing the citizens up so that more might take part in the government, each tribe drawing on all three regions of Attica through thirty parts assigned to them by lot.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The eponymous heroes were chosen by the Pythian priestess from a list of a hundred, and the tribe became the unit by which the Council was filled and the generals were elected.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>",
"Deme": "A deme was a local unit of the Athenian countryside, over which the people lived scattered in independent townships, and which Cleisthenes made the official designation of a citizen in place of his father's name.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The <i>Constitution of the Athenians</i> says the demes were named from their localities or from their founders, that demarchs were given the duties the ship-commissioners had had, and that each one's clans, brotherhoods and priesthoods were left untouched.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> A man became a citizen by being entered on his deme's roll at eighteen, the demesmen voting on oath on his age and his free birth, with an appeal to the jury-court for anyone rejected.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>",
"Trittys": "A trittys was a third of a tribe in the civic divisions of ancient Athens, an arrangement that already existed under the four older tribes, each of which had three assigned to it along with twelve ship-boards to each third.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The <i>Constitution of the Athenians</i> says Cleisthenes portioned out the land among the demes into thirty parts, ten belonging to the suburbs, ten to the coast and ten to the inland district, and gave these parts the name of Thirds.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> They were assigned among his ten tribes by lot, three to each, in order that every tribe might have a share in all the districts.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>",
"Council_of_Five_Hundred": "The Council of Five Hundred was the deliberative council of ancient Athens, made by Cleisthenes in place of one of four hundred and drawn by lot, fifty men from each of his ten tribes.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The <i>Constitution of the Athenians</i> says the presidency passed from tribe to tribe in an order settled by lot, its presidents dining in the Round-house at public expense and convening the meetings of Council and people.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> It prepared the resolutions the assembly voted on, no measure being valid that it had not published in advance, and it lost the power to fine, imprison and kill when a rescued prisoner was acquitted by a jury.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>",
"Isonomia": "Isonomia is the Greek word for equality under the law, and Herodotus has Otanes tell the Persian conspirators that the rule of the multitude has the loveliest name of all.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Otanes defines it by what it does — determining offices by lot, holding power accountable and conducting all deliberating publicly — each the opposite of what a ruler answerable to nobody does.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Herodotus reports two men proclaiming it: Maeandrius offered it to the Samians while reserving six talents and a priesthood for himself, and Aristagoras gave it to Miletus so that the city would join his revolt.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>",
"Ostracism": "Ostracism was the Athenian procedure by which the citizens banished one of their number for ten years, credited by the <i>Constitution of the Athenians</i> to Cleisthenes and enacted out of suspicion of the men in power after Peisistratus had made himself tyrant.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The assembly decided in the sixth presidency of each year whether to hold one, and Plutarch describes the vote as sherds carried to a railed enclosure in the market-place.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Fewer than six thousand in all made it void; otherwise the man named most often left the city for ten years, keeping the enjoyment of his estate.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>",
"Naukrary": "A naukrary was one of the units the Athenians were divided into before Cleisthenes, and the <i>Constitution of the Athenians</i> assigns twelve of them to each of the four old tribes, with an office set over them whose holders handled the levies and the expenditure.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The same work says clauses ordering that officer to levy or to spend out of his fund recurred in the laws of Solon, and that Cleisthenes put the demes in their place and gave demarchs the same duties.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Herodotus calls the presidents of the naukraroi the rulers of Athens at the time of Cylon's attempt, where Thucydides gives that charge to the nine archons.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>",
"Peisistratid_building_programme": "The Peisistratid building programme is the work the tyrants of Athens are credited with putting up in the city, and the piece Aristotle names is the temple of Olympian Zeus.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> He lists that building beside the pyramids in Egypt and the votive offerings of the Cypselids as instances of a device of tyranny, which is to keep subjects poor and busy enough to have no leisure for plotting.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Thucydides says the spring below the citadel was known as Fairwater while it lay open and as Nine Pipes after the tyrants altered it, and Pausanias, who saw the temple standing only because Hadrian dedicated it, says Peisistratus embellished the fountain.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>",
"Old_Temple_of_Athena": "The Old Temple of Athena is the modern name for a large set of foundations that lie on the acropolis at Athens, between the Erechtheion and the Parthenon.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Xenophon dates a year by an eclipse of the moon and by the burning of the old temple of Athena at Athens, in the year Callias was archon and Pityas ephor at Sparta.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Herodotus, writing earlier, says the fetters of Chalcidian and Boeotian prisoners still hung in his own day from acropolis walls that the Persians' fire had charred, opposite the temple which faces west.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>",
"Laurion": "Laurion is the mining district of Attica, whose silver Xenophon calls a gift of divine providence, since of the many states near Athens by land and sea not one has even a thin vein of the ore.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> He reports that the mines had been worked for so many generations that nobody attempted to date the beginning of the work, and that Nicias once owned a thousand men there and let them out for an obol a day each.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Thucydides has Alcibiades advise the Spartans that fortifying Decelea would cut the Athenians off from the revenues of those mines.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>",
"Athenian_owls": "Athenian owls are the silver coins of Athens, named from the reverse, which shows an owl to the right with its head facing and its wings closed, an olive-spray behind it, and the letters ΑΘΕ.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The obverse carries the head of Athena in a crested helmet, and the series is remarkable for its uniformity of style and type, running from the decadrachm down through the tetradrachm to fractions of an obol.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Xenophon says a merchant at Athens could export silver in place of a return cargo and be sure of a profit wherever he sold it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>",
"Enmity_between_Athens_and_Aegina": "The enmity between Athens and Aegina is the quarrel Herodotus traces to a request from Epidaurus for Athenian olive wood, out of which the Aeginetans stole the images of Damia and Auxesia when they revolted.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> An Athenian trireme sent to fetch them back lost its whole crew, and the one survivor was stabbed to death by the wives of the dead with their brooch-pins, after which Athenian women were put into a pinless linen dress.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> An oracle told Athens to wait thirty years before making war, and the Aeginetans later ambushed the sacred ship at Sunium.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>",
"Orientalising_period": "The <b>Orientalising period</b> is the phase of Greek art conventionally placed in the 7th century BCE, when motifs and techniques from the Near East were taken up in Greek workshops, the change setting in towards the end of the 8th.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> What was borrowed runs well beyond decoration: pottery and metalwork technologies, ivory carving, monumental sculpture in an eastern manner, masonry, burial and banqueting customs, and alphabetic writing all belong to it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Scholarship separates eastern imports and their imitations from orientalising work proper, which is Greek in manufacture and eastern only in inspiration, and ties its rise to the forming city-states.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>",
"Corinthian_pottery": "<b>Corinthian pottery</b> is the painted ware made at Corinth between roughly 750 and 550 BCE, its earlier phase called Protocorinthian, and much of it consists of small vessels for scented oil: the pear-shaped lekythos or alabastron, the round aryballos, and cups and boxes beside them.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The decoration runs to friezes of animals and eastern monsters set against a field strewn with rosettes and other filling ornament, with details cut through the black silhouette and touches of purple added.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> It travelled widely across the Mediterranean, and Athenian workshops took up its shapes and its incised line before displacing it in the 6th century.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>",
"Black-figure_technique": "The <b>black-figure technique</b> paints figures as silhouettes in a slip of fine clay on the pot's own body, with details cut through the slip by a graver and further touches added in red and white.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Its colours are made in the kiln rather than mixed on a palette: three stages of firing, oxidising then reducing then oxidising again, leave the coarse body red and the vitrified slip black.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> First used at Corinth and taken up at Athens, it was the leading way of decorating Greek pots until red-figure reversed the scheme late in the 6th century BCE.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>",
"Exekias": "<b>Exekias</b> was an Athenian potter and vase-painter of the middle of the 6th century BCE, ten of whose works carry his name, and who twice signed as having both painted and made the pot rather than only made it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> His subjects are the common stock of the day, distinguished by the delicacy and minuteness of the incising, a sparing use of added colour, and the naming of nearly every figure.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> He is credited with bringing the wide-bowled eye-cup to Athens, and narrative in Attic vase-painting is held to reach its full development with him.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>",
"François_Vase": "The François Vase is an Attic black-figure volute krater of about 570 BCE, signed by Ergotimos as potter and Kleitias as painter, and named after Alessandro François, who gathered its fragments from a plundered tomb near Chiusi in the 1840s.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> It is 66 centimetres (26 inches) high with a rim 57 centimetres (22 inches) across, put back together from those pieces, and it has been in Florence ever since.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Its wealth of subjects fills every available space, six figured registers running from the neck to the foot with further scenes on the handles, and the names painted beside the figures are lettered in an unmistakably Athenian alphabet.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>",
"Kleitias": "Kleitias was an Athenian vase-painter of about 570 BCE, and one of the earliest Attic painters whose name is known, since he wrote it on his own work in the black-figure technique.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The two pieces that carry his signature were both potted by Ergotimos: the great volute krater in Florence called the François Vase, and a stand in New York small enough to sit in the palm of a hand.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> He drew small figures with great precision and gave them names in quantity, the krater alone carrying 130 painted inscriptions, and his work is placed among the finest in Attic black-figure.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>",
"Amasis_Painter": "The Amasis Painter is the name given to an Athenian black-figure vase-painter at work from about 560 to 515 BCE, taken from the potter Amasis, whose signature the vases carry.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Since that signature is a potter's formula, meaning Amasis made me, the painter's own name is unrecorded, and museums credit the potting to Amasis and the drawing to the Amasis Painter.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> His manner is individual enough that many unsigned vases have been attributed to him, his drawing careful and delicate, his figures rather stiff, and his work full of hints of a connection with Ionia.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>",
"Attic_pottery_trade": "The Attic pottery trade was the export of Athenian painted pottery around the Mediterranean from the late 7th century BCE onwards, and it is why far more Athenian vases have come to light abroad, above all in the cemeteries of Etruria, than in Greece itself.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The merchants who handled it left a record on the pots, scratching signs under the foot and sometimes a note of the batch, with the shapes named, counted and priced in drachmas and obols.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Those prices are low, and the ware turns up in ordinary houses and in sanctuaries as well as in rich tombs, so it is no longer described as a luxury good.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>",
"Red-figure_technique": "The red-figure technique leaves the figures in the colour of the fired clay and covers the surface around them with a black slip, so that they stand out light against a dark ground.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> It was invented at Athens in the last quarter of the 6th century BCE, and its detail is drawn with a brush rather than cut with a graver, in relief lines for the firm markings and gloss thinned to a warm brown for the softer ones.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The freedom that gave the draughtsman carried it through the 5th century and into the 4th, by which time the busiest workshops lay in southern Italy and Sicily.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>",
"Chinese_mythology": "Chinese mythology is the body of myth preserved in China’s classical literature, where it survives not as a collection of its own but scattered through geographies, ritual compendia and philosophical treatises.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Its richest single repertory is the <i>Shanhaijing</i>, or <i>Classic of Mountains and Seas</i>, one of several compilations made around the turn of the era and the only one given wholly to cosmography.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Accounts of the world’s origin are sparse, the creator figure Pangu being first written down only in the 3rd century CE, and the recurring flood is told as an inundation to be set in order rather than a punishment for human sin.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>",
"Three_Sovereigns_and_Five_Emperors": "Three Sovereigns and Five Emperors are the rulers Chinese tradition places before the first dynasty, credited in the annals with instituting marriage, nets for hunting and fishing, domesticated animals and the offices of state.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Which figures belong on the lists was never settled: the <i>Records of the Grand Historian</i> opens with five emperors and no sovereigns, and the chapter supplying the missing three was prefixed to it eight centuries afterwards.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> That arrangement follows five-phases cosmology rather than any record, and the traditional chronologies disagree with one another everywhere before 841 BCE, the earliest year Chinese dating fixes securely.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>",
"Pangu": "Pangu is the giant of Chinese myth whose growth pushed heaven and earth apart and whose body, at his death, became the sun and moon, the mountains, the rivers and the plants.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> He is named in no surviving text of the Han period or earlier, and the first written accounts of him, a fragment of Wei Zhao's lost <i>Dongji</i> and Xu Zheng's <i>Sanwu liji</i>, belong to the 3rd century CE.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Daoism later made him a god in his own right, private histories from the Song onwards treated him as the first ruler of all, and versions of his story are still told among peoples of southern China.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>",
"Nüwa": "Nüwa is the goddess of Chinese myth who made the first people by moulding them out of yellow earth and who patched the sky when it broke.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> She is almost always shown beside Fuxi, the two with human faces and the bodies of snakes and their tails sometimes twined together, a pairing that the texts naming them earliest do not make and that took its familiar form under the Han.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Carved through Han funerary art and later carried west to the tombs of Gansu and Turfan, she was also written into the dynastic past as one of the Three Sovereigns.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>",
"Fuxi": "Fuxi is the culture hero of Chinese myth credited with devising the eight trigrams, the three-line figures of unbroken and broken strokes that underlie the <i>Book of Changes</i>.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Later accounts add that he knotted cords into nets for hunting and fishing, put writing in place of knotted-cord records, set the rite of marriage and named his officials after dragons, and they give him a human head on a snake's body.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> He heads most lists of the Three Sovereigns and is almost always shown beside Nüwa in Han funerary art, though the texts naming the two earliest keep them apart.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>",
"Shennong": "Shennong, the Divine Husbandman, is the culture hero of Chinese myth who taught farming: the Great Appendix to the <i>Book of Changes</i> has him cut wood into a ploughshare, bend wood into a plough-handle, and set the market at midday.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Later accounts give him the body of a man and the head of an ox, make him reign by the power of fire under the name Yandi, and have him test the hundred plants to find the first drugs that cure.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The herbal that bears his name, listing 365 drugs, was compiled long afterwards under the Han.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>",
"Yellow_Emperor": "The Yellow Emperor, Huangdi, is the sovereign of Chinese legend with whom Sima Qian chose to begin the <i>Shiji</i>, opening it on the Annals of the Five Emperors.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The account makes him a son of Shaodian who mastered the beasts, defeated Yandi at Banquan and Chiyou at Zhuolu, took his title from the power of earth whose colour is yellow, and named his officials after the clouds.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Every later ruler of that chapter descends from him, though no part of it can be dated: exact Chinese chronology reaches back only to 841 BCE.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>",
"Chiyou": "Chiyou is the rebel of Chinese legend whom the Yellow Emperor defeated and killed at Zhuolu, and the oldest name the <i>Book of Documents</i> records, which says that, according to the teachings of antiquity, he was the first to produce disorder.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Sima Qian allows him only four lines, saying that he was the most terrible and remained invincible until he disobeyed the imperial orders and was seized, and never saying what he was.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Later texts make him a monster: a book quoted by a Tang scholar gives him 81 brothers with heads of bronze and foreheads of iron, who forged the five kinds of weapon.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>",
"Battle_of_Zhuolu": "The Battle of Zhuolu is the fight of Chinese legend in which the Yellow Emperor destroyed Chiyou, and in the <i>Shiji</i> the act that made him emperor: the lords raised him to Son of Heaven afterwards, in place of the house of Shennong.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> It is the second of a pair, Banquan having been fought first against Yandi, and the commentators place both fields in what is now Zhuolu in Hebei, only a few li apart.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The fighting was known long before the histories, since a diviner in the <i>Zuo Commentary</i> calls a favourable omen that of the Yellow Emperor's battle at Banquan.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>",
"Yao": "Yao is the sage-king with whom the <i>Book of Documents</i> begins, its first chapter calling him reverential, intelligent, accomplished and thoughtful, naturally and without effort.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The same chapter has him set the Xi and He brothers at the four quarters to watch the heavens and deliver the seasons, telling them that a round year is 366 days, to be squared with the seasons by an intercalary month.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> After seventy years he passed over his own son and gave the throne to a commoner, Shun, and it is that refusal the tradition kept, Confucius calling his virtue so vast the people could find no name for it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>",
"Shun": "Shun is the second Chinese sage-king, a commoner Yao raised to the throne over his own son, and the <i>Book of Documents</i> gives him a canon of its own, opening on a man styled Chonghua, profound, wise and intelligent.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> That chapter has him regulate the pitch-pipes and the measures of length, capacity and weight, divide the land into twelve provinces, banish four criminals to the frontiers, and appoint ministers examined every third year.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Later ages made him the pattern of two things at once: filial conduct towards a father who tried to kill him, and rule so effortless that Confucius said he did nothing but gravely occupy his seat.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>",
"Yu_the_Great": "Yu the Great is the legendary figure with whom Chinese tradition closes its sequence of sage rulers and opens its dynastic record, credited with draining a great flood, dividing the land into nine provinces and founding the Xia.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The classics dwell on his labour rather than his majesty: he is said to have travelled by carriage, boat, sledge and spiked shoe, and to have spent his strength on ditches and water channels while passing his own door without entering.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> When the throne went to his son rather than to the minister he had named, the sequence of abdications ended, a change the tradition itself found it necessary to explain.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>",
"Chinese_flood_myth": "The Chinese flood myth is the tradition of a great inundation in the reigns of Yao and Shun, whose waters embraced the hills and drove people to nest in trees and shelter in caves.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> It is not a deluge sent in punishment and survived in a boat but a disorder of the river channels: Gun failed to check it in nine years and was banished, and his son Yu dug the channels open and led the waters to the sea.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The tradition makes that draining the start of ordered government, following it with a survey of the land, its division into nine provinces and the fixing of tribute.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>",
"Houyi": "Houyi is the archer of Chinese legend, and the name is borne by more than one figure: a bowman serving the legendary emperor Ku, a descendant under Yao who shot into the sky to scatter false suns, and the prince of Qiong who seized the Xia throne.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The classics are concerned with the last of these, who relied on his archery, neglected the people's business and gave himself to hunting until the minister he had raised turned the court against him.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> He was killed by his own servants, and Confucius's questioner named him as proof that eminent prowess does not secure a natural death.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>",
"Chang'e": "Chang'e is the woman of the moon in Chinese tradition, known from the Han dynasty as the wife of the archer Houyi, who stole the drug of immortality he had begged from the Queen Mother of the West and fled with it to the moon.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> In the oldest surviving telling, by the astronomer Zhang Heng, her arrival is a punishment rather than an escape, for she is turned there into the toad whose outline observers traced on the lunar face.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Her name was first Heng, after a line in the ancient odes, and was altered because two Tang emperors bore that character.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>",
"Kuafu": "Kuafu is a giant of early Chinese myth who pursued the sun and died of thirst, a story the <i>Classic of Mountains and Seas</i> tells in a few dozen characters: he drank the Yellow River and the Wei dry, made north for the Great Marsh, died on the road, and his cast-off staff became a forest.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> His name describes rather than names him, kua meaning large and fu being a word for a man, and the same book gives him a lineage and a country of giants.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> A second telling has the winged dragon Yinglong kill him as it killed Chiyou, so he dies in battle rather than of thirst.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>",
"Jingwei": "Jingwei is a bird of Chinese myth that carries wood and stones from the western hills out over the Eastern Sea and drops them in, meaning to fill it up.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The <i>Classic of Mountains and Seas</i> places her on Mount Fajiu and describes a bird shaped like a crow with a patterned head, a white beak and red feet, whose cry is its own name, and says she was the youngest daughter of Yandi, drowned while wandering on that sea and changed into the bird.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The endless labour became a set phrase, read both as wasted effort and as unbreakable resolve.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>",
"Gonggong": "Gonggong is a figure of early Chinese tradition remembered as a rebel who raised a great flood and later worshipped as a god of water, his name being also the title of an office, roughly Minister of Works or overseer of the waters.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Defeated in a contest for the throne, he struck Mount Buzhou, the sky-pillar of the north-west, so that heaven tilted that way and the earth fell short in the south-east, which accounts in this tradition for the courses of the stars and the rivers.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The classics also carry a historicised Gonggong whom Yao declined to employ and Shun banished as one of four great criminals.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>",
"Kunlun": "Kunlun is the mountain at the centre of the world in Chinese tradition, placed in the far west and named as the source of the great rivers.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> It enters the record as an ordinary geographical name, listed in the Tribute of Yu among the places from which the western tribes brought hair-cloth and skins, and only later did the cosmogonists raise it into a paradise walled in nine gradations, with jade towers, a tree of immortality and a spring whose water defeats death.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The Queen Mother of the West keeps her court there, and King Mu of Zhou is said to have driven out to meet her.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>",
"Queen_Mother_of_the_West": "The Queen Mother of the West, Xiwangmu in Chinese, is a goddess who keeps the elixir of immortality and holds court on the western mountain Kunlun at the head of the immortals.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Beside her Lake of Gems grows the peach tree whose fruit confers immortality, ripening once in three thousand years, and the feast at which it is served is kept as her birthday.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Her name appears in a handful of very early texts, which say almost nothing about her, and the elaborate figure was built up by Taoist writers over the centuries that followed.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>",
"Penglai": "Penglai is the chief of the three isles of the immortals, which Chinese tradition places in the Eastern Sea opposite the coast of Shandong, its neighbours being Fangzhang and Yingzhou.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Its people are immortals whose shining bodies are sustained by the gems lying scattered along the shores and by a fountain of life that runs there without ceasing, and the herbs of long life are held to grow on the island.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The First Emperor of Qin sent a fleet under the magician Xu Fu to find it, an expedition that by one account sighted the islands and was driven home by the wind.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>",
"Eight_Immortals": "The Eight Immortals are a group of figures of Taoist legend — Zhongli Quan, Zhang Guolao, Lü Dongbin, Li Tieguai, Han Xiangzi, Lan Caihe, Cao Guojiu and He Xiangu — who between them stand for the old and the young, the rich and the poor, the soldier and the scholar, the crippled and the female.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Each is recognised in art by a personal emblem rather than by a face, and few subjects have been painted, moulded and carved more often in China.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> As a fixed set they cannot be traced beyond the Yuan dynasty, although several of the eight had been celebrated singly as immortals long before that.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>",
"Chinese_dragon": "The Chinese dragon, the long, is a scaled and horned creature of the waters that brings rain and cloud, and it is benevolent rather than a monster to be killed, being the essence of the yang principle and chief of the four divinely constituted creatures.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Tradition gives it nine resemblances taken from other animals, among them a deer's horns, a camel's head, a snake's neck and a hawk's claws, though the authorities do not agree on all nine.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> It became the emblem of the emperor, whose throne was the dragon seat and whose robes alone carried a beast with five claws to a foot.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>",
"Fenghuang": "The Fenghuang is a bird of Chinese legend, one of the four supernatural creatures, whose male is the feng and female the huang and whose compound name English usually renders as the Chinese phoenix.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Descriptions differ and none settles on a single animal, giving it a hen's head, a serpent's neck, a swallow's brow, a tortoise's back and a fish-like tail, with five colours in its plumage and five notes in its song.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> It is above all an omen, said to appear only where a kingdom is tranquil and its ruler fond of peace, which made a reported sighting a useful thing for a courtier to arrange.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>",
"Qilin": "The qilin is a creature of Chinese legend, one of the four supernatural animals, whose male is the qi and female the lin, and which English has long called the Chinese unicorn.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Descriptions vary and none settles on a single animal, an early glossary giving it a deer's body, an ox's tail and one horn, and later pictures a pair of horns above a scaled body.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Its meaning lies in when it comes rather than what it looks like: it is said to appear only where a wise ruler reigns or a sage is about to be born, and is invoked in popular practice to grant a son.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>",
"Four_Symbols": "The Four Symbols are the four quarters of the Chinese sky, each named for a creature: the Azure Dragon in the east, the Vermilion Bird in the south, the White Tiger in the west and the Dark Warrior in the north.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> They are a division of the stars rather than a bestiary, the 28 lunar mansions being shared out seven to a quarter, and each carries a season and a direction together.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The northern quarter is in fact symbolised by a tortoise, its other name belonging to an emperor who in one legend defeated a demon king that had taken a tortoise's shape.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>",
"Taotie": "The taotie is the monster face found on Chinese ritual bronzes of the Shang and Zhou, a symmetrical mask of two great eyes, brows and heavy jaws set about a ridge down the middle.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Its name means glutton, and the earliest text to use it means a man rather than a mask: one of four wicked ones whom the Zuo Commentary says Shun banished to the four distant regions for insatiable greed.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> What the face meant to the people who cast it is not established, the tiger, a storm god and the Tibetan mastiff having all been proposed for it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>",
"Classic_of_Mountains_and_Seas": "The <i>Classic of Mountains and Seas</i> is an anonymous Chinese survey of hills, waterways, minerals, plants, animals, peoples and gods, arranged as a journey outward from a centre and traditionally said to describe charts on nine bronze vessels cast by Yu the Great after the flood.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Its date is unsettled, 19th-century sinology placing it in the Zhou or earlier, and the surviving text was edited in the 4th century by Guo Pu, who also annotated the <i>Elegies of Chu</i>.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Long read with distrust as geography, it is the wellspring of early Chinese myth, describing figures and omen beasts recorded nowhere else so fully.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>",
"Chuci": "The <i>Chuci</i>, or Elegies of Chu, is an anthology of poetry from the southern state of Chu, treated in Chinese bibliography as its own class, chiefly the work of the minister Qu Yuan and including the <i>Li Sao</i> and the <i>Nine Songs</i>.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Its verse is irregular in metre and so allusive that much of it is unintelligible without commentary, and poems by Song Yu and Jing Cha, both nearly contemporary, make up the rest.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> It was first assembled by Liu Xiang in the 1st century BCE and survives earliest in Wang Yi's edition in 17 books, which carries his commentary and an appendix of later imitators.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>",
"Jade_Emperor": "The Jade Emperor, or Yuhuang Shangdi, is the supreme god of the popular Taoist pantheon, jade standing for purity in his name, and he is worshipped as ruler of heaven and granter of pardons.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Legend makes him the son of a childless queen who dreamed that Lord Lao brought her a boy, and who gave up his throne within days to live as a hermit and heal the sick.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> His place in the state cult is late: the Song emperor Zhenzong, after a humiliating peace with the Khitan, announced letters from him and had the name entered among the divinities in 1012, though one manual dates the worship to 1116.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>",
"Diyu": "<b>Diyu</b> is the underworld of Chinese popular religion, its name meaning earth-prison and rendering the Sanskrit Naraka, the place of punishment for the wicked that Buddhism brought into China.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> It is not a final state but a stage of transmigration, closer to a purgatory than to an eternal hell, since sentences end and a soul that has served its term is reborn among the six classes of living beings.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Its ruler is Yanluo, the Sanskrit Yama, and later tradition divided it into ten courts under the Jade Emperor, gave it an entrance in a mountain near Fengdu in Sichuan, and so made it Chinese.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>",
"Ten_Kings_of_Hell": "The <b>Ten Kings of Hell</b> are the ten presiding judges of the Chinese underworld, one to each of its ten courts, through which the dead are passed in turn.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The scheme is set out in a Taoist handbook and arranged like a magistrate's court: the first king keeps the register of the living and the dead, the middle courts hold sixteen small hells apiece with their own lists of offences, and the tenth settles where a soul is reborn.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Several of the kings are dead Chinese officials whom popular opinion promoted, and the living could petition them for a dead relative in the hope of a lighter sentence.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>",
"Zao_Jun": "<b>Zao Jun</b> is the god of the kitchen in Chinese popular religion, a household deity whose printed image is pasted up beside the cooking range and honoured with incense on the first and fifteenth of each month.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> His office is less cooking than watching: he keeps a record of the good and evil done by the family he lodges with, and at the end of each year he goes up to report on their conduct.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The household sees him off by burning his picture, having first fed him honey or sticky rice so that his lips will seal, and pastes up a new one for his return.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>",
"Menshen": "<b>Menshen</b> are the guardian gods of the door in Chinese popular religion, a pair of armed figures painted or pasted one to each leaf of a double door and renewed at the New Year.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The name is plural and works as an office rather than a personal name: its oldest holders are Shen Shu and Yu Lu, two spirits who in an early legend guarded the ghosts' gate in a peach tree and fed wrongdoers to the tigers.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> They were later displaced in popular favour by two generals of the Tang emperor Taizong, and what a household puts up is sometimes the pictures and sometimes only the two names.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>",
"Chinese_zodiac": "The <b>Chinese zodiac</b> is a cycle of twelve animals — rat, ox, tiger, hare, dragon, serpent, horse, goat, monkey, cock, dog and pig — attached one apiece to the twelve earthly branches, a counting series that also numbers the double hours of the day and twelve points of the compass.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Because the branches name years as well, a person born in a given year is said to belong to that year's animal, which fortune-tellers weigh against the five elements.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> It is not a zodiac in the astronomical sense, and where the cycle began, in China or among the Turkic peoples who also use it, was long disputed.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>",
"Cowherd_and_Weaver_Girl": "The <b>Cowherd and Weaver Girl</b> are two stars on opposite sides of the Milky Way whom Chinese tradition makes a married couple kept apart, allowed to meet only on the seventh night of the seventh month, when magpies flock together and bridge the river of heaven for her.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The Weaver Girl is Vega, while the Cowherd was placed by some in Aquila and by others among stars drawn from Capricorn and Sagittarius.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Their oldest notice is a single line in a Han treatise, and their meeting is marked by a festival at which women and girls make offerings and ask for skill at the needle.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>",
"Legend_of_the_White_Snake": "The <b>Legend of the White Snake</b> is a Chinese folk tale in which a snake spirit takes the form of a woman, marries a young man in Hangzhou, and is finally imprisoned beneath a lakeside pagoda by a Buddhist monk.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> It reached its settled form in a late Ming story known from a woodblock print of 1624, in which the snake is a menace who possesses her husband and he ends the tale as a monk.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> From the middle of the 18th century the retellings turned on the marriage instead, making her a devoted wife and mother whose son eventually secures her release.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>",
"Mazu": "<b>Mazu</b> is a Chinese sea goddess, worshipped by sailors, fishermen and traders, whose cult began on the coast of Fujian under the Song dynasty and spread with emigration to Taiwan and Southeast Asia.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> She is remembered as a woman of Meizhou Island, known in her lifetime for filial piety, for healing and for saving people from disaster at sea, and honoured after her death by successive emperors with a long series of official titles.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Her temples show her flanked by two assistants, Thousand-mile-eye and Favourable-wind-ear, and seafarers carried embers from her censer aboard ship and called on her by name in a storm.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>",
"Guandi": "<b>Guandi</b> is the Chinese god of war, the deified form of Guan Yu, a general of the 3rd century CE who was captured and beheaded in 219.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Successive dynasties raised him by decree — a duke early in the 12th century, a prince in 1128, and in 1594 a <i>di</i>, meaning 'god' — after which the state worshipped him officially in some 1,600 temples.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> He is a patron of scholars as well as soldiers, on a legend that he could recite the <i>Zuo Commentary</i> from beginning to end, and Buddhists count him a guardian of monasteries.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>",
"Chinese_legendary_period": "The <b>Chinese legendary period</b> is the division of early history that tradition furnishes with named rulers and exact years of accession, set apart from the purely mythical epochs of sovereigns placed before it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Standard tables open it with Fuxi in 2852 BCE and carry it through Shennong, the Yellow Emperor, Yao and Shun to 2255 BCE, although the compilers who drew up such schemes disagreed with one another throughout.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Sima Qian, writing in the 1st century BCE, held that the chronology of the three dynasties could not be made precise, and began his own exact years only with the Gonghe regency in 841 BCE.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>",
"Middle_Bronze_Age": "The Middle Bronze Age is the second of the three phases — Early, Middle and Late — into which archaeologists divide the Bronze Age of a region. Defined by a technology rather than a date, the phase falls at different times in different places;<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> radiocarbon dating puts its beginning in central Europe at about 1700 BCE,<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> while in the Aegean the same phase is Middle Minoan on Crete, Middle Helladic on the Greek mainland and Middle Cycladic in the islands.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> On Crete it is the phase in which the first palaces were built, at Knossos, Phaistos and Mallia, at the opening of Middle Minoan IB.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>",
"Late_Bronze_Age": "The Late Bronze Age is the last of the three phases — Early, Middle and Late — into which archaeologists divide the Bronze Age of a region. In the Aegean it is called Late Minoan on Crete, Late Helladic or Mycenaean on the Greek mainland and Late Cycladic in the islands, each subdivided into I, II and III, and during Late Minoan IB every Cretan palace but Knossos was destroyed and abandoned.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Across the eastern Mediterranean the phase closes around 1200 BCE in a wave of destructions, argued to follow some three centuries of drought,<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> though its dates elsewhere depend on when bronze reached the region.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>",
"Early_Iron_Age": "The Early Iron Age is the first of the phases into which archaeologists divide the Iron Age of a region, beginning wherever iron replaced bronze as the ordinary material for tools and weapons. Its dates are local: in Britain the phase runs from roughly 750 to 400 BCE,<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> while in the Aegean it opens with the Subminoan and Submycenaean pottery that follows the Mycenaean collapse and continues through the Protogeometric and Geometric styles.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Those Aegean dates are still argued over, because little between Late Helladic IIIC and the Greek colonisation of Italy and Sicily can be dated independently of the pottery, leaving rival schemes up to a century apart.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>",
"Middle_Iron_Age": "The Middle Iron Age is the middle phase in those regional schemes that divide the Iron Age into three, and it is a local label rather than a universal one, since many regions use only an early and a late stage or none at all. In Britain the sequence runs Early, Middle and Late, with the earliest of the three placed at about 750 to 400 BCE and the middle phase following it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The same three labels are used in southern Africa for entirely different centuries, dividing the ironworking communities of the 1st and 2nd millennia CE,<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> where iron came first and no Bronze Age preceded it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>",
"Late_Iron_Age": "The Late Iron Age is the final phase in those regional schemes that divide the Iron Age into more than one stage, and like the others it is defined locally rather than by any single date. In southern Britain it is the period of the Durotriges, who held the central southern coast from about 100 BCE to 100 CE and, unusually for the island, buried their dead in formal cemeteries, often placing substantial grave goods with women.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Elsewhere the label covers quite different centuries: in Botswana it belongs to settlements radiocarbon-dated between the 7th and 14th centuries CE,<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> while in the Aegean the sequence closes in the 8th century BCE.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>",
"Minoan_chronology": "Minoan chronology is the relative dating scheme that divides the Bronze Age of Crete into three main periods — Early, Middle and Late Minoan — each cut again into numbered and lettered phases distinguished chiefly by pottery. Arthur Evans coined the term Minoan and proposed the threefold scheme at the British Association in 1904, laying an outline of it before the Archaeological Congress at Athens the following year.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Because it was built on the stratigraphy of his own excavation at Knossos it fits some regions of Crete poorly, and a second scheme named for the palaces — Prepalatial, Protopalatial, Neopalatial, Postpalatial — is used alongside it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>",
"Early_Minoan_I": "Early Minoan I is the earliest phase of the Bronze Age on Crete in Arthur Evans's Minoan scheme, a division defined by styles of pottery rather than by any change in building or settlement. Its wares are separated from the preceding Neolithic by the arrival of the jug and by painted decoration in quantity, in four groups: pattern-burnished Pyrgos ware, dark-surfaced Incised ware, dark-on-light Ayios Onouphrios ware, and Lebena ware carrying white patterns over solid red.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Running to roughly 2700 BCE, it opens the Prepalatial period, the long span before any Cretan palace was built.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>",
"Early_Minoan_II": "Early Minoan II is the second and by far the longest phase of the Cretan Early Bronze Age, subdivided on ceramic grounds into an earlier IIA and a later IIB. Ayios Onouphrios and Lebena wares carry on from the previous phase while Pyrgos and Incised wares drop out, and two new fabrics mark it: fine grey ware, polished and incised, and Vasiliki ware, whose dark surfaces were mottled deliberately in the firing.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Substantial settlements at Vasiliki and at Fournou Korifi near Myrtos reached their fullest extent late in the phase and burnt at or near its end.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>",
"Early_Minoan_IIA": "Early Minoan IIA is the earlier of the two subdivisions of Early Minoan II, told from the later one by which of the painted fabrics dominates its deposits. Its characteristic pottery is fine grey ware — very fine, polished, grey, worked into spherical and cylindrical pyxides and decorated by incision — while Vasiliki ware, which defines the phase that follows, occurs only in small amounts and only in eastern Crete.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The first of the two building periods at Fournou Korifi near Myrtos belongs here, well inside the Prepalatial period that precedes the first palaces.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>",
"Early_Minoan_IIB": "Early Minoan IIB is the later subdivision of Early Minoan II, the phase in which settlement on Crete reached its greatest extent before the widespread destructions that close the Early Bronze Age there. Vasiliki ware becomes overwhelmingly dominant across eastern and southern Crete: flat-bottomed jugs, teapots, dishes, spouted bowls and goblets with deliberately mottled dark surfaces, some carrying applied pellets like eyes beside the spout.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Both Vasiliki itself and Fournou Korifi near Myrtos were at their largest now, and both were destroyed by fierce fire at or near the close of the phase, around 2150 BCE.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>",
"Early_Minoan_III": "Early Minoan III is the last and shortest phase of the Early Minoan sequence, falling between the destructions that closed Early Minoan II and the start of the Middle Minoan period. Evans defined it by white-on-dark painted pottery unlike anything before it, working from eastern Cretan sites rather than from Knossos, which left it thinly attested in the north until a deposit called the Upper East Well was published in 1978.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Eastern Crete carries curvilinear white-on-dark ornament; the north-central sites favour plain banding and a footed goblet, and neither barbotine nor polychromy appears until Middle Minoan IA.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>",
"Middle_Minoan_I": "Middle Minoan I is the first phase of the Middle Minoan period on Crete, divided into an earlier IA and a later IB, and the phase across which the island's first palaces appear. Middle Minoan IA grows directly out of Early Minoan III and brings in polychrome painting in a light-on-dark style along with barbotine relief decoration, though its pottery is still made entirely by hand.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> In Middle Minoan IB the fast potter's wheel comes into use and the first certain palaces are built at Knossos and Phaistos, opening the Protopalatial period.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>",
"Middle_Minoan_IA": "Middle Minoan IA is the opening phase of the Middle Minoan period on Crete, a division that develops directly out of Early Minoan III with no break in the pottery sequence. Polychrome painting begins here, white and red or orange over a solid dark ground though still rare, as does barbotine relief decoration, and the straight-sided drinking cup later called the Vapheio or Keftiu cup makes its first appearance.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> All the pottery is still handmade and curvilinear ornament is far commoner than before; the phase closes the Prepalatial period, before any palace stood on the island.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>",
"Middle_Minoan_IB": "Middle Minoan IB is the phase of the Cretan Bronze Age in which the island's first palaces were built, at Knossos and at Phaistos. The fast potter's wheel is used for the first time, giving thinner walls, painting becomes a more complex polychromy that scholars call Early Kamares, and crinkled rims on some vessels imitate hammered metal; Cretan sherds of this date reached Kahun and Harageh in Egypt.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The carinated cup appears now and lasts into Middle Minoan IIIA, and the palaces begun here open the Protopalatial period, which runs on to the end of Middle Minoan IIB.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>",
"Middle_Minoan_II": "Middle Minoan II is a phase of the Cretan Bronze Age defined by ceramic styles found almost only at the palaces themselves, so that most Minoan sites pass straight from Middle Minoan IB to Middle Minoan IIIA. Its pottery is the finest the Aegean Bronze Age produced: eggshell-thin Kamares ware, so delicate that some argue it was moulded rather than thrown, painted in white with reds, oranges and yellows over a black ground.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The phase ends in a major destruction horizon, probably an earthquake, which closes the Protopalatial period; Quartier Mu at Mallia burnt violently at about the same time.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>",
"Middle_Minoan_III": "Middle Minoan III is the last phase of the Middle Minoan period on Crete, the interval between the destruction that ended the first palaces and the start of the Late Minoan sequence. The palaces at Knossos, Phaistos and probably Mallia were rebuilt now and the palace at Zakro was built for the first time, work that opens the Neopalatial period.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Pottery ceased to be a major art: most is unpainted, solidly painted or carries white patterns on dark, polychromy is rare, the carinated cup disappears, and drinking is done from straight-sided and semiglobular cups.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>",
"Late_Minoan_I": "Late Minoan I is the first of the three major divisions of the Cretan Late Bronze Age, cut into an earlier LM IA and a later LM IB. Its opening is marked in painted pottery by the change from light-on-dark decoration to dark-on-light, the convention that had governed Cretan vases through the Kamares wares of the preceding centuries.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> It is the phase in which Minoan influence across the southern Aegean reaches its height, from the Peloponnese through the Cyclades and Dodecanese to south-western Anatolia, and it ends late in LM IB with the violent destruction of every Cretan palace but Knossos.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>",
"Late_Minoan_IA": "Late Minoan IA is the earlier subdivision of Late Minoan I, and the phase during which the volcano on Thera erupted and buried the town at Akrotiri. Its commonest decorated shapes are the straight-sided cup, known as the Vapheio or Keftiu cup, the semiglobular cup, the bridge-spouted jar, the beaked jug and the ewer.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Its favourite motifs are spirals, often picked out with details in added white, and floral patterns such as the foliate band, the reed or grass pattern and the rosette, beside a ripple pattern carried over from the phase before.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>",
"Late_Minoan_IB": "Late Minoan IB is the later subdivision of Late Minoan I, distinguished less by ordinary pottery, which is hard to tell from Late Minoan IA, than by two fine painted styles that many hold to be the height of Minoan ceramic art. The Marine Style packs naturalistic sea creatures across the vase — argonaut, octopus, dolphin, murex, seaweed and sea anemone — while the sparer Alternating Style sets two isolated motifs turn about around the body.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> It closes in a destruction horizon that reached almost every substantial Cretan site, palaces, villas and towns alike.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>",
"Late_Minoan_II": "Late Minoan II is a short phase of the Cretan Late Bronze Age known from only a handful of sites and in quantity from Knossos alone, so that some have doubted it is a period at all rather than a local style. Stratified deposits at Knossos and Kommos show it is real, and its new shapes — the Cretan Ephyraean goblet, the horizontal-handled bowl, the krater and the squat alabastron — several of them of mainland ancestry, carry a turn away from naturalism toward abstract, zoned decoration.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The rich Warrior Graves around Knossos belong to the same moment.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>",
"Late_Minoan_III": "Late Minoan III is the last of the three major divisions of the Cretan Late Bronze Age, cut into LM IIIA, IIIB and IIIC and covering most of the post-palatial centuries on the island. Its pottery keeps certain Cretan habits, notably a preference for drinking cups with one handle rather than two, but is otherwise increasingly hard to separate from the wider Aegean tradition, of which it becomes one regional school among several.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Ruined sites abandoned at the end of Late Minoan IB were mostly reoccupied in this period, though not one destroyed palace was rebuilt as a palace.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>",
"Late_Minoan_IIIA": "Late Minoan IIIA is the first phase of Late Minoan III, developing smoothly out of Late Minoan II with motifs growing more abstract and decoration confined largely to a band on the vessel's shoulder. Its pottery is markedly more uniform across Crete than that of Late Minoan I, which had been strongly regional, and it is sometimes divided into IIIA1 and IIIA2, the break falling shortly before a great fire destroyed much of the palace at Knossos.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> A good marker of the later half is the champagne cup, a plain short-stemmed goblet with a single handle.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>",
"Late_Minoan_IIIB": "Late Minoan IIIB is the second phase of Late Minoan III, so lightly separated from the phase before it that many excavated groups are simply labelled Late Minoan IIIA2 to IIIB. What marks it out is the deep bowl, which becomes as common an open shape as the cups and stemmed goblets carried over from earlier, and panelled decoration, both of them borrowed from the Greek mainland.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Large coarse stirrup jars painted with Linear B inscriptions, made mostly in western Crete to ship oil or wine, are all but confined to this phase.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>",
"Late_Minoan_IIIC": "Late Minoan IIIC is the last phase of the Cretan Bronze Age, beginning at about the time the mainland Mycenaean palaces were destroyed and never rebuilt. Its pottery follows on gradually from what went before, the deep bowl now dominant and panelled patterns and facing spirals the commonest ornament, with a Fringed Style of short bars outlining the motifs developing late in the phase.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> What changes sharply is where people lived: low coastal settlements give way to defensible heights such as Karphi above the Lasithi plain and Kastri near Palaikastro.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>",
"Helladic_chronology": "Helladic chronology is the relative dating framework for the Bronze Age of mainland Greece, named from Hellas, the Greeks’ own word for their country, and cut into Early, Middle and Late Helladic phases. It was drawn up by Alan Wace and Carl Blegen in 1918 in imitation of the Minoan scheme Arthur Evans had built for Crete, and each of its divisions is subdivided again: Early Helladic into I, II and III, Late Helladic into I, IIA–B, IIIA1–2, IIIB and IIIC.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Middle Helladic is only occasionally subdivided, no single scheme having been agreed, and the label Mycenaean is used interchangeably with Late Helladic.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>",
"Early_Helladic": "Early Helladic is the first of the three divisions of the mainland Greek Bronze Age, running from about 3100 to about 2000 BCE and cut into EH I, II and III. Colin Renfrew proposed in 1972 that its phases be named for type sites rather than numbered, giving the Eutresis culture to EH I, the Korakou culture to EH II and the Tiryns culture to EH III.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Its middle phase is much the richest, producing fortified coastal settlements, quantities of clay sealings and the two-storeyed buildings known as Corridor Houses, whose tiled roofs disappear along with the building type at the end of EH II.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>",
"Early_Helladic_I": "Early Helladic I is the opening phase of the mainland Greek Bronze Age, known as the Eutresis culture after the Boeotian site at which it is best defined stratigraphically. The assemblage was first recognised by Carl Blegen at Korakou in 1915–16, and its type shape is a red-slipped and burnished hemispherical bowl found from the Peloponnese to Thessaly.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> For the first phase of a Bronze Age it is very poor in metal, and its regional variants may prove numerous and distinctive: one, the Talioti group of the central Argolid, has only recently been defined<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>; its calendar years, unlike that relative label, are not yet securely fixed.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>",
"Early_Helladic_II": "Early Helladic II is the middle and by far the richest phase of the mainland Greek Bronze Age, called the Korakou culture and sometimes split into an earlier IIA and a later IIB. Its diagnostic vessel is the sauceboat, a deep cup with an unusually long high-swung spout, made in the dark-coated Early Helladic Urfirnis and in the light-slipped Yellow Mottled ware.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Several coastal settlements are fortified, and the phase produces the Corridor House, a free-standing two-storeyed building with a line of halls flanked by corridors that double as stairwells; the House of the Tiles at Lerna is the type example.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>",
"Early_Helladic_III": "Early Helladic III is the last phase of the mainland Greek Early Bronze Age, known as the Tiryns culture after the Argolid site where it is well stratified. In the Argolid it lies directly over Korakou culture settlements destroyed by fire, and at Lerna a tumulus was raised over the burnt ruins of the House of the Tiles and left unbuilt on for some time.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Its pottery is pattern-painted in two classes, dark-on-light Patterned ware in the Peloponnese and light-on-dark Ayia Marina ware in central Greece, and its houses are apsidal or rectangular megara of mudbrick on a rubble socle.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>",
"Middle_Helladic": "Middle Helladic is the middle of the three divisions of the mainland Greek Bronze Age, and may be as much as five centuries long. No single scheme for subdividing it has been agreed; one common threefold division runs MH I to about 1900 BCE, MH II to about 1700 and MH III to the period’s end, and a redating of the Thera eruption would shorten all three.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Its hallmark is Minyan ware, a monochrome burnished pottery Heinrich Schliemann named after the mythical king Minyas of Orchomenos; the typical house is a free-standing megaron, and the dead are commonly buried within the settlement.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>",
"Late_Helladic": "Late Helladic is the last of the three divisions of the mainland Greek Bronze Age, and the phase whose culture is usually called Mycenaean. It is cut into LH I, IIA and IIB, IIIA1 and IIIA2, IIIB and IIIC, the last being subdivided again into at least three and sometimes as many as five sub-phases.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> It opens with a fine ware painted in lustrous dark-on-light patterns borrowed from Crete, sees that dependence end and reverse by LH IIB, and closes long after the palaces burn, its final stage in Attica and the Argolid being called Submycenaean.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>",
"Late_Helladic_I": "Late Helladic I is the opening phase of the mainland Greek Late Bronze Age. What most clearly separates its pottery from that of the late Middle Helladic period is a fine ware painted with lustrous patterns in a dark-on-light style, whose favourite shapes — the Vapheio cup, the semiglobular cup, the alabastron and the piriform jar — are largely derived from Late Minoan IA.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The pottery varies from region to region, the Minoan element being strongest in the south, and whether the first Mycenaean style arose in the Argolid or in Laconia is still argued<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>; Mycenae’s shaft graves span this phase and the late Middle Helladic before it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>",
"Late_Helladic_II": "Late Helladic II is the second phase of the mainland Greek Late Bronze Age, cut into an earlier IIA and a later IIB. In IIA lustrous painted fine ware becomes much commoner and the style grows uniform across the Peloponnese, though it still leans on Late Minoan IB motifs while central Greece stays more Helladic than Minoanising<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>; the earliest tholos tombs at Mycenae go up in IIA.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> IIB is marked by the Ephyraean style on goblets, jugs and ewers, and by the end of the mainland’s dependence on Crete: Knossian potters now imitate the Ephyraean goblet, so influence has begun to run the other way.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>",
"Late_Helladic_III": "Late Helladic III is the last and longest phase of the mainland Greek Late Bronze Age, divided into IIIA, IIIB and IIIC. It covers the whole life of the Mycenaean palaces and the widest export of Mycenaean pottery: the early goblet lengthens into the kylix, the stirrup jar is made in quantity, and the deep bowl becomes the commonest decorated shape.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The palaces of Mycenae and Tiryns and the citadel at Midea burn at the end of IIIB, after which pottery grows markedly regional and, by the close of IIIC, poor in both technique and range.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>",
"Late_Helladic_IIIA": "Late Helladic IIIA is the first phase of Late Helladic III, divided into IIIA1 and IIIA2. In IIIA1 the early Mycenaean goblet lengthens its stem and shallows its bowl on the way to becoming the kylix, the Vapheio cup is transformed into the mug and grows rare, and the stirrup jar first appears in appreciable numbers.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> In IIIA2 the kylix dominates settlement deposits, two stylised motifs — the whorl-shell and the Late Helladic III flower — take over painted decoration, and the large deposits of Aegean pottery from Tell el-Amarna in Egypt illustrate the shape range<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>; the first fortifications at Mycenae and Tiryns go up late in IIIA.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>",
"Late_Helladic_IIIB": "Late Helladic IIIB is the middle phase of Late Helladic III and the height of the Mycenaean palaces. Its pottery is told from that of IIIA by the deep bowl in both painted and unpainted wares and by the unpainted conical kylix; in the Argolid it is cut again into IIIB1, with an equal mix of painted deep bowls and kylikes, and IIIB2, with no pattern-decorated kylikes at all.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Mycenae, Tiryns and the citadel at Midea are destroyed at the end of IIIB2, and Pylos and the Menelaion near Sparta late in IIIB.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>",
"Late_Helladic_IIIC": "Late Helladic IIIC is the last phase of the mainland Greek Bronze Age, following the destruction of the palaces. Its pottery becomes strongly regional, reflecting the collapse of a centralised political system and of the communications that went with it, and the many site-wide destructions allow it to be cut into an early, a developed and a late phase.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The developed phase brings a brief renaissance of regional styles — Close, Octopus, Pictorial and Fringed — and warrior scenes such as the Warrior Vase from Mycenae; the impoverished final phase is called Submycenaean in Attica and the Argolid.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>",
"Lefkandi_I": "Lefkandi I is an Early Bronze Age cultural assemblage of central Greece and the neighbouring islands, named from the basal level of the Euboean site at which it was recognised in the mid-1960s. It is defined by red- and black-burnished shapes derived from western Anatolian prototypes, among them the two-handled depas amphikypellon, and is the mainland counterpart of the Cycladic Kastri Group; the fast potter’s wheel arrives with it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Where it belongs is disputed: Colin Renfrew placed it in Early Helladic III beside the Tiryns culture, while Jeremy Rutter argued in 1979 for late Early Helladic II, the position now labelled EH IIB.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>",
"Troy_I": "Troy I is the lowest and earliest of the nine numbered settlements stacked on the mound of Hisarlık in north-western Anatolia, some four metres (13 feet) of deposit divided into ten building phases. What marks it off from the levels above is that the place is a fortress from the very start, ringed by a battered stone wall later pushed outwards and given projecting towers, and that its pottery is entirely handmade, dark and burnished and cut with straight-line patterns filled with white paste.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Its houses are freestanding long rooms, its tools mostly of stone, and the level ends in fire.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>",
"Troy_II": "Troy II is the second settlement on the mound of Hisarlık, a citadel of eight building phases whose walls and gates were twice pushed outwards and whose houses were twice levelled and rebuilt on a grander scale. What sets it apart from its neighbours is display and wealth: parallel great halls stood in a walled inner compound entered by a paved ramp, the potter's wheel arrived, and at least half of its copper-based metalwork is already high-tin bronze.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> It burned down entirely, and the hoards of gold and silver that Schliemann called Priam's treasure belong to that destruction.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>",
"Troy_III": "Troy III is the third settlement on the mound of Hisarlık, three or four building phases raised over the burnt wreck of the citadel below and pulled down again at their end for no reason the excavators could find. It is set apart from the levels around it by its masonry, since its builders worked almost wholly in stone where those above and below used mudbrick on a stone footing, and by the poverty of its cramped little houses, one of which was long shown to visitors as the palace of Priam.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Its pottery is barely distinguishable from Troy II's, and deer become the commonest animal eaten.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>",
"Troy_IV": "Troy IV is the fourth settlement on the mound of Hisarlık, five building phases set out on a fresh orientation after the level beneath it was demolished. What distinguishes it is a return to mudbrick walls on stone footings and a new kind of town plan, a row of about four two-roomed dwellings fronting one street, together with the arrival of the domed oven and of straw tempering in the potter's clay.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Most vessels are by now wheelmade and new shapes appear, but the buildings themselves are poor, the huts of farmers or shepherds who made no use of what had stood before.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>",
"Troy_V": "Troy V is the fifth settlement on the mound of Hisarlık, three or four building phases and the last of the Early Bronze Age levels there. It differs from those below it in comfort rather than in kind: the walls are more neatly built, the rooms more regularly laid out and larger, fixed hearths, ovens and benches are commoner, and the floors were swept clean enough to leave the excavators little to find.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Analysis shows that tin bronze is by now the ordinary metal, and, unusually for the mound, the level came to an end with no destruction of any kind.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>",
"Troy_VI": "Troy VI is the sixth settlement on the mound of Hisarlık and the most imposing of the nine, eight building phases under which the earlier levels lie buried. It stands apart from everything below it in both walls and pottery: a limestone circuit over four metres (13 feet) thick, faced in fitted blocks and broken by shallow vertical offsets, rings terraces climbing to a summit later cut away, while 90 of its 98 vessel shapes are new.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Its grey wheelmade ware resembles the Minyan pottery of Greece but belongs to a local Anatolian tradition, and the level was brought down by what most excavators read as an earthquake.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>",
"Troy_VII": "Troy VII is the settlement raised in the wreckage of Troy VI at Hisarlık, keeping to the same terrace inside the patched circuit wall of the level it replaced. What sets it apart is that its people gave up fortifying: the practice of building fresh defences, kept up on the mound since early in the 3rd millennium, came to a halt, and small chambers sharing party walls were packed against the inner face of the old wall with storage jars sunk into their floors.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Excavators divide it differently, Dörpfeld reading two periods within a single stratum and Blegen three sub-phases, VIIa, VIIb1 and VIIb2.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>",
"Troy_VIIb1": "Troy VIIb1 is the phase at Hisarlık that followed the burning of Troy VIIa, its houses refounded on the same walls and to much the same plans. It is marked off from the phase before it by a handmade Coarse Ware with no local ancestry, argued to have come from the middle Danube, and by a bronze seal cut with Hittite hieroglyphs, the first securely identified writing from prehistoric Troy.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> It ended with no destruction at all, an unsolved mystery to its excavator, and Dörpfeld had already read the break at which it began as the sharpest contrast in the whole history of the mound.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>",
"Troy_VIIb2": "Troy VIIb2 is the last prehistoric phase at Hisarlık, its dwellings enlarged by extensions and by doorways cut through the party walls of the phase before. It is told apart by a handmade Knobbed Ware, dark-surfaced and ornamented with knobs and grooves, whose parallels lie across the Hellespont in Thrace, and by bronzes whose closest matches are Hungarian, though most of its pottery is still of the older Trojan wares.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Its houses rest on a distinctive course of upright slabs, which Dörpfeld read as the mark of Greek settlers on the mound by about 800 BCE, where the later excavation makes the phase Trojan with newcomers from the north.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>",
"Troy_VIII": "Troy VIII is the Greek settlement on the mound of Hisarlık, the eighth of the nine numbered levels and the first there to be a Greek town rather than an Anatolian one. It is cut off from the prehistoric levels below by a long interval in which the hill lay empty and its Bronze Age circuit collapsed, and it is recognised by its pottery, which opens with Geometric ware and thins markedly through the Greek styles that follow.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Ancient writers disagreed about when it began, some putting a Greek presence directly after the fall of Troy and others only under Lydian rule.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>",
"Troy_IX": "Troy IX is the uppermost of the nine numbered levels at Hisarlık, the acropolis of the Roman city of Ilium. It differs from every level beneath it in being monumental rather than domestic: the middle of the mound was cut down to make a level platform, destroying the summit of the Bronze Age citadel, and on it stood the sanctuary of Ilian Athena with its temple, great altar, colonnades and gateway, ringed by massive retaining walls.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The temple is thought to have been raised under Lysimachus about 300 BCE, wrecked when Fimbria sacked the city in 85 BCE, and rebuilt under Augustus.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>",
"Berlin": "Berlin is the capital of Germany and one of the sixteen states of the federation in its own right. The country's constitution names it outright, providing that Berlin is the capital of the Federal Republic, that the federation is responsible for representing the nation as a whole there, and that the details are to be settled by federal law.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> At the end of 2025 the city held 3,913,644 inhabitants, 16,499 more than a year earlier, of whom 976,419 were foreign nationals, spread across the twelve boroughs into which it is divided for administration.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>",
"Carl_Schuchhardt": "Carl Schuchhardt was a German archaeologist whose work joined classical excavation to the study of prehistoric Europe. Born at Hanover in 1859, he travelled the Mediterranean on a stipend from the German Archaeological Institute in 1886 and 1887, meeting Humann, Conze, Dörpfeld and Schliemann; he became the first director of the Kestner-Museum in Hanover in 1888 and took charge of the prehistoric department of Berlin's ethnological museum in 1908.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> His best-known book gathered Schliemann's excavations at Troy, Tiryns, Mycenae, Orchomenos and Ithaca into a single narrative, and reached English readers in 1891.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>",
"Harriet_Boyd_Hawes": "Harriet Boyd Hawes was an American archaeologist who directed one of the first excavations of a Minoan town. Born at Boston in 1871, she worked as a teacher and as a war nurse as well as an excavator, and was among the leading figures of the earliest digging seasons on Crete, where in 1901 she found the settlement of Gournia; her scientific contribution has been reassessed by a run of studies since the 1990s.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> With her husband Charles Henry Hawes she wrote <i>Crete, the Forerunner of Greece</i>, a short account of the island's prehistory published in 1911 for readers outside the subject.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>",
"Jeremy_Rutter": "Jeremy Rutter is an archaeologist of the prehistoric Aegean, known above all for his work on Bronze Age pottery. He is Professor Emeritus of Classical Studies at Dartmouth College and Sherman Fairchild Professor Emeritus in the Humanities, took his first degree at Haverford and his doctorate at the University of Pennsylvania, and gives his expertise as the Stone and Bronze Age Aegean, Minoan art and ancient Mediterranean ceramics.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> He also wrote <i>Aegean Prehistoric Archaeology</i>, a course of 29 lesson narratives published openly online by Dartmouth, which works through the period lesson by lesson.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>",
"John_Papadopoulos": "John Papadopoulos is an archaeologist of the Greek world whose fieldwork has ranged from northern Greece into Albania. He took his doctorate at the University of Sydney in 1988, joined the excavation of Torone in 1979 and was its field director from 1986 to 1995, and later co-directed the digging of the burial tumulus at Lofkënd; he gives his interests as Athenian topography, Greek colonization and the Aegean Bronze Age.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Writing on that topography, he argued that the original potters' quarter of Athens lay where the Classical Agora was later laid out.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>",
"Ludwig_Borchardt": "Ludwig Borchardt was a German Egyptologist and architect who put the study of ancient Egyptian building on a systematic footing. Born at Berlin in 1863, he trained as an architect and read Egyptology under Adolf Erman, worked from 1897 on the general catalogue of the Cairo Museum, and directed the German institute for Egyptian antiquities in Cairo from 1907 to 1928.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> He excavated the sun temple of Neuserre and, for the German Oriental Society, the mortuary temples at Abusir and the city at Tell el-Amarna, publishing the Amarna seasons himself.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>",
"Monograph": "A monograph is a long-form scholarly book devoted to a single topic or theme rather than to a collection of separate studies. One funder's working definition puts it in those terms: a long-form publication communicating an original contribution to academic scholarship on one topic or theme, designed for a primarily academic audience, and written by one author or by several.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Monographs are issued mainly by university presses, some of which now publish them openly, as UCL Press did on its relaunch in May 2015 as what it called the first fully open-access university press in the United Kingdom.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>",
"National_Archaeological_Museum,_Athens": "The National Archaeological Museum in Athens holds the largest collection of ancient Greek art in Greece, gathered from excavations across the whole country. The museum describes itself as the largest archaeological museum in Greece and one of the most important in the world devoted to that art, and stands on Patission Street north of the city centre.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Founded in 1829 as the first museum of the Greek state, first at Aegina and from 1834 at Athens, it moved in 1866 onto donated land, opened its present building in 1889 and was extended eastwards between 1932 and 1939.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>",
"Neues_Museum": "The Neues Museum is a museum building on the island in the Spree at Berlin, and its opening marked a turn in nineteenth-century museum design and technology. Designed by Friedrich August Stüler and built between 1843 and 1855, it was badly damaged in the Second World War and left an abandoned ruin until restoration by the office of David Chipperfield began in 2003, the scars of the bombing deliberately kept rather than patched over; it reopened in 2009.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> It shows material from three state collections, among them the Egyptian Museum and Papyrus Collection.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>",
"Sturt_Manning": "Sturt Manning is an archaeologist who applies scientific dating methods to the chronology of the ancient eastern Mediterranean. He is Distinguished Professor of Arts and Sciences in Classics at Cornell University, where he directs the Cornell Tree-Ring Laboratory, and gives his interests as Aegean, Cypriot and Anatolian archaeology, complex societies, radiocarbon and dendrochronology.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> His work has proposed absolute dates that older schemes placed elsewhere, among them a Second Intermediate Period date for the eruption of Thera<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> and a severe drought lasting several years around 1198–1196 BCE, read from tree rings and matched to the collapse of the Hittite state.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>",
"Thalassocracy": "A thalassocracy is a state whose power rests on command of the sea rather than on the ground it holds. Ancient writers reach for the idea sparingly: Herodotus says that Polycrates of Samos was the first Greek known to him to aim at mastery of the sea, setting aside Minos of Cnossus and whoever may have ruled it before him.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The Greek word is rare, and a study of where it does occur argues that the concept was worked out at Athens during its own empire rather than in Ionia, classical prose preferring other ways of naming the thing.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>",
"University_of_the_Witwatersrand": "The University of the Witwatersrand, usually shortened to Wits, is a public research university in Johannesburg, South Africa. It grew out of the South African School of Mines, established at Kimberley in 1896 and moved to Johannesburg in 1904, which was renamed several times before taking full university status under its present name in 1922; building then began at Milner Park on a site given by the municipality.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> It now spans seven campuses in the suburbs of Parktown and Braamfontein, and its holdings reach beyond them to a rural campus, a private hospital and the Sterkfontein Caves.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>",
"Cape_Sounion": "Cape Sounion is the southern tip of Attica, a headland facing the Cyclades and the Aegean and crowned by a marble temple visible far out to sea. Pausanias made it his first landfall coming to Athens, describing a harbour below and a temple on the peak that he assigned to Athena, with the silver mines of Laurion a little further on.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The promontory also carried a walled fortress, whose masonry has never been examined systematically, so that the dates proposed for it rest on general photographs rather than on study of the walls.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>",
"Corinthia": "Corinthia was the territory of the city of Corinth, covering the isthmus that joins the Peloponnese to the Greek mainland together with the hills and coasts on either side of it. It held a harbour on each sea, Lechaeum on the Corinthian Gulf and Cenchreae on the Saronic, with a haulway across the narrow neck between them and the sanctuary of Isthmian Poseidon in its pine grove.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Command of these approaches made the city rich on goods moving between Italy and Asia and on the duties levied at the crossing.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>",
"Dipylon_Gate": "The Dipylon Gate was the main gate of ancient Athens, a double gateway in the north-west stretch of the city wall through which the road from the Academy entered the city. A traveller passing through it in the 2nd century CE found porticoes running inward towards the potters' quarter, fronted by bronze statues of men and women held worthy of fame.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Potters' workshops of the 6th and 5th centuries BCE clustered in its immediate vicinity and spread north-west,<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> and the courtyard building where processions were marshalled stood just inside it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>",
"Kalapodi": "Kalapodi is a sanctuary site in Phocis, central Greece, standing about a kilometre from the modern village at a crossing of routes between the Euboean gulf, Opountian Locris and Boeotia. Excavated by the German Archaeological Institute from 1973, it was first published as the sanctuary of Artemis and Apollo of Hyampolis and has since been argued to be instead the oracular sanctuary of Apollo at Abai.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Herodotus records that the Persians plundered and burned a richly endowed temple of Apollo at Abai, where there was also a place of divination.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>",
"Krisa": "Krisa was a settlement in Phocis on a spur below Delphi, commanding the plain that runs down to the gulf named after it. Pausanias notes that Homer calls the place by this older name where later writers say Cirrha, and that the plain below it was left bare of trees, whether from a curse or from poor ground.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> French excavations on its acropolis in 1936 uncovered a substantial prehistoric settlement but no trace of the Greek city tradition placed on the same spot, Byzantine houses and graves resting straight on the earlier remains.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>",
"Mantineia": "Mantineia was a city of eastern Arcadia, in the central Peloponnese, lying in a high enclosed plain ringed by mountains whose streams drained away underground rather than to the sea. An inscribed stone of the early 4th century BCE found on the site records the neighbouring community of Helisson entering into union with Mantineia, its people becoming Mantineians equal and alike and bringing their land and town with them.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The Mantineians fought alongside Athens during the Peloponnesian War, later broke with Sparta for the Achaean League, and renamed their city Antigoneia in honour of Antigonus of Macedonia.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>",
"Mount_Hymettus": "Mount Hymettus is a long ridge of Attica running south-east of Athens, known in antiquity for the honey gathered on it and quarried for a grey-blue marble. Pausanias says it grows the pasture most suitable for bees, ranking its produce below only that of the Alazones.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Strabo agrees that it yields the best honey and that the marble quarries near the city are excellent, but singles out the honey of the silver-mining district of Attica as finer still, called unsmoked because it was taken from the hive without the use of smoke.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>",
"Mount_Pentelicus": "Mount Pentelicus is a mountain of Attica rising north-east of Athens, quarried since antiquity for the white marble that carries its name and that faced the classical buildings of the city. Pausanias lists it first among the Attic mountains and notes the quarries there, while Strabo counted the Pentelic and Hymettian workings the most excellent near the city.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Greek builders chose their marbles by use rather than treating them as interchangeable, reserving Pentelic and the stone of Carystos in Euboea for architecture where the marble of Paros was preferred for sculpture.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>",
"Orchomenos": "Orchomenos was a city of Boeotia in central Greece, on the northern edge of the Copais basin, and one of the leading centres of Mycenaean Greece. Pausanias called the domed tomb there known as the treasury of Minyas a wonder second to none in Greece or anywhere else, describing its round stone chamber rising to a blunt apex closed by a keystone.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Rescue excavations near it have found Middle Helladic walls with Minyan and matt-painted pottery, a later Bronze Age level with figurines and painted plaster, and a Hellenistic peristyle house with a mosaic floor.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>",
"Pelargikon": "The Pelargikon was an ancient fortification on the west approach to the Athenian Acropolis, built of large unworked blocks and credited by later Greeks to a people before their own. Writers used the forms Pelasgikon and Pelargikon interchangeably, and the pun on the word for stork is attested only from the later 5th century BCE.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Thucydides records that the ground beneath the citadel called Pelasgian was under a curse and an oracle forbidding its occupation, though refugees crowding into Athens at the start of the Peloponnesian War settled it regardless, the city having proved far too small to hold them all.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>",
"Pompeion": "The Pompeion was a large courtyard building in Athens, standing inside the city wall between the Sacred Gate and the Dipylon Gate and named for the processions it served. Its chief use was the marshalling of the Panathenaic procession, and a traveller entering the city by that gate described a building where processions were prepared, some yearly and others at longer intervals.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Work began with the wall of 480 BCE over an older cemetery, halted, and finished only between 403 and 393 BCE, giving a peristyle court of unfluted stuccoed columns and a marble porch.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>",
"Salamis": "Salamis is an island in the Saronic Gulf lying close against the coast of Attica, about seventy stadia long by ancient reckoning and holding a town of the same name. Strabo records earlier names for it and says its fame rests on the Aiacidae who ruled there, on Ajax son of Telamon, and on the sea fight in which the Greeks defeated Xerxes nearby.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Herodotus has the Greek fleet withdraw to it from Artemisium in 480 BCE at Athenian request, so that the Athenians could take their women and children out of Attica and take counsel about what to do next.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>",
"Dromos": "A dromos is an entrance passage leading into a tomb, most familiar from the long approach corridors that run into the hillside in front of the chamber tombs and tholos tombs of the Aegean Bronze Age. The Greek word means a course or a run, and ancient writers applied it to a racecourse, to a colonnaded public walk and, in Egypt, to the avenue of sphinxes set before a temple entrance.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> In tomb architecture the passage narrows abruptly at its inner end into a doorway called the stomion, beyond which lies the burial chamber; its side walls may be bare rock or lined with masonry.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>",
"Corbel": "A corbel is a projecting stone or piece of timber set into a wall so that it carries a weight above it, such as a beam, a parapet or a cornice. The word derives from the Latin <i>corbis</i>, a basket, and a row of such projections supporting a parapet is called a corbel-table.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Corbelling is the related roofing technique in which each course of masonry oversails the one below until the two sides meet overhead, dispensing with a true arch; every Mycenaean tholos tomb, including examples up to fourteen metres across, was roofed in stone this way.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>",
"Vestibule": "A vestibule is a lobby or passage lying between the entrance of a building and its interior, giving shelter to those about to go in and, in cold climates, keeping draughts out of the rooms beyond.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The Roman <i>vestibulum</i> from which the word descends was the opposite arrangement, a recess or sheltered forecourt outside the outer doors rather than within them, sometimes columned and large enough to hold a crowd of callers.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Archaeologists also use the term for the short antechamber that stands between the entrance passage and the main room of a tomb or shrine.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>",
"Stele": "A stele is an upright monument of one or two stones, usually a slender pillar or a tall narrow slab, set up to mark a place or to carry an inscription or a carving in relief. In Greek the word first meant a block used as a prop, and it appears in Homer for the stone standing over a grave; the largest known examples were raised over individual burials, while others recorded boundaries, laws and treaties between states.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The sculpted stelae above the shaft graves at Mycenae are the earliest large-scale relief carving known from the Greek mainland.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>",
"Diadem": "A diadem is a band or fillet worn round the head as a mark of rank, and above all the plain white ribbon that served as the badge of kingship across the Greek and Hellenistic world. Greek writers first use the word for the band tied about the tiara of the Persian king; Alexander adopted it and his successors kept it, knotted at the back so that its two ends hung down behind.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Because it stood so plainly for monarchy the Romans detested it, and Julius Caesar publicly refused the laurel crown wreathed with a diadem that Mark Antony offered him.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>",
"Scabbard": "A scabbard is the sheath in which a sword or dagger is carried, protecting the blade and its owner and allowing the weapon to be worn at the belt or on a shoulder strap. Ancient examples were made either of metal or of leather with metal mountings; the Greek word is <i>koleos</i>, which in Homer takes the form <i>kouleon</i>, and the Latin is <i>vagina</i>.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The Greeks hung the sword on the left side from a belt passing over the right shoulder and drew it across the body, so that the Persian akinakes, worn on the right, struck them as odd.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>",
"Citadel": "A citadel is the strongest part of a fortified place: in a walled city, the section of the defences that can be held on its own even after the rest of the town has been taken. Fortification writers use the word of any such inner stronghold capable of separate defence,<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> and in the ancient Mediterranean the part was usually played by a height inside the walls, the Greek <i>akropolis</i> or Latin <i>arx</i>, which early on was not walled off from the city below.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> One city might contain several such heights, so the term settled on whichever was the principal one.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>",
"Tomb_of_the_Lions": "The Tomb of the Lions is one of the nine tholos tombs at Mycenae in southern Greece, a circular stone chamber roofed by a corbelled vault and entered along an open passage. It takes its name from finds made inside it, faces north near the entrance to the citadel, and belongs to the middle of the three groups into which Alan Wace sorted the Mycenae tholoi by their masonry.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Alone among them it closed with a wooden door hung on pivots cut into the outer lintel rather than a rubble wall, and like all of them it was found robbed, antiquity remembering the group only as the treasuries of Atreus.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>",
"Tomb_of_Aegisthus": "The Tomb of Aegisthus is one of the nine tholos tombs at Mycenae in southern Greece, a corbel-vaulted circular chamber set into a hillside and reached along an open entrance passage. It belongs to the earliest of Alan Wace's three groups of Mycenae tholoi, but stands transitional towards the second, since a facing of cut conglomerate and soft limestone was added to its doorway later and bears none of the weight above it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Its name is a modern convention taken from the ruling house of Mycenaean legend, whose Aegisthus and Clytemnestra were said to lie a little way outside the citadel wall, thought unworthy of a place within it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>",
"Tomb_of_Clytemnestra": "The Tomb of Clytemnestra is one of the nine tholos tombs at Mycenae in southern Greece, a corbel-vaulted circular chamber about 13 metres (44 feet) across, entered along an open passage. With the Treasury of Atreus it belongs to the last and most elaborate of the three groups into which Alan Wace sorted the Mycenae tholoi: its blocks are largely sawn rather than hammer-dressed, and its doorway carried a facade of gypsum, bluish limestone and greenish-grey marble.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Its name is a modern convention drawn from the tradition that Clytemnestra lay outside the citadel wall; long after the tomb went out of use a theatre was built across its entrance passage.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>",
"Grave_Gamma": "Grave Gamma is a shaft grave in Grave Circle B at Mycenae in southern Greece, the older of that site's two shaft-grave cemeteries, which lies outside the later fortification wall. Its third burial wore a face-mask of electrum, the natural alloy of gold and silver, rather than the gold used for the masks of the neighbouring Grave Circle A, and the piece is plainer and cruder than those.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The mask lay behind the dead man's head rather than over his face, and the burial is the earliest of the masked ones at Mycenae, made near the start of a cemetery in use from about 1650 to 1550 BCE.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>",
"Shaft_Grave_V": "Shaft Grave V is one of the six shaft graves of Grave Circle A at Mycenae in southern Greece, a rectangular rock-cut pit that held several male burials of the earliest Mycenaean elite. Heinrich Schliemann opened it in 1876 as the first of the five graves he found, and it yielded two of the circle's five gold funerary masks, among them the piece long called the Mask of Agamemnon.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> That mask lay on the last burial, at the southern end of the shaft; the grave held some 2,300 grams (5 pounds) of gold, against seven kilograms (15 pounds) in the neighbouring Shaft Grave IV.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>",
"Plinth": "A plinth is the low square block set beneath the base mouldings of a column, pilaster or pedestal, and by extension any plain continuous surface carrying a moulded member above it. In joinery the word is used of the flat plain member at the foot of an architrave or a dado, while the square abacus of a Doric capital has sometimes been called a plinth in error.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Plinths appear early in Greek building: the wooden columns of the palace at Tiryns stood on stone plinths pinned with wood, and Ionic column bases were commonly set on square ones.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>",
"Fresco": "A fresco is a wall painting made on fresh wet plaster, so that the colour is taken up by the plaster as it dries rather than resting on a surface already hard. Lime burnt in a kiln becomes porous and takes up whatever touches it; as the plaster turns back into carbonate of lime a thin crust forms over the painting and encases the particles of colour, which is why all sorts of wall painting are loosely called frescoes.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Vitruvius noticed the same permanence, writing that colours laid on stucco while it is still wet do not fade.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>",
"Stucco": "Stucco is a plaster covering laid wet on a wall or a ceiling and left to dry hard, used both to smooth a rough surface and to carry moulded or painted decoration. Vitruvius prescribes a rough rendering coat, then not fewer than three coats of sand mortar, then layers mixed with powdered marble, and notes that Greek workers beat the material with wooden beetles before laying it on.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The word is generally applied to work out of doors, where its practical value is great because a hard coat is very nearly impervious to water.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>",
"Amphora": "An amphora is a large jar with two handles and a narrow neck, made chiefly to hold and carry wine and oil, and the commonest storage vessel of the ancient Mediterranean. Most were earthenware, though Homer mentions amphorae of gold and of stone and glass examples have been found at Pompeii; the largest stand about 1.5 metres (5 feet) high, and the maker's name or the place of manufacture was sometimes stamped on the clay.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The word also names a measure of capacity, the Roman amphora being two thirds of the Greek, and a ship's size was reckoned in amphorae.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>",
"Shipshed": "A shipshed is a roofed slipway in which an ancient warship was hauled out of the water and kept dry when not at sea, sparing its light hull from waterlogging and shipworm. The best known are those in the Piraeus harbours of Zea and Mounichia, where parallel stone ramps sloped down below sea level and rows of columns, alternately tall and short, carried the ridges and valleys of a continuous roof.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The dockyards were called <i>neoria</i> and the covered sheds themselves <i>neosoikoi</i>; those at Piraeus were destroyed after the Peloponnesian War and restored under Lycurgus.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>",
"Kouloura": "A kouloura is one of the large circular walled pits sunk in the paved west courts of the Minoan palaces, the name being the word Cretan workmen used for them at Knossos. Three stand in a line there, the largest just over six metres (20 feet) across with walling some three metres (10 feet) deep, and another was found in the west court at Phaistos.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> They were sunk in the Middle Minoan period to take broken pottery from the rubbish heaps beside the court, and the complete absence of stratification in their contents shows it was tipped in in quantity at a time.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>",
"Andokides_Painter": "The <b>Andokides Painter</b> is the name given to an anonymous Athenian vase-painter of the later 6th century BCE, borrowed from the potter Andokides, who employed him and whose <i>epoiesen</i> signature, 'made it', is cut into the foot of the vases.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Fewer than twenty vases survive from his hand, mostly amphorae with a few cups, and he is generally held the first to work regularly in red-figure after its invention, and possibly its inventor.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> He is best known for bilingual vases, which carry one design twice, in red-figure and in black-figure; the black-figure faces are attributed to the Lysippides Painter, though the two have been proposed as one artist.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>",
"Kouros": "A <b>kouros</b> is a free-standing statue of a nude young man made in the archaic Greek world, carved in the round and standing frontally with the left foot advanced, the arms held down at the sides and the hands clenched.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The pose is close to invariable across the type, and nearly every archaic male statue in Greece follows it, so a figure is identified as one by that scheme rather than by any attribute.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Such statues were set over graves and dedicated in sanctuaries, were commonly cut from island marble and painted, and range from well under life-size to over three metres (ten feet) tall.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>",
"Kore": "A <b>kore</b> is a free-standing statue of a standing young woman made in the archaic Greek world, carved in the round and always clothed, the draped counterpart of the nude kouros.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The pose varies little across the type: the figure stands erect and looks straight ahead, one hand drawing the skirt tight across the legs while the other is held forward at the elbow to offer something.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Such statues stood over graves and were dedicated in sanctuaries, above all on the Athenian Acropolis, and their surfaces were painted and fitted with metal ornament.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>",
"Archaic_smile": "The <b>archaic smile</b> is the mouth turned up at both corners that Greek sculptors gave to almost every figure they carved between roughly 600 and 480 BCE, on gods, men and grave monuments alike.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> It appears on figures doing anything at all, including warriors fighting and dying, so it is generally read as a convention of the type rather than as an expression of feeling.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Its simplest form is a single curve, while Attic carvers divided the mouth into three curves and blended the ends of the lips into the cheek, and the convention was abandoned in the sculpture of the early 5th century.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>",
"Peplos_Kore": "The Peplos Kore is a Parian marble statue of a standing young woman, found on the Athenian Acropolis in 1886 and standing 1.18 metres (3 feet 10 inches) without its plinth.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> It is nearly complete, lacking only the separately carved left forearm and part of the skirt and feet, and it takes its modern name from the heavy garment worn over the chiton, which is divided along one arm and unbroken along the other.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Much of its paint was still visible at discovery and was recorded in a watercolour, and later readings of that colouring have suggested the garment is a robe of a kind worn by gods rather than by women.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>",
"Anavysos_Kouros": "The Anavysos Kouros is a Parian marble statue of a naked young man, 1.94 metres (6 feet 4 inches) high, named after the district of Attica it came from and now in the National Archaeological Museum in Athens.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> It keeps the frontal stance of the type, with the left foot advanced and the fists closed, but the chest, thighs, calves and buttocks are fuller and rounder than in earlier examples, and traces of red paint survive on the hair and the fillet binding it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> An inscribed base asking a passer-by to pity Kroisos, killed among the front ranks, is usually read with it, though whether the two belong together has been questioned.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>",
"Moschophoros": "The Moschophoros, or calf-bearer, is an archaic Greek marble statue of a bearded man carrying a calf across his shoulders, made in Attica about 570 BCE and dedicated on the Athenian Acropolis.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Cut from Hymettian marble and standing 1.65 metres (5 feet 5 inches) high without its base, it holds the frontal stance of a kouros, with the left leg advanced, while a thin himation, a beard and the animal itself distinguish the man from that type.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> An inscription on the poros base names Rhombos, son of Palos, as the dedicator, and the figure is generally read as Rhombos bringing his offering rather than as a god.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>",
"Archaic_temple": "An archaic temple is a Greek temple of the archaic period, roughly the 8th century BCE to 480 BCE. The oldest known example, at Dreros on Crete, is a single walled room of small dressed stones dated to the second quarter of the 8th century, with a stone-lined hearth in the floor, one or two axial columns and a bench in a corner for offerings.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> By about 700 BCE the temple of Poseidon at Isthmia was surrounded by seven by nineteen wooden columns<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>, and late in the 7th century the temple at Thermos carried terracotta roof tiles, moulded antefixes and painted metope plaques.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>",
"Doric_order": "The <b>Doric order</b> is one of the classical Greek systems of column and entablature, marked by a shaft that rises from the platform with no base, carries twenty shallow flutes meeting in sharp edges, and ends in a convex echinus under a square abacus.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Above the columns runs a plain architrave closed by a fillet with strips and rows of peg-like guttae, then a frieze alternating channelled triglyphs with the roughly square panels called metopes.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Ancient writers derived these from carpentry, the triglyphs standing for boards over sawn beam ends, and noted that a triglyph cannot both centre on every column and close the frieze at a corner.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>",
"Ionic_order": "The <b>Ionic order</b> is one of the classical Greek systems of column and entablature, marked by a shaft that rises from a moulded base, carries about 24 flutes parted by flat fillets, and ends in a pair of spiral volutes.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Above the columns the architrave is stepped back into three overlapping bands called fasciae, while the frieze runs unbroken, taking the name zophoros where it carries figure sculpture.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Ancient writers made it the slender order, allowing its column nine diameters of height, and its entablature varies by region, since builders in Asia Minor could set dentils straight on the architrave with no frieze at all.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>",
"Peripteros": "A <b>peripteros</b> is a Greek temple whose walls are surrounded on all four sides by a single row of columns, one of the seven forms Vitruvius sets out and defined by him as six columns front and rear with eleven along each flank, standing clear of the walls.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The ring leaves a walk, the pteroma, round an inner building with porches of its own; doubling it gives a dipteros, and engaged columns imitating it a pseudoperipteros.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The same ring was laid round cellas of markedly different shapes, which has been read as evidence that it was applied as a mark of distinction rather than reached by degrees from simpler porches.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>",
"Temple_of_Hera,_Olympia": "The <b>Temple of Hera at Olympia</b>, or Heraion, is a Doric peripteral temple of about 600 BCE in the sanctuary of Zeus, six columns by sixteen, and the earliest monumental temple in Greece.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Its walls were sun-dried brick and its columns and entablature wood; the oaken columns were exchanged for stone one at a time as the timbers rotted, so its capitals vary in profile with the date of each replacement.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Pausanias saw one oak column still standing in its rear chamber, records that the Sixteen Women held the Heraea there, and lists among its contents the quoit of Iphitus and a table of gold and ivory by Colotes.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>",
"Temple_of_Artemis,_Corfu": "The <b>Temple of Artemis at Corfu</b> was a Doric temple raised about 580 BCE on the Greek island of Korkyra, measuring roughly 47.9 by 22.4 metres (157 by 73 feet) with eight columns across the front and seventeen along each side.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Its west gable holds the earliest sculptured pediment surviving from a Greek temple, a limestone composition 22.16 metres (73 feet) wide centred on a colossal winged Gorgon flanked by Pegasus, Chrysaor and two great cats.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Smaller scenes fill the corners, where Zeus swings his thunderbolt at a giant at one end and a seated woman sits before a dead man at the other, and the sculpture is now displayed in the Archaeological Museum of Corfu.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>",
"Siphnian_Treasury": "The <b>Siphnian Treasury</b> was a small marble building put up at Delphi by the people of Siphnos towards the end of the 6th century BCE, on the south side of the Sacred Way just west of the Sicyonian treasury.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Ionic and in antis, about 8.90 by 6.35 metres (29 by 21 feet), it carried draped female figures in place of its two front columns, a sculptured frieze 0.64 metres (2 feet 1 inch) high round all four sides, and a pediment showing Heracles and Apollo struggling over the Delphic tripod.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Herodotus reports that the Siphnians, then the richest of the islanders from gold and silver mines at home, made it out of a tenth of their income.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>",
"Treasuries_at_Delphi": "The <b>treasuries at Delphi</b> were small stone buildings put up inside the sanctuary of Apollo by individual cities, and by individuals, to hold the votive offerings, weapons and other valuables dedicated there.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> They lined the Sacred Way that climbs through the precinct to the temple, most of them built as small Doric or Ionic temples with two columns standing between the ends of their side walls, their outer faces often covered with inscriptions.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Pausanias reports that the Thebans raised theirs from the spoils of Leuctra and the Athenians theirs from Marathon, and that by his own day nothing of value was left to be seen in any of them.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>",
"Pediment": "A <b>pediment</b> is the triangular gable end of a building in the classical orders, the field enclosed between the horizontal cornice above the entablature and the two sloping cornices of the roof; its flat back wall is the tympanum.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> In Greek temples this field is one of only two places the orders prescribe for sculpture, the other being the frieze, and acroteria were set on its three angles.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Its varying height, from a centre seeming to require a colossal figure to angles with barely room for a reclining one, made it the hardest field a Greek sculptor was asked to fill, and it shaped the compositions put into it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>",
"Metope": "A <b>metope</b> is the roughly square panel between two triglyphs in the frieze of a building in the Doric order, a field left plain or filled with sculpture.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Many were a plain white surface of stucco or marble, but the square face was inviting for a decorator, and the projecting triglyphs either side framed whatever was carved in it, some panels having an individual frame besides.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The field shapes what can be shown, practically limiting a composition to two figures or three, so favoured subjects were those dividing easily into separate scenes, such as the labours of Heracles or combats of Greeks with centaurs.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>",
"Aeolic_capital": "An <b>Aeolic capital</b> is an Archaic column head on which two spiral volutes rise vertically out of the shaft and spring outward, with a palmette between them and a girdle of pendant leaves below.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Because it is strictly two-faced, with no ornament on its narrow sides, it could not be turned at a building's corner, and it seems to have served interior colonnades and free-standing votive columns.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Most belong to the second half of the 6th century BCE and to the north-eastern Aegean; the origin is generally traced to Levantine models, and the term 'proto-Aeolic' used of Cypriot and Levantine pieces reflects that view.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>",
"Lost-wax_casting": "<b>Lost-wax casting</b> is a metalworking method in which the object is first modelled in wax, encased in clay or plaster, and then heated so that the wax runs out through vents, leaving a cavity into which molten metal is poured.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The mould must be broken open to free the casting, so each piece is unique unless the wax model is itself reproduced from a reusable mould.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> In the hollow version the wax is laid as a thin skin over a fireproof core pinned in place by metal rods, which saves costly metal, reduces the weight enormously and allows figures far larger than solid casting can manage.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>",
"Greek_lyric_poetry": "<b>Greek lyric poetry</b> is the modern name for the verse of ancient Greece that was neither epic nor drama, where the Greeks of the classical age said <i>melic</i>, a term covering all poetry sung to a musical accompaniment.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Alexandrian scholars fixed a canon of nine melic poets, from Alkman and Sappho to Pindar and Bacchylides, and divided the surviving work into monody, sung by one voice, and choral song performed by a trained chorus that danced as it sang.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Almost none of it survives entire, the fragments being preserved in quotation and scattered through the manuscripts of more than eighty later authors, grammarians and scholiasts.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>",
"Ancient_Egypt": "Ancient Egypt was the civilisation of the lower Nile and its delta, which lasted under a long succession of kings for roughly three thousand years, ending with the Roman annexation of 30 BCE.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Its farming depended on the river’s summer flood, which before modern damming rose at Aswan as early as June, peaked from August to September, and receded by the end of October, when sowing began.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Historians divide the span into an Old, a Middle and a New Kingdom, each a stretch of unified rule, separated by Intermediate Periods in which central authority came apart, and then a Late Period, Persian rule and the Ptolemaic dynasty.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>",
"Emmer": "Emmer is a hulled wheat, <i>Triticum turgidum</i> subspecies <i>dicoccum</i>, whose grains stay wrapped in tough glumes, so threshing yields spikelets that must be pounded apart before the grain comes free.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> It is one of the founder crops of southwest Asian farming, cultivated in the Levant from about 9700 BCE, and the traits that separate it from wild emmer arose there rather than where it was later carried.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Its hulled ears made every harvest a matter of threshing, winnowing and sieving, and it was the principal bread and beer grain of ancient Egypt, sown after the Nile flood had drained and reaped between February and May.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>",
"Naqada_culture": "The Naqada culture is the archaeological culture of Upper Egypt through the 4th millennium BCE, named after a cemetery near the town of Naqada and divided into three phases that are defined by their pottery.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Its first phase resembles the Badarian culture before it, with the dead in shallow oval pits, while later phases bring marl and rough wares, copper, stone vessels, well-furnished tombs and the earliest Egyptian writing.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Over its final phase Naqadan material spread north into the Nile Delta, displacing the Lower Egyptian tradition there, and whether the culture was ever a single state or a group of rival chiefdoms is still debated.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>",
"Narmer_Palette": "The Narmer Palette is a shield-shaped slab of dark greywacke about 63 cm (25 inches) long, carved in low relief on both faces and recovered from a mass of votive objects buried beneath the temple at Hierakonpolis in southern Egypt.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> It belongs to a large class of Egyptian palettes on which malachite was ground into eye-paint, and it keeps that working hollow on one face, framed by the entwined necks of two long-necked leopards.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Its scenes show a king in the red crown and the white crown, striking a kneeling captive and inspecting rows of decapitated enemies, and they are among the earliest examples of Egyptian monumental art.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>",
"Unification_of_Egypt": "The <b>unification of Egypt</b> is the joining of the Nile valley and the Delta under one king, conventionally placed at the start of the 1st Dynasty about 3100 BCE.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Egyptian tradition credited it to a first king called Menes, and royal annals such as the Palermo Stone open each reign with a Union of the Two Lands, a rite performed afresh at every accession.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Excavation records no single moment, and radiocarbon modelling, which places the accession of Aha between 3111 and 3045 BCE, reads the change as a shift from cultural to political unity spread over several centuries.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>",
"Egyptian_hieroglyphs": "<b>Egyptian hieroglyphs</b> are the pictorial script of ancient Egypt, in use from the late 4th millennium BCE until the Roman Period, in which each sign is a recognisable image whose function ranges from the purely pictorial to the strictly phonetic.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Sound signs carry one, two or three consonants and never vowels, so most words close with a determinative marking the class of thing meant, and a consonant already present is often repeated as a reading aid.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Full hieroglyphic forms belonged to monuments and religious texts, while the cursive hands that Greek writers named hieratic and demotic carried the everyday writing of accounts, letters and literature.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>",
"Rosetta_Stone": "<b>Rosetta Stone</b> is a broken slab of inscribed stone, found in Egypt in 1799 and held in the British Museum, that carries a decree passed by an assembly of priests at Memphis in 196 BCE, the ninth year of Ptolemy V.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The same decree appears on it three times, in hieroglyphs, in the cursive Egyptian script known as demotic and in Greek, because its closing clause ordered that copies be cut in all three writings and set up in temples throughout the country.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Since Greek remained legible while the Egyptian scripts did not, the parallel wording became the principal foundation of the 19th-century recovery of hieroglyphic writing.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>",
"Papyrus": "Papyrus is a writing material made from <i>Cyperus papyrus</i>, a tall sedge of African wetlands, whose white pith is peeled of its green rind, cut into thin strips, laid in two crossed layers, pressed until the plant's own sap binds them, and dried into a sheet.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Sheets were pasted edge to edge into rolls, and the same plant supplied boats, sails, mats, coverlets and rope.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> It was the usual writing support of ancient Egypt and, widely exported, of the Greek and Roman worlds, until parchment and later cloth-based paper displaced it between the 7th and 11th centuries CE.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>",
"Pharaoh": "Pharaoh is the English title for the king of ancient Egypt, from the Egyptian <i>per-aa</i>, 'great house', a phrase that named the royal palace before it came to stand for the ruler himself, and which reached English through Hebrew.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The Egyptians' own words for the king were <i>nswt</i> and <i>ity</i>; he was identified with the god Horus, titled 'son of Ra', and held to be divine in nature although housed in a mortal body.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> From the Middle Kingdom he bore five names, two of them written inside the oval the Egyptians called <i>shen</i> and Egyptologists the cartouche, and though normally a male office, a few women held it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>",
"Old_Kingdom_of_Egypt": "The Old Kingdom is the modern name for the period of ancient Egyptian history covering the Third to Sixth Dynasties, conventionally about 2686 to 2181 BCE<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>. It is the age in which the pyramid became the standard royal tomb, from the stepped structure raised for Netjerikhet at Saqqara to the true pyramids of Sneferu and Khufu, each of them the centre of a complex of temples where priests maintained the dead king’s cult<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>. Its administration was built around the palace and the royal cult, and it ended in the Sixth Dynasty when the centre lost its hold on the provinces<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>.",
"Pyramid_of_Djoser": "The Pyramid of Djoser is a stepped stone monument at Saqqara in Egypt, raised in the Third Dynasty for the king whose contemporary name was Netjerikhet, and the earliest pyramidal structure to survive there<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>. It began as a rectangular mastaba and was widened and then raised in successive stages, each smaller than the one beneath, producing a terraced form between the flat tomb and the smooth-sided pyramids that followed<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>. It stands within a large funerary enclosure of dressed stone whose design was ascribed in later Egyptian tradition to Imhotep, a minister of the reign who was afterwards worshipped as a god of medicine<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>.",
"Great_Pyramid_of_Giza": "The Great Pyramid of Giza is the tomb built for Khufu, a king of Egypt’s Fourth Dynasty, on the desert plateau opposite modern Cairo, and the largest of the three pyramids there<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>. Made almost wholly of limestone, it rose about 147 metres (481 feet) on a square base roughly 230 metres (755 feet) a side, laid out so accurately that the average error in their equality, squareness and level is under a ten-thousandth<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>. The dressed casing that once sheathed it was quarried away in later centuries, exposing the stepped core, and it stood at the centre of a complex that also held temples, a causeway, boat burials and smaller pyramids<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>.",
"Great_Sphinx_of_Giza": "The Great Sphinx of Giza is a colossal statue with a lion’s body and a king’s head, cut from the limestone bedrock of the Giza plateau in Egypt during the Fourth Dynasty<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>. Egyptian sphinxes portrayed the reigning king in a lion’s body as a statement of power, and this is the oldest and largest known, ascribed on the archaeological evidence to the reign of Khafre<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>. Buried in sand for much of its history, it was worshipped by the Eighteenth Dynasty as Horemakhet, a form of the sun god, and a granite slab between its forepaws records a clearance under Thutmose IV<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>.",
"Mummification": "Mummification is the deliberate preservation of a dead body against decay, achieved in ancient Egypt by drawing out the internal organs, drying the corpse in natron and wrapping it in linen bandages.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The organs were placed in four jars whose stoppers took the form of the four sons of Horus, and the drying alone took about forty days within a procedure that ran to some seventy.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Chemical analysis of labelled vessels from an embalming workshop has since identified several of the substances used, among them conifer resins and materials carried from far beyond Egypt.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>",
"Book_of_the_Dead": "Book of the Dead is a modern name, taken in the 19th century from the German <i>Totenbuch</i>, for more than 190 Egyptian funerary spells; the Egyptians called them the Spells for Going Forth by Day, the soul's power to leave the tomb by day.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> It was never a fixed book but a repertoire from which each owner chose a selection, so no two manuscripts agree, and the spells were copied onto papyrus, linen, coffins, amulets, figurines and tomb walls.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Grown out of the Pyramid Texts and Coffin Texts, they are first attested in the Thirteenth Dynasty and were last copied, in Demotic, in the 1st and 2nd centuries CE.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>",
"Ancient_Egyptian_religion": "Ancient Egyptian religion was the polytheistic system of belief and practice followed in the Nile valley for more than three thousand years, and it had no founder, no creed and no body of scripture. Its governing concept was <i>maat</i>, the right order of the cosmos and of human society at once, personified as a goddess and set against <i>isfet</i>, disorder; the reigning king was charged with maintaining it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Gods were freely combined, Amun with the sun god Re and Ptah with Sokar and Osiris, and their cult centred on statues housed in temples that ordinary worshippers could rarely enter, so devotion gathered at gateways and outer walls.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>",
"First_Intermediate_Period": "The First Intermediate Period is the phase of ancient Egyptian history between the collapse of the Old Kingdom and the reunification of Egypt under the Middle Kingdom.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Central authority at Memphis gave way to provincial governors ruling for themselves, and two rival houses emerged, one at Herakleopolis in the north and one at Thebes in the south; later kinglists number its rulers as the seventh to eleventh dynasties.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Middle Kingdom writers recall it as an age of famine and disorder, though the archaeological record shows vigorous regional styles and provincial wealth; it closes when Mentuhotep II of Thebes took the whole country.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>",
"Middle_Kingdom_of_Egypt": "The Middle Kingdom of Egypt is the phase of ancient Egyptian history that begins with the reunification of the country under Mentuhotep II of Thebes and covers the later eleventh, twelfth and thirteenth dynasties.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Its kings ruled from a new residence called Itjtawy near modern Lisht, curbed the provincial governors who had grown powerful, reclaimed farmland in the Fayum and pushed the southern frontier into Lower Nubia, where a chain of fortresses guarded the border and regulated trade.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> It is regarded as a cultural high point, above all for a body of literature whose grammar, known as Middle Egyptian, stayed the language of carved official inscriptions for centuries afterwards.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>",
"Hyksos": "Hyksos is the Greek form of the ancient Egyptian title heqa khasut, ruler of foreign lands, and it names the fifteenth dynasty, a line of Near Eastern origin that governed northern Egypt from Avaris in the eastern Nile Delta.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Their material culture is a variant of the Levantine Middle Bronze Age, and Levantine migrants had been settling the Delta for generations before they took power.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The later Greek account of an invasion is not borne out by the burials at Avaris, whose strontium isotope ratios point to a foreign elite born and raised in Egypt.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>",
"New_Kingdom_of_Egypt": "The New Kingdom is the period of ancient Egyptian history running from about 1550 to 1069 BCE, made up of the eighteenth, nineteenth and twentieth dynasties.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> It was the age of Egypt's widest reach, with a southern frontier at the Fourth Cataract in Nubia and holdings north into what is now Syria, and the wealth that came of it paid for building across the country, above all at the temple of Amun at Karnak.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Its kings were buried in rock-cut tombs in the Valley of the Kings at Thebes, and the period closed with the country divided, the dynasty that followed ruling only the north.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>",
"Hatshepsut": "Hatshepsut was a ruler of ancient Egypt's eighteenth dynasty, reigning from about 1479 to 1458 BCE, first on behalf of her young stepson Thutmose III and then, having taken a full royal titulary and the throne name Maatkare, as a king alongside him.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> She is remembered chiefly for building, above all for the terraced mortuary temple at Deir el-Bahari on the west bank at Thebes, whose reliefs record a seaborne expedition to the land of Punt and present the god Amun as her father.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> After her death her name and her image were cut from many monuments and left out of the later king lists.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>",
"Thutmose_III": "Thutmose III was a king of the eighteenth dynasty of ancient Egypt who reigned from about 1479 to 1425 BCE, one of the longest reigns recorded for an Egyptian ruler.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> He came to the throne as a child and his aunt Hatshepsut ruled beside him as king for 21 years, after which he governed alone for more than thirty years and led at least 17 campaigns into Syria and Palestine, reaching the Euphrates.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> He built or rebuilt temples the length of the Nile valley and in Nubia, added extensively to the Amun temple at Karnak, and was buried in the Valley of the Kings.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>",
"Archaic_period_(North_America)": "The Archaic period is the division of North American prehistory that follows the Paleoindian period and closes with the adoption of farming in the north and of pottery in the south, dated in New England to roughly 9,000 to 2,500 years before the present.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Its people were descendants of the Paleoindians but generalists rather than specialists, drawing on a broad range of wild game and plant foods as the glaciers went and the climate warmed.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Grinding tools such as manos and metates become common on their sites, and by the late Archaic the settlements are larger and more sedentary as reliance on plant foods grows.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>",
"Forum_Boarium": "Forum Boarium was the cattle market of ancient Rome, the low ground between the Palatine, Capitoline and Aventine hills running down to the Tiber, where the roads from the north and east met the river crossing.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The open space was public property and needed boundary stones to defend it from the buildings crowding in, until the name covered a whole district rather than a market.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Its cults were among the oldest in the city: it held the Ara Maxima, the earliest centre of the worship of Hercules at Rome,<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> and the twin temples of Fortuna and Mater Matuta that tradition credited to Servius Tullius.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>",
"Casa_Romuli": "Casa Romuli was a hut of straw with a thatched roof at the south-west corner of the Palatine hill in Rome, near the top of the Scalae Caci, which the Romans kept as the house of Romulus.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Dionysius of Halicarnassus records that nothing was ever added to make it grander, and that whenever storm or age damaged it those in charge repaired it back to what it had been.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> It burned and was rebuilt more than once, was still on record in the 4th century CE, and a second one, probably a copy of the first, stood on the Capitoline.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>",
"Founding_of_Rome": "Founding of Rome is the act with which Roman writers began their own history: a rite they said came from Etruria, in which a plough drawn by a bull and a cow cut a furrow round the line of the future wall.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The Romans kept 21 April as the day and celebrated it as their country's birthday, but never agreed on the year; the reckoning that became standard placed it in 753 BCE.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Modern study of early Rome finds people on the site before the 8th century BCE and no single state there until the 7th, giving as a process what the tradition gives as a moment.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>",
"Romulus_and_Remus": "Romulus and Remus are the twin brothers whom Roman tradition made the founders of Rome, born to a Vestal at Alba Longa, exposed on the Tiber by a king's order, and suckled by a she-wolf until a shepherd took them in.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Grown, they restored their grandfather at Alba and set out to build a city where they had been left, but the augury taken to settle which should name it ended in Remus's death.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> A deflating version survived beside the marvel, in which the wolf was the shepherd's wife, nicknamed for her unchaste life;<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> the story was public in Rome long before it was written down.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>",
"Capitoline_Wolf": "Capitoline Wolf is the bronze she-wolf about 75 centimetres (30 inches) high in the Palazzo dei Conservatori in Rome, the twins beneath her added in the Renaissance and traditionally given to Antonio del Pollaiolo.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The traditional attribution placed the bronze in an Etruscan workshop of the 5th century BCE, but its restoration showed it cast in a single piece, a technique typical of the Middle Ages, and radiocarbon analysis gave a medieval date.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> A medieval date severs the statue from the she-wolves that ancient writers describe,<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> though it had never been unanimously accepted as an early or middle Republican work and dating proposals are still being made.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>",
"Aeneas": "Aeneas is the Trojan whom Roman tradition made the ancestor of the Romans: spared at the sack of Troy, carried after long wandering to the Laurentian coast, married to the daughter of King Latinus, and buried by the Numicius as Jupiter Indiges.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The legend bridged the four centuries between the fall of Troy and the founding of Rome, his son Ascanius leaving Lavinium to build Alba Longa, whose kings run down to the grandfather of Romulus.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> It was also a usable claim: the Julian house took its name from Ascanius as Iulus, and Julius Caesar told a funeral crowd that his family went back to Venus.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>",
"Alba_Longa": "Alba Longa is the city Roman tradition placed on the Alban Hills as the mother of Rome, built by Ascanius thirty years after Lavinium, named for the long ridge it stood along, and ruled by kings named Silvius.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> It was destroyed in a war with Rome that Livy calls a war between parent and child, both peoples being of Trojan descent; the buildings were levelled, the temples spared and the people moved to Rome.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The absorption doubled the Roman citizen body and put Alban nobles into the senate, which is how the tradition accounts for patrician houses at Rome that were not Roman in origin.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>",
"Ab_urbe_condita": "Ab urbe condita is Latin for 'from the founding of the city' and names the practice of counting years from the beginning of Rome itself, with an epoch of 753 BCE on the reckoning that became standard.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> It was not how Romans ordinarily dated anything, since a year was identified by naming its two consuls, and an era running from the founding belonged to scholars and historians rather than to everyday record.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> It surfaced in public when a round number fell due, as when Claudius kept the eight hundredth year with Secular Games in 47 CE<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> and Philip the thousandth in April 248.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>",
"The_Rape_of_the_Sabine_Women": "The Rape of the Sabine Women is an episode of Roman foundation legend in which Romulus's followers carried off women from neighbouring peoples during games held in honour of Consus.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Ancient writers explained it by the new city's want of women and its neighbours' refusal of intermarriage,<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> but disagreed over the year, over the number seized — Dionysius of Halicarnassus counted 683 — and over whether the aim was marriage or war.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The fighting that followed was said to have been stopped by the women coming between the armies, and to have ended in the union of the two peoples under a shared kingship.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>",
"Titus_Tatius": "Titus Tatius is the Sabine king whom Roman tradition places on the throne of Rome beside Romulus, after the war that followed the seizure of the Sabine women ended in a treaty joining the two peoples.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> In the accounts he takes the Roman citadel by bribery, gives his name to the Titienses among the early centuries of knights, and gives the citizens theirs, Quirites, after his town of Cures.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> After five years of shared rule he was killed at an altar at Lavinium by friends of envoys whom his kinsmen had murdered and whom he had shielded, leaving Romulus to rule alone.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>",
"Romulus": "Romulus is the founder and first king of Rome in Roman tradition, a figure the Romans credited with the shape of their earliest institutions rather than with the city alone.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The accounts give him twelve lictors, a refuge below the Capitol that filled the new city, a hundred senators called Patres from whom the patricians took their name, and a bodyguard of three hundred Celeres.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> After a reign of 37 years he vanished in a storm and was worshipped as the god Quirinus, though ancient writers also record the charge that senators had killed him and hidden the body.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>",
"Numa_Pompilius": "Numa Pompilius is the second king of Rome in Roman tradition, a Sabine from Cures said to have been chosen for his justice and piety after the death of Romulus.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The accounts credit him with the city's religious order — the flamens of Jupiter, Mars and Quirinus, the Vestal virgins, the twelve Salii and the office of Pontifex Maximus<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> — and with a calendar of twelve lunar months.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> His reign of 43 years was remembered as one of unbroken peace, in which the temple of Janus stood shut, and he was said to have taken his counsel from the nymph Egeria.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>",
"Roman_calendar": "The Roman calendar is the reckoning of months and days used at Rome before Caesar's reform, a year of twelve months and 355 days that ran out of step with the sun.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Roman writers traced it to Numa, the second king, said to have replaced an older ten-month year beginning in March by adding January and February, though their accounts of that older year disagree.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> An intercalary month of 22 or 23 days went into February every other year to hold it to the seasons, and the pontiffs who came to control it used the power so loosely that the civil year drifted from the natural one.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>",
"Tullus_Hostilius": "Tullus Hostilius is the third king of Rome in Roman tradition, remembered as a warlike ruler who is said to have gone looking for a pretext to fight rather than avoiding one.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> His reign turned on the destruction of Alba Longa: its people were moved to Rome, doubling the number of citizens, its nobles were enrolled among the senators, and the Caelian hill was taken into the city.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Ancient accounts give him 32 years and a death by fire that consumed his whole household, ascribed by some to a thunderbolt sent for sacred rites performed wrongly and by others to arson by the man who succeeded him.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>",
"Horatii": "The Horatii are the Roman triplets who, with the Alban Curiatii, were set to decide the war between Rome and Alba Longa by a combat of three against three in place of a battle between the armies.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Two of the Romans were killed at once, and the survivor separated his three wounded opponents by running from them and killed each in turn, so that the sovereignty passed to Rome.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Returning with the spoils he killed his own sister for mourning her betrothed among the Curiatii, was condemned for treason by appointed judges and then acquitted on appeal to the people, which is the earliest such appeal Livy records.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>",
"Ancus_Marcius": "Ancus Marcius is the fourth king of Rome in Roman tradition, a grandson of Numa through his daughter, credited with having Numa's religious commentaries copied out and set up in public.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> He is said to have written down the fetial procedure by which Rome declared war, and to have fought the Latins, carrying the populations of several captured towns to Rome and settling them on the Aventine.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The works ascribed to him took the city to the sea and across the river: the port of Ostia at the Tiber mouth, salt pits on both banks, a walled Janiculum, and the Pons Sublicius, the first bridge over the Tiber.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>",
"Tarquinius_Priscus": "Tarquinius Priscus is the fifth king of Rome in Roman tradition and the first from outside Latium, a wealthy man of the Etruscan city of Tarquinii who moved to Rome and took a Roman name.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The accounts credit him with a hundred new senators, games in the space that became the Circus Maximus, shops around the Forum, a stone wall, the draining of the low ground and a levelled platform on the Capitol for the temple of Jupiter.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> He was killed after 38 years by two shepherds who staged a quarrel at the palace, hired by the sons of the king he had succeeded and whose guardian he had been.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>",
"Cloaca_Maxima": "The Cloaca Maxima is the main drain of ancient Rome, carrying water off the Forum and the valleys between the hills into the Tiber.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Roman writers ascribed it to the Tarquins, Livy setting the plebs to it under the last king and Dionysius crediting the earlier Tarquin with beginning the sewers, and both counted it among the city's greatest works.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Its winding line from the Argiletum through the Forum and the Velabrum follows a stream in a marshy valley, and little if any of the first channel survives in the vaulted stone conduit that stands today.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>",
"Circus_Maximus": "The Circus Maximus is the oldest and largest of the Roman racecourses, laid out in the valley between the Palatine and the Aventine, about 600 m (660 yards) long and 150 m (160 yards) wide.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Roman tradition ascribed its beginnings to the Tarquins, who were said to have marked out the ground and allotted places where spectators could raise wooden stands,<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> though the first permanent starting gates are dated only to 329 BCE.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> By the 1st century BCE it was a building of three storeys with a canal round the track, vaulted stalls all opened by a single rope and, on one ancient count, room for 150,000 people.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>",
"Servius_Tullius": "Servius Tullius is the sixth king of Rome in Roman tradition, said to have been marked out in childhood when flames played about his head as he slept in the palace, and raised there as a son of the house.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> He came to the throne through the queen Tanaquil, who concealed her husband's murder and had the crowd told to obey him, so that he held the power before it was granted him.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The tradition credits him with the temple of Diana on the Aventine, built jointly with the Latin cities, and with a reign of 44 years ended when his daughter's husband seized the throne and had him killed.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>",
"Servian_constitution": "The Servian constitution is the arrangement that Roman tradition ascribes to the king Servius Tullius, under which a citizen's military service and war tax were fixed by the rating of his property instead of falling equally on all.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The rating sorted the people into classes and each class into centuries of younger men, who campaigned, and older men, who guarded the city, the wealthiest bearing the fullest armour and those below the last rating forming one century excused both service and tax.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Dionysius of Halicarnassus counts 193 centuries in all, of which 98 stood in the first class.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>",
"Comitia_centuriata": "The comitia centuriata is the assembly in which Roman citizens voted in the centuries their property rating had placed them in, one vote to a century rather than one to a man.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The centuries were summoned in order of wealth, the knights and the 80 centuries of the first class before any other, so that a question could be settled before the poorer classes were reached at all.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> It met on the Campus Martius, an army not being lawfully commanded within the city boundary, and it chose the senior magistrates, voted on war and heard a citizen's appeal against a capital sentence.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>",
"Servian_Wall": "The Servian Wall is the circuit of wall, rampart and ditch around Rome that tradition ascribes to Servius Tullius, who is said to have surrounded the city with a mound, moats and a wall, and by doing so to have extended the pomerium.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> It took in the Quirinal, Viminal and Esquiline, and Dionysius calls the king it is named after the last to enlarge the circuit of the city.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Its oldest surviving stretches are of grey cappellaccio tufa, usually assigned to the 6th century BCE, while most of what stands is a later reinforcement in Grotta Oscura tufa from the territory of Veii.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>",
"Roman_census": "The Roman census is the registration at which every citizen declared on oath his name, his age, his household and the value of his property, so that his obligations of military service and tax could be fixed by what he owned.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Roman writers ascribe it to Servius Tullius and record that a man who evaded assessment was threatened with imprisonment and death, and under the Republic might be sold by the state as a slave.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> It closed with a purification of the whole people drawn up in arms, the lustrum, which was meant to fall in every fifth year and gave its name to that span.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>",
"Tarquinius_Superbus": "Tarquinius Superbus is the seventh and last king of Rome in Roman tradition, said to have seized the throne by force after the murder of his father-in-law Servius Tullius and to have reigned without election by the people or confirmation by the senate.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Livy and Dionysius both describe a rule held by fear: a standing bodyguard of armed men, capital trials conducted without assessors, and a senate allowed to dwindle because he filled no vacancy in it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> He was driven out after 25 years, and Roman writers date the end of the monarchy from his expulsion.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>",
"Temple_of_Jupiter_Optimus_Maximus": "The Temple of Jupiter Optimus Maximus was the chief temple of Rome, standing on the Capitoline hill and holding three shrines side by side under one pediment, Jupiter in the middle with Juno and Minerva on either hand.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Roman tradition has it vowed by Tarquinius Priscus and built by Tarquinius Superbus, who cleared the older shrines from the site and paid for the work out of the spoils of Suessa Pometia.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> A human head turned up in the foundations, read as a sign that the place would be the head of all the world, and the king was driven out before the work was finished.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>",
"Sibylline_Books": "The Sibylline Books were a collection of oracles kept by the Roman state and opened only at the senate's command, when a prodigy or a disaster called for the gods to be appeased.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Roman writers tell how a stranger brought nine of them to a Tarquin, burned three when he refused her price and three more when he laughed at her, and got that price for the last three before vanishing.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> They were kept in a stone chest under the Capitoline temple, in the charge of two men at first and afterwards of ten and then fifteen, and nobody else might look at them.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>",
"Lucretia": "Lucretia is the Roman noblewoman whose rape by Sextus Tarquinius, the king's son, and death by her own hand are given in Roman tradition as the immediate cause of the fall of the monarchy.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Livy has her send for her father and her husband, tell them plainly what had been done and by whom, and answer their assurance that the mind sins and not the body by saying that she acquitted herself of the fault but not of the penalty.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Dionysius instead has her drive to Rome and die in her father's house before the leading men of the city.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>",
"Expulsion_of_the_kings": "The expulsion of the kings is the rising that Roman tradition places at the end of Tarquinius Superbus's reign, and from which Romans dated the beginning of their Republic.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Livy has it start with an oath sworn over Lucretia's body and go on through a march on Rome, a speech in the forum and a vote to strip the king of his sovereignty and banish him with his wife and children.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Dionysius gives the constitutional sequel: an interrex appointed, and two annual magistrates named to exercise the power the kings had held, in an office called <i>consules</i>.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>",
"Lucius_Junius_Brutus": "Lucius Junius Brutus is the man Roman tradition credits with leading the rising against Tarquinius Superbus and with holding, alongside Collatinus, one of the first two annual magistracies that replaced the kings.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Livy has him take refuge in the appearance of an idiot after his brother was killed on the king's orders, and Dionysius derives the surname itself from that pretence, which he kept up until he judged the time had come to throw it off.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> He was killed early in the war against the exiled king, when he and Arruns Tarquinius drove their spears through each other's shields in a cavalry charge.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>",
"Rex_Sacrorum": "The rex sacrorum was the Roman priest who performed the public sacrifices that had been the king's to make in person, an office created when the monarchy ended so that the rites should not lapse with it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Livy says it was put under the pontifex maximus so that its holder should not be king in anything more than name, and Dionysius has Brutus propose that the man hold the honour for life, exempt from military and civil duty, and do nothing else.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> He was inaugurated rather than elected, in the assembly of the curiae that the pontiffs called for the purpose.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>",
"Comitia_Curiata": "The comitia curiata was the oldest Roman assembly, in which citizens voted in the thirty curiae the people were divided into, each curia carrying one collective vote that a majority of its own members settled.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Roman tradition ascribes the division to Romulus, and the old jurists defined the assembly by it: where the vote is taken by kinds of men, the assembly is curiate.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Its chief business passed to the assembly of centuries under Servius Tullius, and by the Punic Wars it was a form, thirty lictors performing the ceremony in place of the curiae themselves.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>",
"Patres": "The patres were the hundred senators Roman tradition has Romulus create, called fathers for their rank, and their descendants were the patricians.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Under the kings the body they made up was the king's own council, appointed by him and offering advice he could take or leave, and it grew with the city to two hundred and then three hundred members.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> When Brutus refilled the order that Tarquinius Superbus had thinned, the new men of equestrian rank were called <i>conscripti</i> while the old kept the name <i>patres</i>, whence the house was addressed as <i>patres conscripti</i>.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>",
"Patrician": "A patrician was a member of Rome's hereditary aristocracy, the families descended from the <i>patres</i>, who alone could take the auspices by which the state consulted the gods.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Ancient writers traced the name to <i>pater</i>, explaining it by fatherhood, by age, or by the protection these men gave the state; their clans made up the three early tribes, the Ramnes, the Tities and the Luceres.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The order held the priesthoods, the magistracies and the courts, and birth alone conferred it, so that with outsiders admitted only by a rare curiate law fewer than fifty such families survived the Republic, after which Caesar and Augustus made more.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>",
"Plebeian": "A plebeian was a member of the Roman commons, a citizen who lacked the full rights of the state and stood outside the curiae, the older clans, the Senate and every civil and priestly office.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The name carries the root of <i>impleo</i>, to fill, and the order grew from the Latin populations Rome conquered and moved into the city, settled on the Caelian after the fall of Alba Longa and on the Aventine under Ancus Marcius.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Its members served in the army, kept their own rites and their own clans, and were organised by Servius Tullius into thirty local tribes, each under a tribune, with their own assemblies.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>",
"Gens": "A gens was a Roman clan, a group of households sharing one name, one set of rites and a claim on the property of any member who died without nearer heirs.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The pontiff Scaevola defined its members as those bearing the same name who were born free, had no slave among their forebears and had suffered no loss of civil standing.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Its rites bound every member, and a man lost both them and his clan by adoption, adrogation or emancipation, the adrogated renouncing his gens before the curiate assembly; Roman tradition counted three hundred such clans, a hundred to each of the three original tribes.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>",
"Cognomen": "A cognomen was the third name a Roman bore, marking the household to which he belonged inside his clan, after his own given name and the clan name ending in <i>-ius</i>.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Such names were drawn from a peculiarity of body or mind or from a deed of the man reckoned the household's founder, Naso, Caecus, Capito, Cato and Scipio among them, and most passed from father to son.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> A fourth name could be added as an honour for something done, voted by the people or taken by the man himself, and to bear a clan or household name not one's own was a forgery at law.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>",
"Pomerium": "The pomerium was the sacred boundary of a Roman city, a line marked by stones set at intervals within which the auspices of the city could be taken, and the strip beside it could be neither built on nor ploughed.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> It was laid out at a town's founding by yoking a bull and a cow to a plough and drawing a furrow around the site with the clods falling inward, the plough being lifted where the gates were to stand.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Only a man who had taken territory from an enemy might move the line outward, and at Rome the Aventine was left beyond it for centuries.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>",
"Forum_Romanum": "The Forum Romanum was the market place and civic centre of ancient Rome, occupying the low ground between the Palatine and the Capitoline hills, a levelled oblong of about seven <i>iugera</i> ringed by shops, temples, basilicas and porticoes.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The valley was originally a marsh crossed by a stream, with a burial ground on its edge whose latest graves belong to the end of the 7th century or the middle of the 6th century BCE.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Games, funerals, courts and public assemblies were all held there, and the ground around it was divided into building plots under Tarquinius Priscus.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>",
"Lapis_Niger": "The Lapis Niger is a paving of black marble in the Comitium at Rome, the open inaugurated ground before the senate house,<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> which ancient writers explained as marking a place of ill omen, said to be the grave of Romulus, of Faustulus who reared him, or of Tullus Hostilius's grandfather.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Beneath the pavement stands a group of far older monuments: an inscribed four-sided pillar with its top broken away, a conical tufa column and a small shrine.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The inscription is the oldest Latin writing known from Rome, placed at about 500 BCE, and what survives of it appears to concern rites to be performed by the king.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>",
"Annales_maximi": "The <i>annales maximi</i> were the record of each year's events kept by the pontifex maximus at Rome, running from the beginning of the state until the pontificate of Publius Mucius Scaevola in 133 BCE.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> They were the nearest thing to a contemporary account of Rome's earliest centuries, at a time when written records were, in Livy's words, few and scanty.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> What survived of them lay in the pontifical commentaries, which perished almost entirely when the city was burned, so that the earliest Roman historians, writing five centuries after the kings, had little to build on.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>",
"Roman_Republic": "The Roman Republic was the government of Rome between the expulsion of its last king and the rule of the emperors, in which annually appointed magistrates took the place of the king, who had until then been the sole magistrate and held all the power.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Two consuls were elected each year in the assembly of centuries, and Livy refers the beginning of liberty to that annual limit rather than to any weakening of the powers the kings had held.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Polybius held that no one could say with certainty whether the system was monarchical, aristocratic or democratic, since the consuls, the senate and the citizens each seemed to hold it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>",
"Consul": "A consul was the highest magistrate of the Roman Republic, one of two who held the office together for a single year, elected in the assembly of centuries and preceded in public by twelve lictors bearing the fasces.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> On the fall of the monarchy the consuls took over every power the king had held except the priestly one, which was given to the rex sacrorum instead.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> They brought embassies before the senate, summoned and presided over the assemblies of the people and carried out what was decreed, and if one died in office a successor was elected with powers no less than his.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>",
"Fasti_Consulares": "The <i>Fasti Consulares</i> were the official Roman register of the consuls of each year, kept among the public records and providing the framework by which the Republic's years were dated.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The word <i>fasti</i> first meant the days on which legal business could lawfully be done, and then the books that marked them, before widening to cover the registers of consuls, dictators, censors and other magistrates.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The largest surviving copy was cut into the walls of the Regia in the Roman Forum, ran from the expulsion of the kings to the death of Augustus, and is known as the Fasti Capitolini from the Capitol, where its fragments were set up.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>",
"Imperium": "<i>Imperium</i> was the power the Roman state conferred on a man to command its armies, defined by Cicero as that without which military affairs cannot be administered, an army held together or a war waged.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> It was distinct from <i>potestas</i>, which a tribune of the plebs held and which carried no such command, and it was granted not by the election itself but by a law passed in the assembly of curies.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Under the Republic it could not be exercised inside the city except on the day of a triumph, it belonged to no office of itself, and the man who received it was styled <i>imperator</i>.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>",
"Fasces": "The <i>fasces</i> were a bundle of rods bound together with an axe set in the middle, its blade projecting, carried by lictors walking in single file before the higher magistrates of Rome as the outward sign of their authority.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Rome took both the bundle and the office of lictor from the Etruscans, and twelve went before each king and afterwards before each consul.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Inside the city the axes were removed once the people held the right of appeal, and the bundles were lowered to a superior magistrate or to the assembled people, whence the phrase for yielding to another.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>",
"Collegiality": "Collegiality was the principle of Roman republican government by which an office was held by two or more men at once, each of them invested with the same rights as the others.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Because their powers were equal the holders could do nothing unless they agreed, one might hear an appeal against another's sentence or veto his proceedings, and the consuls in practice took the office in alternate months.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Latin called each consul the other's collega and their joint tenure a collegium, though the word properly described an association of at least three men, and the same shape appears in the colleges of tribunes of the plebs and of quaestors.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>",
"Horatius_Cocles": "Horatius Cocles was a Roman of the early Republic remembered for holding the wooden bridge over the Tiber against an Etruscan army until his countrymen had cut it down behind him.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Dionysius of Halicarnassus calls him Publius Horatius, derives the surname from an eye struck out in an earlier battle, and sets two companions beside him, Spurius Larcius and Titus Herminius, who withdrew before the last of the bridge fell.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The accounts part over his end, Livy having him leap into the river in armour and swim across to a statue and a grant of public land, while Polybius has him give up his life there deliberately.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>",
"Lars_Porsena": "Lars Porsena was an Etruscan king of Clusium who made war on Rome at the end of the 6th century BCE, in the tradition on behalf of the Tarquins the Romans had just expelled.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Livy has the siege broken by Roman nerve rather than Roman arms, and gives terms under which Rome surrendered hostages and returned territory to Veii before the king came down from the Janiculum.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Two later writers describe something harsher: Tacitus refers in passing to a city that was surrendered to him, and Pliny quotes a treaty he granted the Roman people confining iron to the tilling of fields.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>",
"Battle_of_Lake_Regillus": "The battle of Lake Regillus was fought in Tusculan territory between Rome and a Latin army in which the exiled Tarquins were serving, and Roman tradition dates it to 499 BCE or, in other authors, 496.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Livy gives it to the dictator Aulus Postumius, describes it as a run of duels in which almost every leader on both sides was wounded, and has the dictator vow a temple to Castor while it was being fought.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Dionysius records that two horsemen of more than human size were seen fighting for Rome and again that afternoon in the Forum, and the temple was built beside the spring where they watered their horses.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>",
"Foedus_Cassianum": "The Foedus Cassianum was the treaty of alliance between Rome and the cities of Latium, named after Spurius Cassius, one of the consuls of the year in which it was sworn, which Roman reckoning places in 493 BCE.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Dionysius of Halicarnassus preserves its terms: perpetual peace, no war or passage granted to an enemy of either party, help with all forces against an attack, an equal share of the spoils of common wars, and private suits settled within ten days where the contract was made.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Cicero recalled the text cut on a bronze column behind the Rostra, standing until shortly before his own day.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>",
"Akhenaten": "Akhenaten was a king of ancient Egypt's eighteenth dynasty who came to the throne as Amenhotep IV, took his new name in his sixth year, and is attested down to a seventeenth regnal year.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> He worshipped the sun as the visible disc, the Aten, shown with rays that end in hands offering life, and founded a new royal city, Akhetaten, in Middle Egypt, while the names and images of Amun and his consort Mut were erased on accessible monuments in Egypt and Nubia.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> That city held perhaps 30,000 people and lasted about 15 years before the court returned to Memphis and its temples and palaces were demolished.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>",
"Tutankhamun": "Tutankhamun was a king of ancient Egypt's eighteenth dynasty who came to the throne as a boy, reigned roughly a decade from about 1336 to 1327 BCE, and died still in his teens of a cause that is not known.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> His importance for Egyptian history is the reinstallation of the gods after the Amarna period, recorded on restoration stelae at Karnak, and the return of the court from Akhetaten to Memphis, where the Aten's cult was abandoned.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> He was buried in tomb 62 in the Valley of the Kings, a small and sparsely decorated chamber into which some 5,000 objects were packed.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>",
"Ramesses_II": "Ramesses II was a king of ancient Egypt's nineteenth dynasty, the son of Sety I, who reigned from about 1279 to 1213 BCE and was buried in tomb 7 in the Valley of the Kings.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> His war against the Hittites produced the battle of Qadesh, recorded at a length unmatched among Egyptian sources, and in his twenty-first year a treaty with the Hittite king Hattusili III, of which both sides' versions survive.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> By the number and scale of his monuments, among them the Great Temple at Abu Simbel and much of the Great Hypostyle Hall at Karnak, he left a larger mark on Egypt than any other king.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>",
"Battle_of_Kadesh": "The Battle of Kadesh was fought in 1274 BCE, the fifth year of Ramesses II, between an Egyptian army and the Hittites under Muwatalli II at the city of Kadesh on the Orontes.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The Egyptians marched in four divisions named for the gods Amun, Ra, Ptah and Seth, and when the Hittite chariotry struck those divisions were strung out over 55 kilometres (34 miles), so that the king came close to defeat without being beaten.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> It is the best documented battle of the late Bronze Age from the Egyptian side, recorded in reliefs and texts on temple walls, and both sides afterwards claimed the victory.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>",
"Valley_of_the_Kings": "The Valley of the Kings is a desert valley on the west bank of the Nile opposite Thebes, modern Luxor, where the rulers of ancient Egypt's eighteenth, nineteenth and twentieth dynasties were buried, along with some relatives and a few high-ranking commoners.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> It was chosen for its isolation, for the pyramid-shaped peak above it, and because the west, where the sun set, carried funerary associations, the Egyptians having seen the earlier pyramids robbed and preferring hidden tombs cut into rock.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> It holds over sixty tombs and about twenty unfinished ones, their chambers and corridors carved out of the cliffs, and most were emptied of their contents in antiquity.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>",
"Karnak": "Karnak is a complex of temples on the east bank at Thebes, modern Luxor, in Upper Egypt, which the ancient Egyptians called Ipet-sut, 'The Most Select of Places'.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Its Great Temple of Amun housed the cult of Amun of Thebes and was extremely wealthy, its priesthood holding great political power, and the site also holds precincts of Mut and of Montu and a temple to the Aten.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Building ran from the reign of Intef II to the Ptolemaic period, with additions under Rome, and its Great Hypostyle Hall is a forest of 134 columns raised chiefly by Sety I and Ramesses II.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>",
"Nubia": "Nubia is the stretch of the Nile valley upstream of Egypt, its lower part running from the First to the Second Cataract and its upper part beyond, in modern southern Egypt and Sudan.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Egypt was drawn to it by gold and copper and by the hard wood and ivory traded through it, conquering Lower Nubia in the Middle Kingdom and holding a fortified border at Semna, then pushing as far as the Fourth Cataract under Thutmose I.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Upstream lay the Kerma culture, which had formed a state centred on Kerma before that conquest destroyed it, after which the province was governed by a 'viceroy of Kush'.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>",
"Third_Intermediate_Period": "The Third Intermediate Period is the phase of ancient Egyptian history between the New Kingdom and the Late Period, running from about 1069 to 664 BCE, when the country was for much of that time divided.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Kings ruled from Tanis in the Delta while Upper Egypt governed itself under the high priest of Amun at Thebes, and from about 945 BCE dynasties of Libyan origin held power from Bubastis and later from Sais.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> From about 725 BCE the Kushite kings of Napata invaded and ruled Egypt as its twenty-fifth dynasty, until the Neo-Assyrians conquered the country and installed the kings of Sais in their place.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>",
"Indus_Valley_Civilisation": "The Indus Valley Civilisation, or Harappan civilisation, was the Bronze Age urban society of the north-western Indian subcontinent, its remains spread across what is now Pakistan and north-western India over a range exceeding those of contemporary Mesopotamia and Egypt.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Archaeologists divide it into Early, Mature and Late phases, of which the mature or urban phase, from about 2600 to 1900 BCE, produced the first cities in South Asia.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Those cities are known for gridded streets and drainage, shared standards of measurement and recording, and long-distance exchange, and for the absence of palaces, exclusionary temples, rich tombs and monuments to individuals.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>",
"Harappa": "Harappa is an archaeological site in the Sahiwal district of Punjab, Pakistan, standing on the left bank of a former channel of the Ravi River, and one of five major urban centres of the Indus Valley Civilisation.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> It was occupied from about 3700 to 1300 BCE, through a Ravi phase, a Kot Dijian phase and an urban phase of about 2600 to 1900 BCE, its quarters shifting so that the remains form a group of mounds.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Excavation has found massive gated walls around each neighbourhood and cemeteries of both the urban and the post-urban phases, although colonial brick-mining removed much of the architecture.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>",
"Mohenjo-daro": "Mohenjo-daro is an archaeological site near the Indus River on Pakistan's Larkana Plain, and one of the largest cities of the Indus Valley Civilisation, built up during the urban phase of about 2600 to 1900 BCE.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Its neighbourhoods stood on massive platforms and were served by wells, pipes, gutters and soak pits draining bathing platforms inside individual houses, while its largest structures, among them the Great Bath, stand on the western mound.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> No palace, exclusionary temple, lavish burial or individual-glorifying art has been found there, and the spread of its house sizes narrowed over the life of the city.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>",
"Indus_script": "The Indus script is the undeciphered sign system of the Indus Valley Civilisation, surviving mainly on small stone stamp seals and on objects of pottery, terracotta and copper.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Counts of its texts run from about 3,000 to about 3,800, a single line carries no more than 14 signs, and around 400 distinct signs have been identified, though one tally reaches 676.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> No bilingual text has been found and the underlying language is unknown, so readings still range from an Indo-Aryan language through a Dravidian one to a purely numerical system, and it has even been argued that the signs are not writing at all.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>",
"Indus_urban_planning": "Indus urban planning is the set of shared building conventions behind the cities of the Indus Valley Civilisation, comprising well-organised streets, sanitation and water management, and a standardised system of measurement.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Wide streets ran north to south and narrower lanes east to west, neighbourhoods stood on massive platforms, and households ran a pipe from an indoor bathing platform to drains laid beneath the lanes.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Bricks were made to a fixed proportion of two widths to the length, and stone weights from about 1 gram to well over 10 kilograms (0.04 to 22 pounds) were standardised closely enough to work as one system.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>",
"Dholavira": "Dholavira is an archaeological site in the island belt of the Rann of Kachchh, in Gujarat in western India, and one of the five major urban centres so far identified in the Indus Valley Civilisation.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Its excavated plans show city walls and gateways, an extensive open clearing enclosed within the walls, and a series of reservoirs cut deep into the bedrock around the town and joined to one another.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The settlement stood for about 1,500 years, from roughly 4,950 to 3,450 years ago, and an earthquake damaged it between about 4,450 and 4,150 years ago.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>",
"Lothal": "Lothal is an archaeological site of the Indus Valley Civilisation on the Gujarat coast of western India, one of the smaller settlements rather than one of its five cities, and among those that have produced human remains and burials.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Its excavator called it a harbour town at the head of the Gulf of Cambay, and a circular steatite seal found there, neither wholly Indian nor Sumerian, resembles the seals of the Persian Gulf islands.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The town grew over a tidal salt marsh and was left high and dry as the sea level fell, and kilns with vitrified inner surfaces and high-grade copper ingots dug there point to skilled smelting.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>",
"Decline_of_the_Indus_civilisation": "The decline of the Indus civilisation is the contraction of its cities and its exchange network after about 1900 BCE, through the Late Harappan phase to about 1300 BCE, when the interaction sphere retracted, urban centres lost population and agrarian village life took over.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Settlement moved from many small sites around a few large centres to a de-nucleated pattern, and in parts of north-western India the density of sites may have risen.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> No single cause is agreed, though an increase in drought episodes from about 4,200 years ago is the explanation most often made.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>",
"Indo-Aryan_migrations": "The Indo-Aryan migrations are the movements that carried Indo-European speech into the Indian subcontinent, a late event of the middle to late Bronze Age.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Ancient DNA from 523 individuals finds no steppe pastoralist ancestry at the Bactria-Margiana sites of Central Asia before 2100 BCE, and places that ancestry in the Swat valley further south in the first half of the 2nd millennium BCE, where it now makes up as much as 30 per cent of the ancestry of some groups.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> An individual of the Indus civilisation carries little if any of it, the influx was strongly male-driven, and how much immigration took place remains contested.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>",
"Vedic_period": "The Vedic period is the age of the Sanskrit texts called the Vedas, which follows the Indus civilisation in northern South Asia and is named for a body of literature rather than for any state.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Three literary strata are distinguished within it: the four Vedas, collections of hymns and prayers made chiefly to accompany the pressing of soma and the offering of melted butter, then the Brahmanas, then the Sutras.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Its earlier half centred on the Punjab and its later half on the valley of the Ganges, and its language, Vedic Sanskrit, is the oldest recorded stage of Indo-Aryan.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>",
"Rigveda": "The Rigveda is the oldest of the four Vedas and the oldest surviving book of the Indian subcontinent, a collection of hymns in Vedic Sanskrit.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The surviving Shakala recension holds 1,017 hymns, or 1,028 counting the eleven Valakhilya hymns inserted in the eighth book, grouped in ten books called mandalas; books 2 to 7 are family books, each handed down within the family of seers that made it, while every hymn of the ninth is addressed to Soma.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> About 250 hymns celebrate Indra and at least 200 Agni, the fire of the sacrifice.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>",
"Sanskrit": "Sanskrit is the classical language of the Indian subcontinent, its name, samskrita, meaning literally 'put together', so 'refined', and set against the Prakrits, the popular dialects.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Vedic Sanskrit is the oldest recorded stage of Indo-Aryan, from which Middle Indo-Aryan and then the modern languages of northern South Asia descend, and its alphabet, worked out on phonetic principles, has not been altered since it was settled.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Panini's grammar, of about the 4th century BCE, casts it into some four thousand rules written like algebraic formulae in which brevity alone is sought, and it superseded every predecessor and shaped the whole later literature.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>",
"Erlitou_culture": "The Erlitou culture is the Bronze Age culture of the central plains of northern China, dated to about 1900 to 1500 BCE and named after its type site at Yanshi in Henan province.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Its heartland was the Yiluo river basin, where a four-level settlement hierarchy placed the great site itself above 10 small centres, 43 supravillages and 148 large villages, holding a population estimated at between about 28,000 and 82,000.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> It is the earliest bronze-casting culture of central China, and whether its remains belong to the Xia or to the Shang of the transmitted records has been argued since the site was found in 1959.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>",
"Xia_dynasty": "The Xia dynasty is the first of the dynasties in the transmitted Chinese records, placed before the Shang and dated in Chinese scholarship to the 21st to 16th centuries BCE.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> It takes its name from a small principality in Henan, its rulers are said to have held the throne for 439 years, and the last of them, Jie, was dethroned and banished by Tang the Successful, who founded the Shang.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Whether it can be found in the ground is unsettled: Chinese scholarship has generally identified it with the Bronze Age remains at Erlitou in Henan, and whether those are Xia or early Shang has been argued for decades.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>",
"Shang_dynasty": "The Shang dynasty ruled the central plains of northern China along the Yellow River from about 1600 to 1046 BCE, and it is the earliest dynasty of Chinese history that archaeological evidence supports.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Its capitals included Zhengzhou, where a walled city of about 1509 to 1315 BCE has been excavated, and Anyang, the seat of the late dynasty, from which tens of thousands of inscribed oracle bones survive.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Its burials are sharply graded, its copper objects carry highly radiogenic lead whose source is unresolved, and by the transmitted account it was overthrown by King Wu of Zhou in about 1046 BCE.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>",
"Oracle_bone_script": "Oracle bone script, jiaguwen, is the earliest form of systematic Chinese writing, carved on turtle plastrons and the shoulder blades of cattle in the late Shang, from about 1250 to 1045 BCE.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The bones were drilled with pits and cracked with heat for divination, and what is written on them are the divination records of royalty and nobility, most of the pieces coming from Anyang.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> More than 160,000 inscribed pieces are known, carrying some four thousand distinct signs of which only about a thousand have been deciphered, their meanings and modern Chinese equivalents confirmed.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>",
"Yinxu": "Yinxu, the ruins of Yin, is the site of the last capital of the Shang dynasty, at Anyang in northern Henan, occupied from about 1250 to 1045 BCE and divided by archaeologists into four phases.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> All the kingly tombs lie in one cemetery at Xibeigang, thousands of lesser graves fill a western cemetery of lineage members, and rank shows in the metal, the sacrifices and the size of each chamber.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> It was among the largest metal consumers in Eurasia in the 2nd millennium BCE, drawing metal from more than 500 km (300 miles) away; Chinese excavation began there in 1928.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>",
"Chinese_ritual_bronzes": "Chinese ritual bronzes are the vessels cast in great numbers under the Shang and Zhou dynasties, between about 1600 and 221 BCE, for banqueting and for offering food and drink in ancestor worship.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The preference for putting bronze into vessels rather than into weapons and tools sets China apart from the rest of Bronze Age Eurasia, and making them was the most prominent handicraft industry of the age.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> They were cast in ceramic moulds and cores and buried with the elite dead, and about 21,000 of them carry inscriptions, from short clan emblems to Western Zhou texts of up to 500 characters.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>",
"Fu_Hao": "Fu Hao was a queen of the Shang and one of the royal consorts of King Wu Ding, identified from inscriptions cast into bronze ritual vessels and matched against the records on the oracle bones.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Her tomb at Yinxu in Anyang is the only intact top royal burial so far excavated there, and some 1.6 tonnes (1.8 tons) of metal came out of it, alloyed from very pure copper.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> She was not the greatest of the Shang dead, the looted kingly tombs at Xibeigang running to seven times the size of hers, and a jade parrot from her grave gives the first scientific evidence that the Shang burnt jade in sacrifice.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>",
"Sanxingdui": "Sanxingdui is a Bronze Age site at Guanghan in Sichuan, on the south bank of the Yazi river, and the leading representative of the ancient Shu culture, which runs from the late Neolithic to the early Western Zhou.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Its sacrificial pits, of about 1200 to 1000 BCE, held ivory, seashells, bronze, pottery, jade, stone, gold, bone and horn, the ivory laid over everything else and layers of ash above that.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Over half the bronzes found there carry the same highly radiogenic lead as the ritual vessels of the Central Plains, and its carnelian beads were cut from stone quarried more than 1,000 km (600 miles) north.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>",
"Psychology": "Psychology is the scientific study of mind and behaviour, applying observation, experiment and measurement to how people and other animals sense, learn, remember, feel, decide and act.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Its questions belonged to philosophy until the late 1800s, the conventional marker of its independence being the institute for experimental psychology that Wilhelm Wundt founded at Leipzig in 1879.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Because behaviour is biological at root and shaped by dealings with other people, the field sits between the natural and the social sciences, and it now spans basic research and applied specialisms such as clinical, forensic, educational, health, occupational and sport psychology.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>",
"Behaviour": "Behaviour is what a whole living organism does: its internally coordinated responses, actions and inactions alike, to stimuli inside or outside itself, excluding changes better understood as development.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> No definition is agreed on, and a survey of 174 members of three behaviour-focused scientific societies found respondents contradicting the published definitions, one another and themselves.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> In psychology it is the observable side of what the discipline studies, covering anything a person or an animal does that can be watched or recorded, from a key press to a change in heart rate, and a programme published in 1913 proposed to make it the whole subject.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>",
"Mental_process": "A mental process is an operation of a mind — perceiving, attending, remembering, reasoning, judging, deciding or feeling — and it is not open to observation from outside.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Those to do with thinking are grouped together as cognition, which takes in perception, knowledge, problem solving, judgment, language and memory, and not all of it is consciously experienced.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Such a process can only be inferred, from observable behaviour, from biological measures and from what people report, and from timing: since 1868 the difference between two reaction times has been read as the duration of whatever operation separates them.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>",
"Scientific_method": "The scientific method is the procedure by which a claim about the world is made answerable to evidence rather than to authority or intuition.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> A theory proposes an explanation for observed phenomena; a hypothesis drawn from it is a testable prediction of how the world will behave if the theory is right, and it must be falsifiable, capable of being shown incorrect.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Ideas are carried into the world and tested while observations suggest new ideas, and the knowledge that results is empirical, grounded in evidence observable again whoever is observing — which is why replication and open reporting are treated as part of the procedure.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>",
"Level_of_analysis": "A level of analysis is the grain at which a phenomenon is described and explained, one and the same event being open to accounts pitched anywhere from molecules to societies.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> One widely used scheme separates three: a computational level asking what problem is being solved, an algorithmic level asking how it is solved, and an implementational level asking how that is realised in the underlying hardware.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The levels are not a ranking, since an account at one does not replace an account at another, and the same function may be mediated by more than one underlying substrate, so similar outward behaviour can rest on different mechanisms.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>",
"Applied_psychology": "Applied psychology is the use of psychological knowledge and method to address a practical problem, as against research undertaken for the advancement of knowledge regardless of any particular use.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The division is one of purpose rather than of subject or rigour, and it is porous: many researchers work at both ends, and a problem in the world often shapes the hypotheses taken into a laboratory.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Its recognised specialisms in Britain number nine, among them clinical, forensic, educational, health and occupational psychology, and where practice meets research the standard asks that the best available evidence be integrated with professional expertise and with the client's own characteristics and preferences.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>",
"Subfields_of_psychology": "The subfields of psychology are the areas the discipline divides into, some named for what they study — biopsychology, sensation and perception, cognition, development, personality, social behaviour — and some for the settings their problems come from, such as clinical, forensic, health, occupational, and sport and exercise work.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The map is national as well as intellectual, the British professional body recognising nine applied specialisms where the American association has 54 divisions.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> What the divisions do not mark is separate methods, and one criticism holds that the branches are held together by a shared methodology rather than by any connection between their subject matters.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>",
"Hierarchy_of_the_sciences": "The hierarchy of the sciences is the proposal, made by Auguste Comte, that disciplines can be ordered by the complexity of their subject and the precision of the knowledge they reach, with the physical sciences at the top, the social sciences at the bottom and the biological sciences between them.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> It has been tested rather than asserted: papers reporting a result that supports their hypothesis are commoner towards the lower end, and bibliometric properties such as reference age order the same way.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> On that evidence the ordering tracks how far a field can reach consensus as its subject grows complex, rather than how carefully its practitioners work.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>",
"Hindsight_bias": "Hindsight bias is the tendency to treat an outcome as having been likely once it is known, so that judgements of probability made in hindsight exceed those made in foresight.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> It is studied by comparing people who know the outcome with people who do not, or by having one person judge, learn what happened and then recall the judgement, which shifts towards the outcome; the leading accounts hold that the knowledge alters the memory rather than only the report of it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> It has been measured outside the laboratory in recalled election forecasts, and it reaches perception too, a prior clear view making a degraded one easier to read.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>",
"Nature_versus_nurture": "Nature versus nurture is the question of how far differences between people arise from what they inherit and how far from what happens to them, now generally treated as a false opposition.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Inheritance is taken to set boundaries within which experience decides where somebody falls, genes influence the environments a person meets while those environments influence which genes are expressed, and one genotype can be expressed in more than one way.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The two are hard to separate because parents supply both the DNA and the rearing home, so an apparently environmental effect cannot be read off without controlling for the genes a family shares.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>",
"Determinism": "Determinism is the claim that events, including decisions and actions, are causally determined by what went before them, or determined for all practical purposes.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> It is set against a free will strong enough to require that some decisions are both undetermined and themselves the causes of what follows; a separate and sharper worry is epiphenomenalism, on which decisions are not the causes of actions at all.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> How far it excuses anything is also an empirical question: across 5,268 people in 20 countries responsibility was ascribed regardless, with the deterministic case treated differently in Western countries and not in most Asian ones.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>",
"Reductionism": "Reductionism is the strategy of explaining something by taking it apart and accounting for it through its components, on the premise that the properties of a whole follow from the properties of its parts.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> It has a long record of success, having identified specific disease agents and produced treatments aimed at them, so the argument around it concerns limits rather than worth.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Those limits are two: emergence, in which novel structures and properties arise from self-organisation so that a whole becomes very different from the sum of its parts, and the fact that one function may be realised by more than one underlying substrate.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>",
"Mind-body_problem": "The mind-body problem is the question of how mental states stand to physical ones.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Three answers are usually distinguished: dualism, on which minds are in principle independent of the bodies they are temporarily attached to; monism, on which the mind is the same thing as the brain; and emergentism, on which the mind is based on the brain while being more than its activity.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Its scientific form is the explanatory gap, the difficulty of saying how neuronal activity gives rise to cognition; its practical form is a dilemma about mental disorder, which an independent mental sphere makes dualist and a purely physical account leaves without the word mental.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>",
"Introspection": "Introspection is the examination of one's own conscious experience as objectively as possible, and it was the founding method of experimental psychology.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> It was abandoned on a practical ground rather than a philosophical one: despite efforts to train people in the process, it stayed highly subjective and produced very little agreement between individuals.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Self-report survived as one route to a mental process, and disciplined versions have since been built, among them a method in which a randomised beeper cues a report of inner experience at the moment it sounds, so that what is described is not reconstructed afterwards from belief and memory.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>",
"Objectivity_(science)": "Objectivity in science is the demand that a finding should not depend on who produced it, which is why scientific knowledge is called empirical: it rests on evidence observable time and again regardless of who observes.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Where evidence passes through a person it is guarded procedurally rather than by good intention, with criteria for what will be recorded fixed in advance and the observations of different people compared, a consistency measured as inter-rater reliability.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The wider discretion lies in analysis, one imaging dataset put through 6,912 pipelines yielding maps on which no location was significant throughout and most were significant somewhere.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>",
"Scientific_theory": "A scientific theory is a well-developed set of ideas proposing an explanation for observed phenomena, from which a hypothesis is drawn as a testable prediction of how the world will behave if the idea is right.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The prediction must be falsifiable, capable of being shown incorrect, or no evidence can bear on it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Whether a field accumulates such theories varies: in psychology the complaint of a theory crisis holds that theories come and go with little cumulative progress, being neither decisively refuted nor accepted, for want of robust phenomena to constrain them and of validated constructs to state them in.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>",
"Hypothesis": "A hypothesis is a testable prediction about how the world will behave if an idea is right, often worded as an if-then statement and drawn from a theory.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> It must be falsifiable, meaning capable of being shown incorrect, since a claim no observation could count against is not being tested by any of them.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Research divides according to how it treats one: exploratory work is small and flexible, with hypotheses loosely articulated or evolving across experiments, while confirmatory work uses rigid pre-specified designs and hypotheses stated in advance, and reading the first as though it were the second is the standard error.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>",
"Operational_definition": "An operational definition is a precise description of a variable, stating exactly how and what is measured in a particular study, which is what lets other people interpret the data and repeat the work.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> In observational research it takes the form of criteria fixed before recording begins, saying which behaviours count and how they are to be classified.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Because one question can be operationalised in many defensible ways, the choice is not a matter of wording: two studies using the same word may measure different things, which is the jingle fallacy, and two words may name one thing, which is the jangle fallacy.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>",
"Psychologist": "A psychologist is a person who studies or applies psychology, working either as a researcher into behaviour and mental processes or as a practitioner who assesses and treats.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Whether the word is legally protected varies from country to country: the United Kingdom reserves nine compound titles, among them clinical, forensic and occupational psychologist, rather than the bare word; the United States gates clinical and counselling practice behind a licensing examination; and most European states regulate clinical psychology while leaving neuropsychology unregulated.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Practitioners are distinguished from psychiatrists, who hold a medical degree and may prescribe medication, and roughly a quarter of psychologists work in universities rather than in clinical settings.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>",
"Empiricism": "Empiricism is the thesis that, for a given subject, we have no source of knowledge, and no source of the concepts used in it, other than experience.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The experience meant is of two kinds, sense experience through the outward senses, which yields knowledge of external objects, and reflective experience, the conscious awareness of one's own mental operations; the thesis is always relative to a subject area, so it may be held about the physical world and withheld about mathematics.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> In the sciences the related adjective has a narrower, methodological life, marking knowledge grounded in objective and tangible evidence that can be observed repeatedly, whoever is doing the observing.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>",
"Rationalism": "Rationalism is the view that, in some subject area, reason supplies knowledge or concepts that sense experience could not have provided.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Three theses are essential to it: that some propositions are knowable by intuition alone and others by deduction from them; that we know certain truths as part of our own nature, experience merely triggering awareness of them; and that some of the concepts we use belong to that same nature, so experience neither supplies them nor settles what they contain.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Plato's doctrine of recollection is an early version, answering the paradox that one can inquire neither into what is already known nor into what is not.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>",
"Associationism": "Associationism connects learning to thought through an organism's causal history: pairs of thoughts become associated by past experience, so that bringing one to mind brings the other.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> It is not a single theory but a constellation of separable theses, serving as accounts of learning, of thinking, of mental structure and of how thought is implemented, tied together by a commitment to a certain arationality, since states are associated because of the history that caused them rather than any rational relation between what they mean.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Its classical statement counts three principles of connection among ideas: resemblance, contiguity in time or place, and cause or effect.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>",
"Tabula_rasa": "Tabula rasa, the blank slate, is the doctrine that the mind brings no ideas or principles into the world with it and is furnished entirely by experience.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Its best-known statement supposes the mind to be white paper, void of all characters and without any ideas, filled from two fountains, sensation and the mind's reflection on its own operations, and argues that agreement among people would prove nothing innate even were it universal.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The slate is blank of ideas rather than of powers, since that same account equips the mind with faculties for working on whatever experience supplies.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>",
"Mind-body_dualism": "Mind-body dualism, in its substance form, is the view that minds and bodies are fundamentally distinct kinds of substance, the mind characterised by thought and the body by extension.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> A substance here stands in need of nothing beyond itself in order to exist, so such a mind could continue to be all that it is even if its body were not, and is known through one principal attribute.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Its standing objection is interaction, pressed from the beginning by Elisabeth of Bohemia: accounts of causation tie causal power to extension, and two radically different kinds of thing seem to lack what they need in common to act on one another.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>",
"Materialism": "Materialism is the thesis that everything is physical, offered as a claim about the nature of the world rather than about how it should be described.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> It does not deny that the world holds items seeming otherwise, whether biological, psychological, moral or mathematical, but insists these are in the end physical or bear an important relation to the physical; the near-synonym physicalism was coined only in the 1930s, where materialism entered English late in the 17th century.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Applied to mind it yields the identity theory, on which experiences just are brain processes rather than merely correlated with them.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>",
"Psychological_nativism": "Psychological nativism holds that some body of concepts or knowledge belongs to an individual's initial state, or develops from that state without needing input from the environment.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Its key word is unusually slippery: innate has been used to mean present at birth, caused by a genetic difference, adapted over evolutionary time, unchanging through development, shared by every member of a species, and simply not learned, with instinct serving as an older name for the same cluster.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The complaint against it is less that ambiguity than the equivocation it invites, and there is near-universal agreement that the everyday notion of innateness is out of step with recent biology and psychology.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>",
"Immanuel_Kant": "Immanuel Kant was a Prussian philosopher, born and resident in Königsberg, who is generally taken to be the central figure of modern philosophy and who synthesised early modern rationalism and empiricism.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The fundamental idea of his critical philosophy, set out in three Critiques, is human autonomy: the understanding is itself the source of the general laws of nature that structure all experience, and reason gives itself the moral law.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> He also held a demanding view of what a science is, on which only cognition of apodictic certainty qualifies, and on that measure he denied the title to chemistry and, more firmly still, to empirical psychology.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>",
"Phrenology": "Phrenology professed to be a philosophy of the human mind, holding that the brain is the organ of mind, that it consists of distinct organs each answering to a faculty, and that the size of every organ can be read from the shape of the head.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> It was first presented to public consideration on the Continent of Europe in 1796 and in Britain in 1814, its founder counting 27 such organs and his chief collaborator later declaring 35.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The central claim has since been tested directly with structural magnetic resonance imaging, and local scalp curvature predicted none of the lifestyle measures matched to phrenological faculties.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>",
"Functional_specialization_(brain)": "Functional specialization, or localisation of function, is the claim that a particular mental function depends on a particular part of the brain rather than on the organ as a whole.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The classical case against it came from ablation, where a fairly extensive portion of the cerebral hemispheres could be removed without abolishing intelligence, which instead weakened as more was taken, a result later named equipotentiality and bounded by a law of mass action under which performance falls with the extent of injury.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Modern work keeps the specialisation without the seats, mapping specialised functions onto large-scale distributed networks.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>",
"Broca's_area": "Broca's area is the posterior part of the third frontal convolution of the left hemisphere, identified in the 1860s as the seat of articulate language.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The evidence was a symptom its describer called aphémie, the loss of articulate speech in a patient with no paralysis of the muscles of articulation, who understands what is said and can write, having lost only the coordinating faculty; at autopsy such cases showed a lesion in that convolution, about nineteen times in twenty on the left.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Imaging of the first such patient's preserved brain later showed damage extending well beyond it, and language circuitry is now described as a distributed and dynamically interconnected system.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>",
"Wernicke's_area": "Wernicke's area lies in the posterior superior temporal gyrus of the left hemisphere and is associated with understanding language rather than producing it, damage to it leaving speech fluent but empty of sense.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Its 1874 description was a model rather than a second centre: the sensory and motor images of speech sit apart and are joined by fibres, from which a third aphasia followed as a prediction, produced by cutting the arcuate bundle rather than either centre.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The tract has held up, its integrity bearing on speech, word retrieval, repetition and comprehension after stroke, while the classical map has been complemented and often challenged by four decades of neuroimaging.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>",
"Law_of_specific_nerve_energies": "The law of specific nerve energies holds that a sensory nerve carries not the quality of what stimulates it but a quality of its own, so what is sensed is the state of the nerve rather than of the world outside.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The argument is that one stimulus applied to different organs of sense yields a different sensation in each — light in one nerve, sound in another, pain in a third — so a nerve is no passive conductor but has powers the exciting cause merely renders manifest.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> It survives as labelled-line coding, argued modality by modality, and has given ground where cortical areas handle content whatever sense delivers it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>",
"Hermann_von_Helmholtz": "Hermann von Helmholtz was a German physician who became professor of physiology at Königsberg and Heidelberg and professor of physics at Berlin, and who applied the methods of physics to the senses.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> He stated the conservation of force in an 1847 pamphlet, invented the ophthalmoscope in 1851 and the ophthalmometer in 1855, and set out the physics, physiology and psychology of vision in his <i>Handbuch der physiologischen Optik</i> of 1867.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> He held that sensations are signs rather than pictures and that perception is an unconscious judgement about what would ordinarily have produced them, a view that placed him among the empiricists on whether spatial vision is learned or given.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>",
"Nerve_conduction_velocity": "Nerve conduction velocity is the speed at which an impulse travels along a nerve fibre, and it varies widely from one fibre to another rather than taking a single value.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> In the fastest human nerves, the large sheathed fibres that carry the sense of where the limbs are, it runs at something like 80 to 120 metres a second, while thin unsheathed fibres are far slower.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The chief determinant is myelin, which insulates the axon so that the impulse leaps between the gaps called nodes of Ranvier rather than creeping along the membrane, and the diameter of the axon and the spacing of those nodes enter as well.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>",
"Ernst_Heinrich_Weber": "Ernst Heinrich Weber was a German anatomist and physiologist at Leipzig who was the first to put the senses of the skin to methodical and measuring investigation.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Working with a compass whose ends were ground flat so as to press rather than prick, he mapped across the body the separation at which two touches begin to be felt as one, and by comparing lifted weights he found that the smallest difference noticed stands in a ratio to the standard rather than being fixed.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> With his brother Eduard he also showed that stimulating the vagus nerve slows the heart, the first clear case of a nerve whose action is to inhibit.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>",
"Gustav_Fechner": "Gustav Theodor Fechner was a German professor at Leipzig who founded psychophysics, defining it as an exact doctrine of the relations of dependence between the physical and the psychical world.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> He held the two to be modes of appearance of a single underlying being, and the search for a functional relation between them arose while he was writing a book on that view, issuing in the formula that a sensation goes with the logarithm of the stimulus.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> He also wrote satires as Dr Mises, argued that plants have a soul-life of their own, and founded an aesthetics that settled questions of taste by asking people rather than deducing from principles.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>",
"Charles_Darwin": "Charles Darwin was an English naturalist who sailed aboard HMS Beagle and whose <i>On the Origin of Species</i> of 1859 argued that species change over time, the chief agent of that change being natural selection.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> He foresaw at the close of that book that psychology would rest on a new foundation, the acquirement of each mental power by gradation.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Two later books carried the argument to human beings: the <i>Descent of Man</i> of 1871, which holds the difference in mind between man and the higher animals to be one of degree and not of kind, and <i>The Expression of the Emotions in Man and Animals</i> of 1872.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>",
"Herbert_Spencer": "Herbert Spencer was an English philosopher whose <i>Principles of Psychology</i> of 1855 set out an account of mind as a product of evolution four years before the <i>Origin of Species</i> appeared.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> It defines life as the continuous adjustment of internal relations to external relations and treats mind as the highest reach of that adjustment, handling instinct as compound reflex action and explaining innate structure as experience accumulated across a race.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> He coined the phrase 'survival of the fittest', which Darwin adopted in the later editions of the <i>Origin</i> as more accurate than his own term, crediting him there with laying the foundation on which psychology would rest.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>",
"Francis_Galton": "Francis Galton was an English polymath and early researcher into human heredity, who argued in <i>Hereditary Genius</i> of 1869 that natural ability is inherited and a gifted race could be bred by judicious marriages.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> He coined the word eugenics in 1883 for the science of improving stock, and the movement that grew from it reached the statute book in a British act of 1913 and an American Supreme Court ruling of 1927 upholding enforced sterilisation.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The same work produced lasting statistical tools: setting parents against children gave him regression, the finding that offspring stand nearer the general average than their parents do, and correlation grew out of it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>",
"Psychological_testing": "Psychological testing is the practice of putting people through standardised tasks and scoring the results, so that one person's performance can be set beside another's or against a norm.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The earliest such series, proposed in 1890 under the name mental tests, measured sensory and motor capacities such as grip strength, the two-point threshold, reaction time for sound and the number of letters repeated after one hearing, on the assumption that simple functions would reveal general ability.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> They did not, and a study of 1901 found that these tests correlated little with one another and not with students' marks in their college classes.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>",
"Eugenics": "Eugenics is the attempt to improve a population by controlling who has children, a word coined in 1883 for the science of improving stock and defined to take in every influence tending to favour what its author called the more suitable strains.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> As an organised movement it drew on family studies of inherited defect and later on psychological testing, and it reached legislation: Indiana passed the first compulsory sterilisation law in 1907 and California followed in 1909.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Over the 20th century the health authorities of California alone recommended sterilisation for more than 20,000 people held in state institutions, and comparable programmes ran in other countries.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>",
"Wilhelm_Griesinger": "Wilhelm Griesinger was a German physician and psychiatrist whose textbook <i>Die Pathologie und Therapie der psychischen Krankheiten</i>, published in 1845 and revised in 1861, is generally taken to open the scientific phase of German psychiatry.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> He was among the leading somaticists, who held that mental disorder arises from bodily conditions and is open to ordinary medical study, and his opening section argues that the organ diseased in madness can only be the brain.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> He was not a strict materialist, treating anger, fright and sorrow as frequent immediate causes of insanity, and his conviction that the insane were sick people made him an advocate of humane asylum care.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>",
"Animal_magnetism": "Animal magnetism was the doctrine that a subtle invisible fluid fills the universe and passes between bodies, that illness arises when its flow through a person is blocked, and that an operator can redirect it and restore health.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Advanced from the 1770s by the physician Franz Anton Mesmer, it was applied by passes of the hands, which brought on a crisis of convulsions, laughter or sleep taken for the cure, and it drew a large fashionable following in Paris.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> A French royal commission reported in 1784 that imagination without magnetism produces convulsions while magnetism without imagination produces nothing, and that the existence of the fluid was destitute of proof.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>",
"Moral_treatment": "Moral treatment was a regime for the care of the insane, adopted at the end of the 18th century, that replaced chains, purges and terror with routine, occupation, recreation and a kindly authority.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> It rested on the view that most patients keep a considerable degree of self-command, which can be strengthened by treating them as far as possible as rational beings and by appealing to their desire of esteem rather than to fear.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> It was the founding principle of the public asylums built across Europe and America in the first half of the 19th century, institutions that grew overcrowded and turned to custody as the promise of cure went unmet.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>",
"Wilhelm_Wundt": "Wilhelm Wundt was a German physiologist and philosopher whose <i>Grundzüge der physiologischen Psychologie</i> of 1873 and 1874 announced a borderline science between physiology and psychology, and is usually taken to mark the birth of experimental psychology.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Rooms for his laboratory were set apart at Leipzig in 1879, and the institute that grew from it drew students from around the world and made the new science a discipline with a workforce.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> He held the experimental method to reach the sensory side of mental life and to fail beyond it, assigning the higher processes to <i>Völkerpsychologie</i>, the study of language, myth and custom.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>",
"Mental_chronometry": "Mental chronometry is the measurement of the time course of mental operations, chiefly by recording and comparing how long people take to respond in simple cognitive tasks.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Its founding technique is subtraction, set out by Donders in 1868, who took the time to answer a single unvarying stimulus from the time to answer one of two and called the difference the time required to discriminate.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The assumption that inserting a stage leaves the others unchanged has been disputed since Donders raised it himself, but the method endures, with thousands of papers a year still turning on simple and choice reaction times.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>",
"Structuralism_(psychology)": "Structuralism was a school of experimental psychology, named by Edward Titchener, that set itself to analyse consciousness into its elements on the model of morphology in biology.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> It asked what is present in a conscious state and in what quantity rather than what it is for, admitted sensation and affection as the only elements, and treated an element as nothing apart from attributes such as quality, intensity and duration.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Its method was introspection by trained observers, and the school did not outlive Titchener, since the training never produced agreement about what observers found.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>",
"Edward_B._Titchener": "Edward Titchener was an English psychologist who studied under Wilhelm Wundt at Leipzig and then directed the psychological laboratory at Cornell, where he led the American school of structuralism.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> He held that the method of every science is observation, that the sciences differ in the point of view they take on human experience rather than in their subject matter, and that psychology's business is to analyse a conscious state into its elements rather than ask what those elements are for.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> He built a teaching laboratory, wrote manuals of laboratory practice, trained observers in introspection and defended the method against behaviourism, and the school did not outlast him.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>",
"Imageless_thought": "Imageless thought is thinking that carries no sensory content, neither picture nor sound nor feeling, and whether it occurs was disputed in the first decades of experimental psychology.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Observers at Oswald Külpe's laboratory in Würzburg, set simple tasks of judgment, reported states they could describe by no image at all and which were named <i>Bewusstseinslagen</i>, or conscious attitudes.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Trained observers elsewhere reported finding imagery in the same tasks, and because neither laboratory had any procedure for convicting the other of error the disagreement stood, and is still cited as the standard case against first-person method.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>",
"Washington,_D.C.": "Washington, D.C. is the capital of the United States, laid out on the Potomac on ground ceded by Maryland after Congress passed a Residence Act and President George Washington chose the site.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The French engineer Pierre Charles L'Enfant planned the city, placing the Capitol on a rise at the eastern end of a broad mall, and a competition suggested by Thomas Jefferson eventually produced the building's design; the federal government moved in from Philadelphia in 1800, occupying a Capitol whose north wing alone was habitable.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> British troops burned the Capitol and the President's House in August 1814, and both were rebuilt by the architects who had raised them.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>",
"Islamabad": "Islamabad is the capital of Pakistan, a city built for the purpose on the Potohar Plateau in the north-west of the country, beside the older town of Rawalpindi.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The government was deliberately moved there from Karachi, which had held it since independence, the intention being to set the administrative centre apart from the country's commercial life while keeping it accessible; a Capital Development Authority established in 1960 built the city and still administers it and the master plan drawn for its wider territory.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The United Nations counts roughly 1.1 million people in it, which makes it a small capital for a country of more than 250 million.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>",
"Brasília": "Brasília is the capital of Brazil, a city built for the purpose on the interior plateau and made the seat of government in 1960, when the National Congress moved into the Palácio do Congresso Nacional there.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The seat it replaced was Rio de Janeiro, which had held the government since the Portuguese court crossed the Atlantic in the Napoleonic wars, and kept it through the empire and the republic.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> It is a planned city rather than a grown one, its Congress standing on the Praça dos Três Poderes — the square of the three powers — and the United Nations counts about 4.6 million people in its urban area.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>",
"Addis_Ababa": "Addis Ababa is the capital of Ethiopia and the seat of the African Union, whose Commission — the secretariat that carries out the union's daily work — is based there.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> It was the capital of the one African state that kept its independence through the European partition of the continent, and the interruption came late: Italian forces occupied the city in May 1936, and the American legation, opened there in 1909, was closed the following year and did not reopen until 1943.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> In May 1963 the heads of 32 independent African states met there to sign the charter of the Organisation of African Unity, the continent's first post-independence institution.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>",
"Cairo": "Cairo is the capital of Egypt and, for three years from 1958, was also the capital of the United Arab Republic, the union Egypt formed with Syria before Syria seceded.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The United States kept an Agent and Consul General in the city from 1849, Egyptian foreign relations then running through the Ottoman Sultan at Constantinople and, after the British protectorate of 1882, through London; the post became a legation only on the recognition of Egyptian independence in 1922.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Egypt broke relations with Washington after Israel’s victory in the war of June 1967, and an American interests section worked inside the Spanish embassy there until the embassy reopened in 1974.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>",
"Manila": "Manila is the capital of the Philippines, and the first battle of the Spanish-American War was fought in its bay on 1 May 1898, when Commodore George Dewey’s Asiatic Squadron defeated the Spanish naval force defending the islands.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Spain afterwards agreed at Paris to sell the archipelago to the United States for twenty million dollars, and Filipinos who had been fighting for their independence found it a change of colonial ruler rather than an end to colonial rule, and fought on for more than three years.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The Republic of the Philippines was recognised as independent on 4 July 1946, when the first American ambassador presented his credentials.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>",
"Kinshasa": "Kinshasa is the capital of the Democratic Republic of the Congo and was called Léopoldville when the country became independent of Belgium on 30 June 1960, the day the American embassy opened there.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Within a week soldiers of the Force Publique had mutinied against their Belgian commanders, Belgium had sent troops without asking the new government, and the Security Council had authorised an intervention force and called on the Belgians to withdraw.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The city was one end of the first Cold War proxy confrontation over the newly independent states of Africa: the CIA chief of station there reported a communist takeover, and Washington set about removing Prime Minister Patrice Lumumba.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>",
"Hanoi": "Hanoi is the capital of Vietnam and had an American consulate from 1947, while the country was still part of French Indochina and the Viet Minh and French troops were already at war in the north.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> After the French garrison at Dien Bien Phu fell in 1954 the powers meeting at Geneva divided the country at the 17th parallel, and the United States, not a signatory to those accords, refused to recognise the government here and dealt only with Saigon.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> No American mission stood in the city for the two decades after the war; a liaison office opened on 28 January 1995 and the embassy followed that August.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>",
"Tehran": "Tehran is the capital of Iran and has had an American diplomatic mission since June 1883, when the chargé d’affaires S. G. W. Benjamin presented his credentials to the Shah of Persia.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> It was the setting for the first meeting of the three Allied leaders of the Second World War, Roosevelt, Churchill and Stalin spending four days there at the end of November 1943 settling the timing of the invasion of northern France.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Student militants seized the American embassy and its staff on 4 November 1979; the hostages were freed fifteen months later, relations were severed in 1980, and Switzerland has been the protecting power for American interests ever since.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>",
"Ankara": "Ankara is the capital of Turkey and was called Angora in English when the United States and the republic that had replaced the Ottoman Empire exchanged notes there in February 1927 to restore relations between them.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The American mission had sat at Constantinople for almost a century before that, from David Porter’s arrival as chargé d’affaires in 1831 until the Ottoman government severed relations in April 1917.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The list of American chiefs of mission shows the gap exactly: nobody succeeded Abram Elkus until Joseph Clark Grew presented his credentials in October 1927, a decade later, and every ambassador since has been accredited to the republic.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>",
"Bangkok": "Bangkok is the capital of Thailand and is where the United States and Siam exchanged ratifications, in April 1836, of a treaty of amity and commerce that was the first the Americans made with any Asian country.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The first American legation in the kingdom opened in the city in October 1882, when John A. Halderman presented his credentials as minister resident and consul general, and it was raised to an embassy only in 1947.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> From 1954 the city was the headquarters of the Southeast Asia Treaty Organization, which kept no forces of its own but ran joint exercises each year and paid for fellowships for southeast Asian scholars.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>",
"London": "London is the capital of the United Kingdom and was the seat of the first American mission in Europe, established on 1 June 1785 when John Adams presented his credentials to George III.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The legation was raised to an embassy on 22 June 1893, when Thomas Bayard presented his credentials to the Court of St James, and an American consulate ran in the city separately from 1790 until 1967.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The first session of the United Nations General Assembly opened there on 10 January 1946 and adopted its first resolution, on the discovery of atomic energy, before resuming at Flushing Meadow in New York that year.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>",
"Dodoma": "Dodoma is the official capital of the United Republic of Tanzania, sitting inland on the central plateau several hundred kilometres from the coast where the government still largely works.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The Commonwealth Secretariat records the arrangement by listing two capitals for the country, this one marked official and Dar es Salaam marked acting, while the United Nations Statistics Division gives this city alone.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The United Nations counts about 262,000 people in it for 2025, which makes it far smaller than the coastal city that holds the country's foreign missions and much of its commerce.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>",
"Dar_es_Salaam": "Dar es Salaam is the largest city of the United Republic of Tanzania and its acting capital, the Commonwealth Secretariat listing it that way beside the official capital inland at Dodoma.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> An American consulate general stood in the city before independence and was raised to embassy rank on 9 December 1961, the day President John F. Kennedy congratulated Prime Minister Julius Nyerere on Tanganyika's independence from British trusteeship.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> That embassy and the American embassy at Nairobi were bombed on 7 August 1998, and the Security Council adopted a resolution six days later condemning the attacks in Kenya and Tanzania.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>",
"Paris": "Paris is the capital of France and the city after which the Kellogg-Briand Pact of 27 August 1928 is sometimes named, an agreement to outlaw war that fifteen nations signed there.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> It hosted the conference that produced the Treaty of Versailles at the end of the First World War, and a summit planned there for May 1960 collapsed when the Soviet delegation walked out over an American reconnaissance flight shot down days earlier.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The French government left it for Bordeaux when Germany invaded in May 1940, and the United Nations counts about 11 million people in the city and its surroundings.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>",
"Pretoria": "Pretoria is the administrative capital of South Africa, the city where the President and the Cabinet meet, one of three capitals between which the country divides the branches of its government.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The American legation moved here from Cape Town on 20 June 1930, six months after it was first opened, and Ralph J. Totten presented his credentials as envoy extraordinary and minister plenipotentiary that September.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The two countries' legations were raised to embassies in 1949, when North Winship presented ambassadorial credentials to the Union government, and the United States has kept its mission in the city ever since.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>",
"Cape_Town": "Cape Town is the legislative capital of South Africa, the city in which its parliament sits, one of three capitals between which the country divides the branches of its government.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> It was also the first seat of American diplomacy in the country: a legation was established there on 19 December 1929, when the consul general Ralph J. Totten was appointed minister resident, and he presented his credentials on 18 February 1930.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> That legation was moved inland to Pretoria on 20 June the same year and Totten was promoted with it, so the American mission stayed in the city for only half a year and has never returned to it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>",
"Bloemfontein": "Bloemfontein is the judicial capital of South Africa, the seat of the Supreme Court of Appeal, and that court is the reason it counts among the country’s three capitals.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The Commonwealth Secretariat sets the division out institution by institution, parliament at Cape Town and the President and Cabinet at Pretoria accounting for the other two, while the United Nations records the same arrangement in a footnote to its country profile.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> It is the least visible of the three from abroad, the American record of its own missions in the country naming only the other two cities.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>",
"Nairobi": "Nairobi is the capital of Kenya and became the seat of an independent government on 12 December 1963, when the country emerged from nearly seventy years of British rule; an American embassy opened in the city the following March.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> On 7 August 1998 that embassy was bombed, killing 12 American citizens, 32 locally engaged staff and 247 Kenyan citizens and injuring about 5,000 Kenyans, with the American embassy at Dar es Salaam attacked within minutes.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The Security Council adopted a resolution six days later condemning the attacks in Kenya and Tanzania, and the United Nations counts about 4.6 million people in the city.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>",
"Naypyidaw": "Naypyidaw is the capital of Myanmar and is far smaller than the country it governs, the United Nations counting about 547,000 people in it against nearly 55 million nationally.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The same profile spells the name Nay Pyi Taw in three words, one of several English forms the city goes under.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Foreign missions have not followed the government to it: the United States established relations at Rangoon in September 1947, raising its consulate general there to an embassy some months before the country became formally independent, and its record of those relations names no other city.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>",
"Bogotá": "Bogotá is the capital of Colombia and has been the seat of a government the United States recognises since 19 June 1822, when President James Monroe received Manuel Torres as chargé d'affaires of a federation that then took in today's Colombia, Ecuador, Venezuela and Panama.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The American mission opened in the city on 16 December 1823 and stayed through four changes of the state's name and the departures of Ecuador, Venezuela and Panama, being raised to an embassy in 1939.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The United Nations counts about 10.8 million people there, a figure it footnotes as covering the built-up area running out into Soacha, Chía and Funza.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>",
"Seoul": "Seoul is the capital of South Korea and is named in the document that recognised the state around it, a White House statement of 1 January 1949 recognising the Republic of Korea, with its capital at Seoul, as an independent state.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Diplomatic relations with the United States followed on 25 March that year, when Ambassador John Myun Chang presented his credentials to President Truman.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The United Nations counts just under 10 million people in the city, about a fifth of the country's population, and the state itself took a seat only in September 1991, admitted alongside North Korea in a single resolution.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>",
"Khartoum": "Khartoum is the capital of Sudan, standing where the Blue Nile and the White Nile meet, with about 5.7 million people by the United Nations count.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> It is where the United States recognised the country: on 1 January 1956 the American liaison officer in the city, Arthur Beach, delivered a letter to the president of the Supreme Commission of Sudan, and the liaison office became an embassy under him six weeks later.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> That mission has been interrupted repeatedly, closing in June 1967 when Sudan severed relations, reopening in 1972, suspending operations again in 1996 and resuming them only in May 2002.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>",
"Kampala": "Kampala is the capital and largest city of Uganda, on hills a short way north of Lake Victoria, with an urban population of about 3.1 million by the United Nations count, roughly one Ugandan in sixteen.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> It became the seat of an independent government in 1962, when the country left British rule and joined the Commonwealth, and the American consulate general there was raised to embassy rank on 9 October.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> That embassy closed on 10 November 1973 after repeated public threats against its officials and the expulsion of its Marine guard, American interests being represented by the Federal Republic of Germany until it reopened on 18 June 1979.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>",
"Madrid": "Madrid is the capital and largest city of Spain, on the Meseta at the centre of the Iberian peninsula, with an urban population of about 6.6 million by the United Nations count.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> It is where Spain recognised the United States, on 20 February 1783, by receiving William Carmichael as chargé d'affaires — one act the American record treats as recognition, the opening of relations and the founding of the legation.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> That mission closed on 21 April 1898 when Spain severed relations at the start of the Spanish-American War, returned the next year, became an embassy in 1913, and left again in the civil war for Barcelona and then France.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>",
"Algiers": "Algiers is the capital and largest city of Algeria, standing on the Mediterranean below the Atlas mountains, with an urban population of about 2.7 million by the United Nations count.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Before the French conquest of 1830 it was a nominal Ottoman vassal that conducted its own foreign relations, and it recognised the United States by the Treaty of Peace and Amity signed there on 5 September 1795, whose original was in Turkish.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The American consulate opened in 1796, closed with the conquest, and became an embassy on 29 September 1962 after independence; Algeria severed relations in June 1967 and both embassies reopened in November 1974.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>",
"Baghdad": "Baghdad is the capital and largest city of Iraq, on the Tigris above its junction with the Euphrates, with an urban population of about 7.0 million by the United Nations count.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> An American consul was appointed to the city in 1888, while it was still Ottoman, and a legation opened in 1931, the year after the United States recognised Iraq by treaty; it became an embassy in 1946.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> That mission has broken three times since: Iraq severed relations in June 1967, an interests section stood in the Belgian embassy from 1972 until an embassy returned in 1984, and it closed again in 1991 and reopened only in June 2004.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>",
"Buenos_Aires": "Buenos Aires is the capital and largest city of Argentina, standing on the southern bank of the Río de la Plata, with an urban population of about 15.1 million by the United Nations count, roughly a third of the country.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The United States recognised the government of the city itself, rather than a country, on 27 January 1823, appointing a minister plenipotentiary to a state that had declared independence from Spain in 1816.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The American legation left the city for Paraná in 1857, when the Centralists here stood apart from the Argentine Confederation, and returned in 1862 after the country was reunified; it became an embassy in 1914.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>",
"Kabul": "Kabul is the capital and largest city of Afghanistan, lying in a valley of the Hindu Kush, with an urban population of about 4.1 million by the United Nations count, roughly one Afghan in eleven.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The United States recognised Afghanistan in 1921 but opened a legation in the city only in 1942, raising it to an embassy in 1948; its ambassador there, Adolph Dubs, was assassinated at post in February 1979.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> That embassy closed in January 1989 as Soviet forces withdrew, returned as a liaison office in December 2001 after the city fell to a coalition campaign, became an embassy again in January 2002, and suspended operations in August 2021.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>",
"Ottawa": "Ottawa is the capital of Canada, standing on the Ottawa River where it forms the boundary with Quebec, with an urban population of about 1.4 million by the United Nations count, roughly one Canadian in 30.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> It did not receive a foreign mission for six decades after the Dominion of Canada was formed in 1867, because the country's external affairs were controlled from London until the United Kingdom acknowledged Canadian autonomy in 1926.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The American legation there opened on 1 June 1927, a few months after recognition, and was raised to embassy rank on 19 November 1943; relations have run unbroken since.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>",
"Sana'a": "Sana'a is the recognised capital and largest city of Yemen, in the highlands above the Red Sea coast, with an urban population of about 2.9 million by the United Nations count.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> It became the capital of the Yemen Arab Republic in the 1960s, and the American embassy moved to it from Taiz, the older capital, in 1966; relations were severed in June 1967 and the mission reopened in July 1972.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Yemen's United Nations seat dates from 30 September 1947, the northern state's admission carried through the union of north and south announced in May 1990, and the Security Council has acted on the war there since 2015.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>",
"Rabat": "Rabat is the capital of Morocco, standing on the Atlantic at the mouth of the Bou Regreg, with an urban population of about 1.9 million by the United Nations count, roughly one Moroccan in 21.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> An American consulate opened there on 25 June 1881, one of eight the United States maintained in the country at various times, of which only Casablanca now remains besides this one.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> It became an embassy city on 11 June 1956, three months after the United States recognised Moroccan independence, and the older legation at Tangier was reduced to a consulate general the same day.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>",
"Luanda": "Luanda is the capital and largest city of Angola, standing on the Atlantic coast in the north-west of the country, and it holds about 8 million people, roughly a fifth of the national population.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> It grew as the seat of Portuguese administration in Angola and as the colony's chief Atlantic port during the centuries of the slave trade, in which the territory had become the largest source of enslaved people shipped to the Americas by the 19th century.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Portuguese rule ended in 1975, when independence was granted amid fighting between rival nationalist movements and the Portuguese left the city, and it has been the seat of government since.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>",
"Kyiv": "Kyiv is the capital and largest city of Ukraine, standing on the Dnieper in the north of the country, and it holds about three million people by both the United Nations and World Bank counts.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The state governed from it held a United Nations seat from 24 October 1945, taken while it was a Soviet republic, so its membership of the organisation preceded international recognition of its independence by 46 years.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> An American embassy opened in the city on 23 January 1992, a month after recognition, and in 1994 the government seated here signed the memorandum by which Ukraine gave up the nuclear weapons on its territory.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>",
"Neolithic_China": "Neolithic China is the phase of the East Asian past in which cultivated crops, domestic animals and settled villages took hold, and stone tools were finished by grinding rather than flaking alone.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Its cultures fall into two groups: those of the Yellow River valley in the north, where the millets were the staple, and those of the Yangtze valley in the south, where rice was, divided by the Qinling mountains and the Huai River.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Pottery there is older than farming, about sixteen sites having yielded vessels made between roughly 20,000 and 10,000 years ago, while the Early Neolithic proper is dated from about 7000 BCE by the first domesticated grain.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>",
"Origins_of_Chinese_agriculture": "The origins of Chinese agriculture are the transition from foraging to farming on the East Asian mainland, which produced its own domesticates: rice, the two millets, pigs, dogs and chickens.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> It began at the Pleistocene–Holocene boundary<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> and was among the slowest such changes known, the millets being domesticated between about 8300 and 4300 BCE<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> and wild plants losing their dominant place in the diet only about 6,000 to 5,000 years ago.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Whether farming started once, in the Yangtze, and spread, or in many places at almost the same time is argued, and broomcorn millet’s wild ancestor is unidentified.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>",
"Millet_and_rice_farming": "Millet and rice farming are the two crop systems of early China: millet sown on the dry loess of the north from about 5800 BCE,<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> and rice grown in standing water in the south.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The rice fields had to be made — the oldest, about 6,000 years old, were pits in freshwater wetland with simple water controls, and over the next two thousand years they became bunded rectangular paddies laid out for irrigation and drainage.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The two overlapped rather than dividing the country: both crops were grown together on the Middle Yellow River by about 7,800 years ago, and rice later travelled south along the coast and the rivers.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>",
"Jiahu": "Jiahu is an early Neolithic village site in central Henan, on the upper reaches of the Huai, lived in from about 9,000 to 7,500 years ago.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> It has yielded the earliest domesticated rice known in northern China, residues of a fermented drink of rice, honey and fruit from the 7th millennium BCE, silk protein in graves 8,500 years old, and marks that have been read, questioningly, as the earliest Chinese pictographic writing.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Some thirty flutes cut from the ulnae of red-crowned cranes were found there, most of them drilled with seven finger holes, and the best preserved still plays.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>",
"Banpo": "Banpo is a Neolithic settlement on the Guanzhong Plain at Xi'an in Shaanxi, China, lived in by farmers of the Yangshao culture between roughly 4800 and 4300 BCE.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Its house floors carry broad tracts of red burnt earth, fired deliberately to harden and warm them,<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> and earthen structures in the middle of the settlement have been read as pens in which the village kept its pigs communally rather than household by household.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The name has since been extended to the Banpo phase, the opening stage of the Yangshao culture, marked by pointed-bottom bottles and round-bottomed basins,<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> and to the Banpo culture, whose cemeteries are found across the Guanzhong region.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>",
"Hongshan_culture": "The Hongshan culture is a Neolithic culture of the Liao and Daling river valleys in northeastern China, dated between about 4500 and 3000 BCE and among the earliest complex societies of East Asia.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> It is known for low ceremonial platforms of circular or square tiers, sometimes set with hundreds of painted pottery cylinders, whose burial crypts held offerings almost entirely of carved jade and few everyday goods, and for the wattle-and-daub building at Niuheliang that housed a life-size female statue.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Its villages farmed millet and kept pigs and sheep, and formed small districts of a few hundred people with no sign that any one of them ruled the rest.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>",
"Liangzhu_culture": "The Liangzhu culture is a late Neolithic culture of the Yangtze delta in China, lasting from about 3300 to 2300 BCE and best known for the walled city it raised near what is now Hangzhou, one of the earliest in East Asia.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Around and beyond that city its people built dams, levees and some 30 kilometres of moats and canals to keep water off the low ground and feed the rice fields, the earliest formal waterworks known in China, and they buried their leading dead with finely carved jade.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The city was abandoned in an unusually wet spell around 2400 BCE and buried under a metre of clay.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>",
"Liangzhu_jade": "Liangzhu jade is the worked nephrite buried with the dead of the Liangzhu culture of the Yangtze delta in China, made between about 3300 and 2300 BCE.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Its two chief forms are the <i>cong</i>, a tube square outside and hollow within, often carved at the corners with a figure over a beast mask, and the <i>bi</i>, a wide plain disc with a small central hole; the two together furnish a high-ranking grave, the largest discs laid on the chest.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Nephrite is too tough to carve, so pieces were worn to shape with bamboo drills, water and quartz sand, and cords drawn under an abrasive paste.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>",
"Chinese_Neolithic_pottery": "Chinese Neolithic pottery is the fired clay of China's long Neolithic, a craft older there than farming and first made by foragers rather than farmers.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The earliest pots were pinched by hand from coarse clay tempered with crushed quartz or calcite, their walls up to 3.6 centimetres (1.4 inches) thick and fired at about 600 to 700 °C (1,100 to 1,300 °F); slab building and coiling followed, and later potters used a turning wheel and kilns of wood and mud.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> By the early Neolithic vessels were being shaped for particular jobs, and the wares of each region are still what archaeologists use to tell one culture of the period from another.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>",
"Central_Plain": "The Central Plain, or Zhongyuan, is the region of the middle and lower Yellow River around Henan in China, which tradition makes the seat of the first dynasty and the source from which civilisation spread across the country.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Archaeology has complicated that account: complex societies with monuments, walled towns and richly furnished burials arose in several regions at once, some of them well before Erlitou, the 400-hectare site on the plain whose sequence carries into the Bronze Age.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The plain's distinction is now held to be less that it began first than that its line of settlement ran on unbroken into the Shang.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>",
"Shaokang": "Shaokang is a king of the Xia in the traditional Chinese account, remembered for recovering the dynasty a generation after usurpers had taken it and its ruler Xiang had been killed.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The story is told in the Zuo Commentary: his mother escaped through a hole in the wall while pregnant and bore him in her own state, where he kept the herds and later served as a cook to escape pursuit.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Given a town ten <i>li</i> square and five hundred men, he gathered what remained of the Xia people, destroyed the usurper’s two sons and sacrificed once more to the dynasty’s founder, though the <i>Shiji</i> records none of it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>",
"Tribute_of_Yu": "Tribute of Yu is a chapter of the <i>Book of Documents</i> that describes the ordering of the land after a great flood, and is the oldest geographical treatise in the Chinese tradition.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> It divides the country into nine provinces and treats each in the same order — its limits, the works done in it, its soil, the tribute it owes and the roads that tribute travels — before turning to nine mountain ranges and nine watercourses.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> It closes with a scheme of concentric squares five hundred <i>li</i> wide reaching out to the wild lands, and though placed under Yu it names him only twice, at its opening and its close.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>",
"Qi_of_Xia": "Qi of Xia is the second king of the traditional Chinese king list, the son of Yu, who took his place by inheritance where earlier rulers had named successors from outside the family.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Yu had named his minister Yi to follow him, but at the end of the mourning the lords went to Qi as their sovereign’s son, and it is that inheritance rather than Yu’s reign that makes the Xia a dynasty.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> His reign is remembered for a battle at Kan, before which, tradition says, he made the speech preserved in the <i>Book of Documents</i>, charging his enemy with abandoning the calendar and calling the war Heaven’s punishment.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>",
"Jie_of_Xia": "Jie of Xia is the last Xia king of the traditional Chinese account and the first of the bad last rulers on whom the dynastic cycle turns.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The lords had been falling away for four reigns, and Jie is said to have cared nothing for virtue but to have terrorised his people until they would not bear him; he imprisoned Tang, the lord who would supplant him, then released him.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Tang raised an army, drove him to Mingtiao andk the throne; the speech Tang made beforehand survives in the <i>Book of Documents</i>, quoting Jie’s subjects wishing the sun would expire and saying they would perish with it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>",
"Songs_of_the_Five_Sons": "Songs of the Five Sons is a chapter of the <i>Book of Documents</i> in which the five brothers of the Xia king Taikang lament his lost throne.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Taikang is said to have hunted beyond the Lo for a hundred days without returning, and a rival lord to have barred his way home, whereupon his brothers put their ancestor Yu’s Cautions into songs.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The first carries the line most often quoted from the collection, that the people are the root of a country and the root being firm the country is tranquil; the chapter belongs to the disputed half of the <i>Documents</i>, presented to the throne in 97 BCE.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>",
"Bamboo_Annals": "Bamboo Annals is a chronicle of early China running from the Yellow Emperor down to 298 BCE, recovered in 279 CE when robbers opened a king of Wei's grave and found tablets carrying more than 100,000 words.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Fifteen works came out of that tomb; most were legend, but the annals and a copy of the <i>Book of Changes</i> were transcribed into current script over two years, the tablets having been damaged and disordered in the finding.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Their chronology differs from the received one, opening the Xia in 1989 BCE rather than 2205, and they have been called both a forgery and, where they disagree with the classics, the better witness.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>",
"Doubting_Antiquity_School": "Doubting Antiquity School was a movement in Chinese historical scholarship of the 1920s that examined the early dynasties as evidence and questioned whether the first of them existed.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Its founder observed that the later a source is, the longer the legendary age it describes — the Western Zhou knew only Yu, Confucius two sage rulers before him, the Warring States older kings still — and concluded that the record had grown at the front.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> It published seven volumes of <i>Debating Ancient History</i>, weeding out entries it judged unauthentic; later Chinese scholarship has moved the other way, and the argument over the first dynasty is not settled.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>",
"Erlitou_site": "Erlitou site is a Bronze Age settlement in the Yiluo basin of western Henan, occupied from about 1750 to 1530 BCE and the largest of its time in the Central Plain.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> It grew from over 100 hectares to about 400 at its height, acquiring a planned core in its second phase: a palace and temple district of 10.8 hectares ringed by four intersecting roads, with a walled bronze and turquoise workshop beside it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> It never had an outer wall, its public building came to a fraction of the older centre at Taosi, and its burials fall into four or five grades, the richest holding bronze found at no other site.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>",
"Erlitou_bronze": "<b>Erlitou bronze</b> is the metalwork produced at the Erlitou site in Henan, China, and it includes the earliest cast bronze ritual vessels known in the country, appearing from about 1700 BCE.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Metal had been worked in China for centuries beforehand as ornaments, small tools and weapons, but the multipiece mould-casting used at Erlitou allowed shapes and surfaces that a single stone mould could not give.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The foundry occupied about a hectare in the south of the site, enclosed in rammed earth with a turquoise workshop, and yielded slag, crucibles and clay moulds for vessels up to 36 centimetres (14 inches) across the rim.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>",
"Xia–Shang–Zhou_Chronology_Project": "<b>Xia–Shang–Zhou Chronology Project</b> is a Chinese state research programme that ran from 1996 to 2000, bringing some 200 specialists in history, astronomy, archaeology and radiocarbon dating together to fix absolute dates for the three dynasties preceding the imperial age.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Firm Chinese chronology had begun only in 841 BCE, and at least 44 dates had been proposed for the Zhou conquest of the Shang, from 1130 to 1018.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The table it published in November 2000 dated that conquest to 1046 BCE and set the Xia within a framework of 2070 to 1600 BCE, a result debated for its government backing and for how much precision the evidence will bear.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>",
"Basic_Annals_of_Yin": "<b>Basic Annals of Yin</b> is the third chapter of the <i>Shiji</i>, the history Sima Qian completed in about 100 BCE, and the fullest connected account of the Shang dynasty any transmitted Chinese text gives.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> It runs from the ancestor Xie to the last king, and it carries the dynasty’s two names, Shang from the land granted to Xie and Yin from the place Pan Geng moved his people to.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Its historian names his own sources at the close, the sacrificial odes for Xie and the Documents and the Odes thereafter, and his frame is moral, the mandate of Heaven passing to Tang and away from the 31st king.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>",
"Tang_of_Shang": "<b>Tang of Shang</b> is the founder of the Shang dynasty in the traditional Chinese account, the lord of Bo who overthrew the last Xia king and took the mandate of Heaven from him.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Tang is a posthumous title glossed as the one who puts away cruelty and suppresses the tyrant; the annals give his own name as Tian Yi and make him the fourteenth in descent from the ancestor Xie.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The stories told of him turn on restraint: coming upon a hunter’s nets drawn across all four sides of a plain, he took three of them away, and the lords said his virtue reached the birds and the beasts.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>",
"Discovery_of_the_oracle_bones": "The <b>discovery of the oracle bones</b> is the recognition, in 1899, that bones sold and ground up as medicine in China carried writing, and that the writing was the Shang dynasty’s own.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Before it the Shang was a literary tradition, known from texts composed a thousand years and more after the events, with no Chinese writing surviving from any earlier time.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The pieces were traced to Anyang, where excavation began in 1928; about 160,000 inscribed fragments have since been recovered, carrying more than 4,600 distinct characters of which only about a thousand have been deciphered.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>",
"Shang_divination": "<b>Shang divination</b> is the practice by which the late Shang court put questions to its ancestors and to the powers, by burning bone until it cracked and reading the crack.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Cattle shoulder blades and turtle plastrons were hollowed on the back so that heat applied there split the face in a controlled way, and beside the crack was cut the day, the diviner who tested it, and the proposition put.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Diviners worked in named groups, and the kings divined continually to settle that offerings to the dead were right in kind, number, colour and combination.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>",
"Wu_Ding": "<b>Wu Ding</b> is the Shang king with whom the dynasty’s own written record begins, the 22nd of its line, reigning by the standard chronology from about 1250 to 1192 BCE.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> No inscription found at Anyang names any of his three predecessors there, so the site’s writing and his reign start together, and five lunar eclipses recorded by his diviners have been calculated to a window between 1201 and 1181 BCE.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The transmitted account makes him the king who revived the dynasty, keeping silence for three years until a dream showed him the sage he then found among convicts and made his counsellor.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>",
"Tomb_of_Fu_Hao": "<b>tomb of Fu Hao</b> is burial M5 at Xiaotun in Anyang, China, the grave of a consort of the Shang king Wu Ding, lying away from the royal cemetery at Xibeigang and small beside its graves.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> It held about 1,500 objects, among them weapons, bells, blades, mirrors, bronze tigers and a great quantity of jade, some of it made by Neolithic cultures and already ancient when it was buried.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Neither the count nor the name is settled, since one study gives 468 bronzes and another 210, the two almost certainly counting different things, and several scholars read the owner’s graph as Zi rather than Hao.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>",
"Shang_kingship": "<b>Shang kingship</b> is the authority the Shang king held over the ruling house, its armies and the rites owed to the royal dead, power passing from a king to his brothers or his sons.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The king stood at the head of a society ordered by kinship, leading its apex lineage rather than holding an office over subjects, and what raised him above every other head of a family was his monopoly of approach to the royal ancestors.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> His delegates were mostly princes graded by military or official rank, and the emblems on their bronzes have lately been re-read as the names of some 70 government offices.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>",
"Shangdi": "<b>Shangdi</b> is the high god of the Shang, a name meaning 'High Sovereign', known first from the oracle-bone inscriptions, where he is sovereign over the natural world and over the ancestral spirits.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> He granted the rain a crop needed and held a river's flood short of ruin, but stood so far from the living that no one could approach him directly, a request being laid before the ancestors to carry up.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The king alone could receive his spirit and rule as his surrogate, though whether Di was a single god is unsettled, the graph being used of heads of lineages in the plural.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>",
"Shang_ancestor_worship": "<b>Shang ancestor worship</b> is the cult the Shang paid to their own dead, held to have belonged to people of every rank, its work being to make the dead into proper ancestors who would then guide the nature powers and the high god.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> It took several forms at once, among them burials, sacrificial pits, architecture, writing and feasting, and the rites ran on a calendar addressed to high ancestors, dead rulers and consort queens alike.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Cattle were its chief offering, and since far fewer lie in the pits than the inscriptions record being given, the living seem to have eaten them at the feast.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>",
"Human_sacrifice": "<b>human sacrifice</b> is the killing of a person as an offering, known across many societies and undertaken to mark a royal funeral, to answer a disaster, to atone for a wrong, to consecrate a building, or to secure fertility and health.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Victims were also killed to serve a high-ranking person as retainers in the next world, so one offering may be commemorative, expiatory, propitiatory and dedicatory at the same time.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The bodies are what the practice is now studied from, and where preservation or ancient DNA allows, victims can be aged, sexed and traced to a population, which has overturned identifications made from the bones alone.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>",
"Xibeigang": "<b>Xibeigang</b> is the royal cemetery of the late Shang, the group of largest burials at Anyang, lying north-west of the settlement and set apart from it, and their number identifies nine kings as having ruled at Yinxu.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The tombs are shaft graves cut with four ramps running out to the cardinal directions, a layout belonging to the ritual rather than to the convenience of digging.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Many were looted in antiquity and the royal ones emptied over and over, though M1004 still held two monumental four-legged ding at the very bottom and M260 gave up the tallest and heaviest bronze the Shang ever cast.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>",
"Piece-mould_casting": "<b>Piece-mould casting</b> is a founding method in which a clay model is enclosed in an outer mould built of several fitted sections, and molten metal is poured into the space between the two.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Metal spacers hold the model and the mould apart while the metal goes in, and a large vessel may be built up in several pours, its parts cast separately and then cast onto one another.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Mould sections carrying ornament have been found, so the decoration was made in the mould rather than worked onto the finished metal, and the method set what shapes were possible.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>",
"Ding_(vessel)": "<b>ding</b> is the Chinese ritual cauldron, standing on three legs or four, and among Shang bronzes the one vessel used specifically to prepare and offer meat to the dead.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> In the oracle-bone inscriptions its graph works as a verb of sacrifice, taking the king as subject, a dead king or queen as indirect object and the offering itself as direct object, and burnt animal bone has been found inside examples taken from burials.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Four-legged ding were far rarer than three-legged ones and belonged to the ruling class and its heirs, and out of that scarcity grew the myth that made the vessel the token of a dynasty's right to rule.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>",
"Shang_bronze_decoration": "<b>Shang bronze decoration</b> is the dense animal ornament cast into the surface of ritual vessels, its commonest motif a face of beasts with open fanged mouths whose horns mark them as plant-eaters.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Much of it is set as a split image, a face seen from the front resolving into an animal in profile on either side, and it was cut into the mould rather than worked on the finished metal.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> What the creatures mean is disputed: the reading that they show the animals given in sacrifice fails on the list, since buffalo, tigers, owls and elephants were almost never offered, and what they share instead is wildness.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>",
"Shang_jade": "<b>Shang jade</b> is the worked stone of the Shang, made into weapons and sceptres far more than into vessels, the commonest forms being blades called zhang, taken to be sceptres, along with ge and yue axes, some of them inscribed with clan insignia.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Nothing shows the weapons were ever used, and what marks them as ceremonial is their decoration, birds and animal masks cut in fine incisions.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> More than 2,000 pieces are known from Yinxu, where two houses in the palace precinct held over 600 unfinished objects and 260 grinding stones, though the stone itself had to be carried in from far away.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>",
"Shang_chariot": "<b>Shang chariot</b> is the light horse-drawn vehicle that appears at Anyang and at no earlier Chinese centre on present evidence, a western origin having long been proposed for it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The elite are found riding in the hunt, a large-scale event closely paralleling warfare and run with dogs, nets, fire and beaters, and the bones from the palace area match the wild game the oracle bones record the king killing.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The vehicle went into the ground with its owner, the large tombs at Anyang being frequently accompanied by chariot pits, where more than four in five graves at the site are small pits with a coffin and a few pots.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>",
"Shang_warfare": "<b>Shang warfare</b> is the campaigning of the Shang king and his allies against the polities the oracle bones call fang, a relational label rather than an ethnic one, since a group counted as fang while it was an enemy and lost the word once it became an ally.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Force was raised rather than kept, noblemen being sent to levy troops from friendly cities, and the scale varied with the enemy: 3,000 to 5,000 men against most fang, but 13,000 in one campaign against the Qiang.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The wars were fought for hegemony and for resources, and they ended with the Zhou, whose alliance included the Qiang.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>",
"Shang_capitals": "<b>Shang capitals</b> are the successive seats of the Shang court, semipermanent cities serving as centres of political, military and religious power, among them Yanshi and Zhengzhou early, a walled town at Huanbei in the middle period, and Yinxu at the end.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Which excavated site answers to which name in the transmitted record is unsettled, Zhengzhou having been argued to be both the first Shang capital and the second.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Why the court moved is argued too, between chasing sources of metal, planting fortified outposts in the periphery, and using relocation itself as an instrument of policy.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>",
"Zhengzhou_Shang_City": "<b>Zhengzhou Shang City</b> is a walled Bronze Age settlement in central Henan, China, covering about 24 square kilometres (9 square miles) inside two circuits of rammed earth, the inner one enclosing a few dozen foundations of 100 to 2,000 square metres taken for palaces and temples.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Two bronze foundries lie in the outer city, one of them at Nanguanwai, and across some 0.8 hectares have yielded crucibles, slag, clay moulds, copper ore and the remains of smelting furnaces.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Three caches of monumental four-legged ding were buried there in adapted wells and pits, and the site is identified with one of the early Shang capitals the later texts name.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>",
"Panlongcheng": "<b>Panlongcheng</b> is a Bronze Age settlement in Hubei, in the middle Yangtze valley of China, whose wall encloses 7.5 hectares (19 acres) within a much larger site variously measured at 64 and 110 hectares.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> It stands close to abundant copper deposits and has produced evidence of bronze making, though only of weapons and tools cast in stone moulds, while the bronze vessels in its elite burials resemble those of the Central Plain.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> It is usually read as an outpost planted near metal sources, secured for the networks along which copper moved rather than to control the mining itself.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>",
"Shang_agriculture": "<b>Shang agriculture</b> was the dry farming of the two millets on the loess plains of northern China, the crops that region had grown since the Neolithic and the base on which the Shang state rested.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Wheat and barley, brought from south-west Asia after 2000 BCE, appear at Shang sites in quantity for the first time in central China, but their part in what people actually ate stayed minimal until the Zhou.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Late in the dynasty a cooler, drier climate was met with deforestation and multicropping, marked by a sharp rise in the number of stone knives and sickles.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>",
"Shang_countryside": "The <b>Shang countryside</b> is the settled landscape outside the Shang capitals in Bronze Age China — villages, hamlets and lesser regional centres — of which very little has been excavated.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The early Shang pattern includes medium-sized centres placed at the main water-transport nodes near metal sources, while for the late Shang the evidence rests largely on one nearly complete village, Guandimiao in Henan, whose 22 houses and 23 kilns produced pottery far beyond local need.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The polity such settlements composed is read less as a bounded territory than as a network of lineages with the king at the head of the senior one.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>",
"Shang_calendar": "The <b>Shang calendar</b> is the reckoning of time behind the oracle-bone inscriptions of Bronze Age China, and it counts days rather than years, those inscriptions carrying day notations and nothing else.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> A day is named by pairing ten signs called the heavenly stems with twelve called the earthly branches, each taken in turn, so the pairs run out at sixty and begin again; the ten stems also give a week of ten days, the xun.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> There is no era count in the system, which was not applied to numbering years until the Han dynasty, so a date on a bone has to be recovered from astronomy instead.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>",
"Shang_writing": "<b>Shang writing</b> is the earliest Chinese writing, surviving as oracle-bone inscriptions incised into shell and bone and as inscriptions on cast bronze, the latter often written into the soft clay of the mould itself.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> It is the ancestor of the characters written today, Chinese being the world’s only primary script still in continuous use, and the stages between are conventionally counted as five: oracle bone, bronze, seal, clerical and regular.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The script spread beyond China as well, and wooden tablets from the Korean kingdom of Paekche carry both practice at individual characters and attempts at composition in Sinitic.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>",
"Shang_neighbours": "The <b>Shang neighbours</b> are the peoples and polities surrounding the Shang state in Bronze Age China, known chiefly from materials that moved rather than from written description.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> A rare, highly radiogenic lead supplied over half the lead used from the Yellow River to the Yangtze, appearing at nearly the same moment in the Central Plain and at sites in Sichuan, Hubei and Jiangxi before vanishing under the Zhou, and its source is still argued over.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Carnelian buried in Sichuan came from more than 1,000 kilometres (600 miles) north, placing several regions in one exchange sphere between 1500 and 1000 BCE.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>",
"Shang_bronze": "<b>Shang bronze</b> is an alloy of copper with tin and lead, cast in enormous quantity by the late Shang state, whose capital at Anyang was among the largest consumers of metal anywhere in Eurasia in the 2nd millennium BCE.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Its chemistry follows rank: the ritual vessels of the highest elite were alloyed from very pure copper with tin in controlled amounts, while metal further down the order was remelted and mixed, and lead was added deliberately at around 2 to 5 per cent to lower the melting point.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Much of that lead is unusually radiogenic and appears suddenly around 1400 BCE, its source still disputed.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>",
"Cowrie_shell": "A <b>cowrie shell</b> is the smooth oval shell of a marine snail of the family Cypraeidae, an animal that can draw its mantle over the outside of the shell and that belongs to the tropical Indo-Pacific, ranging from the east coast of Africa to Polynesia.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Coastal peoples have harvested the animals for food and used the shells in handicraft and as currency.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Seashells recovered from inland graves are non-local material and are read as evidence of long-distance trade, and in studies that cost grave goods by the labour they represent a marine shell is rated well above a freshwater one.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>",
"Shang_music": "<b>Shang music</b> is known from the instruments buried with the dead in Bronze Age China, chief among them the bronze bell called nao, which came in graded sets of three.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Instruments are a small part of what a Shang grave holds: at one Anyang cemetery they are absent from the bronzes of the earliest period and then run between about 5 and 7 per cent of them, a share that stays low throughout.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Under the following Zhou dynasty the number of bells a person might own, like the number of vessels, was fixed by rank.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>",
"Shang_craft_workshops": "<b>Shang craft workshops</b> are the production quarters of the late Shang capital in Bronze Age China, which grew as social stratification intensified and the division of labour increased.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Their commoners appear to have been sorted into occupational groups living in neighbourhoods segregated by trade, identified from the workshops beside them and, at one site, from the casting tools buried with the dead.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Skill within them ran as a continuum rather than a divide, from expedient tools anybody could make, through the careful work of a local specialist, to objects mass-produced from cattle bone and distributed far beyond the city where they were cut.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>",
"Shang_burial_practice": "<b>Shang burial practice</b> is how the dead were buried under the Shang in Bronze Age China, following one formula in more or less detail, poorer graves simplifying the rich standard rather than differing in kind.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The body lay on its back, extended, in a rectangular shaft oriented north–south, usually within a timber chamber holding one or two coffins, a small pit beneath and a ledge round it, the shaft refilled and rammed layer by layer.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Rank showed in the shaft, the greatest tombs having up to four ramps and lesser ones none, a range running on into the graves of non-Shang peoples.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>",
"Sacramento": "Sacramento is the capital of California and its sixth largest city, with about 536,000 residents<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>, standing in the Central Valley where the American River joins the Sacramento.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> It grew out of Sutter's Fort, built in 1839 on Nisenan land under a Mexican grant<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>, and was founded as a town in December 1848 by John A. Sutter Jr.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>, months after a gold strike at nearby Coloma set off the gold rush.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Enriched as the distribution point for the gold country, it became the terminus of the stagecoach and riverboat lines, the telegraph, the pony express and the first transcontinental railroad, and has been the state capital since 1854.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>",
"Hawaii": "Hawaii is a volcanic island chain in the central Pacific and the only American state outside North America, with about 1.45 million people<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> on 28,313 square kilometres (10,932 square miles) of territory, most of it sea rather than land.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Settled long before any written record by people who divided the land into mountain-to-sea holdings called ahupuaʻa, the islands were united under Kamehameha I by 1810 and formed an independent kingdom the United States recognised by treaty in 1826.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Queen Liliʻuokalani was forced from power in January 1893<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>, the islands were annexed in 1898 and organised as a territory in 1900, and Hawaii became the 50th state on 21 August 1959.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>",
"Austin": "Austin is the capital of Texas, on the Colorado River at the eastern edge of the Hill Country, with just under a million residents.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> It was founded in 1839, when the Republic of Texas chose the site for a permanent capital and named it for Stephen F. Austin, and became the state capital in law on 19 February 1846<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>, having kept the government through the Archives War of 1842.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Its granite Capitol, paid for with three million acres of public land, was dedicated in 1888<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>, and the University of Texas, opened here in 1883, later drew the electronics industry to a city built on government and learning.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>",
"Michigan": "Michigan is an American state bordered by four of the five Great Lakes<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>, so that of its 250,487 square kilometres (96,714 square miles) almost 99,000 are lake water rather than land<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>; some 10.1 million people live there.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Copper has been worked on its Keweenaw Peninsula for more than 7,000 years, first by Indigenous people and then in an 1800s mineral rush<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>, and Michigan entered the Union as a free state on 26 January 1837.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> From the 1900s Detroit and the towns around it became the centre of the world's motor industry, where the modern assembly line made cars affordable and built both a middle class and a labour movement.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>",
"Tallahassee": "Tallahassee is the capital of the American state of Florida, in the north-western panhandle<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>, and with about 205,000 residents it is only the ninth largest of its cities.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Its site was Anhaica, the chief town of the Apalachee, where Hernando de Soto wintered in 1539, and from 1656 to 1704 the neighbouring mission of San Luis was the western capital of Spanish Florida before Carolina raiders destroyed it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The town itself was laid out after the United States took Florida in 1821, as a road was cut between the two old colonial capitals of Pensacola and St Augustine, and it has held the government ever since.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>",
"Louisiana": "Louisiana is an American state at the mouth of the Mississippi, covering 135,659 square kilometres (52,378 square miles) of which nearly a fifth is water<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>, with about 4.6 million residents<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>, New Orleans its largest city and Baton Rouge its capital.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Held by Spain from 1762 and returned to France in 1800, it passed to the United States in 1803 in the Louisiana Purchase, 530 million acres for $15 million<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>, and joined the Union in 1812 as the eighteenth state.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Named by Robert de La Salle after Louis XIV, it carries a Cajun culture and a Creole culture of mixed French, African American and Native American heritage.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>",
"Juneau": "Juneau is the capital of the American state of Alaska, on the Gastineau Channel at the foot of Mount Roberts, and one of only two state capitals that cannot be reached by road.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> About 31,600 people live in the city and borough, which makes it the third largest place in Alaska.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> It began in 1880 as a gold camp, when Joe Juneau and Richard Harris found gold on the creek they named Gold Creek<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>; the placers gave out but the quartz lode beneath became two of the world's most famous hard-rock mines, and the town they paid for took the capital from Sitka, holding it from 1906.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>",
"Maine": "Maine is the largest of the New England states and the north-eastern corner of the United States<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>, covering 91,633 square kilometres (35,380 square miles)<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> with about 1.4 million residents<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>, its capital at Augusta and its largest city at Portland.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Pierre Dugua's French expedition wintered on Saint Croix Island in 1604 and lost 35 of its 79 men before spring, and that is where the French presence in North America began.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Maine entered the Union on 15 March 1820 under the Missouri Compromise<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>, which admitted it as a free state to balance the admission of Missouri with slavery.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup>",
"Honolulu": "Honolulu is the capital of the American state of Hawaii and its largest city, on the south coast of Oʻahu; the census counts about 345,000 people in its urban core<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>, while the city and county, which covers the whole island, holds nearly a million.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> It has been the seat of government since 1845, when Kamehameha III moved the royal residence there from Lahaina, and ʻIolani Palace, begun in 1879 for King Kalākaua, is the only royal palace in the United States.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> West of the city lies Pearl Harbor, where the attack of 7 December 1941 brought the United States into the Second World War.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>",
"Oklahoma": "Oklahoma is an American state on the southern plains, covering 181,037 square kilometres (69,899 square miles)<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> with about 4.1 million residents and its capital and largest city alike at Oklahoma City.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Its name is a Choctaw word meaning red people, and the land, American since the Louisiana Purchase of 1803, was reserved by Congress in 1828 for Native Americans, who were removed onto it from the south-east.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Tribal land was taken back into United States territory act by act until statehood came in 1907, and the droughts of the 1930s then made a Dust Bowl of its rural counties.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>",
"Lansing": "Lansing is the capital of the American state of Michigan, a city of about 114,000 in the middle of the state and only its sixth largest.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> It was chosen in 1847 because the state constitution required a permanent capital by then and no rival town could carry a majority, so the legislature settled on almost undeveloped ground.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The capitol built there between 1872 and 1879 to Elijah E. Myers's design was among the first modelled on the national capitol<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>, and Ransom Eli Olds, who built his first petrol car in the town in 1896, founded the Reo plant here in 1904.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>",
"New_York_(state)": "New York is a state in the north-eastern United States, covering 141,297 square kilometres (54,555 square miles)<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> with about 19.9 million residents, its capital at Albany and 8.5 million of its people in New York City.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The Dutch West India Company founded New Amsterdam at the southern tip of Manhattan by 1626, and the colony that grew from it declared independence on 9 July 1776 as one of the original thirteen states.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> A canal system running unbroken since 1825 opened the continental interior and made the state the country’s foremost commercial centre, while Ellis Island in its harbour admitted over 12 million immigrants between 1892 and 1924.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup>",
"Baton_Rouge": "Baton Rouge is the capital of the American state of Louisiana, a city of about 221,000 on the east bank of the Mississippi and, after New Orleans, the second largest in the state.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Louisiana was governed from New Orleans until the 1840s, when a castellated Gothic capitol designed by James Dakin was built here between 1847 and 1849; its interiors destroyed in 1862, it was rebuilt and served until 1932.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Its replacement, a skyscraper of 34 storeys rising 137 metres (450 feet) that Huey Long commissioned from Weiss, Dreyfous and Seiferth, was the tallest building in the South when dedicated, and Long was shot in a corridor of it in 1935.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>",
"Idaho": "Idaho is a state of the American north-west covering 216,443 square kilometres (83,569 square miles), fourteenth largest of the fifty,<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> with about two million residents and Boise as both its capital and largest city.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Its name is thought to come from a Shoshone phrase meaning ‘gem of the mountains’, and Shoshonean peoples have fished the Snake River for salmon and sturgeon since time immemorial, until the Oregon Trail brought settlers west in the mid-19th century.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> It was admitted as the forty-third state on 3 July 1890 and was the fourth to give women the vote; its lava fields at Craters of the Moon have been a national monument since 1924.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup>",
"Augusta": "Augusta is the capital of the American state of Maine, a city of about 19,000 on the Kennebec and only the tenth largest in the state.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> It grew from Fort Western, a fortified fur trading post raised in 1754 for the Proprietors of the Kennebec Purchase, which in 1775 became the supply base of Benedict Arnold’s expedition against Quebec.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Congress voted an arsenal here in January 1827 for its central position on the river, and a month later it was chosen for the state capital for the same reason; the arsenal built between 1828 and 1838 answered a border dispute with New Brunswick that nearly brought war with Britain.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>",
"West_Virginia": "West Virginia is an Appalachian state of the United States covering 62,756 square kilometres (24,230 square miles)<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> with about 1.77 million residents,<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> its capital and largest city alike at Charleston.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> It exists because Virginia seceded in 1861 and its western counties would not go with it: delegates from 27 of them met at Wheeling that May, whose custom house then served as the capitol of loyal Virginia until Abraham Lincoln signed the proclamation admitting the new state on 20 June 1863.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Coal has shaped it since, most violently at Matewan in 1920, where a shooting between miners and company detectives led on to the Battle of Blair Mountain the next year.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup>",
"Oklahoma_City": "Oklahoma City is the capital of the American state of Oklahoma and much the largest city in it, with about 713,000 residents.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> It was founded in a single afternoon: President Benjamin Harrison set noon on 22 April 1889 for the opening of nearly two million acres to settlers, and tens of thousands raced for claims on foot, on horseback and by train, wagon and bicycle.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Guthrie was the capital until a statewide vote on 11 June 1910 moved it here, by which time the city had grown to nearly 60,000; oil was struck beneath it in 1928, and a well was later drilled on the plaza of the Capitol itself.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>",
"Maryland": "Maryland is a mid-Atlantic state of the United States covering 32,131 square kilometres (12,406 square miles),<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> nearly a fifth of it water rather than land, with about 6.26 million residents, its capital at Annapolis and its largest city Baltimore.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> It joined the Union as the seventh state on 28 April 1788 and gave up land of its own in 1791 for the national capital at Washington.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Its state house, begun in 1772, is the oldest still in use as a seat of state government, and Congress sat in it in 1783 and 1784; thirty years later Francis Scott Key watched the bombardment of Fort McHenry and wrote what became the national anthem.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup>",
"Albany_(New_York)": "Albany is the capital of the American state of New York, a city of about 101,000 on the Hudson and only the sixth largest in the state.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The Dutch held this stretch of the river first, within the patroonship of Rensselaerswyck, and the fur post of Fort Orange stood where the city now does.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Philip Schuyler’s house here saw Burgoyne held prisoner after Saratoga in 1777 and the wedding of Elizabeth Schuyler to Alexander Hamilton in 1780;<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> the New York State Capitol above it was begun in 1867, reviewed midway by Olmsted, Eidlitz and Richardson, and finished in 1899 in a different style from the one it started in.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>",
"Nevada_(state)": "Nevada is the seventh largest of the American states, 286,380 square kilometres (110,572 square miles) of mostly grassland and barren country<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> with about 3.27 million residents, its capital at Carson City and its largest city Las Vegas.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Silver made it: Virginia City on the Comstock Lode was the first silver rush town in the country, and in August 1860, four years before statehood, the whole territory held 6,857 people.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> It became a state on 31 October 1864,<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> and Hoover Dam, finished on the Colorado in 1935 and the highest dam in the world when built, changed the scale of what the desert could support.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup>",
"Boise": "Boise is the capital of the American state of Idaho and much the largest in it, with about 238,000 residents.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Gold brought it into being: strikes on the Clearwater in 1860 and in the Boise Basin in 1863 drew miners in such numbers that Congress made Idaho a territory on 4 March 1863, and the difficulty of getting the metal to market led to a federal assay office here, funded in 1869 and working from 1872.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The statehouse was completed in 1920, and outside it stands a bronze Lincoln given in 1915, on a sandstone base from nearby Table Rock, marking the president who signed the act creating the territory.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>",
"Utah": "Utah is the eleventh largest of the American states, covering 219,882 square kilometres (84,897 square miles)<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> with about 3.5 million residents and its capital and largest city alike at Salt Lake City.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Brigham Young led the Mormon trek west through the Wasatch Mountains in 1847, down Emigration Canyon into the valley, and the route became the Mormon Trail; the temple on Temple Square took from 1853 to 1893 to build.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> The transcontinental railroad was completed at Promontory on 10 May 1869,<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup> statehood followed on 4 January 1896,<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> and the open pit at Bingham Canyon still yields about 14 percent of American copper.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"8\"></sup>",
"Minnesota": "Minnesota is the twelfth largest American state,<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> covering 225,163 square kilometres (86,936 square miles)<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> with about 5.8 million residents, its capital at St. Paul and its largest city Minneapolis.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Nicknamed the North Star State, it entered the Union on 11 May 1858;<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> the Grand Portage carried the fur trade inland from Lake Superior between 1731 and 1804,<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> and its pipestone quarries have been worked for over 3,000 years.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> Its later wealth came from white pine floated to the St. Croix Boom after 1856,<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup> the hard spring wheat milled at Minneapolis after Cadwallader Washburn's innovations of the 1870s,<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"8\"></sup> and the Mesabi Range, over half the American iron output from 1892 to 1961.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"9\"></sup>",
"Annapolis": "Annapolis is the capital of the American state of Maryland, a city of about 41,000 on a peninsula at the mouth of the Severn River.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The colonial capital was moved there from St. Mary's in 1695 by the royal governor Sir Francis Nicholson, and the town was laid out to a modified baroque plan whose two circles, for the state house and the church, still order its streets.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Its State House, begun in 1772, is the oldest American building still serving as a seat of state government and was the nation's capital in 1783–84,<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> while the United States Naval Academy has stood on the site of Fort Severn since 1845.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>",
"New_Jersey": "New Jersey is the 47th largest American state, covering only 22,591 square kilometres (8,723 square miles)<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> but holding about 9.5 million people, its capital at Trenton and its largest city Newark.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Nicknamed the Garden State, it joined the Union on 18 December 1787, and Trenton became the official capital in 1790.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Its landmarks run from Nassau Hall at Princeton, where the Continental Congress sat from June to November 1783,<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> through Alexander Hamilton's Society for Establishing Useful Manufactures, chartered in 1791 to harness the Great Falls of the Passaic,<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> to the marl pit at Haddonfield that yielded the world's first relatively complete dinosaur skeleton in 1858.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup>",
"Carson_City": "Carson City is the capital of Nevada, a city of about 58,000 in Eagle Valley and the state's sixth largest place, far behind Las Vegas.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Abraham Curry and three partners bought the Eagle Ranch in 1858 and had a townsite surveyed that September; it became the capital when Nevada gained statehood on 31 October 1864.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The silver of the Comstock Lode paid for what followed — a state capitol of native stone begun under an act of 1869, a branch of the United States Mint, built because hauling bullion to San Francisco was risky, and, from 1890, the Stewart Indian School, the only off-reservation boarding school in Nevada's history.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup>",
"Massachusetts": "Massachusetts is the seventh smallest American state, covering 27,336 square kilometres (10,554 square miles)<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> with about 7.1 million residents and its capital and largest city alike at Boston.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Officially a Commonwealth rather than a state, it joined the Union on 6 February 1788, and Harvard College, established in 1636, was the first colonial institution of higher learning.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Much of the early American factory system grew here: the federal arsenal at Springfield, created in 1794, helped settle the principle of interchangeable parts,<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> and Lowell, laid out from 1822 on the water power of the Pawtucket Falls, became the country's cotton-textile centre and the first American city developed primarily to be industrial.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup>",
"Salt_Lake_City": "Salt Lake City is the capital of Utah and much its largest city, with about 218,000 people.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> It was founded by the Mormon emigration Brigham Young led west from the Missouri Valley in 1847, and the Beehive House was built for him as second president of the Church of Jesus Christ of Latter-day Saints and first governor of Deseret, later Utah Territory.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The territory's dealings with Washington were the most turbulent in the history of the American territorial system, and the city's Council Hall, built between 1864 and 1866, served as the territorial capitol until 1894, while Fort Douglas was planted in the foothills above it in 1862.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup>",
"Ohio": "Ohio is the 34th largest American state, covering 116,098 square kilometres (44,826 square miles)<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> with about 11.9 million residents and its capital and largest city alike at Columbus.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Nicknamed the Buckeye State, it became the 17th state on 1 March 1803, and long before that the Hopewell raised geometric earthworks in its southern valleys, the largest of them the Newark group.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> The Ohio and Erie Canal, finished in 1832, tied Lake Erie to the Ohio River and drew towns and trade after it;<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> at Ripley the minister John Rankin kept one of the first stations of the Underground Railroad;<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup> and in 1905 the Wright brothers built the first aeroplane fit for practical use.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"8\"></sup>",
"St._Paul": "St. Paul is the capital of Minnesota and its second largest city, with about 307,000 people on the Mississippi.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Zebulon Pike bought the site of the future Fort Snelling from the Sioux in 1805, and that post, on the bluff commanding where the Minnesota meets the Mississippi, was the first American fort in the state.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The city was the seat of James J. Hill, who merged his north-western railways into the Great Northern in 1890,<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> of Frank Billings Kellogg, whose pact of 1928 renouncing war brought him the Nobel Peace Prize,<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> and for a time of F. Scott Fitzgerald, who returned to the family house on Summit Avenue in 1919.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup>",
"Illinois": "Illinois is the 25th largest American state, covering 149,995 square kilometres (57,914 square miles)<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> with about 12.7 million residents, its capital at Springfield and its largest city Chicago.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Nicknamed the Prairie State and the Land of Lincoln, it became a state on 3 December 1818, and Cahokia had been the largest Mississippian community in eastern North America, lived in from about 800 to 1500 CE.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Two experiments of the industrial age belong to it: the model workers' town George Pullman built south of Chicago between 1880 and 1884, whose strike of 1894 marked the labour movement,<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> and Hull-House, which Jane Addams and Ellen Gates Starr opened in a slum in 1889.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup>",
"Trenton": "Trenton is the capital of New Jersey, a city of about 91,000 people on the Delaware and the fifth largest place in the state.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The Scottish merchant William Trent bought land at the Falls of the Delaware in 1714, built a house there in 1719 and laid out a township he called Trent's Town, and the barracks that still stand were raised in 1758 and 1759 to winter British troops.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Washington crossed the Delaware on Christmas night 1776 and took the town's German garrison the next morning, a stroke that gave his often-beaten army a taste of victory,<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> and Trenton became the state's official capital in 1790.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>",
"Virginia_(state)": "Virginia is the 35th largest American state, covering 110,787 square kilometres (42,775 square miles)<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> with about 8.8 million residents, its capital at Richmond and its largest city Virginia Beach.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Nicknamed the Old Dominion, it entered the Union as the tenth state on 25 June 1788 and is the birthplace of more American presidents than any other.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> England planted its first permanent colony at Jamestown in 1607, an English privateer put ashore the first Africans there in 1619,<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> and Williamsburg, which began as a palisaded barrier in 1633 and was the colonial capital from 1699, proved the training ground of Washington, Patrick Henry, George Wythe, Jefferson and George Mason.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup>",
"Boston": "Boston is the capital of Massachusetts, its largest city and its oldest, with about 673,000 people.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Its Old State House of 1712 and 1713 is the oldest Georgian public building left in the country and was the seat of the Commonwealth's government from 1776 to 1798,<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> while the Old South Meeting House held the session of 16 December 1773 that ended in the Boston Tea Party.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The African Meeting House on Beacon Hill, built in 1806 by black labourers and craftsmen, is the oldest surviving African American church building in the country,<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> and the Tremont Street Subway of 1897 was the country's first underground public transport system.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup>",
"Washington_(state)": "Washington is the 18th largest American state, covering 184,661 square kilometres (71,298 square miles)<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> with about 8 million residents and its largest city at Seattle.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Statehood came on 11 November 1889,<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> the American claim to the north-west coast resting on the fur trader Robert Gray's finding of the Columbia in 1792, the climax of a long search for the legendary Great River of the West.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Sovereignty over the San Juan archipelago, under joint British and American occupation for thirteen years, was resolved only by the Treaty of Washington of 1871 and the arbitration that followed,<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> while Seattle's Pioneer Square keeps some of the handsomest Richardsonian Romanesque fronts in the north-west.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup>",
"Columbus_(Ohio)": "Columbus is the capital of Ohio and its largest city, with about 933,000 people.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Fear of losing the capital ended on 26 January 1838 when the statehouse bill passed, but the panic of 1837 stopped work for years, and the city secured its Capitol only in the winter of 1846, the legislature appropriating no money but turning the state penitentiary's workforce over to the building.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Occupied in 1857, it is reckoned an outstanding statement of Greek Revival architecture;<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> the Ohio Theatre by Thomas White Lamb is among the few picture palaces still in use;<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> and Eddie Rickenbacker, the American Ace of Aces, grew up in a plain frame house there.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>",
"Oregon": "Oregon is the 9th largest American state, covering 254,799 square kilometres (98,379 square miles)<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> with about 4.3 million residents,<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> its capital at Salem and its largest city Portland.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The Oregon Trail carried the Great Migration of nearly a thousand people west in the spring of 1843, crossing the Rocky Mountains at South Pass, and Britain ceded Oregon Country to the United States in 1846;<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Congress admitted the state to the Union in 1859.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Placer gold found in the Rogue River valley raised Jacksonville from a tent camp in 1852,<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> the mines drew a Chinese community to John Day,<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup> and on Mount Hood the Works Progress Administration built Timberline Lodge between 1935 and 1938.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"8\"></sup>",
"Springfield_(Illinois)": "Springfield is the capital of Illinois and its 7th largest city, with about 113,000 people.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Its Old State Capitol, the fifth in the state, served from 1839 until 1876, and it was there that Abraham Lincoln sat in the legislature, pleaded more than 200 cases and made the House Divided speech of 16 June 1858.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The plain frame house at Eighth and Jackson streets is the only one he ever owned,<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> his tomb in the city was dedicated in 1874 and an attempt to steal the body for ransom was foiled two years later,<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> and Frank Lloyd Wright built the Dana House of 1902 around the older home it replaced.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup>",
"Arizona_(state)": "Arizona is the 6th largest American state,<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> covering 295,234 square kilometres (113,990 square miles)<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> with about 7.6 million residents, its capital and largest city alike at Phoenix.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> It was admitted on 14 February 1912, the last of the 48 conterminous states, and women won the vote there in the same year;<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> long before that the Hohokam farmed the desert valleys by irrigation, raising at Pueblo Grande a platform building and canals fed from the Salt River.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Mission San Xavier del Bac was founded in 1700 at the northern edge of Spain's empire,<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> and the silver strike of 1877 raised Tombstone, remembered for the gun battle at the OK Corral in 1881.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup>",
"Richmond": "Richmond is the capital of Virginia and its 3rd largest city, with about 234,000 people.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Patrick Henry made the Liberty or Death speech in St John's Church there in March 1775, carrying the Virginia Convention from conciliation to a state of defence,<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> and the state Capitol that Thomas Jefferson designed on the model of a Roman temple at Nimes, begun in 1785, later housed the Confederate Congress.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Iron made the city worth holding, and the Tredegar Iron Works ranked third among American ironworks in 1861,<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> and in 1903 Maggie Lena Walker founded the Saint Luke Penny Savings Bank, the first American woman known to found and head a bank.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup>",
"New_Mexico": "New Mexico is the 5th largest American state,<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> covering 314,917 square kilometres (121,590 square miles)<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> with about 2.1 million residents, its capital at Santa Fe and its largest city Albuquerque.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Santa Fe has been the capital since 1610 and the territory was admitted to the Union on 6 January 1912;<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Taos Pueblo is the last site of a Puebloan occupation of its valley reaching back to at least 900 CE.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> The mission church of San Estevan del Rey at Acoma, built between 1629 and 1642, is Spanish in plan and Puebloan in construction and ornament,<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> and the world's first nuclear device was fired at the Trinity Site on 16 July 1945.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup>",
"Olympia_(Washington)": "The capital of Washington is a city of about 56,000 people, the 24th largest in the state.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The first territorial legislature, meeting in 1854 in a room above a restaurant, ratified the choice of the town as the seat of government, and Edmond Sylvester, who had founded it, gave the territory ground on Capitol Hill to build a capitol on.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> A wooden pioneer Capitol served until after statehood and the county courthouse was bought as a second one in 1901,<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> while the domed group standing on the campus today follows the plan that Wilder and White of New York won a national competition with.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>",
"Georgia_(state)": "Georgia is the 24th largest American state, covering 153,910 square kilometres (59,425 square miles)<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> with about 11.2 million residents, its capital and largest city alike at Atlanta.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The colony was established in 1732 and settled at Savannah the year after, the last of the thirteen and the only one ruled by a board of trustees in London;<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> long before it, Etowah was a Mississippian centre of platform mounds above its river in the north.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> New Echota was the Cherokee capital from 1825 until the treaty signed there led to removal in 1838,<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> and Martin Luther King Jr. was born on Auburn Avenue in Atlanta, where he later preached at Ebenezer Baptist Church.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup>",
"Salem": "Salem is the capital of Oregon and its 2nd largest city, with about 180,000 people, some 80 kilometres (50 miles) south of Portland.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The Reverend Jason Lee and a band of Methodist missionaries founded a station on the Willamette in 1834, the first mission anywhere in the American north-west, and moved it to the site of the town in 1841.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Willamette University grew out of the mission school and was founded in 1842, the oldest institution of higher learning west of the Missouri River,<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> while the first full textile factory on the Pacific coast opened here in 1857 and the Thomas Kay woollen mill followed in 1889.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>",
"South_Carolina": "South Carolina is the 40th largest American state, at 82,933 square kilometres (32,020 square miles)<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> with about 5.5 million residents, its capital Columbia and its largest city Charleston.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> One of the thirteen original colonies, named for King Charles I and split from North Carolina in 1710, it entered the Union in 1788, and more battles of the Revolution were fought there than in any other.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Santa Elena on its coast was the capital of Spanish Florida from 1566 to 1587,<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> the Stono rising of 1739 was among the worst slave insurrections of the colonial era,<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> and Robert Smalls, born into slavery at Beaufort, went from carrying off a steamer to Congress.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup>",
"Phoenix_(Arizona)": "Phoenix is the capital of Arizona and its largest city, with about 1.7 million people.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Under the modern city lies Pueblo Grande, where Hohokam farmers raised a platform building and dug large canals to lead water out of the Salt River;<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> no later town was founded through the Spanish and Mexican periods, and farmers moved into the valley only in 1867, laying out a townsite in 1870 for 240 people.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The territorial capital, which had moved between Prescott and Tucson since 1864, was fixed here in 1889,<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> and the Phoenix Indian School, founded in 1891, was the only federal boarding school in Arizona away from a reservation.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>",
"Charleston_(West_Virginia)": "Charleston is the capital of the American state of West Virginia and its largest city, a place of about 46,000 people on the flat ground where the Elk River joins the Great Kanawha.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> George Clendenin and his brothers were the first settlers on the site in 1788, building a fort renamed Fort Lee in 1792, and salt made the valley’s early money before coal and timber followed it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The capital came here from Wheeling in 1870 and went back again, until a statewide vote in 1877 settled it: the first capitol in the city opened in 1885, and its replacement, after a fire in 1921, was designed by Cass Gilbert.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>",
"North_Carolina": "North Carolina is the 28th largest American state, covering 139,391 square kilometres (53,819 square miles)<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> with about 11 million residents, its capital at Raleigh and its largest city Charlotte.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> It became the twelfth state after the Revolution, lying on the Atlantic coast between Virginia and South Carolina, and in 1903 the Wright brothers flew the first aeroplane at Kitty Hawk.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Gold found on a farm there in 1799 opened the earliest documented gold mining in the present United States and the country's first gold rush,<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> and seven black men founded the North Carolina Mutual Life Insurance Company at Durham in 1898.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup>",
"Santa_Fe": "Santa Fe is the capital of New Mexico and its 4th largest city, with about 91,000 people,<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> and has been the capital since 1610.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Don Pedro de Peralta founded the town in the winter of 1609 and 1610 with a fortified palacio, a plaza before it and a wall around the whole, and the palace survives as one of the oldest public buildings of European origin in America;<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Pueblo people held it for the thirteen years of the revolt from 1680 to 1693,<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> and the Barrio de Analco across the river, settled in 1620, is among the oldest neighbourhoods of European inception in the country.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>",
"Pennsylvania": "Pennsylvania is the 33rd largest American state, covering 119,280 square kilometres (46,054 square miles)<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> with about 13.1 million residents, its capital at Harrisburg and its largest city Philadelphia.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> One of the original thirteen colonies, established by William Penn in 1681, it saw Philadelphia serve as the first capital of the new nation before the state moved its own business to Lancaster in 1799 and Harrisburg in 1812.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Material from the lower levels of Meadowcroft Rockshelter is radiocarbon dated to 16,000 BP,<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> the army was remade at Valley Forge in the winter of 1777 and 1778,<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> and Edwin L. Drake struck oil near Titusville in the world's first oil well in 1859.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup>",
"Atlanta": "Atlanta is the capital of Georgia and its largest city, with about 520,000 people.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The present capitol was finished in 1889 on a five-acre lot the city gave for it, paid for by a million dollars the legislature appropriated in 1883.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> John Wesley Dobbs coined the name Sweet Auburn for Auburn Avenue, a district whose success was bound up with the pattern of residence forced on black Atlantans by Jim Crow,<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> where Alonzo F. Herndon, the wealthiest black man in the city, ran its largest barber shop and led an insurance company;<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> the Fox Theatre, a movie palace the size of a block, opened on Christmas Day 1929.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>",
"Wisconsin_(state)": "Wisconsin is the 23rd largest American state, covering 169,635 square kilometres (65,496 square miles)<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> with about 6.0 million residents,<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> its capital at Madison and its largest city Milwaukee;<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> nearly a fifth of that area is its share of the Great Lakes and its inland waters.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> It joined the Union on 29 May 1848,<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> having been organised as a territory in 1836, when the lead-mining country of the south-west was its most populous corner<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> and Cornish miners were settling Mineral Point.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup> Effigy mounds were raised across the region between about 700 and 1000 CE,<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> and dairying, which the university's butterfat research put on a scientific footing from 1880, made it America's Dairyland.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"8\"></sup>",
"Columbia_(South_Carolina)": "Columbia is the capital of South Carolina and its second largest city, with about 145,000 residents to Charleston's 158,000.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The colony's first State House stood at Charleston from 1753; a second was built here in 1790, and the legislature met in the new town that year,<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> with South Carolina College chartered eleven years later.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The Convention of the People declared for secession in the First Baptist Church on 17 December 1860;<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Sherman's army occupied the city in February 1865 and burned public buildings including the old State House, sparing the unfinished capitol that John Niernsee had been raising in Congaree River granite since 1851.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>",
"Indiana": "Indiana is the 38th largest American state, covering 94,326 square kilometres (36,420 square miles)<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> with about 6.9 million residents,<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> and its capital and largest city alike at Indianapolis.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Nicknamed the Hoosier State, it became the 19th state on 11 December 1816, its first capital being Corydon until the seat of government moved to Indianapolis in 1825.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> William Henry Harrison governed the territory from Grouseland at Vincennes, where Tecumseh pressed his confederation before the defeat at Tippecanoe in 1811;<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Eugene Debs founded the American Railway Union at Terre Haute in 1893,<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> and Sarah Breedlove, as Madame C. J. Walker, brought her cosmetics company to Indianapolis in 1910.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup>",
"Raleigh": "Raleigh is the capital of North Carolina and its second largest city, with about 500,000 residents to Charlotte's 943,000.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> A convention of 1787 required the capital to stand within 16 kilometres (10 miles) of Isaac Hunter's tavern in Wake County, and in 1792 commissioners bought 400 hectares (1,000 acres) of Senator Joel Lane's plantation for the site, which Governor Alexander Martin named for Sir Walter Raleigh.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> William Christmas laid the town out around Union Square, kept for the State House;<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> the building there burned in 1831 and was replaced between 1833 and 1840 by a granite Capitol designed by Ithiel Town and Alexander Jackson Davis.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>",
"Kentucky": "Kentucky is the 37th largest American state, covering 104,656 square kilometres (40,408 square miles)<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> with about 4.6 million residents,<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> its capital at Frankfort and its largest city Louisville.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> It was admitted on 1 June 1792 as the 15th state and the first west of the Appalachian Mountains, its government organised three days later in a Lexington tavern before Frankfort was chosen as the permanent seat.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Big Bone Lick drew mammoths and mastodons to its sulfur springs and yielded the bones Jefferson sent William Clark to dig in 1807;<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> enslaved African Americans mined saltpetre in Mammoth Cave and guided its tourists,<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> and Mary Breckinridge founded the Frontier Nursing Service at Wendover in 1925.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup>",
"Harrisburg": "Harrisburg is the capital of Pennsylvania and only its ninth largest city, with about 50,000 residents against Philadelphia's 1.6 million.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The state moved its business from Philadelphia to Lancaster in 1799 and settled at Harrisburg in 1812,<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> on ground given by John Harris and William Maclay, where Stephen Hills built two fireproof offices and, between 1816 and 1822, a capitol.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> That building burned on 2 February 1897 and the present one was dedicated in 1906;<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> 16 kilometres (10 miles) to the south-east<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> the Three Mile Island Unit 2 reactor partially melted down on 28 March 1979, the most serious accident in American commercial nuclear power.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>",
"Madison": "Madison is the capital of Wisconsin and its second largest city, with about 285,000 residents against Milwaukee's 564,000.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The territorial legislature, sitting at Belmont in the autumn of 1836, chose it as the capital over some twenty other places that wanted the honour.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Its university opened North Hall in 1851 and by the 1900s had made a state university's service to its community into the Wisconsin Idea;<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> the Republican convention that renominated Robert La Follette in the Armory in 1904 is of national importance to the Progressive movement,<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> and Frank Lloyd Wright built the first Usonian house in the country here in 1936.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>",
"Tennessee": "Tennessee is the 36th largest American state, covering 109,153 square kilometres (42,144 square miles)<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> with about 7.2 million residents,<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> and its capital and largest city alike at Nashville.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> It joined as the 16th state in 1796, after a convention at which William Blount, governor of the Southwest Territory, presided,<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> and it holds the most visited national park in the country, the Great Smoky Mountains.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Fisk University's Jubilee Singers toured Europe and America with spirituals and paid for Jubilee Hall;<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> the X-10 reactor at Oak Ridge, the world's first full-scale one, began work in 1943;<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup> and Sam Phillips recorded Elvis Presley and Howlin' Wolf at Memphis.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"8\"></sup>",
"Missouri_(state)": "Missouri is the 21st largest American state, covering 180,540 square kilometres (69,707 square miles)<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> with about 6.2 million residents,<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> its capital Jefferson City and its largest city Kansas City.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Organised as a territory in 1812, it followed Louisiana as the second state admitted from the Louisiana Purchase, on 10 August 1821.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The Pony Express opened from the Patee House at St. Joseph in April 1860;<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Eads Bridge at St. Louis was the first major structure to use steel as its main metal;<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> and a flat in that city gave its name to Shelley v. Kraemer, in which the Supreme Court barred state courts in 1948 from enforcing racial covenants.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup>",
"Indianapolis": "Indianapolis is the capital of Indiana and much the largest city in it, with about 891,000 residents against Fort Wayne's 273,000.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The state's first capital was Corydon, and the seat of government moved here in 1825.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Benjamin Harrison accepted the Republican nomination for the presidency in his house on North Delaware Street in 1888;<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Joseph Cole turned his carriage works over to luxury motor cars in 1907 and had the first Cole on the market by 1909;<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> and Butler Fieldhouse, of 1927 and 1928, is the earliest of the great college basketball fieldhouses.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>",
"Louisiana_Purchase": "The Louisiana Purchase was the transaction, signed on 30 April 1803, by which the United States bought from France 2,145,000 square kilometres (828,000 square miles) west of the Mississippi for $15 million and doubled its own size.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Robert Livingston and James Monroe had been authorised to pay up to $10 million for New Orleans and the Floridas alone, and took the whole territory instead.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> It made up all or part of fifteen modern states between the Mississippi and the Rocky Mountains, and France had held it only since 1800;<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> the survey of its lands was run from a base established in an Arkansas swamp on 10 November 1815.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>",
"Arkansas": "Arkansas is the 29th largest American state, covering 137,732 square kilometres (53,179 square miles)<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> with about 3.1 million residents,<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> and its capital and largest city alike at Little Rock.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> It became the 25th state on 15 June 1836, and the routes west went round it, so for its first 150 years it stayed rural and without a large city.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Arkansas Post of 1686 was the first European foothold in the lower Mississippi valley;<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> the survey of the Louisiana Purchase was run from a base laid in an eastern swamp in 1815;<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> and in 1957 federal troops brought nine Black students into Little Rock Central High School.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup>",
"Frankfort": "Frankfort is the capital of Kentucky and only its fourteenth largest city, with about 29,000 residents against Louisville's 641,000.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> A committee chose it as the permanent seat of government and the General Assembly met there for the first time on 1 November 1793,<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> and John Brown built Liberty Hall in the new capital between 1796 and 1800, one of the most perfect Federal houses in the country.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Gideon Shryock, aged 25, won the competition for a new state house and raised it between 1827 and 1830;<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> the present Capitol was dedicated in 1910,<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> and an obelisk of 1924 in Greenhill Cemetery names 140 of the county's Black Union soldiers.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup>",
"Alabama": "Alabama is the 30th largest American state, covering 135,767 square kilometres (52,420 square miles)<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> with about 5.2 million residents,<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> its capital at Montgomery and its largest city Huntsville.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Set off as a territory in 1817 and admitted to the Union on 14 December 1819,<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> it saw delegates from six seceded states meet in its capitol in February 1861 to adopt a provisional constitution for the Confederate States of America.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Blast furnaces at Birmingham made it a centre of pig iron rather than of steel, its ores carrying too much phosphorus for the ordinary Bessemer converter,<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> while Huntsville later tested the rockets that put the first American satellite in orbit.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup>",
"Nashville": "Nashville is the capital of Tennessee and its largest city, the balance of its consolidated metropolitan government holding 704,963 residents.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Settlers under James Robertson built a fort above the Cumberland River in about 1780,<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> and the town has been called the Athens of the South since the 1820s, an epithet it made literal in 1897 with a full-size Parthenon put up for the state's centennial exposition.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> William Strickland's Greek Revival capitol stands above the city,<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> and from 1943 to 1974 the Ryman Auditorium housed the Grand Ole Opry, whose broadcasts carried country music from a southern regional style to a worldwide one.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>",
"Greek_Revival_architecture": "Greek Revival architecture is a classical manner of the early 19th century in which a building is composed as a Greek temple rather than dressed in classical detail: a columned portico under a pediment, with the entablature carried round the whole.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Its architects worked from the measured proportions of particular ancient buildings, so that the Ionic portico Gideon Shryock gave the old Kentucky state house takes its proportions from the temple of Athena Polias at Priene in Ionia.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> In the United States it belongs to the Romantic era of about 1820 to 1860, and its most noted practitioner was William Strickland, under whom Shryock trained in Philadelphia.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>",
"Pig_iron": "Pig iron is the crude iron tapped from a blast furnace and led along a cross runner into rows of sand moulds, an arrangement whose fancied likeness to a sow suckling her pigs gave the metal its name.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> It carries far more carbon than wrought iron or steel, usually between 3.4 and 4 per cent, so it cannot be worked directly and is either cast in a foundry or refined by burning the carbon out.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> What can be done with it depends on its ore, since iron carrying too much phosphorus will not serve an ordinary Bessemer converter and iron carrying too little will not serve the basic one.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>",
"Mississippi_(state)": "Mississippi is the 32nd largest American state, covering 125,438 square kilometres (48,432 square miles)<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> with about 2.9 million residents<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> and its capital and largest city alike at Jackson.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> It was admitted to the Union on 10 December 1817 as the 20th state,<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> and the Married Women's Property Act its legislature passed at Jackson in 1839 was the first law by any state to protect the property of married women.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Vicksburg, on a bluff commanding the Mississippi River, fell after a siege of 47 days on 4 July 1863,<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> and in 1962 federal troops fought rioters at Oxford over the court-ordered admission of James Meredith to the state university.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup>",
"Mississippi_River": "The Mississippi River is the second longest river in North America, running about 3,780 kilometres (2,350 miles) from Lake Itasca in Minnesota to the Gulf of Mexico and draining most of the country between the Appalachians and the Rockies.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> It discharges 16,792 cubic metres (593,003 cubic feet) a second, which makes it the fifteenth largest river in the world by volume, while its own tributary the Missouri is about 160 kilometres (100 miles) longer than it is.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Its length is not a settled figure: published measurements run from 3,700 to 3,780 kilometres (2,300 to 2,350 miles), and eighteen cutoffs between 1766 and 1885 took 350 kilometres (218 miles) out of its lower course.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>",
"Jefferson_City": "Jefferson City is the capital of Missouri and only its sixteenth largest city, with 42,564 residents against Kansas City's 516,032.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The state's second capitol, in use by 1840, burned in February 1911, and a national competition held the next year was won by Egerton Swartwout and Evarts Tracy, whose building went up between 1914 and 1917 and was dedicated in 1924.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> It stands on a high bluff above the Missouri River, faced in pale grey limestone and carrying 134 columns of the Corinthian order, three streets from the governor's mansion George Ingham Barnett built in 1871.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>",
"Corinthian_order": "The Corinthian order is the third of the classical Greek column systems, distinguished by its capital: an Ionic capital is a third of the column's thickness, this one as tall as the shaft is thick, so the column reads as more slender.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> It is a bell wrapped in two rows of curled acanthus leaves, small spirals rising to the corners of a scalloped abacus; the developed form stands at the Olympieion in Athens.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The order never had cornices of its own, taking them from Doric or Ionic practice, and Vitruvius credits it to Callimachus, said to have seen an acanthus grow round a basket on a girl's tomb at Corinth.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>",
"Delaware_(state)": "Delaware is the 49th largest American state, covering 6,446 square kilometres (2,489 square miles)<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> with about 1.05 million residents,<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> its capital at Dover and its largest city Wilmington.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> It is nicknamed the First State because it was the first to ratify the Constitution, on 7 December 1787, and it is named for Thomas West, Lord De La Warr, after whom the explorer Samuel Argall named the bay.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Swedish Lutherans built Holy Trinity at Wilmington in 1698 and 1699,<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> and Éleuthère Irénée du Pont de Nemours founded his powder company on the Brandywine in 1802, where about twenty of his mills still stand.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup>",
"Little_Rock": "Little Rock is the capital of Arkansas and its largest city, with 204,774 residents.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> It takes its name from a steep outcrop of clean limestone on the Arkansas River, which Bénard de la Harpe's party called the French Rock in 1722 and which served from 1818 as a landmark and as the starting point for the land surveys south of the river.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Gideon Shryock's Greek Revival state house of 1833 stands in the town,<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> as does the arsenal begun in 1838, which was given up to the state in February 1861 and in which Douglas MacArthur was born in 1880.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>",
"Connecticut": "Connecticut is the 48th largest American state, covering 14,357 square kilometres (5,543 square miles)<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> with about 3.7 million residents,<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> its capital at Hartford and its largest city Bridgeport.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> One of the six New England states and one of the original thirteen colonies, it became a state on 9 January 1788.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Samuel Clemens wrote his major books in the Hartford house he kept from 1874 to 1891,<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> the last wooden American whaleship lies at Mystic,<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> and on 17 January 1955 the Nautilus left the submarine base on the Thames under nuclear power, the first ship ever moved by the atom.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup>",
"Montgomery_(Alabama)": "Montgomery is the capital of Alabama and its fourth largest city, with 195,818 residents.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Jefferson Davis was sworn in as president of the Confederacy on the west portico of its capitol on 18 February 1861, and the permanent Confederate constitution was adopted in the building three weeks later.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Martin Luther King held the pastorate of Dexter Avenue Baptist Church there from 1954, and the boycott that began with Rosa Parks's arrest in December 1955 ran for 381 days under the Montgomery Improvement Association and ended when the Supreme Court held bus segregation illegal.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>",
"Salmon_P._Chase": "Salmon P. Chase was an American lawyer and politician who served in all three branches of the federal government: as an antislavery United States senator before the Civil War, as Secretary of the Treasury through most of it, and afterwards as Chief Justice of the United States.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Born on 13 January 1808 at Cornish in New Hampshire, he died in New York on 7 May 1873; Abraham Lincoln had nominated him to the chief justiceship on 6 December 1864, to the seat Roger B. Taney had left.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Less than six months later he administered the presidential oath to Andrew Johnson, and in 1868 he presided over Johnson's impeachment trial.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>",
"New_Hampshire": "New Hampshire is the 46th largest American state, covering 24,214 square kilometres (9,349 square miles)<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> with about 1.4 million residents,<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> its capital at Concord and its largest city Manchester.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Nicknamed the Granite State, it lies in the north-eastern corner of the country and became a state on 21 June 1788.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Manchester was planned and built by the group of Boston men behind the Amoskeag Manufacturing Company, which at its height early in the 20th century was the largest textile plant in the world and employed as many as 17,000 people in some thirty mills.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>",
"Jackson_(Mississippi)": "Jackson is the capital of Mississippi and its largest city,<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> with 141,449 residents.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The governor's mansion that William Nichols raised there between 1839 and 1841 is one of only seven built in the 19th century that governors still live in, and the most distinguished piece of architecture among the four of those that predate the Civil War.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Medgar Evers, the first field secretary the National Association for the Advancement of Colored People had in the state, was shot dead in 1963 in the driveway of the house built for his family there in 1956.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>",
"Vermont": "Vermont is the 45th largest American state, covering 24,906 square kilometres (9,616 square miles)<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> with about 648,000 residents,<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> its capital at Montpelier and its largest city Burlington.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Delegates meeting in a tavern at Windsor in the summer of 1777 adopted a constitution for a self-governing republic, the first in the country to prohibit slavery and to establish universal manhood suffrage, and a legislature took office under it in March 1778.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> It entered the Union as the 14th state on 4 March 1791,<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> and granite quarried and worked around Barre has been among its major industries since the late 19th century.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup>",
"Dover": "Dover is the capital of the American state of Delaware and, with about 40,000 residents, its second largest city after Wilmington.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Its plan of 1717 set aside two public squares for houses of worship,<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> and it has been the seat of Delaware's government since 1777, the state house on The Green serving as the sole seat of that government from 1792 until 1932.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Among the older buildings near it are Woodburn, a country seat of about 1790 that is now the governor's official residence,<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> and Loockerman Hall, a Georgian plantation house of 1730 or earlier.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>",
"Montana": "Montana is the 4th largest American state, covering 380,831 square kilometres (147,040 square miles)<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> with about 1.14 million residents,<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> its capital at Helena and its largest city Billings.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Abraham Lincoln signed the act creating the territory on 26 May 1864, when Fort Benton on the Missouri was the head of steamboat navigation and supplies went overland to the gold camps;<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> it became the 41st state on 8 November 1889.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Butte drew gold from 1864, silver from 1874 and copper from 1883, and its claims were gathered under the Anaconda Company between 1884 and 1906, making one of the world's largest underground mining districts.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup>",
"Hartford": "Hartford is the capital of Connecticut and its fourth largest city, with about 122,000 residents, after Bridgeport, Stamford and New Haven.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The congregation that founded it gathered at Newtown in Massachusetts in 1632, moved as a body to the west bank of the Connecticut River in 1636 under Thomas Hooker, and in 1637 named the place after Hertford in England.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Its Wadsworth Atheneum, built between 1842 and 1844 to a castellated Gothic design by Ithiel Town, was the first free art museum in the country,<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> and the city has long called itself the insurance capital of the world.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>",
"Wyoming": "Wyoming is the 10th largest American state, covering 253,335 square kilometres (97,813 square miles)<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> and the least populous of the fifty, with about 588,000 residents,<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> its capital and largest city both at Cheyenne.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> It is nicknamed the Equality State: the first victory for women's suffrage in the United States came in its territorial legislature, which passed a suffrage bill in 1869, and the elections of 1870 went off without incident.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> When Congress balked in 1889 at admitting a full-suffrage state, the legislature answered that it would stay out of the Union a hundred years rather than come in without the women; it was admitted as the 44th state on 10 July 1890.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>",
"Concord": "Concord is the capital of New Hampshire and its third largest city, with about 45,000 residents, after Manchester and Nashua;<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> the legislature first sat there on 13 March 1782.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Lewis Downing built the first Concord wagon there in 1813 and, with J. Stephens Abbot from 1826, the Concord coach, a stage coach made entirely by hand and ornamented individually, which grew famous quickly.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Franklin Pierce spent his last years in a house on South Main Street, boarding with a mechanic from one of the coach works; Nathaniel Hawthorne stayed with him there in 1864 shortly before his own death, and Pierce died in the house on 8 October 1869.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>",
"Colorado_(state)": "Colorado is the 8th largest American state, covering 269,601 square kilometres (104,094 square miles)<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> with about 5.96 million residents,<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> and Denver is both its capital and its largest city.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Nicknamed the Centennial State, it became the 38th state on 1 August 1876;<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> in its south-western corner stand cliff dwellings raised from about 1200 CE above Soda Canyon, where Balcony House holds 38 rooms and two kivas around a pair of plazas.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Robert Womack struck a rich gold vein in the south-central mountains in 1890 after fifteen years' prospecting, and by 1893 Cripple Creek was the focus of a rush that made it one of the most productive gold districts in American history.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup>",
"Montpelier": "Montpelier is the capital of Vermont and the smallest state capital in the United States, with 7,915 residents.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The township was chartered in 1781, and mills grew along the Winooski and its North Branch until steam and the railway arrived in the later 19th century; the National Clothespin Company's works of 1918 on Granite Street is the last of its kind in the country.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Its state house, designed by Ammi B. Young on the Grecian Doric of the Theseion and built between 1833 and 1838, was gutted by fire in 1857 and rebuilt by 1859 under a taller gold-leafed dome, above walls of Barre granite.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>",
"Kansas_(state)": "Kansas is the 15th largest American state, covering 213,100 square kilometres (82,278 square miles)<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> with about 2.97 million residents,<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> its capital at Topeka and its largest city Wichita.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The Kansas-Nebraska Act of 1854 left the territory to decide for itself whether it would be free or slave, and rival constitutions were framed in mass assemblies at Topeka, Lecompton, Lawrence and Wyandotte<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> before it entered the Union as a free state on 29 January 1861.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> At Topeka in 1954 the case of Brown v. Board of Education ended with the Supreme Court holding that separate educational facilities are inherently unequal, which took away the legal basis for segregation in 21 states.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup>",
"Helena_(Montana)": "Helena is the capital of Montana and its sixth largest city, with 34,729 residents.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Gold was found in Last Chance Gulch in the summer of 1864, and within months a citizens' committee had laid out streets, elected commissioners and given the camp its present name.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Two mansions of 1885 survive from the boom that followed: the house at 6th and Ewing that the state bought in 1913 as its first official governor's residence,<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> and Samuel T. Hauser's, built the year Grover Cleveland made him the first resident of the territory to govern it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>",
"Nebraska_(state)": "Nebraska is the 16th largest American state, covering 200,330 square kilometres (77,348 square miles)<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> with about 2 million residents,<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> its capital at Lincoln and its largest city Omaha.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Nicknamed the Cornhusker State, it became a state on 1 March 1867,<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> and a commission of three of its officers chose the site of Lincoln that July, though whether the place would survive stayed uncertain until the depression of the 1870s had passed.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> William Jennings Bryan went to Congress from Lincoln in 1891 and led his party for thirty years without winning an election;<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> Willa Cather grew up at Red Cloud, whose people and prairies are the raw material of her novels.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup>",
"Cheyenne_(Wyoming)": "Cheyenne is the capital of Wyoming and its largest city, with 65,704 residents.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> It was laid out in July 1867 where the Union Pacific's line would cross Crow Creek, and by the time the construction crew arrived it already held some 4,000 people, two daily newspapers, more than 200 businesses and a working town government.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Its Richardsonian Romanesque depot of 1886 and 1887, by the Boston firm of Van Brunt & Howe, faces the state capitol ten blocks north along Capitol Avenue, a building begun in 1886 and finished in stages down to 1917.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>",
"Union_Pacific_Railroad": "The Union Pacific Railroad was incorporated at Boston in July 1862, with Oliver Ames as its first president and his brother Oakes directing Credit Mobilier, which did the building.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Congress paid for it in land grants and loans running from $16,000 a mile on the flat to $48,000 in the mountains, and by its completion in 1869 the companies had drawn nearly $27 million, about half the legitimate cost.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Grenville Dodge took its route west from Omaha along the Platte and then across southern Wyoming rather than down to Denver, to reach coal and avoid the roughest of the Rockies; his depot at Cheyenne still faces the capitol.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>",
"South_Dakota": "South Dakota is the 17th largest American state, covering 199,729 square kilometres (77,116 square miles)<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> with about 925,000 residents,<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> its capital at Pierre and its largest city Sioux Falls.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Nicknamed the Mount Rushmore State, it became the 40th state on 2 November 1889,<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> and on 29 December 1890 the Seventh Cavalry met the followers of the Miniconjou chief Big Foot at Wounded Knee Creek on the Pine Ridge reservation, in an event the nomination says is called a massacre or a battle depending on the writer's perspective.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> The Ingalls family settled at De Smet in 1879, and the town and the years to 1882 are the subject of three Laura Ingalls Wilder books.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup>",
"Denver": "Denver is the capital of Colorado and its largest city, with 729,019 residents.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> William E. Larimer Jr. founded Denver City on a jumped claim in spring 1858, with four cabins at the corners of Larimer and Fifteenth Streets,<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> and the rival town of Auraria was laid out across Cherry Creek that October, a territorial act of 1864 making one city of them.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Congress provided for a branch mint there in 1862, but it struck no coin for over thirty years, only melting and assaying bullion for whoever brought it, until an act of 1895 established a United States Mint and a new building went up between 1897 and 1904.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>",
"North_Dakota": "North Dakota is the 19th largest American state, covering 183,108 square kilometres (70,698 square miles)<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> with about 797,000 residents,<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> its capital at Bismarck and its largest city Fargo.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> It and South Dakota were both part of Dakota Territory, organised in 1861, and each became a state on 2 November 1889.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The Nonpartisan League, founded in 1915 to free wheat farmers from the milling and railway companies of Minnesota, won the elections of 1916 and carried through the legislature of 1918 a programme that produced the Bank of North Dakota and a state flour mill at Grand Forks, opened on 30 October 1922 and still the only one owned by an American state.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>",
"Topeka": "Topeka is the capital of Kansas and the fifth largest city in the state, with 125,467 residents.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Settled in December 1854, it was the seat of the territory's Free State movement: the convention that framed the antislavery Topeka Constitution sat in Constitution Hall from 23 October to 11 November 1855, and on 4 July 1856 federal troops dispersed the Free State legislature meeting there.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Charles Curtis, born in North Topeka in 1860 and brought up partly on the Kaw reservation, sat in Congress from 1893 and in 1929 became the first vice president from west of the Mississippi and the first of Native American ancestry.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>",
"Iowa": "Iowa is the 26th largest American state, at 145,746 square kilometres (56,273 square miles)<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> with about 3.24 million residents,<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> and Des Moines its capital and largest city.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Named after the Iowa people, it became a state on 28 December 1846,<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> and its first governor was inaugurated and its constitution drafted in the Greek Revival Old Capitol at Iowa City, which the University of Iowa took over when the legislature moved to Des Moines in 1857.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> From 1855 the Community of True Inspiration, a German pietist group under Christian Metz, founded six communal villages there and bought a seventh, incorporated in 1859 as the Amana Society, the most successful of 19th-century America's utopian communities.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup>",
"Lincoln_(Nebraska)": "Lincoln is the capital of Nebraska and, with 300,619 residents, the second largest city in the state after Omaha.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Its capitol, built between 1922 and 1932 to designs by Bertram Grosvenor Goodhue, is a tower 122 metres (400 feet) high over low wings, carrying Lee Lawrie's figure of the Sower and a sculptural programme written by Hartley Burr Alexander that runs from Moses on Sinai to the admission of Nebraska to the Union.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The legislature that sits there is the only single-chamber one in the United States, carried by an amendment of 1934 for which George Norris campaigned, and nonpartisan besides, so that no candidate's party appears on the ballot.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>",
"Pierre_(South_Dakota)": "Pierre is the capital of South Dakota<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> and, with 13,788 residents, the second smallest state capital in the country after Montpelier.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> On a hill across the Missouri at Fort Pierre, the La Vérendrye brothers buried a lead plate on 30 March 1743 bearing the names of Louis XV and their own, to found a French claim to the upper Missouri; children turned it up in 1913.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The town itself was pressed on the state by Charles L. Hyde, who traded land and raised nine business blocks after 1887, opening the St. Charles Hotel in 1911, the year he was indicted for advertising fraud and pardoned by President William Howard Taft.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>",
"Bismarck_(North_Dakota)": "Bismarck is the capital of North Dakota<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> and, with 77,772 residents, the second largest city in the state after Fargo.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> It grew where the Northern Pacific Railway crossed the Missouri: the army set up Camp Hancock on the line on 16 April 1872, whose supply building of that year still stands and appears to be the oldest in the town.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Colonel Clement A. Lounsberry brought out the first Bismarck Tribune on 11 July 1873, and on 5 July 1876 he telegraphed the New York Herald the first news of the Little Bighorn, brought down the Yellowstone by the steamer Far West.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>",
"Des_Moines": "Des Moines is the capital of Iowa<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> and its largest city, with 213,096 residents.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The legislature moved there from the Old Capitol at Iowa City in 1857,<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> and its capitol complex stands above the meeting of the Des Moines and Raccoon rivers, with the Beaux-Arts State Historical Building of 1898 to 1910 across Grand Avenue from the capitol itself.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Henry Wallace edited Wallaces' Farmer there from 1895 until his death in 1916, argued for scientific farming and agricultural extension, and sat on Theodore Roosevelt's Country Life Commission of 1908 beside Gifford Pinchot.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>",
"Northern_Pacific_Railway": "The Northern Pacific Railway reached Bismarck from the east in 1873 and, on its completion a decade later, linked North Dakota to the west coast.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The army set up Camp Hancock on its line at Bismarck on 16 April 1872 to guard the gangs building it, and kept the post garrisoned until 1877, after which it supplied the forts further west.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Its Bismarck depot, raised after the fire of 8 August 1898 had levelled much of the town centre, was designed by Charles Reed and Allen Stem of St. Paul in a Spanish mission manner uncommon on the northern plains, replacing a hotel that had served as passenger depot too.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>",
"Hong_Kong": "Hong Kong is a territory of 1,107 km² (427 sq mi) on the southern coast of China with about 7.4 million people, settled at roughly 6,700 to the square kilometre.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> A British colony from the 1840s, it passed to Chinese sovereignty on 1 July 1997 under a joint declaration of 1984, to which the United Kingdom gave effect by Act of Parliament.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> It is governed as a special administrative region under a Basic Law that authorises a high degree of autonomy, including final adjudication, and keeps the previous capitalist system and way of life unchanged for fifty years; it belongs to the World Trade Organization in its own right.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>",
"Puerto_Rico": "Puerto Rico is a Caribbean island of 8,868 km² (3,424 sq mi) with about 3.2 million people, governed from San Juan, whose metropolitan area holds around three-quarters of them.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> It is a territory of the United States, which took sovereignty over it from Spain in the treaty ending the war of 1898, and its figures are kept apart from the American ones, the United Nations profile for the United States expressly excluding this area.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> In 1953 the General Assembly resolved that information about it need no longer be transmitted under Article 73e of the Charter, and its population has since fallen by about a tenth between 2015 and 2025.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>",
"Macau": "Macau is a special administrative region of China of 33 km² (13 sq mi) with roughly 720,000 people at 22,563 to the square kilometre.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Made up of a peninsula and islands in the Pearl River Delta, its land has grown by reclamation from about 29.5 km² (11 sq mi) in 2010 to 32.9 km² (12.7 sq mi) in 2018, and since 2015 it has held jurisdiction over 85 km² (33 sq mi) of sea.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> At the by-census of 2016 nearly nine in ten of its people were of Chinese ethnicity and 1.8 per cent Portuguese, four in five speak Cantonese at home, and it is governed under the Basic Law by a chief executive limited to two five-year terms.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>",
"Western_Sahara": "Western Sahara is a territory of 266,000 km² (103,000 sq mi) in northwestern Africa which the United Nations treats as a colonial territory awaiting decolonization, with about 601,000 people and its largest settlement at El Aaiún.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> It is desert of 2.3 people to the square kilometre, only 2.5 per cent of it forested, and the Moroccan dirham is the currency in use.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The International Court of Justice advised in 1975 that the land had not been unowned when Spain colonised it and that neither Morocco nor the Mauritanian entity had held sovereignty over it, and in 1991 the Security Council created a mission to run a self-determination referendum still not held.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>",
"New_Caledonia": "New Caledonia is a group of Pacific islands of 19,100 km² (7,375 sq mi) about 1,500 km (930 miles) east of Australia, with about 295,000 people and its capital at Nouméa, administered by France as a non-self-governing territory under the United Nations Charter.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Its exclusive economic zone covers 1,422,543 km² (549,000 sq mi), about 41 per cent of its people are Melanesian, mainly Kanak, and some 27 Kanak languages are spoken beside official French.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> France annexed it in 1853; after the violence of the 1980s the Matignon Agreements of 1988 and the Nouméa Accord of 1998 transferred powers to it and provided for three referendums on accession to full sovereignty, held in 2018, 2020 and 2021.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>",
"French_Polynesia": "French Polynesia is a group of 118 islands in five archipelagos spread over 2.5 million km² (970,000 sq mi) of the South Pacific, with about 3,687 km² (1,424 sq mi) of land, some 282,000 people and its capital at Papeete, administered by France as a non-self-governing territory.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Its exclusive economic zone covers 5.5 million km² (2.1 million sq mi), and its people are about 65 per cent Polynesian, speaking Tahitian, Marquesan and several other languages beside official French.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The Pomare dynasty signed a protectorate treaty with France in 1842 and ceded sovereignty in 1880; the islands have been called French Polynesia since 1957, and the General Assembly returned the territory to its list of non-self-governing territories in 2013.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>",
"Guam": "Guam is an island of 541 km² (209 sq mi) in the western Pacific, the southernmost and largest of the Mariana Islands, with about 169,000 people and its capital at Hagåtña, listed by the United Nations as a non-self-governing territory administered by the United States.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Its northern half is a coralline limestone plateau and its southern half mountainous, and its revenue comes chiefly from tourism and the United States military.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The CHamoru lived there when missionaries from Spain arrived in the late 17th century, the United States Navy administered the island from 1899 to 1950, and the Organic Act of Guam then gave it institutions of local government as an unincorporated territory.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>",
"Curaçao": "Curaçao is an island of 444 km² (171 sq mi) in the southern Caribbean, with its capital at Willemstad and a population the United Nations puts at about 186,000 and the World Bank at 156,000.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> It is one of the four countries of the Kingdom of the Netherlands, responsible for its own governance, education and judicial system, while the Kingdom answers for foreign relations, defence and Dutch nationality law.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The Netherlands Antilles were dissolved on 10 October 2010, when Curaçao and Sint Maarten became autonomous countries and Bonaire, Sint Eustatius and Saba became special municipalities of the Netherlands.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>",
"Aruba": "Aruba is an island of 180 km² (69 sq mi) in the southern Caribbean with about 108,000 people and its capital at Oranjestad, and it is one of the four countries of the Kingdom of the Netherlands.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> It has its own government headed by a Governor who represents the King, appointed for six years and reappointable once, and a Minister Plenipotentiary in The Hague who sits with the Dutch ministers in the Council of Ministers for the Kingdom.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The Kingdom as a whole answers for foreign relations, defence and Dutch nationality law, while Aruba decides its own policy on matters such as healthcare, tourism and employment.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>",
"United_States_Virgin_Islands": "The United States Virgin Islands are four main islands in the eastern Caribbean, Saint Croix, Saint John, Saint Thomas and Water Island, with a land area of about 352 km² (136 sq mi), roughly 104,000 people and their capital at Charlotte Amalie on Saint Thomas.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The United Nations lists them as a non-self-governing territory, administered by the United States as an unincorporated, organized territory through the Office of Insular Affairs, and tourism accounts for 80 per cent of output and of employment.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> They belonged to Denmark from 1754 to 1917, as the Danish West Indies, and on 31 March 1917 the Danish government transferred ownership to the United States.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>",
"Isle_of_Man": "The Isle of Man is an island of 572 km² (221 sq mi) with about 84,000 people and its capital at Douglas, and it is one of the three Crown Dependencies of the United Kingdom.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The Crown Dependencies are not part of the United Kingdom but self-governing dependencies of the Crown, with their own directly elected legislative assemblies, their own administrative, financial and legal systems and their own courts of law, though most rules and regulations match the United Kingdom’s.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The United Kingdom’s constitutional relationship with them is managed by its Ministry of Justice, and the island belongs, with Ireland and the United Kingdom, to the Common Travel Area.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>",
"Cayman_Islands": "The Cayman Islands are three islands of 264 km² (102 sq mi) some 290 km (180 miles) west of Jamaica — Grand Cayman, Cayman Brac and Little Cayman — with about 76,000 people and their capital at George Town.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> They are a non-self-governing territory administered by the United Kingdom, whose appointed Governor answers for external affairs, defence and internal security, and income per head was 97,583 US dollars in 2023.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> First settled by the British between 1661 and 1671, they built up self-government from 1734, established an assembly in 1831, abolished slavery in 1834 and became a dependency of Jamaica in 1863, remaining under the Crown when Jamaica became independent.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>",
"Bermuda": "Bermuda is a group of 8 major and 130 smaller islands in the western Atlantic, with a land area of 53.35 km² (21 sq mi) and roughly 65,000 people, all counted as urban.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> It is a non-self-governing territory under the United Nations Charter, administered by the United Kingdom, whose Governor answers for defence, external affairs, internal security and the police, while a premier heads a cabinet drawn from an elected House of Assembly.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Sighted by Juan de Bermúdez in 1505 and settled from a shipwreck in 1609, it passed to the British Crown in 1684 and lives on international insurance and tourism, with income per head of 125,842 US dollars in 2023.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>",
"Faroe_Islands": "The Faroe Islands are an archipelago of 18 islands in the North Atlantic, 1,393 km² (538 sq mi) in all, with about 55,000 people and their capital at Tórshavn.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Streymoy is the largest island at 374.89 km² (144.7 sq mi) and Slættaratindur on Eysturoy the highest point at 880 m (2,890 feet), while Lítla Dímun, the smallest, is the only one with no residents.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The Danish krone is the currency, the United Nations excludes the islands from its figures for Denmark, and European Union law does not reach them, Article 355 of the Treaty on the Functioning of the European Union providing that the Treaties shall not apply to them.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>",
"American_Samoa": "American Samoa is seven islands in the South Pacific — Tutuila, Aunuu, the three Manu’a Islands and two atolls — 200 km² (77 sq mi) in all, with 46,000 people and its seat of government at Pago Pago.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> It is a non-self-governing territory under the United Nations Charter and an unincorporated, unorganized United States territory, so not all American law applies there and most people born on it are United States nationals, not citizens.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The islands are thought to have been settled some 3,000 years ago from South-East Asia, and the Treaty of Berlin of 1899 apportioned them to the United States; the population has fallen by an eighth since 2015.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>",
"Turks_and_Caicos_Islands": "The Turks and Caicos Islands are 40 islands and cays of 948 km² (366 sq mi) some 145 km (90 miles) north of Haiti and the Dominican Republic, with about 47,000 people, most of them on Providenciales, and their capital at Cockburn Town on Grand Turk.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> They are a non-self-governing territory administered by the United Kingdom, whose Governor keeps defence, external affairs, internal security and the regulation of international financial services, with fifteen elected members in a House of Assembly.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Their first inhabitants were the Arawak-speaking Taino; Great Britain annexed them in 1799 as part of the Bahamas, they became a separate colony in 1962, and tourism and financial services carry the economy.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>",
"Northern_Mariana_Islands": "The Northern Mariana Islands are a commonwealth in political union with the United States, 457 km² (176 sq mi) in Micronesia, with about 44,000 people, a government seated at Capital Hill on Saipan and Garapan named as the capital by the United Nations.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> They formed part of the Trust Territory of the Pacific Islands, which the United States administered for the United Nations from 1947, until the Security Council determined on 22 December 1990 that the Trusteeship Agreement had ceased to apply to them.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> A federal Covenant of 1975 made them a United States territory, their constitution followed in 1977, and their population has fallen in every year since 2015.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>",
"Sint_Maarten": "Sint Maarten is one of the four countries of the Kingdom of the Netherlands, 34 km² (13 sq mi) in the Caribbean with about 44,000 people, all of them counted as urban, and its capital at Philipsburg.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> It became an autonomous country on 10 October 2010, when the Netherlands Antilles was dissolved and Bonaire, Sint Eustatius and Saba became special municipalities of the Netherlands instead.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The Kingdom answers for foreign relations, defence and Dutch nationality law while the country decides its own policy on matters such as healthcare and tourism, and hurricane Irma destroyed much of its housing and infrastructure on 6 September 2017.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>",
"British_Virgin_Islands": "The British Virgin Islands are some 60 islands, islets and cays of 151 km² (58 sq mi) in the Caribbean, twenty inhabited, with about 40,000 people and their capital at Road Town on Tortola.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> They are a non-self-governing territory administered by the United Kingdom, whose governor answers for defence, internal security, external affairs and the courts under the Virgin Islands Constitution Order 2007, and financial services and tourism carry the economy.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The Dutch made the first permanent European settlement in 1648, British planters took control in 1666, ministerial government came with the constitution of 1967, and a Commission of Inquiry into the territory’s governance reported in 2022 with 49 recommendations.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>",
"Gibraltar": "Gibraltar is a narrow peninsula of about 6 km² (2.3 sq mi) running south from the south-western coast of Spain, with roughly 40,000 people and 4,012.6 of them to the square kilometre.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> It is a non-self-governing territory administered by the United Kingdom, to which Spain ceded sovereignty by the Treaty of Utrecht in 1713, though Spain holds that article 10 ceded only the city, castle, port and fortifications, and still claims the territory.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Under the Gibraltar Constitution Order 2006 the Governor answers for external affairs, defence and internal security and the elected government for all else; with no farmland and no known natural resource, the economy rests on tourism and financial services.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>",
"Åland": "Åland is an autonomous, demilitarised, Swedish-speaking region of Finland: an archipelago of 1,586 km² (612 sq mi) in the Baltic between Finland and Sweden, with 30,836 residents and its only town at Mariehamn.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Its autonomy rests on an Act on the Autonomy of Åland passed by the Parliament of Finland, the third of which took effect in 1993 and can be amended only with the consent of Åland’s own 30-member Lagting.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Demilitarised by a Russian commitment at Paris in 1856 and neutralised by a convention drawn up when the League of Nations settled its affiliation in 1921, it joined the European Union with Finland in 1995 under a protocol of its own.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>",
"Anguilla": "Anguilla is a flat Caribbean island of 96 km² (37 sq mi) some 240 km (150 miles) east of Puerto Rico, with about 16,000 people and its capital at The Valley.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> It is a non-self-governing territory under the United Nations Charter, administered by the United Kingdom, whose Governor answers for defence, external affairs, internal security, international financial services and their regulation, and public service appointments.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Originally inhabited by the Arawaks and settled by the British and Irish in 1650, it was repeatedly attached to Saint Kitts and Nevis before becoming a United Kingdom dependency in 1980, and tourism and financial services now carry its economy.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>",
"Cook_Islands": "The Cook Islands are a self-governing state in free association with New Zealand, in Polynesia between French Polynesia and American Samoa, with about 13,000 people, 236 km² (91 sq mi) of land and a capital at Avarua.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The arrangement dates from 1965: the islands conduct their own affairs and their own international relations, while New Zealand answers requests for help with foreign affairs, disasters and defence, and Cook Islanders hold New Zealand citizenship.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> They graduated in 2019 to the category of high-income country, the first Pacific island state or territory to do so since 2000, and the United States recognised them as sovereign and independent on 25 September 2023.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>",
"Saint_Pierre_and_Miquelon": "Saint Pierre and Miquelon is a French overseas collectivity of 242 km² (93 sq mi) in the North Atlantic east of the Canadian coast, with about 6,000 people and its capital at Saint-Pierre.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> It became a collectivity under the organic law of 21 February 2007: a Territorial Council of 19 members elected for five years holds local power, taxation and customs fall to the territory, and a prefect represents the French State, whose law applies directly.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The archipelago is one of the European Union’s overseas countries and territories, listed in Annex II to the Treaty on the Functioning of the European Union, and fishing, processing and aquaculture remain its main economic hope.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>",
"Saint_Helena": "Saint Helena is a volcanic island in the South Atlantic, 1,900 km (1,200 miles) from Angola, with about 4,100 people and its capital at Jamestown; it is the seat of a non-self-governing territory administered by the United Kingdom.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> That territory takes in Ascension, 1,200 km (750 miles) to the north-west, and the Tristan da Cunha group 2,400 km (1,500 miles) to the south, together 309 km² (119 sq mi) of land.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Claimed by the Netherlands in 1633 but settled by the British East India Company in 1659, it became a Crown colony in 1833 and a British overseas territory in 2002, and the constitution of September 2009 gave the three island groups equal standing under one name.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>",
"Montserrat": "Montserrat is a wholly volcanic island of 103 km² (40 sq mi) in the eastern Caribbean, of which only 40.1 km² are habitable, with about 4,400 people; it is a non-self-governing territory administered by the United Kingdom.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Its capital, Plymouth, was abandoned in 1997 after the volcanic eruption, and most government offices now stand at Brades, while the volcano keeps the best farmland and fishing grounds out of reach.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Settled from 1632 by mostly Irish colonists, confirmed as British in 1783 and a Crown colony from 1871, it chose Crown colony status over associated statehood when the Federation of the West Indies broke up in 1962.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>",
"Falkland_Islands": "The Falkland Islands, which the United Nations calls the Falkland Islands (Malvinas), are East Falkland, West Falkland and hundreds of smaller islands totalling 12,173 km² (4,700 sq mi) in the South Atlantic, with 3,662 residents at the 2021 census and their capital at Stanley.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> They are a non-self-governing territory administered by the United Kingdom and have been on the United Nations list since 1946, under a constitution approved in 2008 and in force from 1 January 2009.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Argentina and the United Kingdom each entered reservations in December 1946 refusing to recognise the other’s sovereignty, and the General Assembly records the dispute in resolution 2065 (XX) and later resolutions.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>",
"Niue": "Niue is a raised coral island of 260 km² (100 sq mi) in the South Pacific with about 2,000 people and its capital at Alofi, self-governing in free association with New Zealand since 1974.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> It belongs to the Realm of New Zealand, so the two share a head of state and all Niueans are New Zealand citizens, and under the Niue Constitution Act 1974 New Zealand supplies economic and administrative help and assists with foreign affairs and defence on request.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> In return the two consult on matters of mutual concern; the United States recognised Niue as sovereign and independent on 25 September 2023.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>",
"Pitcairn_Islands": "The Pitcairn Islands are four islands in the South Pacific midway between New Zealand and South America — Pitcairn, Henderson, Ducie and Oeno — of which only Pitcairn is inhabited, by a few dozen people at Adamstown, its only settlement.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> They are a non-self-governing territory administered by the United Kingdom, with 35.5 km² (13.7 sq mi) of land inside an exclusive economic zone of 842,000 km² that is now a marine protected area.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Named after the midshipman who sighted them in 1767 and settled in 1790 by mutineers from the <i>Bounty</i> and their Tahitian companions, whose descendants most islanders are, they are governed under a constitution that took effect in March 2010.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>",
"Elegy": "An <b>elegy</b> in modern usage is a poem of lament, but in Greek and Latin verse the name belonged to a metre rather than a mood: any poem in couplets pairing a dactylic hexameter with a shorter pentameter.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The Greek <i>elegos</i> named a song of mourning close to the dirge, while <i>elegeion</i> was the metrical term for the couplet, which enters literature early in the 7th century BCE and was carried by the pipe, not the lyre.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Poems in the form ran from calls to arms and political argument to love, drinking and the epitaph, so the sorrowful tone the word now carries did not mark the ancient genre out.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>",
"Iambus": "<b>Iambus</b> is the archaic Greek genre of blame poetry, defined by what it does rather than by how it scans: its poets proclaim <i>psogos</i> against people who break the rules a community lives by, naming them outright.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> It appears suddenly in the middle of the 7th century BCE with Archilochus of Paros, is Ionian, and had a place in the popular festivals of Demeter and Dionysus that also lie behind Attic comedy.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Its staple line is the iambic trimeter, which Aristotle called the most conversational of metres and which tragedy and comedy took over for dialogue.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>",
"Archilochus": "<b>Archilochus</b> of Paros was a Greek poet of the middle of the 7th century BCE, the first who speaks in his own voice about his own life, and antiquity ranked him beside Homer and Hesiod.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> He worked chiefly in iambic and elegiac verse, which appears in his hands already finished and exact, and his most quoted lines shrug at a shield he left behind in flight, since he had saved himself and could get another no worse.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Paros later worshipped him as a hero in an open-air precinct called the Archilocheion, whose inscription preserves oracles and an account of his life.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>",
"Sappho": "<b>Sappho</b> was a Greek poet of Lesbos, born at Eresos and living at Mytilene around 600 BCE, who was reckoned in antiquity the greatest love poet of the Greek world and called simply the Poetess.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> She wrote in the Aeolic dialect actually spoken on the island, in a great variety of metres, one of them the four-line stanza that carries her name.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Alexandria edited her into nine books, of which almost nothing survives whole: she is read in quotations by later critics and in torn papyri, with one complete poem, a hymn to Aphrodite.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>",
"Alcaeus": "<b>Alcaeus</b> was a Greek poet of Mytilene on Lesbos, active around 600 BCE, and the one direct witness to the faction fighting that convulsed his island, in which he took part on the side of the aristocrats.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> His songs were sung at the drinking parties of the political clubs, and the best known of them are the <i>stasiotika</i>, verses of faction attacking one ruler after another.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> He worked in the Aeolic tradition of the island beside Sappho and in the same dialect, wrote hymns, drinking songs and love songs as well, and gave his name to a four-line stanza Horace made famous.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>",
"Lesbos": "<b>Lesbos</b> is a large Greek island in the north-eastern Aegean, lying close to the coast of Asia Minor, whose Aeolian population was divided in antiquity between five cities, a sixth having been enslaved by its neighbours at Methymna.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The Greeks reckoned it the most musical ground they knew, and it produced Terpander of Antissa, Arion of Methymna, Sappho of Eresos and Alcaeus of Mytilene within about a century of one another.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The metres its poets used are still classified as Aeolic, among them the hendecasyllabic line Sappho and Alcaeus shared and the four-line stanzas that carry their two names.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>",
"Mimnermus": "<b>Mimnermus</b> was a Greek elegist of the later 7th century BCE, whom the ancient notices place at Colophon or at Smyrna, the city Colophon had settled, and who turned elegy from war towards pleasure and its shortness.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> His best-known work was the <i>Nanno</i>, named after a woman who played the reed pipe, and he was remembered as a singer rather than a reciter, his elegy performed to that pipe.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The theme that runs through the surviving fragments is that youth is brief and old age hateful, and the plan of his book of elegies gathered under a beloved's name shaped Alexandrian and Roman elegy after him.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>",
"Theognis": "<b>Theognis</b> of Megara is the name under which about 1,400 lines of archaic Greek elegy have come down, by far the largest such body and by far the best preserved of any Greek elegist.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The collection was assembled as a schoolbook of maxims and shows the interpolation a schoolbook attracts, so editors use the name for what the manuscripts attribute rather than as a claim about a single author.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Most of the poems address a younger man, Cyrnus, whose name is called a seal set on the verses, and their politics are those of a beaten aristocrat who calls the men now in charge base.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>",
"Anacreon": "<b>Anacreon</b> was a Greek lyric poet of Teos in Ionia, one of the nine of the Alexandrian canon, who sang of wine, desire and the drinking party and spent his working life at the courts of tyrants.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> He left Teos with his fellow citizens when the Persians came in 545 BCE, helped found Abdera in Thrace, and appears afterwards at the court of Polycrates on Samos and then at Athens with Hipparchus.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Alexandria divided him into five books, and a later body of imitations known as the <i>Anacreontea</i>, first printed in 1554, so shaped European taste that its genial old toper has largely displaced the poet himself.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>",
"Ibycus": "<b>Ibycus</b> was a Greek lyric poet of the 6th century BCE, born at Rhegion in southern Italy and counted among the nine of the Alexandrian canon of melic verse.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> He wandered like the rhapsodes before settling on Samos at the court of Polycrates, where Anacreon was with him, the two being the first poets known to have put their art at a tyrant's disposal, and it is from that association that his ancient dates were reckoned.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> His early work followed Stesichorus into heroic legend and the later fragments are full of roses, spring and desire, but he is best remembered for a late story that cranes avenged his murder.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>",
"Stesichorus": "Stesichorus was a Greek choral poet of the 6th century BCE, connected in ancient accounts with Matauros in southern Italy and with Himera in Sicily, and known by a name meaning chorus-setter that may be a title rather than a birth name.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> He composed long narrative poems on heroic legend, among them a <i>Geryoneis</i>, an <i>Oresteia</i> and treatments of the Theban story, in stanzas built from lengths of the epic hexameter, which is why antiquity called him a lyric Homer.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Little survives except through quotation and papyrus finds, and his dates rest on the calculations of ancient chronographers rather than on any record of his life.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>",
"Simonides": "Simonides of Ceos was a Greek lyric and elegiac poet, born at Iulis on the Aegean island of Ceos, whose long career took him from the court of Hipparchus at Athens to the princes of Thessaly and finally to Syracuse.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> He worked in almost every choral and elegiac kind, is credited with giving the victory ode its artistic form, and was charged with avarice for writing at a fixed price rather than for unspecified patronage.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> He is best remembered for commemorative verse on the Persian Wars, including the epitaph for the Spartan dead at Thermopylae and an elegy on Plataea recovered from papyrus.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>",
"Symposium": "A symposium was the drinking party that followed dinner in an ancient Greek house, at which the guests reclined on couches rather than sitting, an eastern fashion the Greeks adopted in the second half of the 7th century BCE.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The company was as small as the number of couches, the evening opened with libations and a sung hymn, and wine was drunk mixed with water in proportions that were argued over.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Much Athenian fine pottery takes its shapes from this room, and the songs and talk that filled it are the setting of works by Plato and Xenophon and the occasion of much archaic lyric.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>",
"Skolion": "A skolion was a song sung over the wine at an ancient Greek drinking party, the name meaning 'crooked' for a reason the ancients themselves disputed, explaining it variously by the zigzag order of the singing or by the winding of the melody.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Ancient accounts separate the paean the whole company sang after the libations from the songs each guest took up in turn, a myrtle branch passing from hand to hand in place of a lyre, and from the harder songs left to skilled singers.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> A collection of Attic examples preserved by Athenaeus, made in Athens shortly before 450 BCE, includes the celebrated song on Harmodius and Aristogeiton.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>",
"Aesop": "Aesop is the name under which the Greek beast fable was collected, and the ancient record points to a legendary figure supplied to account for the stories rather than to an author whose writings survive.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Herodotus, the earliest witness, describes him as a story-writer and a slave of Iadmon of Samos whom the Delphians killed, and ancient chronographers placed that death in 564 BCE.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> A fictional biography composed between the 1st century BCE and the 2nd century CE made him a grotesquely ugly slave granted speech by a goddess, while the fables themselves are anonymous popular matter, about a hundred of them touching on religion.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>",
"Presocratic_philosophy": "Presocratic philosophy is the modern name for the natural inquiry pursued in the Greek cities during the 6th and 5th centuries BCE by thinkers whom antiquity took to be the first philosophers of its tradition.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Where Hesiod had generated the world by births and unions among gods, they looked for principles inherent in the world itself, and Aristotle reports that most of the earliest recognised only material ones.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> No complete work survives, so the subject rests on quoted fragments and on later reports, and the label is an 18th-century coinage that fails as chronology, the last of these thinkers being contemporaries of Socrates.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>",
"Thales": "Thales of Miletus was an Ionian thinker of the early 6th century BCE whom Aristotle named as the first to inquire into nature in the manner that became Greek natural philosophy.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Aristotle reports his doctrine that the permanent entity is water and that the earth floats upon it, but supplies the supporting reasons himself, since Thales is said to have written no book.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Herodotus credits him with foretelling the loss of daylight that stopped a battle between Lydians and Medes, a prediction the tradition dates to 585 BCE and which he can hardly have made.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>",
"Anaximander": "Anaximander of Miletus was an Ionian thinker remembered as the successor of Thales, and the first Greek of whom a sentence survives in something close to his own words.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> That sentence, quoted by Simplicius, makes the first principle neither water nor any other familiar element but the indefinite, from which the heavens and the worlds in them arise and into which they pass away, giving one another penalty and recompense for their injustice.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Aristotle reports his answer to what holds the earth up, that it has no reason to move, and antiquity credited him with the sundial gnomon, a globe and the first map of land and sea.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>",
"Anaximenes": "Anaximenes of Miletus, son of Eurystratus, was the third and last of the Milesian thinkers, remembered as the pupil of Anaximander and placed by Apollodorus at the time of the fall of Sardis.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> He made air the first principle, and Aristotle set him beside Diogenes of Apollonia as holding air to be prior to water and most truly first among the bodily elements.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> His distinctive contribution is a stated mechanism of change, condensation and rarefaction, by which air rarefied becomes fire and air condensed becomes in turn wind, cloud, water, earth and finally stone, with the rest coming to be from these.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>",
"Milesian_school": "The Milesian school is the name given to three thinkers of the Ionian city of Miletus, Thales, Anaximander and Anaximenes, active across the 6th century BCE and conventionally counted the first Greek philosophers.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Ancient doxographers arranged them as a master-and-pupil succession, which may not be true, though their views resemble one another closely enough to justify treating the three together.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Each named an originating stuff and explained the world from it without appeal to anything outside nature, and each pursued measurement and observation alongside the questions about causes that Aristotle later read as a concern with material principles.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>",
"Pythagoras": "Pythagoras was a Greek thinker who grew up on Samos and moved at about forty to Croton in southern Italy, where most of his activity took place.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> He wrote nothing and no contemporary described his thought in detail, so almost everything later ascribed to him comes from a tradition that had already turned him into a semi-divine originator of all Greek philosophy.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The earliest evidence makes his fame rest instead on the fate of the soul after death, which his contemporary Xenophanes already has him teaching, on religious ritual, on wonder-working, and on a strict way of life that Plato says his followers were still keeping generations later.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>",
"Pythagoreanism": "Pythagoreanism names several things at once: the way of life and doctrine of the soul taught by Pythagoras, the philosophy of a group active in the 5th and 4th centuries BCE whom Aristotle calls the so-called Pythagoreans, and a later habit of ascribing almost any early idea to Pythagoras.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Aristotle reports the group as holding that the principles of mathematics are the principles of everything, that numbers are the ultimate things in the universe, and that ten pairs of opposites underlie it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Its communities split between those who kept the inherited rules of life and those who pursued mathematics, and died out about the middle of the 4th century BCE.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>",
"Xenophanes": "Xenophanes of Colophon was a Greek poet of the late 6th and early 5th centuries BCE who travelled the Greek world reciting his own verse and criticising what the poets said about the gods.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> He is best known for attacking the habit of imagining gods in human form, observing that Ethiopians make theirs snub-nosed and black and Thracians theirs red-haired, and that horses able to draw would produce horse-shaped gods.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Against that he set one god greatest among gods and men, unlike mortals in body or thought, and he held that no mortal will ever know anything about such matters with certainty.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>",
"Heraclitus": "Heraclitus was a Greek philosopher of Ephesus in Ionia who flourished around 500 BCE, was nobody's pupil and said he had inquired of himself.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> He wrote one book, deposited in the temple of Artemis and divided into three discourses, on the universe, politics and theology, which he was said to have made deliberately obscure so that none but adepts should approach it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Nothing of it survives whole, but over a hundred quotations do, turning on the unity of opposites, on a world-order no god or man made but which is everliving fire kindled and quenched in measures, and on a <i>logos</i> common to all that most fail to grasp.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>",
"Parmenides": "Parmenides was a Greek philosopher of Elea in southern Italy, born about 515 BCE, whom Diogenes Laertius calls the son of Pyres and a pupil of Xenophanes who followed him in nothing.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> He wrote a single poem in epic hexameters, perhaps 800 verses long and surviving in about 160, in which a goddess sets out the unshaken heart of well-rounded reality and, separately, the notions of mortals, in which there is no genuine trustworthiness.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Its central argument is that What Is is ungenerated and deathless, whole and uniform, still and perfect, and that the road of not-being can be neither thought nor spoken.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>",
"Zeno_of_Elea": "Zeno of Elea was a Greek philosopher of Elea in southern Italy, born about 490 BCE, whom Diogenes Laertius calls the pupil and adopted son of Parmenides and reports that Aristotle named the inventor of dialectic.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> He wrote a book of arguments, all directed against the common assumption that things are many and cast as antinomies: if existences are many, they must be both like and unlike, which is impossible.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Four arguments against motion survive in Aristotle's summary, among them the quickest runner who never overtakes the slowest and the flying arrow that is motionless, and these are what are now called his paradoxes.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>",
"Eleatics": "The Eleatics are a group of Greek thinkers linked to Elea, a colony in southern Italy, and credited in antiquity with the thesis that what there is, is one.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Plato is the earliest writer to treat them as a group, calling them a sect of his own region and tracing them back to Xenophanes and even earlier, and the membership later fixed on is Parmenides, Zeno and Melissus.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The name is a matter of doctrine rather than geography, since Xenophanes came from Colophon in Ionia and Melissus was a Samian, and Aristotle already separates their positions, making Parmenides' unity finite and Melissus' unlimited.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>",
"Empedocles": "Empedocles was a Greek philosopher and poet of Acragas in Sicily, active in the middle of the 5th century BCE, who wrote in the hexameters of epic and was remembered as a doctor and an orator as well.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> His system makes everything out of four roots, which he names as gods rather than as fire, earth, air and water, mixed and parted by two forces called Love and Strife in a cycle without end.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Aristotle credits him with being the first to make the material elements four and the first to give motion two contrary causes instead of one, while objecting that he uses neither consistently.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>",
"Hecataeus_of_Miletus": "Hecataeus of Miletus was a Greek prose writer of the late 6th and early 5th centuries BCE who appears in Herodotus both as the adviser who alone opposed the Ionian revolt and as an authority to be contradicted.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Antiquity knew a prose history under his name and a description of the earth, and nothing of either survives except in later quotation; Strabo, following Eratosthenes, pairs him with Anaximander as the two who came after Homer in geography.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Demetrius preserves his opening: I write these things as they seem to me to be true, for the tales told by the Greeks are, as it appears to me, many and absurd.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>",
"Alcmaeon_of_Croton": "Alcmaeon of Croton was a Greek writer of the early 5th century BCE from a city in southern Italy, described by Diogenes Laertius as writing chiefly on medicine, though whether he practised it is disputed.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> He is the earliest figure on record to place understanding in the brain and to distinguish understanding from perception, holding that the sense organs are joined to the brain by channels.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> He defined health in the language of politics, as the equality of the powers in the body — wet and dry, hot and cold, bitter and sweet — with the sole rule of any one of them producing disease.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>",
"Achaemenid_Empire": "The Achaemenid Empire was the Persian state ruled by the house of Achaemenes, reaching from Ionia in the west to Gandara in the east.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Darius set out its extent in his own words on the rock at Behistun, naming 23 lands that had come to him and that brought him tribute, and titling himself the great king, the king of kings, the king of the provinces.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Herodotus describes it instead as an administrative machine, saying that Darius divided it into 20 provinces, which they call satrapies, and fixed for each a yearly tribute reckoned by the talent.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>",
"Cyrus_the_Great": "Cyrus the Great founded the Persian empire, and on his clay cylinder from Babylon he takes the old Babylonian titles and traces himself through Cambyses and an earlier Cyrus to Teispes, all kings of Anshan.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> That cylinder is a Babylonian text of a conventional kind, denouncing Nabonidus for neglecting the cult until Marduk chose Cyrus and brought him into the city without battle.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Herodotus gives his death as a defeat by the Massagetae one year short of thirty years on the throne, and Greek writers treat him unusually warmly, Aeschylus calling him blessed and Plato crediting his rule with a due measure between slavery and freedom.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>",
"Lydia": "Lydia was a kingdom of western Anatolia centred on Sardis, beneath Mount Tmolus on the Hermus plain, whose kings came to rule the peoples west of the river Halys and to take tribute from the Greek cities of the coast.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Its language, Lydian, belonged to the Anatolian branch of Indo-European and was written in an alphabet of Greek-looking letters that is not descended straight from the Phoenician script.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Tombs are the largest class of monument it left, scores of mounds standing at Bin Tepe north of the city, and Sardis fell to Cyrus about 547 BCE, after which Lydia was ruled by Persian satraps.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>",
"Phocaea": "Phocaea was an Ionian city of the Asia Minor coast whose people, Herodotus says, were the earliest Greeks to make long sea voyages, finding the Adriatic, Tyrrhenia, Iberia and Tartessus in fifty-oared ships rather than round freighters.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Thucydides has them defeating a Carthaginian fleet while they were founding Marseilles, in whose citadel Strabo describes temples of Ephesian Artemis and Delphian Apollo.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Besieged by the Persian general Harpagus, its people asked for a day to deliberate and used it to load their families, goods and temple statues aboard and sail; survivors of that flight later founded the Italian town called Hyele.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>",
"Cambyses_II": "Cambyses II was the second king of the Achaemenid Persian empire, the son and successor of Cyrus the Great, reigning seven years and five months.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> His reign is remembered above all for the conquest of Egypt in 525 BCE, which ended the country’s history under native dynasties.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Greek writers, and Herodotus in particular, present him as violent and unbalanced, telling how he stabbed the sacred Apis bull at Memphis and killed his own brother, while the one Persian record of his reign, the inscription Darius cut at Behistûn, confirms only that he had a brother Smerdis and slew him in secret.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>",
"Darius_I": "Darius I was the Achaemenid king of Persia who took the throne on the death of Cambyses and held it for 36 years.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> On a cliff at Behistun he had his own account of that accession carved in three languages, declaring that a Magian called Gaumata had lied by taking the name of Cyrus' son Smerdis, and that nine further rebel kings lied in the same way.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Herodotus instead makes him one of seven conspirators who killed the Magian and won the throne by a groom's trick, while Plato remembers a man who was no king's son who divided the empire and fixed its tribute by law.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>",
"Satrapy": "A satrapy was a province of the Achaemenid Persian empire, governed by a satrap whom the king appointed, a title Darius uses at Behistun of the men he set over Bactria and Arachosia.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Herodotus says Darius divided his dominions into 20 such provinces and fixed each one's yearly tribute, while Xenophon describes a governor answering at a yearly review for the cultivation of his land as well as its garrisons.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Because one man held both the revenue and the troops of a whole region, the office could rival the throne, and Herodotus tells how Darius had Oroetes of Sardis killed by his own guards with a sealed letter.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>",
"Royal_Road": "The Royal Road was the highway of the Achaemenid Persian empire, running from Sardis in western Anatolia to Susa, which Herodotus reckoned at 111 posting stations and 450 parasangs, or 90 days of walking.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> It carried a relay courier post the Persians called the angareion, with a man and a horse waiting at each day's interval, of which Herodotus says that nothing mortal travels faster, stopped by neither snow nor rain nor heat nor darkness.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Xenophon credits Cyrus with the posting stations, and the administrative tablets from Persepolis show the system working, recording rations issued to travellers a day's journey apart and express horses kept permanently at the posts.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>",
"Achaemenid_army": "The Achaemenid army was the military force of the Persian empire, drawn from its many subject peoples and marching, as Xenophon saw it at Cunaxa, in national divisions, each a solid square.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Herodotus equips its Persian troops with iron-scaled tunics, wicker shields, short spears, long bows and reed arrows, and Greek writers took its weapon to be the bow, Aeschylus setting the drawing of the bow against the might of the spear-head.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Greek figures for its size are enormous, Herodotus giving 1,700,000 for the land army and then wondering how so many were fed, and at Plataea judging its men no less brave but unarmoured against opponents fully armed.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>",
"Immortals": "The Immortals were a corps of ten thousand picked Persian soldiers in the Achaemenid army, known from Herodotus, who says the number was kept exact by choosing a replacement whenever one of them died or fell sick.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> He calls them the best men in the army and the most richly adorned, marching about the king with golden and silver pomegranates on their spear-shafts, and names Hydarnes son of Hydarnes as their general.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The name is Greek, and Xenophon describes a royal guard of the same size without using it, while at Thermopylae Herodotus says the corps fared no better than the Medes it was sent in to relieve.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>",
"Scythian_campaign_of_Darius_I": "The Scythian campaign of Darius I was a Persian expedition across the Bosphorus and the Danube into the country north of the Black Sea, dated by convention to about 513 BCE.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Herodotus is the main source, giving the army as 700,000 men with 600 ships and saying that the Scythians refused battle and withdrew before it until Darius turned back to the Danube bridge his Ionian tyrants guarded.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Strabo says he was nearly cut off for want of water, Justin gives the war a different cause and a heavy loss, and Darius' own inscription at Behistun records instead a Scythian war he won, taking the leader Skunkha prisoner.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>",
"Aristagoras": "Aristagoras was deputy tyrant of Miletus, a city Strabo gives four harbours and colonies throughout the Black Sea, and he raised the Ionian cities against Darius I after a failed Persian expedition against Naxos that he had proposed.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Herodotus has him carry to mainland Greece a bronze tablet engraved with a map of all the earth, which Cleomenes of Sparta refused on learning the king's seat lay three months inland, and which persuaded Athens to vote twenty ships.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> He abandoned the revolt he had begun, left Miletus in another man's charge and sailed to Thrace, where he was killed besieging a town, and Herodotus judges he had shown little courage.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>",
"Histiaeus": "Histiaeus was tyrant of Miletus under Darius I, rewarded for keeping the Persian bridge over the Danube and then held at Susa as the king's counsellor.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> With the roads watched, he sent word to Aristagoras that Ionia should revolt by shaving a trusted slave's head, marking it, waiting for the hair to grow, and telling him only to have it shaved again on arrival.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Sent down to settle the revolt he had begun, he was met at Sardis by Artaphernes, who told him he had stitched the shoe Aristagoras put on; he fled, was captured and impaled, and his head was sent embalmed to the king.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>",
"Ionian_Revolt": "The Ionian Revolt was the rising of the Greek cities of Asia Minor against Persian rule, beginning in 499 BCE when Aristagoras of Miletus gave up his own tyranny and put down the tyrants the king had installed elsewhere.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Athens sent twenty ships and Eretria five, Sardis was burnt, and the rising spread to Cyprus, the Hellespont and Caria before the Persians took the cities back one by one and netted the islands.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Artaphernes then fixed each city's tribute and made the Ionians settle disputes by law, Mardonius replaced their tyrants with democracies, and Nepos gives the burning of Sardis as the reason Darius alleged for invading Greece.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>",
"Sardis": "Sardis was a city in western Anatolia, on the northern slope of Mount Tmolus above the plain of the Hermus, capital in turn of the Lydian kingdom and seat of Persian government in the region.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Strabo describes it as a large city with a strong citadel, and says the Pactolus once carried down from the mountain above the gold dust behind the proverbial wealth of Croesus.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> It was burned in 498 BCE by Ionian, Athenian and Eretrian troops who took the lower town but not the citadel, and the fire, which destroyed a temple of the goddess Cybebe, was afterwards given as a Persian reason for burning Greek temples.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>",
"Battle_of_Lade": "The Battle of Lade was the sea fight of 494 BCE, off the small island of that name near Miletus, in which a Persian fleet destroyed the combined navy of the Greek cities of Asia and ended their revolt.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Herodotus counts 353 Ionian triremes against 600 on the Persian side, and describes an alliance that broke before the fighting: the crews refused their commander's drill, the Samians withdrew on terms arranged beforehand, and the Lesbians and most of the rest went with them.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The Chians fought on and lost most of their ships, and Herodotus declines to say who behaved well, since the cities afterwards blamed one another.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>",
"Didyma": "Didyma, also called Branchidae after the priestly family that served it, was the oracle and sanctuary of Apollo near Miletus, joined to the city by a Sacred Way and holding one of the largest temples of the archaic age.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Strabo records that the Branchidae gave up the god's treasures to the Persian king and went away with him to escape punishment for the sacrilege, and that the temple was burnt on the order of Xerxes.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Herodotus instead puts the plundering and burning with the fall of Miletus in 494 BCE, and Pausanias says Xerxes carried off its bronze Apollo, which Seleucus afterwards sent home.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>",
"Phrynichus_(tragic_poet)": "Phrynichus was an Athenian tragic poet of the early 5th century BCE whose plays are lost, and whose best-known work was a tragedy on the fall of Miletus to the Persians.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Herodotus records that the audience wept at it, that the city fined him a thousand drachmas for recalling a calamity it felt as its own, and that the play was forbidden for ever; Strabo, citing Callisthenes, reports the same fine and gives the title differently.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> He nonetheless won the tragic prize in 476 BCE with Themistocles as his choregos, and Athenian audiences were still singing his songs when Aristophanes wrote.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>",
"Mardonius": "Mardonius was a Persian commander of the early 5th century BCE, son of Gobryas and married to a daughter of Darius, who led the expedition of 492 BCE that carried a Persian army into Europe against Athens and Eretria.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Herodotus says he deposed the tyrants of Ionia and set up democracies on his way, and that his fleet was wrecked rounding Athos and his camp attacked in Macedonia, so that he turned back without reaching Greece.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> He commanded again in the invasion of 480 BCE and was left in Greece with the army when Xerxes withdrew, and the campaign ended in his defeat in Boeotia the following year.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>",
"Earth_and_water": "Earth and water were the tokens a Persian king's heralds demanded as a sign of submission, sent to peoples as far apart as the Scythians on the steppe, the king of Macedonia and the cities of Greece.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Herodotus says Darius used the demand to learn whether the Greeks would fight or submit, that many on the mainland and every island approached gave the tokens, and that Athens and Sparta instead killed the heralds who brought the request.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The phrase is a Greek one, and Darius' own inscription at Behistun states submission differently, listing the lands that became subject to him and brought him tribute.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>",
"Western_Zhou": "Western Zhou is the first phase of the Zhou dynasty in China, commonly dated from 1045 to 771 BCE and followed by the Eastern Zhou from 770 to 221 BCE.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Its royal domain lay in the Guanzhong basin of present-day central Shaanxi, where a capital called Fengyi stood on the west bank of the Feng river and has yielded rammed-earth palace foundations, cemeteries, buried bronzes and craft workshops.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The court granted land to members of the royal Ji clan and to allied lineages, whose regional states served as political alliances and military outposts, and bronze inscriptions distinguish rulers of the royal lineage from the heads of merely affiliated states.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>",
"Mandate_of_Heaven": "Mandate of Heaven, tianming, is the Chinese doctrine that Heaven grants a house the right to rule and takes it back from one that governs badly, so power is held on terms, not by right.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The Zhou proclaimed it to justify their conquest of the Shang, arguing from an earlier transfer from the Xia, and the <i>Book of Documents</i> states that the Supreme Ruler is not invariable in his decree.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Mencius turned it into a theory of history in which only a moral man receives it, Xunzi rejected it outright, and it went on shaping how disasters were answered into the Han period and long after.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>",
"Chinese_characters": "Chinese characters are the graphic units of the writing system used for Chinese, the oldest script still in daily use and one of only three known to have been invented independently rather than adapted from a neighbour.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The earliest large body of them survives on oracle bones and bronzes of the late Shang and Western Zhou, where the shapes work by visual metaphor and metonymy rather than by recording the sounds of speech.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Five stages are conventionally distinguished, from oracle bone through bronze, seal and official forms to the regular script written today, and a measurement over half a million images finds no consistent simplification across that span.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>",
"Silk": "Silk is a protein fibre spun by moth caterpillars into a cocoon, most of the thread being the protein fibroin, and it has been a luxury commodity throughout recorded history.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The oldest direct evidence for it is biomolecular rather than textile, peptides of silk fibroin recovered by mass spectrometry from soil in three 8,500-year-old tombs at the Neolithic site of Jiahu in central China.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Where and when the rearing of silkworms began is still an open question, since before the mulberry silkworm was domesticated silk was taken from a range of wild moth species with regional specificity.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>",
"Jade": "Jade is a hard ornamental stone, in East Asia chiefly nephrite, which is made of the two amphibole minerals tremolite and actinolite, told apart by their ratio of magnesium to iron.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> It was worked across the Chinese Neolithic from about 6200 BCE, first in the Liao River region and later most elaborately in the Hongshan and Liangzhu cultures, whose prestige forms include the cong tube and the bi disc.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Early pieces are largely personal ornaments, but from the middle of the 4th millennium BCE ritual items dominate the assemblages and accompany the first elite groups, and no text before the Eastern Zhou records what the abstract shapes meant.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>",
"Yamnaya_culture": "Yamnaya culture is the archaeological complex that appeared across the steppes north of the Black and Caspian seas about 3300 BCE and by 3000 BCE ran from Hungary in the west to Kazakhstan in the east.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Ancient DNA from 435 individuals traces its ancestry to three genetic clines among the preceding Eneolithic peoples rather than to any single earlier population, with about four-fifths coming from a Caucasus-lower Volga cline.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> It is known chiefly from the burial mounds that dominate the region, whose bones carry isotopic evidence of a herding economy of sheep, goat and cattle, and its people rode horses of the line from which modern domestic stock descends.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>",
"Horse_domestication": "Domestication of the horse is the process that turned a hunted grassland animal into a means of transport, and modern domestic stock descends from a population of the western Eurasian steppes, above all the lower Volga-Don.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The horses bridled, milked and corralled at Botai in Central Asia about 3500 BCE left no descendants among that stock, which spread rapidly across Eurasia and replaced almost every local lineage.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Reproductive control of the modern lineage is dated to about 2200 BCE by close-kin mating and shortened generation times, though it is disputed whether a genetic shift alone marks domestication, since horses were managed and ridden long before.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>",
"Chariot": "A chariot is a light two-wheeled vehicle drawn by a team of horses, and the spoke-wheeled form built by the Sintashta culture of the Eurasian steppes around 2000 BCE is the earliest of the kind.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> It spread rapidly across Eurasia alongside the modern domestic horse line and the rest of the equestrian material culture, and by the close of the 2nd millennium BCE harnessed warriors were common across most ancient civilisations.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Teams were driven in war and in racing, from the funeral games of Homer’s <i>Iliad</i> to the Roman circuses, and keeping them demanded a training regime that Hittite tablets set down day by day.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>",
"Hattusa": "Hattusa was the capital of the Hittite kingdom and empire in central Anatolia, the political and religious centre of one of the major powers of the ancient Near East between about 1650 and 1200 BCE.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Its walled site is on the World Heritage list for its urban organisation and for its surviving temples, royal residences and fortifications, together with the carved Lions', Sphinxes' and Royal Gates, and the rock sanctuary of Yazılıkaya lies about 3 kilometres (2 miles) to the north.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Archaeological work now indicates that the city was emptied by the royal administration and burnt only afterwards, and the reasons for its abandonment remain unclear.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>",
"Proto-Sinaitic_script": "The Proto-Sinaitic script is the earliest known alphabetic writing, linear pictographic signs cut on statuettes, stone panels and rock faces at Serabit el-Khadem, an Egyptian mining site in the Sinai, with a closely similar script at Wadi el-Hol in Upper Egypt.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Its signs take their shapes from Egyptian hieroglyphs without being used as Egyptian used them, and each is generally held to stand for the first sound of the Semitic word its picture names, a principle some dispute.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Neither group of inscriptions has a stratified context, so proposed dates run from the opening of the 2nd millennium BCE to the 14th century.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>",
"Amarna_letters": "The Amarna letters are the diplomatic correspondence of the Egyptian court in the 14th century BCE, 382 clay tablets from the city Akhenaten built as his capital, covering a window of only fifteen to thirty years.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> They are written mostly in Akkadian, the language diplomacy used across the region whatever the sender spoke at home, and some forty are to or from the rulers Egypt treated as great kings.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Between those kings the language is brotherhood and the business is goods and royal marriages, while the mayors of the Syrian and Palestinian towns write instead about raids and rivals and ask for troops.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>",
"Bell_Beaker_culture": "<b>Bell Beaker culture</b> is an assemblage of grave goods that spread across western and central Europe in the 3rd millennium BCE, defined by an inverted-bell drinking vessel buried with copper daggers, flint arrowheads, stone wristguards and V-perforated buttons.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Its oldest radiocarbon dates come from Atlantic Iberia; by 2500 BCE it reached from northwest Africa to central Europe, and Britain and Ireland a century later.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Ancient DNA shows it spread differently in different regions: affinity between Iberia and central Europe is slight and Iberia changed little from the Neolithic to the Bronze Age, while in Britain its arrival brought steppe-related ancestry and replaced about 90 per cent of the gene pool.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>",
"Únětice_culture": "<b>Únětice culture</b> is the Early Bronze Age culture of central Europe, current from about 2200 to 1550 BCE across Bohemia, Moravia, central Germany and western Poland, and named after a village near Prague.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Most of its dead were buried in flat inhumation graves, but a few lay under large mounds with gold and bronze, among them the tomb at Leubingen whose timber chamber has been tree-ring dated to 1942 BCE.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Ancient DNA from its cemeteries shows communities organised patrilineally, with men remaining where they were born and women marrying in from elsewhere, so that sons inherited the farmstead and daughters married out.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>",
"Nebra_sky_disc": "<b>Nebra sky disc</b> is a bronze disc about 32 centimetres (13 inches) across, inlaid with gold showing a sun or full moon, a crescent and a scatter of stars, among which a cluster of seven is widely read as the Pleiades.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> It was buried on the Mittelberg hill in Saxony-Anhalt with two swords, two axes, two spiral arm-rings and a chisel, and is held to be the oldest concrete depiction of cosmic phenomena known anywhere.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Its copper came from the Mitterberg mine in the Austrian Alps and its gold from the Carnon River in Cornwall, and the image was altered in stages long after it was first hammered out.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>",
"Oxus_civilization": "<b>Oxus civilisation</b>, also called the Bactria-Margiana Archaeological Complex, is the Bronze Age culture of southern Central Asia, whose walled mud-brick centres stood in the irrigated oases of what are now Turkmenistan, Uzbekistan and northern Afghanistan.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> It belonged to a wide network of exchange reaching the Indus civilisation, Mesopotamia and the Syro-Anatolian world, and its bronze was made from Iranian copper and from the copper-tin mine at Mushiston in Tajikistan.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Genetically its people descend from the local Chalcolithic population rather than from incomers, and the Indo-Iranian speakers of Central Asia today draw their ancestry from them.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>",
"Neo-Assyrian_Empire": "The <b>Neo-Assyrian Empire</b> was the Mesopotamian state on the Tigris, in what is now northern Iraq, that dominated the Near East from about 912 to 609 BCE and at its height ran from central Anatolia to Egypt and eastward to western Iran.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Beaten kingdoms were usually held as tribute-paying clients and then annexed as provinces under governors, a post an indigenous ruler was sometimes given, and deportees were resettled in capitals larger than any earlier Mesopotamian city.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Its royal inscriptions, written for the palace and given to inflating their figures between editions, are the main narrative source, and they stop at a collapse that took Nineveh in 612 BCE.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>",
"Nineveh": "<b>Nineveh</b> was an ancient city on the Tigris, at what is now Mosul in northern Iraq, which Sennacherib made the Assyrian capital and rebuilt from about 704 BCE behind double walls with 15 gates.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Watering a city that size meant fetching it from far off, and his inscriptions record eighteen canals dug into the Khosr, the small tributary running through it, and a further supply drawn from the mountains and carried across a valley on a stone aqueduct at Jerwan.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> It fell to the armies of Babylon and the Medes in 612 BCE, after which what was left of the Assyrian kingdom held out at Harran further west.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>",
"Ashurbanipal": "<b>Ashurbanipal</b> was king of Assyria from 668 to about 631 BCE, ruling from Nineveh at the height of an empire that had become the largest the world had yet seen.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> His inscriptions are unusual in claiming scholarship for a king, saying he read the script of Sumer and the obscure Akkadian that is hard to master, though the same annals were re-edited between versions to enlarge his campaigns.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The tablets he gathered at Nineveh, about 30,000 pieces of correspondence, medicine, astronomy, omens and literature now in the British Museum, carry the fixed versions of many compositions on which modern reconstructions of them rely.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>",
"Mass_deportation": "<b>Mass deportation</b> is the forced movement of a whole population out of its homeland by a state, carried out to break resistance, to suppress revolt and to place the people moved where the state wants them.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> It is documented in Mesopotamia as early as the 3rd millennium BCE, though rarely, and had become a regular instrument of rule by the 1st, when one campaign could claim tens of thousands carried off with their livestock.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Those moved were normally resettled and absorbed rather than killed, so the practice worked as a labour and population policy as much as a punishment, filling the deporting state's own cities.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>",
"Neo-Babylonian_Empire": "The <b>Neo-Babylonian Empire</b>, also called the Chaldaean monarchy, was the state ruled from Babylon between the fall of Assyria and the Persian conquest, founded by Nabopolassar, whose war against Assyria took Nineveh in 612 BCE.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Its kings styled themselves providers of the great temples rather than conquerors, Nebuchadnezzar II, who ruled from 604 to 562 BCE, calling himself king of Babylon, provider of Esagila and Ezida, first-born son of Nabopolassar.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Its most visible remains are walls of glazed brick, where lions, dragons and bulls in white and ochre stand against a blue ground; it ended in 539 BCE when Cyrus II took Babylonia.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>",
"Nebuchadnezzar_II": "<b>Nebuchadnezzar II</b> was king of Babylon from 605 to 562 BCE, the longest reign of the Neo-Babylonian dynasty, and he came to the throne in the year he defeated Necho II of Egypt at Carchemish.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> He is known chiefly from building inscriptions rather than from annals of campaigns, and his foundation bricks call him king of Babylon, provider of Esagila and Ezida, first-born son of Nabopolassar.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The Ishtar Gate at Babylon carries his name on the bricks of its earliest phase, and the magnetism recorded in that fired clay puts the building of the whole complex at about 569 BCE, within his reign.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>",
"Hanging_Gardens_of_Babylon": "The <b>Hanging Gardens of Babylon</b> are a terraced garden described by Greek and Roman writers as one of the seven wonders of the world, planted on vaults and watered by engines that raised water from the river below.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> No Babylonian text locates or describes them and nothing found at Babylon has settled the question, so they are known only from accounts written long after the city they are placed in.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> One argument moves them to Nineveh, where Sennacherib describes a great park beside his palace and had cut canals that could water it, later writers having confused the two cities often enough for the swap to be possible.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>",
"Babylonian_captivity": "The <b>Babylonian captivity</b> was the deportation of Judaeans to Babylonia in a series of removals between 597 and 582 BCE, after the kingdom of Judah rebelled, and is conventionally reckoned to end when Persia took Babylon in 539 BCE.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Those taken were largely an elite of the royal house, officials, priests and skilled workers, so the land was not emptied: peasants remained and some fled to Egypt.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The deported can be read about directly in tablets from Al-Yahudu, a country town probably between Nippur and Uruk, where Judaean families farmed, took on Babylonian names and customs, and went on giving children names built round the name of their god.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>",
"Ancient_Israel_and_Judah": "<b>Ancient Israel and Judah</b> were two neighbouring kingdoms in the southern Levant during the Iron Age II, roughly 950 to 586 BCE, the northern one ruled from Samaria and the southern from Jerusalem.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The northern kingdom had the wider territory and sat across the trade routes, and the personal names recorded in it are more varied than those of the south, which has been read as a more cosmopolitan society.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Assyria conquered the north in the 8th century BCE and Babylon destroyed Jerusalem in 586 BCE, ending the southern kingdom, and both campaigns left burnt layers datable from the magnetic direction locked into their bricks.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>",
"Hebrew_Bible": "The <b>Hebrew Bible</b> is the collection of Jewish scriptures, written mostly in Hebrew with a few passages in Aramaic and arranged in three parts that were already being spoken of together by the 2nd century BCE as the Law, the Prophets and the remaining books.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> It was built up over centuries from texts composed, copied and revised by many hands, and analysis of word frequencies can separate distinct bodies of scribal writing within its first nine books.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Its wording was still somewhat fluid in antiquity and had settled by late Roman times into the consonantal text that medieval scribes transmitted and that most printed editions follow.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>",
"Judaism": "<b>Judaism</b> is the religion of the Jewish people, centred on the worship of one God and on the Torah, the body of law and narrative that its communities read, interpret and apply.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> It took much of its lasting shape in the Second Temple period, when a written law became the framework of communal life while remaining open to interpretation, revision and outright dispute.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> After the temple in Jerusalem was destroyed in 70 CE its practice moved to the reading and application of that law in local assemblies, the pattern by which it has been carried on since.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>",
"Phoenicia": "<b>Phoenicia</b> is the name given to the Iron Age culture of the Levantine coast, a line of independent city-states that lived by sea trade and had no single capital, their own inhabitants disputing whether Tyre or Sidon held the first place.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Its best-known product was a purple dye extracted from marine molluscs, although direct archaeological evidence for making it is scarce and only one excavated site anywhere shows sustained large-scale production.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Settlements carrying Phoenician language, script and religion spread across the central and western Mediterranean, yet ancient DNA from them holds almost no Levantine ancestry, so what travelled was a culture rather than a population.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>",
"Carthage": "<b>Carthage</b> was a city on the North African coast, in what is now Tunisia, founded from the Phoenician city of Tyre and dated by ancient convention to about 814 BCE.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> It belonged to a westward Phoenician expansion that also produced Utica and Gadir, became the leading city of the Punic west, and fought three wars with Rome, the last of which ended in its destruction in 146 BCE.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Genome-wide data from Punic sites shows its people drawing most of their ancestry from a profile resembling that of Sicily and the Aegean rather than from the Levant, with North African ancestry a minority contributor at every site sampled.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>",
"Urartu": "<b>Urartu</b> was an Iron Age kingdom in the mountains around Lake Van, in what is now eastern Turkey and Armenia, at its height between about 800 and 600 BCE.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Its kings set down a language of their own in the cuneiform script of Mesopotamia and built fortresses across the highlands, one of which is thought to survive as a massive mudbrick wall at Artaxata.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Most of what was written about it comes from Assyria, whose kings campaigned against it and recorded in detail the canals, orchards and shrines they destroyed there.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>",
"Medes": "The Medes were an Iranian-speaking people of the Zagros mountains and the western Iranian plateau, named in Assyrian annals from the 9th century BCE onwards and described there as a country of many local chiefs and mayors rather than as a single kingdom.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> In the Babylonian chronicle of Nabopolassar they appear under Cyaxares, taking Ashur and then joining the Babylonians in the destruction of Nineveh in 612 BCE.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Herodotus instead gives them four kings in succession and a capital at Ecbatana ringed by seven walls, ending when Cyrus deposed Astyages, and Darius later met a rebel in Media claiming the family of Cyaxares.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>",
"Guangdong": "Guangdong is a coastal province in the far south of mainland China, running along the South China Sea between roughly 20°09′ and 25°31′ north, divided into 21 prefecture-level cities and administered from Guangzhou.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Its position at the mouth of the Pearl River made it the point at which seaborne trade reached the empire, and under the Qing foreign merchants were confined to that one city until the treaties of the 1840s opened four other ports.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> In July 1979 the central government chose it, with Fujian, to lead the opening of the economy, and by August 1980 Shenzhen, Zhuhai and Shantou had been designated special economic zones.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>",
"Guangzhou": "Guangzhou is a city in southern China and the capital of Guangdong province, standing at the northern edge of the Pearl River Delta where the Beijiang, Xijiang and Dongjiang converge before reaching the South China Sea.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Known to Europeans as Canton, it was under the Qing the single port city through which foreign merchants could trade with the empire at all, a monopoly ended by the treaties that closed the First Opium War and opened five ports instead.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The 2020 census counted 18,676,605 residents in the municipality, which holds 446 nationally protected heritage sites, from Han-dynasty tombs to Republican-era arcades.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>",
"Shandong": "Shandong is a coastal province of northern China, a peninsula and its hinterland on the Bohai Gulf and the Yellow Sea, covering 157,900 square kilometres (61,000 square miles) and administered from Jinan.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> It is the agricultural heartland of the north, with light and warmth enough for two crop cycles a year, and the second most populous of the mainland's provincial-level divisions, with 101,527,453 residents at the 2020 census.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Germany took the territory of Kiaochow, with railway and mining rights across the province, by treaty in 1898, the Treaty of Versailles passed those rights to Japan rather than to China in 1919, and a treaty at the Washington Conference returned them in 1922.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>",
"Henan": "Henan is a province at the heart of China's Central Plains, spanning the middle and lower reaches of the Yellow River, covering about 167,000 square kilometres (64,500 square miles) and administered from Zhengzhou.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Plains make up 55.7 per cent of it and the Yellow River and Huai basins cross it, and the 2020 census counted 99,365,519 permanent residents, the third largest provincial population on the mainland.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Twenty dynasties ruled from within it and it holds seven ancient capitals, among them Anyang with its oracle bone inscriptions, Luoyang which served thirteen dynasties, and Zhengzhou, beneath which lies an early Shang capital of about 1509 to 1315 BCE.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>",
"Jiangsu": "Jiangsu is a coastal province of eastern China on the Yellow Sea, in the lower reaches of the Yangtze and the Huai, covering 107,200 square kilometres (41,400 square miles) and administered from Nanjing.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> It is almost flat, alluvial plain making up 69 per cent of it and most of it lying under 20 metres (66 feet), and it is crossed by the Yangtze for 433 kilometres (269 miles) and by the Beijing–Hangzhou Grand Canal for 718 kilometres (446 miles).<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Its capital gave its name to the treaty of 1842 that closed the First Opium War, and was the seat of the Nationalist Government from 1928 until the Japanese capture of the city in 1937, and again from 1946.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>",
"Sichuan": "Sichuan is a province of south-western China covering about 486,000 square kilometres (188,000 square miles), and it forms the transition between the Qinghai-Tibet Plateau and the plains of the middle and lower Yangtze.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Its land descends in steps from western highlands to eastern lowlands, stacking climates by altitude from subtropical in the basin to alpine in the west, and the 2020 census counted 83,674,866 residents there, the fifth largest provincial population on the mainland.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The basin in its east holds Sanxingdui, identified as a capital of the ancient kingdom of Shu, whose bronzes date to the late Shang and whose successor capital at Jinsha lies inside modern Chengdu.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>",
"Jinan": "Jinan is the capital of Shandong province in eastern China, standing where the mountains of the province's south meet the plain of its north.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> A barrier of impermeable plutonic rock cutting across a karst aquifer beneath the city governs where its groundwater comes to the surface, and the result is more than 800 springs, of which 136 well known ones rise within 2.6 square kilometres (1.0 square miles) of the urban centre.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Written Tsinanfu in the older romanisation, it was the inland end of the railway the Germans built from Tsingtao, which the Treaty of Versailles assigned to Japan before a treaty at the Washington Conference returned it in 1922.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>",
"Hebei": "Hebei is a province of northern China on the North China Plain, wrapping around Beijing and Tianjin and running from the Bohai Sea in the east to the Taihang Mountains in the west, covering 188,800 square kilometres (72,900 square miles) and administered from Shijiazhuang.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> It is heavily agricultural, sowing about 6.39 million hectares (15.8 million acres) of grain in 2019, and the 2020 census counted 74,610,235 residents there, the sixth largest provincial population on the mainland.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Growing that on a dry plain drew down one of the world's most severely depleted aquifers, whose levels have risen again since 2020 as surface water has been diverted from the humid south.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>",
"Zhengzhou": "Zhengzhou is the capital of Henan province in central China, a city of 7,446 square kilometres (2,875 square miles) standing where the western hills of the province give way to the eastern plain.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> It is one of Henan's seven ancient capitals, and beneath the modern city lies Zhengzhou Shang City, the capital of the early Shang from about 1509 to 1315 BCE, with inner and outer walls, palaces, workshops and cellars of bronzes.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> After the extreme rainstorm of 20 July 2021 the city developed three zones of land subsidence and two of uplift, the subsidence keeping step with falling groundwater.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>",
"Hunan": "Hunan is a province of south-central China, lying south of the middle Yangtze and covering about 211,800 square kilometres (81,800 square miles) in the transition from the Yunnan-Guizhou Plateau to the hills of Jiangnan and from the Nanling Mountains to the Jianghan Plain.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> It is hot and wet, averaging 16 to 18 degrees Celsius (61 to 64 degrees Fahrenheit) with 1,100 to 1,800 millimetres (43 to 71 inches) of rain a year, and forest covered 61.1 per cent of it in 2020.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The 2020 census counted 66,444,864 permanent residents, 4.71 per cent of the mainland's people, and its capital is Changsha.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>",
"Nanjing": "Nanjing is a city on the lower Yangtze in eastern China and the capital of Jiangsu province, with a river frontage of 280.82 kilometres (174.5 miles) across banks of mountains, hills and plains.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Its right bank holds the administrative and economic core and its left the newer districts and industrial clusters, and urban centres, industrial zones and nature reserves are interwoven along the shoreline.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Written Nanking in the older romanisation, it gave its name to the treaty that ended the First Opium War in 1842, and Ming-dynasty city walls still stand among modern buildings in the middle of the city.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>",
"Zhejiang": "Zhejiang is a coastal province of eastern China at the southern edge of the Yangtze delta, covering 105,500 square kilometres (40,700 square miles) and bordering Shanghai and Jiangsu to the north.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> It is mountainous rather than deltaic, with hills and mountains taking up 74.6 per cent of its territory, water another 5.1 per cent and plains only the remaining 20.3 per cent, and it ranks fourth among China's provincial-level divisions in industrial added value.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The 2020 census counted 64,567,588 permanent residents, 4.57 per cent of the mainland's people and a gain of more than ten million in a decade, second only to Guangdong, and its capital is Hangzhou.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>",
"Chengdu": "Chengdu is a city of south-western China and the capital of Sichuan province, standing on the Chengdu Plain in the Sichuan Basin.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The plain is the most populous part of the province and, through a system of artificial irrigation fed by the division of the Min river where it leaves the mountains, its garden and its granary.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> About 100 kilometres (60 miles) of the Min run through the modern city, draining some 30,000 square kilometres (11,600 square miles), and three smaller rivers take only 14 per cent of that area while carrying about 45 per cent of the city's people.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>",
"Anhui": "Anhui is an inland province of eastern China at the head of the Yangtze delta, covering about 141,000 square kilometres (54,400 square miles) between 114 and 120 degrees east and 29 and 35 degrees north.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> It divides into five regions, the northern plain, the plain along the Yangtze, the hills between the Yangtze and the Huai, and mountains in the south and the west, the northern plain being the largest, the most densely peopled and the chief farmland.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The 2020 census counted 61,027,171 permanent residents across its 16 prefecture-level cities and 61 counties, 4.32 per cent of the mainland's people, and its capital is Hefei.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>",
"Shijiazhuang": "Shijiazhuang is the capital and largest city of Hebei province, in northern China, and lies on the North China Plain within the Beijing-Tianjin-Hebei urban agglomeration, a province of 74,610,235 people at the 2020 census.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Rapid growth in population and in industry has left it with a standing air-pollution problem that bears directly on the health of its residents.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Its central district, Qiaoxi, in the south-west of the city, holds 970,000 people in about 76 square kilometres (29 square miles) and is the political, economic, cultural, educational and financial centre of the municipality, whose road emissions peak in the morning and evening rush hours.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>",
"Hubei": "Hubei is a province of central China at the core of the middle reaches of the Yangtze, a hub connecting the east, west, north and south of the country that is known as the Nine Provinces' Thoroughfare.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The Yangtze and its tributaries form a network of waterways across it, out of which came the Jing and Chu cultures, and it holds more than 36,000 immovable cultural heritage sites, the seventh largest tally in China.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The 2020 census counted 57,752,557 permanent residents, 4.09 per cent of the mainland's people, and its capital is Wuhan, on which that heritage clusters most densely.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>",
"Changsha": "Changsha is a city of south-central China and the capital of Hunan province, standing on the Xiang river in the lower reaches of the Dongting Lake system.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> It has long been called a city of mountain and water, for its abundant surface water and for the basin it sits in with hills around the built-up zones, and its mean annual temperature is about 17.2 degrees Celsius (63.0 degrees Fahrenheit).<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> It had passed 10.04 million people by 2020, about 2.2 times its population in the 1980s, and the land it covers grew nearly threefold between 2000 and 2023.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>",
"Guangxi": "Guangxi is an autonomous region of southern China named for the Zhuang, and the 2020 census counted 50,126,804 permanent residents there, 3.55 per cent of the mainland's people.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> It holds the second largest concentration of ethnic minorities in the country, 15.0 per cent of the national total, settled in large mixed communities and small concentrated ones, and it carried 342 nationally listed traditional villages by 2024.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Its northern third, the 58,200 square kilometres (22,500 square miles) around Guilin, Liuzhou and Hezhou, is karst mountain country whose land is 97 per cent forest, cropland and grass, and its capital is Nanning.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>",
"Hangzhou": "Hangzhou is a city of eastern China and the capital of Zhejiang province, at the southern edge of the Yangtze delta.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> It is known above all for West Lake, a shallow urban lake of 6.37 square kilometres (2.46 square miles) and an average depth of 2.27 metres (7.4 feet) that was added to the World Heritage List in 2011, the first Chinese entry in the lake category.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The Beijing-Hangzhou Grand Canal ends in the city, and the walled city of the Liangzhu culture, of about 5,300 to 4,500 years ago, stood on ground that is now part of it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>",
"Yunnan": "Yunnan is a plateau province in the south-western corner of China, at the crossroads of temperate China, north-eastern India and tropical mainland Southeast Asia.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Nearly half of China's animal and plant species are found within it, including 192 of the country's key protected wild animals and 144 of its key protected wild plants, and by 2014 it held 159 nature reserves over about 7.4 per cent of its land.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The 2020 census counted 47,209,277 permanent residents, 3.34 per cent of the mainland's people, and its capital, Kunming, anchors a plateau cluster of cities holding almost half of them.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>",
"Hefei": "Hefei is a city of eastern China and the capital of Anhui province, standing in the middle of it and covering 11,500 square kilometres (4,440 square miles) of hills and low mounds.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The Jianghuai Watershed runs east to west across the whole of its territory, so it sits astride the divide between the Yangtze basin and the Huai, and Chao Lake, the fifth largest freshwater lake in China, lies within its administrative area.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> It held 9.853 million people at the end of 2023, its urbanisation rate having risen from 32.64 per cent in 2000 to 85.55 per cent, and it is one of the country's four principal cities of science and education.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>",
"Jiangxi": "Jiangxi is a province of south-eastern China lying along the southern bank of the middle and lower Yangtze, with 45,188,635 permanent residents at the 2020 census and its capital at Nanchang.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Poyang Lake, the largest freshwater lake in China, lies in the north of the province and is fed by five river systems, the Gan, Xin, Fu, Xiu and Rao, whose basin of 162,200 square kilometres (62,600 square miles) covers more than 97 per cent of its territory.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Two caves in the north, Xianrendong and Diaotonghuan, have produced pottery more than 12,000 years old, and the kilns at Jingdezhen became one of the principal centres of porcelain production in Chinese history.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>",
"Wuhan": "Wuhan is a city of central China and the capital of Hubei province, standing at the confluence of the Yangtze and the Han in the eastern Jianghan Plain and covering 8,494 square kilometres (3,279 square miles) across thirteen municipal districts.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> It formed from three separate towns, Hankou, Wuchang and Hanyang, which were merged into a single municipality in 1927, and the first bridge ever built across the Yangtze opened there in 1957.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Known as the City of a Hundred Lakes, it has at least 65 lakes whose catchments exceed 5 square kilometres (1.9 square miles), and it is the only sub-provincial city in central China.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>",
"Liaoning": "Liaoning is a province of north-eastern China facing the Bohai Sea and the Yellow Sea, with 42,591,407 permanent residents at the 2020 census and its capital at Shenyang.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Its coastal belt runs through six cities and 2,920 kilometres (1,810 miles) of shore, and holds jurisdiction over 41,300 square kilometres (15,900 square miles) of sea, which in 2016 yielded half the province's output.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> It is one of the anchors of the country's heavy industrial base, home to the Anshan steelworks and long dominated by metal mining and smelting, and the valleys of its Liao and Daling rivers held the Neolithic Hongshan culture.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>",
"Nanning": "Nanning is a city of southern China and the capital of the Guangxi Zhuang Autonomous Region, standing in the south-central part of it and serving as its political and economic centre.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Its main urban districts cover 1,321 square kilometres (510 square miles) of alluvial basin ringed by hills, crossed east to west by the Yongjiang River, a major tributary of the Pearl River system.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> A subtropical humid monsoon climate brings it about 1,321 millimetres (52 inches) of rain a year, more than four fifths of it between April and October, and its 8.74 million residents were recorded in 2020.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>",
"Fujian": "Fujian is a province on the south-eastern coast of China facing Taiwan across the strait, with 41,540,086 permanent residents at the 2020 census and its capital at Fuzhou.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Mountainous and long-shored at once, it is described as eight parts mountain, one part water and one part field, and its forests cover 65.12 per cent of the land, the highest proportion of any province.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Its coast runs to 6,128 kilometres (3,808 miles) including the islands, and its port of Quanzhou was among the greatest maritime trading cities of the Song and Yuan dynasties.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>",
"Kunming": "Kunming is a city of south-western China and the capital of Yunnan, standing on the Yunnan-Guizhou Plateau with mountains on three sides and Dianchi Lake to the south.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Most of its ground lies between 1,500 and 2,800 metres (4,900 and 9,200 feet) above sea level, and that altitude at a low latitude gives it a mean annual temperature of about 15 degrees Celsius (59 Fahrenheit) and the smallest yearly temperature range of any city in the country.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The 2020 census counted 8,460,088 residents, and Dianchi, the largest body of fresh water in south-western China at 309.5 square kilometres (119.5 square miles), lies at the lowest point of its territory.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>",
"Shaanxi": "Shaanxi is a province in the heartland of China along the middle reaches of the Yellow River, covering 205,600 square kilometres (79,400 square miles), with 39,528,999 permanent residents at the 2020 census and its capital at Xi'an.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Two mountain systems divide it into three: the Loess Plateau of Shaanbei in the north, the densely settled Guanzhong Plain across the middle, and the Qinba mountains of Shaannan in the south.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The Qinling range across its middle is conventionally taken, with the Huai River, as the dividing line between northern and southern China, and its forests hold the densest wild giant panda population in the country.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>",
"Nanchang": "Nanchang is a city of eastern China and the capital of Jiangxi, standing in the north-centre of the province with a municipal territory of 7,195 square kilometres (2,778 square miles) and 6.25 million residents in 2020.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> It is the core city of the urban cluster of the middle Yangtze and the political, economic, cultural and transport hub of its province, and the Gan River runs north through it towards Poyang Lake.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Its climate is subtropical and humid, and because monsoon strength varies sharply from year to year the city meets heat waves, drought and cold spells in turn.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>",
"Guizhou": "Guizhou is an inland province of south-western China on the western edge of the Yunnan-Guizhou Plateau, with 38,562,148 permanent residents at the 2020 census and its capital at Guiyang.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> It covers about 176,200 square kilometres (68,000 square miles) of which 92.5 per cent is mountain and hill, making it the only province in the country without plains, and karst formations cover roughly 73 per cent of its landscape, the core of that terrain in south-western China.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> It is among the most ethnically varied provinces in China, home to about 4.5 million of the country's Miao and 2.71 million of its Bouyei.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>",
"Shenyang": "Shenyang is a city of north-eastern China and the capital of Liaoning, standing in the middle of the province where a branch of the Changbai Mountains meets the flood plain of the Liao.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> It is the communication, commercial, scientific and cultural centre of the north-east and the only city of its size there, with more than eight million people, and it was designated a key industrial base in 1948.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Its winters are severe, the coldest month averaging minus 11.0 degrees Celsius (12.2 Fahrenheit) against a July mean of 24.9 degrees Celsius (76.8 Fahrenheit).<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>",
"Shanxi": "Shanxi is a province of northern China on the eastern side of the Loess Plateau, covering 156,700 square kilometres (60,500 square miles), with 34,915,616 permanent residents at the 2020 census and its capital at Taiyuan.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> About 80 per cent of it is mountain and hill, its shape often described as two ranges flanking a trough, with the Taihang along the eastern edge, the Lüliang along the western and a chain of basins between them.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Coal underlies more than 40 per cent of its surface, making it one of the country's critical energy supply bases and leaving wide areas of mining subsidence behind it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>",
"Fuzhou": "Fuzhou is a city of south-eastern China and the capital of Fujian, standing on the province's eastern coast and covering 11,969 square kilometres (4,621 square miles) at an average of 84 metres (276 feet) above sea level.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The ground is high in the north-west and low in the south-east, running from mountain through hill and plain to an irregular 800-kilometre coast, and the Min, the largest river in Fujian, crosses it to enter the East China Sea at Mawei.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Its climate is subtropical monsoon, averaging 21.2 degrees Celsius (70.2 Fahrenheit) over the year, and it is counted one of the four furnace cities of China for the severity of its summers.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>",
"Chongqing": "Chongqing is one of the four municipalities of China, governed directly from Beijing rather than through a province, with 32,054,159 permanent residents at the 2020 census.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> It is a city by rank and a region by size, covering 82,400 square kilometres (31,800 square miles) across 38 districts and counties, among them 26 districts, eight counties and four autonomous counties, only part of which is urban at all.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Standing in the low hills at the eastern end of the Sichuan basin where the Jialing River joins the Yangtze, it is the standing example in China of a mountain city.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>",
"Xi'an": "Xi'an is a city of northern China and the capital of Shaanxi, standing on the Guanzhong Plain in the middle of the Yellow River Basin and governing 11 districts and 2 counties.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Its climate is warm temperate and semi-humid continental monsoon, averaging 13.1 to 14.3 degrees Celsius (55.6 to 57.7 Fahrenheit) over the year, and its people are heavily concentrated in an old centre where density passes 32,000 to the square kilometre.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Outside the city stands the mausoleum of Qin Shihuang, first emperor of a unified China, with the three pits of life-sized ceramic warriors buried beside it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>",
"Heilongjiang": "Heilongjiang is the northernmost and easternmost province of China, covering 473,000 square kilometres (183,000 square miles) across twelve prefecture-level cities and the Great Khingan Mountains region, with 31,850,088 permanent residents at the 2020 census and its capital at Harbin.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Its cold-temperate continental monsoon climate gives mean annual temperatures between minus 5 and 5 degrees Celsius (23 and 41 Fahrenheit), so plant matter accumulates faster than it decomposes and forms the black humus layer the province is known for.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> That soil is rich in organic matter and stores a great deal of carbon, but is at high risk of degradation from erosion, tillage and freeze-thaw cycles.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>",
"Guiyang": "Guiyang is a city of south-western China and the capital of Guizhou, standing among the hills at the middle of the province on the watershed between the Yangtze and the Pearl.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> It covers 8,043 square kilometres (3,105 square miles), of which about 52 per cent is mountain and a further 35 per cent hill, and it held 5.99 million people at the end of 2020.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Forest covers nearly half its territory, the rock beneath it is Triassic dolomite and limestone, and the karst formed in that carbonate runs through the whole of its catchment.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>",
"Xinjiang": "Xinjiang is an autonomous region in the far north-west of China and much the largest of the country's provincial-level divisions, covering 1,664,900 square kilometres (642,800 square miles) with 25,852,345 permanent residents at the 2020 census and its capital at Ürümqi.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Its structure is usually put as three mountain ranges flanking two basins, the Altai in the north, the Tianshan across the middle and the Kunlun along the southern rim, with the Junggar and Tarim basins between them.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The climate is strictly temperate continental and most of the ground is desert, gobi and thin scrub, so settlement follows water, and the region grows the greater part of China's cotton on its irrigated oases.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>",
"Taiyuan": "Taiyuan is a city of northern China and the capital of Shanxi, standing at the northern end of the Jinzhong Basin in the middle of the province and covering 6,988 square kilometres (2,698 square miles) of mountain, hill, plain, basin and valley.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Its 5.435 million people live under a warm temperate continental monsoon climate that is markedly dry, with 390 to 423 millimetres (15 to 17 inches) of rain a year against well over two thousand hours of sunshine.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Its old core has a recorded lineage of more than 2,500 years and served as the seat of a succession of dynasties, and the city is now a coal and energy hub with a large industrial workforce.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>",
"Gansu": "Gansu is a province deep inland in north-western China, with 25,019,831 permanent residents at the 2020 census and its capital at Lanzhou.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Plateau, mountain and desert are interleaved across it, arid and semi-arid country covers three quarters of its area, and its temperate continental climate brings anywhere between 40 and 760 millimetres (1.6 and 30 inches) of rain in a year.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Its north-western half is the Hexi Corridor, a trench 1,100 kilometres (680 miles) long where three inland rivers rise and where oases strung along them have supported both nomadic and farming ways of life for two thousand years.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>",
"Harbin": "Harbin is a city of north-eastern China and the capital of Heilongjiang, standing on the Northeast China Plain in the basin of the Songhua, its municipal territory covering about 53,000 square kilometres (20,500 square miles) and holding 9,431,700 people in 2021.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Its mesothermal continental monsoon climate gives four sharply marked seasons, an annual mean of 4.9 degrees Celsius (40.8 Fahrenheit) and a cold season of more than three months, which makes it the standing Chinese example of a cold-region city.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> It is a plain city grown up since the beginning of the 20th century, an important industrial centre and a transport hub on the first Eurasian land bridge.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>",
"Shanghai": "Shanghai is a municipality on the eastern coast of China, where the Yangtze River reaches the East China Sea, and the 2020 census counted 24,870,895 permanent residents in it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> It covers roughly 6,341 square kilometres (2,448 square miles) of estuarine deltaic alluvial plain averaging about 4 metres (13 feet) above sea level, under a subtropical monsoon climate with four marked seasons, a mean of about 16 degrees Celsius (61 Fahrenheit) and some 1,100 millimetres (43 inches) of rain a year.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The estuary before it is one of the world's largest alluvial estuaries, divided by Chongming Island into two branches, and the tidal flats along it have shrunk sharply as land was claimed from them.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>",
"Ürümqi": "Ürümqi is the capital of the Xinjiang Uygur Autonomous Region in north-western China, standing on the southern margin of the Junggar Basin with the northern slopes of the Tianshan mountains around it on three sides.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> It is the inland city furthest from any sea in the world, its 13,788 square kilometres (5,324 square miles) an elongated basin falling from 5,445 metres (17,864 feet) in the south-east to 490 (1,608 feet) in the north-west, under a mid-temperate semi-arid continental climate with roughly 280 millimetres (11 inches) of rain a year.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The mountains that shelter it also trap cold air, making winter temperature inversions frequent and severe, and the river it is named for rises at a glacier monitored since 1959.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>",
"Jilin": "Jilin is a province in the middle of north-eastern China, some 187,400 square kilometres (72,400 square miles) divided among nine prefecture-level divisions, and the 2020 census counted 24,073,453 permanent residents in it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The land tilts down from south-east to north-west, mountain and hill in the east giving way to plain across the centre and west, under a temperate continental monsoon climate that turns from humid in the south-east through semi-humid to semi-arid in the north-west.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Much of that plain is black soil, a dark earth rich in organic matter and fertile enough to make the province one of the country's principal grain-growing regions.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>",
"Lanzhou": "Lanzhou is the capital of Gansu province in north-western China, standing at about 36.05 degrees north and 103.67 degrees east, near the geometric centre of the country's continental land map.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Mountain ranges rise to its north and south with the Yellow River running between them, a valley-basin setting that leaves the city nowhere to spread but along its own axis, so it has grown as a belt and its road network has taken the same strip shape.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The same valley traps its air, the atmosphere above it being stably stratified with calm conditions frequent, which long kept industrial and heating emissions from dispersing.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>",
"Inner_Mongolia": "Inner Mongolia is an autonomous region stretched along the northern edge of China, some 1.183 million square kilometres (457,000 square miles) administered as twelve leagues and prefecture-level cities, and the 2020 census counted 24,049,155 permanent residents in it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Most of it is plateau, high in the south-west and low in the north-east, far from any ocean and rimmed with mountains, so the climate is markedly continental and rainfall falls away westward until most of the region is arid or semi-arid.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Grassland, forest and desert all cover large areas, and the grassland alone runs to 51.4 million hectares (127 million acres), much of which has been degrading since the 1960s.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>",
"Changchun": "Changchun is the capital of Jilin province and a sub-provincial city of north-eastern China, standing on open flat ground 250 to 350 metres (820 to 1,150 feet) above the sea.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Its climate is temperate continental monsoon and the seasons pull far apart, the 1991 to 2020 normals giving a mean of 6.6 degrees Celsius (44 Fahrenheit) over the year, 21.9 (71 Fahrenheit) in July and 12.0 below freezing (10 Fahrenheit) in January.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> It stands on the Yitong, a tributary within the Songhua basin, has grown outwards in rings rather than along an axis, and counted 4,866,500 people in its urban area at the end of 2020.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>",
"Beijing": "Beijing is a municipality at the northern edge of the North China Plain covering about 16,400 square kilometres (6,300 square miles), and the 2020 census counted 21,893,095 permanent residents in it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Its ground is high in the north-west and low in the south-east, with continuous mountains across the west and north and a plain 20 to 60 metres (66 to 200 feet) above the sea filling the south-east, laid down by five rivers whose alluvial fans overlap.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Its climate is warm temperate continental monsoon, half humid and half arid, and water has long been scarce enough that groundwater was heavily overdrawn until a diversion canal from the south began refilling it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>",
"Hohhot": "Hohhot is the capital of the Inner Mongolia Autonomous Region, standing on the northern border of China where the ground inclines gradually from north-east to south-west.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The Daqing and Manhan mountains close around it and leave a pocket of land with one wide opening facing south-west, a setting that disperses very little of what is put into the air above it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Its climate is mid-temperate continental monsoon with short hot summers and dry cold winters, a mean of 3.5 to 8 degrees Celsius (38 to 46 Fahrenheit) over the year and 337 to 418 millimetres (13 to 16 inches) of rain.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>",
"Tianjin": "Tianjin is a municipality on the western shore of Bohai Bay at the eastern edge of the North China Plain, its 12,000 square kilometres (4,600 square miles) low and flat with most of the ground only 5 to 10 metres (16 to 33 feet) above the sea, and the 2020 census counted 13,866,009 permanent residents in it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The whole Haihe river system reaches the sea here, and beneath the plain lie hundreds of metres of river and marine sediment in which sand aquifers alternate with silt and clay.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Pumping those aquifers from the 1950s sank the ground by metres in several places, a subsidence that has eased since water began arriving from the south.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>",
"Haikou": "Haikou is the capital of Hainan province and stands on the northern shore of Hainan Island, across the Qiongzhou Strait and 18 kilometres (11 miles) from the south coast of the Chinese mainland.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> It is the only tropical coastal provincial capital in China, its year averaging 24.4 degrees Celsius (76 Fahrenheit) with a relative humidity above 80 per cent and about 1,664 millimetres (66 inches) of rain, most of it falling between May and October.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Its air is among the cleanest of any Chinese city, and on its eastern side lies Dongzhaigang, the first mangrove reserve established in the country.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>",
"Hainan": "Hainan is the southernmost province of China, and the island it is named for lies at the northern end of the South China Sea shelf, facing Guangdong across the Qiongzhou Strait and Vietnam across the Gulf of Tonkin.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Its 33,900 square kilometres (13,100 square miles) make it the second largest island in the country after Taiwan, low around the rim and rising through terraces and hills to mountains at its core, under a tropical monsoon climate with a long summer and no winter.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> More than three fifths of it stays forested the year round, and a seventh of its land became a tropical rainforest national park in 2020.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>",
"Yinchuan": "Yinchuan is the capital of the Ningxia Hui Autonomous Region and stands on the Ningxia Plain in western China, with the Helan mountains along its western side and the Ordos Plateau away to the east.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The Yellow River runs through it, and the oasis around the city is the artesian irrigation district of that river's alluvial plain, flat and standing 1,090 to 1,200 metres (3,580 to 3,940 feet) up on a subsidence basin opened by faulting.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Its climate is arid, with long cold winters and only 180 to 200 millimetres (7.1 to 7.9 inches) of rain in a year against a pan evaporation many times that, so its farms and factories draw on the river instead.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>",
"Ningxia": "Ningxia is an autonomous region in north-western China, and the 2020 census counted 7,202,654 permanent residents in it, the third fewest of any province-level division on the mainland.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The Yellow River crosses it from south-west to north-east, watering an irrigated alluvial plain that stands 1,100 to 2,500 metres (3,600 to 8,200 feet) above the sea, while the land east of the river runs into the Mu Us sands and the Ordos.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Its climate is continental and arid, with only 180 to 220 millimetres (7.1 to 8.7 inches) of rain a year but 2,750 to 2,950 hours of sunshine, so its farming depends on the river rather than the weather.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>",
"Xining": "Xining is the capital of Qinghai province and stands on the north-eastern margin of the Qinghai-Tibet Plateau, in the valley of the Huangshui River at an average elevation of about 2,261 metres (7,418 feet).<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Its ground is shallow hills and river terraces rather than open plain, high in the north-west and low in the south-east, and the mountains around it hold the built city inside a narrow valley corridor.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Its climate is the cold-temperate kind of high plateaus, with long severe winters, a year averaging about 6 degrees Celsius (43 Fahrenheit) and rain of about 500 millimetres (20 inches) against a far greater evaporation.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>",
"Qinghai": "Qinghai is a province of the north-eastern Qinghai-Tibet Plateau, named for Qinghai Lake, the largest inland saltwater lake in China, which lies in a closed mountain-ringed basin fed by about fifty rivers.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Its southern half is the Sanjiangyuan, 363,000 square kilometres (140,000 square miles) of high plateau where the Yangtze, the Yellow River and the Mekong all rise, and which yields between a quarter and a half of what those rivers carry.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Its north-west is the Qaidam Basin, an enclosed depression taking as little as 34 millimetres (1.3 inches) of rain a year, so the province as a whole is high, cold and thinly peopled.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>",
"Lhasa": "Lhasa is the capital of the Tibet Autonomous Region and stands in the south-central Qinghai-Tibet Plateau at about 3,650 metres (11,980 feet) above the sea, with a permanent population of 867,900 in 2020.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Its climate is cold semi-arid, with cold dry winters and springs, a short summer in which the rain arrives, and thin air that lets solar radiation and ultraviolet through with little to stop them.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The Lhasa River gives the city its name and its water, running 568 kilometres (353 miles) and carrying an average of 288 cubic metres (10,200 cubic feet) a second through a basin that takes about 545 millimetres (21 inches) of rain a year.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>",
"Tibet": "Tibet is an autonomous region occupying the core of the Qinghai-Tibet Plateau in south-western China, its average elevation about 4,000 metres (13,100 feet) and more than nine tenths of it standing above 2,000 metres (6,600 feet).<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Height, cold, dryness, thin air and steady wind make its ecosystems distinctive and fragile, running from cold desert through alpine steppe, meadow and shrub to montane forest, with its rivers running out to the east and draining inward to lakes and wetlands in the centre and north.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The plateau holds the world's largest unbroken area of alpine permafrost and has warmed at roughly twice the global rate, so that permafrost is now thawing downwards faster than it was.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>",
"Persepolis": "Persepolis was the palace terrace of the Achaemenid kings of Persia, in southern Iran, an artificial platform of roughly 455 by 300 metres (1,490 by 980 feet) founded by Darius I and built on by his successors, carrying an audience hall, palaces, a treasury and a garrison quarter.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Its stairways are carved with processions of tribute-bearing delegations from 23 nations of the empire, and its royal inscriptions were cut in three languages, Old Persian, Elamite and Babylonian.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Clay archives kept on the terrace record silver paid to workmen there under Darius I and his successors<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>, and Greek accounts describe the site plundered and its palaces burned when Alexander took it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>",
"Zoroastrianism": "Zoroastrianism is the religion of the Avesta, whose teaching is ascribed to Zarathustra and whose supreme god is Ahura Mazda, later called Ormazd.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Its tenets set a good principle against an evil one, divide all beings between them, and look to a world of limited duration ending in evil's defeat and the resurrection of the dead<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>, while its priests, the Magi, are described by Greek writers sacrificing on high places and tending a fire nobody may breathe upon.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> After the Arab conquest it survived in Persia in a diminished community and among the Parsis of India, whose tradition dates their landing at Sanjan to about 716 CE.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>",
"Behistun_Inscription": "The Behistun Inscription is a relief and text cut into a cliff in western Iran about 150 metres (500 feet) above the plain, in which Darius I gives his account of the year he took the Achaemenid throne.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The panel has the king standing over a fallen rival with nine roped captives before him, and beneath and beside it the same account runs in three languages: five columns of Old Persian, three of Elamite and one of Babylonian.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> A short label beside each captive says that man lied<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>, and the three parallel versions were the ground on which cuneiform was deciphered in the nineteenth century.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>",
"Xerxes_I": "Xerxes I was a king of the Achaemenid Empire of Persia, the son and successor of Darius I, whose inscriptions style him great king, king of kings, and king of countries containing many kinds of people.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> At Persepolis he completed unfinished work and added buildings of his own, among them the terrace gate whose trilingual inscription calls it a colonnade for all countries, a palace, and the quarters excavators named the Harem.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Greek writers supply almost all that is told of his invasion of Greece in 480 and 479 BCE and of his killing by the captain of his own bodyguard.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>",
"Aramaic": "Aramaic is a Semitic language of the Near East, its earliest substantial texts being the royal inscriptions of small kingdoms in northern Syria, set down in an alphabet adopted from Phoenician.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> While the Assyrian, Babylonian and Persian empires ruled it served for commerce, diplomacy and administration, and it survives in bulk only where the climate spared papyrus, chiefly in Egypt, giving way to Greek in Ptolemaic times.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Its alphabet outlasted that official standing: the Jews adopted it along with the language after the Exile, and the square characters of Hebrew manuscripts, the Nabataean script and the Palmyrene all grew out of it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>",
"Darius_III": "Darius III was the last king of the Achaemenid Empire of Persia, put on the throne by the chiliarch Bagoas after the royal house had been extinguished, being a member of the court circle descended from a brother of Artaxerxes II.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Diodorus says he answered Bagoas's attempt to poison him by handing him the cup and making him drink it himself.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> He lost Asia to Alexander of Macedon, wrote to him asking for his captured mother, wife and children and offering alliance, and was killed in flight by Bessus, his own satrap of Bactria.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>",
"Ancient_Greece": "Ancient Greece is the civilisation of the Greek-speaking cities of the mainland, the Aegean islands and the Ionian coast of Asia Minor, together with the colonies they planted in Italy, Sicily and elsewhere.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Its political unit was the polis, a settlement with its farmland governed as an independent state, whose citizens were those entitled to share in judgement and office, so that resident foreigners and enslaved people lived in it without belonging to it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Herodotus puts what these cities shared not in any common government but in kinship of blood and speech, shrines and sacrifices held in common, and a like way of life.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>",
"Classical_Athens": "Classical Athens is the Greek city of Attica between the Persian Wars and Alexander's conquests, whose people counted as citizens of one polity because Theseus, Thucydides writes, had merged the council chambers of the Attic townships into a single town-hall.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> At the outbreak of the Peloponnesian War its allied tribute came to 600 talents, beside 6,000 talents of coined silver on the Acropolis, 13,000 hoplites and 300 galleys fit for service.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Its sea power made the harbour at Piraeus a gathering point for Mediterranean goods, and a hostile Athenian writer remarked that the city, hearing every kind of dialect, mixed the speech and dress of Greeks and foreigners.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>",
"Athenian_democracy": "Athenian democracy is the constitution under which the adult male citizens of Athens governed themselves directly, filling most offices by lot and settling public business in the assembly and the jury courts, a system the Constitution of the Athenians says had made the people master of everything.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Aristotle's Politics gives its marks as election by lot to any office not needing skill, no property qualification, no office held twice, short tenure, and judicial duties exercised by all citizens.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> It was carried at public expense, the empire's tributes and taxes being reckoned to feed more than 20,000 men, among them 6,000 jurymen and 500 councillors.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>",
"Greco-Persian_Wars": "The Greco-Persian Wars were fought between the Achaemenid Empire and the Greek cities in the early 5th century BCE, running from the revolt of the Ionians to the failure of the invasion led by Xerxes, a conflict Thucydides calls the greatest achievement of past times and one that nevertheless found a speedy decision in two actions by sea and two by land.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The Greeks who resisted met and exchanged guarantees, resolving to end all their feuds and sending envoys as far as Sicily to seek help.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> A tithe of the spoils was set apart for Delphi and made into a tripod resting on a bronze three-headed serpent.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>",
"Battle_of_Marathon": "The Battle of Marathon was fought on the plain of that name in Attica, where an Athenian force with Plataean help defeated the expedition sent against Athens and Eretria, the Persians having landed there on Hippias' guidance as the ground best suited to horses.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Herodotus says the Athenians charged at a run across the eight stades between the armies, and calls them the first Greeks known to have done so and the first to endure the sight of Median dress.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> About 6,400 of the invaders fell and 192 Athenians, and Epizelus was said to have lost his sight in the fighting without any wound.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>",
"Battle_of_Thermopylae": "The Battle of Thermopylae was the Greek attempt to hold the coastal pass into central Greece against the army of Xerxes, chosen because the invaders could use neither their whole force nor their horsemen there.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Leonidas commanded, bringing the three hundred Spartans allotted him and choosing men who had living sons, and the assaults failed for two days until a man of Malis named Epialtes told the king of a path across the mountain.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The allies were then dismissed, only the Thespians staying willingly and the Thebans as hostages, and the last defenders were buried under missiles on a hill at the mouth of the pass.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>",
"Battle_of_Salamis": "The Battle of Salamis was the sea fight in the straits between Attica and the island of Salamis, where a Greek fleet of 378 triremes destroyed the far larger Persian one.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Themistocles sent his servant Sicinnus to tell the Persian generals that the Greeks were terrified and meant to flee, and they answered by closing the whole passage in silence through the night.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Aeschylus, who fought in it, has the Persian ships crowded into the narrows until they could not help one another and smashed their own banks of oars, and the sea hidden by wrecks and slaughtered men.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>",
"Delian_League": "The Delian League was an alliance of Greek states led by Athens and formed after the Persian invasion, its members contributing money or ships and its treasury kept on Delos, where the congresses met in the temple.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The tribute was first fixed at 460 talents and received by officers called the Treasurers for Hellas, and the allies were at first independent, acting on the resolutions of a common congress.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Most of them chose to pay money rather than serve, so the Athenian navy grew on their contributions while a revolt left them without ships, Naxos being the first member besieged and made to return.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>",
"Pericles": "Pericles was the Athenian statesman who led the city in the middle of the 5th century BCE and into the war with Sparta, and whom Thucydides calls the first man of his time, ablest in counsel and action.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The Constitution of the Athenians credits him with taking functions from the Areopagus, urging the state towards naval power, making jury service a paid office, and confining citizenship to those of citizen birth on both sides.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Thucydides writes that his rank, ability and known integrity let him lead the multitude rather than be led, so that what was nominally a democracy became in his hands government by the first citizen.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>",
"Parthenon": "The Parthenon is the temple of Athena on the acropolis of Athens, built after the Persian sack out of the tribute of Athens' allies, with Callicrates and Ictinus as its architects and Pheidias overseeing the work.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Pausanias found the front pediment given to the birth of Athena and the rear to her contest with Poseidon for the land, and inside a standing statue of ivory and gold with a Victory in one hand and a spear in the other.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Thucydides reckoned forty talents of pure gold on that statue, all of it removable, which the city might use to save itself and would afterwards have to restore in full.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>",
"Greek_theatre": "Greek theatre means the plays performed in competition at the Athenian festivals of Dionysus, for which the chief archon appointed the chorus-leaders of the tragedies from among the wealthiest men in the city.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Aristotle traces tragedy and comedy alike to improvisation, the one from the prelude to the dithyramb and the other from the prelude to the phallic songs, and credits Aeschylus with the second actor and Sophocles with the third and with scene-painting.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> He defines tragedy as a representation of an action heroic and complete, acted rather than narrated, whose six parts are plot, character, diction, thought, spectacle and song.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>",
"Socrates": "Socrates was an Athenian philosopher who wrote nothing himself and is known from the writings of others, and whom Aristotle credits with disregarding the physical universe, confining his study to moral questions and concentrating first upon definition.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Plato has him tell the court that the oracle at Delphi denied anyone was wiser, and that on questioning men of reputation he found himself wiser only in this, that what he did not know he did not think he knew.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> He was tried on a charge of rejecting the gods the state acknowledged, bringing in strange deities and corrupting the youth, and was put to death by hemlock.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>",
"Peloponnesian_War": "The Peloponnesian War was fought from 431 to 404 BCE between Athens with its maritime empire and Sparta with the Peloponnesian League, most of the Greek world being drawn in on one side or the other.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Thucydides, who lived through it and wrote its history, treats it as one war of 27 years, holding that the interval of the Peace of Nicias cannot rationally be counted peace.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> It ended with the Athenian fleet gone and the city surrendered on terms requiring the Long Walls and those of Piraeus to be pulled down, all but twelve ships given up, and Sparta followed by land and sea.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>",
"Philip_II_of_Macedon": "Philip II was king of Macedon from 359 to 336 BCE, taking the throne after a defeat that had left the kingdom exposed to the Illyrians and building it, in Diodorus' judgement, into the greatest of the dominions in Europe.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> He remade the army, devising the compact order and equipment of the phalanx and fielding it alongside cavalry, archers, mercenaries and siege artillery in campaigns that Demosthenes complained kept no season for inaction.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Victory over the Athenians and Boeotians at Chaeronea in 338 BCE left him master of the Greek mainland, and two years later he was killed at a festival by a man nursing a private grievance.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>",
"Alexander_the_Great": "Alexander the Great was king of Macedon from 336 to 323 BCE, a reign Diodorus reckons at twelve years and seven months, and he crossed into Asia with a force the accounts put variously at 30,000 to 43,000 foot and 4,000 to 5,000 horse.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> In Parthia he took to a dress devised midway between the Persian and the Median, which Plutarch says his Macedonians found offensive, and in the Punjab his own army halted the advance by refusing to cross the Ganges.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> He died at Babylon after a fever whose course the court Journals record day by day, and the accounts of his death did not agree even in antiquity.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>",
"Library_of_Alexandria": "The Library of Alexandria was the Ptolemaic kings' book collection at Alexandria, attached to the Museum, a community of scholars quartered in the palaces, holding property in common under a royal priest.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> What survives is oblique: Strabo sets out that Museum, its walk, its hall and its priest without naming a library, and the usual figure of nearly 700,000 volumes comes from Aulus Gellius and Ammianus Marcellinus.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Those later notices disagree about where the books stood and how they were lost, one putting them in the Serapeum, one blaming an accidental fire during a sack, and Plutarch the fire Caesar used against the enemy fleet.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>",
"Ancient_Rome": "Ancient Rome was the city on the Tiber and the state that grew out of it, which Polybius says had brought nearly the whole inhabited world under its sole government in less than 53 years.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Its constitution changed shape repeatedly: Tacitus lists kings, then the consulship established by Lucius Brutus, then dictatorships, decemvirs and the brief despotisms of Cinna and Sulla, until Augustus subjected a world wearied by civil strife to empire under the title of Prince.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Roman writers treated that history as a moral one, Livy telling his readers to mark through what men and by what policies the empire was enlarged, and how discipline afterwards relaxed.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>",
"Roman_Senate": "The Roman Senate was the standing council of the Roman state, its members men who had held office and been entered on its roll by the censors<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>, and it met in a building called a <i>curia</i>.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Its resolutions were named from the consul's act of consulting it, were void if passed before sunrise or after sunset, and once carried were laid up on tablets in the treasury, though a tribune interposing could stop one, what had been agreed being recorded instead as an <i>auctoritas</i>.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> They dealt with religion, administration, provincial matters and foreign relations, and under Augustus such decrees began to take the place of laws.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>",
"Twelve_Tables": "The Twelve Tables were the first written code of Roman law, drawn up in 451 BCE by a board of ten men from whose decisions there was no appeal.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Ten tables were exhibited to the people, amended on their comment and passed by the assembly of centuries; two more were added under a second board the next year, and the whole was engraved on metal and set up in the comitium.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> No copy survives, and the contents are known from later quotation, among them graded penalties for theft under which a thief caught in the act could be killed if he stole by night or drew a weapon.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>",
"Conflict_of_the_Orders": "The Conflict of the Orders was the struggle by which the plebeians of the Roman Republic gained access to the offices, priesthoods and legal protections the patricians had reserved to themselves.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> It opened in 494 BCE with a secession, the plebs withdrawing from the city until they were granted magistrates of their own, inviolable in person and closed to patricians.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The quaestorship opened to plebeians in 421 BCE, the consulship in 367, the censorship in 351, the praetorship in 336 and the chief priesthoods in 300, and after a last secession in 286 the dictator Quintus Hortensius gave their resolutions the force of law.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>",
"Roman_legion": "The Roman legion was the main formation of the Roman army, in the middle Republic a body of about 4,200 foot and 300 cavalry, raised to 5,000 foot when the danger was great.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Its men were conscripted citizens sorted by age and means into velites, hastati, principes and triarii, and divided into maniples of which each had two centurions, so that one was always present to command.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Service was owed for sixteen years in the infantry or ten in the cavalry, and discipline rested on penalties as severe as the fustuarium, under which a condemned man was beaten or stoned by the whole camp.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>",
"Punic_Wars": "The Punic Wars were three wars fought between Rome and Carthage for the western Mediterranean, the first running 24 years without a break and ending with the loss of Sicily to Rome.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The second was argued over on both sides, Rome holding that the destruction of Saguntum breached the treaties and Carthage that the earlier seizure of Sardinia justified her, and its peace took her fleet and elephants and set 10,000 talents over fifty years.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> For the third the Romans had made up their minds long before and waited only for a pretext that would look just to other nations, and it ended with Carthage crushed.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>",
"Hannibal": "Hannibal was the Carthaginian general who commanded against Rome in the second Punic War, taking an army from Iberia over the Alps into the plain of the Po in 218 BCE, the first year of the war.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> He is said to have sworn an oath against Rome at the altar at nine, when his father was setting out on campaign, and to have told the story years later at the court of Antiochus.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Polybius calls him the single cause of all that befell both nations, and records that some accused him of extreme cruelty and others of avarice.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>",
"Battle_of_Cannae": "The Battle of Cannae was fought in Apulia in 216 BCE between a Roman army of about 80,000 foot and rather more than 6,000 cavalry and a smaller Carthaginian force under Hannibal.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Hannibal drew his Iberian and Celtic infantry into a crescent with its convex face towards the enemy and his Africans on either side, so that when the crescent gave way and the legions pressed into it the Africans stood on both their flanks.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> About 70,000 Romans died, some 10,000 were taken at their camp and seventy of the cavalry escaped with one consul, and the defeat brought Tarentum and Campanian towns over to Hannibal.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>",
"Destruction_of_Carthage": "The destruction of Carthage was the end of the third war between Rome and Carthage, when a Roman army under Scipio Aemilianus stormed the city in 146 BCE after a siege of three years.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The fighting went street by street and roof by roof up to the citadel, which Scipio fired, and Hasdrubal's wife killed her children and threw herself into the flames.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The Senate then ordered whatever remained obliterated and forbade anyone to live there, and Polybius records that opinion in his own country was divided over whether Rome had shown statesmanship or something close to treachery.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>",
"Roman_conquest_of_Greece": "The Roman conquest of Greece was the process by which Roman power replaced the independence of the Greek cities and kingdoms, beginning after Rome defeated Philip of Macedon and ending with the sack of Corinth.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> It opened with a declaration rather than an annexation: at the Isthmian games a herald proclaimed the Corinthians, Phocians, Locrians, Euboeans, Thessalians and others free, without garrisons and paying no tribute, and the crowd made him read it twice.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> It closed with Corinth taken and its works of art carried off or ruined, after which Macedonia and Achaia were governed from Rome and Mummius triumphed in 146 BCE.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>",
"Achaean_League": "The Achaean League was a federation of cities in the Peloponnese whose institutions and name were taken up so widely that Polybius asks how all the Peloponnesians came to be called Achaeans.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> He answers that no political system was so favourable to equality and freedom of speech, since the league reserved no privileges for its original members and put every new adherent on the same footing.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> It went to war with Rome under leaders Polybius calls demented and perverse, and after Corinth was taken in 146 BCE the Peloponnese passed with the rest into the Roman province of Achaia.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>",
"Gracchi_brothers": "The Gracchi brothers were Tiberius and Gaius Sempronius Gracchus, tribunes of the Roman plebs whose attempts to redistribute publicly owned land ended in the violent deaths of both.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Tiberius carried a law in 133 BCE limiting any holder of Roman public land to 500 iugera, with half as much again for each of two sons, and appointed three elected commissioners to divide the remainder among poorer citizens.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> He was beaten to death that year by senators led by Scipio Nasica; Gaius, tribune from 123 BCE, revived the programme with grain distributions and colonies and died in 121 BCE after armed conflict on the Aventine.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>",
"Marius_and_Sulla": "Marius and Sulla were rival Roman commanders of the early 1st century BCE whose quarrel produced the first war fought between Roman armies.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Gaius Marius, the first man elected consul seven times, secured the command against Mithridates for himself after it had fallen by lot to the consul Lucius Cornelius Sulla, who answered by leading six legions on Rome and fighting the first pitched battle inside the city.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Marius died in 86 BCE during a seventh consulship; Sulla returned, proscribed senators and knights by published list, and had himself made dictator without a fixed term before laying the office down.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>",
"Third_Servile_War": "The Third Servile War is the modern name for the slave and gladiator rising against Rome that ancient writers called the war against Spartacus.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> It began when gladiators broke out of a training school at Capua, seized weapons and took refuge on Vesuvius, where escaped slaves and country labourers joined them; ancient accounts of the breakout give between about 30 and 78 men.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The rebels defeated several Roman commanders over three years before Licinius Crassus destroyed them in a final battle in which Spartacus was killed, and 6,000 prisoners were crucified along the road to Rome.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>",
"Julius_Caesar": "Julius Caesar was a Roman general, politician and writer, born about 100 BCE, who rose through the offices of the Republic to hold a dictatorship and was killed by conspirators on 15 March 44 BCE, on his way to a meeting of the senate.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> As a young man he refused the dictator Sulla's order to divorce his wife, losing a priesthood and his inheritances, and was later captured by pirates and ransomed for 50 talents.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Among his acts in power was a reform of the calendar, which fixed the year at 365 days with one more added every fourth year.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>",
"Gallic_Wars": "The Gallic Wars were the campaigns Julius Caesar fought in Gaul from 58 BCE, in which Plutarch reckons he stormed more than 800 cities and subdued 300 nations in under ten years.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The main account of them is the memoirs Caesar wrote himself, which open by dividing Gaul between the Belgae, the Aquitani and the peoples the Romans called Gauls, and which a contemporary judged carelessly made and untruthful about their author's own actions.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The fighting culminated in 52 BCE at Alesia, where a general revolt under Vercingetorix was besieged and forced to surrender.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>",
"Caesars_civil_war": "Caesar's civil war was the conflict of 49 to 45 BCE between Julius Caesar and the Roman commanders the senate backed under Pompey, which ended with Caesar unopposed in power.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> It began when Caesar led troops across the Rubicon, the boundary of his province, having told those with him that once over the bridge the whole issue would be with the sword.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Pompey was routed at Pharsalus in 48 BCE and murdered soon afterwards, and the remaining resistance was broken at Thapsus in 46 BCE and at Munda in Hispania in 45 BCE, after which Caesar celebrated five triumphs.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>",
"Assassination_of_Julius_Caesar": "The assassination of Julius Caesar was the killing of the Roman dictator on 15 March 44 BCE, at a meeting of the senate held in the hall attached to Pompey's theatre.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> More than 60 men are said to have joined the conspiracy, led by Gaius Cassius and by Marcus and Decimus Brutus, and the attack began when one of them caught the dictator's toga at the shoulders as a signal.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Ancient accounts give him 23 wounds, of which a physician who examined the body judged only one to have been fatal.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>",
"Augustus": "Augustus was the first Roman emperor, born Gaius Octavius and given the name Augustus by decree of the senate in 27 BCE, after which he held power until his death in 14 CE.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> He came to power through a series of civil wars, ruling first alongside Mark Antony and Lepidus, then with Antony alone, and finally by himself.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> In his own record of his deeds he says he transferred the state from his own control to the will of the senate and people, and afterwards took precedence of all in rank while holding no more power than his colleagues.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>",
"Roman_Empire": "The Roman Empire was the Roman state under rule by one man, conventionally dated from 27 BCE, when the senate granted Octavian the name Augustus.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> At its height it held the lands around the whole Mediterranean and its islands, reaching from the islands of the north-western ocean to the Euphrates and the Caucasus, and southward up the Nile.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Its rulers were styled imperatores rather than kings, and the territory was administered as provinces held by garrisons, whose numbers, revenues and costs Augustus recorded in a document read to the senate after his death.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>",
"Pax_Romana": "Pax Romana is the name given to the long period of relative peace within the Roman Empire that began under Augustus and lasted about two centuries.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Its emblem was the gate of Janus Quirinus at Rome, shut only when there was peace secured by victory throughout the whole Roman domain by land and sea, which Augustus records was shut three times during his rule and only twice before his birth.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Writers under Roman rule described the resulting prosperity, while Tacitus gave a conquered chieftain the line that the Romans made a solitude and called it peace.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>",
"Roman_roads": "Roman roads were the paved public highways built and maintained by the Roman state, the first of the great ones being the Appian Way, begun in the censorship of Appius Claudius in 312 BCE.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> A paved road was laid between two shallow trenches marking its width, dug down to firm ground, and carried four courses: large stones, broken stone in lime, brick and pottery fragments, and a surface of close-fitted polygonal blocks.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Their construction fell to the censors or, in their absence, to other magistrates, and stone pillars set at every mile marked the distances along them.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>",
"Roman_aqueduct": "A Roman aqueduct was a conduit that brought water to a city by gravity, laid at a slight and nearly uniform fall from source to delivery, driven through hills in tunnel and carried over valleys on solid substructures or on arches in two or three tiers.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The channel itself was usually a trough of brick or stone lined with waterproof cement under an arched covering, and most of a long line ran underground rather than on the arcades that survive.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Nine of them served Rome by the late 1st century CE, the earliest brought in during the censorship of Appius Claudius in 312 BCE.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>",
"Colosseum": "The Colosseum is the amphitheatre at Rome built by the emperor Vespasian on ground that had held the ornamental lake of Nero's Golden House, dedicated before his death in 79 CE and enlarged by his son Titus, who opened it in 80 with a hundred days of games.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Elliptical and 188 by 156 metres (617 by 512 feet) across, it has an outer wall of travertine in four storeys, the lower three open arcades.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Romans knew it as the <i>Amphitheatrum Flavium</i>; the name Colosseum, taken from a colossal statue of Nero that stood nearby, belongs to the Middle Ages.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>",
"Roman_law": "Roman law is the law of the Roman state and the jurisprudence built on it, divided by the jurists into public law, which concerns the state, and private law, which concerns the individual citizen.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Its sources were listed as statutes, plebiscites, decrees of the senate, constitutions of the emperors, the edicts of magistrates entitled to issue them, and the opinions of jurists, with unwritten custom beside them.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> A state's own rules were called the <i>ius civile</i> and those natural reason established among all peoples the <i>ius gentium</i>; Justinian had the whole gathered into the <i>Digest</i> and <i>Institutes</i> of 533 CE.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>",
"Roman_citizenship": "Roman citizenship was the legal status carrying the full rights of a member of the Roman state, its private side being the <i>commercium</i>, the capacity to own and bequeath by Roman title, and the <i>connubium</i>, the capacity to contract a marriage the law recognised.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Free persons were divided into citizens, Latins and peregrines, a Latin holding the first of those rights without the second; a freed slave entered one class or another according to how he had been held and freed.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> It spread outward from the city, to all Italy by a law of 90 BCE and at last to everyone in the empire under Caracalla.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>",
"Pompeii": "Pompeii was a town of Campania on the river Sarno below Vesuvius, held in turn by Oscans, Tyrrhenians and Pelasgians, and Samnites before it came under Rome.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The river carried cargoes inland and out to sea again, so the town served as the port of Nola, Nuceria and Acerrae behind it, and an earthquake under Nero demolished a large part of what Tacitus calls a populous place.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Vesuvius erupted in 79 CE and buried it with neighbouring Herculaneum under ash, which by one account fell while the people of Pompeii were sitting in the theatre.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>",
"Nero": "Nero was a Roman emperor and the last of the Julio-Claudian line, which Suetonius says came to an end with him.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> He had his mother Agrippina killed, performed in public as a singer to the lyre before soldiers and a full house, and drove members of the old families to sing, to drive teams and to fight as gladiators.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> A great fire destroyed much of Rome in his reign, which Tacitus says may have been accidental or contrived by the emperor, since authors give both accounts; Nero fastened the guilt on the Christians.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>",
"Trajan": "Trajan was a Roman emperor under whom the empire reached its furthest extent, conquering Dacia and campaigning east beyond the Euphrates as far as the Persian Gulf.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Dio admired above all the stone bridge he threw across the Danube, and records that he reigned 19 years, 6 months and 15 days and died at Selinus in Cilicia.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> His rescripts to the younger Pliny in Bithynia, and those quoted in the <i>Digest</i>, are the fullest surviving record of a Roman emperor answering a governor: that Christians were not to be sought out, that anonymous informations were not to be received, and that nobody should be convicted on suspicion.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>",
"Hadrians_Wall": "Hadrian's Wall was the frontier work the Romans drew across the north of the province of Britannia, given in both the inscriptions and the literary record as 80 Roman miles long.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> A dedication from Jarrow says the necessity of keeping the empire within its limits had been laid on Hadrian by divine precept, and that the army of the province built the work under Aulus Platorius Nepos.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Building stones along the line record who raised each stretch, from a legion at a milecastle down to a single century credited with 112 Roman feet of rampart.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>",
"Marcus_Aurelius": "Marcus Aurelius was a Roman emperor whose reign was taken up by war on the Danube and by a pestilence brought home from the east, and who is remembered chiefly for a notebook of Stoic reflections he kept for himself.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> He wrote it not in Latin but in the language of the Greeks, and it survives in twelve books under a title meaning simply 'to himself'.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Dio judged that he did not meet the good fortune he deserved, being frail and beset by troubles, and admired him the more for bringing the empire through them.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>",
"Crisis_of_the_Third_Century": "The Crisis of the Third Century is the modern name for the years between 235 and 284 CE, when Roman emperors followed one another in rapid succession and rival regimes held parts of the empire. The Augustan History's life of the two Gallieni describes armies ranging about on all sides while a Roman emperor was held as a slave in Persia, Valerian having been taken alive by Sapor,<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> and its Thirty Pretenders names usurpers whose count its author reduces to 22.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Milestones in Britannia salute Postumus as emperor of a breakaway west,<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> and the crisis closed as Aurelian defeated Zenobia, recovered the east and walled the city of Rome.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>",
"Diocletian": "Diocletian was a Roman emperor who reorganised the state after a long stretch of usurpation and invasion, and who is remembered above all for sharing power rather than holding it alone. The Augustan History says the army acclaimed him in 284 CE, and that he then took Maximian as a fellow emperor and set Galerius and Constantius beneath them, so that there were two <i>Augusti</i> and two <i>Caesares</i>.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Lactantius, who wrote against him, describes an empire quartered between multiplying armies and officials, an edict fixing maximum prices that was abandoned, and an abdication outside Nicomedia<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> that an inscription dates to 1 May 305 CE.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>",
"Constantine_the_Great": "Constantine the Great was the Roman emperor who ended the persecution of the Christians, took the faith up himself, and founded the city that took his name. A milestone cut in Cornwall within a year of his accession styles him <i>nobilissimo Caesari</i> and son of the deified Constantius, the junior rank rather than the throne.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Lactantius quotes entire the letter he issued from Milan with Licinius, which gave Christians liberty to follow their worship and gave the same liberty to everyone else,<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> while Eusebius says he was baptised only as he lay dying.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>",
"Christianity": "Christianity is the religion whose adherents worship Christ as divine and take their name from him. The earliest outside account of its practice, written by a provincial governor, has them meeting before dawn on a fixed day, singing to Christ as to a god, swearing not to steal or cheat or break faith, and afterwards eating an ordinary meal together.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Tertullian, writing from inside it, calls it a body knit together by a common profession, a common discipline and a common hope, which assembles to pray and to read its sacred writings under the presidency of its elders.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>",
"First_Council_of_Nicaea": "The First Council of Nicaea was an assembly of bishops held in 325 CE at Nicaea in Bithynia, summoned by the emperor Constantine, who called them from every quarter of the empire and gave them the imperial post to travel by.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Its creed described the Son as begotten and not made, of the substance of the Father, and <i>homoousios</i>, of one substance with him; its synodal letter anathematised the teaching of Arius and settled a common date for Easter.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> It also issued twenty canons of church discipline, and a church history of the next century records that 318 bishops subscribed while five refused over that one word.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>",
"Fall_of_the_Western_Roman_Empire": "The fall of the Western Roman Empire is the name given to the end of imperial rule in the western provinces, conventionally dated to 476 CE, when the last emperor in Italy was deposed. Jordanes wrote that the western empire of the Roman race perished with that emperor, Romulus Augustulus, and that kings of the Goths held Rome and Italy from then on.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Procopius describes the same events as a mutiny over land in which the boy was left alive, and says the Gothic king who followed never took the imperial name and yet ruled as truly an emperor as any before him.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>",
"Romulus_Augustulus": "Romulus Augustulus was the last man to hold the title of Roman emperor in the west, put on the throne as a boy by his father Orestes and deposed within the year. Procopius says the Romans called him by the diminutive Augustulus because he took the empire while still a lad, and that when the troops killed Orestes and made Odoacer king, the boy was left alive as a private citizen.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Jordanes has him driven from the throne and condemned to exile in Campania, and says the western empire of the Roman race perished with him.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>",
"Mahajanapadas": "The mahajanapadas were the states of northern India in the age of the Buddha and before it, remembered in a stock list of sixteen great countries that recurs in the Buddhist canon. The names on that list are names of peoples rather than of places, and it is older than the books that carry it: three of the sixteen had already lost their independence by the Buddha's lifetime.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Some were monarchies and some were clan states whose business was done in a public assembly under an elected chief, and they ran from Gandhara in the north-west to Avanti in the centre.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>",
"Upanishads": "The Upanishads are Sanskrit texts standing at the end of the Brahmanas, whose name means sitting down beside and came to mean a secret doctrine, because they were imparted to selected pupils rather than to the wider circle.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> They set the sacrificial religion of the Vedas aside for enquiry into the self, and teach that the self in each person is identical with the self of the universe, put as 'that art thou' and as 'I am brahma'.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Thirteen are counted as the principal ones,<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> and the older of them are dated on the whole no later than 550 BCE.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>",
"Hinduism": "Hinduism, the religion of about a billion people, 15% of the world, takes its name from a river: the Old Persian and Avestan <i>Hindu</i>, from Sanskrit <i>Sindhu</i>, meant the land beyond the Indus.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> There is no one book: the Vedas count as revelation, <i>shruti</i>, and the rest of the tradition as <i>smriti</i>, what is remembered; from that literature come samsara, the round of rebirth, and karma, the action deciding it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The classical law-books set out four orders of life and a fourfold division of society, while practice varies enormously; 94% of adherents live in India, and they are a majority in three countries.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>",
"Varna_(Hinduism)": "Varna is the fourfold ordering of society set out in classical Sanskrit texts, into Brahmana, Kshatriya, Vaishya and Shudra, and the word means 'colour'.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> A late hymn of the Rigveda derives the four from the body of a primeval man, and the law-books give each its work, making the first three twice-born, the Shudra a servant of the others, and denying that there is a fifth.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The groups people are actually born into are the <i>jati</i>, hundreds of endogamous bodies each with a common name and a traditional occupation, of which the Rigveda knows nothing, so the scheme is a frame rather than a description.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>",
"Gautama_Buddha": "Gautama Buddha is the teacher whose discourses the Buddhist tradition preserves, and Buddha is a title rather than a name, marking the night when \"the ascetic Gotama had become the Buddha, the awakened\".<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> He was a Sakya of Kapilavatthu on the Ganges plain, made a prince by later legend although the oldest texts give his father as a wealthy landowner, and taught for 44 of his eighty years before dying at Kusinara.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> His dates are reckoned back from Ashoka rather than recorded, so open works give 483 or about 480 BCE for the death; the earliest external evidence is Ashoka's pillar at Lumbini.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>",
"Buddhism": "Buddhism is the religion built on the teaching of the Buddha, which a person enters by taking refuge three times over in the Buddha, the Dhamma and the Sangha.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Its core is the four truths of the first discourse, suffering and its origin in craving, its cessation and the eightfold path, offered as a middle way between indulgence and self-affliction; rebirth is accepted and karma decides it, though no permanent self is admitted.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Its monks live by begging, recite their list of offences twice a month and settle in one place for the rains, and lay believers support them without entering the order.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>",
"Jainism": "Jainism is the religion of the Jina, the victor, whose followers count 24 <i>tirthankaras</i> teaching the same law in succession, the last of them Mahavira, a contemporary of the Buddha.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Its central discipline is non-injury: its oldest text holds that no living creature should be slain, treated with violence, abused, tormented or driven away, and a monk's first great vow renounces all killing, whether caused or merely consented to.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The church later divided into the Svetambaras, whose monks wear white clothes, and the Digambaras, who are sky-clad, and the two disagree about the canon itself.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>",
"Maurya_Empire": "The Maurya Empire was the first state to hold most of the Indian subcontinent, founded by Chandragupta about 321 BCE on the kingdom of Magadha and ruled from Pataliputra on the Ganges.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Its inscriptions survey India from the Hindu Kush to Ceylon but distinguish the king's own dominions, governed from the capital and from viceroyalties at Takshasila, Ujjayini and Kalinga, from border peoples counted within his influence rather than his rule.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The Puranas disagree about the succession but agree that the dynasty lasted 137 years, ending about 184 BCE when the last Maurya was killed by his own commander.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>",
"Chandragupta_Maurya": "Chandragupta Maurya founded the Maurya Empire by overthrowing the Nanda king of Magadha and taking Pataliputra on the Ganges, and the Greeks knew him as Sandrocottus.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> About 305 BCE Seleucus crossed the Indus, found him master of all Hindustan, and settled for a safe retirement, a marriage alliance and 500 elephants in exchange for the Greek dominions as far as the Kabul valley.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> His reign is put at 24 years, but the Puranas and the Buddhist books are not independent witnesses, the traditions disagree about the year it began, and no likeness of him survives.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>",
"Arthashastra": "The <i>Arthashastra</i> is a Sanskrit manual of statecraft in fifteen books, ascribed to Kautilya, also called Chanakya and Vishnu Gupta, and it defines its own subject as artha, the subsistence of mankind.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> It prescribes rather than narrates, covering ministers, forts, revenue, law and war, and gives whole chapters to espionage, with standing institutes of spies set to test the character of the king's own servants.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> It also tells a king that in the happiness of his subjects lies his happiness; the text nowhere dates itself, and was recovered only in the 20th century.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>",
"Ashoka": "Ashoka was the third emperor of the Maurya dynasty in India, reigning from about 274 to 236 BCE,<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> and is known chiefly from his own inscriptions, cut on rock faces and stone pillars from the north-west frontier to the south.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> In them he calls himself Beloved-of-the-Gods, King Piyadasi, records his remorse at the killing and deportation caused by his conquest of Kalinga,<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> and urges dhamma, a public morality of restraint, care for living things and forbearance between religions.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> He supported the Buddhist order and later tradition remembered him as its great patron, though the edicts appoint officers to work among people of every religion.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>",
"Edicts_of_Ashoka": "The Edicts of Ashoka are a group of inscriptions cut into rock faces and free-standing stone pillars in the 3rd century BCE, found at more than thirty places across India, Nepal, Pakistan and Afghanistan.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> They are the earliest corpus of written documents from India that can be read: nearly all in Prakrit and mostly in Brahmi script, with one bilingual edict in Afghanistan written in Aramaic and Greek.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Issued in the first person by a king who calls himself \"Beloved-of-the-Gods, King Piyadasi\", they proclaim dhamma, record particular acts of the reign, and are dated by his regnal years.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>",
"Third_Buddhist_Council": "The Third Buddhist Council is a gathering that the Pali chronicles place at Pataliputra in the nineteenth year from Ashoka's coronation, held under his patronage and presided over by the monk Moggaliputta Tissa.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> It was called by sectarian differences, among which as many as eighteen divisions are named, deliberated for nine months and decided for the school of the Sthaviras, which afterwards prevailed in Ceylon.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The canon is said to have been closed on the occasion and the Kathavatthu composed at it; the northern Buddhist texts ignore the council, so it is known from one tradition only.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>",
"Stupa": "A stupa is a solid domed monument built over relics, set on a plinth with a narrow ledge between the two that serves as a processional path; it is walked round rather than entered.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The earliest account has the Buddha's relics divided among princes and nobles at his death, each of whom raised one and instituted a festival, a cult left to the laity rather than to the order.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> At the Great Stupa at Sanchi the dome is almost hemispherical and stands about 16 metres (54 feet) high without its crowning umbrella, which sits inside a square stone rail, while a massive rail rings the base.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>",
"Indo-Greek_Kingdom": "The Indo-Greek Kingdom is the modern name for the states that Greek rulers of Bactria established south of the Hindu Kush from the early 2nd century BCE, in the Kabul valley, the north-west frontier and the Punjab.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> They are known almost entirely from coins: the legends of about thirty kings and two queens, many of them bilingual, with Greek on one face and an Indian language and alphabet on the other.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The best remembered of their rulers is Menander, identified with the Milinda of the Pali <i>Milindapanha</i>, in which he questions the elder Nagasena and is converted.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>",
"Kushan_Empire": "The Kushan Empire was the state built by one of the five Yueh-chi tribes of Bactria, whose chief won the supremacy over the rest and gave the united kingdom his own tribe's name.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> From about 25 CE its kings crossed the Hindu Kush at the expense of the Parthian rulers there and carried it from the Kabul valley into north-west India, styling themselves \"Great King, Supreme King of Kings, Son of the Gods\".<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Its best remembered ruler is Kanishka, whose reign is placed near the end of the 1st century CE and who almost certainly founded the era beginning in 78 CE.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>",
"Greco-Buddhist_art": "Greco-Buddhist art is the Buddhist sculpture made in Gandhara and the north-west of the subcontinent, in which Hellenistic form was turned to Indian religious use.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> It sprang up under Scythian and Parthian rule and produced most of its known work under the Kushans, by whose time the types of the Buddha peculiar to it were already fixed and standardised.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Not one of its many thousands of sculptures bears a date in any known era, so its chronology rests on the rough rule that the earlier a piece is the more nearly it approaches Hellenistic work.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>",
"Gupta_Empire": "The Gupta Empire was the north Indian power whose era begins on 26 February 320 CE, the day taken for the coronation of Chandragupta I, and which was reckoned in for centuries afterwards.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Founded in Magadha and the Gangetic valley, it was carried much further by his son Samudragupta, whose conquests were engraved on one of Ashoka's own pillars in a Sanskrit panegyric of about 360 CE.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> A Chinese pilgrim who spent six years in it early in the 5th century described a government that fined criminals instead of maiming them, though he never named the king.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>",
"Hindu-Arabic_numerals": "The Hindu-Arabic numerals are the ten signs in use today together with the place-value system that goes with them, in which a digit's position fixes its value and a zero holds an empty place.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The earliest undisputed zero in India is in a Gwalior inscription of 876 CE, though the arithmetic is older evidence still: Brahmagupta treated the properties of zero early in the 7th century.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The system reached Baghdad with Indian astronomy in the 8th century and Europe through al-Khwarizmi's arithmetic, whose author's name in its Latin form gave such books the name algorism.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>",
"Sanskrit_literature": "Sanskrit literature is the body of writing in that language, divided between the Vedic texts and the classical literature that follows them — court epic, drama, lyric and fable — running from about 200 BCE to 1100 CE.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Sanskrit was a learned language beside living vernaculars, and the plays show the arrangement: kings and men of rank speak it while women and men of the people answer in the popular dialects.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Its dates are unusually hard to fix — for the most famous classical poets there is no historical authority, and Kalidasa's own date was argued over within a range of a thousand years.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>",
"Eastern_Zhou": "Eastern Zhou is the second phase of the Zhou dynasty in China, usually dated from 770 to 256 BCE and named for the court's move east to Luoyang after the western capital was lost in 771 BCE.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Royal authority became ceremonial: the king kept the dynastic sacrifices while the regional states kept the armies, and the graded scheme of rites, music and ritual bronzes that ranked king, lord, minister and gentleman ceased to be observed in tombs.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The period divides into the Spring and Autumn and Warring States phases, and ended when the last king died in 256 BCE and Qin annexed the royal land three years later.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>",
"Spring_and_Autumn_period": "Spring and Autumn period is the earlier half of the Eastern Zhou in China, named after the <i>Chunqiu</i> or <i>Spring and Autumn Annals</i>, the chronicle of the state of Lu, whose entries run from 722 to 481 BCE.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Royal authority was by then nominal, no new state arose and their number fell as large neighbours absorbed small ones; in 706 BCE the earl of Zheng gave battle to the king and wounded him.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> To hold the order together the states raised up a presiding chief, one lord directing the rest and doing the king's work in his name, of whom five are usually counted, though the list is disputed.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>",
"Warring_States_period": "Warring States period is the later half of the Eastern Zhou in China, usually dated from 475 to 221 BCE, and takes its name from the <i>Zhanguo Ce</i>, a collection of the era's speeches and stratagems.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> After three families divided the state of Jin and were recognised by the Zhou king in 403 BCE, fourteen states remained, seven of them dominant, and their rulers took in turn the royal title that had belonged to the Zhou alone.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Frontier walls, mounted troops and itinerant diplomats belong to these centuries, which ended when Qin defeated the last of its rivals and united China in 221 BCE.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>",
"Confucius": "Confucius is the Latinized form of Kong Fuzi, the Philosopher Kong, a teacher of the state of Lu in China whose personal name was Qiu and who was styled Zhongni.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> He held minor offices and rose to be minister of Crime in Lu, taught that a ruler should govern by example and ritual propriety rather than by force, and gave reciprocity as the one word to guide a life: do not do to others what you would not want done to yourself.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> He wrote no book, the <i>Analects</i> being a record of his conversations compiled after his death.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>",
"Confucianism": "Confucianism is the tradition that grew from the teaching of Confucius in China, and it dominated Chinese thought and government for more than two thousand years.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Its central terms are ren, the inner moral sentiment usually rendered benevolence, and li, the outward rule of conduct usually rendered ritual propriety, and its programme runs from a rectified heart through the cultivated person and family to the ordered state.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> It became the official system of thought under the Han, its canon settling as the Five Classics and later the Four Books, while its followers divided over whether human nature is good, as Mencius held, or bad, as Xunzi held.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>",
"Taoism": "Taoism is the Chinese tradition built on the Tao, the Way, whose founding book opens by declaring that the Tao which can be trodden is not the enduring and unchanging Tao.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Its central term is wu wei, rendered as doing nothing or inaction, and its two founding books are the <i>Tao Te Ching</i>, ascribed to Laozi, and the <i>Zhuangzi</i>, which prize gentleness, economy and shrinking from taking precedence of others.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> A religion with scriptures and a priesthood of its own grew out of the philosophy, and Taoism is today counted one of China's five major religions.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>",
"Legalism_(Chinese_philosophy)": "Legalism is the usual English rendering of <i>fajia</i>, the Chinese tradition of statecraft holding that a state should be governed by published standards and measured rewards and punishments rather than by its ruler's moral quality.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The historian Sima Tan coined the label and the librarian Liu Xiang applied it to ten works in the Han imperial catalogue, so it names a bibliographic category rather than a self-aware school.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Its core texts are associated with Shang Yang, Shen Buhai, Shen Dao and Han Fei, and argue for punishments knowing no degree or grade and for rewards granted only in exchange for military attainment.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>",
"Shang_Yang": "Shang Yang, also called Gongsun Yang and the Lord of Shang, was the statesman who held high office in Qin between 359 and 338 BCE and rewrote its laws.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The measures credited to him made emolument, office and rank follow military attainment, organised households into groups of five and ten held responsible for one another's crimes, and applied punishments without regard to degree or grade.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> When his patron Duke Xiao died he was accused of rebellion, refused a bed by his own identity law as he fled, and killed, his body then torn apart by chariots;<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Sima Qian wrote that he deserved his ill fame.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>",
"The_Art_of_War": "The <i>Art of War</i> is a Chinese treatise on strategy in thirteen short chapters, ascribed to Sun Wu, a general said to have served King Helü of Wu in the late 6th century BCE.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> It holds that war is a matter of life and death for a state, that all warfare is based on deception, and that supreme excellence lies in breaking an enemy's resistance without fighting.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Whether Sun Wu existed has been doubted since the Song, when it was noted that the Zuo Commentary never names him;<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> the book reached Europe in a French translation of 1772.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>",
"Qin_Shi_Huang": "Qin Shi Huang was the king of Qin who conquered the last of the warring states in 221 BCE, becoming the first ruler of a united China and taking the new title huangdi.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> He divided the empire into 36 commanderies under appointed officials, unified the laws, weights, measures, cart axles and script, and in 213 BCE had the histories of the other states burned.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> He died on tour in 210 BCE and was buried at Mount Li, east of modern Xi'an, and the dynasty he meant to last ten thousand generations fell within four years of his death.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>",
"Qin_dynasty": "The Qin dynasty ruled a united China from 221 to 206 BCE, the shortest of the great dynasties, after the king of Qin conquered the last of the warring states.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> It abolished the hereditary fiefs of the Zhou in favour of 36 commanderies under appointed officials, unified law, weights, measures and script, and built roads, frontier walls and the First Emperor's tomb with conscript and convict labour.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Revolts began in 209 BCE and the eunuch Zhao Gao killed the Second Emperor in 207, after which the empire passed to the Han, which kept the commanderies, the laws and the offices Qin had made.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>",
"Terracotta_Army": "The Terracotta Army is an array of about 7,000 life-sized ceramic warriors, officers and horses buried in three pits beside the mausoleum of the First Emperor of China, near modern Xi'an.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The figures stand in battle formation and carried working bronze weapons, among them as many as 40,000 arrowheads and more than 260 crossbow triggers from Pit 1 alone.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Their clay was processed centrally and then distributed to local workshops,<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> and the weapons were made in batches and marked, some of them with the name of the Qin central workshop.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>",
"Great_Wall_of_China": "The <b>Great Wall of China</b> is a discontinuous system of frontier fortifications in northern China, made up of walls, watchtowers, beacon towers, passes and forts raised by successive states and dynasties rather than by any single builder.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The oldest surviving stretches are Warring States work in rammed earth, one Qin section in Shaanxi running 426 kilometres (265 miles) with 347 watchtowers, whose solid earthen platforms differ sharply from the hollow brick towers of the Ming.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Much of the earthen fabric endures because cyanobacterial and moss crusts sheath roughly two-thirds of it, cutting its erodibility and raising its mechanical strength considerably.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>",
"Han_dynasty": "The <b>Han dynasty</b> ruled China for roughly four centuries, from 206 BCE to 220 CE, interrupted only by Wang Mang’s Xin dynasty of 9 to 23 CE, which divides it into the Western or Former Han and the Eastern or Later Han.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> It was the first dynasty of a unified China to outlive its founder, and most of the lasting characteristics of the imperial Chinese state and of Chinese society were settled during it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Its mark went deep enough that the Chinese still take their ethnic name from it, and its scholars fixed both the classical texts and the categories in which earlier thought has been discussed ever since.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>",
"Silk_Road": "The <b>Silk Road</b> is the modern name for the network of overland and maritime routes along which goods, people, ideas and diseases moved between China, Central Asia, India, Iran and the Mediterranean.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> It was never one road: the geographer who coined <i>die Seidenstrasse</i> in 1877 used the plural as readily as the singular, warning that no route was the only one or even the most important.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Its mountain sections follow paths worn by centuries of seasonal herding rather than laid out by any state, and a latrine at a Han relay station has yielded the eggs of a parasite carried there from at least 1,500 kilometres (930 miles) away.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>",
"Scholar-official": "A <b>scholar-official</b> was one of the ranked and salaried men, schooled in the classics, through whom the Chinese empire was administered.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Han emperors founded central schools and began bringing scholars into government by examination, on a far smaller scale than later dynasties would, and by the Song these <i>shi dafu</i> stood at the centre of political life, with entry turning on a doctoral concours in which prose style alone could disqualify a candidate.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The post carried a duty as well as a career, since under every dynasty an official could send up a memorial criticising the emperor’s own decisions, a practice called remonstrance and distinct from the censure of subordinates.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>",
"Papermaking": "<b>Papermaking</b> macerates plant fibre into a pulp and mats a thin layer of it into a sheet, giving a writing surface that is cheap to make and light to carry.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Its raw materials have included tree bark, hemp, ramie, rags and worn-out fishing nets, and the oldest sheets that survive are of hemp fibre with a little ramie mixed in.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> What a sheet was made of, and how, stays legible in it long afterwards, so that the fibres and the make of the paper a manuscript is written on can be used to date the manuscript itself.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>",
"End_of_the_Han_dynasty": "The <b>end of the Han dynasty</b> ran from the Yellow Turban rising of 184 CE to the abdication of the last emperor in 220, when the throne passed to the son of the warlord who had been holding him.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The court had already been paralysed by the sale of offices and by a struggle between the palace eunuchs and the officials that ended in 189 with a massacre inside the palace and the seizure of the capital by a general from the frontier.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The emperor reigned on for three decades as a prisoner and a prize fought over by rival commanders, and a long period of disunity followed.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>",
"Three_Kingdoms": "The <b>Three Kingdoms</b> were the three states that divided China between 220 and 280 CE: Cao Wei in the north, Shu Han in the west and Sun Wu in the south-east, none of which ever mastered the others.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The period is known from a history in 65 scrolls compiled by Chen Shou, who had served Shu before entering Jin service, and whose account gives the Wei rulers imperial annals while the other two rulers get only biographies.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Most of what a reader is likely to know of it, however, comes from a novel written more than a thousand years later.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>",
"Chinese_Buddhism": "Chinese Buddhism is the body of doctrine, practice and writing that grew out of the transmission of the religion from India and Central Asia into China, where it took root through the translation of sutras from the 1st century CE onwards.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> It flourished in the centuries of disunity after the Han, and Yang Xuanzhi's memoir of about 545 describes some forty great monasteries at Luoyang between 494 and 534.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> China's official dynastic histories were slow to treat it as a subject in itself, so most of what is known comes instead from the Buddhist canon and from the manuscripts recovered at Dunhuang and Turfan.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>",
"Kingdom_of_Kush": "The Kingdom of Kush was the state of the middle Nile, in what is now northern Sudan, whose kings and queens ruled hundreds of kilometres of the upper Nile valley.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Its kings descended from local Nubian chiefs who had built an independent state in the 9th century BCE, and between about 750 and 650 BCE they ruled Egypt too, as its 25th dynasty.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Historians divide its history into a Napatan phase, centred below the holy mountain of Gebel Barkal near the Fourth Cataract, and a Meroitic one, though the name Kush is far older than the kingdom and is first attested about 1925 BCE.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>",
"Meroë": "Meroë was the royal city of the kingdom of Kush, on the middle Nile in what is now Sudan, and it gave its name both to the Meroitic period, the later and longer of the kingdom’s two phases, and to Meroitic, the chief tongue of the middle Nile in antiquity.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Kings and queens were buried in cemeteries beside the city, and the southern royal necropolis is where the change from the earlier Napatan phase can be followed.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> It stood among temples of Amun, one of them at el-Hassa some 25 km (16 miles) to the south, which has a processional way and a ceremonial palace of its own.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>",
"Kingdom_of_Aksum": "The Kingdom of Aksum was a highland state centred on the town of Aksum, in what is now the Tigray region of northern Ethiopia, where a civilisation flourished for some 850 years between 150 BCE and 700 CE.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Its builders carved and raised the largest stelae on earth, the greatest of them 33 m (108 feet) tall and weighing 520 tons, hauled 5 km (3 miles) from the hills of Gobo Dura.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> It struck its own gold and silver coinage, and the conversion of its king Ezana in the middle of the 4th century marks the beginning of the Christian era in Ethiopia.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>",
"Nok_culture": "The Nok culture is an archaeological culture of central Nigeria whose sites are dated from about 1500 to about 1 BCE.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> It is known above all for terracotta figures of people and animals, the earliest large figurative sculpture made in Africa outside Egypt, the first of them appearing about 900 BCE.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Its sites also carry early evidence of iron working in West Africa and plant remains dominated by pearl millet, which arrived already threshed and has been read as feasting rather than everyday settlement, while acid soils have destroyed all bone and left it unknown whether its people kept animals.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>",
"Bantu_expansion": "The Bantu expansion is the spread of the Bantu languages and of their speakers across most of sub-Saharan Africa, beginning in western Africa between about 6,000 and 4,000 years ago.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> It is the largest demographic event of the late Holocene on the continent, and more than 500 Bantu languages are spoken today by some 240 million people.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The first spread is generally taken to have been demic rather than a spread of words alone, running through the Congo rainforest before the savannas east and south, and genome-wide work shows the speakers taking in substantial gene flow from the groups already living where they went.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>",
"Garamantes": "The Garamantes were the people of the Fezzan, in what is now south-western Libya, whose kingdom had its capital at Garama, still visible at Germa, and whose reach ran well beyond the modern borders.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> They watered their oases with foggaras, underground channels dug between pairs of vertical shafts to tap an aquifer and carry it out to daylight, a technique older than they were.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Greek and Roman writers knew them as raiders and were unreliable about them, and what political form they had, whether tribe, chiefdom or a Saharan state on its own terms, is still argued over.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>",
"Land_of_Punt": "The Land of Punt was a country the ancient Egyptians traded with for well over a thousand years, calling it the land of the god and prizing it above all for incense.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Its position has been argued over for as long as it has been studied, and was long placed in Somalia or on the southern coast of Arabia.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Chemical and genetic work on the baboons the Egyptians brought home and mummified has since pointed to the African shore of the Red Sea, in a region taking in Eritrea, Ethiopia and their neighbours.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>",
"Trans-Saharan_trade": "Trans-Saharan trade is the exchange of goods, people and ideas across the Sahara, linking the Mediterranean and Atlantic coasts of North Africa with the Sahel and savanna to the south.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Its best-recorded phase belongs to the Islamic Middle Ages, when caravans carried gold, salt, cloth, copper and enslaved people between the two margins, and beads of Egyptian, Levantine and Middle Eastern glass reached sites in Mali and Senegal between the 7th and 13th centuries CE.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Whether regular exchange crossed the desert before the Arab conquest of North Africa is disputed, since classical writers report almost nothing of the lands beyond its southern fringe and the archaeology remains sparse.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>",
"Olmecs": "The Olmecs were the earliest monument-building society of Mesoamerica, occupying the humid Gulf lowlands of southern Veracruz and Tabasco in Mexico between about 1500 and 400 BCE.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Their three principal centres, San Lorenzo, La Venta and Tres Zapotes, raised earthen platforms and basalt sculpture, above all the colossal human heads, seventeen of which are known and which most specialists read as portraits of rulers.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> How far Olmec art and organisation shaped the rest of Mesoamerica is disputed, since objects in their style are found across the region while several institutions once credited to them appear as early or earlier elsewhere.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>",
"Mesoamerica": "Mesoamerica is a historically and culturally defined region of the Americas covering central and southern Mexico, Belize, Guatemala and El Salvador together with the border country of Honduras, western Nicaragua and north-western Costa Rica.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> It is a culture area rather than a political unit, its many peoples and languages connected by farming and commerce and sharing such traits as a 260-day ritual count kept beside a 365-day year<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> and a rubber ballgame, of which more than 2,300 probable courts are known.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Its conventional periods run from the Archaic through the Preclassic, Classic and Postclassic, the last of which ended with the Spanish conquest between 1519 and 1521.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>"
};

